// AUTO-GENERATED by scripts/build.mjs from /content. Edit the Markdown, then run: npm run build
window.CL_LESSONS = [{"id":"setup-welcome","title":"How to learn to code (properly)","level":"beginner","minutes":8,"summary":"What experienced developers actually know, how this site works, and the learning loop that gets you there.","html":"<h2 id=\"what-15-years-of-experience-really-means\">What &quot;15 years of experience&quot; really means</h2>\n<p>Experienced developers have not memorised every function. They look things up constantly. What they have is a set of habits and instincts:</p>\n<ul><li>They <strong>break big problems into small ones</strong> until each piece is obvious.</li><li>They <strong>read error messages</strong> calmly and fully, because the error usually says what is wrong.</li><li>They <strong>know where to look</strong>: official docs, the source code, the Network tab, the logs.</li><li>They <strong>build things</strong>, ship them, and learn from what breaks.</li><li>They use the <strong>same core tools every day</strong> (editor, terminal, Git, browser DevTools) so well that those tools disappear.</li></ul>\n<p>You cannot download years. You can copy the habits from day one, and that is what Code Ladder is built around.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-you-will-be-able-to-build\">What you will be able to build</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Track</th><th>You will be able to</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Foundations</td><td>Use the terminal, Git, packages and debugging tools like a professional</td></tr><tr><td>Web Development</td><td>Build fast, accessible, responsive websites and full-stack apps</td></tr><tr><td>Cryptography &amp; Blockchain</td><td>Understand hashes, keys and signatures, then write, test and secure smart contracts</td></tr><tr><td>Tokenomics</td><td>Design and stress-test token supply, vesting, liquidity and incentives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"the-learning-loop\">The learning loop</h2>\n<p>Every lesson follows the same loop. Do not skip steps.</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Read</strong> the lesson once without coding.</li><li><strong>Type</strong> every example yourself (no copy-paste) in the <a href=\"#playground\">Playground</a> or VS Code.</li><li><strong>Break it on purpose.</strong> Delete a bracket, misspell a name, and read the error you get.</li><li><strong>Pass the quiz.</strong> Passing marks the lesson complete.</li><li><strong>Build</strong> one of the level's <a href=\"#projects\">projects</a> without the lesson open.</li></ol>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">The 20-minute rule</p><p>When you get stuck, try for 20 focused minutes: read the error, search it, simplify the problem. Then ask for help. Never suffer for hours, and never ask after 30 seconds.</p></aside>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"how-to-ask-a-good-question\">How to ask a good question</h2>\n<p>Whether you ask a friend, a forum or an AI, include these four things. Writing them down often solves the problem before you send it.</p>\n<ol><li><strong>What I am trying to do</strong> (the goal, not just the step).</li><li><strong>What I expected</strong> to happen.</li><li><strong>What actually happened</strong>, with the exact error text.</li><li><strong>What I already tried.</strong></li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"a-realistic-timeline\">A realistic timeline</h2>\n<p>At one to two focused hours a day, most people can build and deploy solid websites within 4 to 6 months and write and test simple smart contracts within 8 to 10. The <a href=\"#roadmap\">12-month roadmap</a> lays it out. Consistency beats intensity: 45 minutes every day beats a 7-hour Sunday.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-pro\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Using AI while you learn</p><p>AI assistants are excellent tutors. Ask them to explain an error, quiz you, or review code you wrote. Do not ask them to write projects you could not explain line by line. If you keep code you do not understand, you are borrowing skill you will have to repay later.</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"What is the main thing that separates experienced developers from beginners?","options":["They have memorised the documentation","Habits: breaking problems down, reading errors, knowing where to look, shipping","They type faster"],"answer":1,"explain":"Seniors look things up all the time. The difference is how they approach problems and how well they use their tools."},{"q":"You have been stuck on a bug for 20 focused minutes. What should you do?","options":["Keep going alone for the rest of the day","Delete the project and start again","Ask for help, explaining the goal, what you expected, what happened and what you tried"],"answer":2,"explain":"The 20-minute rule balances struggling (which teaches you) with not wasting hours."},{"q":"Why type the examples instead of pasting them?","options":["Pasting is against the rules","Typing forces you to notice every detail, so it sticks","Pasted code does not run"],"answer":1,"explain":"Your brain processes code far more deeply when your hands write it."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Your very first line of code","file":"hello.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Every lesson has exercises like this one. Type the code in the editor, then press <strong>Check my code</strong> (or <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Enter</kbd>). Wrong answers get a tip; after <strong>5 wrong tries in a row</strong> the answer appears as faded ghost text for you to type over.</p>\n<p>Type this exact line: <code>console.log('Hello, world!');</code></p>\n<p>It tells the computer to print the text <em>Hello, world!</em>. After it passes, look at the <strong>Output</strong> box.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"console.log('Hello, world!');","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*console","text":"Start with the word <code>console</code>, all lower case."},{"re":"console\\.log","text":"After <code>console</code> comes a dot and then <code>log</code>: <code>console.log</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(","text":"Open a round bracket straight after <code>log</code>, with no space: <code>console.log(</code>"},{"re":"['\"]Hello, world!['\"]","text":"The text goes inside quotes, with the comma and exclamation mark: <code>'Hello, world!'</code>"},{"re":"\\)\\s*;?\\s*$","text":"Close the round bracket at the end and finish with a semicolon: <code>);</code>"}],"explain":"<p><code>console.log(...)</code> is a built-in instruction that prints whatever is inside the brackets. Text goes in quotes so the computer knows it is text, not more code. Almost every programmer started with this exact line.</p>"},{"title":"Make the computer do maths","file":"maths.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Print the answer to 7 times 6. In code, multiply is written with a star: <code>*</code>. Numbers do not need quotes.</p>\n<p>Type: <code>console.log(7 * 6);</code></p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"console.log(7 * 6);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"console\\.log\\(","text":"Start with <code>console.log(</code> exactly like the last exercise."},{"re":"7\\s*\\*\\s*6","text":"Inside the brackets: <code>7 * 6</code>. No quotes, because these are numbers."},{"re":"\\)\\s*;?\\s*$","text":"Close the bracket and add a semicolon: <code>);</code>"}],"explain":"<p>The computer works out <code>7 * 6</code> first, then prints the result: <strong>42</strong>. Without quotes, <code>7 * 6</code> is a calculation. With quotes, <code>'7 * 6'</code> would just be text and would print as it is.</p>"}],"text":"What 15 years of experience really means Experienced developers have not memorised every function. They look things up constantly. What they have is a set of habits and instincts: They break big problems into small ones until each piece is obvious. They read error messages calmly and fully, because the error usually says what is wrong. They know where to look : official docs, the source code, the Network tab, the logs. They build things , ship them, and learn from what breaks. They use the same core tools every day (editor, terminal, Git, browser DevTools) so well that those tools disappear. You cannot download years. You can copy the habits from day one, and that is what Code Ladder is built around. What you will be able to build Track You will be able to Foundations Use the terminal, Git, packages and debugging tools like a professional Web Development Build fast, accessible, responsive websites and full-stack apps Cryptography Blockchain Understand hashes, keys and signatures, then write, test and secure smart contracts Tokenomics Design and stress-test token supply, vesting, liquidity and incentives The learning loop Every lesson follows the same loop. Do not skip steps. Read the lesson once without coding. Type every example yourself (no copy-paste) in the Playground or VS Code. Break it on purpose. Delete a bracket, misspell a name, and read the error you get. Pass the quiz. Passing marks the lesson complete. Build one of the level's projects without the lesson open. The 20-minute rule When you get stuck, try for 20 focused minutes: read the error, search it, simplify the problem. Then ask for help. Never suffer for hours, and never ask after 30 seconds. How to ask a good question Whether you ask a friend, a forum or an AI, include these four things. Writing them down often solves the problem before you send it. What I am trying to do (the goal, not just the step). What I expected to happen. What actually happened , with the exact error text. What I already tried. A realistic timeline At one to two focused hours a day, most people can build and deploy solid websites within 4 to 6 months and write and test simple smart contracts within 8 to 10. The 12-month roadmap lays it out. Consistency beats intensity: 45 minutes every day beats a 7-hour Sunday. Using AI while you learn AI assistants are excellent tutors. Ask them to explain an error, quiz you, or review code you wrote. Do not ask them to write projects you could not explain line by line. If you keep code you do not understand, you are borrowing skill you will have to repay later.","track":"setup"},{"id":"setup-coding-basics","title":"Coding basics 1: integers, decimals, characters and strings","level":"beginner","minutes":35,"summary":"Every value in a program has a type. Learn integers, decimals (floats), characters, strings and booleans by typing real code.","html":"<h2 id=\"programs-are-instructions-plus-data\">Programs are instructions plus data</h2>\n<p>A program is a list of instructions that work on <strong>data</strong>: prices, names, quantities, true-or-false answers. Every piece of data has a <strong>type</strong>, and the type decides what you can do with it. You can multiply two numbers; you cannot multiply two names.</p>\n<p>These five types exist, under slightly different names, in almost every language:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>What it holds</th><th>JavaScript</th><th>Python</th><th>Java / C</th><th>Solidity</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Integer</strong></td><td>Whole numbers: <code>12</code>, <code>-3</code>, <code>0</code></td><td><code>number</code> (or <code>bigint</code>)</td><td><code>int</code></td><td><code>int</code>, <code>long</code></td><td><code>uint256</code>, <code>int256</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Decimal (float)</strong></td><td>Numbers with a fractional part: <code>19.99</code></td><td><code>number</code></td><td><code>float</code></td><td><code>double</code>, <code>float</code></td><td>none (no decimals)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Character</strong></td><td>One letter or symbol: <code>'A'</code></td><td>a string of length 1</td><td>a string of length 1</td><td><code>char</code></td><td><code>bytes1</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>String</strong></td><td>Text: <code>'Amara Studio'</code></td><td><code>string</code></td><td><code>str</code></td><td><code>String</code> / <code>char[]</code></td><td><code>string</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Boolean</strong></td><td><code>true</code> or <code>false</code></td><td><code>boolean</code></td><td><code>bool</code> (<code>True</code>)</td><td><code>boolean</code> / <code>bool</code></td><td><code>bool</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>We will use <strong>JavaScript</strong> for the exercises, because it runs right here in your browser. Everything you learn transfers to other languages.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">How exercises work</p><p>Each exercise has an editor that works like VS Code. Type the code, then press <strong>Check my code</strong> (or <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Enter</kbd>). Wrong answers get a tip. After <strong>5 wrong tries in a row</strong>, the answer appears as faded <strong>ghost text</strong> and you type over it, character by character. <strong>Run it</strong> shows what your code prints.</p></aside>\n<h2 id=\"variables-naming-your-data\">Variables: naming your data</h2>\n<p>A <strong>variable</strong> is a labelled box that holds a value. In JavaScript you create one with <code>const</code> (it will not be reassigned) or <code>let</code> (it can change).</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const brand = 'Amara Studio';   // a string\nlet stock = 12;                 // an integer\nconst price = 34.5;             // a decimal\nconst inStock = true;           // a boolean</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>console.log(...)</code> prints a value so you can see it. You will use it constantly.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"integers-whole-numbers\">Integers: whole numbers</h2>\n<p>Integers are counts: items in stock, people, days, votes. The maths operators:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operator</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>+</code></td><td>add</td><td><code>7 + 2</code></td><td><code>9</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>-</code></td><td>subtract</td><td><code>7 - 2</code></td><td><code>5</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>*</code></td><td>multiply</td><td><code>7 * 2</code></td><td><code>14</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>/</code></td><td>divide</td><td><code>7 / 2</code></td><td><code>3.5</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>%</code></td><td>remainder (modulo)</td><td><code>7 % 2</code></td><td><code>1</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>**</code></td><td>power</td><td><code>7 ** 2</code></td><td><code>49</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>In JavaScript, <code>7 / 2</code> gives <code>3.5</code>. To get a whole-number answer, round <strong>down</strong> with <code>Math.floor(7 / 2)</code>, which gives <code>3</code>. (Python has a special operator for this: <code>7 // 2</code>.)</p>\n<p>The <strong>remainder</strong> operator <code>%</code> is more useful than it looks: <code>n % 2 === 0</code> tells you if a number is even, and it answers &quot;how many are left over?&quot;</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"decimals-floats-and-their-famous-trap\">Decimals (floats) and their famous trap</h2>\n<p>Numbers with a decimal point are called <strong>floating-point</strong> numbers, or <strong>floats</strong>. Computers store them in binary, which cannot represent some decimals exactly, just as 1/3 cannot be written exactly in decimal (0.3333...).</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">console.log(0.1 + 0.2);   // 0.30000000000000004</code></pre></div>\n<p>That tiny error is why professionals <strong>never store money as a float</strong>. Store pence (or cents) as integers, and convert only for display. To show a decimal neatly, use <code>.toFixed(2)</code>, which rounds to 2 decimal places and returns a <strong>string</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"2\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"characters-letters-are-secretly-numbers\">Characters: letters are secretly numbers</h2>\n<p>A <strong>character</strong> (often called a <strong>char</strong>) is a single letter, digit, symbol or space: <code>'A'</code>, <code>'7'</code>, <code>'?'</code>, <code>' '</code>.</p>\n<p>Computers only store numbers, so every character has a number code. The standard is <strong>Unicode</strong> (its first 128 codes are the older <strong>ASCII</strong> table):</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Character</th><th>Code</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>'0'</code> to <code>'9'</code></td><td>48 to 57</td></tr><tr><td><code>'A'</code> to <code>'Z'</code></td><td>65 to 90</td></tr><tr><td><code>'a'</code> to <code>'z'</code></td><td>97 to 122</td></tr><tr><td>space</td><td>32</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>So <code>'A'</code> is stored as 65 and <code>'a'</code> as 97. That is why sorting can put &quot;Zebra&quot; before &quot;apple&quot;: 90 comes before 97.</p>\n<p>Languages like Java and C have a real <code>char</code> type (<code>char grade = 'A';</code>). JavaScript and Python simply use a string of length 1. To go between characters and codes:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>JavaScript</th><th>Python</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Character to code</td><td><code>'A'.charCodeAt(0)</code> gives <code>65</code></td><td><code>ord('A')</code> gives <code>65</code></td></tr><tr><td>Code to character</td><td><code>String.fromCharCode(66)</code> gives <code>'B'</code></td><td><code>chr(66)</code> gives <code>'B'</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"3\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"strings-text\">Strings: text</h2>\n<p>A <strong>string</strong> is a sequence of characters. You can write it with single quotes <code>'...'</code>, double quotes <code>&quot;...&quot;</code> or backticks (called template literals, which let you insert values with <code>${}</code>).</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const brand = 'amara studio';\nbrand.length;             // 12 (characters, including the space)\nbrand[0];                 // 'a' (position 0 is the first character)\nbrand.toUpperCase();      // 'AMARA STUDIO'\nbrand.includes('studio'); // true\nbrand.slice(0, 5);        // 'amara'\n`Welcome to ${brand}!`    // 'Welcome to amara studio!'</code></pre></div>\n<p>Positions start at <strong>0</strong>, not 1. That trips up every beginner once.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"4\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"booleans-true-or-false\">Booleans: true or false</h2>\n<p>A <strong>boolean</strong> is either <code>true</code> or <code>false</code>. Comparisons produce booleans, and booleans drive every decision a program makes.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">12 &gt; 5            // true\n12 === 12         // true  (=== means &quot;is exactly equal to&quot;)\n'12' === 12       // false (a string is not a number)\n12 !== 5          // true  (!== means &quot;is not equal to&quot;)</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"converting-between-types\">Converting between types</h2>\n<p>Data from forms, files and URLs always arrives as <strong>text</strong>, even when it looks like a number. <code>'42' + 1</code> gives <code>'421'</code> (joining text), not <code>43</code>. Convert first:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Conversion</th><th>JavaScript</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Text to number</td><td><code>Number('42')</code></td><td><code>42</code></td></tr><tr><td>Text to whole number, ignoring letters after it</td><td><code>parseInt('42px')</code></td><td><code>42</code></td></tr><tr><td>Number to text</td><td><code>String(42)</code></td><td><code>'42'</code></td></tr><tr><td>What type is this?</td><td><code>typeof 42</code></td><td><code>'number'</code></td></tr><tr><td>A failed conversion</td><td><code>Number('abc')</code></td><td><code>NaN</code> (&quot;Not a Number&quot;)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"5\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"the-same-ideas-in-python\">The same ideas in Python</h2>\n<p>You will meet Python for scripts, data and tokenomics models. The concepts are identical; only the spelling changes:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\"><pre><code class=\"language-python\">stock = 12                 # int (no const or let in Python)\nprice = 34.5               # float\nletter = 'A'               # a string of length 1\nin_stock = True            # bool: capital T\nprint(ord(letter))         # 65\nprint(stock // 5)          # 2  (whole-number division)\nprint(int('42') + 1)       # 43</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"6\"></div>","quiz":[{"q":"Which value is an integer?","options":["19.99","12","'12'"],"answer":1,"explain":"19.99 is a decimal (float) and '12' is a string because of the quotes."},{"q":"What does 17 % 5 give?","options":["3.4","2","3"],"answer":1,"explain":"% gives the remainder: 5 goes into 17 three times (15), with 2 left over."},{"q":"Why should you store £34.50 as 3450 pence instead of 34.5?","options":["Floats cannot store some decimals exactly, which causes rounding errors with money","Integers use more memory","JavaScript cannot show decimals"],"answer":0,"explain":"0.1 + 0.2 is 0.30000000000000004 in floating point. Whole pence are always exact."},{"q":"What is 'B'.charCodeAt(0)?","options":["65","66","98"],"answer":1,"explain":"Capital A is 65, so B is 66. Lower-case b is 98."},{"q":"A form field gives '5'. What does '5' + 1 produce in JavaScript?","options":["6","'51'","An error"],"answer":1,"explain":"With a string involved, + joins text. Convert with Number('5') first."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Store an integer","file":"stock.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Create a <strong>constant</strong> called <code>stock</code> that holds the integer <code>12</code>. Then print it with <code>console.log</code>.</p>\n<p>Two lines. No quotes around <code>12</code>: quotes would make it text, not a number.</p>","starter":"// Line 1: create the constant\n// Line 2: print it","html":"","solution":"const stock = 12;\nconsole.log(stock);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*const\\s","text":"Start the first line with the keyword <code>const</code>."},{"re":"const\\s+stock\\s*=","text":"Name the constant <code>stock</code>, followed by <code>=</code>."},{"re":"stock\\s*=\\s*12\\s*;?\\s*$","text":"Give it the value <code>12</code> with no quotes."},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*stock\\s*\\)","text":"Print it on the second line with <code>console.log(stock);</code>."}],"explain":"<p><code>const</code> creates a variable that cannot be reassigned. <code>stock</code> is its name. <code>=</code> stores the value <code>12</code> in it. <code>console.log(stock)</code> looks up the value in the box and prints it: <strong>12</strong>.</p>"},{"title":"Boxes and leftovers","file":"cupcakes.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>A bakery has <strong>40</strong> cupcakes and each box holds <strong>6</strong>. Write three lines:</p>\n<ol><li>A constant <code>boxes</code> equal to <code>Math.floor(40 / 6)</code> (the number of full boxes).</li><li>A constant <code>leftover</code> equal to <code>40 % 6</code> (the cupcakes that do not fit).</li><li>Print both: <code>console.log(boxes, leftover);</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const boxes = Math.floor(40 / 6);\nconst leftover = 40 % 6;\nconsole.log(boxes, leftover);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+boxes\\s*=","text":"Line 1 starts with <code>const boxes =</code>."},{"re":"Math\\.floor\\(\\s*40\\s*\\/\\s*6\\s*\\)","text":"Use <code>Math.floor(40 / 6)</code>. Capital M, and a dot before <code>floor</code>."},{"re":"const\\s+leftover\\s*=\\s*40\\s*%\\s*6","text":"Line 2: <code>const leftover = 40 % 6;</code> The <code>%</code> sign gives the remainder."},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*boxes\\s*,\\s*leftover\\s*\\)","text":"Print both values, separated by a comma, inside one <code>console.log(...)</code>."}],"explain":"<p>40 divided by 6 is 6.67. <code>Math.floor</code> rounds down to <strong>6</strong> full boxes. 6 boxes hold 36, so <code>40 % 6</code> is the <strong>4</strong> left over. The output is <code>6 4</code>.</p>"},{"title":"See the float trap yourself","file":"floats.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write three lines:</p>\n<ol><li>A constant <code>total</code> equal to <code>0.1 + 0.2</code>.</li><li>Print <code>total</code>.</li><li>Print <code>total.toFixed(2)</code>.</li></ol>\n<p>Then press <strong>Run it</strong> and compare the two outputs.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const total = 0.1 + 0.2;\nconsole.log(total);\nconsole.log(total.toFixed(2));","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+total\\s*=\\s*0\\.1\\s*\\+\\s*0\\.2","text":"Line 1: <code>const total = 0.1 + 0.2;</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*console\\.log\\(\\s*total\\s*\\)\\s*;?\\s*$","text":"Line 2 prints the raw value: <code>console.log(total);</code>"},{"re":"total\\.toFixed\\(\\s*2\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3 uses the method <code>toFixed(2)</code> on <code>total</code>: <code>total.toFixed(2)</code>."}],"explain":"<p>The first line of output is <code>0.30000000000000004</code>: a real floating-point rounding error. <code>toFixed(2)</code> rounds it for display to <code>&quot;0.30&quot;</code>. For money, store <code>30</code> pence instead and you never meet this problem.</p>"},{"title":"Characters as numbers","file":"chars.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write three lines:</p>\n<ol><li>A constant <code>letter</code> holding the character <code>'A'</code>.</li><li>Print its code with <code>letter.charCodeAt(0)</code>.</li><li>Print the character for code <code>66</code> with <code>String.fromCharCode(66)</code>.</li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const letter = 'A';\nconsole.log(letter.charCodeAt(0));\nconsole.log(String.fromCharCode(66));","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+letter\\s*=\\s*['\"]A['\"]","text":"Line 1: <code>const letter = 'A';</code> with the A inside quotes (it is text)."},{"re":"letter\\.charCodeAt\\(\\s*0\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>console.log(letter.charCodeAt(0));</code> The <code>0</code> means &quot;the first character&quot;."},{"re":"String\\.fromCharCode\\(\\s*66\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3: <code>console.log(String.fromCharCode(66));</code> Capital S on <code>String</code>, capital C on <code>Char</code> and <code>Code</code>."}],"explain":"<p><code>charCodeAt(0)</code> reads the number stored for the character at position 0: <strong>65</strong>. <code>String.fromCharCode(66)</code> goes the other way and gives <strong>B</strong>. Every string you type is stored as numbers like these, which matters later for hashing and encryption.</p>"},{"title":"Work with a string","file":"brand.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write four lines:</p>\n<ol><li>A constant <code>brand</code> holding <code>'amara studio'</code> (all lower case).</li><li>Print it in capitals with <code>brand.toUpperCase()</code>.</li><li>Print how many characters it has with <code>brand.length</code>.</li><li>Print its first character with <code>brand[0]</code>.</li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const brand = 'amara studio';\nconsole.log(brand.toUpperCase());\nconsole.log(brand.length);\nconsole.log(brand[0]);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+brand\\s*=\\s*['\"]amara studio['\"]","text":"Line 1: <code>const brand = 'amara studio';</code>"},{"re":"brand\\.toUpperCase\\(\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2 needs <code>brand.toUpperCase()</code> inside <code>console.log(...)</code>. It is a method, so it ends with <code>()</code>."},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*brand\\.length\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3: <code>console.log(brand.length);</code> No brackets after <code>length</code>: it is a property, not a method."},{"re":"brand\\[\\s*0\\s*\\]","text":"Line 4: square brackets with the position: <code>brand[0]</code>."}],"explain":"<p>Output: <code>AMARA STUDIO</code>, then <code>12</code>, then <code>a</code>. Methods like <code>toUpperCase()</code> do something and need <code>()</code>. Properties like <code>length</code> just hold a value. Square brackets pick a character by position, starting at 0.</p>"},{"title":"Turn text into a number","file":"convert.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>A form gives you the text <code>'42'</code>. Write four lines:</p>\n<ol><li>A constant <code>input</code> holding the <strong>string</strong> <code>'42'</code>.</li><li>A constant <code>qty</code> equal to <code>Number(input)</code>.</li><li>Print <code>qty + 1</code>.</li><li>Print <code>typeof qty</code>.</li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const input = '42';\nconst qty = Number(input);\nconsole.log(qty + 1);\nconsole.log(typeof qty);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+input\\s*=\\s*['\"]42['\"]","text":"Line 1: the value is text, so it needs quotes: <code>const input = '42';</code>"},{"re":"const\\s+qty\\s*=\\s*Number\\(\\s*input\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>const qty = Number(input);</code> Capital N on <code>Number</code>."},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*qty\\s*\\+\\s*1\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3: <code>console.log(qty + 1);</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*typeof\\s+qty\\s*\\)","text":"Line 4: <code>typeof</code> is a keyword, followed by a space and the variable: <code>console.log(typeof qty);</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Output: <code>43</code> and <code>number</code>. Without <code>Number(...)</code>, <code>input + 1</code> would have given <code>&quot;421&quot;</code>, because <code>+</code> joins text. Converting input before doing maths is a habit that prevents a whole family of bugs.</p>"},{"title":"Write the same thing in Python","file":"basics.py","lang":"python","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Python does not need <code>const</code>, <code>let</code> or semicolons. Write three lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>stock = 12</code></li><li><code>letter = 'A'</code></li><li>Print the character code of <code>letter</code> with <code>print(ord(letter))</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"stock = 12\nletter = 'A'\nprint(ord(letter))","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^stock\\s*=\\s*12\\s*$","text":"Line 1: just <code>stock = 12</code>. No <code>const</code>, no semicolon."},{"re":"^letter\\s*=\\s*['\"]A['\"]\\s*$","text":"Line 2: <code>letter = 'A'</code>"},{"re":"print\\(\\s*ord\\(\\s*letter\\s*\\)\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3: Python prints with <code>print(...)</code>, and <code>ord(...)</code> gives a character's code: <code>print(ord(letter))</code>."}],"explain":"<p>This would print <strong>65</strong>, just like <code>charCodeAt(0)</code> in JavaScript. Python runs on your computer rather than in the browser, so there is no Run button here. Install Python with uv from the <a href=\"#toolkit\">Install everything</a> page and try it with <code>uv run python</code>.</p>"}],"text":"Programs are instructions plus data A program is a list of instructions that work on data : prices, names, quantities, true-or-false answers. Every piece of data has a type , and the type decides what you can do with it. You can multiply two numbers; you cannot multiply two names. These five types exist, under slightly different names, in almost every language: Type What it holds JavaScript Python Java / C Solidity Integer Whole numbers: 12 , -3 , 0 number (or bigint ) int int , long uint256 , int256 Decimal (float) Numbers with a fractional part: 19.99 number float double , float none (no decimals) Character One letter or symbol: 'A' a string of length 1 a string of length 1 char bytes1 String Text: 'Amara Studio' string str String / char[] string Boolean true or false boolean bool ( True ) boolean / bool bool We will use JavaScript for the exercises, because it runs right here in your browser. Everything you learn transfers to other languages. How exercises work Each exercise has an editor that works like VS Code. Type the code, then press Check my code (or Ctrl + Enter ). Wrong answers get a tip. After 5 wrong tries in a row , the answer appears as faded ghost text and you type over it, character by character. Run it shows what your code prints. Variables: naming your data A variable is a labelled box that holds a value. In JavaScript you create one with const (it will not be reassigned) or let (it can change). const brand = 'Amara Studio'; // a string let stock = 12; // an integer const price = 34.5; // a decimal const inStock = true; // a boolean console.log(...) prints a value so you can see it. You will use it constantly. Integers: whole numbers Integers are counts: items in stock, people, days, votes. The maths operators: Operator Meaning Example Result + add 7 + 2 9 - subtract 7 - 2 5 * multiply 7 * 2 14 / divide 7 / 2 3.5 % remainder (modulo) 7 % 2 1 ** power 7 ** 2 49 In JavaScript, 7 / 2 gives 3.5 . To get a whole-number answer, round down with Math.floor(7 / 2) , which gives 3 . (Python has a special operator for this: 7 // 2 .) The remainder operator % is more useful than it looks: n % 2 === 0 tells you if a number is even, and it answers how many are left over? Decimals (floats) and their famous trap Numbers with a decimal point are called floating-point numbers, or floats . Computers store them in binary, which cannot represent some decimals exactly, just as 1/3 cannot be written exactly in decimal (0.3333...). console.log(0.1 + 0.2); // 0.30000000000000004 That tiny error is why professionals never store money as a float . Store pence (or cents) as integers, and convert only for display. To show a decimal neatly, use .toFixed(2) , which rounds to 2 decimal places and returns a string . Characters: letters are secretly numbers A character (often called a char ) is a single letter, digit, symbol or space: 'A' , '7' , '?' , ' ' . Computers only store numbers, so every character has a number code. The standard is Unicode (its first 128 codes are the older ASCII table): Character Code '0' to '9' 48 to 57 'A' to 'Z' 65 to 90 'a' to 'z' 97 to 122 space 32 So 'A' is stored as 65 and 'a' as 97. That is why sorting can put Zebra before apple : 90 comes before 97. Languages like Java and C have a real char type ( char grade = 'A'; ). JavaScript and Python simply use a string of length 1. To go between characters and codes: JavaScript Python Character to code 'A'.charCodeAt(0) gives 65 ord('A') gives 65 Code to character String.fromCharCode(66) gives 'B' chr(66) gives 'B' Strings: text A string is a sequence of characters. You can write it with single quotes '...' , double quotes ... or backticks (called template literals, which let you insert values with ${} ). const brand = 'amara studio'; brand.length; // 12 (characters, including the space) brand[0]; // 'a' (position 0 is the first character) brand.toUpperCase(); // 'AMARA STUDIO' brand.includes('studio'); // true brand.slice(0, 5); // 'amara' `Welcome to ${brand}!` /","track":"setup"},{"id":"setup-coding-basics-2","title":"Coding basics 2: decisions, loops and functions","level":"beginner","minutes":35,"summary":"The three ideas behind every program ever written. Make decisions with if, repeat work with loops, and package logic into functions.","html":"<h2 id=\"three-ideas-run-everything\">Three ideas run everything</h2>\n<p>Every program, from a to-do list to a blockchain, is built from three ideas on top of the data types you just learned:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Decisions</strong>: do this <em>if</em> something is true, otherwise do that.</li><li><strong>Loops</strong>: repeat something many times without writing it out many times.</li><li><strong>Functions</strong>: give a set of steps a name, so you can reuse it.</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"thinking-before-typing-pseudocode\">Thinking before typing: pseudocode</h2>\n<p>Professionals often sketch logic in plain English first. This is called <strong>pseudocode</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">for each product in the basket:\n    add (price x quantity) to the total\nif the total is at least 5000 pence:\n    shipping is free\notherwise:\n    shipping costs 399 pence</code></pre></div>\n<p>Once the steps are clear, turning them into code is mostly spelling. When you feel stuck, write pseudocode first.</p>\n<h2 id=\"decisions-if-else-if-else\">Decisions: if, else if, else</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const stock = 3;\n\nif (stock === 0) {\n  console.log('Sold out');\n} else if (stock &lt; 5) {\n  console.log('Only a few left');\n} else {\n  console.log('In stock');\n}</code></pre></div>\n<ul><li>The condition goes in round brackets <code>( )</code>.</li><li>The code to run goes in curly braces <code>{ }</code>.</li><li>Only the <strong>first</strong> branch whose condition is true runs.</li><li>Indent the code inside braces by two spaces. The computer does not care, but humans do.</li></ul>\n<p>Combine conditions with <code>&amp;&amp;</code> (and), <code>||</code> (or) and <code>!</code> (not): <code>if (age &gt;= 18 &amp;&amp; hasId)</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"loops-repeat-without-rewriting\">Loops: repeat without rewriting</h2>\n<p>A <code>for</code> loop has three parts in its brackets, separated by semicolons:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">for (let i = 1; i &lt;= 3; i++) {\n  console.log('Lap', i);\n}\n//  let i = 1   start: create a counter\n//  i &lt;= 3      keep going while this is true\n//  i++         after each lap, add 1 to i</code></pre></div>\n<p>Output: <code>Lap 1</code>, <code>Lap 2</code>, <code>Lap 3</code>.</p>\n<p>When you have a list, <code>for...of</code> gives you each item in turn, with no counter to manage:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const sizes = ['S', 'M', 'L'];\nfor (const size of sizes) {\n  console.log(size);\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>And a <code>while</code> loop repeats as long as a condition stays true. Make sure something inside it eventually makes the condition false, or it runs forever (an <strong>infinite loop</strong>, which freezes the page).</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"2\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"functions-name-your-steps\">Functions: name your steps</h2>\n<p>A function packages steps under a name. It takes <strong>inputs</strong> (parameters) and usually <strong>returns</strong> an output.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">function priceWithVat(pence) {\n  return Math.round(pence * 1.2);\n}\n\npriceWithVat(1000);   // 1200\npriceWithVat(4500);   // 5400</code></pre></div>\n<ul><li><code>function</code> keyword, then the name, then parameters in brackets.</li><li><code>return</code> sends a value back to whoever called the function, and ends it.</li><li>Defining a function does nothing on its own. <strong>Calling</strong> it, <code>priceWithVat(1000)</code>, runs it.</li></ul>\n<p>Good function names are verbs that say what they do: <code>calculateShipping</code>, <code>isEven</code>, <code>formatPrice</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"3\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"the-same-three-ideas-in-python\">The same three ideas in Python</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\"><pre><code class=\"language-python\">total = 4200\nif total &gt;= 5000:\n    print('Free shipping')\nelse:\n    print('Shipping: 3.99')\n\nfor i in range(1, 6):     # 1 to 5\n    print(i)\n\ndef is_even(n):\n    return n % 2 == 0</code></pre></div>\n<p>Python uses a colon and <strong>indentation</strong> (4 spaces) instead of curly braces, so indentation is not just style there: it is the structure.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"4\"></div>","quiz":[{"q":"In an if / else if / else chain, how many branches run?","options":["All of them whose condition is true","Only the first one whose condition is true","Always the else"],"answer":1,"explain":"Once a branch matches, the rest are skipped."},{"q":"What does this print? for (let i = 0; i &lt; 3; i++) { console.log(i); }","options":["1 2 3","0 1 2","0 1 2 3"],"answer":1,"explain":"It starts at 0 and stops when i &lt; 3 becomes false, so 3 is never printed."},{"q":"What does return do inside a function?","options":["Sends a value back to the caller and ends the function","Prints the value","Restarts the function"],"answer":0,"explain":"return hands a result back. console.log is what prints."},{"q":"What causes an infinite loop?","options":["A loop with more than 1,000 items","A loop whose condition never becomes false","Using let instead of const"],"answer":1,"explain":"If nothing inside the loop eventually makes its condition false, it never stops."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Free shipping or not?","file":"shipping.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write an <code>if</code> / <code>else</code> that decides shipping:</p>\n<ol><li>A constant <code>total</code> equal to <code>4200</code> (pence).</li><li><code>if (total &gt;= 5000)</code> print <code>'Free shipping'</code>.</li><li>Otherwise (<code>else</code>) print <code>'Shipping: 3.99'</code>.</li></ol>\n<p>Use the usual layout: braces on the same line as <code>if</code> and <code>else</code>, and each <code>console.log</code> indented on its own line.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const total = 4200;\nif (total >= 5000) {\n  console.log('Free shipping');\n} else {\n  console.log('Shipping: 3.99');\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+total\\s*=\\s*4200","text":"Line 1: <code>const total = 4200;</code>"},{"re":"if\\s*\\(\\s*total\\s*>=\\s*5000\\s*\\)\\s*\\{","text":"The <code>if</code> line: <code>if (total &gt;= 5000) {</code> with the condition in round brackets and an opening curly brace."},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*['\"]Free shipping['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"Inside the <code>if</code>: <code>console.log('Free shipping');</code>"},{"re":"\\}\\s*else\\s*\\{","text":"Close the <code>if</code> block and open the else on the same line: <code>} else {</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*['\"]Shipping: 3\\.99['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"Inside the <code>else</code>: <code>console.log('Shipping: 3.99');</code>"}],"explain":"<p>4200 is not at least 5000, so the condition is <code>false</code>, the first block is skipped and the <code>else</code> block runs: <strong>Shipping: 3.99</strong>. Change <code>4200</code> to <code>5200</code> and run it again to see the other branch.</p>"},{"title":"Count to five","file":"count.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write a <code>for</code> loop that prints the numbers 1 to 5:</p>\n<ol><li><code>for (let i = 1; i &lt;= 5; i++) {</code></li><li>Inside it, indented: <code>console.log(i);</code></li><li>Close it with <code>}</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"for (let i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {\n  console.log(i);\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*for\\s*\\(","text":"Start with the keyword <code>for</code> followed by round brackets."},{"re":"let\\s+i\\s*=\\s*1\\s*;","text":"First part: <code>let i = 1;</code> (start counting at 1). Note the semicolon."},{"re":"i\\s*<=\\s*5\\s*;","text":"Second part: <code>i &lt;= 5;</code> (keep going while i is 5 or less)."},{"re":"i\\+\\+\\s*\\)\\s*\\{","text":"Third part: <code>i++</code> then close the round bracket and open a curly brace: <code>i++) {</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*i\\s*\\)","text":"Inside the loop: <code>console.log(i);</code>"}],"explain":"<p>The loop starts with <code>i</code> at 1, checks <code>i &lt;= 5</code>, runs the body, then adds 1. When <code>i</code> reaches 6 the check fails and the loop stops. Output: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5.</p>"},{"title":"Add up a basket","file":"basket.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Use a <code>for...of</code> loop to total some prices:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const prices = [2000, 1500, 4500];</code></li><li><code>let total = 0;</code> (use <code>let</code>, because it will change)</li><li>A loop: <code>for (const price of prices) {</code></li><li>Inside it: <code>total = total + price;</code></li><li>Close the loop with <code>}</code></li><li>After the loop: <code>console.log(total);</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const prices = [2000, 1500, 4500];\nlet total = 0;\nfor (const price of prices) {\n  total = total + price;\n}\nconsole.log(total);","also":["const prices = [2000, 1500, 4500];\nlet total = 0;\nfor (const price of prices) {\n  total += price;\n}\nconsole.log(total);"],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+prices\\s*=\\s*\\[\\s*2000\\s*,\\s*1500\\s*,\\s*4500\\s*\\]","text":"Line 1 creates the list in square brackets: <code>const prices = [2000, 1500, 4500];</code>"},{"re":"let\\s+total\\s*=\\s*0","text":"Line 2: <code>let total = 0;</code> It must be <code>let</code> because the loop changes it."},{"re":"for\\s*\\(\\s*const\\s+price\\s+of\\s+prices\\s*\\)\\s*\\{","text":"The loop line: <code>for (const price of prices) {</code> (singular <code>price</code>, plural <code>prices</code>)."},{"re":"total\\s*(\\+=|=\\s*total\\s*\\+)\\s*price","text":"Inside the loop, add each price to the total: <code>total = total + price;</code>"},{"re":"\\}\\s*\\n\\s*console\\.log\\(\\s*total\\s*\\)","text":"Close the loop with <code>}</code>, then print the total after it, on its own line."}],"explain":"<p><code>total</code> starts at 0. The loop runs three times, adding 2000, then 1500, then 4500. Printing after the loop shows <strong>8000</strong> pence: £80.00. If you put the <code>console.log</code> inside the loop, it would print the running total three times.</p>"},{"title":"Write your first function","file":"even.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write a function that tells you whether a number is even, then use it:</p>\n<ol><li><code>function isEven(n) {</code></li><li>Inside it: <code>return n % 2 === 0;</code></li><li>Close it with <code>}</code></li><li>Print <code>isEven(10)</code>.</li><li>Print <code>isEven(7)</code>.</li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"function isEven(n) {\n  return n % 2 === 0;\n}\nconsole.log(isEven(10));\nconsole.log(isEven(7));","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"function\\s+isEven\\s*\\(\\s*n\\s*\\)\\s*\\{","text":"The first line: <code>function isEven(n) {</code> (capital E in <code>isEven</code>)."},{"re":"return\\s+n\\s*%\\s*2\\s*===\\s*0","text":"Inside: <code>return n % 2 === 0;</code> A number is even when its remainder after dividing by 2 is 0."},{"re":"isEven\\(\\s*10\\s*\\)","text":"After the function, call it: <code>console.log(isEven(10));</code>"},{"re":"isEven\\(\\s*7\\s*\\)","text":"Then: <code>console.log(isEven(7));</code>"}],"explain":"<p><code>10 % 2</code> is 0, so <code>0 === 0</code> is <code>true</code>. <code>7 % 2</code> is 1, so the result is <code>false</code>. One function, reused with any number. That is the whole point of functions.</p>"},{"title":"A Python loop","file":"loop.py","lang":"python","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write a Python loop that prints 1 to 5. Two lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>for i in range(1, 6):</code></li><li>Indented by <strong>4 spaces</strong>: <code>print(i)</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"for i in range(1, 6):\n    print(i)","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^for\\s+i\\s+in\\s+range\\(\\s*1\\s*,\\s*6\\s*\\)\\s*:","text":"Line 1: <code>for i in range(1, 6):</code> ending with a colon. <code>range(1, 6)</code> stops just before 6."},{"re":"^ {4}print\\(\\s*i\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2 must start with exactly 4 spaces (press Tab once), then <code>print(i)</code>."}],"explain":"<p><code>range(1, 6)</code> produces 1, 2, 3, 4, 5. It never includes the end number, which is a classic source of off-by-one bugs. The indented line is the loop's body.</p>"}],"text":"Three ideas run everything Every program, from a to-do list to a blockchain, is built from three ideas on top of the data types you just learned: Decisions : do this if something is true, otherwise do that. Loops : repeat something many times without writing it out many times. Functions : give a set of steps a name, so you can reuse it. Thinking before typing: pseudocode Professionals often sketch logic in plain English first. This is called pseudocode : for each product in the basket: add (price x quantity) to the total if the total is at least 5000 pence: shipping is free otherwise: shipping costs 399 pence Once the steps are clear, turning them into code is mostly spelling. When you feel stuck, write pseudocode first. Decisions: if, else if, else const stock = 3; if (stock === 0) { console.log('Sold out'); } else if (stock 5) { console.log('Only a few left'); } else { console.log('In stock'); } The condition goes in round brackets ( ) . The code to run goes in curly braces { } . Only the first branch whose condition is true runs. Indent the code inside braces by two spaces. The computer does not care, but humans do. Combine conditions with (and), || (or) and ! (not): if (age = 18 hasId) . Loops: repeat without rewriting A for loop has three parts in its brackets, separated by semicolons: for (let i = 1; i = 3; i++) { console.log('Lap', i); } // let i = 1 start: create a counter // i = 3 keep going while this is true // i++ after each lap, add 1 to i Output: Lap 1 , Lap 2 , Lap 3 . When you have a list, for...of gives you each item in turn, with no counter to manage: const sizes = ['S', 'M', 'L']; for (const size of sizes) { console.log(size); } And a while loop repeats as long as a condition stays true. Make sure something inside it eventually makes the condition false, or it runs forever (an infinite loop , which freezes the page). Functions: name your steps A function packages steps under a name. It takes inputs (parameters) and usually returns an output. function priceWithVat(pence) { return Math.round(pence * 1.2); } priceWithVat(1000); // 1200 priceWithVat(4500); // 5400 function keyword, then the name, then parameters in brackets. return sends a value back to whoever called the function, and ends it. Defining a function does nothing on its own. Calling it, priceWithVat(1000) , runs it. Good function names are verbs that say what they do: calculateShipping , isEven , formatPrice . The same three ideas in Python total = 4200 if total = 5000: print('Free shipping') else: print('Shipping: 3.99') for i in range(1, 6): # 1 to 5 print(i) def is_even(n): return n % 2 == 0 Python uses a colon and indentation (4 spaces) instead of curly braces, so indentation is not just style there: it is the structure.","track":"setup"},{"id":"setup-how-web-works","title":"How the web works","level":"beginner","minutes":12,"summary":"Browsers, servers, URLs, DNS and HTTP, explained with a single click on a link.","html":"<h2 id=\"what-happens-when-you-visit-a-website\">What happens when you visit a website</h2>\n<p>You type <code>https://shop.example.com/products?colour=red</code> and press Enter. In under a second:</p>\n<ol><li>Your <strong>browser</strong> (the client) asks <strong>DNS</strong> which IP address <code>shop.example.com</code> lives at. DNS is the internet's phone book.</li><li>The browser opens a secure connection (the <strong>S</strong> in HTTPS means encrypted with TLS) to the <strong>server</strong> at that address.</li><li>It sends an <strong>HTTP request</strong>: &quot;GET /products?colour=red please&quot;.</li><li>The server runs some code, maybe reads a database, and sends back an <strong>HTTP response</strong>: a status code plus some HTML.</li><li>The browser reads the HTML, then requests every CSS file, JavaScript file and image it mentions.</li><li>It builds the page and runs the JavaScript, which might fetch even more data.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"anatomy-of-a-url\">Anatomy of a URL</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Example</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Protocol</td><td><code>https://</code></td><td>How to talk (encrypted HTTP)</td></tr><tr><td>Domain</td><td><code>shop.example.com</code></td><td>Which server (via DNS)</td></tr><tr><td>Path</td><td><code>/products</code></td><td>Which resource on that server</td></tr><tr><td>Query string</td><td><code>?colour=red</code></td><td>Extra options, as key=value pairs</td></tr><tr><td>Fragment</td><td><code>#reviews</code></td><td>A spot within the page (never sent to the server)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"http-methods-and-status-codes\">HTTP methods and status codes</h2>\n<p>Requests use a <strong>method</strong> to say what they want:</p>\n<ul><li><code>GET</code>: read something (load a page, fetch products)</li><li><code>POST</code>: create something (submit an order)</li><li><code>PUT</code> / <code>PATCH</code>: update something</li><li><code>DELETE</code>: remove something</li></ul>\n<p>Responses carry a <strong>status code</strong>. Learn these by heart:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Meaning</th><th>You will see it when</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>200</td><td>OK</td><td>Everything worked</td></tr><tr><td>301 / 302</td><td>Redirect</td><td>A page moved</td></tr><tr><td>400</td><td>Bad request</td><td>You sent invalid data</td></tr><tr><td>401 / 403</td><td>Not logged in / not allowed</td><td>Auth problems</td></tr><tr><td>404</td><td>Not found</td><td>Wrong URL</td></tr><tr><td>500</td><td>Server error</td><td>The server's code crashed</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"the-three-languages-of-the-browser\">The three languages of the browser</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>HTML</strong> is the structure and content: headings, paragraphs, images, buttons.</li><li><strong>CSS</strong> is the presentation: colours, fonts, layout, animation.</li><li><strong>JavaScript</strong> is the behaviour: respond to clicks, fetch data, update the page.</li></ul>\n<p>Think of a house: HTML is the walls and rooms, CSS is the paint and furniture, JavaScript is the electricity and plumbing.</p>\n<h2 id=\"front-end-back-end-and-where-blockchain-fits\">Front end, back end, and where blockchain fits</h2>\n<ul><li>The <strong>front end</strong> runs in the user's browser. Anyone can view its code (right-click, View Source).</li><li>The <strong>back end</strong> runs on servers you control: databases, payments, secrets. Users cannot see it.</li><li>A <strong>blockchain</strong> is like a shared back end that nobody owns. Smart contracts are programs stored on it, and your front end talks to them through the user's wallet.</li></ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-try\"><p class=\"callout-title\">See it for yourself</p><p>Open any website, press <kbd>F12</kbd> (or right-click and choose Inspect), go to the <strong>Network</strong> tab and refresh. Every line is one HTTP request. Click one to see its method, status code, headers and response.</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"What does DNS do?","options":["Encrypts your connection","Turns a domain name into the IP address of a server","Stores the website's HTML"],"answer":1,"explain":"DNS is the internet's phone book: names in, addresses out."},{"q":"A page loads but shows &quot;404&quot;. What happened?","options":["The server crashed","You are not logged in","The server could not find anything at that URL"],"answer":2,"explain":"404 means Not Found. 500 would mean the server crashed, 401 or 403 would be an auth problem."},{"q":"Which part of the browser stack controls behaviour, like what happens when you click &quot;Add to bag&quot;?","options":["HTML","CSS","JavaScript"],"answer":2,"explain":"HTML is structure, CSS is presentation, JavaScript is behaviour."},{"q":"Why must API keys and database passwords live on the back end, never in front-end code?","options":["Anyone can read front-end code in their browser","Front-end code runs too slowly","Browsers delete secrets automatically"],"answer":0,"explain":"Everything sent to the browser can be inspected. Secrets belong on servers."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Ask a DNS server for an address","file":"dns.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Step 1 above is a <strong>DNS lookup</strong>: turning a name into an IP address. You can do one yourself. Google runs a public DNS service you can ask over HTTPS, and it answers in JSON. Write three lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const res = await fetch('https://dns.google/resolve?name=example.com&amp;type=A');</code></li><li><code>const data = await res.json();</code></li><li><code>console.log(data.Answer[0].data);</code></li></ol>\n<p><code>fetch</code> sends an HTTP request, and <code>await</code> means &quot;wait for the reply&quot;. Live requests work when you run Code Ladder on your own computer; the hosted preview may block them.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const res = await fetch('https://dns.google/resolve?name=example.com&type=A');\nconst data = await res.json();\nconsole.log(data.Answer[0].data);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+res\\s*=\\s*await\\s+fetch\\(","text":"Line 1 starts <code>const res = await fetch(</code>"},{"re":"dns\\.google/resolve\\?name=example\\.com&type=A","text":"The address, in quotes, is <code>'https://dns.google/resolve?name=example.com&amp;type=A'</code>. Check the <code>?</code> and the <code>&amp;</code>."},{"re":"const\\s+data\\s*=\\s*await\\s+res\\.json\\(\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>const data = await res.json();</code> turns the reply into data you can read."},{"re":"data\\.Answer\\[\\s*0\\s*\\]\\.data","text":"Line 3: the IP address is at <code>data.Answer[0].data</code>. Capital A on <code>Answer</code>."}],"explain":"<p><code>fetch</code> sends an HTTP GET request asking: what is the <strong>A record</strong> (IPv4 address) for example.com? The JSON reply has an <code>Answer</code> list, and the first answer's <code>data</code> is the IP address. Your browser does this lookup automatically, in milliseconds, for every new domain you visit.</p>"},{"title":"Take a URL apart","file":"url.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>JavaScript can split any URL into the parts in the table above. Write four lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const url = new URL('https://shop.example.com/products?colour=red');</code></li><li>Print <code>url.hostname</code></li><li>Print <code>url.pathname</code></li><li>Print <code>url.searchParams.get('colour')</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const url = new URL('https://shop.example.com/products?colour=red');\nconsole.log(url.hostname);\nconsole.log(url.pathname);\nconsole.log(url.searchParams.get('colour'));","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+url\\s*=\\s*new\\s+URL\\(","text":"Line 1 starts <code>const url = new URL(</code> (URL in capitals)."},{"re":"shop\\.example\\.com/products\\?colour=red","text":"The URL in quotes: <code>'https://shop.example.com/products?colour=red'</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*url\\.hostname\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>console.log(url.hostname);</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*url\\.pathname\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3: <code>console.log(url.pathname);</code>"},{"re":"url\\.searchParams\\.get\\(\\s*['\"]colour['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"Line 4: <code>console.log(url.searchParams.get('colour'));</code> Capital P in <code>searchParams</code>."}],"explain":"<p>Output: <code>shop.example.com</code> (the domain DNS looks up), <code>/products</code> (the path), and <code>red</code> (from the query string). Every web framework does this splitting for you behind the scenes.</p>"}],"text":"What happens when you visit a website You type https://shop.example.com/products?colour=red and press Enter. In under a second: Your browser (the client) asks DNS which IP address shop.example.com lives at. DNS is the internet's phone book. The browser opens a secure connection (the S in HTTPS means encrypted with TLS) to the server at that address. It sends an HTTP request : GET /products?colour=red please . The server runs some code, maybe reads a database, and sends back an HTTP response : a status code plus some HTML. The browser reads the HTML, then requests every CSS file, JavaScript file and image it mentions. It builds the page and runs the JavaScript, which might fetch even more data. Anatomy of a URL Part Example Meaning Protocol https:// How to talk (encrypted HTTP) Domain shop.example.com Which server (via DNS) Path /products Which resource on that server Query string ?colour=red Extra options, as key=value pairs Fragment #reviews A spot within the page (never sent to the server) HTTP methods and status codes Requests use a method to say what they want: GET : read something (load a page, fetch products) POST : create something (submit an order) PUT / PATCH : update something DELETE : remove something Responses carry a status code . Learn these by heart: Code Meaning You will see it when 200 OK Everything worked 301 / 302 Redirect A page moved 400 Bad request You sent invalid data 401 / 403 Not logged in / not allowed Auth problems 404 Not found Wrong URL 500 Server error The server's code crashed The three languages of the browser HTML is the structure and content: headings, paragraphs, images, buttons. CSS is the presentation: colours, fonts, layout, animation. JavaScript is the behaviour: respond to clicks, fetch data, update the page. Think of a house: HTML is the walls and rooms, CSS is the paint and furniture, JavaScript is the electricity and plumbing. Front end, back end, and where blockchain fits The front end runs in the user's browser. Anyone can view its code (right-click, View Source). The back end runs on servers you control: databases, payments, secrets. Users cannot see it. A blockchain is like a shared back end that nobody owns. Smart contracts are programs stored on it, and your front end talks to them through the user's wallet. See it for yourself Open any website, press F12 (or right-click and choose Inspect), go to the Network tab and refresh. Every line is one HTTP request. Click one to see its method, status code, headers and response.","track":"setup"},{"id":"setup-terminal","title":"The terminal is your superpower","level":"beginner","minutes":15,"summary":"Move around, create and inspect files, and run programs by typing, the way every developer works.","html":"<h2 id=\"why-bother-with-a-black-box-of-text\">Why bother with a black box of text?</h2>\n<p>Almost every developer tool (Git, Node, npm, Foundry, deployment tools) is used from the terminal. It is also faster than clicking once you know a dozen commands, and it lets you automate anything.</p>\n<p>Open it: on Mac press <kbd>Cmd</kbd>+<kbd>Space</kbd> and type <em>Terminal</em>. On Windows open <em>Terminal</em> (PowerShell), or Ubuntu if you installed WSL. Inside VS Code press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>`</kbd>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-dozen-commands-you-need\">The dozen commands you need</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Task</th><th>macOS / Linux / WSL</th><th>Windows PowerShell</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Where am I?</td><td><code>pwd</code></td><td><code>pwd</code></td></tr><tr><td>List files</td><td><code>ls -la</code></td><td><code>ls</code></td></tr><tr><td>Go into a folder</td><td><code>cd projects</code></td><td><code>cd projects</code></td></tr><tr><td>Go up one level</td><td><code>cd ..</code></td><td><code>cd ..</code></td></tr><tr><td>Go home</td><td><code>cd ~</code></td><td><code>cd ~</code></td></tr><tr><td>Make a folder</td><td><code>mkdir my-site</code></td><td><code>mkdir my-site</code></td></tr><tr><td>Make an empty file</td><td><code>touch index.html</code></td><td><code>ni index.html</code></td></tr><tr><td>Show a file</td><td><code>cat notes.txt</code></td><td><code>cat notes.txt</code></td></tr><tr><td>Copy</td><td><code>cp a.txt b.txt</code></td><td><code>cp a.txt b.txt</code></td></tr><tr><td>Move or rename</td><td><code>mv old.txt new.txt</code></td><td><code>mv old.txt new.txt</code></td></tr><tr><td>Delete a file</td><td><code>rm file.txt</code></td><td><code>rm file.txt</code></td></tr><tr><td>Clear the screen</td><td><code>clear</code> or <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>L</kbd></td><td><code>cls</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"paths-absolute-and-relative\">Paths: absolute and relative</h2>\n<p>A <strong>path</strong> is an address for a file.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Absolute</strong> paths start from the root: <code>/Users/ada/projects/my-site</code> (Mac) or <code>C:\\Users\\ada\\projects</code> (Windows).</li><li><strong>Relative</strong> paths start from where you are: <code>projects/my-site</code>.</li><li><code>.</code> means &quot;this folder&quot;, <code>..</code> means &quot;the parent folder&quot;, <code>~</code> means &quot;my home folder&quot;.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"your-first-session\">Your first session</h2>\n<p>Type these one at a time, reading the output after each:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">cd ~\nmkdir -p code/hello-terminal\ncd code/hello-terminal\npwd\necho &quot;Hello from the terminal&quot; &gt; hello.txt\nls -la\ncat hello.txt\ncode .</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>echo &quot;...&quot; &gt; file</code> writes text into a file. <code>code .</code> opens the current folder in VS Code (see the Install guide if that command is missing).</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"commands-flags-and-arguments\">Commands, flags and arguments</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">ls -la ~/code</code></pre></div>\n<ul><li><code>ls</code> is the <strong>command</strong>.</li><li><code>-la</code> are <strong>flags</strong> that change its behaviour (<code>-l</code> long format, <code>-a</code> include hidden files).</li><li><code>~/code</code> is the <strong>argument</strong>: what to act on.</li></ul>\n<p>Forgot how a command works? Try <code>ls --help</code>, <code>man ls</code> (press <code>q</code> to quit), or <code>tldr ls</code> if you installed tldr.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">rm does not use the Recycle Bin</p><p>Files deleted with <code>rm</code> are gone. <code>rm -rf folder</code> deletes a folder and everything inside without asking. Never run it with a path you are not 100% sure about, and never paste commands from strangers.</p></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Speed habits from day one</p><p>Press <kbd>Tab</kbd> to autocomplete names. Press <kbd>Up</kbd> to reuse the last command. Press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>R</kbd> to search your history. Press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>C</kbd> to stop a running program.</p></aside>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>","quiz":[{"q":"You are in /Users/ada/code/site. Where does <code>cd ..</code> take you?","options":["/Users/ada","/Users/ada/code","Your home folder"],"answer":1,"explain":"<code>..</code> is the parent folder, one level up."},{"q":"What does the <code>-a</code> in <code>ls -la</code> do?","options":["Sorts alphabetically","Includes hidden files (names starting with a dot, like .gitignore)","Shows all folders on the computer"],"answer":1,"explain":"Flags modify a command. Hidden files matter a lot in development: .git, .env, .gitignore."},{"q":"Which command is dangerous to run without double-checking the path?","options":["pwd","ls -la","rm -rf"],"answer":2,"explain":"rm -rf deletes everything in the path permanently, with no confirmation."},{"q":"A dev server is running in your terminal and you want to stop it. What do you press?","options":["Ctrl+C","Ctrl+Z then Enter","Escape"],"answer":0,"explain":"Ctrl+C sends an interrupt that stops the running program."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Make a project folder","file":"Terminal","lang":"bash","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Type the two commands that create a folder called <code>my-site</code> and then move into it. This editor checks your commands; run them for real in your own terminal afterwards.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"mkdir my-site\ncd my-site","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*mkdir\\s","text":"Line 1 uses <code>mkdir</code> (&quot;make directory&quot;)."},{"re":"mkdir\\s+my-site","text":"Name the folder <code>my-site</code>, with a hyphen and no spaces."},{"re":"^\\s*cd\\s+my-site\\s*$","text":"Line 2 moves into it with <code>cd</code> (&quot;change directory&quot;): <code>cd my-site</code>"}],"explain":"<p><code>mkdir my-site</code> creates the folder. <code>cd my-site</code> moves you inside it, so every command after that runs in <code>my-site</code>. Use <code>pwd</code> to check where you are.</p>"},{"title":"Write and read a file","file":"Terminal","lang":"bash","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Three commands:</p>\n<ol><li>Create a file called <code>notes.txt</code> containing the word <code>Hello</code> (use <code>echo &quot;Hello&quot;</code> and the <code>&gt;</code> symbol).</li><li>List all files, including hidden ones, in long format.</li><li>Print the contents of <code>notes.txt</code>.</li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"echo \"Hello\" > notes.txt\nls -la\ncat notes.txt","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*echo\\s+[\"']Hello[\"']","text":"Line 1 starts with <code>echo &quot;Hello&quot;</code>."},{"re":">\\s*notes\\.txt","text":"Send the output into the file with <code>&gt;</code>: <code>echo &quot;Hello&quot; &gt; notes.txt</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*ls\\s+-la\\s*$","text":"Line 2: <code>ls -la</code> (list, long format, all files)."},{"re":"^\\s*cat\\s+notes\\.txt\\s*$","text":"Line 3: <code>cat notes.txt</code> prints a file's contents."}],"explain":"<p><code>echo</code> prints text, and <code>&gt;</code> redirects that text into a file instead of the screen (replacing anything already in it; <code>&gt;&gt;</code> would add to the end). <code>ls -la</code> shows the new file, and <code>cat</code> reads it back.</p>"}],"text":"Why bother with a black box of text? Almost every developer tool (Git, Node, npm, Foundry, deployment tools) is used from the terminal. It is also faster than clicking once you know a dozen commands, and it lets you automate anything. Open it: on Mac press Cmd + Space and type Terminal . On Windows open Terminal (PowerShell), or Ubuntu if you installed WSL. Inside VS Code press Ctrl + ` . The dozen commands you need Task macOS / Linux / WSL Windows PowerShell Where am I? pwd pwd List files ls -la ls Go into a folder cd projects cd projects Go up one level cd .. cd .. Go home cd ~ cd ~ Make a folder mkdir my-site mkdir my-site Make an empty file touch index.html ni index.html Show a file cat notes.txt cat notes.txt Copy cp a.txt b.txt cp a.txt b.txt Move or rename mv old.txt new.txt mv old.txt new.txt Delete a file rm file.txt rm file.txt Clear the screen clear or Ctrl + L cls Paths: absolute and relative A path is an address for a file. Absolute paths start from the root: /Users/ada/projects/my-site (Mac) or C:\\Users\\ada\\projects (Windows). Relative paths start from where you are: projects/my-site . . means this folder , .. means the parent folder , ~ means my home folder . Your first session Type these one at a time, reading the output after each: cd ~ mkdir -p code/hello-terminal cd code/hello-terminal pwd echo Hello from the terminal hello.txt ls -la cat hello.txt code . echo ... file writes text into a file. code . opens the current folder in VS Code (see the Install guide if that command is missing). Commands, flags and arguments ls -la ~/code ls is the command . -la are flags that change its behaviour ( -l long format, -a include hidden files). ~/code is the argument : what to act on. Forgot how a command works? Try ls --help , man ls (press q to quit), or tldr ls if you installed tldr. rm does not use the Recycle Bin Files deleted with rm are gone. rm -rf folder deletes a folder and everything inside without asking. Never run it with a path you are not 100% sure about, and never paste commands from strangers. Speed habits from day one Press Tab to autocomplete names. Press Up to reuse the last command. Press Ctrl + R to search your history. Press Ctrl + C to stop a running program.","track":"setup"},{"id":"setup-install","title":"Install your developer toolkit","level":"beginner","minutes":30,"summary":"Set up VS Code, Git, Node.js and a GitHub account, then prove each one works.","html":"<h2 id=\"the-day-1-setup\">The Day 1 setup</h2>\n<p>Open the <a href=\"#toolkit\">Install everything</a> page, choose your operating system at the top, and work through the <strong>Day one</strong> section in order. It has copy-paste commands for macOS, Windows and Linux. Tick each item when it works.</p>\n<p>The minimum before your next lesson:</p>\n<ol><li>A package manager (Homebrew on Mac, winget on Windows, apt on Linux)</li><li><strong>VS Code</strong> with Prettier, Error Lens and Live Server</li><li><strong>Git</strong>, with your name and email configured</li><li>A <strong>GitHub</strong> account and the <code>gh</code> CLI, logged in</li><li><strong>Node.js LTS</strong></li></ol>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Windows users</p><p>Install WSL2 too (<code>wsl --install</code> in an administrator PowerShell). You can do web development on plain Windows, but blockchain tools like Foundry expect Linux, and most tutorials assume a Unix terminal.</p></aside>\n<h2 id=\"prove-it-works\">Prove it works</h2>\n<p>Close and reopen your terminal (new installs often need a fresh terminal), then run:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">git --version\nnode -v\nnpm -v\ngh auth status\ncode --version</code></pre></div>\n<p>Each should print a version number or &quot;Logged in&quot;. If one says &quot;command not found&quot;, that tool is not installed or not on your <strong>PATH</strong> (the list of folders your terminal searches for programs). Reopening the terminal fixes most PATH problems; reinstalling fixes most of the rest.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"set-up-vs-code-like-a-professional\">Set up VS Code like a professional</h2>\n<p>Press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>P</kbd> (<kbd>Cmd</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>P</kbd> on Mac), type <em>Open User Settings (JSON)</em>, and add:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"json\"><pre><code class=\"language-json\">{\n  &quot;editor.formatOnSave&quot;: true,\n  &quot;editor.defaultFormatter&quot;: &quot;esbenp.prettier-vscode&quot;,\n  &quot;editor.tabSize&quot;: 2,\n  &quot;editor.wordWrap&quot;: &quot;on&quot;,\n  &quot;editor.minimap.enabled&quot;: false,\n  &quot;files.autoSave&quot;: &quot;onFocusChange&quot;,\n  &quot;editor.bracketPairColorization.enabled&quot;: true\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>Format-on-save means your code always looks consistent, which makes mistakes easier to spot.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"your-first-node-js-program\">Your first Node.js program</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">mkdir -p ~/code/hello-node &amp;&amp; cd ~/code/hello-node\necho 'console.log(&quot;Node is working. 2 + 2 =&quot;, 2 + 2);' &gt; hello.js\nnode hello.js</code></pre></div>\n<p>You just ran JavaScript outside a browser. That is how build tools, servers and smart-contract scripts run.</p>\n<h2 id=\"how-to-organise-your-projects\">How to organise your projects</h2>\n<p>Keep every project in one place, one folder per project:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">~/code/\n  hello-node/\n  about-me/\n  weather-app/\n  my-first-token/</code></pre></div>\n<p>Never put code in Downloads or on the Desktop, and avoid cloud-synced folders (iCloud, OneDrive) for code: they fight with Git and <code>node_modules</code>.</p>","quiz":[{"q":"You install Node but <code>node -v</code> says &quot;command not found&quot;. What should you try first?","options":["Reinstall your operating system","Close and reopen the terminal, then try again","Install a different code editor"],"answer":1,"explain":"New installs update your PATH, but already-open terminals do not see the change."},{"q":"Why turn on format-on-save?","options":["It makes code run faster","Your code stays consistently formatted, so structure and mistakes are easier to see","GitHub requires it"],"answer":1,"explain":"Consistent formatting is a basic professional habit, and Prettier does it for free."},{"q":"Where is the best place to keep your code projects?","options":["On the Desktop","In a OneDrive or iCloud folder","A dedicated folder like ~/code with one subfolder per project"],"answer":2,"explain":"Sync services and code tooling do not get along. Keep code in a plain local folder and back it up with Git and GitHub."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Check your tools are installed","file":"Terminal","lang":"bash","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Type the three commands that print the versions of Node.js, npm and Git, one per line, in that order. (Use the short <code>-v</code> flag for Node and npm, and <code>--version</code> for Git.)</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"node -v\nnpm -v\ngit --version","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*node\\s+-v\\s*$","text":"Line 1: <code>node -v</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*npm\\s+-v\\s*$","text":"Line 2: <code>npm -v</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*git\\s+--version\\s*$","text":"Line 3: Git uses the long form with two hyphens: <code>git --version</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Each command prints a version number if the tool is installed and on your PATH. &quot;command not found&quot; means it is not installed, or your terminal needs reopening.</p>"},{"title":"Write VS Code settings","file":"settings.json","lang":"json","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write a small settings file that turns on format-on-save and sets the tab size to 2. It is JSON: keys in double quotes, a colon, then the value, with a comma between entries.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"json\"><pre><code class=\"language-json\">{\n  &quot;editor.formatOnSave&quot;: true,\n  &quot;editor.tabSize&quot;: 2\n}</code></pre></div>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"{\n  \"editor.formatOnSave\": true,\n  \"editor.tabSize\": 2\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*\\{","text":"Start with an opening curly brace <code>{</code> on its own line."},{"re":"\"editor\\.formatOnSave\"\\s*:\\s*true\\s*,","text":"Line 2: <code>&quot;editor.formatOnSave&quot;: true,</code> with a comma at the end, because another entry follows."},{"re":"\"editor\\.tabSize\"\\s*:\\s*2\\s*$","text":"Line 3: <code>&quot;editor.tabSize&quot;: 2</code> with <strong>no</strong> comma, because it is the last entry."},{"re":"^\\s*\\}\\s*$","text":"Close with <code>}</code> on the last line."}],"explain":"<p>JSON is strict: double quotes only, commas between entries, no comma after the last one. A single misplaced comma makes the whole file invalid, which is why editors highlight JSON errors in red.</p>"}],"text":"The Day 1 setup Open the Install everything page, choose your operating system at the top, and work through the Day one section in order. It has copy-paste commands for macOS, Windows and Linux. Tick each item when it works. The minimum before your next lesson: A package manager (Homebrew on Mac, winget on Windows, apt on Linux) VS Code with Prettier, Error Lens and Live Server Git , with your name and email configured A GitHub account and the gh CLI, logged in Node.js LTS Windows users Install WSL2 too ( wsl --install in an administrator PowerShell). You can do web development on plain Windows, but blockchain tools like Foundry expect Linux, and most tutorials assume a Unix terminal. Prove it works Close and reopen your terminal (new installs often need a fresh terminal), then run: git --version node -v npm -v gh auth status code --version Each should print a version number or Logged in . If one says command not found , that tool is not installed or not on your PATH (the list of folders your terminal searches for programs). Reopening the terminal fixes most PATH problems; reinstalling fixes most of the rest. Set up VS Code like a professional Press Ctrl + Shift + P ( Cmd + Shift + P on Mac), type Open User Settings (JSON) , and add: { editor.formatOnSave : true, editor.defaultFormatter : esbenp.prettier-vscode , editor.tabSize : 2, editor.wordWrap : on , editor.minimap.enabled : false, files.autoSave : onFocusChange , editor.bracketPairColorization.enabled : true } Format-on-save means your code always looks consistent, which makes mistakes easier to spot. Your first Node.js program mkdir -p ~/code/hello-node cd ~/code/hello-node echo 'console.log( Node is working. 2 + 2 = , 2 + 2);' hello.js node hello.js You just ran JavaScript outside a browser. That is how build tools, servers and smart-contract scripts run. How to organise your projects Keep every project in one place, one folder per project: ~/code/ hello-node/ about-me/ weather-app/ my-first-token/ Never put code in Downloads or on the Desktop, and avoid cloud-synced folders (iCloud, OneDrive) for code: they fight with Git and node_modules .","track":"setup"},{"id":"setup-git","title":"Git and GitHub basics","level":"beginner","minutes":20,"summary":"Save snapshots of your work, undo mistakes, and publish your code to GitHub.","html":"<h2 id=\"what-git-is\">What Git is</h2>\n<p>Git is a save-game system for code. Every <strong>commit</strong> is a snapshot of your whole project with a message saying what changed. You can jump back to any snapshot, see exactly what changed between two of them, and work on experiments without breaking the working version.</p>\n<p><strong>GitHub</strong> is a website that hosts Git repositories online. It is your backup, your collaboration space and your public portfolio. Employers and clients look at it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-three-places-your-changes-live\">The three places your changes live</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Working folder</strong>: the files you are editing.</li><li><strong>Staging area</strong>: changes you have chosen to include in the next commit (<code>git add</code>).</li><li><strong>Repository history</strong>: commits saved forever (<code>git commit</code>).</li></ol>\n<p>This two-step add-then-commit lets you save related changes together, even if you edited several things.</p>\n<h2 id=\"your-first-repository\">Your first repository</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">cd ~/code\nmkdir about-me &amp;&amp; cd about-me\ngit init\necho &quot;# About me&quot; &gt; README.md\ngit status\ngit add README.md\ngit commit -m &quot;Add README&quot;\ngit log --oneline</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>git status</code> is the command you will run more than any other. It tells you what changed and what is staged.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"the-daily-loop\">The daily loop</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">git status                # what changed?\ngit diff                  # show the exact lines\ngit add index.html        # stage one file (or: git add . for everything)\ngit commit -m &quot;Add hero section with brand photo&quot;\ngit push                  # upload to GitHub</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"publish-to-github\">Publish to GitHub</h2>\n<p>With the GitHub CLI logged in (<code>gh auth login</code>), one command creates the online repository and pushes your code:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">gh repo create about-me --public --source=. --push</code></pre></div>\n<p>Your code is now at <code>github.com/your-username/about-me</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"ignore-files-that-should-never-be-committed\">Ignore files that should never be committed</h2>\n<p>Create a file called <code>.gitignore</code> in the project root:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">node_modules/\n.env\n.DS_Store\ndist/</code></pre></div>\n<ul><li><code>node_modules/</code> can be rebuilt with <code>npm install</code>, and is huge.</li><li><code>.env</code> holds secrets like API keys. Committing it leaks them.</li><li>Build output (<code>dist/</code>) is generated from your source.</li></ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Secrets in Git are forever</p><p>If you commit an API key, deleting it in the next commit does not remove it: it stays in the history. Treat it as leaked: revoke the key and make a new one. Add <code>.env</code> to <code>.gitignore</code> before your first commit, every time.</p></aside>\n<h2 id=\"branches-in-one-minute\">Branches in one minute</h2>\n<p>A branch is a separate line of work. Keep <code>main</code> always working, and build new things on a branch:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">git switch -c add-contact-form    # create and switch to a new branch\n# ...edit, add, commit...\ngit switch main\ngit merge add-contact-form</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"free-hosting-with-github-pages\">Free hosting with GitHub Pages</h2>\n<p>For plain HTML/CSS/JS sites: on GitHub open your repo, then <strong>Settings, Pages</strong>, choose <strong>Deploy from a branch</strong>, pick <code>main</code> and <code>/ (root)</code>, and save. A minute later your site is live at <code>your-username.github.io/about-me</code>.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-pro\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Good commit messages</p><p>Write them as commands, describing what the commit does: &quot;Add product grid&quot;, &quot;Fix mobile menu overlap&quot;, &quot;Remove unused images&quot;. Future you, reading the log at 11pm, will be grateful.</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>git add</code> do?","options":["Uploads files to GitHub","Moves changes into the staging area, ready for the next commit","Creates a new repository"],"answer":1,"explain":"add stages, commit saves a snapshot, push uploads."},{"q":"You accidentally committed your .env file with an API key and pushed it. What is the right response?","options":["Delete the file in a new commit and move on","Make the repository private","Revoke the key immediately and create a new one, then remove the file and add .env to .gitignore"],"answer":2,"explain":"The key is in the history and may already have been scraped. Revoking is the only safe fix."},{"q":"Why work on a branch instead of directly on main?","options":["So main always stays working while you experiment","Branches make the code run faster","GitHub does not allow commits to main"],"answer":0,"explain":"Branches isolate unfinished work. You merge only when it is ready."},{"q":"Which command do professionals run most often?","options":["git init","git status","git clone"],"answer":1,"explain":"git status shows what changed and what is staged. Run it before every add and commit."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Your first commit","file":"Terminal","lang":"bash","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Inside a project folder that has a <code>README.md</code>, type the three commands that: start a Git repository, stage <code>README.md</code>, and commit it with the message <code>Add README</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"git init\ngit add README.md\ngit commit -m \"Add README\"","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*git\\s+init\\s*$","text":"Line 1: <code>git init</code> creates the repository."},{"re":"^\\s*git\\s+add\\s+README\\.md\\s*$","text":"Line 2: <code>git add README.md</code> stages the file (README in capitals, like the file name)."},{"re":"git\\s+commit\\s+-m\\s+","text":"Line 3 starts <code>git commit -m</code> (m for message)."},{"re":"-m\\s+[\"']Add README[\"']","text":"The message goes in quotes: <code>&quot;Add README&quot;</code>"}],"explain":"<p><code>init</code> creates the hidden <code>.git</code> folder that stores history. <code>add</code> moves changes into the staging area. <code>commit</code> saves a permanent snapshot with your message. Run <code>git log --oneline</code> afterwards to see it.</p>"},{"title":"Work on a branch, then merge it","file":"Terminal","lang":"bash","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Type the five commands for this workflow:</p>\n<ol><li>Create and switch to a new branch called <code>add-contact-form</code>.</li><li>Stage everything.</li><li>Commit with the message <code>Add contact form</code>.</li><li>Switch back to <code>main</code>.</li><li>Merge <code>add-contact-form</code> into it.</li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"git switch -c add-contact-form\ngit add .\ngit commit -m \"Add contact form\"\ngit switch main\ngit merge add-contact-form","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*git\\s+switch\\s+-c\\s+add-contact-form\\s*$","text":"Line 1: <code>git switch -c add-contact-form</code> (<code>-c</code> means create)."},{"re":"^\\s*git\\s+add\\s+\\.\\s*$","text":"Line 2: <code>git add .</code> (the dot means &quot;everything in this folder&quot;)."},{"re":"git\\s+commit\\s+-m\\s+[\"']Add contact form[\"']","text":"Line 3: <code>git commit -m &quot;Add contact form&quot;</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*git\\s+switch\\s+main\\s*$","text":"Line 4: <code>git switch main</code> (no <code>-c</code>: main already exists)."},{"re":"^\\s*git\\s+merge\\s+add-contact-form\\s*$","text":"Line 5: <code>git merge add-contact-form</code>"}],"explain":"<p>The branch let you build the form without touching <code>main</code>. Merging brings those commits into <code>main</code> once they are ready. On teams, step 5 happens through a pull request instead.</p>"}],"text":"What Git is Git is a save-game system for code. Every commit is a snapshot of your whole project with a message saying what changed. You can jump back to any snapshot, see exactly what changed between two of them, and work on experiments without breaking the working version. GitHub is a website that hosts Git repositories online. It is your backup, your collaboration space and your public portfolio. Employers and clients look at it. The three places your changes live Working folder : the files you are editing. Staging area : changes you have chosen to include in the next commit ( git add ). Repository history : commits saved forever ( git commit ). This two-step add-then-commit lets you save related changes together, even if you edited several things. Your first repository cd ~/code mkdir about-me cd about-me git init echo # About me README.md git status git add README.md git commit -m Add README git log --oneline git status is the command you will run more than any other. It tells you what changed and what is staged. The daily loop git status # what changed? git diff # show the exact lines git add index.html # stage one file (or: git add . for everything) git commit -m Add hero section with brand photo git push # upload to GitHub Publish to GitHub With the GitHub CLI logged in ( gh auth login ), one command creates the online repository and pushes your code: gh repo create about-me --public --source=. --push Your code is now at github.com/your-username/about-me . Ignore files that should never be committed Create a file called .gitignore in the project root: node_modules/ .env .DS_Store dist/ node_modules/ can be rebuilt with npm install , and is huge. .env holds secrets like API keys. Committing it leaks them. Build output ( dist/ ) is generated from your source. Secrets in Git are forever If you commit an API key, deleting it in the next commit does not remove it: it stays in the history. Treat it as leaked: revoke the key and make a new one. Add .env to .gitignore before your first commit, every time. Branches in one minute A branch is a separate line of work. Keep main always working, and build new things on a branch: git switch -c add-contact-form # create and switch to a new branch # ...edit, add, commit... git switch main git merge add-contact-form Free hosting with GitHub Pages For plain HTML/CSS/JS sites: on GitHub open your repo, then Settings, Pages , choose Deploy from a branch , pick main and / (root) , and save. A minute later your site is live at your-username.github.io/about-me . Good commit messages Write them as commands, describing what the commit does: Add product grid , Fix mobile menu overlap , Remove unused images . Future you, reading the log at 11pm, will be grateful.","track":"setup"},{"id":"setup-packages","title":"Packages, npm and package.json","level":"intermediate","minutes":18,"summary":"Use other people's code safely, understand package.json, versions and lockfiles, and run project scripts.","html":"<h2 id=\"why-packages-exist\">Why packages exist</h2>\n<p>Nobody writes everything from scratch. A <strong>package</strong> is reusable code someone published: a date library, React, a testing tool, the OpenZeppelin contracts. <strong>npm</strong> (Node Package Manager) installs them from the npm registry, which holds millions of packages.</p>\n<h2 id=\"start-a-project\">Start a project</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">mkdir price-tools &amp;&amp; cd price-tools\nnpm init -y\nnpm install date-fns\nnpm install -D prettier</code></pre></div>\n<p>That creates three things:</p>\n<ul><li><code>package.json</code>: the project's identity card and list of dependencies.</li><li><code>package-lock.json</code>: the exact version of every package installed, including dependencies of dependencies.</li><li><code>node_modules/</code>: the installed code. Never edit it, never commit it.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"reading-package-json\">Reading package.json</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"json\"><pre><code class=\"language-json\">{\n  &quot;name&quot;: &quot;price-tools&quot;,\n  &quot;version&quot;: &quot;1.0.0&quot;,\n  &quot;type&quot;: &quot;module&quot;,\n  &quot;scripts&quot;: {\n    &quot;dev&quot;: &quot;node --watch index.js&quot;,\n    &quot;format&quot;: &quot;prettier --write .&quot;\n  },\n  &quot;dependencies&quot;: {\n    &quot;date-fns&quot;: &quot;^4.1.0&quot;\n  },\n  &quot;devDependencies&quot;: {\n    &quot;prettier&quot;: &quot;^3.3.3&quot;\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<ul><li><strong>dependencies</strong> are needed when the app runs.</li><li><strong>devDependencies</strong> (<code>-D</code>) are only needed while developing: formatters, test runners, build tools.</li><li><strong>scripts</strong> are shortcuts. <code>npm run dev</code> runs <code>node --watch index.js</code>. Every project's README should tell you which scripts to run.</li><li><code>&quot;type&quot;: &quot;module&quot;</code> lets you use modern <code>import</code> syntax in Node.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"version-numbers-semver\">Version numbers (semver)</h2>\n<p>Versions look like <code>MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH</code>, e.g. <code>4.1.0</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Bumped when</th><th>Safe to upgrade?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>PATCH (4.1.<strong>1</strong>)</td><td>Bug fixes</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>MINOR (4.<strong>2</strong>.0)</td><td>New features, nothing broken</td><td>Usually</td></tr><tr><td>MAJOR (<strong>5</strong>.0.0)</td><td>Breaking changes</td><td>Read the changelog first</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><code>^4.1.0</code> means &quot;any 4.x.x at least 4.1.0&quot;. <code>~4.1.0</code> means &quot;any 4.1.x&quot;. The lockfile pins the exact version so everyone on the team gets identical installs. <strong>Commit the lockfile.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"useful-commands\">Useful commands</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">npm install            # install everything in package.json (after cloning a project)\nnpm ci                 # clean install exactly from the lockfile (used in CI)\nnpm run &lt;script&gt;       # run a script from package.json\nnpx &lt;tool&gt;             # run a package's command without installing it globally\nnpm outdated           # see what has newer versions\nnpm audit              # check for known vulnerabilities\nnpm uninstall date-fns # remove a package</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"local-versus-global-installs\">Local versus global installs</h2>\n<p>Install almost everything <strong>locally</strong> (inside the project). That way each project has its own versions and a teammate gets the same setup with one <code>npm install</code>. Global installs (<code>npm install -g</code>) are only for standalone CLI tools like <code>pnpm</code> or <code>vercel</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"choosing-a-package-like-a-senior\">Choosing a package like a senior</h2>\n<p>Before adding a dependency, check on npmjs.com and GitHub:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Weekly downloads</strong> and <strong>last publish date</strong>: is it used and maintained?</li><li><strong>Open issues</strong>: are serious bugs ignored?</li><li><strong>Size and dependencies</strong>: does a tiny feature pull in 200 other packages?</li><li><strong>Licence</strong>: MIT, Apache-2.0 and BSD are business-friendly.</li><li><strong>Do I need it at all?</strong> Five lines of your own code beat a dependency you have to trust forever.</li></ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Supply-chain attacks are real</p><p>Attackers publish look-alike packages (<code>reactt</code>, <code>ether-js</code>) and sometimes hijack real ones. Check the exact name before installing, prefer well-known packages, keep the lockfile committed, and run <code>npm audit</code>. In crypto projects this matters even more: malicious packages hunt for wallet keys.</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"Which file pins the exact version of every installed package so all machines match?","options":["package.json","package-lock.json","node_modules/.version"],"answer":1,"explain":"package.json lists ranges like ^4.1.0; the lockfile records exactly what was installed."},{"q":"A testing tool you only use during development should go in:","options":["dependencies","devDependencies (npm install -D)","A global install"],"answer":1,"explain":"devDependencies are not needed when the app runs in production."},{"q":"A package goes from 2.8.3 to 3.0.0. What should you expect?","options":["Only bug fixes","New features with nothing broken","Breaking changes: read the changelog before upgrading"],"answer":2,"explain":"A MAJOR bump signals breaking changes."},{"q":"Should node_modules be committed to Git?","options":["Yes, so the project works offline","No: it is rebuilt from package.json and the lockfile with npm install","Only for small projects"],"answer":1,"explain":"node_modules goes in .gitignore. The lockfile makes the rebuild exact."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Start a project and add packages","file":"Terminal","lang":"bash","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Type three commands: create a <code>package.json</code> with the default answers, install <code>date-fns</code> as a normal dependency, and install <code>prettier</code> as a <strong>dev</strong> dependency.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"npm init -y\nnpm install date-fns\nnpm install -D prettier","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*npm\\s+init\\s+-y\\s*$","text":"Line 1: <code>npm init -y</code> (<code>-y</code> says yes to every question)."},{"re":"^\\s*npm\\s+install\\s+date-fns\\s*$","text":"Line 2: <code>npm install date-fns</code>"},{"re":"npm\\s+install\\s+-D\\s+prettier","text":"Line 3: dev dependencies use <code>-D</code>: <code>npm install -D prettier</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"Add scripts to package.json","file":"package.json","lang":"json","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write the <code>&quot;scripts&quot;</code> section with two scripts: <code>dev</code> runs <code>node --watch index.js</code>, and <code>format</code> runs <code>prettier --write .</code></p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"\"scripts\": {\n  \"dev\": \"node --watch index.js\",\n  \"format\": \"prettier --write .\"\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*\"scripts\"\\s*:\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>&quot;scripts&quot;: {</code>"},{"re":"\"dev\"\\s*:\\s*\"node --watch index\\.js\"\\s*,","text":"Line 2: <code>&quot;dev&quot;: &quot;node --watch index.js&quot;,</code> with a comma at the end."},{"re":"\"format\"\\s*:\\s*\"prettier --write \\.\"","text":"Line 3: <code>&quot;format&quot;: &quot;prettier --write .&quot;</code> with no comma (it is the last one)."},{"re":"^\\s*\\}\\s*$","text":"Close the section with <code>}</code>."}],"explain":"<p>Now <code>npm run dev</code> starts your app and restarts it on every save, and <code>npm run format</code> tidies every file. Scripts mean nobody on a team has to remember long commands.</p>"}],"text":"Why packages exist Nobody writes everything from scratch. A package is reusable code someone published: a date library, React, a testing tool, the OpenZeppelin contracts. npm (Node Package Manager) installs them from the npm registry, which holds millions of packages. Start a project mkdir price-tools cd price-tools npm init -y npm install date-fns npm install -D prettier That creates three things: package.json : the project's identity card and list of dependencies. package-lock.json : the exact version of every package installed, including dependencies of dependencies. node_modules/ : the installed code. Never edit it, never commit it. Reading package.json { name : price-tools , version : 1.0.0 , type : module , scripts : { dev : node --watch index.js , format : prettier --write . }, dependencies : { date-fns : ^4.1.0 }, devDependencies : { prettier : ^3.3.3 } } dependencies are needed when the app runs. devDependencies ( -D ) are only needed while developing: formatters, test runners, build tools. scripts are shortcuts. npm run dev runs node --watch index.js . Every project's README should tell you which scripts to run. type : module lets you use modern import syntax in Node. Version numbers (semver) Versions look like MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH , e.g. 4.1.0 : Part Bumped when Safe to upgrade? PATCH (4.1. 1 ) Bug fixes Yes MINOR (4. 2 .0) New features, nothing broken Usually MAJOR ( 5 .0.0) Breaking changes Read the changelog first ^4.1.0 means any 4.x.x at least 4.1.0 . ~4.1.0 means any 4.1.x . The lockfile pins the exact version so everyone on the team gets identical installs. Commit the lockfile. Useful commands npm install # install everything in package.json (after cloning a project) npm ci # clean install exactly from the lockfile (used in CI) npm run script # run a script from package.json npx tool # run a package's command without installing it globally npm outdated # see what has newer versions npm audit # check for known vulnerabilities npm uninstall date-fns # remove a package Local versus global installs Install almost everything locally (inside the project). That way each project has its own versions and a teammate gets the same setup with one npm install . Global installs ( npm install -g ) are only for standalone CLI tools like pnpm or vercel . Choosing a package like a senior Before adding a dependency, check on npmjs.com and GitHub: Weekly downloads and last publish date : is it used and maintained? Open issues : are serious bugs ignored? Size and dependencies : does a tiny feature pull in 200 other packages? Licence : MIT, Apache-2.0 and BSD are business-friendly. Do I need it at all? Five lines of your own code beat a dependency you have to trust forever. Supply-chain attacks are real Attackers publish look-alike packages ( reactt , ether-js ) and sometimes hijack real ones. Check the exact name before installing, prefer well-known packages, keep the lockfile committed, and run npm audit . In crypto projects this matters even more: malicious packages hunt for wallet keys.","track":"setup"},{"id":"setup-debugging","title":"Debugging like a professional","level":"intermediate","minutes":20,"summary":"Read errors and stack traces, use breakpoints and the Network tab, and find any bug with a calm, repeatable method.","html":"<h2 id=\"bugs-are-normal\">Bugs are normal</h2>\n<p>Professional developers spend a large share of their time debugging. The difference is that they have a <strong>method</strong>, so bugs feel like puzzles rather than emergencies.</p>\n<h2 id=\"step-1-read-the-whole-error\">Step 1: read the whole error</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">Uncaught TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'price')\n    at getTotal (cart.js:14:22)\n    at renderCart (cart.js:31:17)\n    at HTMLButtonElement.&lt;anonymous&gt; (main.js:8:5)</code></pre></div>\n<p>Read it top to bottom:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Type</strong>: <code>TypeError</code>, something was the wrong type.</li><li><strong>Message</strong>: you tried to read <code>.price</code> from something that is <code>undefined</code>.</li><li><strong>Location</strong>: <code>cart.js</code>, line 14, column 22, inside <code>getTotal</code>.</li><li><strong>Stack trace</strong>: how we got there. <code>main.js</code> line 8 called <code>renderCart</code>, which called <code>getTotal</code>.</li></ul>\n<p>So on line 14 of cart.js, the thing before <code>.price</code> is undefined. Maybe an item is missing, or an array index is out of range.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-errors-you-will-meet-most\">The errors you will meet most</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Error</th><th>Usually means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>SyntaxError</code></td><td>A typo in the code: missing bracket, quote or comma</td></tr><tr><td><code>ReferenceError: x is not defined</code></td><td>Misspelt name, or used before it was declared</td></tr><tr><td><code>TypeError: x is not a function</code></td><td>Calling something that is not a function (often a typo or wrong import)</td></tr><tr><td><code>TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined</code></td><td>The object you expected is not there</td></tr><tr><td><code>Failed to fetch</code> / CORS error</td><td>Network request blocked or server unreachable</td></tr><tr><td><code>404</code> in the Network tab</td><td>Wrong file path or URL</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"step-2-look-inside-with-console-and-breakpoints\">Step 2: look inside with console and breakpoints</h2>\n<p>Label your logs so you know what you are looking at:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">console.log({ cart, index });     // shows names and values\nconsole.table(cart);              // arrays of objects as a table\nconsole.trace('how did we get here?');</code></pre></div>\n<p>Better still, <strong>pause the code</strong>. Add <code>debugger;</code> on a line, open DevTools, and reload. Execution stops there and you can hover over any variable, step line by line (<kbd>F10</kbd> step over, <kbd>F11</kbd> step into) and watch values change. You can also click a line number in the DevTools <strong>Sources</strong> panel to set a breakpoint without editing code.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"step-3-check-the-network-tab\">Step 3: check the Network tab</h2>\n<p>If data is not appearing, open DevTools, <strong>Network</strong>, and reload. Click the request and check:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Status</strong>: 200? 404? 500?</li><li><strong>Response</strong>: what did the server actually send?</li><li><strong>Payload</strong>: what did you actually send?</li></ul>\n<p>Half of &quot;my code is broken&quot; bugs are really &quot;the request failed&quot; bugs.</p>\n<h2 id=\"step-4-the-scientific-method\">Step 4: the scientific method</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Reproduce</strong> the bug reliably. If you cannot make it happen on demand, you cannot know you fixed it.</li><li><strong>Form a hypothesis</strong>: &quot;I think <code>items</code> is empty when the page first loads.&quot;</li><li><strong>Test it</strong> with a log or breakpoint.</li><li><strong>Narrow down</strong>: comment out half the code. Still broken? The bug is in the other half. Repeat.</li><li><strong>Fix one thing at a time</strong>, then retest.</li><li><strong>Prevent it</strong>: add a check, a test, or a clearer error message.</li></ol>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Check the boring stuff first</p><p>Did you save the file? Is the server restarted? Is the script tag pointing at the right file? Are you editing the same folder you are running? Is it cached (try <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>R</kbd>)? A surprising number of bugs are one of these.</p></aside>\n<h2 id=\"make-a-minimal-reproduction\">Make a minimal reproduction</h2>\n<p>When asking for help, strip the problem down to the smallest code that still shows the bug. Often you will find the bug while doing this. If not, people can now actually help you.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-try\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Practise</p><p>Open the <a href=\"#playground\">Playground</a>, load the <strong>To-do list</strong> template, and break it: rename <code>render</code> to <code>rendr</code> in one place only. Read the error in the console, find the line, fix it. Then try a missing bracket.</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"&quot;TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'price')&quot;. What is undefined?","options":["The price property","The object you wrote before .price","The whole file"],"answer":1,"explain":"You tried to read .price from something that does not exist. Find out why that object is undefined."},{"q":"Your product list is empty but there are no errors in the Console. Where should you look next?","options":["The Elements tab","The Network tab, to see if the data request failed or returned something unexpected","Your CSS file"],"answer":1,"explain":"Silent data bugs are usually failed or unexpected network responses."},{"q":"What is the first step of debugging methodically?","options":["Reproduce the bug reliably","Rewrite the function from scratch","Add try/catch everywhere"],"answer":0,"explain":"If you cannot trigger the bug on demand, you cannot confirm the fix."},{"q":"What does the <code>debugger;</code> statement do with DevTools open?","options":["Deletes the bug automatically","Pauses execution on that line so you can inspect variables and step through","Prints every variable to the console"],"answer":1,"explain":"It acts as a breakpoint written in code."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Read the error, fix the typo","file":"greeting.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Press <strong>Run it</strong> first. This code throws <code>ReferenceError: greting is not defined</code>. Read the error, find the misspelt name, and fix it so it prints the greeting.</p>","starter":"const greeting = 'Hello';\nconsole.log(greting);","html":"","solution":"const greeting = 'Hello';\nconsole.log(greeting);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+greeting\\s*=\\s*['\"]Hello['\"]","text":"Keep line 1 as it is: <code>const greeting = 'Hello';</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*greeting\\s*\\)","text":"The error names the problem: <code>greting</code> is missing an &quot;e&quot;. It must match the variable: <code>greeting</code>."}],"explain":"<p>A <code>ReferenceError</code> means you used a name the program has never seen. Nine times out of ten it is a typo. The error message told you exactly which name, which is why you always read it first.</p>"},{"title":"Fix the off-by-one bug","file":"total.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>This code should print a total of <strong>70</strong> but prints <code>NaN</code> (&quot;Not a Number&quot;). Press <strong>Run it</strong>, then fix the bug. (Ask yourself: how many items are in <code>prices</code>, and what is <code>prices[3]</code>?)</p>\n<p>When it works, change the last line to the labelled log <code>console.log({ total });</code>.</p>","starter":"const prices = [20, 35, 15];\nlet total = 0;\nfor (let i = 0; i <= prices.length; i++) {\n  total = total + prices[i];\n}\nconsole.log(total);","html":"","solution":"const prices = [20, 35, 15];\nlet total = 0;\nfor (let i = 0; i < prices.length; i++) {\n  total = total + prices[i];\n}\nconsole.log({ total });","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"i\\s*<\\s*prices\\.length","text":"The loop runs once too often. With <code>&lt;=</code>, <code>i</code> reaches 3, but the last item is <code>prices[2]</code>. Use <code>&lt;</code> instead of <code>&lt;=</code>."},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*\\{\\s*total\\s*\\}\\s*\\)","text":"Change the last line to <code>console.log({ total });</code> so the output shows the variable's name as well as its value."}],"explain":"<p>An array of 3 items has positions 0, 1 and 2. With <code>&lt;=</code> the loop also read <code>prices[3]</code>, which is <code>undefined</code>, and <code>70 + undefined</code> is <code>NaN</code>. This &quot;off by one&quot; mistake is one of the most common bugs in all of programming.</p>"}],"text":"Bugs are normal Professional developers spend a large share of their time debugging. The difference is that they have a method , so bugs feel like puzzles rather than emergencies. Step 1: read the whole error Uncaught TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'price') at getTotal (cart.js:14:22) at renderCart (cart.js:31:17) at HTMLButtonElement. anonymous (main.js:8:5) Read it top to bottom: Type : TypeError , something was the wrong type. Message : you tried to read .price from something that is undefined . Location : cart.js , line 14, column 22, inside getTotal . Stack trace : how we got there. main.js line 8 called renderCart , which called getTotal . So on line 14 of cart.js, the thing before .price is undefined. Maybe an item is missing, or an array index is out of range. The errors you will meet most Error Usually means SyntaxError A typo in the code: missing bracket, quote or comma ReferenceError: x is not defined Misspelt name, or used before it was declared TypeError: x is not a function Calling something that is not a function (often a typo or wrong import) TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined The object you expected is not there Failed to fetch / CORS error Network request blocked or server unreachable 404 in the Network tab Wrong file path or URL Step 2: look inside with console and breakpoints Label your logs so you know what you are looking at: console.log({ cart, index }); // shows names and values console.table(cart); // arrays of objects as a table console.trace('how did we get here?'); Better still, pause the code . Add debugger; on a line, open DevTools, and reload. Execution stops there and you can hover over any variable, step line by line ( F10 step over, F11 step into) and watch values change. You can also click a line number in the DevTools Sources panel to set a breakpoint without editing code. Step 3: check the Network tab If data is not appearing, open DevTools, Network , and reload. Click the request and check: Status : 200? 404? 500? Response : what did the server actually send? Payload : what did you actually send? Half of my code is broken bugs are really the request failed bugs. Step 4: the scientific method Reproduce the bug reliably. If you cannot make it happen on demand, you cannot know you fixed it. Form a hypothesis : I think items is empty when the page first loads. Test it with a log or breakpoint. Narrow down : comment out half the code. Still broken? The bug is in the other half. Repeat. Fix one thing at a time , then retest. Prevent it : add a check, a test, or a clearer error message. Check the boring stuff first Did you save the file? Is the server restarted? Is the script tag pointing at the right file? Are you editing the same folder you are running? Is it cached (try Ctrl + Shift + R )? A surprising number of bugs are one of these. Make a minimal reproduction When asking for help, strip the problem down to the smallest code that still shows the bug. Often you will find the bug while doing this. If not, people can now actually help you. Practise Open the Playground , load the To-do list template, and break it: rename render to rendr in one place only. Read the error in the console, find the line, fix it. Then try a missing bracket.","track":"setup"},{"id":"setup-pro-workflow","title":"The professional workflow","level":"advanced","minutes":25,"summary":"Pull requests, code review, conventional commits, CI with GitHub Actions, secrets management and documentation.","html":"<h2 id=\"how-teams-ship-code\">How teams ship code</h2>\n<p>On a professional team, nobody pushes straight to <code>main</code>. The flow looks like this:</p>\n<ol><li>Pull the latest <code>main</code> and create a branch: <code>git switch -c feat/checkout-validation</code>.</li><li>Make small commits as you go.</li><li>Push the branch and open a <strong>pull request</strong> (PR) describing what changed and why.</li><li><strong>Automated checks</strong> (lint, type-check, tests, build) run on the PR.</li><li>A teammate <strong>reviews</strong> the code and comments.</li><li>You address feedback, checks go green, the PR is merged.</li><li>Merging to <code>main</code> triggers a <strong>deployment</strong>.</li></ol>\n<p>Even working alone, using PRs on your own projects builds the habit and gives you a clean history.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">git push -u origin feat/checkout-validation\ngh pr create --fill\ngh pr checks          # watch the automated checks\ngh pr merge --squash</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"conventional-commits\">Conventional commits</h2>\n<p>Many teams prefix commit messages with a type. It makes history scannable and lets tools generate changelogs:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">feat: add discount code field to checkout\nfix: round VAT to the nearest penny\ndocs: explain local setup in README\nrefactor: extract price formatting helper\ntest: cover empty cart edge case\nchore: bump vite to 6.0.5</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"code-review-what-reviewers-look-for\">Code review: what reviewers look for</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Correctness</strong>: does it do what it claims? What about empty, huge or invalid inputs?</li><li><strong>Security</strong>: is input validated? Are secrets kept out? Could a user see someone else's data?</li><li><strong>Readability</strong>: would a new teammate understand it? Are names clear?</li><li><strong>Tests</strong>: is the new behaviour covered?</li><li><strong>Scope</strong>: is the PR focused on one thing? Small PRs get better reviews.</li></ul>\n<p>Review your own diff first (<code>git diff main</code>). You will catch half the issues yourself.</p>\n<h2 id=\"continuous-integration-with-github-actions\">Continuous integration with GitHub Actions</h2>\n<p>CI runs your checks on every push, so broken code never reaches <code>main</code>. Save this as <code>.github/workflows/ci.yml</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\"><pre><code class=\"language-yaml\">name: CI\non:\n  pull_request:\n  push:\n    branches: [main]\n\njobs:\n  check:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - uses: actions/setup-node@v4\n        with:\n          node-version: lts/*\n          cache: npm\n      - run: npm ci\n      - run: npm run lint\n      - run: npm test\n      - run: npm run build</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"environment-variables-and-secrets\">Environment variables and secrets</h2>\n<p>Configuration that changes between environments (local, staging, production), and anything secret, lives outside the code:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\"># .env  (listed in .gitignore, never committed)\nDATABASE_URL=postgres://localhost:5432/shop\nSTRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_...</code></pre></div>\n<ul><li>Commit a <code>.env.example</code> with the variable <strong>names</strong> and fake values, so others know what to set.</li><li>In production, set real values in your host's dashboard (Vercel, Netlify, Railway) or a secrets manager.</li><li>In GitHub Actions, use <strong>repository secrets</strong> (<code>${{ secrets.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY }}</code>).</li><li>For smart contracts, never put a private key with real funds in a <code>.env</code> file. Use Foundry's encrypted keystore (<code>cast wallet import</code>) or a hardware wallet.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"documentation-that-pays-off\">Documentation that pays off</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>README.md</strong>: what the project is, how to run it, how to test it, how to deploy it.</li><li><strong>Decision records</strong>: a short note whenever you make a significant choice (&quot;We chose Postgres over MongoDB because...&quot;). Six months later this is gold.</li><li><strong>Comments</strong> explain <em>why</em>, not <em>what</em>. The code already says what.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"guard-rails-that-catch-mistakes-early\">Guard rails that catch mistakes early</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Prettier</strong> and <strong>ESLint</strong> in the project, run in CI.</li><li><strong>TypeScript</strong> in strict mode.</li><li><strong>Pre-commit hooks</strong> (e.g. with <code>husky</code> and <code>lint-staged</code>) that format and lint staged files before every commit.</li><li><strong>Branch protection</strong> on GitHub: require passing checks and a review before merging to <code>main</code>.</li></ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-pro\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Working with AI tools professionally</p><p>AI coding assistants are now part of most teams' workflow. Treat their output exactly like a teammate's PR: read every line, run the tests, check for security issues and invented APIs. You are responsible for every line you merge, whoever or whatever typed it.</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"Why do teams use pull requests instead of pushing straight to main?","options":["GitHub charges for direct pushes","So changes are reviewed and automatically checked before they reach the shared, deployable branch","Pull requests make the code smaller"],"answer":1,"explain":"PRs combine human review and automated checks as a gate in front of main."},{"q":"Which commit message follows conventional commits?","options":["&quot;stuff&quot;","&quot;Fixed it!!&quot;","&quot;fix: prevent negative quantities in cart&quot;"],"answer":2,"explain":"type: short imperative description."},{"q":"What belongs in .env.example?","options":["Your real API keys, so teammates can use them","The variable names with placeholder values","Nothing: it should stay empty"],"answer":1,"explain":"It documents what configuration is needed without exposing secrets."},{"q":"What is the main job of CI?","options":["Automatically run lint, tests and build on every push or PR so broken code is caught before merging","Write code for you","Back up your laptop"],"answer":0,"explain":"Continuous integration checks every change the same way, every time."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Ship a fix the professional way","file":"Terminal","lang":"bash","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Type the four commands to: create a branch <code>fix/vat-rounding</code>, commit with the conventional message <code>fix: round VAT to the nearest penny</code>, push the branch to <code>origin</code> (setting it as upstream), and open a pull request that fills in its title and body from your commits.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"git switch -c fix/vat-rounding\ngit commit -m \"fix: round VAT to the nearest penny\"\ngit push -u origin fix/vat-rounding\ngh pr create --fill","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*git\\s+switch\\s+-c\\s+fix/vat-rounding\\s*$","text":"Line 1: <code>git switch -c fix/vat-rounding</code>"},{"re":"git\\s+commit\\s+-m\\s+[\"']fix: round VAT to the nearest penny[\"']","text":"Line 2: the message starts with the type <code>fix:</code> then a space."},{"re":"^\\s*git\\s+push\\s+-u\\s+origin\\s+fix/vat-rounding\\s*$","text":"Line 3: <code>git push -u origin fix/vat-rounding</code> (<code>-u</code> links the local branch to the remote one)."},{"re":"^\\s*gh\\s+pr\\s+create\\s+--fill\\s*$","text":"Line 4: <code>gh pr create --fill</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"Write a .env.example","file":".env.example","lang":"bash","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write a <code>.env.example</code> with two variables, using placeholder values only:</p>\n<ul><li><code>DATABASE_URL</code> set to <code>postgres://localhost:5432/shop</code></li><li><code>STRIPE_SECRET_KEY</code> set to <code>sk_test_replace_me</code></li></ul>\n<p>No spaces around the <code>=</code> sign.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"DATABASE_URL=postgres://localhost:5432/shop\nSTRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_replace_me","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^DATABASE_URL=postgres://localhost:5432/shop\\s*$","text":"Line 1: <code>DATABASE_URL=postgres://localhost:5432/shop</code>"},{"re":"^STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_replace_me\\s*$","text":"Line 2: <code>STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_replace_me</code>"}],"explain":"<p>This file is committed so teammates know which variables to set. The real <code>.env</code>, with real secrets, stays in <code>.gitignore</code>.</p>"}],"text":"How teams ship code On a professional team, nobody pushes straight to main . The flow looks like this: Pull the latest main and create a branch: git switch -c feat/checkout-validation . Make small commits as you go. Push the branch and open a pull request (PR) describing what changed and why. Automated checks (lint, type-check, tests, build) run on the PR. A teammate reviews the code and comments. You address feedback, checks go green, the PR is merged. Merging to main triggers a deployment . Even working alone, using PRs on your own projects builds the habit and gives you a clean history. git push -u origin feat/checkout-validation gh pr create --fill gh pr checks # watch the automated checks gh pr merge --squash Conventional commits Many teams prefix commit messages with a type. It makes history scannable and lets tools generate changelogs: feat: add discount code field to checkout fix: round VAT to the nearest penny docs: explain local setup in README refactor: extract price formatting helper test: cover empty cart edge case chore: bump vite to 6.0.5 Code review: what reviewers look for Correctness : does it do what it claims? What about empty, huge or invalid inputs? Security : is input validated? Are secrets kept out? Could a user see someone else's data? Readability : would a new teammate understand it? Are names clear? Tests : is the new behaviour covered? Scope : is the PR focused on one thing? Small PRs get better reviews. Review your own diff first ( git diff main ). You will catch half the issues yourself. Continuous integration with GitHub Actions CI runs your checks on every push, so broken code never reaches main . Save this as .github/workflows/ci.yml : name: CI on: pull_request: push: branches: [main] jobs: check: runs-on: ubuntu-latest steps: - uses: actions/checkout@v4 - uses: actions/setup-node@v4 with: node-version: lts/* cache: npm - run: npm ci - run: npm run lint - run: npm test - run: npm run build Environment variables and secrets Configuration that changes between environments (local, staging, production), and anything secret, lives outside the code: # .env (listed in .gitignore, never committed) DATABASE_URL=postgres://localhost:5432/shop STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_... Commit a .env.example with the variable names and fake values, so others know what to set. In production, set real values in your host's dashboard (Vercel, Netlify, Railway) or a secrets manager. In GitHub Actions, use repository secrets ( ${{ secrets.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY }} ). For smart contracts, never put a private key with real funds in a .env file. Use Foundry's encrypted keystore ( cast wallet import ) or a hardware wallet. Documentation that pays off README.md : what the project is, how to run it, how to test it, how to deploy it. Decision records : a short note whenever you make a significant choice ( We chose Postgres over MongoDB because... ). Six months later this is gold. Comments explain why , not what . The code already says what. Guard rails that catch mistakes early Prettier and ESLint in the project, run in CI. TypeScript in strict mode. Pre-commit hooks (e.g. with husky and lint-staged ) that format and lint staged files before every commit. Branch protection on GitHub: require passing checks and a review before merging to main . Working with AI tools professionally AI coding assistants are now part of most teams' workflow. Treat their output exactly like a teammate's PR: read every line, run the tests, check for security issues and invented APIs. You are responsible for every line you merge, whoever or whatever typed it.","track":"setup"},{"id":"setup-bits-bytes","title":"Bits, bytes and integer types: what uint256 really means","level":"advanced","minutes":40,"summary":"Binary and hex, bytes and encodings, fixed-size integers, overflow, BigInt and bitwise operators. The ground floor under hashing, smart contracts and token maths.","html":"<h2 id=\"why-this-matters\">Why this matters</h2>\n<p>Web developers can go years without thinking about bits. Blockchain developers cannot. Hashes are 256 bits, addresses are 20 bytes, token balances are <code>uint256</code>, and one wrong assumption about integer size or precision has drained real money from real contracts. This lesson is the foundation for all of it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"binary-counting-with-two-digits\">Binary: counting with two digits</h2>\n<p>Computers store everything as <strong>bits</strong>: 0 or 1. In binary, each position is worth double the one to its right:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Position value</th><th>128</th><th>64</th><th>32</th><th>16</th><th>8</th><th>4</th><th>2</th><th>1</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>13</code> in binary</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>8 + 4 + 1 = 13, so 13 is <code>00001101</code>. Eight bits are a <strong>byte</strong>, which holds 2^8 = <strong>256</strong> values: 0 to 255.</p>\n<h2 id=\"hexadecimal-the-developer-s-shorthand\">Hexadecimal: the developer's shorthand</h2>\n<p>Binary is long, so developers write bytes in <strong>hexadecimal</strong> (base 16), using digits 0 to 9 and letters a to f (for 10 to 15). One hex digit is exactly 4 bits, so one byte is always exactly <strong>two</strong> hex digits: <code>00</code> to <code>ff</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Decimal</th><th>Binary</th><th>Hex</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td><code>00000000</code></td><td><code>00</code></td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td><code>00001010</code></td><td><code>0a</code></td></tr><tr><td>255</td><td><code>11111111</code></td><td><code>ff</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Hex is usually written with a <code>0x</code> prefix: <code>0xff</code>. Ethereum addresses (<code>0x71C7...</code>) and hashes are hex for this reason. A 256-bit hash is 64 hex characters; a 20-byte address is 40.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"text-is-bytes-too\">Text is bytes too</h2>\n<p>Strings are stored as bytes using an <strong>encoding</strong>. The web's standard is <strong>UTF-8</strong>: ASCII characters take one byte each; accented letters, other scripts and emoji take two to four.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">new TextEncoder().encode('Hi');   // Uint8Array [72, 105]\nnew TextEncoder().encode('£');    // Uint8Array [194, 163]  (two bytes)</code></pre></div>\n<p>Hash functions and signatures work on <strong>bytes</strong>, not text, which is why crypto code always encodes strings first.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"fixed-size-integers\">Fixed-size integers</h2>\n<p>Most languages store integers in a <strong>fixed number of bits</strong>. That sets a minimum and maximum:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Bits</th><th>Range</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>uint8</code> (unsigned)</td><td>8</td><td>0 to 255</td></tr><tr><td><code>int8</code> (signed)</td><td>8</td><td>-128 to 127</td></tr><tr><td><code>int32</code></td><td>32</td><td>about -2.1 billion to 2.1 billion</td></tr><tr><td><code>uint64</code></td><td>64</td><td>0 to about 1.8 x 10^19</td></tr><tr><td><code>uint256</code></td><td>256</td><td>0 to about 1.16 x 10^77</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li><strong>Unsigned</strong> (<code>uint</code>) types hold only zero and positives.</li><li><strong>Signed</strong> (<code>int</code>) types use one bit's worth of range for negatives (stored using a scheme called <strong>two's complement</strong>).</li><li>Solidity's default <code>uint</code> is <code>uint256</code>: big enough for any realistic token balance with 18 decimals.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"overflow-when-numbers-wrap-around\">Overflow: when numbers wrap around</h2>\n<p>What is 255 + 1 in a <code>uint8</code>? There is no room for 256, so in many languages it silently <strong>wraps</strong> to 0, like a car odometer rolling over. Subtracting 1 from 0 in a <code>uint</code> wraps to the maximum value.</p>\n<p>In older Solidity (before 0.8), this was a real attack: in 2018 an overflow bug in a token's batch-transfer function (the &quot;BatchOverflow&quot; bug) let attackers create astronomically large balances from nothing. Since Solidity 0.8, arithmetic <strong>reverts on overflow</strong> by default. Code inside an <code>unchecked { }</code> block skips that check to save gas, so it must be proven safe.</p>\n<h2 id=\"javascript-numbers-and-bigint\">JavaScript numbers and BigInt</h2>\n<p>JavaScript's <code>number</code> is a 64-bit float. It stores whole numbers exactly only up to <code>Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER</code>, which is 2^53 - 1 = 9,007,199,254,740,991. Beyond that, it silently loses precision.</p>\n<p>One ether is 10^18 wei, far beyond that limit for real balances. So blockchain JavaScript uses <strong>BigInt</strong>: integers of any size, written with an <code>n</code> suffix: <code>10n ** 18n</code>. You cannot mix BigInt and normal numbers in one calculation without converting.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"2\"></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"3\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"bitwise-operators\">Bitwise operators</h2>\n<p>Bitwise operators work on the individual bits of integers:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operator</th><th>Name</th><th>Example</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>&amp;</code></td><td>AND (both bits 1)</td><td><code>0b1100 &amp; 0b1010</code></td><td><code>0b1000</code> (8)</td></tr><tr><td><code>|</code></td><td>OR (either bit 1)</td><td><code>0b1100 | 0b1010</code></td><td><code>0b1110</code> (14)</td></tr><tr><td><code>^</code></td><td>XOR (bits differ)</td><td><code>0b1100 ^ 0b1010</code></td><td><code>0b0110</code> (6)</td></tr><tr><td><code>~</code></td><td>NOT (flip bits)</td><td><code>~0</code></td><td><code>-1</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>&lt;&lt;</code></td><td>shift left</td><td><code>1 &lt;&lt; 3</code></td><td><code>8</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>&gt;&gt;&gt;</code></td><td>unsigned shift right</td><td><code>16 &gt;&gt;&gt; 2</code></td><td><code>4</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Uses you will actually meet: packing several true/false <strong>flags</strong> into one number (permissions, settings), hash function internals (SHA-256 is mostly rotations, XORs and ANDs; read <code>sha256</code> in this site's <code>assets/tools.js</code>), and gas-saving tricks in Solidity.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"4\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"the-same-types-in-solidity\">The same types in Solidity</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"solidity\"><pre><code class=\"language-solidity\">uint8 small = 255;            // 0 to 255\nuint256 big = 1e18;           // one token with 18 decimals\nint256 temperature = -5;      // signed: negatives allowed\naddress owner;                // 20 bytes\nbytes32 id = keccak256(abi.encodePacked(&quot;ladder&quot;));   // 32 bytes, e.g. a hash\nbool isLive = true;</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"5\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"fixed-point-maths-decimals-without-floats\">Fixed-point maths: decimals without floats</h2>\n<p>Tokens use integers plus a <strong>decimals</strong> setting (usually 18), exactly like pence with 2 decimals. Rules that prevent real bugs:</p>\n<ul><li>Multiply before you divide: <code>amount * feeBps / 10_000</code>, not <code>amount / 10_000 * feeBps</code>.</li><li>Decide which way rounding should go, and make it favour the protocol.</li><li>Tokens have different decimals (USDC uses 6). Always scale before combining amounts.</li></ul>","quiz":[{"q":"How many values can one byte hold?","options":["8","255","256"],"answer":2,"explain":"2^8 = 256 values, from 0 to 255."},{"q":"How many hex characters represent a 32-byte hash?","options":["32","64","256"],"answer":1,"explain":"Each byte is two hex digits: 32 x 2 = 64."},{"q":"In Solidity 0.8+, what happens if a uint256 subtraction would go below zero?","options":["It wraps to the maximum value","The transaction reverts, unless the code is inside an unchecked block","It becomes negative"],"answer":1,"explain":"Checked arithmetic has been the default since 0.8."},{"q":"Why do Ethereum JavaScript libraries return balances as bigint?","options":["Wei amounts exceed the largest integer a normal JavaScript number stores exactly","bigint is faster","JSON requires it"],"answer":0,"explain":"Number is exact only up to 2^53 - 1; 18-decimal balances go far beyond that."},{"q":"What is 6 &amp; 3 (bitwise AND)?","options":["9","7","2"],"answer":2,"explain":"6 is 110 and 3 is 011. Only the middle bit is 1 in both: 010 = 2."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Convert between bases","file":"bases.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write three lines that convert the number 255:</p>\n<ol><li>Print it in binary: <code>console.log((255).toString(2));</code></li><li>Print it in hex: <code>console.log((255).toString(16));</code></li><li>Print the hex string <code>'ff'</code> back as a number: <code>console.log(parseInt('ff', 16));</code></li></ol>\n<p>(The extra brackets around <code>255</code> stop JavaScript reading the dot as a decimal point.)</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"console.log((255).toString(2));\nconsole.log((255).toString(16));\nconsole.log(parseInt('ff', 16));","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"\\(\\s*255\\s*\\)\\.toString\\(\\s*2\\s*\\)","text":"Line 1: <code>(255).toString(2)</code> converts to base 2. Wrap it in <code>console.log(...)</code>."},{"re":"\\(\\s*255\\s*\\)\\.toString\\(\\s*16\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: the same, with base <code>16</code>."},{"re":"parseInt\\(\\s*['\"]ff['\"]\\s*,\\s*16\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3: <code>parseInt('ff', 16)</code>. The second argument tells it the text is base 16."}],"explain":"<p>Output: <code>11111111</code>, <code>ff</code>, <code>255</code>. The same number, three spellings. <code>toString(base)</code> and <code>parseInt(text, base)</code> are your tools for moving between them.</p>"},{"title":"See the bytes inside a string","file":"bytes.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write two lines:</p>\n<ol><li>A constant <code>bytes</code> equal to <code>new TextEncoder().encode('Hi')</code>.</li><li>Print it as a plain array: <code>console.log(Array.from(bytes));</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const bytes = new TextEncoder().encode('Hi');\nconsole.log(Array.from(bytes));","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+bytes\\s*=\\s*new\\s+TextEncoder\\(\\s*\\)","text":"Line 1 starts <code>const bytes = new TextEncoder()</code>. <code>new</code> creates an encoder object."},{"re":"\\.encode\\(\\s*['\"]Hi['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"Then call <code>.encode('Hi')</code> on it, all on the same line."},{"re":"Array\\.from\\(\\s*bytes\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>console.log(Array.from(bytes));</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Output: <code>[72, 105]</code>. 'H' is 72 and 'i' is 105, the same character codes you met in Coding basics 1, now as raw bytes.</p>"},{"title":"Find the edge of safe integers","file":"safe.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write three lines:</p>\n<ol><li>Print <code>Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER</code>.</li><li>Print <code>2 ** 53 + 1</code> (watch for the wrong answer).</li><li>Print <code>2n ** 53n + 1n</code> (the BigInt version).</li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"console.log(Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER);\nconsole.log(2 ** 53 + 1);\nconsole.log(2n ** 53n + 1n);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"Number\\.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER","text":"Line 1: <code>console.log(Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER);</code> all capitals after the dot, with underscores."},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*2\\s*\\*\\*\\s*53\\s*\\+\\s*1\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>console.log(2 ** 53 + 1);</code> (<code>**</code> means &quot;to the power of&quot;)."},{"re":"2n\\s*\\*\\*\\s*53n\\s*\\+\\s*1n","text":"Line 3: every number gets an <code>n</code>: <code>2n ** 53n + 1n</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Line 2 prints <code>9007199254740992</code>, which is wrong: the true answer ends in 3. The float cannot represent it, so it rounds silently. The BigInt line prints the correct <code>9007199254740993</code>. This is exactly why viem returns balances as <code>bigint</code>.</p>"},{"title":"Work in wei, like a wallet does","file":"wei.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Represent 2.5 ether in wei using only integers:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const oneEther = 10n ** 18n;</code></li><li><code>const amount = 25n * oneEther / 10n;</code> (2.5 = 25 / 10, multiplying <strong>before</strong> dividing)</li><li><code>console.log(amount.toString());</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const oneEther = 10n ** 18n;\nconst amount = 25n * oneEther / 10n;\nconsole.log(amount.toString());","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+oneEther\\s*=\\s*10n\\s*\\*\\*\\s*18n","text":"Line 1: <code>const oneEther = 10n ** 18n;</code> (10 to the power 18, as BigInts)."},{"re":"const\\s+amount\\s*=\\s*25n\\s*\\*\\s*oneEther\\s*\\/\\s*10n","text":"Line 2: <code>const amount = 25n * oneEther / 10n;</code> Multiply first, then divide."},{"re":"amount\\.toString\\(\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3: print <code>amount.toString()</code>."}],"explain":"<p>Output: <code>2500000000000000000</code>. There is no decimal point anywhere: 2.5 ether is simply 2.5 x 10^18 wei. Multiplying before dividing matters with integers: <code>25n / 10n</code> alone would round down to <code>2n</code> and lose the half.</p>"},{"title":"Permission flags","file":"flags.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Store two permissions as bits in one number, then test one:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const CAN_READ = 1;</code></li><li><code>const CAN_WRITE = 2;</code></li><li><code>const perms = CAN_READ | CAN_WRITE;</code> (combine the flags with OR)</li><li><code>console.log(perms &amp; CAN_WRITE ? 'can write' : 'read only');</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const CAN_READ = 1;\nconst CAN_WRITE = 2;\nconst perms = CAN_READ | CAN_WRITE;\nconsole.log(perms & CAN_WRITE ? 'can write' : 'read only');","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+CAN_READ\\s*=\\s*1","text":"Line 1: <code>const CAN_READ = 1;</code> (binary 01)."},{"re":"const\\s+CAN_WRITE\\s*=\\s*2","text":"Line 2: <code>const CAN_WRITE = 2;</code> (binary 10)."},{"re":"const\\s+perms\\s*=\\s*CAN_READ\\s*\\|\\s*CAN_WRITE","text":"Line 3 combines them with a single bar: <code>CAN_READ | CAN_WRITE</code> (binary 11 = 3)."},{"re":"perms\\s*&\\s*CAN_WRITE\\s*\\?","text":"Line 4 tests the bit with a single <code>&amp;</code>, then a <code>?</code> for the two possible answers."}],"explain":"<p><code>1 | 2</code> is binary <code>11</code>, which is 3. <code>3 &amp; 2</code> keeps only the write bit, giving 2, which counts as true, so it prints <strong>can write</strong>. Single <code>&amp;</code> and <code>|</code> are bitwise; double <code>&amp;&amp;</code> and <code>||</code> are logical. Mixing them up is a classic bug.</p>"},{"title":"Declare Solidity types","file":"Types.sol","lang":"solidity","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write these three state-variable declarations, one per line (Solidity needs the semicolons):</p>\n<ol><li>A <code>uint8</code> called <code>small</code> set to <code>255</code>.</li><li>A <code>uint256</code> called <code>big</code> set to <code>1e18</code>.</li><li>An <code>int256</code> called <code>temperature</code> set to <code>-5</code>.</li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"uint8 small = 255;\nuint256 big = 1e18;\nint256 temperature = -5;","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*uint8\\s+small\\s*=\\s*255\\s*;","text":"Line 1: type first, then name: <code>uint8 small = 255;</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*uint256\\s+big\\s*=\\s*1e18\\s*;","text":"Line 2: <code>uint256 big = 1e18;</code> (1e18 means 1 followed by 18 zeros)."},{"re":"^\\s*int256\\s+temperature\\s*=\\s*-5\\s*;","text":"Line 3: signed type <code>int256</code> (no u), because the value is negative: <code>int256 temperature = -5;</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Solidity puts the type before the name and requires a semicolon after every statement. Trying <code>uint8 small = 256;</code> would fail to compile, because 256 does not fit in 8 bits.</p>"}],"text":"Why this matters Web developers can go years without thinking about bits. Blockchain developers cannot. Hashes are 256 bits, addresses are 20 bytes, token balances are uint256 , and one wrong assumption about integer size or precision has drained real money from real contracts. This lesson is the foundation for all of it. Binary: counting with two digits Computers store everything as bits : 0 or 1. In binary, each position is worth double the one to its right: Position value 128 64 32 16 8 4 2 1 13 in binary 0 0 0 0 1 1 0 1 8 + 4 + 1 = 13, so 13 is 00001101 . Eight bits are a byte , which holds 2^8 = 256 values: 0 to 255. Hexadecimal: the developer's shorthand Binary is long, so developers write bytes in hexadecimal (base 16), using digits 0 to 9 and letters a to f (for 10 to 15). One hex digit is exactly 4 bits, so one byte is always exactly two hex digits: 00 to ff . Decimal Binary Hex 0 00000000 00 10 00001010 0a 255 11111111 ff Hex is usually written with a 0x prefix: 0xff . Ethereum addresses ( 0x71C7... ) and hashes are hex for this reason. A 256-bit hash is 64 hex characters; a 20-byte address is 40. Text is bytes too Strings are stored as bytes using an encoding . The web's standard is UTF-8 : ASCII characters take one byte each; accented letters, other scripts and emoji take two to four. new TextEncoder().encode('Hi'); // Uint8Array [72, 105] new TextEncoder().encode('£'); // Uint8Array [194, 163] (two bytes) Hash functions and signatures work on bytes , not text, which is why crypto code always encodes strings first. Fixed-size integers Most languages store integers in a fixed number of bits . That sets a minimum and maximum: Type Bits Range uint8 (unsigned) 8 0 to 255 int8 (signed) 8 -128 to 127 int32 32 about -2.1 billion to 2.1 billion uint64 64 0 to about 1.8 x 10^19 uint256 256 0 to about 1.16 x 10^77 Unsigned ( uint ) types hold only zero and positives. Signed ( int ) types use one bit's worth of range for negatives (stored using a scheme called two's complement ). Solidity's default uint is uint256 : big enough for any realistic token balance with 18 decimals. Overflow: when numbers wrap around What is 255 + 1 in a uint8 ? There is no room for 256, so in many languages it silently wraps to 0, like a car odometer rolling over. Subtracting 1 from 0 in a uint wraps to the maximum value. In older Solidity (before 0.8), this was a real attack: in 2018 an overflow bug in a token's batch-transfer function (the BatchOverflow bug) let attackers create astronomically large balances from nothing. Since Solidity 0.8, arithmetic reverts on overflow by default. Code inside an unchecked { } block skips that check to save gas, so it must be proven safe. JavaScript numbers and BigInt JavaScript's number is a 64-bit float. It stores whole numbers exactly only up to Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER , which is 2^53 - 1 = 9,007,199,254,740,991. Beyond that, it silently loses precision. One ether is 10^18 wei, far beyond that limit for real balances. So blockchain JavaScript uses BigInt : integers of any size, written with an n suffix: 10n ** 18n . You cannot mix BigInt and normal numbers in one calculation without converting. Bitwise operators Bitwise operators work on the individual bits of integers: Operator Name Example Result AND (both bits 1) 0b1100 0b1010 0b1000 (8) | OR (either bit 1) 0b1100 | 0b1010 0b1110 (14) ^ XOR (bits differ) 0b1100 ^ 0b1010 0b0110 (6) ~ NOT (flip bits) ~0 -1 shift left 1 3 8 unsigned shift right 16 2 4 Uses you will actually meet: packing several true/false flags into one number (permissions, settings), hash function internals (SHA-256 is mostly rotations, XORs and ANDs; read sha256 in this site's assets/tools.js ), and gas-saving tricks in Solidity. The same types in Solidity uint8 small = 255; // 0 to 255 uint256 big = 1e18; // one token with 18 decimals int256 temperature = -5; // signed: negatives allowed address owner; // 20 bytes bytes32 id = keccak256(abi.encodePacked( ladder )); // 32 bytes, e.g. a ha","track":"setup"},{"id":"web-html","title":"HTML: the structure of every page","level":"beginner","minutes":20,"summary":"Elements, attributes, semantic tags, links, images and forms. Write a real page from scratch.","html":"<h2 id=\"elements-and-tags\">Elements and tags</h2>\n<p>HTML describes <strong>what things are</strong>: this is a heading, this is a paragraph, this is a button. You wrap content in <strong>tags</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"html\"><pre><code class=\"language-html\">&lt;p&gt;This is a paragraph.&lt;/p&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>&lt;p&gt;</code> opens, <code>&lt;/p&gt;</code> closes, and the whole thing is an <strong>element</strong>. Some elements have no content and no closing tag, like <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;br&gt;</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Attributes</strong> add information to an element:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"html\"><pre><code class=\"language-html\">&lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot;&gt;My GitHub&lt;/a&gt;\n&lt;img src=&quot;dress.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;Green linen wrap dress on a model&quot;&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"the-page-skeleton\">The page skeleton</h2>\n<p>Every page starts the same way. In VS Code, type <code>!</code> and press <kbd>Tab</kbd> to generate it.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"html\" data-try=\"1\"><pre><code class=\"language-html\">&lt;!doctype html&gt;\n&lt;html lang=&quot;en&quot;&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n  &lt;meta charset=&quot;utf-8&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;meta name=&quot;viewport&quot; content=&quot;width=device-width, initial-scale=1&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;title&gt;Amara Studio | Handmade jewellery&lt;/title&gt;\n  &lt;meta name=&quot;description&quot; content=&quot;Handmade brass jewellery from London.&quot;&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;header&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Amara Studio&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;nav&gt;\n      &lt;a href=&quot;#shop&quot;&gt;Shop&lt;/a&gt;\n      &lt;a href=&quot;#about&quot;&gt;About&lt;/a&gt;\n    &lt;/nav&gt;\n  &lt;/header&gt;\n\n  &lt;main&gt;\n    &lt;section id=&quot;shop&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;h2&gt;New collection&lt;/h2&gt;\n      &lt;p&gt;Hand-finished brass earrings, made in small batches.&lt;/p&gt;\n      &lt;ul&gt;\n        &lt;li&gt;Sun hoops, £34&lt;/li&gt;\n        &lt;li&gt;Tide drops, £28&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;/ul&gt;\n    &lt;/section&gt;\n\n    &lt;section id=&quot;about&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;h2&gt;About&lt;/h2&gt;\n      &lt;p&gt;Started at a kitchen table in &lt;strong&gt;2021&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;/section&gt;\n  &lt;/main&gt;\n\n  &lt;footer&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;&amp;copy; 2026 Amara Studio&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;/footer&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<ul><li><code>&lt;head&gt;</code> holds information <strong>about</strong> the page: title (shown in the tab and Google results), character set, the viewport setting that makes it work on phones.</li><li><code>&lt;body&gt;</code> holds everything you see.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"semantic-html-use-the-right-element\">Semantic HTML: use the right element</h2>\n<p>Professionals pick elements by <strong>meaning</strong>, not appearance. Screen readers, search engines and your future self all depend on it.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Use it for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>&lt;header&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;footer&gt;</code></td><td>The top and bottom of a page or section</td></tr><tr><td><code>&lt;nav&gt;</code></td><td>A group of navigation links</td></tr><tr><td><code>&lt;main&gt;</code></td><td>The main content (one per page)</td></tr><tr><td><code>&lt;section&gt;</code></td><td>A themed group of content with a heading</td></tr><tr><td><code>&lt;article&gt;</code></td><td>Self-contained content: a product card, a blog post</td></tr><tr><td><code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> to <code>&lt;h6&gt;</code></td><td>Headings in order. One <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> per page; do not skip levels</td></tr><tr><td><code>&lt;button&gt;</code></td><td>Anything clicked to do something</td></tr><tr><td><code>&lt;a&gt;</code></td><td>Anything clicked to go somewhere</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Avoid &quot;div soup&quot;</p><p>A page made of nothing but <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> elements works visually but is meaningless to assistive tech and search engines. Use <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> only when no meaningful element fits, usually for layout wrappers.</p></aside>\n<h2 id=\"images-done-right\">Images done right</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"html\"><pre><code class=\"language-html\">&lt;img src=&quot;images/sun-hoops.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;Brass sun hoop earrings on a linen cloth&quot; width=&quot;800&quot; height=&quot;1000&quot; loading=&quot;lazy&quot;&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<ul><li><code>alt</code> describes the image for blind users and when the image fails to load. Describe what matters; use <code>alt=&quot;&quot;</code> for purely decorative images.</li><li><code>width</code> and <code>height</code> stop the page jumping as images load.</li><li><code>loading=&quot;lazy&quot;</code> delays off-screen images, making pages faster.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"forms\">Forms</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"html\" data-try=\"1\"><pre><code class=\"language-html\">&lt;form&gt;\n  &lt;label for=&quot;email&quot;&gt;Email&lt;/label&gt;\n  &lt;input id=&quot;email&quot; name=&quot;email&quot; type=&quot;email&quot; required placeholder=&quot;you@example.com&quot;&gt;\n\n  &lt;label for=&quot;size&quot;&gt;Size&lt;/label&gt;\n  &lt;select id=&quot;size&quot; name=&quot;size&quot;&gt;\n    &lt;option&gt;S&lt;/option&gt;\n    &lt;option&gt;M&lt;/option&gt;\n    &lt;option&gt;L&lt;/option&gt;\n  &lt;/select&gt;\n\n  &lt;button type=&quot;submit&quot;&gt;Join the waitlist&lt;/button&gt;\n&lt;/form&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Every input needs a <code>&lt;label&gt;</code> linked with <code>for</code> and <code>id</code>. Clicking the label focuses the input, and screen readers announce it. Input <code>type</code>s like <code>email</code>, <code>tel</code>, <code>number</code> and <code>date</code> give mobile users the right keyboard and free validation.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"2\"></div>","quiz":[{"q":"Which element should wrap a site's main navigation links?","options":["div","nav","section"],"answer":1,"explain":"nav tells browsers, screen readers and search engines that these links are navigation."},{"q":"What is the alt attribute on an image for?","options":["The image's file name","A text description for screen-reader users and when the image fails to load","A tooltip on hover"],"answer":1,"explain":"alt text is essential for accessibility and helps SEO too."},{"q":"&quot;Add to bag&quot; adds an item without leaving the page. Which element should it be?","options":["button","a","div with a click handler"],"answer":0,"explain":"Buttons do things; links go places. A div is not keyboard-accessible by default."},{"q":"Where does the page title shown in the browser tab go?","options":["In an h1 in the body","In a title element inside head","In the footer"],"answer":1,"explain":"The title element lives in head and is also what Google shows in results."}],"exercises":[{"title":"A heading and a paragraph","file":"index.html","lang":"html","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write two elements: a main heading (<code>h1</code>) that says <code>Amara Studio</code>, and under it a paragraph (<code>p</code>) that says <code>Handmade brass jewellery.</code> Press <strong>Run it</strong> to see them rendered.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"<h1>Amara Studio</h1>\n<p>Handmade brass jewellery.</p>","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"<h1>","text":"Open the heading with <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code>."},{"re":"<h1>\\s*Amara Studio\\s*</h1>","text":"Close it with <code>&lt;/h1&gt;</code> (note the slash): <code>&lt;h1&gt;Amara Studio&lt;/h1&gt;</code>"},{"re":"<p>\\s*Handmade brass jewellery\\.\\s*</p>","text":"Line 2: <code>&lt;p&gt;Handmade brass jewellery.&lt;/p&gt;</code> including the full stop."}],"explain":"<p>Every element opens with <code>&lt;name&gt;</code> and closes with <code>&lt;/name&gt;</code>. The browser shows <code>h1</code> large and bold because it is the most important heading, not because of how you typed it.</p>"},{"title":"An image with good alt text","file":"index.html","lang":"html","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Add an image of the file <code>hoops.jpg</code>. Give it the alt text <code>Brass sun hoop earrings</code> and lazy loading. Write the attributes in this order: <code>src</code>, <code>alt</code>, <code>loading</code>.</p>\n<p>Press <strong>Run it</strong>: the image file does not exist here, so you will see your alt text instead, exactly as a screen reader user would hear it.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"<img src=\"hoops.jpg\" alt=\"Brass sun hoop earrings\" loading=\"lazy\">","also":["<img src=\"hoops.jpg\" alt=\"Brass sun hoop earrings\" loading=\"lazy\" />"],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*<img\\s","text":"Images use the <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> element, which has no closing tag."},{"re":"src=[\"']hoops\\.jpg[\"']","text":"First attribute: <code>src=&quot;hoops.jpg&quot;</code>"},{"re":"alt=[\"']Brass sun hoop earrings[\"']","text":"Second: <code>alt=&quot;Brass sun hoop earrings&quot;</code>"},{"re":"loading=[\"']lazy[\"']","text":"Third: <code>loading=&quot;lazy&quot;</code>, then close the tag with <code>&gt;</code>."}],"explain":""},{"title":"A labelled email field","file":"index.html","lang":"html","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write a label and an email input that are linked together:</p>\n<ol><li>A <code>label</code> with <code>for=&quot;email&quot;</code> and the text <code>Email</code>.</li><li>An <code>input</code> with these attributes in this order: <code>id=&quot;email&quot;</code>, <code>name=&quot;email&quot;</code>, <code>type=&quot;email&quot;</code>, and <code>required</code>.</li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"<label for=\"email\">Email</label>\n<input id=\"email\" name=\"email\" type=\"email\" required>","also":["<label for=\"email\">Email</label>\n<input id=\"email\" name=\"email\" type=\"email\" required />"],"hints":[{"re":"<label\\s+for=[\"']email[\"']\\s*>","text":"Line 1 opens with <code>&lt;label for=&quot;email&quot;&gt;</code>"},{"re":">\\s*Email\\s*</label>","text":"Then the text <code>Email</code> and the closing <code>&lt;/label&gt;</code>."},{"re":"<input\\s+id=[\"']email[\"']","text":"Line 2 starts <code>&lt;input id=&quot;email&quot;</code>. The <code>id</code> must match the label's <code>for</code>."},{"re":"name=[\"']email[\"']\\s+type=[\"']email[\"']","text":"Then <code>name=&quot;email&quot; type=&quot;email&quot;</code>."},{"re":"required\\s*/?>\\s*$","text":"End with the word <code>required</code> and close the tag with <code>&gt;</code>."}],"explain":"<p><code>for=&quot;email&quot;</code> on the label points at <code>id=&quot;email&quot;</code> on the input. Clicking the label now focuses the field, and screen readers announce &quot;Email&quot;. <code>type=&quot;email&quot;</code> gives phone users an @ keyboard and free validation.</p>"}],"text":"Elements and tags HTML describes what things are : this is a heading, this is a paragraph, this is a button. You wrap content in tags : p This is a paragraph. /p p opens, /p closes, and the whole thing is an element . Some elements have no content and no closing tag, like img and br . Attributes add information to an element: a href= https://github.com target= _blank My GitHub /a img src= dress.jpg alt= Green linen wrap dress on a model The page skeleton Every page starts the same way. In VS Code, type ! and press Tab to generate it. !doctype html html lang= en head meta charset= utf-8 meta name= viewport content= width=device-width, initial-scale=1 title Amara Studio | Handmade jewellery /title meta name= description content= Handmade brass jewellery from London. /head body header h1 Amara Studio /h1 nav a href= #shop Shop /a a href= #about About /a /nav /header main section id= shop h2 New collection /h2 p Hand-finished brass earrings, made in small batches. /p ul li Sun hoops, £34 /li li Tide drops, £28 /li /ul /section section id= about h2 About /h2 p Started at a kitchen table in strong 2021 /strong . /p /section /main footer p copy; 2026 Amara Studio /p /footer /body /html head holds information about the page: title (shown in the tab and Google results), character set, the viewport setting that makes it work on phones. body holds everything you see. Semantic HTML: use the right element Professionals pick elements by meaning , not appearance. Screen readers, search engines and your future self all depend on it. Element Use it for header , footer The top and bottom of a page or section nav A group of navigation links main The main content (one per page) section A themed group of content with a heading article Self-contained content: a product card, a blog post h1 to h6 Headings in order. One h1 per page; do not skip levels button Anything clicked to do something a Anything clicked to go somewhere Avoid div soup A page made of nothing but div elements works visually but is meaningless to assistive tech and search engines. Use div only when no meaningful element fits, usually for layout wrappers. Images done right img src= images/sun-hoops.jpg alt= Brass sun hoop earrings on a linen cloth width= 800 height= 1000 loading= lazy alt describes the image for blind users and when the image fails to load. Describe what matters; use alt= for purely decorative images. width and height stop the page jumping as images load. loading= lazy delays off-screen images, making pages faster. Forms form label for= email Email /label input id= email name= email type= email required placeholder= you@example.com label for= size Size /label select id= size name= size option S /option option M /option option L /option /select button type= submit Join the waitlist /button /form Every input needs a label linked with for and id . Clicking the label focuses the input, and screen readers announce it. Input type s like email , tel , number and date give mobile users the right keyboard and free validation.","track":"web"},{"id":"web-css","title":"CSS: styling and the box model","level":"beginner","minutes":25,"summary":"Selectors, the cascade, colours, fonts, units and the box model that controls every element's size.","html":"<h2 id=\"how-css-works\">How CSS works</h2>\n<p>CSS is a list of <strong>rules</strong>. Each rule picks elements with a <strong>selector</strong> and sets <strong>properties</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"css\"><pre><code class=\"language-css\">h1 {\n  color: #1f1a17;\n  font-size: 2.5rem;\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>Link a stylesheet from your HTML <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"html\"><pre><code class=\"language-html\">&lt;link rel=&quot;stylesheet&quot; href=&quot;styles.css&quot;&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"selectors-you-will-use-daily\">Selectors you will use daily</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Selector</th><th>Matches</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>p</code></td><td>Every paragraph</td></tr><tr><td><code>.card</code></td><td>Elements with <code>class=&quot;card&quot;</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>#hero</code></td><td>The element with <code>id=&quot;hero&quot;</code> (use sparingly)</td></tr><tr><td><code>.card h2</code></td><td>h2 elements inside a .card</td></tr><tr><td><code>.card &gt; h2</code></td><td>h2 elements that are direct children of a .card</td></tr><tr><td><code>button:hover</code></td><td>Buttons while the mouse is over them</td></tr><tr><td><code>a:focus-visible</code></td><td>Links focused with the keyboard</td></tr><tr><td><code>li:first-child</code></td><td>The first li in its list</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Style mostly with <strong>classes</strong>. They are reusable and keep specificity low.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-cascade-and-specificity\">The cascade and specificity</h2>\n<p>When two rules target the same element, the browser decides:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Specificity</strong>: <code>#id</code> beats <code>.class</code> beats <code>element</code>.</li><li>If equal, <strong>the later rule wins</strong>.</li><li><code>!important</code> overrides almost everything. Avoid it; it starts specificity wars.</li></ol>\n<p>Some properties (like <code>color</code> and <code>font-family</code>) are <strong>inherited</strong>: set them on <code>body</code> and every child gets them.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-box-model\">The box model</h2>\n<p>Every element is a rectangular box with four layers:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">+------------------- margin -------------------+\n|  +---------------- border ----------------+  |\n|  |  +------------- padding -------------+ |  |\n|  |  |            content                | |  |\n|  |  +-----------------------------------+ |  |\n|  +----------------------------------------+  |\n+----------------------------------------------+</code></pre></div>\n<ul><li><strong>content</strong>: the text or image</li><li><strong>padding</strong>: space inside the border</li><li><strong>border</strong>: the edge</li><li><strong>margin</strong>: space outside, pushing other elements away</li></ul>\n<p>By default, <code>width</code> only sets the content size, so padding and border make the box bigger than you asked. Every professional stylesheet starts with this fix:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"css\"><pre><code class=\"language-css\">*, *::before, *::after {\n  box-sizing: border-box;\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>Now <code>width: 300px</code> means 300px total, padding and border included.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"colours-fonts-and-units\">Colours, fonts and units</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"css\" data-try=\"1\"><pre><code class=\"language-css\">:root {\n  --brand: #b4532a;     /* custom property: reuse it everywhere */\n  --ink: #1f1a17;\n  --paper: #fbf7f3;\n}\n\nbody {\n  margin: 0;\n  background: var(--paper);\n  color: var(--ink);\n  font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, &quot;Segoe UI&quot;, sans-serif;\n  font-size: 1rem;       /* rem = relative to the root font size (16px default) */\n  line-height: 1.6;\n}\n\n.button {\n  display: inline-block;\n  padding: 0.75rem 1.25rem;\n  background: var(--brand);\n  color: white;\n  border-radius: 999px;\n  text-decoration: none;\n  font-weight: 600;\n}\n\n.button:hover {\n  background: var(--ink);\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Unit</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Use for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>px</code></td><td>Pixels</td><td>Borders, small fixed details</td></tr><tr><td><code>rem</code></td><td>Multiples of the root font size</td><td>Font sizes, spacing</td></tr><tr><td><code>%</code></td><td>Percentage of the parent</td><td>Widths</td></tr><tr><td><code>vw</code> / <code>vh</code></td><td>1% of the viewport width / height</td><td>Full-screen sections (carefully)</td></tr><tr><td><code>ch</code></td><td>Width of the &quot;0&quot; character</td><td>Readable line lengths, e.g. <code>max-width: 65ch</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">The red outline trick</p><p>When a layout misbehaves, add <code>* { outline: 1px solid red; }</code> temporarily. You will instantly see every box and what is overflowing.</p></aside>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"2\"></div>","quiz":[{"q":"Which selector matches every element with class=&quot;price&quot;?","options":["#price",".price","price"],"answer":1,"explain":"A dot selects by class, a hash by id, a bare word by element name."},{"q":"With box-sizing: border-box, a box has width: 300px, padding: 20px and border: 2px. How wide is it on screen?","options":["300px","344px","320px"],"answer":0,"explain":"border-box makes width include padding and border. Without it, the box would be 344px."},{"q":"Two rules set a paragraph's colour: <code>.intro { color: red }</code> and <code>p { color: blue }</code>. Which colour wins?","options":["Red, because a class is more specific than an element selector","Blue, because it comes later","Whichever is first in the file"],"answer":0,"explain":"Specificity is checked before source order."},{"q":"Why define brand colours as custom properties like --brand?","options":["They load faster","One change updates the colour everywhere it is used","Browsers require it"],"answer":1,"explain":"Variables make design systems and rebrands easy."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Your first CSS rule","file":"styles.css","lang":"css","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write a rule that styles every <code>h1</code>: colour <code>#b4532a</code> and font size <code>2.5rem</code>. One property per line, each ending with a semicolon. Press <strong>Run it</strong> to see it applied to a sample page.</p>","starter":"","html":"<h1>Amara Studio</h1>\n<p>Handmade brass jewellery from London.</p>","solution":"h1 {\n  color: #b4532a;\n  font-size: 2.5rem;\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*h1\\s*\\{","text":"Start with the selector and an opening brace: <code>h1 {</code>"},{"re":"color\\s*:\\s*#b4532a","text":"<code>color: #b4532a;</code> (American spelling: color)."},{"re":"font-size\\s*:\\s*2\\.5rem","text":"<code>font-size: 2.5rem;</code> with a hyphen in the property name."},{"re":"^\\s*\\}\\s*$","text":"Close the rule with <code>}</code>."}],"explain":""},{"title":"The border-box reset","file":"styles.css","lang":"css","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write the rule every professional stylesheet starts with: make every element, and every <code>::before</code> and <code>::after</code> pseudo-element, use <code>box-sizing: border-box</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"*, *::before, *::after {\n  box-sizing: border-box;\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*\\*\\s*,","text":"The selector starts with <code>*</code> (every element), then a comma."},{"re":"\\*::before\\s*,\\s*\\*::after","text":"Then <code>*::before, *::after</code> (two colons each)."},{"re":"box-sizing\\s*:\\s*border-box","text":"Inside: <code>box-sizing: border-box;</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"Brand colours with a variable","file":"styles.css","lang":"css","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write two rules:</p>\n<ol><li>On <code>:root</code>, define a custom property <code>--brand</code> with the value <code>#b4532a</code>.</li><li>On <code>.button</code>, set <code>background</code> to <code>var(--brand)</code> and <code>color</code> to <code>white</code>.</li></ol>","starter":"","html":"<a class=\"button\" href=\"#\">Shop now</a>","solution":":root {\n  --brand: #b4532a;\n}\n.button {\n  background: var(--brand);\n  color: white;\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*:root\\s*\\{","text":"Rule 1 starts <code>:root {</code>"},{"re":"--brand\\s*:\\s*#b4532a","text":"Custom properties start with two hyphens: <code>--brand: #b4532a;</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*\\.button\\s*\\{","text":"Rule 2 starts <code>.button {</code> (a dot, because it is a class)."},{"re":"background\\s*:\\s*var\\(\\s*--brand\\s*\\)","text":"Use the variable with <code>var(--brand)</code>: <code>background: var(--brand);</code>"},{"re":"color\\s*:\\s*white","text":"Then <code>color: white;</code>"}],"explain":""}],"text":"How CSS works CSS is a list of rules . Each rule picks elements with a selector and sets properties : h1 { color: #1f1a17; font-size: 2.5rem; } Link a stylesheet from your HTML head : link rel= stylesheet href= styles.css Selectors you will use daily Selector Matches p Every paragraph .card Elements with class= card #hero The element with id= hero (use sparingly) .card h2 h2 elements inside a .card .card h2 h2 elements that are direct children of a .card button:hover Buttons while the mouse is over them a:focus-visible Links focused with the keyboard li:first-child The first li in its list Style mostly with classes . They are reusable and keep specificity low. The cascade and specificity When two rules target the same element, the browser decides: Specificity : #id beats .class beats element . If equal, the later rule wins . !important overrides almost everything. Avoid it; it starts specificity wars. Some properties (like color and font-family ) are inherited : set them on body and every child gets them. The box model Every element is a rectangular box with four layers: +------------------- margin -------------------+ | +---------------- border ----------------+ | | | +------------- padding -------------+ | | | | | content | | | | | +-----------------------------------+ | | | +----------------------------------------+ | +----------------------------------------------+ content : the text or image padding : space inside the border border : the edge margin : space outside, pushing other elements away By default, width only sets the content size, so padding and border make the box bigger than you asked. Every professional stylesheet starts with this fix: *, *::before, *::after { box-sizing: border-box; } Now width: 300px means 300px total, padding and border included. Colours, fonts and units :root { --brand: #b4532a; /* custom property: reuse it everywhere */ --ink: #1f1a17; --paper: #fbf7f3; } body { margin: 0; background: var(--paper); color: var(--ink); font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, Segoe UI , sans-serif; font-size: 1rem; /* rem = relative to the root font size (16px default) */ line-height: 1.6; } .button { display: inline-block; padding: 0.75rem 1.25rem; background: var(--brand); color: white; border-radius: 999px; text-decoration: none; font-weight: 600; } .button:hover { background: var(--ink); } Unit Meaning Use for px Pixels Borders, small fixed details rem Multiples of the root font size Font sizes, spacing % Percentage of the parent Widths vw / vh 1% of the viewport width / height Full-screen sections (carefully) ch Width of the 0 character Readable line lengths, e.g. max-width: 65ch The red outline trick When a layout misbehaves, add * { outline: 1px solid red; } temporarily. You will instantly see every box and what is overflowing.","track":"web"},{"id":"web-layout","title":"Layout with Flexbox, Grid and responsive design","level":"beginner","minutes":30,"summary":"Put things side by side, build grids that reflow on phones, and design mobile-first.","html":"<h2 id=\"two-layout-tools-two-jobs\">Two layout tools, two jobs</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Flexbox</strong> arranges items in <strong>one direction</strong>: a row of nav links, a button with an icon, a card's contents.</li><li><strong>Grid</strong> arranges items in <strong>two dimensions</strong>: a product grid, a page with a sidebar.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"flexbox\">Flexbox</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"css\"><pre><code class=\"language-css\">.nav {\n  display: flex;\n  gap: 1rem;                       /* space between items */\n  justify-content: space-between;  /* main axis (horizontal in a row) */\n  align-items: center;             /* cross axis (vertical in a row) */\n  flex-wrap: wrap;                 /* let items wrap on small screens */\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Property</th><th>Common values</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>flex-direction</code></td><td><code>row</code> (default), <code>column</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>justify-content</code></td><td><code>flex-start</code>, <code>center</code>, <code>space-between</code>, <code>space-evenly</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>align-items</code></td><td><code>stretch</code> (default), <code>center</code>, <code>flex-start</code>, <code>flex-end</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>gap</code></td><td>Any length, e.g. <code>1rem</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>On a child, <code>flex: 1</code> means &quot;take up the remaining space&quot;. Load the <strong>Flexbox lab</strong> template in the <a href=\"#playground\">Playground</a> and try every value.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"grid\">Grid</h2>\n<p>The single most useful line of CSS for product grids:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"css\"><pre><code class=\"language-css\">.products {\n  display: grid;\n  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(220px, 1fr));\n  gap: 1.5rem;\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>This says: make as many columns as fit, each at least 220px wide, sharing leftover space equally. Four columns on a laptop, two on a tablet, one on a phone, with <strong>no media queries</strong>.</p>\n<p>A classic page layout:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"css\"><pre><code class=\"language-css\">.page {\n  display: grid;\n  grid-template-columns: 250px 1fr;   /* sidebar, then the rest */\n  grid-template-areas: &quot;side main&quot;;\n  gap: 2rem;\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"responsive-design-mobile-first\">Responsive design, mobile first</h2>\n<p>Most visitors to brand and shop sites are on phones. Write the phone layout first, then add rules for larger screens with <strong>media queries</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"html\" data-try=\"1\"><pre><code class=\"language-html\">&lt;!doctype html&gt;\n&lt;html lang=&quot;en&quot;&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n&lt;meta name=&quot;viewport&quot; content=&quot;width=device-width, initial-scale=1&quot;&gt;\n&lt;style&gt;\n  * { box-sizing: border-box; }\n  body { margin: 0; font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; }\n  .hero { padding: 2rem 1rem; background: #f6efe9; }\n  .hero h1 { font-size: clamp(2rem, 6vw, 3.5rem); margin: 0 0 .5rem; }\n  .grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(160px, 1fr)); gap: 1rem; padding: 1rem; }\n  .card { background: #fff; border: 1px solid #eadfd6; border-radius: 12px; padding: 1rem; }\n  .card .img { aspect-ratio: 4/5; background: #e9c9b4; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: .5rem; }\n\n  /* Larger screens: more breathing room */\n  @media (min-width: 768px) {\n    .hero { padding: 4rem 3rem; }\n    .grid { padding: 2rem 3rem; gap: 1.5rem; }\n  }\n&lt;/style&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;section class=&quot;hero&quot;&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Summer drop&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;Breathable linen, cut for heat.&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;/section&gt;\n  &lt;section class=&quot;grid&quot;&gt;\n    &lt;article class=&quot;card&quot;&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;img&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;Wrap dress, £68&lt;/article&gt;\n    &lt;article class=&quot;card&quot;&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;img&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;Wide trousers, £54&lt;/article&gt;\n    &lt;article class=&quot;card&quot;&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;img&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;Camp shirt, £42&lt;/article&gt;\n    &lt;article class=&quot;card&quot;&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;img&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;Bucket hat, £22&lt;/article&gt;\n  &lt;/section&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Notice <code>clamp(2rem, 6vw, 3.5rem)</code>: the heading scales with the screen but never goes below 2rem or above 3.5rem.</p>\n<p>Press <strong>Run in Playground</strong>, then make the browser window narrower to watch the grid reflow.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"2\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"the-viewport-meta-tag\">The viewport meta tag</h2>\n<p>Without this in your <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>, phones pretend to be a desktop and shrink everything:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"html\"><pre><code class=\"language-html\">&lt;meta name=&quot;viewport&quot; content=&quot;width=device-width, initial-scale=1&quot;&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Test like a pro</p><p>In Chrome DevTools press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>M</kbd> (device toolbar) and check your page at 375px wide (a common phone), 768px (tablet) and 1280px (laptop). Then check on your real phone.</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"You need a row of five nav links spaced evenly across a header. Best tool?","options":["Flexbox","Grid","Absolute positioning"],"answer":0,"explain":"One direction of items: Flexbox."},{"q":"What does <code>grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(220px, 1fr))</code> do?","options":["Always makes exactly 220 columns","Fits as many columns as possible, each at least 220px, sharing leftover space","Makes one column 220px wide"],"answer":1,"explain":"It produces a responsive grid without any media queries."},{"q":"What does &quot;mobile first&quot; mean?","options":["Only build for phones","Write base styles for small screens, then add min-width media queries for bigger ones","Build the desktop version first and hide things on mobile"],"answer":1,"explain":"Base styles serve phones; media queries enhance for larger screens."},{"q":"Your site looks tiny and zoomed out on phones. What is most likely missing?","options":["A media query","The viewport meta tag in head","box-sizing: border-box"],"answer":1,"explain":"Without the viewport tag, mobile browsers render at desktop width and scale down."}],"exercises":[{"title":"A flexbox navigation bar","file":"styles.css","lang":"css","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Style <code>.nav</code> as a flex row: <code>display: flex</code>, a <code>gap</code> of <code>1rem</code>, <code>justify-content: space-between</code> and <code>align-items: center</code>, in that order.</p>","starter":"","html":"<nav class=\"nav\"><strong>Amara</strong><a href=\"#\">Shop</a><a href=\"#\">About</a><a href=\"#\">Bag (0)</a></nav>","solution":".nav {\n  display: flex;\n  gap: 1rem;\n  justify-content: space-between;\n  align-items: center;\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*\\.nav\\s*\\{","text":"Start with <code>.nav {</code>"},{"re":"display\\s*:\\s*flex","text":"<code>display: flex;</code> turns on flexbox."},{"re":"gap\\s*:\\s*1rem","text":"<code>gap: 1rem;</code>"},{"re":"justify-content\\s*:\\s*space-between","text":"<code>justify-content: space-between;</code>"},{"re":"align-items\\s*:\\s*center","text":"<code>align-items: center;</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"A responsive product grid","file":"styles.css","lang":"css","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Make <code>.products</code> a grid that fits as many columns as possible, each at least <code>150px</code> wide, with a <code>1rem</code> gap. Three properties: <code>display</code>, <code>grid-template-columns</code>, <code>gap</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"<style>.p{background:#f6efe9;padding:2rem 1rem;border-radius:10px;font-family:system-ui}</style>\n<section class=\"products\"><div class=\"p\">Wrap dress</div><div class=\"p\">Camp shirt</div><div class=\"p\">Bucket hat</div><div class=\"p\">Slip dress</div><div class=\"p\">Trousers</div></section>","solution":".products {\n  display: grid;\n  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(150px, 1fr));\n  gap: 1rem;\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"display\\s*:\\s*grid","text":"<code>display: grid;</code>"},{"re":"grid-template-columns\\s*:\\s*repeat\\(","text":"<code>grid-template-columns: repeat(...)</code>"},{"re":"repeat\\(\\s*auto-fill\\s*,\\s*minmax\\(\\s*150px\\s*,\\s*1fr\\s*\\)\\s*\\)","text":"Inside: <code>repeat(auto-fill, minmax(150px, 1fr))</code>. Count the brackets: two open, two close."},{"re":"gap\\s*:\\s*1rem","text":"<code>gap: 1rem;</code>"}],"explain":"<p><code>minmax(150px, 1fr)</code> means &quot;at least 150px, otherwise share the space&quot;. <code>auto-fill</code> makes as many columns as fit. Resize your window: the grid reflows with no media queries.</p>"},{"title":"Your first media query","file":"styles.css","lang":"css","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write a media query for screens at least <code>768px</code> wide that gives <code>.hero</code> a padding of <code>4rem 3rem</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"@media (min-width: 768px) {\n  .hero {\n    padding: 4rem 3rem;\n  }\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*@media\\s*\\(\\s*min-width\\s*:\\s*768px\\s*\\)\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>@media (min-width: 768px) {</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*\\.hero\\s*\\{","text":"Inside it, a normal rule: <code>.hero {</code>"},{"re":"padding\\s*:\\s*4rem\\s+3rem","text":"<code>padding: 4rem 3rem;</code> (top/bottom, then left/right)."},{"re":"\\}\\s*\\n\\s*\\}\\s*$","text":"Two closing braces: one for <code>.hero</code>, one for the media query."}],"explain":""}],"text":"Two layout tools, two jobs Flexbox arranges items in one direction : a row of nav links, a button with an icon, a card's contents. Grid arranges items in two dimensions : a product grid, a page with a sidebar. Flexbox .nav { display: flex; gap: 1rem; /* space between items */ justify-content: space-between; /* main axis (horizontal in a row) */ align-items: center; /* cross axis (vertical in a row) */ flex-wrap: wrap; /* let items wrap on small screens */ } Property Common values flex-direction row (default), column justify-content flex-start , center , space-between , space-evenly align-items stretch (default), center , flex-start , flex-end gap Any length, e.g. 1rem On a child, flex: 1 means take up the remaining space . Load the Flexbox lab template in the Playground and try every value. Grid The single most useful line of CSS for product grids: .products { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(220px, 1fr)); gap: 1.5rem; } This says: make as many columns as fit, each at least 220px wide, sharing leftover space equally. Four columns on a laptop, two on a tablet, one on a phone, with no media queries . A classic page layout: .page { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 250px 1fr; /* sidebar, then the rest */ grid-template-areas: side main ; gap: 2rem; } Responsive design, mobile first Most visitors to brand and shop sites are on phones. Write the phone layout first, then add rules for larger screens with media queries : !doctype html html lang= en head meta name= viewport content= width=device-width, initial-scale=1 style * { box-sizing: border-box; } body { margin: 0; font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; } .hero { padding: 2rem 1rem; background: #f6efe9; } .hero h1 { font-size: clamp(2rem, 6vw, 3.5rem); margin: 0 0 .5rem; } .grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(160px, 1fr)); gap: 1rem; padding: 1rem; } .card { background: #fff; border: 1px solid #eadfd6; border-radius: 12px; padding: 1rem; } .card .img { aspect-ratio: 4/5; background: #e9c9b4; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: .5rem; } /* Larger screens: more breathing room */ @media (min-width: 768px) { .hero { padding: 4rem 3rem; } .grid { padding: 2rem 3rem; gap: 1.5rem; } } /style /head body section class= hero h1 Summer drop /h1 p Breathable linen, cut for heat. /p /section section class= grid article class= card div class= img /div Wrap dress, £68 /article article class= card div class= img /div Wide trousers, £54 /article article class= card div class= img /div Camp shirt, £42 /article article class= card div class= img /div Bucket hat, £22 /article /section /body /html Notice clamp(2rem, 6vw, 3.5rem) : the heading scales with the screen but never goes below 2rem or above 3.5rem. Press Run in Playground , then make the browser window narrower to watch the grid reflow. The viewport meta tag Without this in your head , phones pretend to be a desktop and shrink everything: meta name= viewport content= width=device-width, initial-scale=1 Test like a pro In Chrome DevTools press Ctrl + Shift + M (device toolbar) and check your page at 375px wide (a common phone), 768px (tablet) and 1280px (laptop). Then check on your real phone.","track":"web"},{"id":"web-js-basics","title":"JavaScript basics: values, variables and functions","level":"beginner","minutes":25,"summary":"The building blocks of every program, written and run in your browser.","html":"<h2 id=\"where-javascript-runs\">Where JavaScript runs</h2>\n<p>JavaScript runs in every browser and, with Node.js, on servers and your computer. Open any page, press <kbd>F12</kbd>, click <strong>Console</strong>, and type <code>2 + 2</code>. You are programming.</p>\n<p>For this lesson, use the <a href=\"#playground\">Playground</a> (choose the <em>JavaScript console basics</em> template) or the browser console.</p>\n<h2 id=\"values-and-types\">Values and types</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">'Linen dress'      // string: text, in quotes\n68                 // number: integers and decimals are both &quot;number&quot;\ntrue               // boolean: true or false\nnull               // &quot;deliberately nothing&quot;\nundefined          // &quot;not set yet&quot;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Check a type with <code>typeof</code>: <code>typeof 68</code> gives <code>&quot;number&quot;</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"variables\">Variables</h2>\n<p>A variable is a named box for a value.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const brand = 'Amara Studio';   // const: cannot be reassigned\nlet stock = 12;                 // let: can be reassigned\nstock = stock - 1;              // now 11</code></pre></div>\n<p><strong>Use <code>const</code> by default.</strong> Switch to <code>let</code> only when the value must change. Avoid <code>var</code>; it is the old way and has confusing rules.</p>\n<p>Names are written in <strong>camelCase</strong>: <code>cartTotal</code>, <code>isLoggedIn</code>, <code>maxSupply</code>. Make them descriptive.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"operators\">Operators</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">10 + 5      // 15\n10 - 5      // 5\n10 * 5      // 50\n10 / 4      // 2.5\n10 % 3      // 1 (remainder)\n2 ** 3      // 8 (power)\n\n'Hello, ' + 'world'          // joining strings\n`Total: £${(6800 / 100).toFixed(2)}`   // template literal with backticks: &quot;Total: £68.00&quot;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Comparisons give booleans:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">5 === 5        // true   (equal value AND type)\n5 === '5'      // false\n5 !== 3        // true\n7 &gt; 3          // true\nage &gt;= 18 &amp;&amp; hasId     // AND: both must be true\nisMember || hasCode    // OR: at least one true\n!isSoldOut             // NOT: flips true/false</code></pre></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Always use === not ==</p><p><code>==</code> converts types before comparing, giving surprises like <code>'0' == false</code> being <code>true</code>. Use <code>===</code> and <code>!==</code> every time.</p></aside>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"making-decisions\">Making decisions</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const stock = 3;\n\nif (stock === 0) {\n  console.log('Sold out');\n} else if (stock &lt; 5) {\n  console.log(`Only ${stock} left`);\n} else {\n  console.log('In stock');\n}</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"functions\">Functions</h2>\n<p>A function is a reusable recipe. It takes <strong>parameters</strong> and <strong>returns</strong> a result.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-try=\"1\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">// function declaration\nfunction priceWithVat(pence) {\n  return Math.round(pence * 1.2);\n}\n\n// arrow function: the same idea, shorter syntax\nconst formatGBP = (pence) =&gt; `£${(pence / 100).toFixed(2)}`;\n\nconst dress = 5667;\nconsole.log(formatGBP(priceWithVat(dress)));   // £68.00\n\n// a function with a default parameter\nfunction greet(name = 'there') {\n  return `Hi ${name}, welcome back!`;\n}\nconsole.log(greet('Tolu'));\nconsole.log(greet());</code></pre></div>\n<p>Good functions do <strong>one thing</strong> and are named with a verb: <code>calculateTotal</code>, <code>formatDate</code>, <code>isValidEmail</code>.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Money is stored in pence</p><p><code>0.1 + 0.2</code> in JavaScript is <code>0.30000000000000004</code>, because computers store decimals in binary. Store money as whole pence (or cents) and only divide by 100 when displaying it. Blockchains do the same thing with 18 decimal places, as you will see later.</p></aside>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"2\"></div>","quiz":[{"q":"Which keyword should you use by default to declare a variable?","options":["const","let","var"],"answer":0,"explain":"const prevents accidental reassignment. Use let only when the value has to change."},{"q":"What does <code>5 === '5'</code> return?","options":["true","false","An error"],"answer":1,"explain":"=== compares type as well as value. A number is not a string."},{"q":"Why store £68.00 as 6800?","options":["It uses less memory","Decimal maths in binary is imprecise; whole numbers of pence are exact","JavaScript cannot display decimals"],"answer":1,"explain":"Floating-point rounding errors cause real money bugs. Integers avoid them."},{"q":"What does this return: <code>const double = (n) =&gt; n * 2; double(21)</code>","options":["42","21","undefined"],"answer":0,"explain":"An arrow function with one expression returns that expression automatically."}],"exercises":[{"title":"const and let","file":"stock.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write four lines:</p>\n<ol><li>A constant <code>brand</code> holding <code>'Amara Studio'</code>.</li><li>A variable (<code>let</code>) <code>stock</code> holding <code>12</code>.</li><li>Reduce stock by one: <code>stock = stock - 1;</code></li><li>Print both: <code>console.log(brand, stock);</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const brand = 'Amara Studio';\nlet stock = 12;\nstock = stock - 1;\nconsole.log(brand, stock);","also":["const brand = 'Amara Studio';\nlet stock = 12;\nstock -= 1;\nconsole.log(brand, stock);"],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+brand\\s*=\\s*['\"]Amara Studio['\"]","text":"Line 1: <code>const brand = 'Amara Studio';</code>"},{"re":"let\\s+stock\\s*=\\s*12","text":"Line 2 uses <code>let</code>, because the value will change: <code>let stock = 12;</code>"},{"re":"stock\\s*(=\\s*stock\\s*-|-=)\\s*1","text":"Line 3: <code>stock = stock - 1;</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*brand\\s*,\\s*stock\\s*\\)","text":"Line 4: <code>console.log(brand, stock);</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Output: <code>Amara Studio 11</code>. Try changing <code>let</code> to <code>const</code> and run it: you get <code>TypeError: Assignment to constant variable</code>. That error is <code>const</code> protecting you.</p>"},{"title":"Format a price with a template literal","file":"price.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write two lines:</p>\n<ol><li>A constant <code>pence</code> equal to <code>6800</code>.</li><li>Print the text <code>Total: £68.00</code> using a <strong>template literal</strong> (text between backticks) with <code>${(pence / 100).toFixed(2)}</code> inside it.</li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const pence = 6800;\nconsole.log(`Total: £${(pence / 100).toFixed(2)}`);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+pence\\s*=\\s*6800","text":"Line 1: <code>const pence = 6800;</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*`","text":"Line 2 starts <code>console.log(</code> and then a backtick (the key above Tab on most keyboards), not a quote."},{"re":"`Total: £\\$\\{","text":"Inside the backticks: <code>Total: £</code> then a dollar sign and an opening curly brace."},{"re":"\\(\\s*pence\\s*\\/\\s*100\\s*\\)\\.toFixed\\(\\s*2\\s*\\)","text":"Inside the braces: <code>(pence / 100).toFixed(2)</code> then close the brace and the backtick."}],"explain":"<p>Inside backticks, anything in <code>${ }</code> is calculated and dropped into the text. Output: <code>Total: £68.00</code>. Money stays in pence until the moment you display it.</p>"},{"title":"A function and an arrow function","file":"vat.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write:</p>\n<ol><li>A function <code>priceWithVat(pence)</code> that returns <code>Math.round(pence * 1.2)</code>.</li><li>An arrow function constant <code>formatGBP</code> that takes <code>pence</code> and returns <code>(pence / 100).toFixed(2)</code>.</li><li><code>console.log(formatGBP(priceWithVat(5667)));</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"function priceWithVat(pence) {\n  return Math.round(pence * 1.2);\n}\nconst formatGBP = (pence) => (pence / 100).toFixed(2);\nconsole.log(formatGBP(priceWithVat(5667)));","also":["function priceWithVat(pence) {\n  return Math.round(pence * 1.2);\n}\nconst formatGBP = pence => (pence / 100).toFixed(2);\nconsole.log(formatGBP(priceWithVat(5667)));"],"hints":[{"re":"function\\s+priceWithVat\\s*\\(\\s*pence\\s*\\)\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>function priceWithVat(pence) {</code>"},{"re":"return\\s+Math\\.round\\(\\s*pence\\s*\\*\\s*1\\.2\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>return Math.round(pence * 1.2);</code>"},{"re":"const\\s+formatGBP\\s*=\\s*\\(?\\s*pence\\s*\\)?\\s*=>","text":"Line 4: <code>const formatGBP = (pence) =&gt;</code> then the expression to return."},{"re":"=>\\s*\\(\\s*pence\\s*\\/\\s*100\\s*\\)\\.toFixed\\(\\s*2\\s*\\)","text":"After the arrow: <code>(pence / 100).toFixed(2);</code>"},{"re":"formatGBP\\(\\s*priceWithVat\\(\\s*5667\\s*\\)\\s*\\)","text":"Last line calls one function inside the other: <code>formatGBP(priceWithVat(5667))</code>"}],"explain":"<p>5667 x 1.2 = 6800.4, rounded to 6800 pence, formatted as <code>68.00</code>. Functions inside functions run from the inside out.</p>"}],"text":"Where JavaScript runs JavaScript runs in every browser and, with Node.js, on servers and your computer. Open any page, press F12 , click Console , and type 2 + 2 . You are programming. For this lesson, use the Playground (choose the JavaScript console basics template) or the browser console. Values and types 'Linen dress' // string: text, in quotes 68 // number: integers and decimals are both number true // boolean: true or false null // deliberately nothing undefined // not set yet Check a type with typeof : typeof 68 gives number . Variables A variable is a named box for a value. const brand = 'Amara Studio'; // const: cannot be reassigned let stock = 12; // let: can be reassigned stock = stock - 1; // now 11 Use const by default. Switch to let only when the value must change. Avoid var ; it is the old way and has confusing rules. Names are written in camelCase : cartTotal , isLoggedIn , maxSupply . Make them descriptive. Operators 10 + 5 // 15 10 - 5 // 5 10 * 5 // 50 10 / 4 // 2.5 10 % 3 // 1 (remainder) 2 ** 3 // 8 (power) 'Hello, ' + 'world' // joining strings `Total: £${(6800 / 100).toFixed(2)}` // template literal with backticks: Total: £68.00 Comparisons give booleans: 5 === 5 // true (equal value AND type) 5 === '5' // false 5 !== 3 // true 7 3 // true age = 18 hasId // AND: both must be true isMember || hasCode // OR: at least one true !isSoldOut // NOT: flips true/false Always use === not == == converts types before comparing, giving surprises like '0' == false being true . Use === and !== every time. Making decisions const stock = 3; if (stock === 0) { console.log('Sold out'); } else if (stock 5) { console.log(`Only ${stock} left`); } else { console.log('In stock'); } Functions A function is a reusable recipe. It takes parameters and returns a result. // function declaration function priceWithVat(pence) { return Math.round(pence * 1.2); } // arrow function: the same idea, shorter syntax const formatGBP = (pence) = `£${(pence / 100).toFixed(2)}`; const dress = 5667; console.log(formatGBP(priceWithVat(dress))); // £68.00 // a function with a default parameter function greet(name = 'there') { return `Hi ${name}, welcome back!`; } console.log(greet('Tolu')); console.log(greet()); Good functions do one thing and are named with a verb: calculateTotal , formatDate , isValidEmail . Money is stored in pence 0.1 + 0.2 in JavaScript is 0.30000000000000004 , because computers store decimals in binary. Store money as whole pence (or cents) and only divide by 100 when displaying it. Blockchains do the same thing with 18 decimal places, as you will see later.","track":"web"},{"id":"web-js-data","title":"JavaScript data: arrays, objects and loops","level":"beginner","minutes":25,"summary":"Store lists and records, loop over them, and transform data with map, filter and reduce.","html":"<h2 id=\"arrays-ordered-lists\">Arrays: ordered lists</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const sizes = ['XS', 'S', 'M', 'L'];\n\nsizes[0];          // 'XS' (counting starts at 0)\nsizes.length;      // 4\nsizes.push('XL');  // add to the end\nsizes.includes('M');   // true\nsizes.indexOf('L');    // 3</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"objects-named-properties\">Objects: named properties</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const product = {\n  id: 'dr-001',\n  name: 'Linen Wrap Dress',\n  pricePence: 6800,\n  sizes: ['S', 'M', 'L'],\n  inStock: true,\n};\n\nproduct.name;            // 'Linen Wrap Dress'\nproduct['pricePence'];   // 6800 (bracket form works with variables too)\nproduct.colour = 'sage'; // add a property</code></pre></div>\n<p>Real data is usually <strong>an array of objects</strong>. That is exactly what an API sends back:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const products = [\n  { name: 'Wrap dress', pricePence: 6800, category: 'dresses' },\n  { name: 'Camp shirt', pricePence: 4200, category: 'tops' },\n  { name: 'Bucket hat', pricePence: 2200, category: 'accessories' },\n  { name: 'Slip dress', pricePence: 5400, category: 'dresses' },\n];</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"loops\">Loops</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">for (const p of products) {\n  console.log(`${p.name}: £${p.pricePence / 100}`);\n}\n\n// classic counter loop, when you need the index\nfor (let i = 0; i &lt; products.length; i++) {\n  console.log(i + 1, products[i].name);\n}</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"map-filter-reduce-the-professional-way\">map, filter, reduce: the professional way</h2>\n<p>These three methods replace most loops. They do not change the original array; they return a new value.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-try=\"1\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const products = [\n  { name: 'Wrap dress', pricePence: 6800, category: 'dresses' },\n  { name: 'Camp shirt', pricePence: 4200, category: 'tops' },\n  { name: 'Bucket hat', pricePence: 2200, category: 'accessories' },\n  { name: 'Slip dress', pricePence: 5400, category: 'dresses' },\n];\n\n// map: transform every item -&gt; new array of the same length\nconst names = products.map((p) =&gt; p.name);\n\n// filter: keep only items that pass a test\nconst dresses = products.filter((p) =&gt; p.category === 'dresses');\n\n// reduce: boil the array down to one value\nconst totalPence = products.reduce((sum, p) =&gt; sum + p.pricePence, 0);\n\n// find: the first match (or undefined)\nconst hat = products.find((p) =&gt; p.name.includes('hat'));\n\n// some / every: true or false questions\nconst anyUnder25 = products.some((p) =&gt; p.pricePence &lt; 2500);\n\n// sort (copy first: sort changes the array in place)\nconst cheapestFirst = [...products].sort((a, b) =&gt; a.pricePence - b.pricePence);\n\nconsole.log(names);\nconsole.log(dresses);\nconsole.log('Total £' + totalPence / 100);\nconsole.log(hat, anyUnder25);\nconsole.table(cheapestFirst);</code></pre></div>\n<p>You can <strong>chain</strong> them: <code>products.filter(p =&gt; p.category === 'dresses').map(p =&gt; p.name)</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"2\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"destructuring-and-spread\">Destructuring and spread</h2>\n<p>Shortcuts you will see in every modern codebase:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const { name, pricePence } = product;        // pull properties into variables\nconst [first, second] = sizes;               // pull array items into variables\n\nconst updated = { ...product, pricePence: 6000 };  // copy an object, changing one field\nconst allSizes = [...sizes, 'XXL'];                 // copy an array, adding one item</code></pre></div>\n<p>Copying instead of changing (mutating) data avoids a whole family of bugs, and it is how React expects you to work.</p>\n<h2 id=\"json\">JSON</h2>\n<p>JSON is how data travels between servers and browsers. It looks like JavaScript objects, with stricter rules (double quotes, no trailing commas, no functions).</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const text = JSON.stringify(product);   // object -&gt; string (to send or store)\nconst back = JSON.parse(text);          // string -&gt; object (after receiving)</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"3\"></div>","quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>['a', 'b', 'c'][1]</code> return?","options":["'a'","'b'","'c'"],"answer":1,"explain":"Arrays are zero-indexed: position 0 is the first item."},{"q":"You want only the products under £30 from an array. Which method?","options":["map","filter","reduce"],"answer":1,"explain":"filter keeps items that pass a test; map transforms every item; reduce combines into one value."},{"q":"What does <code>products.reduce((sum, p) =&gt; sum + p.pricePence, 0)</code> produce?","options":["An array of prices","The total of all prices in pence","The most expensive product"],"answer":1,"explain":"reduce starts at 0 and adds each price to the running sum."},{"q":"What does <code>{ ...product, pricePence: 6000 }</code> do?","options":["Makes a new object with every property of product, but pricePence set to 6000","Changes product.pricePence to 6000","Deletes the other properties"],"answer":0,"explain":"The spread copies, then the later property overrides. The original is unchanged."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Work with an array","file":"sizes.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write four lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const sizes = ['XS', 'S', 'M', 'L'];</code></li><li>Add <code>'XL'</code> to the end with <code>push</code>.</li><li>Print how many sizes there are.</li><li>Print the first size.</li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const sizes = ['XS', 'S', 'M', 'L'];\nsizes.push('XL');\nconsole.log(sizes.length);\nconsole.log(sizes[0]);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+sizes\\s*=\\s*\\[","text":"Line 1: an array in square brackets: <code>const sizes = ['XS', 'S', 'M', 'L'];</code>"},{"re":"sizes\\.push\\(\\s*['\"]XL['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>sizes.push('XL');</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*sizes\\.length\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3: <code>console.log(sizes.length);</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*sizes\\[\\s*0\\s*\\]\\s*\\)","text":"Line 4: the first item is at position 0: <code>console.log(sizes[0]);</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Output: <code>5</code> then <code>XS</code>. A <code>const</code> array can still have items added: <code>const</code> only stops you replacing the whole array with a new one.</p>"},{"title":"Work with an object","file":"product.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write three lines:</p>\n<ol><li>A constant <code>product</code> that is an object with <code>name: 'Wrap dress'</code> and <code>pricePence: 6800</code>.</li><li>Print <code>product.name</code>.</li><li>Print the price in pounds: <code>product.pricePence / 100</code>.</li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const product = { name: 'Wrap dress', pricePence: 6800 };\nconsole.log(product.name);\nconsole.log(product.pricePence / 100);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+product\\s*=\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1 starts <code>const product = {</code> (curly braces for objects)."},{"re":"name\\s*:\\s*['\"]Wrap dress['\"]\\s*,","text":"<code>name: 'Wrap dress',</code> then a comma before the next property."},{"re":"pricePence\\s*:\\s*6800\\s*\\}","text":"<code>pricePence: 6800 }</code> then close the object."},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*product\\.name\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>console.log(product.name);</code>"},{"re":"product\\.pricePence\\s*\\/\\s*100","text":"Line 3: <code>console.log(product.pricePence / 100);</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"filter and reduce","file":"prices.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write four lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const prices = [6800, 4200, 2200];</code></li><li>A constant <code>cheap</code> holding only the prices under 5000, using <code>filter</code> with an arrow function <code>(p) =&gt; p &lt; 5000</code>.</li><li>A constant <code>total</code> that adds them all up with <code>reduce((sum, p) =&gt; sum + p, 0)</code>.</li><li><code>console.log(cheap, total);</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const prices = [6800, 4200, 2200];\nconst cheap = prices.filter((p) => p < 5000);\nconst total = prices.reduce((sum, p) => sum + p, 0);\nconsole.log(cheap, total);","also":["const prices = [6800, 4200, 2200];\nconst cheap = prices.filter(p => p < 5000);\nconst total = prices.reduce((sum, p) => sum + p, 0);\nconsole.log(cheap, total);"],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+prices\\s*=\\s*\\[\\s*6800\\s*,\\s*4200\\s*,\\s*2200\\s*\\]","text":"Line 1: <code>const prices = [6800, 4200, 2200];</code>"},{"re":"prices\\.filter\\(\\s*\\(?\\s*p\\s*\\)?\\s*=>\\s*p\\s*<\\s*5000\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>const cheap = prices.filter((p) =&gt; p &lt; 5000);</code>"},{"re":"prices\\.reduce\\(\\s*\\(\\s*sum\\s*,\\s*p\\s*\\)\\s*=>\\s*sum\\s*\\+\\s*p\\s*,\\s*0\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3: <code>const total = prices.reduce((sum, p) =&gt; sum + p, 0);</code> The <code>0</code> is the starting value."},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*cheap\\s*,\\s*total\\s*\\)","text":"Line 4: <code>console.log(cheap, total);</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Output: <code>[4200, 2200]</code> and <code>13200</code>. <code>filter</code> keeps items that pass the test; <code>reduce</code> carries a running <code>sum</code> through the array, starting from <code>0</code>.</p>"},{"title":"Copy an object with spread","file":"sale.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write three lines:</p>\n<ol><li>The same <code>product</code> object: <code>{ name: 'Wrap dress', pricePence: 6800 }</code>.</li><li>A constant <code>sale</code> that copies <code>product</code> with spread (<code>...product</code>) but sets <code>pricePence</code> to <code>5400</code>.</li><li><code>console.log(sale);</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const product = { name: 'Wrap dress', pricePence: 6800 };\nconst sale = { ...product, pricePence: 5400 };\nconsole.log(sale);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+product\\s*=\\s*\\{\\s*name\\s*:\\s*['\"]Wrap dress['\"]\\s*,\\s*pricePence\\s*:\\s*6800\\s*\\}","text":"Line 1: <code>const product = { name: 'Wrap dress', pricePence: 6800 };</code>"},{"re":"\\{\\s*\\.\\.\\.product\\s*,","text":"Line 2 starts the new object with three dots: <code>{ ...product,</code>"},{"re":"pricePence\\s*:\\s*5400\\s*\\}","text":"Then override the price: <code>pricePence: 5400 }</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*sale\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3: <code>console.log(sale);</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Output: <code>{ name: &quot;Wrap dress&quot;, pricePence: 5400 }</code>. The original <code>product</code> still costs 6800. Copying instead of changing data is how React expects you to work.</p>"}],"text":"Arrays: ordered lists const sizes = ['XS', 'S', 'M', 'L']; sizes[0]; // 'XS' (counting starts at 0) sizes.length; // 4 sizes.push('XL'); // add to the end sizes.includes('M'); // true sizes.indexOf('L'); // 3 Objects: named properties const product = { id: 'dr-001', name: 'Linen Wrap Dress', pricePence: 6800, sizes: ['S', 'M', 'L'], inStock: true, }; product.name; // 'Linen Wrap Dress' product['pricePence']; // 6800 (bracket form works with variables too) product.colour = 'sage'; // add a property Real data is usually an array of objects . That is exactly what an API sends back: const products = [ { name: 'Wrap dress', pricePence: 6800, category: 'dresses' }, { name: 'Camp shirt', pricePence: 4200, category: 'tops' }, { name: 'Bucket hat', pricePence: 2200, category: 'accessories' }, { name: 'Slip dress', pricePence: 5400, category: 'dresses' }, ]; Loops for (const p of products) { console.log(`${p.name}: £${p.pricePence / 100}`); } // classic counter loop, when you need the index for (let i = 0; i products.length; i++) { console.log(i + 1, products[i].name); } map, filter, reduce: the professional way These three methods replace most loops. They do not change the original array; they return a new value. const products = [ { name: 'Wrap dress', pricePence: 6800, category: 'dresses' }, { name: 'Camp shirt', pricePence: 4200, category: 'tops' }, { name: 'Bucket hat', pricePence: 2200, category: 'accessories' }, { name: 'Slip dress', pricePence: 5400, category: 'dresses' }, ]; // map: transform every item - new array of the same length const names = products.map((p) = p.name); // filter: keep only items that pass a test const dresses = products.filter((p) = p.category === 'dresses'); // reduce: boil the array down to one value const totalPence = products.reduce((sum, p) = sum + p.pricePence, 0); // find: the first match (or undefined) const hat = products.find((p) = p.name.includes('hat')); // some / every: true or false questions const anyUnder25 = products.some((p) = p.pricePence 2500); // sort (copy first: sort changes the array in place) const cheapestFirst = [...products].sort((a, b) = a.pricePence - b.pricePence); console.log(names); console.log(dresses); console.log('Total £' + totalPence / 100); console.log(hat, anyUnder25); console.table(cheapestFirst); You can chain them: products.filter(p = p.category === 'dresses').map(p = p.name) . Destructuring and spread Shortcuts you will see in every modern codebase: const { name, pricePence } = product; // pull properties into variables const [first, second] = sizes; // pull array items into variables const updated = { ...product, pricePence: 6000 }; // copy an object, changing one field const allSizes = [...sizes, 'XXL']; // copy an array, adding one item Copying instead of changing (mutating) data avoids a whole family of bugs, and it is how React expects you to work. JSON JSON is how data travels between servers and browsers. It looks like JavaScript objects, with stricter rules (double quotes, no trailing commas, no functions). const text = JSON.stringify(product); // object - string (to send or store) const back = JSON.parse(text); // string - object (after receiving)","track":"web"},{"id":"web-dom","title":"The DOM and events","level":"beginner","minutes":30,"summary":"Find elements, change the page, respond to clicks and typing, and build an interactive to-do list.","html":"<h2 id=\"what-the-dom-is\">What the DOM is</h2>\n<p>When a browser loads HTML, it builds a live tree of objects called the <strong>Document Object Model</strong>. JavaScript can read and change that tree, and the page updates instantly.</p>\n<h2 id=\"finding-elements\">Finding elements</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const title = document.querySelector('h1');            // first match, CSS selector syntax\nconst button = document.querySelector('#add-to-bag');  // by id\nconst cards = document.querySelectorAll('.card');      // all matches (a NodeList)</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"changing-the-page\">Changing the page</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">title.textContent = 'Summer drop is live';   // change text (safe)\nbutton.classList.add('is-loading');          // add a CSS class\nbutton.classList.toggle('active');           // add if missing, remove if present\nbutton.disabled = true;                      // set a property\ntitle.style.color = '#b4532a';               // inline style (prefer classes)\n\nconst li = document.createElement('li');     // build a new element\nli.textContent = 'New item';\ndocument.querySelector('ul').append(li);     // put it on the page</code></pre></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">textContent versus innerHTML</p><p><code>textContent</code> treats everything as plain text. <code>innerHTML</code> parses HTML. <strong>Never</strong> put user input into <code>innerHTML</code>: someone could type a <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> or an <code>&lt;img onerror=...&gt;</code> and run code on your page. This attack is called XSS (cross-site scripting).</p></aside>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"events\">Events</h2>\n<p>An <strong>event</strong> is something that happens: a click, a key press, a form submit, the page finishing loading. You attach a <strong>listener</strong> that runs when it happens:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">button.addEventListener('click', () =&gt; {\n  console.log('Added to bag');\n});\n\nconst search = document.querySelector('#search');\nsearch.addEventListener('input', (event) =&gt; {\n  console.log('Typed:', event.target.value);\n});\n\nconst form = document.querySelector('form');\nform.addEventListener('submit', (event) =&gt; {\n  event.preventDefault();   // stop the browser reloading the page\n  // handle the form with JavaScript instead\n});</code></pre></div>\n<p>Common events: <code>click</code>, <code>input</code>, <code>change</code>, <code>submit</code>, <code>keydown</code>, <code>DOMContentLoaded</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"the-state-render-action-pattern\">The state, render, action pattern</h2>\n<p>The cleanest way to build interactive UI (and exactly how React thinks):</p>\n<ol><li><strong>State</strong>: one place that holds your data (<code>let todos = [...]</code>).</li><li><strong>Render</strong>: a function that rebuilds the UI from the state.</li><li><strong>Actions</strong>: event handlers change the state, then call render.</li></ol>\n<p>Never store the &quot;truth&quot; in the HTML itself. The HTML is just a picture of your state.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-try=\"1\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">let count = 0;                       // 1. state\n\nconst label = document.createElement('p');\nconst plus = document.createElement('button');\nconst reset = document.createElement('button');\nplus.textContent = '+1';\nreset.textContent = 'Reset';\ndocument.body.append(label, plus, reset);\n\nfunction render() {                  // 2. render\n  label.textContent = `Count: ${count}`;\n  reset.disabled = count === 0;\n}\n\nplus.addEventListener('click', () =&gt; { count++; render(); });   // 3. actions\nreset.addEventListener('click', () =&gt; { count = 0; render(); });\n\nrender();</code></pre></div>\n<p>Now open the <a href=\"#playground\">Playground</a> and load the <strong>To-do list (DOM)</strong> template. It uses the same pattern with an array of objects. Read it line by line, then add a &quot;Clear completed&quot; button.</p>\n<h2 id=\"saving-data-in-the-browser\">Saving data in the browser</h2>\n<p><code>localStorage</code> keeps small amounts of text after the page closes:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">localStorage.setItem('todos', JSON.stringify(todos));             // save\nconst saved = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('todos') || '[]');  // load</code></pre></div>\n<p>It only stores strings (hence <code>JSON.stringify</code>), it is per-browser, and users can clear it. Fine for preferences and drafts; not a database. (This very site saves your progress this way.)</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"2\"></div>","quiz":[{"q":"Which method finds the first element matching a CSS selector?","options":["document.querySelector","document.getAll","document.find"],"answer":0,"explain":"querySelector takes any CSS selector and returns the first match."},{"q":"A user types their name into a field and you show it in a greeting. Which is safe?","options":["greeting.textContent = name","greeting.innerHTML = name","Both are equally safe"],"answer":0,"explain":"innerHTML would run any HTML or script the user typed. That is XSS."},{"q":"Why call event.preventDefault() in a form's submit handler?","options":["To clear the form","To stop the browser's default full page reload so JavaScript can handle it","To validate the inputs"],"answer":1,"explain":"Without it, submitting navigates or reloads the page."},{"q":"In the state, render, action pattern, what does an event handler do?","options":["Edits the HTML directly","Updates the state, then calls render","Reloads the page"],"answer":1,"explain":"Handlers change the data; render keeps the page in sync with it."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Change text on the page","file":"script.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>The page below has <code>&lt;h1 id=&quot;title&quot;&gt;Spring collection&lt;/h1&gt;</code>. Write two lines:</p>\n<ol><li>A constant <code>title</code> that finds it with <code>document.querySelector('#title')</code>.</li><li>Change its text to <code>'Summer drop is live'</code> using <code>textContent</code>.</li></ol>\n<p>Press <strong>Run it</strong> to see the heading change.</p>","starter":"","html":"<h1 id=\"title\">Spring collection</h1>\n<button id=\"buy\">Add to bag</button>","solution":"const title = document.querySelector('#title');\ntitle.textContent = 'Summer drop is live';","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+title\\s*=\\s*document\\.querySelector\\(","text":"Line 1: <code>const title = document.querySelector(</code>"},{"re":"querySelector\\(\\s*['\"]#title['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"The selector is <code>'#title'</code>: a hash because it is an id."},{"re":"title\\.textContent\\s*=\\s*['\"]Summer drop is live['\"]","text":"Line 2: <code>title.textContent = 'Summer drop is live';</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"React to a click","file":"script.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Make the button say <code>Added</code> when clicked:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const button = document.querySelector('#buy');</code></li><li><code>button.addEventListener('click', () =&gt; {</code></li><li>Inside, indented: <code>button.textContent = 'Added';</code></li><li>Close it: <code>});</code></li></ol>\n<p>Run it, then click the button in the preview.</p>","starter":"","html":"<h1 id=\"title\">Linen Wrap Dress</h1>\n<button id=\"buy\">Add to bag</button>","solution":"const button = document.querySelector('#buy');\nbutton.addEventListener('click', () => {\n  button.textContent = 'Added';\n});","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+button\\s*=\\s*document\\.querySelector\\(\\s*['\"]#buy['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"Line 1: <code>const button = document.querySelector('#buy');</code>"},{"re":"button\\.addEventListener\\(\\s*['\"]click['\"]\\s*,","text":"Line 2 starts <code>button.addEventListener('click',</code> (capital L in <code>Listener</code>)."},{"re":",\\s*\\(\\s*\\)\\s*=>\\s*\\{","text":"Then an arrow function with no parameters: <code>() =&gt; {</code>"},{"re":"button\\.textContent\\s*=\\s*['\"]Added['\"]","text":"Inside: <code>button.textContent = 'Added';</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*\\}\\s*\\)\\s*;?\\s*$","text":"Close the function and the call: <code>});</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"Turn data into text and back","file":"storage.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p><code>localStorage</code> only stores text, so you convert with JSON. Write four lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const todos = ['Buy fabric', 'Post photos'];</code></li><li><code>const text = JSON.stringify(todos);</code></li><li><code>const back = JSON.parse(text);</code></li><li><code>console.log(text, back.length);</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const todos = ['Buy fabric', 'Post photos'];\nconst text = JSON.stringify(todos);\nconst back = JSON.parse(text);\nconsole.log(text, back.length);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+todos\\s*=\\s*\\[","text":"Line 1 is an array of two strings."},{"re":"JSON\\.stringify\\(\\s*todos\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>const text = JSON.stringify(todos);</code> (JSON in capitals)."},{"re":"JSON\\.parse\\(\\s*text\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3: <code>const back = JSON.parse(text);</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*text\\s*,\\s*back\\.length\\s*\\)","text":"Line 4: <code>console.log(text, back.length);</code>"}],"explain":"<p><code>stringify</code> turns the array into the text <code>[&quot;Buy fabric&quot;,&quot;Post photos&quot;]</code>, ready to save. <code>parse</code> turns it back into a real array with a <code>length</code> of 2.</p>"}],"text":"What the DOM is When a browser loads HTML, it builds a live tree of objects called the Document Object Model . JavaScript can read and change that tree, and the page updates instantly. Finding elements const title = document.querySelector('h1'); // first match, CSS selector syntax const button = document.querySelector('#add-to-bag'); // by id const cards = document.querySelectorAll('.card'); // all matches (a NodeList) Changing the page title.textContent = 'Summer drop is live'; // change text (safe) button.classList.add('is-loading'); // add a CSS class button.classList.toggle('active'); // add if missing, remove if present button.disabled = true; // set a property title.style.color = '#b4532a'; // inline style (prefer classes) const li = document.createElement('li'); // build a new element li.textContent = 'New item'; document.querySelector('ul').append(li); // put it on the page textContent versus innerHTML textContent treats everything as plain text. innerHTML parses HTML. Never put user input into innerHTML : someone could type a script or an img onerror=... and run code on your page. This attack is called XSS (cross-site scripting). Events An event is something that happens: a click, a key press, a form submit, the page finishing loading. You attach a listener that runs when it happens: button.addEventListener('click', () = { console.log('Added to bag'); }); const search = document.querySelector('#search'); search.addEventListener('input', (event) = { console.log('Typed:', event.target.value); }); const form = document.querySelector('form'); form.addEventListener('submit', (event) = { event.preventDefault(); // stop the browser reloading the page // handle the form with JavaScript instead }); Common events: click , input , change , submit , keydown , DOMContentLoaded . The state, render, action pattern The cleanest way to build interactive UI (and exactly how React thinks): State : one place that holds your data ( let todos = [...] ). Render : a function that rebuilds the UI from the state. Actions : event handlers change the state, then call render. Never store the truth in the HTML itself. The HTML is just a picture of your state. let count = 0; // 1. state const label = document.createElement('p'); const plus = document.createElement('button'); const reset = document.createElement('button'); plus.textContent = '+1'; reset.textContent = 'Reset'; document.body.append(label, plus, reset); function render() { // 2. render label.textContent = `Count: ${count}`; reset.disabled = count === 0; } plus.addEventListener('click', () = { count++; render(); }); // 3. actions reset.addEventListener('click', () = { count = 0; render(); }); render(); Now open the Playground and load the To-do list (DOM) template. It uses the same pattern with an array of objects. Read it line by line, then add a Clear completed button. Saving data in the browser localStorage keeps small amounts of text after the page closes: localStorage.setItem('todos', JSON.stringify(todos)); // save const saved = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('todos') || '[]'); // load It only stores strings (hence JSON.stringify ), it is per-browser, and users can clear it. Fine for preferences and drafts; not a database. (This very site saves your progress this way.)","track":"web"},{"id":"web-modern-js","title":"Modern JavaScript: modules, async and fetch","level":"intermediate","minutes":30,"summary":"Split code into modules, understand promises and async/await, and load real data from APIs.","html":"<h2 id=\"modules-one-job-per-file\">Modules: one job per file</h2>\n<p>Real projects split code into files. Each file is a <strong>module</strong> that <code>export</code>s what others may use and <code>import</code>s what it needs.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">// money.js\nexport function formatGBP(pence) {\n  return new Intl.NumberFormat('en-GB', { style: 'currency', currency: 'GBP' }).format(pence / 100);\n}\nexport const VAT_RATE = 0.2;</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">// main.js\nimport { formatGBP, VAT_RATE } from './money.js';\nconsole.log(formatGBP(6800 * (1 + VAT_RATE)));   // £81.60</code></pre></div>\n<p>In the browser, load the entry file with <code>&lt;script type=&quot;module&quot; src=&quot;main.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</code>. Modules need to be served over http (Live Server, Vite, or <code>npx serve</code>), not opened as a file.</p>\n<p>A file can also have one <strong>default</strong> export: <code>export default function App() {}</code>, imported without braces: <code>import App from './App.js'</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"why-asynchronous-code-exists\">Why asynchronous code exists</h2>\n<p>Some things take time: network requests, reading files, waiting for a user. JavaScript does not freeze while waiting. Instead, slow operations return a <strong>Promise</strong>: an object that will eventually hold a result (or an error).</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const promise = fetch('https://api.example.com/products');   // returns immediately</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"async-await\">async / await</h2>\n<p><code>await</code> pauses <strong>this function</strong> (not the whole page) until the promise settles. It only works inside an <code>async</code> function (or at the top level of a module).</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-try=\"1\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">async function loadWeather() {\n  const url = 'https://api.open-meteo.com/v1/forecast?latitude=51.51&amp;longitude=-0.13&amp;current=temperature_2m';\n  try {\n    const response = await fetch(url);                 // wait for the response headers\n    if (!response.ok) {                                // fetch does NOT throw on 404/500\n      throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`);\n    }\n    const data = await response.json();                // wait for and parse the body\n    console.log(`London is ${data.current.temperature_2m}°C`);\n  } catch (error) {\n    console.error('Could not load the weather:', error.message);\n  } finally {\n    console.log('Request finished (success or failure)');\n  }\n}\n\nloadWeather();</code></pre></div>\n<p>Three things beginners miss:</p>\n<ol><li><code>fetch</code> only rejects on network failure. A 404 or 500 still &quot;succeeds&quot;, so check <code>response.ok</code>.</li><li><code>response.json()</code> is also async, so it needs <code>await</code>.</li><li>Always handle errors. Networks fail all the time, especially on phones.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"running-requests-in-parallel\">Running requests in parallel</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const [products, reviews] = await Promise.all([\n  fetch('/api/products').then((r) =&gt; r.json()),\n  fetch('/api/reviews').then((r) =&gt; r.json()),\n]);</code></pre></div>\n<p>Both requests run at the same time, so this is roughly twice as fast as awaiting them one after another.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sending-data\">Sending data</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">await fetch('/api/newsletter', {\n  method: 'POST',\n  headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },\n  body: JSON.stringify({ email: 'ada@example.com' }),\n});</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"loading-error-and-empty-states\">Loading, error and empty states</h2>\n<p>Every piece of UI that loads data has at least four states. Professionals design all of them:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>State</th><th>Show</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Loading</td><td>A skeleton or spinner</td></tr><tr><td>Error</td><td>A clear message and a retry button</td></tr><tr><td>Empty</td><td>A helpful message (&quot;No orders yet&quot;)</td></tr><tr><td>Success</td><td>The data</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"other-modern-essentials\">Other modern essentials</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const city = user?.address?.city;          // optional chaining: undefined instead of crashing\nconst qty = input.value || 1;              // fallback for any falsy value ('' or 0)\nconst discount = order.discount ?? 0;      // fallback only for null/undefined (keeps 0)\nconst label = isSale ? 'Sale' : 'New';     // ternary: a one-line if/else</code></pre></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">CORS errors</p><p>&quot;Blocked by CORS policy&quot; means the server you called does not allow browsers on other websites to read its responses. It is a server setting, not a bug in your fetch code. Solutions: use an API that allows it, or call it from your own back end.</p></aside>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"2\"></div>","quiz":[{"q":"A fetch to a missing URL returns status 404. What does the fetch promise do?","options":["Rejects, so the catch block runs","Resolves normally; you must check response.ok yourself","Never settles"],"answer":1,"explain":"fetch only rejects for network failures. Always check response.ok or response.status."},{"q":"Why use Promise.all for two independent requests?","options":["They run at the same time, so the total wait is shorter","It retries failed requests","It is required for POST requests"],"answer":0,"explain":"Awaiting one after another doubles the waiting time."},{"q":"What is the difference between <code>||</code> and <code>??</code> for defaults?","options":["There is none","?? only falls back for null or undefined, so it keeps valid values like 0 and ''","?? is slower"],"answer":1,"explain":"discount ?? 0 keeps a real discount of 0; discount || 10 would replace 0 with 10."},{"q":"How do you import a named export called formatGBP from money.js?","options":["import formatGBP from './money.js'","import { formatGBP } from './money.js'","require('formatGBP')"],"answer":1,"explain":"Named exports use braces. Default exports do not."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Export a function from a module","file":"money.js","lang":"js","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write a module that exports a function <code>formatGBP(pence)</code> which returns a template literal: a pound sign followed by <code>${(pence / 100).toFixed(2)}</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"export function formatGBP(pence) {\n  return `£${(pence / 100).toFixed(2)}`;\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*export\\s+function\\s+formatGBP\\s*\\(\\s*pence\\s*\\)\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>export function formatGBP(pence) {</code>"},{"re":"return\\s+`£\\$\\{","text":"Line 2 returns a template literal: <code>return</code> then a backtick, a pound sign, a dollar sign and an opening curly brace."},{"re":"\\(\\s*pence\\s*\\/\\s*100\\s*\\)\\.toFixed\\(\\s*2\\s*\\)\\s*\\}`","text":"Inside the braces: <code>(pence / 100).toFixed(2)</code>, then a closing brace and backtick."}],"explain":"<p>Another file can now write <code>import { formatGBP } from './money.js';</code>. One job per file keeps big projects understandable.</p>"},{"title":"Fetch real data, handling errors","file":"weather.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Fetch London's current temperature from the free Open-Meteo API. Four lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const res = await fetch('https://api.open-meteo.com/v1/forecast?latitude=51.51&amp;longitude=-0.13&amp;current=temperature_2m');</code></li><li>If the response is not OK, throw an error: <code>if (!res.ok) throw new Error(...);</code> where the message is a template literal: a backtick, the text <code>HTTP </code> and <code>${res.status}</code>, then a closing backtick.</li><li><code>const data = await res.json();</code></li><li><code>console.log(data.current.temperature_2m);</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const res = await fetch('https://api.open-meteo.com/v1/forecast?latitude=51.51&longitude=-0.13&current=temperature_2m');\nif (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);\nconst data = await res.json();\nconsole.log(data.current.temperature_2m);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+res\\s*=\\s*await\\s+fetch\\(","text":"Line 1 starts <code>const res = await fetch(</code> with the URL in quotes."},{"re":"api\\.open-meteo\\.com/v1/forecast\\?latitude=51\\.51&longitude=-0\\.13&current=temperature_2m","text":"Check the URL carefully: <code>latitude=51.51&amp;longitude=-0.13&amp;current=temperature_2m</code>"},{"re":"if\\s*\\(\\s*!\\s*res\\.ok\\s*\\)\\s*throw\\s+new\\s+Error\\(","text":"Line 2: <code>if (!res.ok) throw new Error(...)</code>. The <code>!</code> means &quot;not&quot;."},{"re":"`HTTP \\$\\{res\\.status\\}`","text":"The error message is a template literal: backtick, <code>HTTP </code>, then the status in <code>${ }</code>, then a backtick."},{"re":"const\\s+data\\s*=\\s*await\\s+res\\.json\\(\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3: <code>const data = await res.json();</code>"},{"re":"data\\.current\\.temperature_2m","text":"Line 4: <code>console.log(data.current.temperature_2m);</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"Optional chaining and a default","file":"city.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>This user has no address. Read the city safely, with <code>'Unknown'</code> as the fallback. Three lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const user = { name: 'Ada' };</code></li><li><code>const city = user.address?.city ?? 'Unknown';</code></li><li><code>console.log(city);</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const user = { name: 'Ada' };\nconst city = user.address?.city ?? 'Unknown';\nconsole.log(city);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+user\\s*=\\s*\\{\\s*name\\s*:\\s*['\"]Ada['\"]\\s*\\}","text":"Line 1: <code>const user = { name: 'Ada' };</code>"},{"re":"user\\.address\\?\\.city","text":"Use <code>?.</code> so a missing address does not crash: <code>user.address?.city</code>"},{"re":"\\?\\?\\s*['\"]Unknown['\"]","text":"Then the fallback with two question marks: <code>?? 'Unknown'</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Without <code>?.</code>, reading <code>.city</code> of <code>undefined</code> throws a TypeError and stops the page. With it, you get <code>undefined</code>, and <code>??</code> swaps in <code>'Unknown'</code>.</p>"}],"text":"Modules: one job per file Real projects split code into files. Each file is a module that export s what others may use and import s what it needs. // money.js export function formatGBP(pence) { return new Intl.NumberFormat('en-GB', { style: 'currency', currency: 'GBP' }).format(pence / 100); } export const VAT_RATE = 0.2; // main.js import { formatGBP, VAT_RATE } from './money.js'; console.log(formatGBP(6800 * (1 + VAT_RATE))); // £81.60 In the browser, load the entry file with script type= module src= main.js /script . Modules need to be served over http (Live Server, Vite, or npx serve ), not opened as a file. A file can also have one default export: export default function App() {} , imported without braces: import App from './App.js' . Why asynchronous code exists Some things take time: network requests, reading files, waiting for a user. JavaScript does not freeze while waiting. Instead, slow operations return a Promise : an object that will eventually hold a result (or an error). const promise = fetch('https://api.example.com/products'); // returns immediately async / await await pauses this function (not the whole page) until the promise settles. It only works inside an async function (or at the top level of a module). async function loadWeather() { const url = 'https://api.open-meteo.com/v1/forecast?latitude=51.51 longitude=-0.13 current=temperature_2m'; try { const response = await fetch(url); // wait for the response headers if (!response.ok) { // fetch does NOT throw on 404/500 throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`); } const data = await response.json(); // wait for and parse the body console.log(`London is ${data.current.temperature_2m}°C`); } catch (error) { console.error('Could not load the weather:', error.message); } finally { console.log('Request finished (success or failure)'); } } loadWeather(); Three things beginners miss: fetch only rejects on network failure. A 404 or 500 still succeeds , so check response.ok . response.json() is also async, so it needs await . Always handle errors. Networks fail all the time, especially on phones. Running requests in parallel const [products, reviews] = await Promise.all([ fetch('/api/products').then((r) = r.json()), fetch('/api/reviews').then((r) = r.json()), ]); Both requests run at the same time, so this is roughly twice as fast as awaiting them one after another. Sending data await fetch('/api/newsletter', { method: 'POST', headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' }, body: JSON.stringify({ email: 'ada@example.com' }), }); Loading, error and empty states Every piece of UI that loads data has at least four states. Professionals design all of them: State Show Loading A skeleton or spinner Error A clear message and a retry button Empty A helpful message ( No orders yet ) Success The data Other modern essentials const city = user?.address?.city; // optional chaining: undefined instead of crashing const qty = input.value || 1; // fallback for any falsy value ('' or 0) const discount = order.discount ?? 0; // fallback only for null/undefined (keeps 0) const label = isSale ? 'Sale' : 'New'; // ternary: a one-line if/else CORS errors Blocked by CORS policy means the server you called does not allow browsers on other websites to read its responses. It is a server setting, not a bug in your fetch code. Solutions: use an API that allows it, or call it from your own back end.","track":"web"},{"id":"web-typescript","title":"TypeScript essentials","level":"intermediate","minutes":30,"summary":"Add types to JavaScript so your editor catches bugs before your users do.","html":"<h2 id=\"why-typescript\">Why TypeScript</h2>\n<p>TypeScript is JavaScript with <strong>type annotations</strong>. It compiles to plain JavaScript, so it runs anywhere JS runs. The point is the editor: it knows the shape of every value, autocompletes properties, and underlines mistakes as you type. Most professional teams use it, and every serious web3 library (viem, wagmi) is built for it.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\"><pre><code class=\"language-ts\">function formatGBP(pence: number): string {\n  return `£${(pence / 100).toFixed(2)}`;\n}\n\nformatGBP('6800');   // error: Argument of type 'string' is not assignable to parameter of type 'number'</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"basic-types\">Basic types</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\"><pre><code class=\"language-ts\">let name: string = 'Amara';\nlet stock: number = 12;\nlet isLive: boolean = true;\nlet sizes: string[] = ['S', 'M'];\nlet maybe: string | null = null;         // union: one OR the other\nlet status: 'draft' | 'live' | 'archived' = 'draft';   // literal union: only these exact strings</code></pre></div>\n<p>You rarely need annotations on variables. TypeScript <strong>infers</strong> them: <code>let stock = 12</code> is already a <code>number</code>. Annotate function parameters, return types of exported functions, and data shapes.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"describing-objects\">Describing objects</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\"><pre><code class=\"language-ts\">type Product = {\n  id: string;\n  name: string;\n  pricePence: number;\n  sizes: string[];\n  salePricePence?: number;      // ? = optional\n};\n\nconst dress: Product = {\n  id: 'dr-001',\n  name: 'Linen Wrap Dress',\n  pricePence: 6800,\n  sizes: ['S', 'M', 'L'],\n};\n\nfunction displayPrice(p: Product): number {\n  return p.salePricePence ?? p.pricePence;\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>interface Product { ... }</code> does almost the same as <code>type Product = { ... }</code>. Pick one style per project; <code>type</code> is more flexible.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"narrowing\">Narrowing</h2>\n<p>When a value could be several types, check it and TypeScript narrows it for you:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\"><pre><code class=\"language-ts\">function label(stock: number | 'unknown') {\n  if (stock === 'unknown') return 'Check back soon';\n  return stock &gt; 0 ? `${stock} left` : 'Sold out';   // here stock is definitely a number\n}</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"generics-in-one-minute\">Generics in one minute</h2>\n<p>Generics let a type take a type, like a function takes an argument:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\"><pre><code class=\"language-ts\">type ApiResponse&lt;T&gt; = { data: T; error: string | null };\n\nconst res: ApiResponse&lt;Product[]&gt; = { data: [], error: null };</code></pre></div>\n<p>You will mostly use generics written by libraries (<code>useState&lt;Product[]&gt;([])</code>, <code>Promise&lt;User&gt;</code>), not write your own at first.</p>\n<h2 id=\"set-up-a-typescript-project\">Set up a TypeScript project</h2>\n<p>The easiest way is a Vite template, which is pre-configured:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">npm create vite@latest shop -- --template react-ts\ncd shop\nnpm install\nnpm run dev</code></pre></div>\n<p>Check types for the whole project without building:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">npx tsc --noEmit</code></pre></div>\n<p>Keep <code>&quot;strict&quot;: true</code> in <code>tsconfig.json</code>. Strict mode catches the most bugs; turning it off defeats the purpose.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Types vanish at runtime</p><p>TypeScript checks your code, not your data. JSON from an API or a form can be anything, whatever type you claim. Validate untrusted data at the boundary with a library like <strong>Zod</strong>, which gives you both a runtime check and a TypeScript type from one schema.</p></aside>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\"><pre><code class=\"language-ts\">import { z } from 'zod';\n\nconst ProductSchema = z.object({\n  id: z.string(),\n  name: z.string().min(1),\n  pricePence: z.number().int().nonnegative(),\n});\ntype Product = z.infer&lt;typeof ProductSchema&gt;;\n\nconst product = ProductSchema.parse(await res.json());   // throws if the shape is wrong</code></pre></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Avoid any</p><p><code>any</code> switches type checking off for that value, and it spreads. If you truly do not know a type yet, use <code>unknown</code>, which forces you to check before using it.</p></aside>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"2\"></div>","quiz":[{"q":"What does TypeScript do with your type annotations when the code runs in a browser?","options":["Checks every value at runtime","Removes them: types exist only while you develop and compile","Sends them to the server"],"answer":1,"explain":"Types are erased at compile time. That is why you validate external data with something like Zod."},{"q":"What does <code>salePricePence?: number</code> mean in a type?","options":["It must be a number greater than zero","The property is optional: it may be missing, but if present it is a number","It can be any type"],"answer":1,"explain":"The question mark marks an optional property."},{"q":"Which type allows only the exact strings 'draft', 'live' or 'archived'?","options":["string","'draft' | 'live' | 'archived'","any"],"answer":1,"explain":"A union of string literals restricts values to that set, and the editor autocompletes them."},{"q":"You do not know the shape of some data yet. What is safer than any?","options":["unknown","object","never"],"answer":0,"explain":"unknown forces you to check the value before using it; any silently disables checking."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Add types to a function","file":"money.ts","lang":"ts","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write a TypeScript function <code>formatGBP</code> that takes <code>pence</code> of type <code>number</code> and returns a <code>string</code>: a template literal with a pound sign and <code>${(pence / 100).toFixed(2)}</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"function formatGBP(pence: number): string {\n  return `£${(pence / 100).toFixed(2)}`;\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"function\\s+formatGBP\\s*\\(\\s*pence\\s*:\\s*number\\s*\\)","text":"Parameter types go after a colon: <code>function formatGBP(pence: number)</code>"},{"re":"\\)\\s*:\\s*string\\s*\\{","text":"The return type goes after the closing bracket: <code>): string {</code>"},{"re":"return\\s+`£\\$\\{\\s*\\(\\s*pence\\s*\\/\\s*100\\s*\\)\\.toFixed\\(\\s*2\\s*\\)\\s*\\}`","text":"Line 2: <code>return</code> then a template literal: backtick, <code>£${(pence / 100).toFixed(2)}</code>, backtick."}],"explain":""},{"title":"Describe a product type","file":"product.ts","lang":"ts","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write a <code>type</code> called <code>Product</code> with four properties: <code>id</code> (string), <code>name</code> (string), <code>pricePence</code> (number), and an <strong>optional</strong> <code>salePricePence</code> (number).</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"type Product = {\n  id: string;\n  name: string;\n  pricePence: number;\n  salePricePence?: number;\n};","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*type\\s+Product\\s*=\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>type Product = {</code>"},{"re":"id\\s*:\\s*string","text":"<code>id: string;</code>"},{"re":"name\\s*:\\s*string","text":"<code>name: string;</code>"},{"re":"pricePence\\s*:\\s*number","text":"<code>pricePence: number;</code>"},{"re":"salePricePence\\?\\s*:\\s*number","text":"Optional properties get a question mark before the colon: <code>salePricePence?: number;</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"A union of allowed values","file":"status.ts","lang":"ts","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Two lines:</p>\n<ol><li>A type <code>Status</code> that only allows <code>'draft'</code>, <code>'live'</code> or <code>'archived'</code> (separate them with the <code>|</code> symbol).</li><li>A variable (<code>let</code>) <code>status</code> of type <code>Status</code>, set to <code>'draft'</code>.</li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"type Status = 'draft' | 'live' | 'archived';\nlet status: Status = 'draft';","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"type\\s+Status\\s*=","text":"Line 1 starts <code>type Status =</code>"},{"re":"['\"]draft['\"]\\s*\\|\\s*['\"]live['\"]\\s*\\|\\s*['\"]archived['\"]","text":"List the three strings with a bar between each: <code>'draft' | 'live' | 'archived'</code>"},{"re":"let\\s+status\\s*:\\s*Status\\s*=\\s*['\"]draft['\"]","text":"Line 2: <code>let status: Status = 'draft';</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Now <code>status = 'deleted'</code> is an error in your editor before the code ever runs, and typing <code>status = '</code> offers the three valid options.</p>"}],"text":"Why TypeScript TypeScript is JavaScript with type annotations . It compiles to plain JavaScript, so it runs anywhere JS runs. The point is the editor: it knows the shape of every value, autocompletes properties, and underlines mistakes as you type. Most professional teams use it, and every serious web3 library (viem, wagmi) is built for it. function formatGBP(pence: number): string { return `£${(pence / 100).toFixed(2)}`; } formatGBP('6800'); // error: Argument of type 'string' is not assignable to parameter of type 'number' Basic types let name: string = 'Amara'; let stock: number = 12; let isLive: boolean = true; let sizes: string[] = ['S', 'M']; let maybe: string | null = null; // union: one OR the other let status: 'draft' | 'live' | 'archived' = 'draft'; // literal union: only these exact strings You rarely need annotations on variables. TypeScript infers them: let stock = 12 is already a number . Annotate function parameters, return types of exported functions, and data shapes. Describing objects type Product = { id: string; name: string; pricePence: number; sizes: string[]; salePricePence?: number; // ? = optional }; const dress: Product = { id: 'dr-001', name: 'Linen Wrap Dress', pricePence: 6800, sizes: ['S', 'M', 'L'], }; function displayPrice(p: Product): number { return p.salePricePence ?? p.pricePence; } interface Product { ... } does almost the same as type Product = { ... } . Pick one style per project; type is more flexible. Narrowing When a value could be several types, check it and TypeScript narrows it for you: function label(stock: number | 'unknown') { if (stock === 'unknown') return 'Check back soon'; return stock 0 ? `${stock} left` : 'Sold out'; // here stock is definitely a number } Generics in one minute Generics let a type take a type, like a function takes an argument: type ApiResponse T = { data: T; error: string | null }; const res: ApiResponse Product[] = { data: [], error: null }; You will mostly use generics written by libraries ( useState Product[] ([]) , Promise User ), not write your own at first. Set up a TypeScript project The easiest way is a Vite template, which is pre-configured: npm create vite@latest shop -- --template react-ts cd shop npm install npm run dev Check types for the whole project without building: npx tsc --noEmit Keep strict : true in tsconfig.json . Strict mode catches the most bugs; turning it off defeats the purpose. Types vanish at runtime TypeScript checks your code, not your data. JSON from an API or a form can be anything, whatever type you claim. Validate untrusted data at the boundary with a library like Zod , which gives you both a runtime check and a TypeScript type from one schema. import { z } from 'zod'; const ProductSchema = z.object({ id: z.string(), name: z.string().min(1), pricePence: z.number().int().nonnegative(), }); type Product = z.infer typeof ProductSchema ; const product = ProductSchema.parse(await res.json()); // throws if the shape is wrong Avoid any any switches type checking off for that value, and it spreads. If you truly do not know a type yet, use unknown , which forces you to check before using it.","track":"web"},{"id":"web-react","title":"React fundamentals","level":"intermediate","minutes":35,"summary":"Components, props, state, effects and lists. Think in components and build UIs that stay in sync with data.","html":"<h2 id=\"the-big-idea\">The big idea</h2>\n<p>In the DOM lesson you wrote state, a render function, and actions. React is that pattern, industrialised. You describe <strong>what the UI should look like for a given state</strong>, and React updates the page efficiently whenever the state changes.</p>\n<p>Start a project:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">npm create vite@latest shop -- --template react-ts\ncd shop &amp;&amp; npm install &amp;&amp; npm run dev</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"components-and-jsx\">Components and JSX</h2>\n<p>A component is a function that returns <strong>JSX</strong>, HTML-like syntax inside JavaScript:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"tsx\"><pre><code class=\"language-tsx\">function ProductCard() {\n  return (\n    &lt;article className=&quot;card&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;h2&gt;Linen Wrap Dress&lt;/h2&gt;\n      &lt;p&gt;£68.00&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;/article&gt;\n  );\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>JSX differences from HTML: <code>className</code> instead of <code>class</code>, <code>htmlFor</code> instead of <code>for</code>, every tag must close (<code>&lt;img /&gt;</code>), and <code>{curly braces}</code> drop in any JavaScript expression.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"props-inputs-to-a-component\">Props: inputs to a component</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"tsx\"><pre><code class=\"language-tsx\">type ProductCardProps = {\n  name: string;\n  pricePence: number;\n  onAdd: () =&gt; void;\n};\n\nfunction ProductCard({ name, pricePence, onAdd }: ProductCardProps) {\n  return (\n    &lt;article className=&quot;card&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;h2&gt;{name}&lt;/h2&gt;\n      &lt;p&gt;£{(pricePence / 100).toFixed(2)}&lt;/p&gt;\n      &lt;button onClick={onAdd}&gt;Add to bag&lt;/button&gt;\n    &lt;/article&gt;\n  );\n}\n\n// used like an HTML tag:\n&lt;ProductCard name=&quot;Camp shirt&quot; pricePence={4200} onAdd={() =&gt; console.log('added')} /&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Props flow <strong>down</strong> from parent to child. Children talk back up by calling functions passed as props (<code>onAdd</code>).</p>\n<h2 id=\"state-data-that-changes\">State: data that changes</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"tsx\"><pre><code class=\"language-tsx\">import { useState } from 'react';\n\nfunction Counter() {\n  const [qty, setQty] = useState(1);\n  return (\n    &lt;div&gt;\n      &lt;button onClick={() =&gt; setQty(qty - 1)} disabled={qty &lt;= 1}&gt;-&lt;/button&gt;\n      &lt;span&gt;{qty}&lt;/span&gt;\n      &lt;button onClick={() =&gt; setQty(qty + 1)}&gt;+&lt;/button&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>useState</code> returns the current value and a setter. Calling the setter re-renders the component with the new value. <strong>Never</strong> change state directly (<code>qty++</code> does nothing visible); always call the setter, and for arrays and objects pass a <strong>new</strong> copy:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"tsx\"><pre><code class=\"language-tsx\">setCart([...cart, newItem]);                                    // add\nsetCart(cart.filter((item) =&gt; item.id !== id));                 // remove\nsetCart(cart.map((item) =&gt; item.id === id ? { ...item, qty: item.qty + 1 } : item)); // update</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"lists-and-keys\">Lists and keys</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"tsx\"><pre><code class=\"language-tsx\">&lt;ul&gt;\n  {products.map((p) =&gt; (\n    &lt;li key={p.id}&gt;{p.name}&lt;/li&gt;\n  ))}\n&lt;/ul&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Each item needs a stable, unique <code>key</code> (an id, not the array index) so React can track which item is which.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"2\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"derived-data-do-not-store-what-you-can-calculate\">Derived data: do not store what you can calculate</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"tsx\"><pre><code class=\"language-tsx\">const [cart, setCart] = useState&lt;CartItem[]&gt;([]);\nconst totalPence = cart.reduce((sum, i) =&gt; sum + i.pricePence * i.qty, 0);   // calculated every render</code></pre></div>\n<p>Storing <code>total</code> in its own state would let it drift out of sync with <code>cart</code>. Calculate it instead.</p>\n<h2 id=\"effects-syncing-with-the-outside-world\">Effects: syncing with the outside world</h2>\n<p><code>useEffect</code> runs code <strong>after</strong> render, for things outside React: fetching data, timers, subscriptions.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"tsx\"><pre><code class=\"language-tsx\">import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';\n\nfunction Products() {\n  const [products, setProducts] = useState&lt;Product[]&gt;([]);\n  const [status, setStatus] = useState&lt;'loading' | 'error' | 'ready'&gt;('loading');\n\n  useEffect(() =&gt; {\n    let ignore = false;                       // avoid setting state after unmount\n    fetch('/products.json')\n      .then((r) =&gt; { if (!r.ok) throw new Error(); return r.json(); })\n      .then((data) =&gt; { if (!ignore) { setProducts(data); setStatus('ready'); } })\n      .catch(() =&gt; { if (!ignore) setStatus('error'); });\n    return () =&gt; { ignore = true; };          // cleanup\n  }, []);                                     // [] = run once, when first shown\n\n  if (status === 'loading') return &lt;p&gt;Loading...&lt;/p&gt;;\n  if (status === 'error') return &lt;p&gt;Could not load products.&lt;/p&gt;;\n  return &lt;ul&gt;{products.map((p) =&gt; &lt;li key={p.id}&gt;{p.name}&lt;/li&gt;)}&lt;/ul&gt;;\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>The array at the end lists <strong>dependencies</strong>: the effect re-runs when any of them change. In real apps, use a data library like <strong>TanStack Query</strong> for fetching; it handles caching, retries and loading states for you.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-pro\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Thinking in components</p><p>Before coding a page, sketch it and draw boxes around pieces: <code>Header</code>, <code>ProductGrid</code>, <code>ProductCard</code>, <code>CartDrawer</code>. Decide which component owns each piece of state: the closest common parent of everything that needs it. Then pass data down as props.</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"How should you add an item to a cart stored in React state?","options":["cart.push(item)","setCart([...cart, item])","cart = [...cart, item]"],"answer":1,"explain":"State must be replaced with a new value through its setter, or React will not re-render."},{"q":"Why does each item in a mapped list need a key?","options":["For CSS styling","So React can track which item is which between renders","Keys make the list sorted"],"answer":1,"explain":"Use a stable id. Array indexes break when items are reordered or removed."},{"q":"The cart total can be calculated from the cart items. Where should it live?","options":["In its own useState, updated whenever the cart changes","Calculated during render from the cart state","In localStorage"],"answer":1,"explain":"Derived data should be computed, not stored, so it can never go out of sync."},{"q":"What does the empty array in useEffect(() =&gt; {...}, []) mean?","options":["Run the effect once, after the component first renders","Run the effect on every render","Never run the effect"],"answer":0,"explain":"The dependency array controls when the effect re-runs. Empty means no dependencies."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Your first component","file":"ProductCard.tsx","lang":"ts","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write a component called <code>ProductCard</code> that returns an <code>h2</code> saying <code>Linen Wrap Dress</code>. Three lines: the function line, a <code>return</code> line, and the closing brace.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"function ProductCard() {\n  return <h2>Linen Wrap Dress</h2>;\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"function\\s+ProductCard\\s*\\(\\s*\\)\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>function ProductCard() {</code> (components start with a capital letter)."},{"re":"return\\s+<h2>\\s*Linen Wrap Dress\\s*</h2>","text":"Line 2: <code>return &lt;h2&gt;Linen Wrap Dress&lt;/h2&gt;;</code> JSX goes straight after <code>return</code>."},{"re":"^\\s*\\}\\s*$","text":"Close the function with <code>}</code>."}],"explain":""},{"title":"State with useState","file":"Counter.tsx","lang":"ts","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write a <code>Counter</code> component:</p>\n<ol><li><code>function Counter() {</code></li><li>Create state: <code>const [qty, setQty] = useState(1);</code></li><li>Return a button that adds one when clicked: <code>return &lt;button onClick={() =&gt; setQty(qty + 1)}&gt;{qty}&lt;/button&gt;;</code></li><li><code>}</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"function Counter() {\n  const [qty, setQty] = useState(1);\n  return <button onClick={() => setQty(qty + 1)}>{qty}</button>;\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"function\\s+Counter\\s*\\(\\s*\\)\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>function Counter() {</code>"},{"re":"const\\s*\\[\\s*qty\\s*,\\s*setQty\\s*\\]\\s*=\\s*useState\\(\\s*1\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>const [qty, setQty] = useState(1);</code> (square brackets on the left)."},{"re":"onClick=\\{\\s*\\(\\s*\\)\\s*=>\\s*setQty\\(\\s*qty\\s*\\+\\s*1\\s*\\)\\s*\\}","text":"The click handler: <code>onClick={() =&gt; setQty(qty + 1)}</code>"},{"re":">\\s*\\{\\s*qty\\s*\\}\\s*</button>","text":"Between the tags show the value: <code>{qty}&lt;/button&gt;;</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"Render a list with keys","file":"ProductList.tsx","lang":"ts","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Inside a <code>ul</code>, map over <code>products</code> and return an <code>li</code> for each, with <code>key={p.id}</code> and the product's name. Three lines: <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code>, the map line, <code>&lt;/ul&gt;</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"<ul>\n  {products.map((p) => <li key={p.id}>{p.name}</li>)}\n</ul>","also":["<ul>\n  {products.map(p => <li key={p.id}>{p.name}</li>)}\n</ul>"],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*<ul>\\s*$","text":"Line 1: <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code>"},{"re":"\\{\\s*products\\.map\\(","text":"Line 2 starts with a curly brace (JavaScript inside JSX), then <code>products.map(</code>"},{"re":"<li\\s+key=\\{\\s*p\\.id\\s*\\}\\s*>","text":"Each item: <code>&lt;li key={p.id}&gt;</code>"},{"re":"\\{\\s*p\\.name\\s*\\}\\s*</li>\\s*\\)\\s*\\}","text":"Then <code>{p.name}&lt;/li&gt;</code>, close the map with <code>)</code> and the brace with <code>}</code>."},{"re":"^\\s*</ul>\\s*$","text":"Line 3: <code>&lt;/ul&gt;</code>"}],"explain":""}],"text":"The big idea In the DOM lesson you wrote state, a render function, and actions. React is that pattern, industrialised. You describe what the UI should look like for a given state , and React updates the page efficiently whenever the state changes. Start a project: npm create vite@latest shop -- --template react-ts cd shop npm install npm run dev Components and JSX A component is a function that returns JSX , HTML-like syntax inside JavaScript: function ProductCard() { return ( article className= card h2 Linen Wrap Dress /h2 p £68.00 /p /article ); } JSX differences from HTML: className instead of class , htmlFor instead of for , every tag must close ( img / ), and {curly braces} drop in any JavaScript expression. Props: inputs to a component type ProductCardProps = { name: string; pricePence: number; onAdd: () = void; }; function ProductCard({ name, pricePence, onAdd }: ProductCardProps) { return ( article className= card h2 {name} /h2 p £{(pricePence / 100).toFixed(2)} /p button onClick={onAdd} Add to bag /button /article ); } // used like an HTML tag: ProductCard name= Camp shirt pricePence={4200} onAdd={() = console.log('added')} / Props flow down from parent to child. Children talk back up by calling functions passed as props ( onAdd ). State: data that changes import { useState } from 'react'; function Counter() { const [qty, setQty] = useState(1); return ( div button onClick={() = setQty(qty - 1)} disabled={qty = 1} - /button span {qty} /span button onClick={() = setQty(qty + 1)} + /button /div ); } useState returns the current value and a setter. Calling the setter re-renders the component with the new value. Never change state directly ( qty++ does nothing visible); always call the setter, and for arrays and objects pass a new copy: setCart([...cart, newItem]); // add setCart(cart.filter((item) = item.id !== id)); // remove setCart(cart.map((item) = item.id === id ? { ...item, qty: item.qty + 1 } : item)); // update Lists and keys ul {products.map((p) = ( li key={p.id} {p.name} /li ))} /ul Each item needs a stable, unique key (an id, not the array index) so React can track which item is which. Derived data: do not store what you can calculate const [cart, setCart] = useState CartItem[] ([]); const totalPence = cart.reduce((sum, i) = sum + i.pricePence * i.qty, 0); // calculated every render Storing total in its own state would let it drift out of sync with cart . Calculate it instead. Effects: syncing with the outside world useEffect runs code after render, for things outside React: fetching data, timers, subscriptions. import { useEffect, useState } from 'react'; function Products() { const [products, setProducts] = useState Product[] ([]); const [status, setStatus] = useState 'loading' | 'error' | 'ready' ('loading'); useEffect(() = { let ignore = false; // avoid setting state after unmount fetch('/products.json') .then((r) = { if (!r.ok) throw new Error(); return r.json(); }) .then((data) = { if (!ignore) { setProducts(data); setStatus('ready'); } }) .catch(() = { if (!ignore) setStatus('error'); }); return () = { ignore = true; }; // cleanup }, []); // [] = run once, when first shown if (status === 'loading') return p Loading... /p ; if (status === 'error') return p Could not load products. /p ; return ul {products.map((p) = li key={p.id} {p.name} /li )} /ul ; } The array at the end lists dependencies : the effect re-runs when any of them change. In real apps, use a data library like TanStack Query for fetching; it handles caching, retries and loading states for you. Thinking in components Before coding a page, sketch it and draw boxes around pieces: Header , ProductGrid , ProductCard , CartDrawer . Decide which component owns each piece of state: the closest common parent of everything that needs it. Then pass data down as props.","track":"web"},{"id":"web-tailwind","title":"Tailwind CSS and design systems","level":"intermediate","minutes":25,"summary":"Style quickly with utility classes, set up brand tokens, and build consistent, reusable components.","html":"<h2 id=\"utility-first-css\">Utility-first CSS</h2>\n<p>Instead of inventing class names and writing CSS in a separate file, Tailwind gives you small single-purpose classes you combine in your markup:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"html\"><pre><code class=\"language-html\">&lt;button class=&quot;rounded-full bg-orange-700 px-5 py-3 font-semibold text-white hover:bg-stone-900 focus-visible:outline-2&quot;&gt;\n  Shop the drop\n&lt;/button&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Each class does one thing: <code>px-5</code> is horizontal padding, <code>bg-orange-700</code> a background colour, <code>hover:</code> applies a style on hover. You rarely leave your HTML, and unused classes are removed from the final CSS automatically.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"set-it-up-in-a-vite-project\">Set it up in a Vite project</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">npm install tailwindcss @tailwindcss/vite</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\"><pre><code class=\"language-ts\">// vite.config.ts\nimport { defineConfig } from 'vite';\nimport react from '@vitejs/plugin-react';\nimport tailwindcss from '@tailwindcss/vite';\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  plugins: [react(), tailwindcss()],\n});</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"css\"><pre><code class=\"language-css\">/* src/index.css */\n@import &quot;tailwindcss&quot;;</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"the-classes-you-will-use-most\">The classes you will use most</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Purpose</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Spacing</td><td><code>p-4</code>, <code>px-6</code>, <code>mt-8</code>, <code>gap-4</code> (scale: 1 = 0.25rem)</td></tr><tr><td>Layout</td><td><code>flex</code>, <code>grid</code>, <code>grid-cols-2</code>, <code>items-center</code>, <code>justify-between</code></td></tr><tr><td>Size</td><td><code>w-full</code>, <code>max-w-screen-lg</code>, <code>h-12</code>, <code>aspect-[4/5]</code></td></tr><tr><td>Type</td><td><code>text-sm</code>, <code>text-4xl</code>, <code>font-bold</code>, <code>tracking-tight</code>, <code>leading-relaxed</code></td></tr><tr><td>Colour</td><td><code>text-stone-900</code>, <code>bg-white</code>, <code>border-stone-200</code></td></tr><tr><td>Effects</td><td><code>rounded-xl</code>, <code>shadow-md</code>, <code>ring-1</code>, <code>transition</code></td></tr><tr><td>States</td><td><code>hover:</code>, <code>focus-visible:</code>, <code>disabled:</code>, <code>dark:</code></td></tr><tr><td>Breakpoints</td><td><code>sm:</code>, <code>md:</code>, <code>lg:</code> (mobile first: <code>md:grid-cols-3</code> applies from medium up)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"a-responsive-product-grid-in-tailwind\">A responsive product grid in Tailwind</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"html\"><pre><code class=\"language-html\">&lt;section class=&quot;grid grid-cols-2 gap-4 p-4 md:grid-cols-3 md:gap-6 lg:grid-cols-4&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;article class=&quot;overflow-hidden rounded-2xl bg-white shadow-sm ring-1 ring-stone-200&quot;&gt;\n    &lt;img src=&quot;dress.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;Sage linen wrap dress&quot; class=&quot;aspect-[4/5] w-full object-cover&quot;&gt;\n    &lt;div class=&quot;space-y-1 p-4&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;h2 class=&quot;font-semibold text-stone-900&quot;&gt;Linen Wrap Dress&lt;/h2&gt;\n      &lt;p class=&quot;text-stone-600&quot;&gt;£68.00&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;/article&gt;\n&lt;/section&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"design-tokens-your-brand-in-one-place\">Design tokens: your brand in one place</h2>\n<p>A <strong>design system</strong> is a set of decisions (colours, type sizes, spacing, radius) used consistently everywhere. In Tailwind v4 you define tokens in CSS with <code>@theme</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"css\"><pre><code class=\"language-css\">@import &quot;tailwindcss&quot;;\n\n@theme {\n  --color-brand: #b4532a;\n  --color-ink: #1f1a17;\n  --color-paper: #fbf7f3;\n  --font-display: &quot;Fraunces&quot;, serif;\n  --radius-card: 1.25rem;\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>Now <code>bg-brand</code>, <code>text-ink</code> and <code>font-display</code> exist as classes. Rebranding a client's site becomes a five-line change.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"components-stop-repetition\">Components stop repetition</h2>\n<p>If you copy the same 15 classes onto every button, make a component instead:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"tsx\"><pre><code class=\"language-tsx\">type ButtonProps = React.ButtonHTMLAttributes&lt;HTMLButtonElement&gt; &amp; { variant?: 'primary' | 'ghost' };\n\nexport function Button({ variant = 'primary', className = '', ...props }: ButtonProps) {\n  const styles = {\n    primary: 'bg-brand text-white hover:bg-ink',\n    ghost: 'bg-transparent text-ink ring-1 ring-ink/20 hover:bg-ink/5',\n  };\n  return &lt;button className={`rounded-full px-5 py-3 font-semibold transition ${styles[variant]} ${className}`} {...props} /&gt;;\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p><strong>shadcn/ui</strong> takes this idea further: it copies accessible, well-designed components (dialogs, menus, forms) into your project so you own and restyle them.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-pro\"><p class=\"callout-title\">What makes a site look professional</p><p>Consistency beats creativity. Use one or two typefaces, a small colour palette, a spacing scale you stick to, generous white space, real high-quality photography, and aligned edges. Most &quot;amateur&quot; sites fail on inconsistency: five font sizes, six greys, random padding.</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"In Tailwind, what does md:grid-cols-3 mean?","options":["Always three columns","Three columns from the medium breakpoint upwards; smaller screens use the base class","Three columns only on medium screens and below"],"answer":1,"explain":"Breakpoint prefixes are mobile first: they apply at that width and above."},{"q":"What is a design token?","options":["A crypto token for designers","A named design decision, like a brand colour or radius, reused everywhere","A Figma plug-in"],"answer":1,"explain":"Tokens keep a design consistent and make changes cheap."},{"q":"You keep pasting the same long list of classes onto every button. What should you do?","options":["Keep pasting; it is the Tailwind way","Make a reusable Button component","Switch to inline styles"],"answer":1,"explain":"Components remove duplication while keeping utility classes."}],"exercises":[{"title":"A button made of utilities","file":"Button.html","lang":"html","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write a <code>button</code> with these Tailwind classes, in this order: <code>rounded-full bg-orange-700 px-5 py-3 font-semibold text-white</code>. The text is <code>Shop the drop</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"<button class=\"rounded-full bg-orange-700 px-5 py-3 font-semibold text-white\">Shop the drop</button>","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"<button\\s+class=[\"']","text":"Start with <code>&lt;button class=&quot;</code>"},{"re":"rounded-full\\s+bg-orange-700\\s+px-5\\s+py-3\\s+font-semibold\\s+text-white","text":"The classes, separated by spaces: <code>rounded-full bg-orange-700 px-5 py-3 font-semibold text-white</code>"},{"re":">\\s*Shop the drop\\s*</button>","text":"Close the quote and tag, then <code>Shop the drop&lt;/button&gt;</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"Brand tokens with @theme","file":"index.css","lang":"css","run":false,"prompt":"<p>After <code>@import &quot;tailwindcss&quot;;</code>, a <code>@theme</code> block defines your brand tokens. Write the block with two colours: <code>--color-brand: #b4532a;</code> and <code>--color-ink: #1f1a17;</code></p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"@theme {\n  --color-brand: #b4532a;\n  --color-ink: #1f1a17;\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*@theme\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>@theme {</code>"},{"re":"--color-brand\\s*:\\s*#b4532a","text":"<code>--color-brand: #b4532a;</code>"},{"re":"--color-ink\\s*:\\s*#1f1a17","text":"<code>--color-ink: #1f1a17;</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Tailwind turns each <code>--color-*</code> token into classes: <code>bg-brand</code>, <code>text-ink</code>, <code>border-brand</code> and so on.</p>"}],"text":"Utility-first CSS Instead of inventing class names and writing CSS in a separate file, Tailwind gives you small single-purpose classes you combine in your markup: button class= rounded-full bg-orange-700 px-5 py-3 font-semibold text-white hover:bg-stone-900 focus-visible:outline-2 Shop the drop /button Each class does one thing: px-5 is horizontal padding, bg-orange-700 a background colour, hover: applies a style on hover. You rarely leave your HTML, and unused classes are removed from the final CSS automatically. Set it up in a Vite project npm install tailwindcss @tailwindcss/vite // vite.config.ts import { defineConfig } from 'vite'; import react from '@vitejs/plugin-react'; import tailwindcss from '@tailwindcss/vite'; export default defineConfig({ plugins: [react(), tailwindcss()], }); /* src/index.css */ @import tailwindcss ; The classes you will use most Purpose Examples Spacing p-4 , px-6 , mt-8 , gap-4 (scale: 1 = 0.25rem) Layout flex , grid , grid-cols-2 , items-center , justify-between Size w-full , max-w-screen-lg , h-12 , aspect-[4/5] Type text-sm , text-4xl , font-bold , tracking-tight , leading-relaxed Colour text-stone-900 , bg-white , border-stone-200 Effects rounded-xl , shadow-md , ring-1 , transition States hover: , focus-visible: , disabled: , dark: Breakpoints sm: , md: , lg: (mobile first: md:grid-cols-3 applies from medium up) A responsive product grid in Tailwind section class= grid grid-cols-2 gap-4 p-4 md:grid-cols-3 md:gap-6 lg:grid-cols-4 article class= overflow-hidden rounded-2xl bg-white shadow-sm ring-1 ring-stone-200 img src= dress.jpg alt= Sage linen wrap dress class= aspect-[4/5] w-full object-cover div class= space-y-1 p-4 h2 class= font-semibold text-stone-900 Linen Wrap Dress /h2 p class= text-stone-600 £68.00 /p /div /article /section Design tokens: your brand in one place A design system is a set of decisions (colours, type sizes, spacing, radius) used consistently everywhere. In Tailwind v4 you define tokens in CSS with @theme : @import tailwindcss ; @theme { --color-brand: #b4532a; --color-ink: #1f1a17; --color-paper: #fbf7f3; --font-display: Fraunces , serif; --radius-card: 1.25rem; } Now bg-brand , text-ink and font-display exist as classes. Rebranding a client's site becomes a five-line change. Components stop repetition If you copy the same 15 classes onto every button, make a component instead: type ButtonProps = React.ButtonHTMLAttributes HTMLButtonElement { variant?: 'primary' | 'ghost' }; export function Button({ variant = 'primary', className = '', ...props }: ButtonProps) { const styles = { primary: 'bg-brand text-white hover:bg-ink', ghost: 'bg-transparent text-ink ring-1 ring-ink/20 hover:bg-ink/5', }; return button className={`rounded-full px-5 py-3 font-semibold transition ${styles[variant]} ${className}`} {...props} / ; } shadcn/ui takes this idea further: it copies accessible, well-designed components (dialogs, menus, forms) into your project so you own and restyle them. What makes a site look professional Consistency beats creativity. Use one or two typefaces, a small colour palette, a spacing scale you stick to, generous white space, real high-quality photography, and aligned edges. Most amateur sites fail on inconsistency: five font sizes, six greys, random padding.","track":"web"},{"id":"web-a11y","title":"Accessibility and forms","level":"intermediate","minutes":25,"summary":"Build sites everyone can use (including with a keyboard or screen reader) and forms that convert.","html":"<h2 id=\"why-accessibility-matters\">Why accessibility matters</h2>\n<p>Around one in five people has a disability. Many more have a temporary or situational one: a broken arm, bright sunlight, a slow connection, holding a baby. Accessible sites are easier for everyone, rank better in search, and are a legal requirement for many businesses (the UK Equality Act, the European Accessibility Act). It is also what separates professional work from amateur work.</p>\n<p>The standard is <strong>WCAG</strong> (Web Content Accessibility Guidelines). Aim for level AA.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-essentials-checklist\">The essentials checklist</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Use semantic HTML.</strong> <code>&lt;button&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;a&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;nav&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;main&gt;</code>, headings in order. This solves most problems for free.</li><li><strong>Every image has alt text</strong> (or <code>alt=&quot;&quot;</code> if decorative).</li><li><strong>Every input has a label.</strong></li><li><strong>Everything works with a keyboard.</strong> Press <kbd>Tab</kbd> through your site: can you reach and use every control? Can you see where focus is?</li><li><strong>Colour contrast</strong>: body text needs at least 4.5:1 against its background. Light grey on white usually fails.</li><li><strong>Do not rely on colour alone.</strong> &quot;Fields in red are required&quot; fails colour-blind users; add text or an icon.</li><li><strong>Respect user settings</strong>: zoom to 200% without breaking, and honour <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code>.</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"visible-focus\">Visible focus</h2>\n<p>Never remove focus outlines without replacing them:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"css\"><pre><code class=\"language-css\">/* bad: keyboard users get lost */\nbutton:focus { outline: none; }\n\n/* good: a clear ring for keyboard users only */\n:focus-visible {\n  outline: 3px solid #2747e0;\n  outline-offset: 2px;\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"a-form-done-properly\">A form done properly</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"html\" data-try=\"1\"><pre><code class=\"language-html\">&lt;form novalidate&gt;\n  &lt;div class=&quot;field&quot;&gt;\n    &lt;label for=&quot;email&quot;&gt;Email address&lt;/label&gt;\n    &lt;input id=&quot;email&quot; name=&quot;email&quot; type=&quot;email&quot; autocomplete=&quot;email&quot; required aria-describedby=&quot;email-hint email-error&quot;&gt;\n    &lt;p id=&quot;email-hint&quot; class=&quot;hint&quot;&gt;We will send your order confirmation here.&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;p id=&quot;email-error&quot; class=&quot;error&quot; hidden&gt;Enter an email address like name@example.com&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;button type=&quot;submit&quot;&gt;Continue to payment&lt;/button&gt;\n&lt;/form&gt;\n&lt;style&gt;\n  body { font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; padding: 1rem; max-width: 420px; }\n  .field { display: grid; gap: .35rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; }\n  input { padding: .7rem; font-size: 1rem; border: 1px solid #8a8a8a; border-radius: 8px; }\n  input[aria-invalid=&quot;true&quot;] { border-color: #b42318; }\n  .hint { margin: 0; color: #555; font-size: .9rem; }\n  .error { margin: 0; color: #b42318; font-size: .9rem; }\n  button { padding: .8rem 1.2rem; font-size: 1rem; border: 0; border-radius: 8px; background: #1f1a17; color: #fff; }\n&lt;/style&gt;\n&lt;script&gt;\n  const form = document.querySelector('form');\n  const email = document.querySelector('#email');\n  const error = document.querySelector('#email-error');\n  form.addEventListener('submit', (e) =&gt; {\n    e.preventDefault();\n    const ok = email.validity.valid;\n    email.setAttribute('aria-invalid', String(!ok));\n    error.hidden = ok;\n    if (!ok) email.focus();\n    else console.log('Valid! Send to the server here.');\n  });\n&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<p>What makes it good:</p>\n<ul><li>The <strong>label</strong> is always visible (placeholders disappear when you type, so they are not labels).</li><li><code>type=&quot;email&quot;</code> and <code>autocomplete=&quot;email&quot;</code> give the right mobile keyboard and let the browser autofill.</li><li>The error says <strong>how to fix it</strong>, sits next to the field, is linked with <code>aria-describedby</code>, and focus moves to the problem.</li><li>Validation runs on submit, not on every keystroke while the user is still typing.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"aria-the-last-resort\">ARIA: the last resort</h2>\n<p>ARIA attributes (<code>aria-label</code>, <code>aria-expanded</code>, <code>role</code>) add meaning for assistive technology when HTML alone cannot. The first rule of ARIA: <strong>if a native element does the job, use it</strong>. A <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> is always better than <code>&lt;div role=&quot;button&quot; tabindex=&quot;0&quot;&gt;</code> with hand-written key handling.</p>\n<p>Useful ones:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"html\"><pre><code class=\"language-html\">&lt;button aria-label=&quot;Close menu&quot;&gt;x&lt;/button&gt;                     &lt;!-- icon-only buttons need a name --&gt;\n&lt;button aria-expanded=&quot;false&quot; aria-controls=&quot;menu&quot;&gt;Menu&lt;/button&gt;  &lt;!-- toggles announce their state --&gt;\n&lt;p role=&quot;status&quot;&gt;Added to bag&lt;/p&gt;                              &lt;!-- announces updates politely --&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Test it yourself in five minutes</p><p>Unplug your mouse and use your site with <kbd>Tab</kbd>, <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Tab</kbd>, <kbd>Enter</kbd> and <kbd>Space</kbd>. Then run Lighthouse's Accessibility audit in Chrome DevTools. Then try your phone's screen reader (VoiceOver on iPhone, TalkBack on Android) on one page.</p></aside>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>","quiz":[{"q":"Why is a placeholder not a replacement for a label?","options":["Placeholders are not supported on phones","It disappears as soon as the user types, and is often low contrast","Labels load faster"],"answer":1,"explain":"Users forget what a field was for once they start typing. Keep a visible label."},{"q":"Minimum contrast ratio for normal body text at WCAG AA?","options":["2:1","4.5:1","10:1"],"answer":1,"explain":"4.5:1 for normal text, 3:1 for large text."},{"q":"You need a clickable control that opens a menu. Best choice?","options":["A button element with aria-expanded","A div with an onclick handler","A link with href=&quot;#&quot;"],"answer":0,"explain":"Native buttons are focusable and keyboard-operable for free. aria-expanded announces open or closed."},{"q":"What is the first rule of ARIA?","options":["Add ARIA to every element","Use a native HTML element instead whenever one exists","Only use aria-label"],"answer":1,"explain":"Native elements come with keyboard support and semantics built in."}],"exercises":[{"title":"A visible focus ring","file":"styles.css","lang":"css","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write a <code>:focus-visible</code> rule with <code>outline: 3px solid #2747e0;</code> and <code>outline-offset: 2px;</code>. Run it, click inside the preview and press Tab to see your focus ring.</p>","starter":"","html":"<p style=\"font-family:system-ui\">Click in the preview, then press Tab to move focus:</p>\n<a href=\"#\">A link</a> <button>A button</button>","solution":":focus-visible {\n  outline: 3px solid #2747e0;\n  outline-offset: 2px;\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*:focus-visible\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>:focus-visible {</code> (starts with a colon)."},{"re":"outline\\s*:\\s*3px\\s+solid\\s+#2747e0","text":"<code>outline: 3px solid #2747e0;</code>"},{"re":"outline-offset\\s*:\\s*2px","text":"<code>outline-offset: 2px;</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"Name icon buttons for screen readers","file":"menu.html","lang":"html","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Two buttons:</p>\n<ol><li>A close button whose visible text is just <code>x</code>, with <code>aria-label=&quot;Close menu&quot;</code>.</li><li>A menu toggle with <code>aria-expanded=&quot;false&quot;</code> and <code>aria-controls=&quot;menu&quot;</code>, and the text <code>Menu</code>.</li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"<button aria-label=\"Close menu\">x</button>\n<button aria-expanded=\"false\" aria-controls=\"menu\">Menu</button>","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"<button\\s+aria-label=[\"']Close menu[\"']\\s*>\\s*x\\s*</button>","text":"Line 1: <code>&lt;button aria-label=&quot;Close menu&quot;&gt;x&lt;/button&gt;</code>"},{"re":"aria-expanded=[\"']false[\"']","text":"Line 2 needs <code>aria-expanded=&quot;false&quot;</code>"},{"re":"aria-controls=[\"']menu[\"']\\s*>\\s*Menu\\s*</button>","text":"then <code>aria-controls=&quot;menu&quot;&gt;Menu&lt;/button&gt;</code>"}],"explain":""}],"text":"Why accessibility matters Around one in five people has a disability. Many more have a temporary or situational one: a broken arm, bright sunlight, a slow connection, holding a baby. Accessible sites are easier for everyone, rank better in search, and are a legal requirement for many businesses (the UK Equality Act, the European Accessibility Act). It is also what separates professional work from amateur work. The standard is WCAG (Web Content Accessibility Guidelines). Aim for level AA. The essentials checklist Use semantic HTML. button , a , nav , main , headings in order. This solves most problems for free. Every image has alt text (or alt= if decorative). Every input has a label. Everything works with a keyboard. Press Tab through your site: can you reach and use every control? Can you see where focus is? Colour contrast : body text needs at least 4.5:1 against its background. Light grey on white usually fails. Do not rely on colour alone. Fields in red are required fails colour-blind users; add text or an icon. Respect user settings : zoom to 200% without breaking, and honour prefers-reduced-motion . Visible focus Never remove focus outlines without replacing them: /* bad: keyboard users get lost */ button:focus { outline: none; } /* good: a clear ring for keyboard users only */ :focus-visible { outline: 3px solid #2747e0; outline-offset: 2px; } A form done properly form novalidate div class= field label for= email Email address /label input id= email name= email type= email autocomplete= email required aria-describedby= email-hint email-error p id= email-hint class= hint We will send your order confirmation here. /p p id= email-error class= error hidden Enter an email address like name@example.com /p /div button type= submit Continue to payment /button /form style body { font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; padding: 1rem; max-width: 420px; } .field { display: grid; gap: .35rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; } input { padding: .7rem; font-size: 1rem; border: 1px solid #8a8a8a; border-radius: 8px; } input[aria-invalid= true ] { border-color: #b42318; } .hint { margin: 0; color: #555; font-size: .9rem; } .error { margin: 0; color: #b42318; font-size: .9rem; } button { padding: .8rem 1.2rem; font-size: 1rem; border: 0; border-radius: 8px; background: #1f1a17; color: #fff; } /style script const form = document.querySelector('form'); const email = document.querySelector('#email'); const error = document.querySelector('#email-error'); form.addEventListener('submit', (e) = { e.preventDefault(); const ok = email.validity.valid; email.setAttribute('aria-invalid', String(!ok)); error.hidden = ok; if (!ok) email.focus(); else console.log('Valid! Send to the server here.'); }); /script What makes it good: The label is always visible (placeholders disappear when you type, so they are not labels). type= email and autocomplete= email give the right mobile keyboard and let the browser autofill. The error says how to fix it , sits next to the field, is linked with aria-describedby , and focus moves to the problem. Validation runs on submit, not on every keystroke while the user is still typing. ARIA: the last resort ARIA attributes ( aria-label , aria-expanded , role ) add meaning for assistive technology when HTML alone cannot. The first rule of ARIA: if a native element does the job, use it . A button is always better than div role= button tabindex= 0 with hand-written key handling. Useful ones: button aria-label= Close menu x /button !-- icon-only buttons need a name -- button aria-expanded= false aria-controls= menu Menu /button !-- toggles announce their state -- p role= status Added to bag /p !-- announces updates politely -- Test it yourself in five minutes Unplug your mouse and use your site with Tab , Shift + Tab , Enter and Space . Then run Lighthouse's Accessibility audit in Chrome DevTools. Then try your phone's screen reader (VoiceOver on iPhone, TalkBack on Android) on one page.","track":"web"},{"id":"web-deploy","title":"Deploying your site to the internet","level":"intermediate","minutes":25,"summary":"Get a real URL with GitHub Pages, Vercel or Netlify, connect a custom domain, and ship with confidence.","html":"<h2 id=\"static-versus-dynamic-sites\">Static versus dynamic sites</h2>\n<ul><li>A <strong>static</strong> site is just files (HTML, CSS, JS, images). Any file host can serve it, usually free: GitHub Pages, Netlify, Vercel, Cloudflare Pages.</li><li>A <strong>dynamic</strong> site runs server code per request (a Next.js app with an API, a database). It needs a platform that runs code: Vercel, Netlify Functions, Railway, Render, Fly.io, or your own server.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"option-1-github-pages-plain-html-css-js\">Option 1: GitHub Pages (plain HTML/CSS/JS)</h2>\n<ol><li>Push your project to GitHub.</li><li>Open the repository's <strong>Settings, Pages</strong>.</li><li>Choose <strong>Deploy from a branch</strong>, branch <code>main</code>, folder <code>/ (root)</code>.</li><li>Wait a minute. Your site is at <code>https://&lt;username&gt;.github.io/&lt;repo&gt;/</code>.</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"option-2-vercel-or-netlify-anything-with-a-build-step\">Option 2: Vercel or Netlify (anything with a build step)</h2>\n<p>Connect your GitHub repo in their dashboard. They detect the framework (Vite, Next.js, Astro), run the build, and give you a URL. Every push to <code>main</code> deploys automatically, and every pull request gets its own <strong>preview URL</strong> you can share with a client before going live.</p>\n<p>From the terminal:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">npm install -g vercel\nvercel          # first deploy creates a preview\nvercel --prod   # deploy to production</code></pre></div>\n<p>Build settings for a Vite project: build command <code>npm run build</code>, output folder <code>dist</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"custom-domains\">Custom domains</h2>\n<ol><li>Buy a domain from a registrar (Cloudflare, Namecheap, Porkbun, Google-partner registrars).</li><li>In your host's dashboard, add the domain. It will tell you which <strong>DNS records</strong> to create.</li><li>In the registrar's DNS settings, add them. Typically:</li></ol>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Record</th><th>Name</th><th>Value</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>A</code></td><td><code>@</code></td><td>An IP address from your host</td><td>Points the root domain (brand.com)</td></tr><tr><td><code>CNAME</code></td><td><code>www</code></td><td>A hostname from your host</td><td>Points www.brand.com</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ol><li>Wait for DNS to propagate (minutes, occasionally hours). HTTPS certificates are issued automatically.</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"environment-variables-in-production\">Environment variables in production</h2>\n<p>Secrets (API keys, database URLs) are set in the host's dashboard, never committed. In Vite, only variables starting with <code>VITE_</code> are exposed to the browser, and anything exposed to the browser is public. Keep real secrets server-side.</p>\n<h2 id=\"pre-launch-checklist\">Pre-launch checklist</h2>\n<ul><li>Title and meta description on every page; a share image (<code>og:image</code>) so links look good on social media.</li><li>A favicon.</li><li>Works at 375px wide, on a real phone.</li><li>Lighthouse scores checked (performance, accessibility, best practices, SEO).</li><li>Forms actually send somewhere, with success and error messages.</li><li>A 404 page.</li><li>Analytics installed (Plausible, Vercel Analytics or Google Analytics), with a cookie notice if required.</li><li>Images compressed (WebP or AVIF) and sized correctly.</li></ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-pro\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Preview deployments sell</p><p>For client work, send a preview URL rather than screenshots. Clients click around on their own phones, give better feedback, and see you as a professional. Always deploy a preview of every change before it goes to production.</p></aside>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>","quiz":[{"q":"Which hosting option can serve a plain HTML, CSS and JavaScript site for free with no build step?","options":["GitHub Pages","Only a paid virtual server","A database host"],"answer":0,"explain":"Static sites are just files. GitHub Pages, Netlify, Vercel and Cloudflare Pages all host them free."},{"q":"You want www.brandname.com to point at your Vercel site. What do you change?","options":["The HTML title","DNS records at your domain registrar","Your package.json"],"answer":1,"explain":"DNS maps names to servers. The host tells you which records to add."},{"q":"A Vite variable named VITE_STRIPE_SECRET is used in front-end code. What is the problem?","options":["Nothing, VITE_ variables are encrypted","Anything exposed to the browser is public, so the secret key is leaked","Vite will refuse to build"],"answer":1,"explain":"Only publishable keys belong in front-end code. Secret keys stay on the server."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Deploy with the Vercel CLI","file":"Terminal","lang":"bash","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Three commands: install the Vercel CLI globally, make a preview deployment, then deploy to production.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"npm install -g vercel\nvercel\nvercel --prod","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*npm\\s+install\\s+-g\\s+vercel\\s*$","text":"Line 1: <code>npm install -g vercel</code> (<code>-g</code> = global)."},{"re":"^\\s*vercel\\s*$","text":"Line 2: just <code>vercel</code> makes a preview."},{"re":"^\\s*vercel\\s+--prod\\s*$","text":"Line 3: <code>vercel --prod</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"SEO and sharing tags","file":"head.html","lang":"html","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write three lines for a page's <code>head</code>:</p>\n<ol><li>A <code>title</code>: <code>Amara Studio | Handmade jewellery</code></li><li>A meta description: <code>name=&quot;description&quot;</code> and <code>content=&quot;Handmade brass jewellery from London.&quot;</code></li><li>A share image: <code>property=&quot;og:image&quot;</code> and <code>content=&quot;https://amarastudio.com/og.jpg&quot;</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"<title>Amara Studio | Handmade jewellery</title>\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Handmade brass jewellery from London.\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://amarastudio.com/og.jpg\">","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"<title>\\s*Amara Studio \\| Handmade jewellery\\s*</title>","text":"Line 1: <code>&lt;title&gt;Amara Studio | Handmade jewellery&lt;/title&gt;</code>"},{"re":"<meta\\s+name=[\"']description[\"']\\s+content=[\"']Handmade brass jewellery from London\\.[\"']","text":"Line 2: <code>&lt;meta name=&quot;description&quot; content=&quot;Handmade brass jewellery from London.&quot;&gt;</code>"},{"re":"<meta\\s+property=[\"']og:image[\"']\\s+content=[\"']https://amarastudio\\.com/og\\.jpg[\"']","text":"Line 3: <code>&lt;meta property=&quot;og:image&quot; content=&quot;https://amarastudio.com/og.jpg&quot;&gt;</code>"}],"explain":""}],"text":"Static versus dynamic sites A static site is just files (HTML, CSS, JS, images). Any file host can serve it, usually free: GitHub Pages, Netlify, Vercel, Cloudflare Pages. A dynamic site runs server code per request (a Next.js app with an API, a database). It needs a platform that runs code: Vercel, Netlify Functions, Railway, Render, Fly.io, or your own server. Option 1: GitHub Pages (plain HTML/CSS/JS) Push your project to GitHub. Open the repository's Settings, Pages . Choose Deploy from a branch , branch main , folder / (root) . Wait a minute. Your site is at https:// username .github.io/ repo / . Option 2: Vercel or Netlify (anything with a build step) Connect your GitHub repo in their dashboard. They detect the framework (Vite, Next.js, Astro), run the build, and give you a URL. Every push to main deploys automatically, and every pull request gets its own preview URL you can share with a client before going live. From the terminal: npm install -g vercel vercel # first deploy creates a preview vercel --prod # deploy to production Build settings for a Vite project: build command npm run build , output folder dist . Custom domains Buy a domain from a registrar (Cloudflare, Namecheap, Porkbun, Google-partner registrars). In your host's dashboard, add the domain. It will tell you which DNS records to create. In the registrar's DNS settings, add them. Typically: Record Name Value Purpose A @ An IP address from your host Points the root domain (brand.com) CNAME www A hostname from your host Points www.brand.com Wait for DNS to propagate (minutes, occasionally hours). HTTPS certificates are issued automatically. Environment variables in production Secrets (API keys, database URLs) are set in the host's dashboard, never committed. In Vite, only variables starting with VITE_ are exposed to the browser, and anything exposed to the browser is public. Keep real secrets server-side. Pre-launch checklist Title and meta description on every page; a share image ( og:image ) so links look good on social media. A favicon. Works at 375px wide, on a real phone. Lighthouse scores checked (performance, accessibility, best practices, SEO). Forms actually send somewhere, with success and error messages. A 404 page. Analytics installed (Plausible, Vercel Analytics or Google Analytics), with a cookie notice if required. Images compressed (WebP or AVIF) and sized correctly. Preview deployments sell For client work, send a preview URL rather than screenshots. Clients click around on their own phones, give better feedback, and see you as a professional. Always deploy a preview of every change before it goes to production.","track":"web"},{"id":"web-nextjs","title":"Full-stack React with Next.js","level":"advanced","minutes":35,"summary":"File-based routing, server and client components, data fetching, server actions and route handlers.","html":"<h2 id=\"what-next-js-adds-to-react\">What Next.js adds to React</h2>\n<p>React draws UI. Next.js turns it into a full application framework:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>File-based routing</strong>: a folder is a URL.</li><li><strong>Server rendering</strong>: pages arrive as HTML, which is fast and great for SEO.</li><li><strong>Server components</strong>: components that run only on the server, so they can read databases and secrets directly.</li><li><strong>API endpoints and server actions</strong> in the same project.</li><li>Image, font and script optimisation built in.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">npx create-next-app@latest shop\ncd shop &amp;&amp; npm run dev</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"the-app-router-folders-are-routes\">The App Router: folders are routes</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">app/\n  layout.tsx              -&gt; wraps every page (header, footer, fonts)\n  page.tsx                -&gt; /\n  products/\n    page.tsx              -&gt; /products\n    [slug]/\n      page.tsx            -&gt; /products/linen-wrap-dress  (dynamic segment)\n  api/\n    newsletter/\n      route.ts            -&gt; POST /api/newsletter</code></pre></div>\n<p>Special files: <code>loading.tsx</code> (shown while a route loads), <code>error.tsx</code> (shown when it throws), <code>not-found.tsx</code> (404).</p>\n<h2 id=\"server-components-by-default\">Server components by default</h2>\n<p>In the App Router, components run <strong>on the server</strong> unless you opt out. That means you can fetch data with plain <code>await</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"tsx\"><pre><code class=\"language-tsx\">// app/products/page.tsx (a server component)\nimport { db } from '@/lib/db';\n\nexport default async function ProductsPage() {\n  const products = await db.product.findMany({ where: { live: true } });   // runs on the server only\n  return (\n    &lt;ul&gt;\n      {products.map((p) =&gt; &lt;li key={p.id}&gt;{p.name}&lt;/li&gt;)}\n    &lt;/ul&gt;\n  );\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>The database code and credentials never reach the browser.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"client-components-for-interactivity\">Client components for interactivity</h2>\n<p>Anything that uses state, effects or browser events needs <code>'use client'</code> at the top of its file:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"tsx\"><pre><code class=\"language-tsx\">'use client';\nimport { useState } from 'react';\n\nexport function AddToBag({ productId }: { productId: string }) {\n  const [added, setAdded] = useState(false);\n  return &lt;button onClick={() =&gt; setAdded(true)}&gt;{added ? 'Added' : 'Add to bag'}&lt;/button&gt;;\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p><strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> keep pages and data loading on the server; push <code>'use client'</code> down to the small interactive leaves (buttons, forms, carousels). Less JavaScript ships to the browser, so pages are faster.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"dynamic-routes\">Dynamic routes</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"tsx\"><pre><code class=\"language-tsx\">// app/products/[slug]/page.tsx\nimport { notFound } from 'next/navigation';\n\nexport default async function ProductPage({ params }: { params: Promise&lt;{ slug: string }&gt; }) {\n  const { slug } = await params;\n  const product = await getProductBySlug(slug);\n  if (!product) notFound();\n  return &lt;h1&gt;{product.name}&lt;/h1&gt;;\n}</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"server-actions-forms-without-writing-an-api\">Server actions: forms without writing an API</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"tsx\"><pre><code class=\"language-tsx\">// app/newsletter/page.tsx\nimport { z } from 'zod';\n\nasync function subscribe(formData: FormData) {\n  'use server';                                               // this function runs on the server\n  const email = z.string().email().parse(formData.get('email'));\n  await db.subscriber.create({ data: { email } });\n}\n\nexport default function Newsletter() {\n  return (\n    &lt;form action={subscribe}&gt;\n      &lt;label htmlFor=&quot;email&quot;&gt;Email&lt;/label&gt;\n      &lt;input id=&quot;email&quot; name=&quot;email&quot; type=&quot;email&quot; required /&gt;\n      &lt;button&gt;Subscribe&lt;/button&gt;\n    &lt;/form&gt;\n  );\n}</code></pre></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Server actions are public endpoints</p><p>Anyone can call a server action directly, not just your form. Always validate the input and check the user is allowed to do the action, exactly as you would for an API route.</p></aside>\n<h2 id=\"route-handlers-a-classic-api\">Route handlers: a classic API</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\"><pre><code class=\"language-ts\">// app/api/products/route.ts\nimport { NextResponse } from 'next/server';\n\nexport async function GET() {\n  const products = await db.product.findMany();\n  return NextResponse.json(products);\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>Use these when something other than your own pages needs the data: a mobile app, a webhook from Stripe, a partner.</p>\n<h2 id=\"seo-metadata\">SEO metadata</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"tsx\"><pre><code class=\"language-tsx\">export const metadata = {\n  title: 'Linen Wrap Dress | Amara Studio',\n  description: 'Hand-finished linen wrap dress, made in London.',\n  openGraph: { images: ['/og/wrap-dress.jpg'] },\n};</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"caching-in-one-paragraph\">Caching, in one paragraph</h2>\n<p>Next.js caches aggressively for speed, which surprises people (&quot;why is my page showing old data?&quot;). Learn the current caching rules in the official docs for your version, and know how to opt a route into fresh data per request, or revalidate after a change with <code>revalidatePath('/products')</code> inside a server action.</p>","quiz":[{"q":"In the App Router, which file creates the page at /about?","options":["pages/about.html","app/about/page.tsx","app/about.tsx"],"answer":1,"explain":"Folders define the URL; page.tsx makes it a page."},{"q":"A component uses useState. What does it need?","options":["'use client' at the top of its file","'use server' at the top of its file","Nothing, all components can use state"],"answer":0,"explain":"Hooks like useState run in the browser, so the component must be a client component."},{"q":"Why is it safe to query the database inside a server component?","options":["Next.js encrypts the query","Server components only run on the server, so the code and credentials never reach the browser","Databases are public anyway"],"answer":1,"explain":"That is the main power of server components."},{"q":"What must every server action do?","options":["Return JSX","Validate its input and check permissions, because anyone can call it","Use useEffect"],"answer":1,"explain":"Server actions are HTTP endpoints under the hood. Treat them as untrusted entry points."}],"exercises":[{"title":"A server component that loads data","file":"app/products/page.tsx","lang":"ts","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write a default-exported <strong>async</strong> page component <code>ProductsPage</code> that awaits <code>getProducts()</code> and returns an <code>h1</code> showing how many products there are: <code>{products.length} products</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"export default async function ProductsPage() {\n  const products = await getProducts();\n  return <h1>{products.length} products</h1>;\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"export\\s+default\\s+async\\s+function\\s+ProductsPage\\s*\\(\\s*\\)\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>export default async function ProductsPage() {</code>"},{"re":"const\\s+products\\s*=\\s*await\\s+getProducts\\(\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>const products = await getProducts();</code>"},{"re":"return\\s+<h1>\\s*\\{\\s*products\\.length\\s*\\}\\s*products\\s*</h1>","text":"Line 3: <code>return &lt;h1&gt;{products.length} products&lt;/h1&gt;;</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"A client component","file":"AddToBag.tsx","lang":"ts","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write a client component (it needs state):</p>\n<ol><li><code>'use client';</code></li><li><code>import { useState } from 'react';</code></li><li><code>export function AddToBag() {</code></li><li><code>const [added, setAdded] = useState(false);</code></li><li>Return a button whose click sets <code>added</code> to <code>true</code> and whose text is <code>{added ? 'Added' : 'Add to bag'}</code>.</li><li><code>}</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"'use client';\nimport { useState } from 'react';\nexport function AddToBag() {\n  const [added, setAdded] = useState(false);\n  return <button onClick={() => setAdded(true)}>{added ? 'Added' : 'Add to bag'}</button>;\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*['\"]use client['\"]","text":"Line 1 is the directive, in quotes: <code>'use client';</code>"},{"re":"import\\s*\\{\\s*useState\\s*\\}\\s*from\\s*['\"]react['\"]","text":"Line 2: <code>import { useState } from 'react';</code>"},{"re":"export\\s+function\\s+AddToBag\\s*\\(\\s*\\)\\s*\\{","text":"Line 3: <code>export function AddToBag() {</code>"},{"re":"const\\s*\\[\\s*added\\s*,\\s*setAdded\\s*\\]\\s*=\\s*useState\\(\\s*false\\s*\\)","text":"Line 4: <code>const [added, setAdded] = useState(false);</code>"},{"re":"onClick=\\{\\s*\\(\\s*\\)\\s*=>\\s*setAdded\\(\\s*true\\s*\\)\\s*\\}","text":"The button's handler: <code>onClick={() =&gt; setAdded(true)}</code>"},{"re":"\\{\\s*added\\s*\\?\\s*['\"]Added['\"]\\s*:\\s*['\"]Add to bag['\"]\\s*\\}","text":"The text uses a ternary: <code>{added ? 'Added' : 'Add to bag'}</code>"}],"explain":""}],"text":"What Next.js adds to React React draws UI. Next.js turns it into a full application framework: File-based routing : a folder is a URL. Server rendering : pages arrive as HTML, which is fast and great for SEO. Server components : components that run only on the server, so they can read databases and secrets directly. API endpoints and server actions in the same project. Image, font and script optimisation built in. npx create-next-app@latest shop cd shop npm run dev The App Router: folders are routes app/ layout.tsx - wraps every page (header, footer, fonts) page.tsx - / products/ page.tsx - /products [slug]/ page.tsx - /products/linen-wrap-dress (dynamic segment) api/ newsletter/ route.ts - POST /api/newsletter Special files: loading.tsx (shown while a route loads), error.tsx (shown when it throws), not-found.tsx (404). Server components by default In the App Router, components run on the server unless you opt out. That means you can fetch data with plain await : // app/products/page.tsx (a server component) import { db } from '@/lib/db'; export default async function ProductsPage() { const products = await db.product.findMany({ where: { live: true } }); // runs on the server only return ( ul {products.map((p) = li key={p.id} {p.name} /li )} /ul ); } The database code and credentials never reach the browser. Client components for interactivity Anything that uses state, effects or browser events needs 'use client' at the top of its file: 'use client'; import { useState } from 'react'; export function AddToBag({ productId }: { productId: string }) { const [added, setAdded] = useState(false); return button onClick={() = setAdded(true)} {added ? 'Added' : 'Add to bag'} /button ; } Rule of thumb: keep pages and data loading on the server; push 'use client' down to the small interactive leaves (buttons, forms, carousels). Less JavaScript ships to the browser, so pages are faster. Dynamic routes // app/products/[slug]/page.tsx import { notFound } from 'next/navigation'; export default async function ProductPage({ params }: { params: Promise { slug: string } }) { const { slug } = await params; const product = await getProductBySlug(slug); if (!product) notFound(); return h1 {product.name} /h1 ; } Server actions: forms without writing an API // app/newsletter/page.tsx import { z } from 'zod'; async function subscribe(formData: FormData) { 'use server'; // this function runs on the server const email = z.string().email().parse(formData.get('email')); await db.subscriber.create({ data: { email } }); } export default function Newsletter() { return ( form action={subscribe} label htmlFor= email Email /label input id= email name= email type= email required / button Subscribe /button /form ); } Server actions are public endpoints Anyone can call a server action directly, not just your form. Always validate the input and check the user is allowed to do the action, exactly as you would for an API route. Route handlers: a classic API // app/api/products/route.ts import { NextResponse } from 'next/server'; export async function GET() { const products = await db.product.findMany(); return NextResponse.json(products); } Use these when something other than your own pages needs the data: a mobile app, a webhook from Stripe, a partner. SEO metadata export const metadata = { title: 'Linen Wrap Dress | Amara Studio', description: 'Hand-finished linen wrap dress, made in London.', openGraph: { images: ['/og/wrap-dress.jpg'] }, }; Caching, in one paragraph Next.js caches aggressively for speed, which surprises people ( why is my page showing old data? ). Learn the current caching rules in the official docs for your version, and know how to opt a route into fresh data per request, or revalidate after a change with revalidatePath('/products') inside a server action.","track":"web"},{"id":"web-databases","title":"Databases and back ends","level":"advanced","minutes":35,"summary":"Model data in tables, write SQL, use Postgres with an ORM, and design clean REST APIs.","html":"<h2 id=\"why-a-database\">Why a database</h2>\n<p><code>localStorage</code> lives in one browser. A <strong>database</strong> lives on a server, is shared by all users, survives crashes, and can answer questions like &quot;the ten best-selling dresses this month&quot; in milliseconds.</p>\n<p><strong>PostgreSQL</strong> (Postgres) is the default choice for most new projects: free, reliable, powerful. Run it locally with Docker:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">docker run --name dev-postgres -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=devpass -p 5432:5432 -d postgres:17</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"tables-rows-and-relations\">Tables, rows and relations</h2>\n<p>A relational database stores data in <strong>tables</strong> (like spreadsheets) with <strong>rows</strong> (records) and <strong>columns</strong> (fields). Tables link to each other with <strong>keys</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">customers                orders                         order_items\n---------                ------                         -----------\nid (PK)                  id (PK)                        id (PK)\nemail (unique)           customer_id (FK -&gt; customers)  order_id (FK -&gt; orders)\nname                     status                         product_id (FK -&gt; products)\ncreated_at               total_pence                    qty\n                         created_at                     unit_price_pence</code></pre></div>\n<ul><li><strong>PK</strong> (primary key): uniquely identifies a row.</li><li><strong>FK</strong> (foreign key): points to a row in another table. One customer has many orders; one order has many items.</li><li>Notice <code>unit_price_pence</code> is copied into <code>order_items</code>. Prices change; the order must remember what was actually paid.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"sql-the-language-of-databases\">SQL: the language of databases</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\"><pre><code class=\"language-sql\">-- read\nSELECT name, email FROM customers WHERE created_at &gt; '2026-01-01' ORDER BY name LIMIT 20;\n\n-- join tables: each order with its customer's email\nSELECT o.id, o.total_pence, c.email\nFROM orders o\nJOIN customers c ON c.id = o.customer_id\nWHERE o.status = 'paid';\n\n-- aggregate: revenue per month\nSELECT date_trunc('month', created_at) AS month, SUM(total_pence) / 100.0 AS revenue_gbp\nFROM orders\nWHERE status = 'paid'\nGROUP BY month\nORDER BY month;\n\n-- write\nINSERT INTO customers (email, name) VALUES ('ada@example.com', 'Ada');\nUPDATE orders SET status = 'shipped' WHERE id = 42;\nDELETE FROM carts WHERE updated_at &lt; now() - interval '30 days';</code></pre></div>\n<p>Learn SQL properly. ORMs help, but every senior developer can read and write SQL, and debugging slow pages usually means reading it.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"using-an-orm\">Using an ORM</h2>\n<p>An ORM lets you query with typed code. With <strong>Prisma</strong>, you describe the schema:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">// prisma/schema.prisma\nmodel Customer {\n  id        String   @id @default(cuid())\n  email     String   @unique\n  name      String?\n  orders    Order[]\n  createdAt DateTime @default(now())\n}\n\nmodel Order {\n  id         String   @id @default(cuid())\n  customer   Customer @relation(fields: [customerId], references: [id])\n  customerId String\n  status     String   @default(&quot;pending&quot;)\n  totalPence Int\n  createdAt  DateTime @default(now())\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">npx prisma migrate dev --name init     # creates the tables and a migration file</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\"><pre><code class=\"language-ts\">const paid = await prisma.order.findMany({\n  where: { status: 'paid' },\n  include: { customer: true },\n  orderBy: { createdAt: 'desc' },\n  take: 20,\n});</code></pre></div>\n<p><strong>Migrations</strong> are version-controlled changes to the database structure. Commit them like code, so every environment's database matches.</p>\n<h2 id=\"designing-a-rest-api\">Designing a REST API</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method and path</th><th>Does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>GET /api/products</code></td><td>List products (support <code>?page=2&amp;category=dresses</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>GET /api/products/:id</code></td><td>One product</td></tr><tr><td><code>POST /api/products</code></td><td>Create (admin only)</td></tr><tr><td><code>PATCH /api/products/:id</code></td><td>Update some fields</td></tr><tr><td><code>DELETE /api/products/:id</code></td><td>Delete</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Return correct status codes (201 created, 400 invalid input, 401 not logged in, 403 not allowed, 404 not found) and consistent JSON error bodies: <code>{ &quot;error&quot;: &quot;Email is required&quot; }</code>.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Never build SQL by gluing strings</p><p><code>&quot;SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = '&quot; + email + &quot;'&quot;</code> lets an attacker type SQL into your form and read or delete your data. That is <strong>SQL injection</strong>. ORMs and parameterised queries (<code>WHERE email = $1</code>) prevent it. Never interpolate user input into a query string.</p></aside>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"2\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"other-back-end-essentials\">Other back-end essentials</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Indexes</strong> make lookups fast: add one to columns you filter or join on often (<code>email</code>, <code>customer_id</code>).</li><li><strong>Transactions</strong> group writes so they all succeed or all fail: charge the card and create the order together.</li><li><strong>Backups</strong>: managed hosts (Neon, Supabase, Railway, RDS) back up automatically. Test restoring once.</li><li><strong>Pagination</strong>: never return 50,000 rows to a browser.</li></ul>","quiz":[{"q":"What does a foreign key do?","options":["Encrypts a column","Links a row to a row in another table, like an order to its customer","Makes a column unique"],"answer":1,"explain":"Foreign keys model relationships and protect them (you cannot point to a customer that does not exist)."},{"q":"Why copy unit_price_pence into order_items instead of reading it from products?","options":["Product prices change later, but the order must record what was actually paid","It makes the database smaller","SQL cannot join tables"],"answer":0,"explain":"Historical records must not change when current data changes."},{"q":"Which of these is vulnerable to SQL injection?","options":["prisma.user.findUnique({ where: { email } })","A query with $1 placeholders and email passed as a parameter","&quot;SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = '&quot; + email + &quot;'&quot;"],"answer":2,"explain":"String-built queries let attackers inject SQL. Use parameters or an ORM."},{"q":"What is a migration?","options":["Moving to a new hosting provider","A version-controlled change to the database structure","A database backup"],"answer":1,"explain":"Migrations keep every environment's schema in sync, the same way Git does for code."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Your first SQL query","file":"query.sql","lang":"sql","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write one query (keywords in capitals): select <code>name</code> and <code>email</code> from <code>customers</code> where <code>created_at</code> is after <code>'2026-01-01'</code>, ordered by <code>name</code>, limited to <code>20</code> rows. End with a semicolon.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"SELECT name, email FROM customers WHERE created_at > '2026-01-01' ORDER BY name LIMIT 20;","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*SELECT\\s+name\\s*,\\s*email","text":"Start with <code>SELECT name, email</code>"},{"re":"FROM\\s+customers","text":"then <code>FROM customers</code>"},{"re":"WHERE\\s+created_at\\s*>\\s*['\"]2026-01-01['\"]","text":"then <code>WHERE created_at &gt; '2026-01-01'</code>"},{"re":"ORDER\\s+BY\\s+name\\s+LIMIT\\s+20\\s*;","text":"then <code>ORDER BY name LIMIT 20;</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"Join two tables","file":"query.sql","lang":"sql","run":false,"prompt":"<p>List paid orders with their customer's email. Four lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>SELECT o.id, o.total_pence, c.email</code></li><li><code>FROM orders o</code></li><li><code>JOIN customers c ON c.id = o.customer_id</code></li><li><code>WHERE o.status = 'paid';</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"SELECT o.id, o.total_pence, c.email\nFROM orders o\nJOIN customers c ON c.id = o.customer_id\nWHERE o.status = 'paid';","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*SELECT\\s+o\\.id\\s*,\\s*o\\.total_pence\\s*,\\s*c\\.email\\s*$","text":"Line 1: <code>SELECT o.id, o.total_pence, c.email</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*FROM\\s+orders\\s+o\\s*$","text":"Line 2: <code>FROM orders o</code> (<code>o</code> is a short nickname for the table)."},{"re":"^\\s*JOIN\\s+customers\\s+c\\s+ON\\s+c\\.id\\s*=\\s*o\\.customer_id\\s*$","text":"Line 3: <code>JOIN customers c ON c.id = o.customer_id</code>"},{"re":"WHERE\\s+o\\.status\\s*=\\s*['\"]paid['\"]\\s*;","text":"Line 4: <code>WHERE o.status = 'paid';</code>"}],"explain":"<p><code>JOIN ... ON</code> matches each order to the customer whose <code>id</code> equals the order's <code>customer_id</code>. That foreign-key link is what makes relational databases powerful.</p>"},{"title":"Insert a row","file":"query.sql","lang":"sql","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Insert a customer with email <code>'ada@example.com'</code> and name <code>'Ada'</code> into <code>customers</code>, naming the columns <code>(email, name)</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"INSERT INTO customers (email, name) VALUES ('ada@example.com', 'Ada');","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*INSERT\\s+INTO\\s+customers","text":"Start with <code>INSERT INTO customers</code>"},{"re":"\\(\\s*email\\s*,\\s*name\\s*\\)\\s*VALUES","text":"then the columns and the keyword: <code>(email, name) VALUES</code>"},{"re":"VALUES\\s*\\(\\s*['\"]ada@example\\.com['\"]\\s*,\\s*['\"]Ada['\"]\\s*\\)\\s*;","text":"then the values in the same order: <code>('ada@example.com', 'Ada');</code>"}],"explain":""}],"text":"Why a database localStorage lives in one browser. A database lives on a server, is shared by all users, survives crashes, and can answer questions like the ten best-selling dresses this month in milliseconds. PostgreSQL (Postgres) is the default choice for most new projects: free, reliable, powerful. Run it locally with Docker: docker run --name dev-postgres -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=devpass -p 5432:5432 -d postgres:17 Tables, rows and relations A relational database stores data in tables (like spreadsheets) with rows (records) and columns (fields). Tables link to each other with keys . customers orders order_items --------- ------ ----------- id (PK) id (PK) id (PK) email (unique) customer_id (FK - customers) order_id (FK - orders) name status product_id (FK - products) created_at total_pence qty created_at unit_price_pence PK (primary key): uniquely identifies a row. FK (foreign key): points to a row in another table. One customer has many orders; one order has many items. Notice unit_price_pence is copied into order_items . Prices change; the order must remember what was actually paid. SQL: the language of databases -- read SELECT name, email FROM customers WHERE created_at '2026-01-01' ORDER BY name LIMIT 20; -- join tables: each order with its customer's email SELECT o.id, o.total_pence, c.email FROM orders o JOIN customers c ON c.id = o.customer_id WHERE o.status = 'paid'; -- aggregate: revenue per month SELECT date_trunc('month', created_at) AS month, SUM(total_pence) / 100.0 AS revenue_gbp FROM orders WHERE status = 'paid' GROUP BY month ORDER BY month; -- write INSERT INTO customers (email, name) VALUES ('ada@example.com', 'Ada'); UPDATE orders SET status = 'shipped' WHERE id = 42; DELETE FROM carts WHERE updated_at now() - interval '30 days'; Learn SQL properly. ORMs help, but every senior developer can read and write SQL, and debugging slow pages usually means reading it. Using an ORM An ORM lets you query with typed code. With Prisma , you describe the schema: // prisma/schema.prisma model Customer { id String @id @default(cuid()) email String @unique name String? orders Order[] createdAt DateTime @default(now()) } model Order { id String @id @default(cuid()) customer Customer @relation(fields: [customerId], references: [id]) customerId String status String @default( pending ) totalPence Int createdAt DateTime @default(now()) } npx prisma migrate dev --name init # creates the tables and a migration file const paid = await prisma.order.findMany({ where: { status: 'paid' }, include: { customer: true }, orderBy: { createdAt: 'desc' }, take: 20, }); Migrations are version-controlled changes to the database structure. Commit them like code, so every environment's database matches. Designing a REST API Method and path Does GET /api/products List products (support ?page=2 category=dresses ) GET /api/products/:id One product POST /api/products Create (admin only) PATCH /api/products/:id Update some fields DELETE /api/products/:id Delete Return correct status codes (201 created, 400 invalid input, 401 not logged in, 403 not allowed, 404 not found) and consistent JSON error bodies: { error : Email is required } . Never build SQL by gluing strings SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = ' + email + ' lets an attacker type SQL into your form and read or delete your data. That is SQL injection . ORMs and parameterised queries ( WHERE email = $1 ) prevent it. Never interpolate user input into a query string. Other back-end essentials Indexes make lookups fast: add one to columns you filter or join on often ( email , customer_id ). Transactions group writes so they all succeed or all fail: charge the card and create the order together. Backups : managed hosts (Neon, Supabase, Railway, RDS) back up automatically. Test restoring once. Pagination : never return 50,000 rows to a browser.","track":"web"},{"id":"web-security","title":"Authentication and web security","level":"advanced","minutes":35,"summary":"The attacks that actually happen (XSS, injection, CSRF, broken access control) and the habits that stop them.","html":"<h2 id=\"think-like-an-attacker\">Think like an attacker</h2>\n<p>Every input to your system is a potential attack: form fields, URL parameters, headers, cookies, uploaded files, webhooks, even data from your own database if users wrote it. The <strong>OWASP Top 10</strong> lists the most common web risks. These are the ones you will meet first.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-broken-access-control-the-most-common\">1. Broken access control (the most common)</h2>\n<p>The server must check <strong>on every request</strong> that this user is allowed to do this thing to this data.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\"><pre><code class=\"language-ts\">// BAD: anyone logged in can read any order by changing the id in the URL\nconst order = await db.order.findUnique({ where: { id: params.id } });\n\n// GOOD: scope the query to the current user\nconst order = await db.order.findFirst({ where: { id: params.id, customerId: session.user.id } });\nif (!order) return notFound();</code></pre></div>\n<p>Hiding a button in the UI is not security. Attackers call your API directly.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"2-injection\">2. Injection</h2>\n<p>Untrusted input interpreted as code: SQL injection, command injection. Use parameterised queries or an ORM, never string-build commands, and validate input with a schema (Zod) at every entry point.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-cross-site-scripting-xss\">3. Cross-site scripting (XSS)</h2>\n<p>An attacker gets their script to run on your page, then steals sessions or acts as the user.</p>\n<ul><li>Use <code>textContent</code>, not <code>innerHTML</code>, for user content. React escapes text by default; avoid <code>dangerouslySetInnerHTML</code> unless you sanitise with a library like DOMPurify.</li><li>Set a <strong>Content-Security-Policy</strong> header to restrict which scripts may run.</li><li>Never put user input into <code>href</code> without checking it is <code>https:</code> (a <code>javascript:</code> URL runs code).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"4-cross-site-request-forgery-csrf\">4. Cross-site request forgery (CSRF)</h2>\n<p>Another site tricks a logged-in user's browser into sending a request to yours. Defences: <code>SameSite=Lax</code> or <code>Strict</code> cookies (the modern default), CSRF tokens on forms, and never changing data on <code>GET</code> requests. Frameworks like Next.js server actions include protections; know what yours does.</p>\n<h2 id=\"authentication-done-right\">Authentication done right</h2>\n<p><strong>Do not build password auth from scratch</strong> for real projects. Use a well-maintained library or service: Auth.js, Better Auth, Clerk, Supabase Auth, Firebase Auth. They handle the many subtle details.</p>\n<p>If you must store passwords, understand the rules:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Never</strong> store plain passwords or encrypt them. <strong>Hash</strong> them with a slow, salted algorithm designed for passwords: <strong>Argon2id</strong> or <strong>bcrypt</strong>. (SHA-256 alone is far too fast; attackers can try billions per second.)</li><li>Rate-limit login attempts.</li><li>Offer multi-factor authentication and passkeys.</li></ul>\n<p>Sessions:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">Set-Cookie: session=abc123; HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Lax; Path=/; Max-Age=604800</code></pre></div>\n<ul><li><code>HttpOnly</code>: JavaScript cannot read it, so XSS cannot steal it.</li><li><code>Secure</code>: only sent over HTTPS.</li><li><code>SameSite</code>: limits cross-site sending (CSRF defence).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"secrets-and-dependencies\">Secrets and dependencies</h2>\n<ul><li>Secrets live in environment variables, never in Git, never in front-end code.</li><li>Rotate any key that might have leaked.</li><li>Keep dependencies updated (<code>npm audit</code>, Dependabot or Renovate on GitHub).</li><li>Give every service the least privilege it needs: a read-only database user for analytics, a restricted API key for each integration.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"security-headers-worth-setting\">Security headers worth setting</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Header</th><th>Protects against</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>Content-Security-Policy</code></td><td>XSS, unwanted third-party scripts</td></tr><tr><td><code>Strict-Transport-Security</code></td><td>Downgrade to insecure HTTP</td></tr><tr><td><code>X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff</code></td><td>Browsers guessing file types</td></tr><tr><td><code>Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin</code></td><td>Leaking URLs to other sites</td></tr><tr><td><code>frame-ancestors</code> (in CSP)</td><td>Clickjacking (your site in a hidden frame)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-pro\"><p class=\"callout-title\">A security review checklist for every feature</p><p>Who can call this? What if they are not logged in? What if they change the id? What if the input is huge, empty, negative, or contains HTML? What happens if it is called 1,000 times a minute? What do the logs reveal? Ask these six questions and you will catch most vulnerabilities before they ship.</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"A logged-in user changes /orders/1001 to /orders/1002 and sees someone else's order. What is this?","options":["XSS","Broken access control","CSRF"],"answer":1,"explain":"The server failed to check that the order belongs to the current user. Scope every query."},{"q":"How should user passwords be stored?","options":["Encrypted with AES","Hashed with plain SHA-256","Hashed with a slow, salted password algorithm like Argon2id or bcrypt"],"answer":2,"explain":"Password hashes must be deliberately slow so stolen databases cannot be brute-forced quickly."},{"q":"What does the HttpOnly cookie flag do?","options":["Stops JavaScript reading the cookie, so XSS cannot steal the session","Forces HTTPS","Deletes the cookie after one hour"],"answer":0,"explain":"HttpOnly protects session cookies from scripts. Secure is the HTTPS one."},{"q":"You hide the &quot;Delete product&quot; button from non-admins. Is the delete endpoint now secure?","options":["Yes, they cannot see the button","No: the server must check the user is an admin on every request","Only if the button is also disabled"],"answer":1,"explain":"Attackers call APIs directly. Authorisation lives on the server."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Scope the query to the user","file":"orders.ts","lang":"ts","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Fix broken access control. Write the safe version that only finds the order if it belongs to the logged-in user, and returns <code>notFound()</code> otherwise:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const order = await db.order.findFirst({</code></li><li><code>where: { id: params.id, customerId: session.user.id },</code></li><li><code>});</code></li><li><code>if (!order) return notFound();</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const order = await db.order.findFirst({\n  where: { id: params.id, customerId: session.user.id },\n});\nif (!order) return notFound();","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+order\\s*=\\s*await\\s+db\\.order\\.findFirst\\(\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>const order = await db.order.findFirst({</code>"},{"re":"where\\s*:\\s*\\{\\s*id\\s*:\\s*params\\.id\\s*,\\s*customerId\\s*:\\s*session\\.user\\.id\\s*\\}","text":"Line 2 filters by both ids: <code>where: { id: params.id, customerId: session.user.id },</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*\\}\\s*\\)\\s*;?\\s*$","text":"Line 3 closes the object and the call: <code>});</code>"},{"re":"if\\s*\\(\\s*!\\s*order\\s*\\)\\s*return\\s+notFound\\(\\s*\\)","text":"Line 4: <code>if (!order) return notFound();</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Someone changing the id in the URL now gets a 404 instead of a stranger's order. Returning &quot;not found&quot; rather than &quot;forbidden&quot; also avoids confirming the order exists.</p>"},{"title":"A secure session cookie","file":"Response header","lang":"text","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write the response header that sets a <code>session</code> cookie with value <code>abc123</code>, plus these flags in this order: <code>HttpOnly</code>, <code>Secure</code>, <code>SameSite=Lax</code>, <code>Path=/</code>. Separate parts with a semicolon and a space.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"Set-Cookie: session=abc123; HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Lax; Path=/","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*Set-Cookie\\s*:\\s*session=abc123","text":"Start: <code>Set-Cookie: session=abc123</code>"},{"re":";\\s*HttpOnly\\s*;\\s*Secure","text":"then <code>; HttpOnly; Secure</code>"},{"re":";\\s*SameSite=Lax\\s*;\\s*Path=/\\s*$","text":"then <code>; SameSite=Lax; Path=/</code>"}],"explain":""}],"text":"Think like an attacker Every input to your system is a potential attack: form fields, URL parameters, headers, cookies, uploaded files, webhooks, even data from your own database if users wrote it. The OWASP Top 10 lists the most common web risks. These are the ones you will meet first. 1. Broken access control (the most common) The server must check on every request that this user is allowed to do this thing to this data. // BAD: anyone logged in can read any order by changing the id in the URL const order = await db.order.findUnique({ where: { id: params.id } }); // GOOD: scope the query to the current user const order = await db.order.findFirst({ where: { id: params.id, customerId: session.user.id } }); if (!order) return notFound(); Hiding a button in the UI is not security. Attackers call your API directly. 2. Injection Untrusted input interpreted as code: SQL injection, command injection. Use parameterised queries or an ORM, never string-build commands, and validate input with a schema (Zod) at every entry point. 3. Cross-site scripting (XSS) An attacker gets their script to run on your page, then steals sessions or acts as the user. Use textContent , not innerHTML , for user content. React escapes text by default; avoid dangerouslySetInnerHTML unless you sanitise with a library like DOMPurify. Set a Content-Security-Policy header to restrict which scripts may run. Never put user input into href without checking it is https: (a javascript: URL runs code). 4. Cross-site request forgery (CSRF) Another site tricks a logged-in user's browser into sending a request to yours. Defences: SameSite=Lax or Strict cookies (the modern default), CSRF tokens on forms, and never changing data on GET requests. Frameworks like Next.js server actions include protections; know what yours does. Authentication done right Do not build password auth from scratch for real projects. Use a well-maintained library or service: Auth.js, Better Auth, Clerk, Supabase Auth, Firebase Auth. They handle the many subtle details. If you must store passwords, understand the rules: Never store plain passwords or encrypt them. Hash them with a slow, salted algorithm designed for passwords: Argon2id or bcrypt . (SHA-256 alone is far too fast; attackers can try billions per second.) Rate-limit login attempts. Offer multi-factor authentication and passkeys. Sessions: Set-Cookie: session=abc123; HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Lax; Path=/; Max-Age=604800 HttpOnly : JavaScript cannot read it, so XSS cannot steal it. Secure : only sent over HTTPS. SameSite : limits cross-site sending (CSRF defence). Secrets and dependencies Secrets live in environment variables, never in Git, never in front-end code. Rotate any key that might have leaked. Keep dependencies updated ( npm audit , Dependabot or Renovate on GitHub). Give every service the least privilege it needs: a read-only database user for analytics, a restricted API key for each integration. Security headers worth setting Header Protects against Content-Security-Policy XSS, unwanted third-party scripts Strict-Transport-Security Downgrade to insecure HTTP X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff Browsers guessing file types Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin Leaking URLs to other sites frame-ancestors (in CSP) Clickjacking (your site in a hidden frame) A security review checklist for every feature Who can call this? What if they are not logged in? What if they change the id? What if the input is huge, empty, negative, or contains HTML? What happens if it is called 1,000 times a minute? What do the logs reveal? Ask these six questions and you will catch most vulnerabilities before they ship.","track":"web"},{"id":"web-testing","title":"Testing that gives you confidence","level":"advanced","minutes":30,"summary":"Unit, component and end-to-end tests with Vitest, Testing Library and Playwright, and what is worth testing.","html":"<h2 id=\"why-test\">Why test</h2>\n<p>Tests let you change code without fear. Without them, every change risks breaking something you will only hear about from an angry customer. With them, you refactor freely and ship faster. Senior developers write tests not because they are told to, but because it saves them time.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-testing-pyramid\">The testing pyramid</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Tests</th><th>Speed</th><th>How many</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Unit</strong></td><td>One function in isolation</td><td>Milliseconds</td><td>Many</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Component / integration</strong></td><td>Several pieces together (a component with its logic)</td><td>Fast</td><td>Some</td></tr><tr><td><strong>End-to-end (E2E)</strong></td><td>The real app in a real browser, like a user</td><td>Seconds</td><td>A few, for critical journeys</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"unit-tests-with-vitest\">Unit tests with Vitest</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">npm install -D vitest</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\"><pre><code class=\"language-ts\">// src/lib/cart.ts\nexport type Item = { pricePence: number; qty: number };\n\nexport function cartTotal(items: Item[], discountPercent = 0): number {\n  const subtotal = items.reduce((sum, i) =&gt; sum + i.pricePence * i.qty, 0);\n  return Math.round(subtotal * (1 - discountPercent / 100));\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\"><pre><code class=\"language-ts\">// src/lib/cart.test.ts\nimport { describe, it, expect } from 'vitest';\nimport { cartTotal } from './cart';\n\ndescribe('cartTotal', () =&gt; {\n  it('adds up price times quantity', () =&gt; {\n    expect(cartTotal([{ pricePence: 2000, qty: 2 }, { pricePence: 1500, qty: 1 }])).toBe(5500);\n  });\n\n  it('returns 0 for an empty cart', () =&gt; {\n    expect(cartTotal([])).toBe(0);\n  });\n\n  it('applies a percentage discount and rounds to the penny', () =&gt; {\n    expect(cartTotal([{ pricePence: 999, qty: 1 }], 10)).toBe(899);\n  });\n});</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">npx vitest          # watch mode: re-runs on every save</code></pre></div>\n<p>Each test follows <strong>Arrange, Act, Assert</strong>: set up the data, run the code, check the result.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"component-tests-with-testing-library\">Component tests with Testing Library</h2>\n<p>Test what the user sees and does, not implementation details:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"tsx\"><pre><code class=\"language-tsx\">import { render, screen } from '@testing-library/react';\nimport userEvent from '@testing-library/user-event';\nimport { Counter } from './Counter';\n\nit('increases the quantity when + is clicked', async () =&gt; {\n  render(&lt;Counter /&gt;);\n  await userEvent.click(screen.getByRole('button', { name: '+' }));\n  expect(screen.getByText('2')).toBeInTheDocument();\n});</code></pre></div>\n<p>Querying by <strong>role</strong> and <strong>accessible name</strong> (<code>getByRole('button', { name: '+' })</code>) also proves your UI is accessible.</p>\n<h2 id=\"end-to-end-tests-with-playwright\">End-to-end tests with Playwright</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">npm init playwright@latest</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\"><pre><code class=\"language-ts\">// tests/checkout.spec.ts\nimport { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';\n\ntest('a shopper can add a dress to the bag', async ({ page }) =&gt; {\n  await page.goto('/products/linen-wrap-dress');\n  await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Add to bag' }).click();\n  await page.getByRole('link', { name: /bag/i }).click();\n  await expect(page.getByText('Linen Wrap Dress')).toBeVisible();\n});</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">npx playwright test\nnpx playwright test --ui     # watch it click through, step by step</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"what-is-worth-testing\">What is worth testing</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Money, maths and dates</strong>: totals, discounts, tax, time zones. Bugs here cost real money.</li><li><strong>Business rules</strong>: who can do what, stock limits, order states.</li><li><strong>Edge cases</strong>: empty, zero, negative, huge, missing, duplicated.</li><li><strong>Every bug you fix</strong>: write a test that fails because of the bug, then fix it. It can never come back silently.</li><li><strong>Critical journeys</strong> end to end: sign up, log in, checkout.</li></ul>\n<p>Skip testing that a framework works (that React renders a div). Do not chase 100% coverage; chase confidence in the parts that matter.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Test-driven development, lightly</p><p>For tricky logic, write the test first. It forces you to decide exactly what the function should do before you get lost in how. Watch it fail, make it pass, then tidy the code with the test as a safety net.</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"Which kind of test should you have the most of?","options":["Unit tests: fast and focused","End-to-end tests","Manual tests"],"answer":0,"explain":"Unit tests are cheap and fast. E2E tests are valuable but slow, so keep a few for critical paths."},{"q":"You fix a bug where discounts rounded the wrong way. What should you also do?","options":["Nothing more, it is fixed","Add a test that reproduces the bug, so it can never return unnoticed","Delete the old tests"],"answer":1,"explain":"Regression tests lock in every fix."},{"q":"Why query elements by role and name in component tests?","options":["It is faster","It tests what users experience and checks accessibility at the same time","CSS classes are not allowed in tests"],"answer":1,"explain":"If a screen reader cannot find the button by role and name, neither can your test."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Write a unit test","file":"cart.test.ts","lang":"ts","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write a Vitest test named <code>'returns 0 for an empty cart'</code> that expects <code>cartTotal([])</code> to be <code>0</code>. Three lines.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"it('returns 0 for an empty cart', () => {\n  expect(cartTotal([])).toBe(0);\n});","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*it\\(\\s*['\"]returns 0 for an empty cart['\"]\\s*,","text":"Line 1 starts <code>it('returns 0 for an empty cart',</code>"},{"re":",\\s*\\(\\s*\\)\\s*=>\\s*\\{","text":"then an arrow function: <code>() =&gt; {</code>"},{"re":"expect\\(\\s*cartTotal\\(\\s*\\[\\s*\\]\\s*\\)\\s*\\)\\.toBe\\(\\s*0\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>expect(cartTotal([])).toBe(0);</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*\\}\\s*\\)\\s*;?\\s*$","text":"Line 3: <code>});</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"An end-to-end step","file":"checkout.spec.ts","lang":"ts","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Two Playwright lines: click the button whose accessible name is <code>'Add to bag'</code>, then expect the text <code>'Linen Wrap Dress'</code> to be visible.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Add to bag' }).click();\nawait expect(page.getByText('Linen Wrap Dress')).toBeVisible();","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"await\\s+page\\.getByRole\\(\\s*['\"]button['\"]\\s*,","text":"Line 1 starts <code>await page.getByRole('button',</code>"},{"re":"\\{\\s*name\\s*:\\s*['\"]Add to bag['\"]\\s*\\}\\s*\\)\\.click\\(\\s*\\)","text":"then <code>{ name: 'Add to bag' }).click();</code>"},{"re":"await\\s+expect\\(\\s*page\\.getByText\\(\\s*['\"]Linen Wrap Dress['\"]\\s*\\)\\s*\\)\\.toBeVisible\\(\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>await expect(page.getByText('Linen Wrap Dress')).toBeVisible();</code>"}],"explain":""}],"text":"Why test Tests let you change code without fear. Without them, every change risks breaking something you will only hear about from an angry customer. With them, you refactor freely and ship faster. Senior developers write tests not because they are told to, but because it saves them time. The testing pyramid Type Tests Speed How many Unit One function in isolation Milliseconds Many Component / integration Several pieces together (a component with its logic) Fast Some End-to-end (E2E) The real app in a real browser, like a user Seconds A few, for critical journeys Unit tests with Vitest npm install -D vitest // src/lib/cart.ts export type Item = { pricePence: number; qty: number }; export function cartTotal(items: Item[], discountPercent = 0): number { const subtotal = items.reduce((sum, i) = sum + i.pricePence * i.qty, 0); return Math.round(subtotal * (1 - discountPercent / 100)); } // src/lib/cart.test.ts import { describe, it, expect } from 'vitest'; import { cartTotal } from './cart'; describe('cartTotal', () = { it('adds up price times quantity', () = { expect(cartTotal([{ pricePence: 2000, qty: 2 }, { pricePence: 1500, qty: 1 }])).toBe(5500); }); it('returns 0 for an empty cart', () = { expect(cartTotal([])).toBe(0); }); it('applies a percentage discount and rounds to the penny', () = { expect(cartTotal([{ pricePence: 999, qty: 1 }], 10)).toBe(899); }); }); npx vitest # watch mode: re-runs on every save Each test follows Arrange, Act, Assert : set up the data, run the code, check the result. Component tests with Testing Library Test what the user sees and does, not implementation details: import { render, screen } from '@testing-library/react'; import userEvent from '@testing-library/user-event'; import { Counter } from './Counter'; it('increases the quantity when + is clicked', async () = { render( Counter / ); await userEvent.click(screen.getByRole('button', { name: '+' })); expect(screen.getByText('2')).toBeInTheDocument(); }); Querying by role and accessible name ( getByRole('button', { name: '+' }) ) also proves your UI is accessible. End-to-end tests with Playwright npm init playwright@latest // tests/checkout.spec.ts import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test'; test('a shopper can add a dress to the bag', async ({ page }) = { await page.goto('/products/linen-wrap-dress'); await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Add to bag' }).click(); await page.getByRole('link', { name: /bag/i }).click(); await expect(page.getByText('Linen Wrap Dress')).toBeVisible(); }); npx playwright test npx playwright test --ui # watch it click through, step by step What is worth testing Money, maths and dates : totals, discounts, tax, time zones. Bugs here cost real money. Business rules : who can do what, stock limits, order states. Edge cases : empty, zero, negative, huge, missing, duplicated. Every bug you fix : write a test that fails because of the bug, then fix it. It can never come back silently. Critical journeys end to end: sign up, log in, checkout. Skip testing that a framework works (that React renders a div). Do not chase 100% coverage; chase confidence in the parts that matter. Test-driven development, lightly For tricky logic, write the test first. It forces you to decide exactly what the function should do before you get lost in how. Watch it fail, make it pass, then tidy the code with the test as a safety net.","track":"web"},{"id":"web-performance","title":"Performance and Core Web Vitals","level":"advanced","minutes":30,"summary":"Measure what users feel, then fix images, fonts, JavaScript and loading order to make sites fast.","html":"<h2 id=\"why-speed-sells\">Why speed sells</h2>\n<p>Slow sites lose visitors and sales. Shoppers abandon pages that take more than a few seconds to load, and Google uses page experience signals in rankings. For brand and e-commerce clients, performance work has a direct, measurable return, which makes it an excellent service to sell.</p>\n<h2 id=\"measure-first-core-web-vitals\">Measure first: Core Web Vitals</h2>\n<p>Google's three user-centred metrics, measured on real visitors:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Measures</th><th>Good</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>LCP</strong> (Largest Contentful Paint)</td><td>When the main content (often the hero image) appears</td><td>Under 2.5 s</td></tr><tr><td><strong>INP</strong> (Interaction to Next Paint)</td><td>How quickly the page responds to taps and clicks</td><td>Under 200 ms</td></tr><tr><td><strong>CLS</strong> (Cumulative Layout Shift)</td><td>How much things jump around while loading</td><td>Under 0.1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Tools: Chrome DevTools <strong>Lighthouse</strong> and <strong>Performance</strong> panels (lab data), and <strong>PageSpeed Insights</strong> (pagespeed.web.dev), which also shows real-user data for popular sites. Always test with mobile throttling: your users are not on your laptop's Wi-Fi.</p>\n<h2 id=\"fix-1-images-usually-the-biggest-win\">Fix 1: images (usually the biggest win)</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"html\"><pre><code class=\"language-html\">&lt;!-- the hero: load it early, at high priority --&gt;\n&lt;img src=&quot;/hero-1600.avif&quot;\n     srcset=&quot;/hero-800.avif 800w, /hero-1600.avif 1600w&quot;\n     sizes=&quot;100vw&quot;\n     width=&quot;1600&quot; height=&quot;900&quot;\n     alt=&quot;Model in the sage wrap dress on a London street&quot;\n     fetchpriority=&quot;high&quot;&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- everything below the fold: lazy --&gt;\n&lt;img src=&quot;/look-2.webp&quot; width=&quot;800&quot; height=&quot;1000&quot; alt=&quot;...&quot; loading=&quot;lazy&quot; decoding=&quot;async&quot;&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<ul><li>Use modern formats: <strong>AVIF</strong> or <strong>WebP</strong> are typically far smaller than JPEG or PNG.</li><li>Serve the right size with <code>srcset</code> and <code>sizes</code>. A phone does not need a 4000px image.</li><li>Always set <code>width</code> and <code>height</code> (or <code>aspect-ratio</code>) to prevent layout shift.</li><li>Never lazy-load the LCP image; give it <code>fetchpriority=&quot;high&quot;</code>.</li><li>Next.js <code>&lt;Image&gt;</code> and services like Cloudinary or Shopify's CDN do most of this automatically.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"fix-2-fonts\">Fix 2: fonts</h2>\n<ul><li>Use one or two families, and only the weights you need.</li><li>Self-host or use <code>next/font</code>, and set <code>font-display: swap</code> so text shows immediately.</li><li>Preload the main font file.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"fix-3-javascript\">Fix 3: JavaScript</h2>\n<p>JavaScript is the most expensive byte: it must be downloaded, parsed and executed, which blocks interactions (hurting INP).</p>\n<ul><li>Ship less: question every dependency (<code>npx vite-bundle-visualizer</code> shows what is big).</li><li>Split code: load heavy components only when needed with <code>import()</code> or <code>React.lazy</code>.</li><li>Keep work off the main thread: break long tasks up, debounce input handlers.</li><li>Audit third-party scripts (chat widgets, trackers, review widgets). They are often the slowest thing on a shop site; load them late or remove them.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"fix-4-loading-order-and-caching\">Fix 4: loading order and caching</h2>\n<ul><li>Put scripts at the end or use <code>defer</code> / <code>type=&quot;module&quot;</code>.</li><li>Inline small critical CSS; avoid huge render-blocking stylesheets.</li><li>Use a CDN (Vercel, Netlify and Cloudflare do this by default).</li><li>Cache static assets for a long time with hashed file names (<code>app.3f9a1c.js</code>), which build tools generate.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"fix-5-layout-stability\">Fix 5: layout stability</h2>\n<ul><li>Reserve space for images, embeds and ads.</li><li>Do not insert banners above existing content after load.</li><li>Use <code>transform</code> for animations, not properties like <code>top</code> or <code>height</code>.</li></ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-pro\"><p class=\"callout-title\">The performance audit as a service</p><p>Run PageSpeed Insights on a prospect's site, screenshot the mobile score, then list three specific fixes with estimated impact (&quot;Hero image is 2.4 MB; converting to AVIF at the right size should cut LCP by about 2 seconds&quot;). Concrete numbers win clients far more often than vague promises.</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"Which metric measures when the main content, often the hero image, becomes visible?","options":["LCP","CLS","INP"],"answer":0,"explain":"Largest Contentful Paint. Good is under 2.5 seconds."},{"q":"Products jump down the page when a banner loads in. Which metric suffers?","options":["LCP","CLS","INP"],"answer":1,"explain":"Cumulative Layout Shift measures unexpected movement. Reserve space for late content."},{"q":"Should the hero image use loading=&quot;lazy&quot;?","options":["Yes, lazy loading is always faster","No: the LCP image should load immediately with fetchpriority=&quot;high&quot;","Only on mobile"],"answer":1,"explain":"Lazy-loading the main image delays LCP. Lazy-load only below-the-fold images."},{"q":"What usually gives the biggest performance win on image-heavy shop sites?","options":["Minifying HTML","Serving correctly sized, modern-format images","Removing CSS comments"],"answer":1,"explain":"Images are typically most of the page weight."}],"exercises":[{"title":"A fast hero image","file":"hero.html","lang":"html","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write the hero <code>img</code> with attributes in this order: <code>src=&quot;/hero.avif&quot;</code>, <code>width=&quot;1600&quot;</code>, <code>height=&quot;900&quot;</code>, <code>alt=&quot;Summer collection on a London street&quot;</code>, <code>fetchpriority=&quot;high&quot;</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"<img src=\"/hero.avif\" width=\"1600\" height=\"900\" alt=\"Summer collection on a London street\" fetchpriority=\"high\">","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"<img\\s+src=[\"']/hero\\.avif[\"']","text":"Start <code>&lt;img src=&quot;/hero.avif&quot;</code>"},{"re":"width=[\"']1600[\"']\\s+height=[\"']900[\"']","text":"then <code>width=&quot;1600&quot; height=&quot;900&quot;</code> (stops layout shift)."},{"re":"alt=[\"']Summer collection on a London street[\"']","text":"then the alt text."},{"re":"fetchpriority=[\"']high[\"']\\s*/?>\\s*$","text":"finally <code>fetchpriority=&quot;high&quot;&gt;</code> (the main image loads first, never lazily)."}],"explain":""},{"title":"Fonts that never block text","file":"fonts.css","lang":"css","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write an <code>@font-face</code> rule: <code>font-family: 'Brand';</code>, <code>src: url('/fonts/brand.woff2') format('woff2');</code> and <code>font-display: swap;</code></p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"@font-face {\n  font-family: 'Brand';\n  src: url('/fonts/brand.woff2') format('woff2');\n  font-display: swap;\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*@font-face\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>@font-face {</code>"},{"re":"font-family\\s*:\\s*['\"]Brand['\"]","text":"<code>font-family: 'Brand';</code>"},{"re":"src\\s*:\\s*url\\(\\s*['\"]/fonts/brand\\.woff2['\"]\\s*\\)\\s*format\\(\\s*['\"]woff2['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"<code>src: url('/fonts/brand.woff2') format('woff2');</code>"},{"re":"font-display\\s*:\\s*swap","text":"<code>font-display: swap;</code> shows fallback text immediately, then swaps in the font."}],"explain":""}],"text":"Why speed sells Slow sites lose visitors and sales. Shoppers abandon pages that take more than a few seconds to load, and Google uses page experience signals in rankings. For brand and e-commerce clients, performance work has a direct, measurable return, which makes it an excellent service to sell. Measure first: Core Web Vitals Google's three user-centred metrics, measured on real visitors: Metric Measures Good LCP (Largest Contentful Paint) When the main content (often the hero image) appears Under 2.5 s INP (Interaction to Next Paint) How quickly the page responds to taps and clicks Under 200 ms CLS (Cumulative Layout Shift) How much things jump around while loading Under 0.1 Tools: Chrome DevTools Lighthouse and Performance panels (lab data), and PageSpeed Insights (pagespeed.web.dev), which also shows real-user data for popular sites. Always test with mobile throttling: your users are not on your laptop's Wi-Fi. Fix 1: images (usually the biggest win) !-- the hero: load it early, at high priority -- img src= /hero-1600.avif srcset= /hero-800.avif 800w, /hero-1600.avif 1600w sizes= 100vw width= 1600 height= 900 alt= Model in the sage wrap dress on a London street fetchpriority= high !-- everything below the fold: lazy -- img src= /look-2.webp width= 800 height= 1000 alt= ... loading= lazy decoding= async Use modern formats: AVIF or WebP are typically far smaller than JPEG or PNG. Serve the right size with srcset and sizes . A phone does not need a 4000px image. Always set width and height (or aspect-ratio ) to prevent layout shift. Never lazy-load the LCP image; give it fetchpriority= high . Next.js Image and services like Cloudinary or Shopify's CDN do most of this automatically. Fix 2: fonts Use one or two families, and only the weights you need. Self-host or use next/font , and set font-display: swap so text shows immediately. Preload the main font file. Fix 3: JavaScript JavaScript is the most expensive byte: it must be downloaded, parsed and executed, which blocks interactions (hurting INP). Ship less: question every dependency ( npx vite-bundle-visualizer shows what is big). Split code: load heavy components only when needed with import() or React.lazy . Keep work off the main thread: break long tasks up, debounce input handlers. Audit third-party scripts (chat widgets, trackers, review widgets). They are often the slowest thing on a shop site; load them late or remove them. Fix 4: loading order and caching Put scripts at the end or use defer / type= module . Inline small critical CSS; avoid huge render-blocking stylesheets. Use a CDN (Vercel, Netlify and Cloudflare do this by default). Cache static assets for a long time with hashed file names ( app.3f9a1c.js ), which build tools generate. Fix 5: layout stability Reserve space for images, embeds and ads. Do not insert banners above existing content after load. Use transform for animations, not properties like top or height . The performance audit as a service Run PageSpeed Insights on a prospect's site, screenshot the mobile score, then list three specific fixes with estimated impact ( Hero image is 2.4 MB; converting to AVIF at the right size should cut LCP by about 2 seconds ). Concrete numbers win clients far more often than vague promises.","track":"web"},{"id":"crypto-intro","title":"Cryptography 101","level":"beginner","minutes":20,"summary":"What cryptography protects, the four building blocks behind HTTPS, WhatsApp and every blockchain, and the one rule you must never break.","html":"<h2 id=\"what-cryptography-is-for\">What cryptography is for</h2>\n<p>Cryptography uses maths to protect information from people who should not read it or change it. It gives you four guarantees:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Goal</th><th>Question it answers</th><th>Everyday example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Confidentiality</strong></td><td>Can anyone else read this?</td><td>Your card number over HTTPS</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Integrity</strong></td><td>Has this been changed?</td><td>Checking a downloaded file is not tampered with</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Authenticity</strong></td><td>Who really sent this?</td><td>Your bank's website certificate</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Non-repudiation</strong></td><td>Can the sender deny it later?</td><td>Signing a blockchain transaction</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"the-four-building-blocks\">The four building blocks</h2>\n<h3 id=\"1-hash-functions\">1. Hash functions</h3>\n<p>Turn any data into a short, fixed-size fingerprint. One-way: you cannot get the data back. Used for integrity, passwords and linking blocks in a blockchain. Next lesson, plus the <a href=\"#hash-lab\">Hash Lab</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"2-symmetric-encryption\">2. Symmetric encryption</h3>\n<p>One shared secret key locks and unlocks. Fast, used for bulk data. The standard is <strong>AES</strong> (in GCM mode, which also detects tampering). The hard part: how do two people agree a secret key over the internet without anyone overhearing?</p>\n<h3 id=\"3-asymmetric-public-key-cryptography\">3. Asymmetric (public-key) cryptography</h3>\n<p>Each person has a <strong>key pair</strong>: a <strong>private key</strong> they keep secret and a <strong>public key</strong> they share. What one key does, only the other can undo. This solves the key-sharing problem and enables signatures.</p>\n<h3 id=\"4-digital-signatures\">4. Digital signatures</h3>\n<p>Sign with your private key; anyone verifies with your public key. Proves who sent a message and that it was not altered. <strong>Every blockchain transaction is a digital signature.</strong></p>\n<p>Real systems combine them. HTTPS uses public-key crypto to agree a shared key, symmetric encryption for the data, hashes for integrity, and signed certificates for authenticity.</p>\n<h2 id=\"try-encryption-in-your-browser\">Try encryption in your browser</h2>\n<p>The Web Crypto API is built into every modern browser. This encrypts and decrypts a message with AES-GCM:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-try=\"1\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const enc = new TextEncoder();\nconst dec = new TextDecoder();\n\n// 1. Make a random 256-bit key (in real life, store or derive this safely)\nconst key = await crypto.subtle.generateKey({ name: 'AES-GCM', length: 256 }, true, ['encrypt', 'decrypt']);\n\n// 2. A fresh random &quot;nonce&quot; (iv) for every message. Never reuse one with the same key.\nconst iv = crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(12));\n\n// 3. Encrypt\nconst secret = enc.encode('Launch date: 14 March');\nconst ciphertext = await crypto.subtle.encrypt({ name: 'AES-GCM', iv }, key, secret);\nconsole.log('Encrypted bytes:', [...new Uint8Array(ciphertext)].slice(0, 16).join(','), '...');\n\n// 4. Decrypt with the same key and iv\nconst plain = await crypto.subtle.decrypt({ name: 'AES-GCM', iv }, key, ciphertext);\nconsole.log('Decrypted:', dec.decode(plain));</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"randomness-matters\">Randomness matters</h2>\n<p>Keys must be unpredictable. Use <code>crypto.getRandomValues()</code> or <code>crypto.randomUUID()</code>. <strong>Never</strong> use <code>Math.random()</code> for anything security-related; it is predictable.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"kerckhoffs-s-principle\">Kerckhoffs's principle</h2>\n<p>A system must be secure <strong>even if everything about it except the key is public</strong>. Good algorithms (AES, SHA-256, Ed25519) are published and studied by thousands of experts. Secret, home-made algorithms are almost always broken.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">The one rule: do not roll your own crypto</p><p>Never invent your own algorithm, and avoid implementing standard ones yourself for real use. Subtle mistakes (reused nonces, timing leaks, bad randomness) break systems completely. Use well-reviewed libraries: the Web Crypto API, libsodium, @noble/hashes and @noble/curves, OpenZeppelin for contracts. (The SHA-256 in this site's Hash Lab is hand-written for learning only.)</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"Which building block lets anyone verify that a message really came from you?","options":["Symmetric encryption","Digital signatures","Hash functions alone"],"answer":1,"explain":"You sign with your private key; anyone checks with your public key."},{"q":"What is the main weakness of symmetric encryption on its own?","options":["It is too slow","Both sides need the same secret key, and sharing it safely is hard","It cannot encrypt text"],"answer":1,"explain":"Public-key cryptography solves key exchange; symmetric encryption then does the fast bulk work."},{"q":"Which should you use to generate a secret key in JavaScript?","options":["Math.random()","crypto.getRandomValues() or crypto.subtle.generateKey()","Date.now()"],"answer":1,"explain":"Only cryptographically secure randomness is unpredictable enough for keys."},{"q":"Why are published algorithms like AES trusted more than secret ones?","options":["Thousands of experts have tried to break them and failed","Secret algorithms are illegal","Published algorithms are faster"],"answer":0,"explain":"Kerckhoffs's principle: security should rest on the key, not on hiding the method."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Encrypt and decrypt with AES-GCM","file":"aes.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Using the example above as your guide, write five lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const key = await crypto.subtle.generateKey({ name: 'AES-GCM', length: 256 }, true, ['encrypt', 'decrypt']);</code></li><li><code>const iv = crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(12));</code></li><li><code>const secret = new TextEncoder().encode('Launch date: 14 March');</code></li><li><code>const box = await crypto.subtle.encrypt({ name: 'AES-GCM', iv }, key, secret);</code></li><li><code>console.log(new TextDecoder().decode(await crypto.subtle.decrypt({ name: 'AES-GCM', iv }, key, box)));</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const key = await crypto.subtle.generateKey({ name: 'AES-GCM', length: 256 }, true, ['encrypt', 'decrypt']);\nconst iv = crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(12));\nconst secret = new TextEncoder().encode('Launch date: 14 March');\nconst box = await crypto.subtle.encrypt({ name: 'AES-GCM', iv }, key, secret);\nconsole.log(new TextDecoder().decode(await crypto.subtle.decrypt({ name: 'AES-GCM', iv }, key, box)));","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"crypto\\.subtle\\.generateKey\\(\\s*\\{\\s*name\\s*:\\s*['\"]AES-GCM['\"]\\s*,\\s*length\\s*:\\s*256\\s*\\}","text":"Line 1 calls <code>crypto.subtle.generateKey({ name: 'AES-GCM', length: 256 }, ...)</code>"},{"re":"\\[\\s*['\"]encrypt['\"]\\s*,\\s*['\"]decrypt['\"]\\s*\\]","text":"The key's allowed uses go in an array: <code>['encrypt', 'decrypt']</code>"},{"re":"const\\s+iv\\s*=\\s*crypto\\.getRandomValues\\(\\s*new\\s+Uint8Array\\(\\s*12\\s*\\)\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: a fresh 12-byte nonce: <code>const iv = crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(12));</code>"},{"re":"new\\s+TextEncoder\\(\\s*\\)\\.encode\\(\\s*['\"]Launch date: 14 March['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3 turns text into bytes with <code>new TextEncoder().encode(...)</code>."},{"re":"const\\s+box\\s*=\\s*await\\s+crypto\\.subtle\\.encrypt\\(\\s*\\{\\s*name\\s*:\\s*['\"]AES-GCM['\"]\\s*,\\s*iv\\s*\\}\\s*,\\s*key\\s*,\\s*secret\\s*\\)","text":"Line 4: <code>const box = await crypto.subtle.encrypt({ name: 'AES-GCM', iv }, key, secret);</code>"},{"re":"crypto\\.subtle\\.decrypt\\(\\s*\\{\\s*name\\s*:\\s*['\"]AES-GCM['\"]\\s*,\\s*iv\\s*\\}\\s*,\\s*key\\s*,\\s*box\\s*\\)","text":"Line 5 decrypts with the same key and iv: <code>crypto.subtle.decrypt({ name: 'AES-GCM', iv }, key, box)</code>, wrapped in <code>new TextDecoder().decode(await ...)</code>."}],"explain":"<p><code>box</code> is unreadable bytes. Only the same key and nonce turn it back into the message. Change one byte of <code>box</code> and decryption throws an error instead: GCM mode detects tampering, giving you integrity as well as confidentiality.</p>"},{"title":"Generate secure random values","file":"random.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Make a 16-byte random key and a random ID the safe way. Three lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const key = crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(16));</code></li><li><code>console.log(Array.from(key));</code></li><li><code>console.log(crypto.randomUUID());</code></li></ol>\n<p>Run it a few times: every result is different and unpredictable.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const key = crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(16));\nconsole.log(Array.from(key));\nconsole.log(crypto.randomUUID());","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+key\\s*=\\s*crypto\\.getRandomValues\\(","text":"Line 1 starts <code>const key = crypto.getRandomValues(</code>"},{"re":"new\\s+Uint8Array\\(\\s*16\\s*\\)","text":"Inside: <code>new Uint8Array(16)</code>: an empty box of 16 bytes for it to fill."},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*Array\\.from\\(\\s*key\\s*\\)\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>console.log(Array.from(key));</code>"},{"re":"crypto\\.randomUUID\\(\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3: <code>console.log(crypto.randomUUID());</code> (UUID in capitals)."}],"explain":"<p>16 bytes is 128 bits of randomness, the size of an AES-128 key. <code>Math.random()</code> would look random but is predictable; these functions use the operating system's cryptographically secure generator.</p>"}],"text":"What cryptography is for Cryptography uses maths to protect information from people who should not read it or change it. It gives you four guarantees: Goal Question it answers Everyday example Confidentiality Can anyone else read this? Your card number over HTTPS Integrity Has this been changed? Checking a downloaded file is not tampered with Authenticity Who really sent this? Your bank's website certificate Non-repudiation Can the sender deny it later? Signing a blockchain transaction The four building blocks 1. Hash functions Turn any data into a short, fixed-size fingerprint. One-way: you cannot get the data back. Used for integrity, passwords and linking blocks in a blockchain. Next lesson, plus the Hash Lab . 2. Symmetric encryption One shared secret key locks and unlocks. Fast, used for bulk data. The standard is AES (in GCM mode, which also detects tampering). The hard part: how do two people agree a secret key over the internet without anyone overhearing? 3. Asymmetric (public-key) cryptography Each person has a key pair : a private key they keep secret and a public key they share. What one key does, only the other can undo. This solves the key-sharing problem and enables signatures. 4. Digital signatures Sign with your private key; anyone verifies with your public key. Proves who sent a message and that it was not altered. Every blockchain transaction is a digital signature. Real systems combine them. HTTPS uses public-key crypto to agree a shared key, symmetric encryption for the data, hashes for integrity, and signed certificates for authenticity. Try encryption in your browser The Web Crypto API is built into every modern browser. This encrypts and decrypts a message with AES-GCM: const enc = new TextEncoder(); const dec = new TextDecoder(); // 1. Make a random 256-bit key (in real life, store or derive this safely) const key = await crypto.subtle.generateKey({ name: 'AES-GCM', length: 256 }, true, ['encrypt', 'decrypt']); // 2. A fresh random nonce (iv) for every message. Never reuse one with the same key. const iv = crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(12)); // 3. Encrypt const secret = enc.encode('Launch date: 14 March'); const ciphertext = await crypto.subtle.encrypt({ name: 'AES-GCM', iv }, key, secret); console.log('Encrypted bytes:', [...new Uint8Array(ciphertext)].slice(0, 16).join(','), '...'); // 4. Decrypt with the same key and iv const plain = await crypto.subtle.decrypt({ name: 'AES-GCM', iv }, key, ciphertext); console.log('Decrypted:', dec.decode(plain)); Randomness matters Keys must be unpredictable. Use crypto.getRandomValues() or crypto.randomUUID() . Never use Math.random() for anything security-related; it is predictable. Kerckhoffs's principle A system must be secure even if everything about it except the key is public . Good algorithms (AES, SHA-256, Ed25519) are published and studied by thousands of experts. Secret, home-made algorithms are almost always broken. The one rule: do not roll your own crypto Never invent your own algorithm, and avoid implementing standard ones yourself for real use. Subtle mistakes (reused nonces, timing leaks, bad randomness) break systems completely. Use well-reviewed libraries: the Web Crypto API, libsodium, @noble/hashes and @noble/curves, OpenZeppelin for contracts. (The SHA-256 in this site's Hash Lab is hand-written for learning only.)","track":"crypto"},{"id":"crypto-hashing","title":"Hash functions hands-on","level":"beginner","minutes":20,"summary":"The properties that make SHA-256 and Keccak-256 secure fingerprints, and where hashes are used everywhere.","html":"<h2 id=\"a-digital-fingerprint\">A digital fingerprint</h2>\n<p>A <strong>hash function</strong> takes input of any size and returns a fixed-size output called a <strong>hash</strong> or <strong>digest</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">SHA-256(&quot;hello&quot;)  = 2cf24dba5fb0a30e26e83b2ac5b9e29e1b161e5c1fa7425e73043362938b9824\nSHA-256(&quot;Hello&quot;)  = 185f8db32271fe25f561a6fc938b2e264306ec304eda518007d1764826381969</code></pre></div>\n<p>Always 256 bits, shown as 64 hexadecimal characters (each hex character is 4 bits), whether the input is one letter or a whole film.</p>\n<p>Open the <a href=\"#hash-lab\">Hash Lab</a> in another tab and type as you read.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-properties-that-matter\">The properties that matter</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Property</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Deterministic</strong></td><td>Same input, same hash, every time</td><td>Anyone can re-check it</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Fast to compute</strong></td><td>Milliseconds for normal data</td><td>Practical to use everywhere</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pre-image resistant</strong></td><td>Given a hash, you cannot find an input that produces it</td><td>One-way: hashes do not reveal data</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Collision resistant</strong></td><td>You cannot find two different inputs with the same hash</td><td>A hash uniquely identifies data in practice</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Avalanche effect</strong></td><td>Change one bit of input and about half the output bits flip</td><td>No patterns to exploit</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"where-hashes-are-used\">Where hashes are used</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>File integrity</strong>: download pages publish a SHA-256. Hash your download; if it matches, nobody tampered with it.</li><li><strong>Git</strong>: every commit is identified by a hash of its contents and its parent's hash.</li><li><strong>Blockchains</strong>: each block contains the hash of the previous block. Change history and every later hash breaks.</li><li><strong>Proof of work</strong>: miners search for an input whose hash starts with many zeros.</li><li><strong>Merkle trees</strong>: summarise thousands of items in one hash, with tiny proofs that an item is included.</li><li><strong>Commitments</strong>: publish a hash of your answer now, reveal the answer later; everyone can check you did not change it.</li><li><strong>Ethereum</strong> uses <strong>Keccak-256</strong> for addresses, function selectors and storage locations.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"hashing-in-code\">Hashing in code</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-try=\"1\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">async function sha256Hex(text) {\n  const bytes = new TextEncoder().encode(text);\n  const digest = await crypto.subtle.digest('SHA-256', bytes);\n  return Array.from(new Uint8Array(digest), (b) =&gt; b.toString(16).padStart(2, '0')).join('');\n}\n\nconsole.log(await sha256Hex('hello'));\nconsole.log(await sha256Hex('hello '));   // one extra space: a completely different hash</code></pre></div>\n<p>In Solidity:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"solidity\"><pre><code class=\"language-solidity\">bytes32 h = keccak256(abi.encodePacked(&quot;hello&quot;));</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"hashes-are-not-encryption\">Hashes are not encryption</h2>\n<p>There is no key and no way back. That makes hashes perfect for checking, not for hiding data you need to read again.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Passwords need slow hashes</p><p>SHA-256 is designed to be fast, which is exactly wrong for passwords: attackers can try billions of guesses per second. Passwords need deliberately slow, salted algorithms like <strong>Argon2id</strong> or <strong>bcrypt</strong>.</p></aside>\n<h2 id=\"broken-hashes\">Broken hashes</h2>\n<p><strong>MD5</strong> and <strong>SHA-1</strong> have practical collision attacks. Do not use them for security. Modern choices: SHA-256, SHA-3 / Keccak-256, BLAKE2 and BLAKE3.</p>","quiz":[{"q":"You change one letter of a 10-page document. What happens to its SHA-256 hash?","options":["One character of the hash changes","It becomes completely different, with about half the bits flipped","It stays the same"],"answer":1,"explain":"The avalanche effect makes every change produce an unpredictable new hash."},{"q":"What does pre-image resistance mean?","options":["Given a hash, you cannot work out an input that produces it","Two inputs can never have the same hash","Hashes are always 256 bits"],"answer":0,"explain":"That one-way property is why hashes can be published safely."},{"q":"Which hash function does Ethereum use for addresses and function selectors?","options":["MD5","SHA-1","Keccak-256"],"answer":2,"explain":"Ethereum uses Keccak-256, a close relative of the SHA-3 standard."},{"q":"Why is plain SHA-256 a bad choice for storing passwords?","options":["It is too slow","It is so fast that attackers can try billions of guesses per second","It produces collisions"],"answer":1,"explain":"Password storage needs slow, salted algorithms like Argon2id or bcrypt."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Hash text with SHA-256","file":"hash.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Four lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const bytes = new TextEncoder().encode('hello');</code></li><li><code>const digest = await crypto.subtle.digest('SHA-256', bytes);</code></li><li><code>const hex = Array.from(new Uint8Array(digest), (b) =&gt; b.toString(16).padStart(2, '0')).join('');</code></li><li><code>console.log(hex);</code></li></ol>\n<p>The output should start with <code>2cf24dba</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const bytes = new TextEncoder().encode('hello');\nconst digest = await crypto.subtle.digest('SHA-256', bytes);\nconst hex = Array.from(new Uint8Array(digest), (b) => b.toString(16).padStart(2, '0')).join('');\nconsole.log(hex);","also":["const bytes = new TextEncoder().encode('hello');\nconst digest = await crypto.subtle.digest('SHA-256', bytes);\nconst hex = Array.from(new Uint8Array(digest), b => b.toString(16).padStart(2, '0')).join('');\nconsole.log(hex);"],"hints":[{"re":"new\\s+TextEncoder\\(\\s*\\)\\.encode\\(\\s*['\"]hello['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"Line 1: <code>const bytes = new TextEncoder().encode('hello');</code>"},{"re":"await\\s+crypto\\.subtle\\.digest\\(\\s*['\"]SHA-256['\"]\\s*,\\s*bytes\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>const digest = await crypto.subtle.digest('SHA-256', bytes);</code>"},{"re":"Array\\.from\\(\\s*new\\s+Uint8Array\\(\\s*digest\\s*\\)\\s*,","text":"Line 3 starts <code>const hex = Array.from(new Uint8Array(digest),</code>"},{"re":"b\\.toString\\(\\s*16\\s*\\)\\.padStart\\(\\s*2\\s*,\\s*['\"]0['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"Each byte becomes two hex characters: <code>b.toString(16).padStart(2, '0')</code>"},{"re":"\\)\\.join\\(\\s*['\"]['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"Join them with an empty string: <code>.join('')</code>"}],"explain":"<p><code>padStart(2, '0')</code> matters: the byte 10 is <code>a</code> in hex, but it must be written <code>0a</code>. Without padding, some hashes would come out shorter than 64 characters and would never match.</p>"},{"title":"Hash in Solidity","file":"Hash.sol","lang":"solidity","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write one Solidity line: a <code>bytes32</code> called <code>h</code> equal to <code>keccak256(abi.encodePacked(&quot;hello&quot;))</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"bytes32 h = keccak256(abi.encodePacked(\"hello\"));","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*bytes32\\s+h\\s*=","text":"Start with the type and name: <code>bytes32 h =</code>"},{"re":"keccak256\\(\\s*abi\\.encodePacked\\(\\s*[\"']hello[\"']\\s*\\)\\s*\\)","text":"<code>keccak256(abi.encodePacked(&quot;hello&quot;))</code>"},{"re":"\\)\\s*;\\s*$","text":"Solidity needs a semicolon at the end."}],"explain":""}],"text":"A digital fingerprint A hash function takes input of any size and returns a fixed-size output called a hash or digest . SHA-256( hello ) = 2cf24dba5fb0a30e26e83b2ac5b9e29e1b161e5c1fa7425e73043362938b9824 SHA-256( Hello ) = 185f8db32271fe25f561a6fc938b2e264306ec304eda518007d1764826381969 Always 256 bits, shown as 64 hexadecimal characters (each hex character is 4 bits), whether the input is one letter or a whole film. Open the Hash Lab in another tab and type as you read. The properties that matter Property Meaning Why it matters Deterministic Same input, same hash, every time Anyone can re-check it Fast to compute Milliseconds for normal data Practical to use everywhere Pre-image resistant Given a hash, you cannot find an input that produces it One-way: hashes do not reveal data Collision resistant You cannot find two different inputs with the same hash A hash uniquely identifies data in practice Avalanche effect Change one bit of input and about half the output bits flip No patterns to exploit Where hashes are used File integrity : download pages publish a SHA-256. Hash your download; if it matches, nobody tampered with it. Git : every commit is identified by a hash of its contents and its parent's hash. Blockchains : each block contains the hash of the previous block. Change history and every later hash breaks. Proof of work : miners search for an input whose hash starts with many zeros. Merkle trees : summarise thousands of items in one hash, with tiny proofs that an item is included. Commitments : publish a hash of your answer now, reveal the answer later; everyone can check you did not change it. Ethereum uses Keccak-256 for addresses, function selectors and storage locations. Hashing in code async function sha256Hex(text) { const bytes = new TextEncoder().encode(text); const digest = await crypto.subtle.digest('SHA-256', bytes); return Array.from(new Uint8Array(digest), (b) = b.toString(16).padStart(2, '0')).join(''); } console.log(await sha256Hex('hello')); console.log(await sha256Hex('hello ')); // one extra space: a completely different hash In Solidity: bytes32 h = keccak256(abi.encodePacked( hello )); Hashes are not encryption There is no key and no way back. That makes hashes perfect for checking, not for hiding data you need to read again. Passwords need slow hashes SHA-256 is designed to be fast, which is exactly wrong for passwords: attackers can try billions of guesses per second. Passwords need deliberately slow, salted algorithms like Argon2id or bcrypt . Broken hashes MD5 and SHA-1 have practical collision attacks. Do not use them for security. Modern choices: SHA-256, SHA-3 / Keccak-256, BLAKE2 and BLAKE3.","track":"crypto"},{"id":"crypto-keys","title":"Keys, signatures and wallets","level":"beginner","minutes":25,"summary":"How a private key becomes an address, how signing works, what seed phrases really are, and how to keep funds safe.","html":"<h2 id=\"from-random-number-to-address\">From random number to address</h2>\n<p>On Ethereum (and Bitcoin), your identity is a key pair on the <strong>secp256k1</strong> elliptic curve:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Private key</strong>: a random 256-bit number. About 10^77 possibilities, so guessing one is hopeless if it is truly random.</li><li><strong>Public key</strong>: calculated from the private key with elliptic-curve multiplication. Easy forwards, practically impossible backwards.</li><li><strong>Address</strong>: the last 20 bytes of the Keccak-256 hash of the public key, written in hex: <code>0x71C7656EC7ab88b098defB751B7401B5f6d8976F</code>.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">private key  --(curve maths, one-way)--&gt;  public key  --(keccak256, last 20 bytes)--&gt;  address</code></pre></div>\n<p>Anyone can know your address. Only the private key can spend from it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"signatures\">Signatures</h2>\n<p>To send a transaction, your wallet <strong>signs</strong> it with the private key using <strong>ECDSA</strong>. Every node then checks the signature against your address. Nobody needs your private key to verify; nobody can forge a signature without it.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\"><pre><code class=\"language-ts\">// npm install viem\nimport { generatePrivateKey, privateKeyToAccount } from 'viem/accounts';\nimport { verifyMessage } from 'viem';\n\nconst privateKey = generatePrivateKey();          // a throwaway key for learning only\nconst account = privateKeyToAccount(privateKey);\nconsole.log('Address:', account.address);\n\nconst message = 'I agree to the Code Ladder terms';\nconst signature = await account.signMessage({ message });\n\nconst valid = await verifyMessage({ address: account.address, message, signature });\nconsole.log('Signature valid?', valid);                                  // true\nconsole.log(await verifyMessage({ address: account.address, message: message + '!', signature })); // false</code></pre></div>\n<p>Change one character of the message and verification fails. That is integrity plus authenticity in one step.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"seed-phrases\">Seed phrases</h2>\n<p>Managing raw private keys is error-prone, so wallets use a <strong>seed phrase</strong> (also called a recovery phrase or mnemonic): 12 or 24 words from a standard list of 2,048 (BIP-39). From that one phrase, a wallet deterministically derives a tree of private keys (BIP-32 / BIP-44). The first Ethereum account uses the path <code>m/44'/60'/0'/0/0</code>.</p>\n<p>That is why restoring your 12 words in any compatible wallet brings back every account.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Your seed phrase IS your money</p><p>Anyone who sees your seed phrase controls every account derived from it, forever. Write it on paper (or metal), store it offline, never photograph it, never type it into a website, chat, email, cloud note or code file. No legitimate support person, airdrop or &quot;wallet sync&quot; will ever ask for it. If one does, it is a scam.</p></aside>\n<h2 id=\"types-of-wallet\">Types of wallet</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>What it is</th><th>Good for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Browser or mobile wallet</strong> (MetaMask, Rabby, Rainbow)</td><td>Keys stored, encrypted, on your device</td><td>Daily use, development</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Hardware wallet</strong> (Ledger, Trezor)</td><td>Keys never leave a separate device; you confirm on its screen</td><td>Holding meaningful amounts</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Smart contract wallet</strong> (Safe, ERC-4337 wallets)</td><td>A contract controls funds, with rules like multi-signature or spending limits</td><td>Teams, treasuries, better recovery</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Custodial</strong> (exchange accounts)</td><td>Someone else holds the keys</td><td>Convenience, with counterparty risk (&quot;not your keys, not your coins&quot;)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"developer-key-hygiene\">Developer key hygiene</h2>\n<ul><li>Make a <strong>separate development wallet</strong> that only ever holds testnet tokens.</li><li>Never paste a private key with real funds into code, a <code>.env</code> file, or a terminal command.</li><li>For deployments, use Foundry's encrypted keystore (<code>cast wallet import</code>) or a hardware wallet.</li><li>Read what you are signing. Malicious sites ask for signatures that approve transfers of your tokens.</li></ul>","quiz":[{"q":"What can someone do with only your Ethereum address?","options":["Send you tokens and see your public balance and history","Spend your tokens","Work out your private key"],"answer":0,"explain":"Addresses are public by design. Only the private key can authorise spending."},{"q":"How does a node know a transaction really came from your account?","options":["You email it your private key","It verifies the transaction's signature against your address","The wallet company confirms it"],"answer":1,"explain":"Signatures prove control of the private key without revealing it."},{"q":"A &quot;support agent&quot; in a Discord DM asks for your 12-word phrase to fix a stuck transaction. What should you do?","options":["Send the first six words only","Send it, then change it afterwards","Refuse and report it: it is a scam"],"answer":2,"explain":"Nobody legitimate ever needs your seed phrase. Six words also massively weakens it."},{"q":"Why does restoring a seed phrase bring back all your accounts?","options":["All private keys are derived deterministically from the seed","The blockchain stores your accounts by seed phrase","Wallets back up keys to the cloud"],"answer":0,"explain":"BIP-32/39/44 derive a whole tree of keys from one seed."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Sign and verify a message","file":"sign.ts","lang":"ts","run":false,"prompt":"<p>With viem's functions already imported, write four lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const account = privateKeyToAccount(generatePrivateKey());</code></li><li><code>const signature = await account.signMessage({ message: 'I agree' });</code></li><li><code>const valid = await verifyMessage({ address: account.address, message: 'I agree', signature });</code></li><li><code>console.log(valid);</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const account = privateKeyToAccount(generatePrivateKey());\nconst signature = await account.signMessage({ message: 'I agree' });\nconst valid = await verifyMessage({ address: account.address, message: 'I agree', signature });\nconsole.log(valid);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"privateKeyToAccount\\(\\s*generatePrivateKey\\(\\s*\\)\\s*\\)","text":"Line 1: <code>const account = privateKeyToAccount(generatePrivateKey());</code>"},{"re":"account\\.signMessage\\(\\s*\\{\\s*message\\s*:\\s*['\"]I agree['\"]\\s*\\}\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>await account.signMessage({ message: 'I agree' })</code>"},{"re":"verifyMessage\\(\\s*\\{\\s*address\\s*:\\s*account\\.address\\s*,\\s*message\\s*:\\s*['\"]I agree['\"]\\s*,\\s*signature\\s*\\}\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3: <code>verifyMessage({ address: account.address, message: 'I agree', signature })</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*valid\\s*\\)","text":"Line 4: <code>console.log(valid);</code>"}],"explain":"<p>This needs viem installed (<code>npm install viem</code>), so there is no Run button here. It prints <code>true</code>. Change the message in line 3 and it prints <code>false</code>: the signature only matches the exact message it signed.</p>"}],"text":"From random number to address On Ethereum (and Bitcoin), your identity is a key pair on the secp256k1 elliptic curve: Private key : a random 256-bit number. About 10^77 possibilities, so guessing one is hopeless if it is truly random. Public key : calculated from the private key with elliptic-curve multiplication. Easy forwards, practically impossible backwards. Address : the last 20 bytes of the Keccak-256 hash of the public key, written in hex: 0x71C7656EC7ab88b098defB751B7401B5f6d8976F . private key --(curve maths, one-way)-- public key --(keccak256, last 20 bytes)-- address Anyone can know your address. Only the private key can spend from it. Signatures To send a transaction, your wallet signs it with the private key using ECDSA . Every node then checks the signature against your address. Nobody needs your private key to verify; nobody can forge a signature without it. // npm install viem import { generatePrivateKey, privateKeyToAccount } from 'viem/accounts'; import { verifyMessage } from 'viem'; const privateKey = generatePrivateKey(); // a throwaway key for learning only const account = privateKeyToAccount(privateKey); console.log('Address:', account.address); const message = 'I agree to the Code Ladder terms'; const signature = await account.signMessage({ message }); const valid = await verifyMessage({ address: account.address, message, signature }); console.log('Signature valid?', valid); // true console.log(await verifyMessage({ address: account.address, message: message + '!', signature })); // false Change one character of the message and verification fails. That is integrity plus authenticity in one step. Seed phrases Managing raw private keys is error-prone, so wallets use a seed phrase (also called a recovery phrase or mnemonic): 12 or 24 words from a standard list of 2,048 (BIP-39). From that one phrase, a wallet deterministically derives a tree of private keys (BIP-32 / BIP-44). The first Ethereum account uses the path m/44'/60'/0'/0/0 . That is why restoring your 12 words in any compatible wallet brings back every account. Your seed phrase IS your money Anyone who sees your seed phrase controls every account derived from it, forever. Write it on paper (or metal), store it offline, never photograph it, never type it into a website, chat, email, cloud note or code file. No legitimate support person, airdrop or wallet sync will ever ask for it. If one does, it is a scam. Types of wallet Type What it is Good for Browser or mobile wallet (MetaMask, Rabby, Rainbow) Keys stored, encrypted, on your device Daily use, development Hardware wallet (Ledger, Trezor) Keys never leave a separate device; you confirm on its screen Holding meaningful amounts Smart contract wallet (Safe, ERC-4337 wallets) A contract controls funds, with rules like multi-signature or spending limits Teams, treasuries, better recovery Custodial (exchange accounts) Someone else holds the keys Convenience, with counterparty risk ( not your keys, not your coins ) Developer key hygiene Make a separate development wallet that only ever holds testnet tokens. Never paste a private key with real funds into code, a .env file, or a terminal command. For deployments, use Foundry's encrypted keystore ( cast wallet import ) or a hardware wallet. Read what you are signing. Malicious sites ask for signatures that approve transfers of your tokens.","track":"crypto"},{"id":"crypto-blockchain","title":"How blockchains work","level":"beginner","minutes":25,"summary":"Blocks, transactions, consensus, gas and accounts, explained with Ethereum, plus your first look at a real transaction.","html":"<h2 id=\"a-shared-ledger-nobody-owns\">A shared ledger nobody owns</h2>\n<p>A blockchain is a database that thousands of independent computers (<strong>nodes</strong>) keep identical copies of. Nobody can change past entries, and anyone can verify everything. It is useful when people who do not trust each other need to agree on who owns what, without a bank or company in the middle.</p>\n<h2 id=\"blocks-chained-by-hashes\">Blocks chained by hashes</h2>\n<p>Transactions are grouped into <strong>blocks</strong>. Each block includes the hash of the previous block:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">[Block 100]          [Block 101]               [Block 102]\n prev: 0x9a..   &lt;--   prev: hash(Block 100)  &lt;--  prev: hash(Block 101)\n txs: ...             txs: ...                     txs: ...</code></pre></div>\n<p>Change one transaction in block 100 and its hash changes, which breaks block 101's link, and 102's, all the way up. Everyone would see the forgery immediately.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-life-of-a-transaction\">The life of a transaction</h2>\n<ol><li>You build a transaction (send 0.1 ETH to Bob) and your wallet <strong>signs</strong> it.</li><li>It is broadcast to the network and waits in the <strong>mempool</strong> (pending transactions).</li><li>A <strong>validator</strong> includes it in a block, choosing transactions that pay higher fees first.</li><li>The block is shared, every node checks every signature and rule, and adds it to their copy.</li><li>After more blocks build on top, it becomes practically irreversible (<strong>finality</strong>).</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"consensus-agreeing-without-a-boss\">Consensus: agreeing without a boss</h2>\n<p>How do thousands of strangers agree on the next block?</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Proof of Work</strong> (Bitcoin): miners burn electricity searching for a hash below a target (try mining in the <a href=\"#hash-lab\">Hash Lab</a>). Rewriting history would need more computing power than the honest majority.</li><li><strong>Proof of Stake</strong> (Ethereum since 2022): validators lock up ETH as collateral (32 ETH per validator). They are randomly chosen to propose blocks and lose stake (&quot;slashing&quot;) if they cheat. It uses vastly less energy than proof of work.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"ethereum-accounts\">Ethereum accounts</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Controlled by</th><th>Has code?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Externally owned account (EOA)</strong></td><td>A private key</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Contract account</strong></td><td>Its code</td><td>Yes: a smart contract</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Every EOA has a <strong>nonce</strong>: a counter of transactions sent. It stops the same signed transaction being replayed twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"units-and-gas\">Units and gas</h2>\n<p>ETH has 18 decimal places. The smallest unit is the <strong>wei</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Unit</th><th>Wei</th><th>Used for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>wei</td><td>1</td><td>Contract maths</td></tr><tr><td>gwei</td><td>1,000,000,000 (10^9)</td><td>Gas prices</td></tr><tr><td>ether</td><td>1,000,000,000,000,000,000 (10^18)</td><td>Human amounts</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Gas</strong> measures computation. A plain ETH transfer costs 21,000 gas; complex contract calls cost more. You pay <code>gas used x gas price</code>. Since EIP-1559, each block has a <strong>base fee</strong> (burned, destroyed forever) plus an optional <strong>priority fee</strong> (tip to the validator).</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">Fee = 21,000 gas x (base 8 gwei + tip 1 gwei) = 189,000 gwei = 0.000189 ETH</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"layer-1-and-layer-2\">Layer 1 and Layer 2</h2>\n<p>Ethereum mainnet (Layer 1) is secure but limited in throughput, so fees rise when busy. <strong>Layer 2 rollups</strong> (Arbitrum, Optimism, Base, zkSync, Starknet and others) execute transactions cheaply, then post compressed data and proofs back to Ethereum. Most everyday activity now happens on L2s. Same Solidity code, same tools.</p>\n<h2 id=\"testnets\">Testnets</h2>\n<p><strong>Sepolia</strong> is Ethereum's main test network. Its ETH is free (from <strong>faucets</strong>) and worthless, so you can make every mistake there. Always deploy to a testnet first.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-try\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Read a real transaction</p><p>Go to sepolia.etherscan.io (or etherscan.io for mainnet), open any recent block, click a transaction, and find: from, to, value, gas used, gas price, the block number, and the status. Every number in this lesson is on that page.</p></aside>\n<p>You can also query the chain from your terminal once Foundry is installed:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">cast block-number --rpc-url $SEPOLIA_RPC_URL\ncast balance 0xYourAddress --ether --rpc-url $SEPOLIA_RPC_URL\ncast to-wei 0.1\ncast from-wei 189000000000000</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>","quiz":[{"q":"Why can't someone quietly edit a transaction in an old block?","options":["Old blocks are encrypted","Its hash would change and break the link in every later block, which every node checks","Only the original sender can read old blocks"],"answer":1,"explain":"Hash links make history tamper-evident."},{"q":"Ethereum's consensus mechanism since 2022 is:","options":["Proof of Work","Proof of Stake","Proof of Authority"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Merge moved Ethereum to proof of stake, cutting its energy use dramatically."},{"q":"How many wei are in 1 ether?","options":["1,000","10^9","10^18"],"answer":2,"explain":"18 decimals. Gwei (10^9 wei) is the unit used for gas prices."},{"q":"What happens to the base fee under EIP-1559?","options":["It goes to the validator","It is burned (destroyed)","It is refunded to the sender"],"answer":1,"explain":"The base fee is burned; only the priority fee (tip) goes to the validator."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Calculate a gas fee in wei","file":"fee.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Work out the fee for a plain transfer (21,000 gas) at 9 gwei, using BigInt (numbers ending in <code>n</code>). Four lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const gasUsed = 21000n;</code></li><li><code>const gasPriceWei = 9n * 10n ** 9n;</code> (9 gwei)</li><li><code>const feeWei = gasUsed * gasPriceWei;</code></li><li><code>console.log(feeWei.toString());</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const gasUsed = 21000n;\nconst gasPriceWei = 9n * 10n ** 9n;\nconst feeWei = gasUsed * gasPriceWei;\nconsole.log(feeWei.toString());","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+gasUsed\\s*=\\s*21000n","text":"Line 1: <code>const gasUsed = 21000n;</code> (the <code>n</code> makes it a BigInt)."},{"re":"const\\s+gasPriceWei\\s*=\\s*9n\\s*\\*\\s*10n\\s*\\*\\*\\s*9n","text":"Line 2: <code>const gasPriceWei = 9n * 10n ** 9n;</code>"},{"re":"const\\s+feeWei\\s*=\\s*gasUsed\\s*\\*\\s*gasPriceWei","text":"Line 3: <code>const feeWei = gasUsed * gasPriceWei;</code>"},{"re":"feeWei\\.toString\\(\\s*\\)","text":"Line 4: <code>console.log(feeWei.toString());</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Output: <code>189000000000000</code> wei, which is 0.000189 ETH. Wallets do exactly this maths with integers, then format it for display.</p>"},{"title":"Query the chain with cast","file":"Terminal","lang":"bash","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Two Foundry commands: get the latest block number using the RPC URL in <code>$SEPOLIA_RPC_URL</code>, then convert <code>0.1</code> ether to wei.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"cast block-number --rpc-url $SEPOLIA_RPC_URL\ncast to-wei 0.1","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*cast\\s+block-number\\s+--rpc-url\\s+\\$SEPOLIA_RPC_URL\\s*$","text":"Line 1: <code>cast block-number --rpc-url $SEPOLIA_RPC_URL</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*cast\\s+to-wei\\s+0\\.1\\s*$","text":"Line 2: <code>cast to-wei 0.1</code>"}],"explain":""}],"text":"A shared ledger nobody owns A blockchain is a database that thousands of independent computers ( nodes ) keep identical copies of. Nobody can change past entries, and anyone can verify everything. It is useful when people who do not trust each other need to agree on who owns what, without a bank or company in the middle. Blocks chained by hashes Transactions are grouped into blocks . Each block includes the hash of the previous block: [Block 100] [Block 101] [Block 102] prev: 0x9a.. -- prev: hash(Block 100) -- prev: hash(Block 101) txs: ... txs: ... txs: ... Change one transaction in block 100 and its hash changes, which breaks block 101's link, and 102's, all the way up. Everyone would see the forgery immediately. The life of a transaction You build a transaction (send 0.1 ETH to Bob) and your wallet signs it. It is broadcast to the network and waits in the mempool (pending transactions). A validator includes it in a block, choosing transactions that pay higher fees first. The block is shared, every node checks every signature and rule, and adds it to their copy. After more blocks build on top, it becomes practically irreversible ( finality ). Consensus: agreeing without a boss How do thousands of strangers agree on the next block? Proof of Work (Bitcoin): miners burn electricity searching for a hash below a target (try mining in the Hash Lab ). Rewriting history would need more computing power than the honest majority. Proof of Stake (Ethereum since 2022): validators lock up ETH as collateral (32 ETH per validator). They are randomly chosen to propose blocks and lose stake ( slashing ) if they cheat. It uses vastly less energy than proof of work. Ethereum accounts Type Controlled by Has code? Externally owned account (EOA) A private key No Contract account Its code Yes: a smart contract Every EOA has a nonce : a counter of transactions sent. It stops the same signed transaction being replayed twice. Units and gas ETH has 18 decimal places. The smallest unit is the wei : Unit Wei Used for wei 1 Contract maths gwei 1,000,000,000 (10^9) Gas prices ether 1,000,000,000,000,000,000 (10^18) Human amounts Gas measures computation. A plain ETH transfer costs 21,000 gas; complex contract calls cost more. You pay gas used x gas price . Since EIP-1559, each block has a base fee (burned, destroyed forever) plus an optional priority fee (tip to the validator). Fee = 21,000 gas x (base 8 gwei + tip 1 gwei) = 189,000 gwei = 0.000189 ETH Layer 1 and Layer 2 Ethereum mainnet (Layer 1) is secure but limited in throughput, so fees rise when busy. Layer 2 rollups (Arbitrum, Optimism, Base, zkSync, Starknet and others) execute transactions cheaply, then post compressed data and proofs back to Ethereum. Most everyday activity now happens on L2s. Same Solidity code, same tools. Testnets Sepolia is Ethereum's main test network. Its ETH is free (from faucets ) and worthless, so you can make every mistake there. Always deploy to a testnet first. Read a real transaction Go to sepolia.etherscan.io (or etherscan.io for mainnet), open any recent block, click a transaction, and find: from, to, value, gas used, gas price, the block number, and the status. Every number in this lesson is on that page. You can also query the chain from your terminal once Foundry is installed: cast block-number --rpc-url $SEPOLIA_RPC_URL cast balance 0xYourAddress --ether --rpc-url $SEPOLIA_RPC_URL cast to-wei 0.1 cast from-wei 189000000000000","track":"crypto"},{"id":"crypto-solidity","title":"Solidity basics","level":"intermediate","minutes":35,"summary":"Write, compile and test your first smart contract with Foundry. Types, functions, errors, events and modifiers.","html":"<h2 id=\"what-a-smart-contract-is\">What a smart contract is</h2>\n<p>A smart contract is a program deployed to a blockchain. Once deployed, its code cannot be changed, it runs exactly as written for anyone who calls it, and its data (state) is public. Every write costs gas, so contracts are small and careful.</p>\n<p><strong>Solidity</strong> is the main language for Ethereum and every EVM chain (Arbitrum, Base, Polygon and many more).</p>\n<h2 id=\"set-up-a-foundry-project\">Set up a Foundry project</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">forge init tip-jar\ncd tip-jar\nforge build\nforge test</code></pre></div>\n<p>You get <code>src/</code> (contracts), <code>test/</code> (tests, also written in Solidity), <code>script/</code> (deployment scripts) and <code>foundry.toml</code> (config). For quick experiments with no install, remix.ethereum.org runs in the browser.</p>\n<h2 id=\"your-first-contract-a-tip-jar\">Your first contract: a tip jar</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"solidity\"><pre><code class=\"language-solidity\">// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\npragma solidity ^0.8.24;\n\n/// @title A tip jar that only its owner can empty\ncontract TipJar {\n    // ---- state (stored on-chain, costs gas to write) ----\n    address public immutable owner;      // set once in the constructor\n    uint256 public totalTips;\n    mapping(address =&gt; uint256) public tipsFrom;\n\n    // ---- events (cheap logs that apps can listen to) ----\n    event Tipped(address indexed from, uint256 amount, string message);\n    event Withdrawn(uint256 amount);\n\n    // ---- custom errors (cheaper and clearer than strings) ----\n    error NotOwner();\n    error NoTipSent();\n    error TransferFailed();\n\n    constructor() {\n        owner = msg.sender;              // whoever deploys is the owner\n    }\n\n    modifier onlyOwner() {\n        if (msg.sender != owner) revert NotOwner();\n        _;                               // run the rest of the function here\n    }\n\n    function tip(string calldata message) external payable {\n        if (msg.value == 0) revert NoTipSent();\n        tipsFrom[msg.sender] += msg.value;\n        totalTips += msg.value;\n        emit Tipped(msg.sender, msg.value, message);\n    }\n\n    function balance() external view returns (uint256) {\n        return address(this).balance;\n    }\n\n    function withdraw() external onlyOwner {\n        uint256 amount = address(this).balance;\n        emit Withdrawn(amount);\n        (bool ok, ) = owner.call{value: amount}(&quot;&quot;);\n        if (!ok) revert TransferFailed();\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"the-pieces-explained\">The pieces, explained</h2>\n<ul><li><code>pragma solidity ^0.8.24;</code> pins the compiler version. Since 0.8, arithmetic overflow reverts automatically.</li><li><strong>Types</strong>: <code>uint256</code> (whole numbers from 0 to 2^256 - 1; there are no decimals), <code>int256</code>, <code>address</code>, <code>bool</code>, <code>bytes32</code>, <code>string</code>, arrays and <code>struct</code>s.</li><li><code>mapping(address =&gt; uint256)</code> is a key-value lookup table. Every possible key exists and defaults to 0; you cannot loop over a mapping.</li><li><strong>Visibility</strong>: <code>public</code> (anyone, and auto-creates a getter for variables), <code>external</code> (only from outside), <code>internal</code> (this contract and children), <code>private</code> (this contract only). Note: private data is still <strong>readable by anyone</strong> on-chain. Nothing on a public chain is secret.</li><li><strong>State mutability</strong>: <code>view</code> reads state, <code>pure</code> reads nothing, <code>payable</code> can receive ETH. View and pure calls are free when called from outside.</li><li><strong>Globals</strong>: <code>msg.sender</code> (who called), <code>msg.value</code> (ETH sent, in wei), <code>block.timestamp</code> (current block time).</li><li><strong>Errors</strong>: <code>revert</code> undoes everything the transaction did. <code>require(condition, &quot;message&quot;)</code> also works; custom errors are cheaper.</li><li><strong>Events</strong>: logs that front ends and indexers read. Indexed parameters can be filtered.</li><li><strong>Data locations</strong>: <code>storage</code> (permanent, expensive), <code>memory</code> (temporary), <code>calldata</code> (read-only input, cheapest).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"test-it\">Test it</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"solidity\"><pre><code class=\"language-solidity\">// test/TipJar.t.sol\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\npragma solidity ^0.8.24;\n\nimport {Test} from &quot;forge-std/Test.sol&quot;;\nimport {TipJar} from &quot;../src/TipJar.sol&quot;;\n\ncontract TipJarTest is Test {\n    TipJar jar;\n    address alice = makeAddr(&quot;alice&quot;);\n\n    function setUp() public {\n        jar = new TipJar();               // this test contract is the owner\n        vm.deal(alice, 10 ether);         // give alice some test ETH\n    }\n\n    receive() external payable {}         // let this test contract receive ETH on withdraw\n\n    function test_TipIsRecorded() public {\n        vm.prank(alice);                  // next call comes from alice\n        jar.tip{value: 1 ether}(&quot;great work&quot;);\n        assertEq(jar.totalTips(), 1 ether);\n        assertEq(jar.tipsFrom(alice), 1 ether);\n    }\n\n    function test_RevertWhen_TipIsZero() public {\n        vm.expectRevert(TipJar.NoTipSent.selector);\n        jar.tip(&quot;free&quot;);\n    }\n\n    function test_RevertWhen_NonOwnerWithdraws() public {\n        vm.prank(alice);\n        vm.expectRevert(TipJar.NotOwner.selector);\n        jar.withdraw();\n    }\n\n    function test_OwnerCanWithdraw() public {\n        vm.prank(alice);\n        jar.tip{value: 2 ether}(&quot;&quot;);\n        uint256 before = address(this).balance;\n        jar.withdraw();\n        assertEq(address(this).balance, before + 2 ether);\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">forge test -vv</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>vm.prank</code>, <code>vm.deal</code> and <code>vm.expectRevert</code> are Foundry <strong>cheatcodes</strong> that let tests act as any address, set balances and assert failures.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Make the compiler your teacher</p><p>Run <code>forge build</code> often and read the warnings, not just the errors. Install the Solidity extension in VS Code for inline errors. When something confuses you, <code>chisel</code> (installed with Foundry) is a Solidity REPL where you can try expressions instantly.</p></aside>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"2\"></div>","quiz":[{"q":"Your contract stores a password in a private variable. Who can read it?","options":["Only the contract owner","Nobody","Anyone, by reading the contract's storage on-chain"],"answer":2,"explain":"private only stops other contracts reading it through code. All on-chain data is public."},{"q":"What does msg.value hold?","options":["The amount of ETH (in wei) sent with the call","The caller's balance","The gas price"],"answer":0,"explain":"msg.value is only non-zero for payable functions that were sent ETH."},{"q":"What happens when a function hits revert?","options":["Only the rest of that function is skipped","Every state change in the whole transaction is undone","The contract is deleted"],"answer":1,"explain":"Reverts are all-or-nothing, though the sender still pays for gas used."},{"q":"What does vm.prank(alice) do in a Foundry test?","options":["Gives alice ETH","Makes the next call appear to come from alice","Deploys a contract as alice"],"answer":1,"explain":"Cheatcodes let tests impersonate any address. vm.deal sets balances."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Write the tip function","file":"TipJar.sol","lang":"solidity","run":false,"prompt":"<p>From memory if you can, write the <code>tip</code> function: it is <code>external payable</code>, takes <code>string calldata message</code>, reverts with <code>NoTipSent()</code> if no ETH was sent, adds <code>msg.value</code> to <code>tipsFrom[msg.sender]</code>, and emits <code>Tipped(msg.sender, msg.value, message)</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"function tip(string calldata message) external payable {\n    if (msg.value == 0) revert NoTipSent();\n    tipsFrom[msg.sender] += msg.value;\n    emit Tipped(msg.sender, msg.value, message);\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"function\\s+tip\\s*\\(\\s*string\\s+calldata\\s+message\\s*\\)","text":"Line 1 starts <code>function tip(string calldata message)</code>"},{"re":"\\)\\s*external\\s+payable\\s*\\{","text":"then <code>external payable {</code>. Without <code>payable</code> the function cannot receive ETH."},{"re":"if\\s*\\(\\s*msg\\.value\\s*==\\s*0\\s*\\)\\s*revert\\s+NoTipSent\\(\\s*\\)\\s*;","text":"Line 2: <code>if (msg.value == 0) revert NoTipSent();</code>"},{"re":"tipsFrom\\[\\s*msg\\.sender\\s*\\]\\s*\\+=\\s*msg\\.value\\s*;","text":"Line 3: <code>tipsFrom[msg.sender] += msg.value;</code>"},{"re":"emit\\s+Tipped\\(\\s*msg\\.sender\\s*,\\s*msg\\.value\\s*,\\s*message\\s*\\)\\s*;","text":"Line 4: <code>emit Tipped(msg.sender, msg.value, message);</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"Write a modifier","file":"TipJar.sol","lang":"solidity","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write the <code>onlyOwner</code> modifier: if <code>msg.sender</code> is not <code>owner</code>, revert with <code>NotOwner()</code>; otherwise continue with <code>_;</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"modifier onlyOwner() {\n    if (msg.sender != owner) revert NotOwner();\n    _;\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*modifier\\s+onlyOwner\\s*\\(\\s*\\)\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>modifier onlyOwner() {</code>"},{"re":"if\\s*\\(\\s*msg\\.sender\\s*!=\\s*owner\\s*\\)\\s*revert\\s+NotOwner\\(\\s*\\)\\s*;","text":"Line 2: <code>if (msg.sender != owner) revert NotOwner();</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*_\\s*;\\s*$","text":"Line 3 is just an underscore and a semicolon: <code>_;</code> It means &quot;now run the function&quot;."}],"explain":""},{"title":"Write a Foundry test","file":"TipJar.t.sol","lang":"solidity","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write <code>test_TipIsRecorded</code>: as <code>alice</code> (<code>vm.prank</code>), tip 1 ether with the message <code>&quot;great work&quot;</code>, then assert <code>jar.totalTips()</code> equals <code>1 ether</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"function test_TipIsRecorded() public {\n    vm.prank(alice);\n    jar.tip{value: 1 ether}(\"great work\");\n    assertEq(jar.totalTips(), 1 ether);\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"function\\s+test_TipIsRecorded\\s*\\(\\s*\\)\\s*public\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>function test_TipIsRecorded() public {</code>"},{"re":"vm\\.prank\\(\\s*alice\\s*\\)\\s*;","text":"Line 2: <code>vm.prank(alice);</code>"},{"re":"jar\\.tip\\{\\s*value\\s*:\\s*1\\s+ether\\s*\\}\\(\\s*[\"']great work[\"']\\s*\\)\\s*;","text":"Line 3 sends ETH with curly braces before the arguments: <code>jar.tip{value: 1 ether}(&quot;great work&quot;);</code>"},{"re":"assertEq\\(\\s*jar\\.totalTips\\(\\s*\\)\\s*,\\s*1\\s+ether\\s*\\)\\s*;","text":"Line 4: <code>assertEq(jar.totalTips(), 1 ether);</code>"}],"explain":""}],"text":"What a smart contract is A smart contract is a program deployed to a blockchain. Once deployed, its code cannot be changed, it runs exactly as written for anyone who calls it, and its data (state) is public. Every write costs gas, so contracts are small and careful. Solidity is the main language for Ethereum and every EVM chain (Arbitrum, Base, Polygon and many more). Set up a Foundry project forge init tip-jar cd tip-jar forge build forge test You get src/ (contracts), test/ (tests, also written in Solidity), script/ (deployment scripts) and foundry.toml (config). For quick experiments with no install, remix.ethereum.org runs in the browser. Your first contract: a tip jar // SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT pragma solidity ^0.8.24; /// @title A tip jar that only its owner can empty contract TipJar { // ---- state (stored on-chain, costs gas to write) ---- address public immutable owner; // set once in the constructor uint256 public totalTips; mapping(address = uint256) public tipsFrom; // ---- events (cheap logs that apps can listen to) ---- event Tipped(address indexed from, uint256 amount, string message); event Withdrawn(uint256 amount); // ---- custom errors (cheaper and clearer than strings) ---- error NotOwner(); error NoTipSent(); error TransferFailed(); constructor() { owner = msg.sender; // whoever deploys is the owner } modifier onlyOwner() { if (msg.sender != owner) revert NotOwner(); _; // run the rest of the function here } function tip(string calldata message) external payable { if (msg.value == 0) revert NoTipSent(); tipsFrom[msg.sender] += msg.value; totalTips += msg.value; emit Tipped(msg.sender, msg.value, message); } function balance() external view returns (uint256) { return address(this).balance; } function withdraw() external onlyOwner { uint256 amount = address(this).balance; emit Withdrawn(amount); (bool ok, ) = owner.call{value: amount}( ); if (!ok) revert TransferFailed(); } } The pieces, explained pragma solidity ^0.8.24; pins the compiler version. Since 0.8, arithmetic overflow reverts automatically. Types : uint256 (whole numbers from 0 to 2^256 - 1; there are no decimals), int256 , address , bool , bytes32 , string , arrays and struct s. mapping(address = uint256) is a key-value lookup table. Every possible key exists and defaults to 0; you cannot loop over a mapping. Visibility : public (anyone, and auto-creates a getter for variables), external (only from outside), internal (this contract and children), private (this contract only). Note: private data is still readable by anyone on-chain. Nothing on a public chain is secret. State mutability : view reads state, pure reads nothing, payable can receive ETH. View and pure calls are free when called from outside. Globals : msg.sender (who called), msg.value (ETH sent, in wei), block.timestamp (current block time). Errors : revert undoes everything the transaction did. require(condition, message ) also works; custom errors are cheaper. Events : logs that front ends and indexers read. Indexed parameters can be filtered. Data locations : storage (permanent, expensive), memory (temporary), calldata (read-only input, cheapest). Test it // test/TipJar.t.sol // SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT pragma solidity ^0.8.24; import {Test} from forge-std/Test.sol ; import {TipJar} from ../src/TipJar.sol ; contract TipJarTest is Test { TipJar jar; address alice = makeAddr( alice ); function setUp() public { jar = new TipJar(); // this test contract is the owner vm.deal(alice, 10 ether); // give alice some test ETH } receive() external payable {} // let this test contract receive ETH on withdraw function test_TipIsRecorded() public { vm.prank(alice); // next call comes from alice jar.tip{value: 1 ether}( great work ); assertEq(jar.totalTips(), 1 ether); assertEq(jar.tipsFrom(alice), 1 ether); } function test_RevertWhen_TipIsZero() public { vm.expectRevert(TipJar.NoTipSent.selector); jar.tip( free ); } function test_RevertWhen_NonOwnerWithdraws() public { vm.prank(alice); v","track":"crypto"},{"id":"crypto-erc20","title":"Build and deploy an ERC-20 token","level":"intermediate","minutes":45,"summary":"Create a token with OpenZeppelin, test it with Foundry, deploy it to Sepolia with an encrypted key and verify it on Etherscan.","html":"<h2 id=\"the-erc-20-standard\">The ERC-20 standard</h2>\n<p>ERC-20 is the interface every fungible token on Ethereum implements, so wallets, exchanges and other contracts can work with any token the same way:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>totalSupply()</code></td><td>Total tokens in existence</td></tr><tr><td><code>balanceOf(account)</code></td><td>Tokens held by an account</td></tr><tr><td><code>transfer(to, amount)</code></td><td>Send your tokens</td></tr><tr><td><code>approve(spender, amount)</code></td><td>Allow another address (like a DEX) to spend up to <code>amount</code> of yours</td></tr><tr><td><code>allowance(owner, spender)</code></td><td>How much a spender may still spend</td></tr><tr><td><code>transferFrom(from, to, amount)</code></td><td>A spender moves tokens it was approved for</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Plus <code>Transfer</code> and <code>Approval</code> events, and <code>name</code>, <code>symbol</code> and <code>decimals</code> (almost always 18).</p>\n<p><strong>Decimals</strong> work like pence: balances are whole numbers. With 18 decimals, &quot;1 token&quot; is stored as <code>1 * 10**18</code>. Displaying it as 1.0 is the front end's job.</p>\n<h2 id=\"create-the-project\">Create the project</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">forge init ladder-token\ncd ladder-token\nforge install OpenZeppelin/openzeppelin-contracts\necho '@openzeppelin/contracts/=lib/openzeppelin-contracts/contracts/' &gt; remappings.txt</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"the-contract\">The contract</h2>\n<p>Never write ERC-20 logic from scratch for real use. Inherit OpenZeppelin's audited implementation and add only your rules:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"solidity\"><pre><code class=\"language-solidity\">// src/LadderToken.sol\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\npragma solidity ^0.8.24;\n\nimport {ERC20} from &quot;@openzeppelin/contracts/token/ERC20/ERC20.sol&quot;;\nimport {Ownable} from &quot;@openzeppelin/contracts/access/Ownable.sol&quot;;\n\ncontract LadderToken is ERC20, Ownable {\n    uint256 public constant MAX_SUPPLY = 1_000_000_000 ether;   // 1 billion tokens (ether = 10**18)\n\n    error MaxSupplyExceeded();\n\n    constructor(address initialOwner) ERC20(&quot;Ladder&quot;, &quot;LDR&quot;) Ownable(initialOwner) {\n        _mint(initialOwner, 100_000_000 ether);   // 10% minted at launch\n    }\n\n    function mint(address to, uint256 amount) external onlyOwner {\n        if (totalSupply() + amount &gt; MAX_SUPPLY) revert MaxSupplyExceeded();\n        _mint(to, amount);\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"test-it\">Test it</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"solidity\"><pre><code class=\"language-solidity\">// test/LadderToken.t.sol\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\npragma solidity ^0.8.24;\n\nimport {Test} from &quot;forge-std/Test.sol&quot;;\nimport {Ownable} from &quot;@openzeppelin/contracts/access/Ownable.sol&quot;;\nimport {LadderToken} from &quot;../src/LadderToken.sol&quot;;\n\ncontract LadderTokenTest is Test {\n    LadderToken token;\n    address owner = makeAddr(&quot;owner&quot;);\n    address alice = makeAddr(&quot;alice&quot;);\n\n    function setUp() public {\n        token = new LadderToken(owner);\n    }\n\n    function test_InitialSupplyGoesToOwner() public view {\n        assertEq(token.balanceOf(owner), 100_000_000 ether);\n        assertEq(token.decimals(), 18);\n    }\n\n    function test_Transfer() public {\n        vm.prank(owner);\n        token.transfer(alice, 50 ether);\n        assertEq(token.balanceOf(alice), 50 ether);\n    }\n\n    function test_RevertWhen_StrangerMints() public {\n        vm.prank(alice);\n        vm.expectRevert(abi.encodeWithSelector(Ownable.OwnableUnauthorizedAccount.selector, alice));\n        token.mint(alice, 1 ether);\n    }\n\n    function test_RevertWhen_MintingPastMax() public {\n        vm.prank(owner);\n        vm.expectRevert(LadderToken.MaxSupplyExceeded.selector);\n        token.mint(owner, 900_000_000 ether + 1);\n    }\n\n    function testFuzz_TransferNeverCreatesTokens(uint256 amount) public {\n        amount = bound(amount, 0, token.balanceOf(owner));\n        uint256 supplyBefore = token.totalSupply();\n        vm.prank(owner);\n        token.transfer(alice, amount);\n        assertEq(token.totalSupply(), supplyBefore);\n        assertEq(token.balanceOf(owner) + token.balanceOf(alice), supplyBefore);\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">forge test -vv\nforge test --gas-report</code></pre></div>\n<p>The last test is a <strong>fuzz test</strong>: Foundry runs it with hundreds of random amounts.</p>\n<h2 id=\"deploy-to-sepolia\">Deploy to Sepolia</h2>\n<ol><li>Create a <strong>development wallet</strong> and store it encrypted (you choose a password):</li></ol>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">cast wallet new                       # prints a new address and private key (testnet use only)\ncast wallet import dev --interactive  # paste that private key; it is saved encrypted as &quot;dev&quot;\ncast wallet address --account dev</code></pre></div>\n<ol><li>Get free Sepolia ETH from a faucet (search &quot;Sepolia faucet&quot;; Alchemy and Google Cloud run reliable ones).</li><li>Get an RPC URL (free Alchemy or Infura account) and an Etherscan API key. Put them in <code>.env</code> (and make sure <code>.env</code> is in <code>.gitignore</code>):</li></ol>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">SEPOLIA_RPC_URL=https://eth-sepolia.g.alchemy.com/v2/your-key\nETHERSCAN_API_KEY=your-etherscan-key\nTOKEN_OWNER=0xYourDevAddress</code></pre></div>\n<ol><li>Write a deploy script:</li></ol>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"solidity\"><pre><code class=\"language-solidity\">// script/DeployLadder.s.sol\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\npragma solidity ^0.8.24;\n\nimport {Script} from &quot;forge-std/Script.sol&quot;;\nimport {LadderToken} from &quot;../src/LadderToken.sol&quot;;\n\ncontract DeployLadder is Script {\n    function run() external returns (LadderToken token) {\n        address owner = vm.envAddress(&quot;TOKEN_OWNER&quot;);\n        vm.startBroadcast();\n        token = new LadderToken(owner);\n        vm.stopBroadcast();\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<ol><li>Deploy and verify:</li></ol>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">source .env\nforge script script/DeployLadder.s.sol --rpc-url $SEPOLIA_RPC_URL --account dev --broadcast --verify</code></pre></div>\n<p>Find the contract address in the output, open it on sepolia.etherscan.io, and import the token into your wallet. You made a token.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Testnet first, real money much later</p><p>Deploying to mainnet costs real money and is permanent. Many tokens also raise legal questions (see the Tokenomics track's launch lesson). Use testnets until you have written tests, run Slither, and understand every line.</p></aside>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"2\"></div>","quiz":[{"q":"A token has 18 decimals. How is a balance of 2.5 tokens stored on-chain?","options":["2.5","2500000000000000000 (2.5 x 10^18)","25"],"answer":1,"explain":"On-chain amounts are integers. The front end divides by 10^decimals for display."},{"q":"Why does a DEX ask you to approve before swapping your tokens?","options":["So it can call transferFrom to move your tokens on your behalf, up to the approved amount","To check your identity","To pay gas for you"],"answer":0,"explain":"approve plus transferFrom lets contracts pull tokens you authorised."},{"q":"Why inherit from OpenZeppelin's ERC20 instead of writing your own?","options":["It is required by Ethereum","It is audited and battle-tested, so subtle bugs are far less likely","It uses less storage"],"answer":1,"explain":"Reusing audited code is the single biggest security win in Solidity."},{"q":"What does <code>cast wallet import dev --interactive</code> do?","options":["Uploads your key to Etherscan","Stores a private key encrypted on your machine under the name &quot;dev&quot;","Creates a new testnet"],"answer":1,"explain":"Keystores avoid having raw private keys in .env files or shell history."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Create the project","file":"Terminal","lang":"bash","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Three commands: create a Foundry project called <code>ladder-token</code>, move into it, and install the OpenZeppelin contracts library.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"forge init ladder-token\ncd ladder-token\nforge install OpenZeppelin/openzeppelin-contracts","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*forge\\s+init\\s+ladder-token\\s*$","text":"Line 1: <code>forge init ladder-token</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*cd\\s+ladder-token\\s*$","text":"Line 2: <code>cd ladder-token</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*forge\\s+install\\s+OpenZeppelin/openzeppelin-contracts\\s*$","text":"Line 3: <code>forge install OpenZeppelin/openzeppelin-contracts</code> (GitHub owner/repo)."}],"explain":""},{"title":"Write the token contract","file":"LadderToken.sol","lang":"solidity","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write the core of the token (imports and pragma omitted): a contract <code>LadderToken</code> that inherits <code>ERC20</code> and <code>Ownable</code>, with a constructor taking <code>address initialOwner</code> that sets the name <code>&quot;Ladder&quot;</code>, symbol <code>&quot;LDR&quot;</code>, owner <code>initialOwner</code>, and mints <code>100_000_000 ether</code> to <code>initialOwner</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"contract LadderToken is ERC20, Ownable {\n    constructor(address initialOwner) ERC20(\"Ladder\", \"LDR\") Ownable(initialOwner) {\n        _mint(initialOwner, 100_000_000 ether);\n    }\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*contract\\s+LadderToken\\s+is\\s+ERC20\\s*,\\s*Ownable\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>contract LadderToken is ERC20, Ownable {</code>"},{"re":"constructor\\(\\s*address\\s+initialOwner\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2 starts <code>constructor(address initialOwner)</code>"},{"re":"ERC20\\(\\s*[\"']Ladder[\"']\\s*,\\s*[\"']LDR[\"']\\s*\\)\\s*Ownable\\(\\s*initialOwner\\s*\\)\\s*\\{","text":"then call both parent constructors: <code>ERC20(&quot;Ladder&quot;, &quot;LDR&quot;) Ownable(initialOwner) {</code>"},{"re":"_mint\\(\\s*initialOwner\\s*,\\s*100_000_000\\s+ether\\s*\\)\\s*;","text":"Line 3: <code>_mint(initialOwner, 100_000_000 ether);</code>"},{"re":"\\}\\s*\\n\\s*\\}\\s*$","text":"Close the constructor and the contract: two <code>}</code>."}],"explain":""},{"title":"Deploy and verify","file":"Terminal","lang":"bash","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write the single command that runs <code>script/DeployLadder.s.sol</code> against <code>$SEPOLIA_RPC_URL</code>, signs with the keystore account <code>dev</code>, broadcasts the transaction, and verifies the contract. Flags in this order: <code>--rpc-url</code>, <code>--account</code>, <code>--broadcast</code>, <code>--verify</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"forge script script/DeployLadder.s.sol --rpc-url $SEPOLIA_RPC_URL --account dev --broadcast --verify","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*forge\\s+script\\s+script/DeployLadder\\.s\\.sol","text":"Start with <code>forge script script/DeployLadder.s.sol</code>"},{"re":"--rpc-url\\s+\\$SEPOLIA_RPC_URL","text":"then <code>--rpc-url $SEPOLIA_RPC_URL</code>"},{"re":"--account\\s+dev","text":"then <code>--account dev</code>"},{"re":"--broadcast\\s+--verify\\s*$","text":"finish with <code>--broadcast --verify</code>"}],"explain":""}],"text":"The ERC-20 standard ERC-20 is the interface every fungible token on Ethereum implements, so wallets, exchanges and other contracts can work with any token the same way: Function Does totalSupply() Total tokens in existence balanceOf(account) Tokens held by an account transfer(to, amount) Send your tokens approve(spender, amount) Allow another address (like a DEX) to spend up to amount of yours allowance(owner, spender) How much a spender may still spend transferFrom(from, to, amount) A spender moves tokens it was approved for Plus Transfer and Approval events, and name , symbol and decimals (almost always 18). Decimals work like pence: balances are whole numbers. With 18 decimals, 1 token is stored as 1 * 10**18 . Displaying it as 1.0 is the front end's job. Create the project forge init ladder-token cd ladder-token forge install OpenZeppelin/openzeppelin-contracts echo '@openzeppelin/contracts/=lib/openzeppelin-contracts/contracts/' remappings.txt The contract Never write ERC-20 logic from scratch for real use. Inherit OpenZeppelin's audited implementation and add only your rules: // src/LadderToken.sol // SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT pragma solidity ^0.8.24; import {ERC20} from @openzeppelin/contracts/token/ERC20/ERC20.sol ; import {Ownable} from @openzeppelin/contracts/access/Ownable.sol ; contract LadderToken is ERC20, Ownable { uint256 public constant MAX_SUPPLY = 1_000_000_000 ether; // 1 billion tokens (ether = 10**18) error MaxSupplyExceeded(); constructor(address initialOwner) ERC20( Ladder , LDR ) Ownable(initialOwner) { _mint(initialOwner, 100_000_000 ether); // 10% minted at launch } function mint(address to, uint256 amount) external onlyOwner { if (totalSupply() + amount MAX_SUPPLY) revert MaxSupplyExceeded(); _mint(to, amount); } } Test it // test/LadderToken.t.sol // SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT pragma solidity ^0.8.24; import {Test} from forge-std/Test.sol ; import {Ownable} from @openzeppelin/contracts/access/Ownable.sol ; import {LadderToken} from ../src/LadderToken.sol ; contract LadderTokenTest is Test { LadderToken token; address owner = makeAddr( owner ); address alice = makeAddr( alice ); function setUp() public { token = new LadderToken(owner); } function test_InitialSupplyGoesToOwner() public view { assertEq(token.balanceOf(owner), 100_000_000 ether); assertEq(token.decimals(), 18); } function test_Transfer() public { vm.prank(owner); token.transfer(alice, 50 ether); assertEq(token.balanceOf(alice), 50 ether); } function test_RevertWhen_StrangerMints() public { vm.prank(alice); vm.expectRevert(abi.encodeWithSelector(Ownable.OwnableUnauthorizedAccount.selector, alice)); token.mint(alice, 1 ether); } function test_RevertWhen_MintingPastMax() public { vm.prank(owner); vm.expectRevert(LadderToken.MaxSupplyExceeded.selector); token.mint(owner, 900_000_000 ether + 1); } function testFuzz_TransferNeverCreatesTokens(uint256 amount) public { amount = bound(amount, 0, token.balanceOf(owner)); uint256 supplyBefore = token.totalSupply(); vm.prank(owner); token.transfer(alice, amount); assertEq(token.totalSupply(), supplyBefore); assertEq(token.balanceOf(owner) + token.balanceOf(alice), supplyBefore); } } forge test -vv forge test --gas-report The last test is a fuzz test : Foundry runs it with hundreds of random amounts. Deploy to Sepolia Create a development wallet and store it encrypted (you choose a password): cast wallet new # prints a new address and private key (testnet use only) cast wallet import dev --interactive # paste that private key; it is saved encrypted as dev cast wallet address --account dev Get free Sepolia ETH from a faucet (search Sepolia faucet ; Alchemy and Google Cloud run reliable ones). Get an RPC URL (free Alchemy or Infura account) and an Etherscan API key. Put them in .env (and make sure .env is in .gitignore ): SEPOLIA_RPC_URL=https://eth-sepolia.g.alchemy.com/v2/your-key ETHERSCAN_API_KEY=your-etherscan-key TOKEN_OWNER=0xYourDevAddress Write a deploy script: // script/DeployLadder.s.","track":"crypto"},{"id":"crypto-dapp","title":"Talking to the chain from JavaScript","level":"intermediate","minutes":35,"summary":"Read contract data with viem, connect wallets with wagmi and RainbowKit, send transactions and handle every state.","html":"<h2 id=\"how-a-dapp-is-wired\">How a dApp is wired</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">Your React app  --reads--&gt;   RPC provider (Alchemy, Infura, public node)  --&gt;  blockchain\n       |\n       +--writes--&gt; user's wallet (MetaMask, Rabby...) --signs and sends--&gt;  blockchain</code></pre></div>\n<ul><li><strong>Reading</strong> (balances, prices, contract state) is free and needs no wallet. You call an RPC endpoint.</li><li><strong>Writing</strong> (transfers, mints, swaps) changes state, costs gas, and must be signed by the user's wallet. Your app never sees their private key.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"reading-with-viem\">Reading with viem</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\"><pre><code class=\"language-ts\">// npm install viem\nimport { createPublicClient, http, erc20Abi, formatUnits } from 'viem';\nimport { sepolia } from 'viem/chains';\n\nconst client = createPublicClient({ chain: sepolia, transport: http(import.meta.env.VITE_SEPOLIA_RPC_URL) });\n\nconst token = '0xYourTokenAddress';\nconst holder = '0xSomeHolderAddress';\n\nconst [symbol, decimals, raw] = await Promise.all([\n  client.readContract({ address: token, abi: erc20Abi, functionName: 'symbol' }),\n  client.readContract({ address: token, abi: erc20Abi, functionName: 'decimals' }),\n  client.readContract({ address: token, abi: erc20Abi, functionName: 'balanceOf', args: [holder] }),\n]);\n\nconsole.log(`${formatUnits(raw, decimals)} ${symbol}`);   // e.g. &quot;50 LDR&quot;\nconsole.log('Latest block:', await client.getBlockNumber());</code></pre></div>\n<ul><li>An <strong>ABI</strong> (Application Binary Interface) is the JSON description of a contract's functions. Foundry writes it to <code>out/YourContract.sol/YourContract.json</code>. viem ships <code>erc20Abi</code> for standard tokens.</li><li>Balances come back as <code>bigint</code>. Use <code>formatUnits</code> to display and <code>parseUnits('2.5', 18)</code> to convert user input. Never use floating-point numbers for token amounts.</li></ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">RPC URLs in front-end code</p><p>An RPC key in front-end code is visible to everyone. Providers expect this: restrict the key to your domain in their dashboard. Never put a <strong>private key</strong> anywhere near front-end code.</p></aside>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"connecting-wallets-with-wagmi-and-rainbowkit\">Connecting wallets with wagmi and RainbowKit</h2>\n<p>wagmi gives React hooks for accounts, reads and writes; RainbowKit adds a polished &quot;Connect wallet&quot; button and modal.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">npm install @rainbow-me/rainbowkit wagmi viem @tanstack/react-query</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"tsx\"><pre><code class=\"language-tsx\">// main.tsx\nimport '@rainbow-me/rainbowkit/styles.css';\nimport { getDefaultConfig, RainbowKitProvider } from '@rainbow-me/rainbowkit';\nimport { WagmiProvider } from 'wagmi';\nimport { sepolia } from 'wagmi/chains';\nimport { QueryClient, QueryClientProvider } from '@tanstack/react-query';\n\nconst config = getDefaultConfig({\n  appName: 'Ladder dApp',\n  projectId: 'YOUR_WALLETCONNECT_PROJECT_ID',   // free from WalletConnect / Reown dashboard\n  chains: [sepolia],\n});\nconst queryClient = new QueryClient();\n\nexport function Providers({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {\n  return (\n    &lt;WagmiProvider config={config}&gt;\n      &lt;QueryClientProvider client={queryClient}&gt;\n        &lt;RainbowKitProvider&gt;{children}&lt;/RainbowKitProvider&gt;\n      &lt;/QueryClientProvider&gt;\n    &lt;/WagmiProvider&gt;\n  );\n}</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"reading-and-writing-with-hooks\">Reading and writing with hooks</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"tsx\"><pre><code class=\"language-tsx\">import { ConnectButton } from '@rainbow-me/rainbowkit';\nimport { useAccount, useReadContract, useWriteContract, useWaitForTransactionReceipt } from 'wagmi';\nimport { erc20Abi, formatUnits, parseUnits } from 'viem';\n\nconst TOKEN = '0xYourTokenAddress' as const;\n\nexport function TokenPanel() {\n  const { address, isConnected } = useAccount();\n\n  const { data: balance, refetch } = useReadContract({\n    address: TOKEN, abi: erc20Abi, functionName: 'balanceOf',\n    args: address ? [address] : undefined,\n    query: { enabled: Boolean(address) },\n  });\n\n  const { writeContract, data: hash, isPending, error } = useWriteContract();\n  const { isLoading: confirming, isSuccess } = useWaitForTransactionReceipt({ hash });\n\n  const send = () =&gt;\n    writeContract({ address: TOKEN, abi: erc20Abi, functionName: 'transfer', args: ['0xFriendAddress', parseUnits('1', 18)] });\n\n  return (\n    &lt;section&gt;\n      &lt;ConnectButton /&gt;\n      {isConnected &amp;&amp; &lt;p&gt;Balance: {balance !== undefined ? formatUnits(balance, 18) : '...'} LDR&lt;/p&gt;}\n      &lt;button onClick={send} disabled={!isConnected || isPending || confirming}&gt;\n        {isPending ? 'Confirm in your wallet...' : confirming ? 'Waiting for the block...' : 'Send 1 LDR'}\n      &lt;/button&gt;\n      {hash &amp;&amp; &lt;a href={`https://sepolia.etherscan.io/tx/${hash}`} target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;View transaction&lt;/a&gt;}\n      {isSuccess &amp;&amp; &lt;p&gt;Sent. &lt;button onClick={() =&gt; refetch()}&gt;Refresh balance&lt;/button&gt;&lt;/p&gt;}\n      {error &amp;&amp; &lt;p role=&quot;alert&quot;&gt;{error.message.split('\\n')[0]}&lt;/p&gt;}\n    &lt;/section&gt;\n  );\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"every-state-a-transaction-goes-through\">Every state a transaction goes through</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>State</th><th>What the user should see</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Not connected</td><td>A clear Connect button</td></tr><tr><td>Wrong network</td><td>&quot;Switch to Sepolia&quot; button</td></tr><tr><td>Waiting for signature</td><td>&quot;Confirm in your wallet&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>Rejected by user</td><td>A calm message, not a scary error</td></tr><tr><td>Pending (in mempool)</td><td>Progress, plus a link to the explorer</td></tr><tr><td>Confirmed</td><td>Success, and refreshed balances</td></tr><tr><td>Reverted</td><td>The reason, in plain English</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Good dApps feel trustworthy because they handle all seven. Most bad ones only handle &quot;confirmed&quot;.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-pro\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Simulate before you send</p><p>viem's <code>simulateContract</code> (and wagmi's <code>useSimulateContract</code>) runs the transaction against current state without sending it. If it would revert, you can show the reason before the user pays gas.</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"Which operation needs the user's wallet to sign?","options":["Reading a token balance","Getting the latest block number","Transferring tokens"],"answer":2,"explain":"Reads are free calls to an RPC node. State changes must be signed and paid for."},{"q":"A balance comes back as 2500000000000000000n with 18 decimals. How do you display it?","options":["formatUnits(balance, 18), giving &quot;2.5&quot;","Number(balance) / 18","balance.toString()"],"answer":0,"explain":"formatUnits divides by 10^decimals safely using bigint maths."},{"q":"What is an ABI?","options":["The contract's source code","A JSON description of a contract's functions and events, so code knows how to call it","An API key"],"answer":1,"explain":"Front ends and scripts need the ABI to encode calls and decode results."},{"q":"Why simulate a transaction before sending it?","options":["It makes it cheaper","To catch reverts and show the reason before the user pays gas","Wallets require it"],"answer":1,"explain":"Failed transactions still cost gas. Simulating saves users money and confusion."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Read a token balance with viem","file":"balance.ts","lang":"ts","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Using <code>client</code>, <code>token</code>, <code>holder</code>, <code>erc20Abi</code> and <code>formatUnits</code> from the example above, read <code>balanceOf(holder)</code> and print it with 18 decimals. Write it over several lines, one property per line, exactly like this shape:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\"><pre><code class=\"language-ts\">const balance = await client.readContract({\n  address: token,\n  abi: erc20Abi,\n  functionName: 'balanceOf',\n  args: [holder],\n});\nconsole.log(formatUnits(balance, 18));</code></pre></div>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const balance = await client.readContract({\n  address: token,\n  abi: erc20Abi,\n  functionName: 'balanceOf',\n  args: [holder],\n});\nconsole.log(formatUnits(balance, 18));","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+balance\\s*=\\s*await\\s+client\\.readContract\\(\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>const balance = await client.readContract({</code>"},{"re":"address\\s*:\\s*token\\s*,","text":"<code>address: token,</code>"},{"re":"abi\\s*:\\s*erc20Abi\\s*,","text":"<code>abi: erc20Abi,</code>"},{"re":"functionName\\s*:\\s*['\"]balanceOf['\"]\\s*,","text":"<code>functionName: 'balanceOf',</code>"},{"re":"args\\s*:\\s*\\[\\s*holder\\s*\\]\\s*,","text":"<code>args: [holder],</code> (arguments always go in an array)."},{"re":"formatUnits\\(\\s*balance\\s*,\\s*18\\s*\\)","text":"Last line: <code>console.log(formatUnits(balance, 18));</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"Send a transfer from React","file":"TokenPanel.tsx","lang":"ts","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write the single <code>writeContract</code> call that transfers 1 token (18 decimals) to <code>to</code>: address <code>TOKEN</code>, abi <code>erc20Abi</code>, functionName <code>'transfer'</code>, args <code>[to, parseUnits('1', 18)]</code>. All on one line.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"writeContract({ address: TOKEN, abi: erc20Abi, functionName: 'transfer', args: [to, parseUnits('1', 18)] });","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*writeContract\\(\\s*\\{","text":"Start <code>writeContract({</code>"},{"re":"address\\s*:\\s*TOKEN\\s*,\\s*abi\\s*:\\s*erc20Abi\\s*,","text":"then <code>address: TOKEN, abi: erc20Abi,</code>"},{"re":"functionName\\s*:\\s*['\"]transfer['\"]\\s*,","text":"then <code>functionName: 'transfer',</code>"},{"re":"args\\s*:\\s*\\[\\s*to\\s*,\\s*parseUnits\\(\\s*['\"]1['\"]\\s*,\\s*18\\s*\\)\\s*\\]","text":"then <code>args: [to, parseUnits('1', 18)]</code>"},{"re":"\\}\\s*\\)\\s*;?\\s*$","text":"Close with <code>});</code>"}],"explain":""}],"text":"How a dApp is wired Your React app --reads-- RPC provider (Alchemy, Infura, public node) -- blockchain | +--writes-- user's wallet (MetaMask, Rabby...) --signs and sends-- blockchain Reading (balances, prices, contract state) is free and needs no wallet. You call an RPC endpoint. Writing (transfers, mints, swaps) changes state, costs gas, and must be signed by the user's wallet. Your app never sees their private key. Reading with viem // npm install viem import { createPublicClient, http, erc20Abi, formatUnits } from 'viem'; import { sepolia } from 'viem/chains'; const client = createPublicClient({ chain: sepolia, transport: http(import.meta.env.VITE_SEPOLIA_RPC_URL) }); const token = '0xYourTokenAddress'; const holder = '0xSomeHolderAddress'; const [symbol, decimals, raw] = await Promise.all([ client.readContract({ address: token, abi: erc20Abi, functionName: 'symbol' }), client.readContract({ address: token, abi: erc20Abi, functionName: 'decimals' }), client.readContract({ address: token, abi: erc20Abi, functionName: 'balanceOf', args: [holder] }), ]); console.log(`${formatUnits(raw, decimals)} ${symbol}`); // e.g. 50 LDR console.log('Latest block:', await client.getBlockNumber()); An ABI (Application Binary Interface) is the JSON description of a contract's functions. Foundry writes it to out/YourContract.sol/YourContract.json . viem ships erc20Abi for standard tokens. Balances come back as bigint . Use formatUnits to display and parseUnits('2.5', 18) to convert user input. Never use floating-point numbers for token amounts. RPC URLs in front-end code An RPC key in front-end code is visible to everyone. Providers expect this: restrict the key to your domain in their dashboard. Never put a private key anywhere near front-end code. Connecting wallets with wagmi and RainbowKit wagmi gives React hooks for accounts, reads and writes; RainbowKit adds a polished Connect wallet button and modal. npm install @rainbow-me/rainbowkit wagmi viem @tanstack/react-query // main.tsx import '@rainbow-me/rainbowkit/styles.css'; import { getDefaultConfig, RainbowKitProvider } from '@rainbow-me/rainbowkit'; import { WagmiProvider } from 'wagmi'; import { sepolia } from 'wagmi/chains'; import { QueryClient, QueryClientProvider } from '@tanstack/react-query'; const config = getDefaultConfig({ appName: 'Ladder dApp', projectId: 'YOUR_WALLETCONNECT_PROJECT_ID', // free from WalletConnect / Reown dashboard chains: [sepolia], }); const queryClient = new QueryClient(); export function Providers({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) { return ( WagmiProvider config={config} QueryClientProvider client={queryClient} RainbowKitProvider {children} /RainbowKitProvider /QueryClientProvider /WagmiProvider ); } Reading and writing with hooks import { ConnectButton } from '@rainbow-me/rainbowkit'; import { useAccount, useReadContract, useWriteContract, useWaitForTransactionReceipt } from 'wagmi'; import { erc20Abi, formatUnits, parseUnits } from 'viem'; const TOKEN = '0xYourTokenAddress' as const; export function TokenPanel() { const { address, isConnected } = useAccount(); const { data: balance, refetch } = useReadContract({ address: TOKEN, abi: erc20Abi, functionName: 'balanceOf', args: address ? [address] : undefined, query: { enabled: Boolean(address) }, }); const { writeContract, data: hash, isPending, error } = useWriteContract(); const { isLoading: confirming, isSuccess } = useWaitForTransactionReceipt({ hash }); const send = () = writeContract({ address: TOKEN, abi: erc20Abi, functionName: 'transfer', args: ['0xFriendAddress', parseUnits('1', 18)] }); return ( section ConnectButton / {isConnected p Balance: {balance !== undefined ? formatUnits(balance, 18) : '...'} LDR /p } button onClick={send} disabled={!isConnected || isPending || confirming} {isPending ? 'Confirm in your wallet...' : confirming ? 'Waiting for the block...' : 'Send 1 LDR'} /button {hash a href={`https://sepolia.etherscan.io/tx/${hash}`} target= _blank rel= noreferrer View trans","track":"crypto"},{"id":"crypto-standards","title":"Token standards: ERC-20, 721, 1155 and friends","level":"intermediate","minutes":25,"summary":"Fungible tokens, NFTs, multi-tokens, vaults and signed approvals, and the risks in each.","html":"<h2 id=\"why-standards-matter\">Why standards matter</h2>\n<p>A standard (an <strong>ERC</strong>, Ethereum Request for Comments) is an agreed interface. Because every ERC-20 token has <code>transfer</code> and <code>balanceOf</code>, any wallet or exchange supports any token without custom code. Use standards, and use OpenZeppelin's implementations of them.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-ones-you-will-meet\">The ones you will meet</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Standard</th><th>What it is</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>ERC-20</strong></td><td>Fungible tokens: every unit is identical</td><td>Stablecoins, governance tokens</td></tr><tr><td><strong>ERC-721</strong></td><td>Non-fungible tokens (NFTs): each token ID is unique</td><td>Art, tickets, memberships, domain names</td></tr><tr><td><strong>ERC-1155</strong></td><td>Many token types in one contract, fungible or not</td><td>Game items, editions, event passes</td></tr><tr><td><strong>ERC-4626</strong></td><td>Tokenised vaults: deposit an asset, receive shares</td><td>Yield vaults, staking wrappers</td></tr><tr><td><strong>EIP-2612</strong></td><td><code>permit</code>: approve with a signature instead of a transaction</td><td>Gasless approvals</td></tr><tr><td><strong>EIP-712</strong></td><td>A standard for signing structured data readably</td><td>Orders, permits, off-chain votes</td></tr><tr><td><strong>ERC-4337</strong></td><td>Account abstraction: smart contract wallets with sponsored gas, batching and recovery</td><td>Modern wallets</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"nfts-erc-721-in-brief\">NFTs (ERC-721) in brief</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"solidity\"><pre><code class=\"language-solidity\">// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\npragma solidity ^0.8.24;\n\nimport {ERC721} from &quot;@openzeppelin/contracts/token/ERC721/ERC721.sol&quot;;\nimport {Ownable} from &quot;@openzeppelin/contracts/access/Ownable.sol&quot;;\n\ncontract StudioPass is ERC721, Ownable {\n    uint256 private _nextId = 1;\n\n    constructor(address initialOwner) ERC721(&quot;Studio Pass&quot;, &quot;PASS&quot;) Ownable(initialOwner) {}\n\n    function mint(address to) external onlyOwner returns (uint256 id) {\n        id = _nextId++;\n        _safeMint(to, id);\n    }\n\n    function _baseURI() internal pure override returns (string memory) {\n        return &quot;ipfs://YOUR_METADATA_FOLDER_CID/&quot;;\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>Each token's <code>tokenURI</code> points to <strong>metadata</strong> JSON (name, description, image). The image is usually on IPFS or Arweave, not on-chain, because storage on-chain is very expensive. If metadata lives on a normal web server, the NFT's appearance depends on that server staying up.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"approvals-powerful-and-dangerous\">Approvals: powerful and dangerous</h2>\n<p>ERC-20's <code>approve</code> lets a contract spend your tokens. Many apps ask for an <strong>unlimited</strong> approval so you never have to approve again. Convenient, but if that contract is later exploited, or was malicious from the start, it can drain every approved token.</p>\n<ul><li>As a user: approve only what you need, and revoke old approvals regularly (revoke.cash, or your wallet's approval manager).</li><li>As a builder: request exact amounts where practical, and support <code>permit</code> signatures.</li></ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Signatures can move money too</p><p>&quot;Just sign this message, it is free&quot; can be a <code>permit</code> signature that approves a transfer of your tokens. EIP-712 makes signatures readable in wallets: read them. Scam sites rely on people signing without reading.</p></aside>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"erc-4626-vaults\">ERC-4626 vaults</h2>\n<p>A vault takes deposits of one asset (say USDC) and mints <strong>shares</strong>. As the vault earns yield, each share is worth more of the asset. A standard interface (<code>deposit</code>, <code>withdraw</code>, <code>convertToAssets</code>) means any app can integrate any vault. The classic bug: rounding and &quot;inflation attacks&quot; on nearly empty vaults. OpenZeppelin's implementation includes protections; read its docs before building one.</p>","quiz":[{"q":"Which standard fits 500 identical concert tickets plus 20 unique backstage passes in one contract?","options":["ERC-20","ERC-721","ERC-1155"],"answer":2,"explain":"ERC-1155 handles many token types, fungible and non-fungible, in a single contract."},{"q":"Why is an unlimited token approval risky?","options":["If the approved contract is malicious or hacked, it can take all of that token from your wallet","It costs more gas every time","It expires after one day"],"answer":0,"explain":"Approvals persist until revoked. Approve only what you need."},{"q":"Where is an NFT's image usually stored?","options":["Inside the token contract's storage","Off-chain (often IPFS or Arweave), referenced by the token's metadata URI","In the buyer's wallet app"],"answer":1,"explain":"On-chain storage is expensive, so contracts store a pointer to metadata."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Mint an NFT","file":"StudioPass.sol","lang":"solidity","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write the <code>mint</code> function: <code>external onlyOwner</code>, takes <code>address to</code>, <code>returns (uint256 id)</code>, sets <code>id = _nextId++;</code> and calls <code>_safeMint(to, id);</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"function mint(address to) external onlyOwner returns (uint256 id) {\n    id = _nextId++;\n    _safeMint(to, id);\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"function\\s+mint\\s*\\(\\s*address\\s+to\\s*\\)","text":"Line 1 starts <code>function mint(address to)</code>"},{"re":"external\\s+onlyOwner\\s+returns\\s*\\(\\s*uint256\\s+id\\s*\\)\\s*\\{","text":"then <code>external onlyOwner returns (uint256 id) {</code>"},{"re":"id\\s*=\\s*_nextId\\+\\+\\s*;","text":"Line 2: <code>id = _nextId++;</code> (use the current value, then add one)."},{"re":"_safeMint\\(\\s*to\\s*,\\s*id\\s*\\)\\s*;","text":"Line 3: <code>_safeMint(to, id);</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"Approve exactly what you need","file":"Approve.sol","lang":"solidity","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Two Solidity lines: approve <code>address(dex)</code> to spend <code>100 ether</code> of <code>token</code>, then read the remaining allowance from <code>address(this)</code> to <code>address(dex)</code> into a <code>uint256 left</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"token.approve(address(dex), 100 ether);\nuint256 left = token.allowance(address(this), address(dex));","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"token\\.approve\\(\\s*address\\(\\s*dex\\s*\\)\\s*,\\s*100\\s+ether\\s*\\)\\s*;","text":"Line 1: <code>token.approve(address(dex), 100 ether);</code>"},{"re":"uint256\\s+left\\s*=\\s*token\\.allowance\\(","text":"Line 2 starts <code>uint256 left = token.allowance(</code>"},{"re":"allowance\\(\\s*address\\(\\s*this\\s*\\)\\s*,\\s*address\\(\\s*dex\\s*\\)\\s*\\)\\s*;","text":"Arguments: owner first, then spender: <code>address(this), address(dex)</code>"}],"explain":""}],"text":"Why standards matter A standard (an ERC , Ethereum Request for Comments) is an agreed interface. Because every ERC-20 token has transfer and balanceOf , any wallet or exchange supports any token without custom code. Use standards, and use OpenZeppelin's implementations of them. The ones you will meet Standard What it is Examples ERC-20 Fungible tokens: every unit is identical Stablecoins, governance tokens ERC-721 Non-fungible tokens (NFTs): each token ID is unique Art, tickets, memberships, domain names ERC-1155 Many token types in one contract, fungible or not Game items, editions, event passes ERC-4626 Tokenised vaults: deposit an asset, receive shares Yield vaults, staking wrappers EIP-2612 permit : approve with a signature instead of a transaction Gasless approvals EIP-712 A standard for signing structured data readably Orders, permits, off-chain votes ERC-4337 Account abstraction: smart contract wallets with sponsored gas, batching and recovery Modern wallets NFTs (ERC-721) in brief // SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT pragma solidity ^0.8.24; import {ERC721} from @openzeppelin/contracts/token/ERC721/ERC721.sol ; import {Ownable} from @openzeppelin/contracts/access/Ownable.sol ; contract StudioPass is ERC721, Ownable { uint256 private _nextId = 1; constructor(address initialOwner) ERC721( Studio Pass , PASS ) Ownable(initialOwner) {} function mint(address to) external onlyOwner returns (uint256 id) { id = _nextId++; _safeMint(to, id); } function _baseURI() internal pure override returns (string memory) { return ipfs://YOUR_METADATA_FOLDER_CID/ ; } } Each token's tokenURI points to metadata JSON (name, description, image). The image is usually on IPFS or Arweave, not on-chain, because storage on-chain is very expensive. If metadata lives on a normal web server, the NFT's appearance depends on that server staying up. Approvals: powerful and dangerous ERC-20's approve lets a contract spend your tokens. Many apps ask for an unlimited approval so you never have to approve again. Convenient, but if that contract is later exploited, or was malicious from the start, it can drain every approved token. As a user: approve only what you need, and revoke old approvals regularly (revoke.cash, or your wallet's approval manager). As a builder: request exact amounts where practical, and support permit signatures. Signatures can move money too Just sign this message, it is free can be a permit signature that approves a transfer of your tokens. EIP-712 makes signatures readable in wallets: read them. Scam sites rely on people signing without reading. ERC-4626 vaults A vault takes deposits of one asset (say USDC) and mints shares . As the vault earns yield, each share is worth more of the asset. A standard interface ( deposit , withdraw , convertToAssets ) means any app can integrate any vault. The classic bug: rounding and inflation attacks on nearly empty vaults. OpenZeppelin's implementation includes protections; read its docs before building one.","track":"crypto"},{"id":"crypto-security","title":"Smart contract security","level":"advanced","minutes":45,"summary":"The vulnerability classes behind billions in losses (reentrancy, access control, oracle manipulation, replay and more) and how to prevent them.","html":"<h2 id=\"why-security-is-different-here\">Why security is different here</h2>\n<p>Web bugs can be patched in minutes. Smart contracts are usually immutable, hold real money, run in public, and can be attacked by anyone, instantly, with borrowed millions (flash loans). Billions of dollars have been lost to contract bugs and key compromises. Security is not a final step; it is the job.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-reentrancy\">1. Reentrancy</h2>\n<p>A contract sends ETH (or calls an unknown contract) <strong>before</strong> updating its own state. The receiver's code calls back in and repeats the withdrawal while the balance still looks full. This drained The DAO in 2016 and still appears today.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"solidity\"><pre><code class=\"language-solidity\">// VULNERABLE\nfunction withdraw() external {\n    uint256 amount = balances[msg.sender];\n    (bool ok, ) = msg.sender.call{value: amount}(&quot;&quot;);   // attacker re-enters here...\n    require(ok);\n    balances[msg.sender] = 0;                             // ...before this runs\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"solidity\"><pre><code class=\"language-solidity\">// SAFE: checks, effects, interactions\nfunction withdraw() external nonReentrant {\n    uint256 amount = balances[msg.sender];\n    if (amount == 0) revert NothingToWithdraw();   // checks\n    balances[msg.sender] = 0;                       // effects (update state first)\n    (bool ok, ) = msg.sender.call{value: amount}(&quot;&quot;); // interactions (external calls last)\n    if (!ok) revert TransferFailed();\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>Follow <strong>checks-effects-interactions</strong> everywhere and add OpenZeppelin's <code>ReentrancyGuard</code> (<code>nonReentrant</code>) to functions that move value. Watch for <strong>read-only reentrancy</strong> too: other protocols reading your state mid-update.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"2-access-control\">2. Access control</h2>\n<p>Missing <code>onlyOwner</code> on a mint or upgrade function. An <code>initialize</code> function anyone can call first. Using <code>tx.origin</code> (the original signer) instead of <code>msg.sender</code> for auth, which lets a malicious contract act on a user's behalf.</p>\n<ul><li>Use OpenZeppelin <code>Ownable2Step</code> or <code>AccessControl</code> with named roles.</li><li>Put privileged roles behind a <strong>multisig</strong> (Safe) and a <strong>timelock</strong> so users can see changes coming.</li><li>Write a test for every privileged function proving strangers are rejected.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"3-oracle-and-price-manipulation\">3. Oracle and price manipulation</h2>\n<p>If your protocol reads a token's price from a single DEX pool's current reserves, an attacker can borrow millions in a <strong>flash loan</strong>, skew that pool, exploit your protocol at the fake price, and repay the loan, all in one transaction.</p>\n<ul><li>Use robust oracles (Chainlink price feeds) or time-weighted average prices (TWAPs), never spot prices from one pool.</li><li>Check oracle data is fresh and within sane bounds.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"4-arithmetic-and-precision\">4. Arithmetic and precision</h2>\n<p>Solidity 0.8+ reverts on overflow, but precision bugs remain:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"solidity\"><pre><code class=\"language-solidity\">uint256 fee = amount / 1000 * 3;   // BAD: divides first, small amounts round to zero\nuint256 fee = amount * 3 / 1000;   // better: multiply before dividing</code></pre></div>\n<ul><li>Decide who rounding should favour (almost always the protocol, never the user who can repeat it).</li><li>Be careful with <code>unchecked</code> blocks and with tokens that have different decimals (USDC has 6, most have 18).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"5-front-running-and-mev\">5. Front-running and MEV</h2>\n<p>Pending transactions are public. Bots can see your large swap and trade before and after it (&quot;sandwich&quot;), profiting from the price movement they caused.</p>\n<ul><li>Swaps need a <strong>slippage limit</strong> (<code>minAmountOut</code>) and a <strong>deadline</strong>.</li><li>Commit-reveal schemes hide sensitive values until it is too late to front-run.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"6-signature-replay\">6. Signature replay</h2>\n<p>A signed message (a permit, an off-chain order) must be usable once, on one chain, for one contract. Include a <strong>nonce</strong>, the <strong>chain ID</strong>, the <strong>contract address</strong> and an <strong>expiry</strong>: exactly what EIP-712 domains and OpenZeppelin's <code>EIP712</code> and <code>Nonces</code> give you. Use <code>ECDSA.recover</code> from OpenZeppelin, which rejects malleable signatures.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-denial-of-service\">7. Denial of service</h2>\n<ul><li>Looping over an array that users can grow will eventually exceed the block gas limit and brick the function.</li><li>Sending ETH to a list of addresses in one loop fails entirely if one recipient reverts. Prefer <strong>pull payments</strong>: record what each user is owed and let them withdraw.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"8-unsafe-token-interactions\">8. Unsafe token interactions</h2>\n<p>Some tokens do not return <code>true</code> from <code>transfer</code>, charge fees on transfer, or rebase balances. Use OpenZeppelin's <code>SafeERC20</code> (<code>safeTransfer</code>, <code>safeTransferFrom</code>), and measure balances before and after if fee-on-transfer tokens are possible.</p>\n<h2 id=\"9-upgrade-and-initialisation-bugs\">9. Upgrade and initialisation bugs</h2>\n<p>Proxies separate storage from logic. Mistakes in storage layout or unprotected initialisers have caused major losses. See the upgradeability lesson before using proxies.</p>\n<h2 id=\"10-keys-and-operations\">10. Keys and operations</h2>\n<p>Many of the largest losses were not code bugs at all but compromised private keys (bridges, exchanges, deployer keys). Use multisigs, hardware wallets, separate deployer and admin keys, monitoring and an incident plan.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-pre-deployment-checklist\">The pre-deployment checklist</h2>\n<ul><li>Every external function: who can call it, and with what inputs?</li><li>Checks-effects-interactions everywhere; <code>nonReentrant</code> where value moves.</li><li>No <code>tx.origin</code> auth. No unbounded loops. Pull over push payments.</li><li>Oracles are robust and freshness-checked. Slippage and deadlines on swaps.</li><li>Signatures include nonce, chain ID, contract and expiry.</li><li>100% of business logic has unit, fuzz and invariant tests.</li><li>Slither and at least one other analyser run clean (or every finding explained).</li><li>An independent audit or competitive audit for anything holding significant value.</li><li>Admin keys in a multisig behind a timelock. A pause switch and an incident plan.</li></ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-pro\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Learn by breaking things</p><p>Play <strong>Ethernaut</strong> (OpenZeppelin), then <strong>Damn Vulnerable DeFi</strong>. Read post-mortems on rekt.news and audit reports from firms like Trail of Bits, OpenZeppelin, Spearbit and Cyfrin. Auditing contests (Code4rena, Sherlock, Cantina) pay for found bugs and are a recognised path into a security career.</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"What is the core fix for reentrancy?","options":["Use tx.origin instead of msg.sender","Update state before making external calls (checks-effects-interactions), plus a reentrancy guard","Use a newer compiler version"],"answer":1,"explain":"If balances are zeroed first, re-entering finds nothing left to take."},{"q":"A lending protocol prices collateral using one DEX pool's current reserves. What attack does this invite?","options":["Signature replay","Flash-loan price manipulation","Denial of service"],"answer":1,"explain":"Spot prices can be moved within one transaction. Use robust oracles or TWAPs."},{"q":"Which fee calculation loses the least precision?","options":["amount / 1000 * 3","amount * 3 / 1000","They are identical"],"answer":1,"explain":"Integer division truncates, so divide last."},{"q":"A signed permit can be reused on another chain where the same contract is deployed. What is missing?","options":["A higher gas price","The chain ID (and contract address) in the signed data, as EIP-712 domains provide","An event"],"answer":1,"explain":"Domain separation and nonces make each signature valid exactly once, in one place."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Fix the reentrancy bug","file":"Vault.sol","lang":"solidity","run":false,"prompt":"<p>This <code>withdraw</code> is vulnerable: it sends ETH <strong>before</strong> zeroing the balance. Fix it: add the <code>nonReentrant</code> modifier after <code>external</code>, and move the line that zeroes the balance so it runs <strong>before</strong> the external call (checks, effects, interactions).</p>","starter":"function withdraw() external {\n    uint256 amount = balances[msg.sender];\n    (bool ok, ) = msg.sender.call{value: amount}(\"\");\n    require(ok);\n    balances[msg.sender] = 0;\n}","html":"","solution":"function withdraw() external nonReentrant {\n    uint256 amount = balances[msg.sender];\n    balances[msg.sender] = 0;\n    (bool ok, ) = msg.sender.call{value: amount}(\"\");\n    require(ok);\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"external\\s+nonReentrant\\s*\\{","text":"Add the guard to line 1: <code>function withdraw() external nonReentrant {</code>"},{"re":"balances\\[\\s*msg\\.sender\\s*\\]\\s*=\\s*0\\s*;[\\s\\S]*msg\\.sender\\.call","text":"Effects before interactions: <code>balances[msg.sender] = 0;</code> must come <strong>before</strong> the <code>.call{value: amount}</code> line."},{"re":"require\\(\\s*ok\\s*\\)\\s*;\\s*\\n\\s*\\}\\s*$","text":"After the call, <code>require(ok);</code> is the last line before the closing brace."}],"explain":"<p>Now, if the receiver re-enters <code>withdraw</code>, its balance is already 0, so there is nothing more to take, and <code>nonReentrant</code> blocks the second call anyway. Two independent defences: that is defence in depth.</p>"},{"title":"Fix the precision bug","file":"Fees.sol","lang":"solidity","run":false,"prompt":"<p>This fee calculation divides first, so small amounts round down to a fee of zero. Rewrite it to multiply before dividing.</p>","starter":"uint256 fee = amount / 1000 * 3;","html":"","solution":"uint256 fee = amount * 3 / 1000;","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"uint256\\s+fee\\s*=\\s*amount\\s*\\*\\s*3","text":"Multiply first: <code>amount * 3</code>"},{"re":"\\*\\s*3\\s*\\/\\s*1000\\s*;","text":"then divide last: <code>/ 1000;</code>"}],"explain":"<p>With <code>amount = 999</code>, the old version computes <code>999 / 1000 = 0</code>, then <code>0 * 3 = 0</code>: no fee at all, repeatable forever. The fixed version gives <code>2997 / 1000 = 2</code>.</p>"}],"text":"Why security is different here Web bugs can be patched in minutes. Smart contracts are usually immutable, hold real money, run in public, and can be attacked by anyone, instantly, with borrowed millions (flash loans). Billions of dollars have been lost to contract bugs and key compromises. Security is not a final step; it is the job. 1. Reentrancy A contract sends ETH (or calls an unknown contract) before updating its own state. The receiver's code calls back in and repeats the withdrawal while the balance still looks full. This drained The DAO in 2016 and still appears today. // VULNERABLE function withdraw() external { uint256 amount = balances[msg.sender]; (bool ok, ) = msg.sender.call{value: amount}( ); // attacker re-enters here... require(ok); balances[msg.sender] = 0; // ...before this runs } // SAFE: checks, effects, interactions function withdraw() external nonReentrant { uint256 amount = balances[msg.sender]; if (amount == 0) revert NothingToWithdraw(); // checks balances[msg.sender] = 0; // effects (update state first) (bool ok, ) = msg.sender.call{value: amount}( ); // interactions (external calls last) if (!ok) revert TransferFailed(); } Follow checks-effects-interactions everywhere and add OpenZeppelin's ReentrancyGuard ( nonReentrant ) to functions that move value. Watch for read-only reentrancy too: other protocols reading your state mid-update. 2. Access control Missing onlyOwner on a mint or upgrade function. An initialize function anyone can call first. Using tx.origin (the original signer) instead of msg.sender for auth, which lets a malicious contract act on a user's behalf. Use OpenZeppelin Ownable2Step or AccessControl with named roles. Put privileged roles behind a multisig (Safe) and a timelock so users can see changes coming. Write a test for every privileged function proving strangers are rejected. 3. Oracle and price manipulation If your protocol reads a token's price from a single DEX pool's current reserves, an attacker can borrow millions in a flash loan , skew that pool, exploit your protocol at the fake price, and repay the loan, all in one transaction. Use robust oracles (Chainlink price feeds) or time-weighted average prices (TWAPs), never spot prices from one pool. Check oracle data is fresh and within sane bounds. 4. Arithmetic and precision Solidity 0.8+ reverts on overflow, but precision bugs remain: uint256 fee = amount / 1000 * 3; // BAD: divides first, small amounts round to zero uint256 fee = amount * 3 / 1000; // better: multiply before dividing Decide who rounding should favour (almost always the protocol, never the user who can repeat it). Be careful with unchecked blocks and with tokens that have different decimals (USDC has 6, most have 18). 5. Front-running and MEV Pending transactions are public. Bots can see your large swap and trade before and after it ( sandwich ), profiting from the price movement they caused. Swaps need a slippage limit ( minAmountOut ) and a deadline . Commit-reveal schemes hide sensitive values until it is too late to front-run. 6. Signature replay A signed message (a permit, an off-chain order) must be usable once, on one chain, for one contract. Include a nonce , the chain ID , the contract address and an expiry : exactly what EIP-712 domains and OpenZeppelin's EIP712 and Nonces give you. Use ECDSA.recover from OpenZeppelin, which rejects malleable signatures. 7. Denial of service Looping over an array that users can grow will eventually exceed the block gas limit and brick the function. Sending ETH to a list of addresses in one loop fails entirely if one recipient reverts. Prefer pull payments : record what each user is owed and let them withdraw. 8. Unsafe token interactions Some tokens do not return true from transfer , charge fees on transfer, or rebase balances. Use OpenZeppelin's SafeERC20 ( safeTransfer , safeTransferFrom ), and measure balances before and after if fee-on-transfer tokens are possible. 9. Upgrade and initialisation bugs Proxies s","track":"crypto"},{"id":"crypto-testing","title":"Fuzzing, invariants and auditing tools","level":"advanced","minutes":40,"summary":"Go beyond unit tests with fuzz, invariant and fork tests in Foundry, then use Slither and an audit mindset.","html":"<h2 id=\"the-testing-ladder-for-contracts\">The testing ladder for contracts</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Question it answers</th><th>Foundry feature</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Unit</td><td>Does this function do what I intended for these inputs?</td><td><code>test_...</code> functions</td></tr><tr><td>Fuzz</td><td>Does it hold for thousands of random inputs?</td><td>Test functions with parameters</td></tr><tr><td>Invariant</td><td>Do core truths hold after any sequence of calls?</td><td><code>invariant_...</code> functions</td></tr><tr><td>Fork</td><td>Does it work against real deployed protocols?</td><td><code>--fork-url</code></td></tr><tr><td>Static analysis</td><td>Are there known bug patterns?</td><td>Slither, Aderyn</td></tr><tr><td>Formal verification</td><td>Can we prove it for all inputs?</td><td>Halmos, Certora, Kontrol</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"fuzz-testing\">Fuzz testing</h2>\n<p>Any test function with parameters is fuzzed: Foundry calls it with many random values, and shrinks any failure to a minimal example.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"solidity\"><pre><code class=\"language-solidity\">function testFuzz_WithdrawNeverExceedsDeposit(uint96 deposit, uint96 withdrawAmount) public {\n    vm.assume(deposit &gt; 0);                       // skip inputs you do not care about\n    withdrawAmount = uint96(bound(withdrawAmount, 0, deposit)); // keep values in a valid range\n    vm.deal(alice, deposit);\n\n    vm.startPrank(alice);\n    vault.deposit{value: deposit}();\n    vault.withdraw(withdrawAmount);\n    vm.stopPrank();\n\n    assertEq(vault.balanceOf(alice), deposit - withdrawAmount);\n    assertEq(address(vault).balance, deposit - withdrawAmount);\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>Prefer <code>bound</code> over <code>vm.assume</code> when possible: <code>assume</code> throws inputs away, <code>bound</code> maps them into range.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"invariant-testing\">Invariant testing</h2>\n<p>An <strong>invariant</strong> is something that must always be true, whatever anyone does. For example:</p>\n<ul><li>The sum of all user balances equals the contract's ETH balance.</li><li>Total token supply never exceeds the cap.</li><li>A vault's share price never decreases (absent losses).</li></ul>\n<p>Foundry calls random sequences of functions on a <strong>handler</strong> contract, then checks your invariants after each call.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"solidity\"><pre><code class=\"language-solidity\">// test/invariant/VaultInvariants.t.sol\ncontract Handler is Test {\n    Vault vault;\n    address[] public actors;\n    uint256 public ghostDeposited;      // &quot;ghost variables&quot; track what the totals should be\n    uint256 public ghostWithdrawn;\n\n    constructor(Vault _vault) {\n        vault = _vault;\n        actors.push(makeAddr(&quot;a&quot;));\n        actors.push(makeAddr(&quot;b&quot;));\n    }\n\n    function deposit(uint256 actorSeed, uint96 amount) external {\n        address actor = actors[actorSeed % actors.length];\n        amount = uint96(bound(amount, 1, 100 ether));\n        vm.deal(actor, amount);\n        vm.prank(actor);\n        vault.deposit{value: amount}();\n        ghostDeposited += amount;\n    }\n\n    function withdraw(uint256 actorSeed, uint96 amount) external {\n        address actor = actors[actorSeed % actors.length];\n        amount = uint96(bound(amount, 0, vault.balanceOf(actor)));\n        vm.prank(actor);\n        vault.withdraw(amount);\n        ghostWithdrawn += amount;\n    }\n}\n\ncontract VaultInvariants is Test {\n    Vault vault;\n    Handler handler;\n\n    function setUp() public {\n        vault = new Vault();\n        handler = new Handler(vault);\n        targetContract(address(handler));    // only call the handler's functions\n    }\n\n    function invariant_SolventAtAllTimes() public view {\n        assertEq(address(vault).balance, handler.ghostDeposited() - handler.ghostWithdrawn());\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>Invariant tests find the bugs nobody thought to write a unit test for: strange orderings, repeated calls, rounding drift.</p>\n<h2 id=\"fork-testing\">Fork testing</h2>\n<p>Test against real mainnet state (real USDC, real Uniswap pools) on a local copy:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">forge test --fork-url $MAINNET_RPC_URL --match-contract ForkTest -vvv</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"coverage-and-gas\">Coverage and gas</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">forge coverage            # which lines and branches your tests hit\nforge test --gas-report   # gas per function\nforge snapshot            # save gas usage; compare after optimising</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"static-analysis\">Static analysis</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">uv tool install slither-analyzer\nslither .</code></pre></div>\n<p>Slither flags reentrancy, unprotected functions, shadowing, dangerous calls and dozens more patterns in seconds. Treat each finding as a question: fix it, or write down why it is safe. Aderyn is another fast analyser worth running.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"how-auditors-think\">How auditors think</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Understand intent first.</strong> Read the docs and specs. What must never happen?</li><li><strong>List the actors and their powers.</strong> Users, admins, keepers, oracles, other contracts.</li><li><strong>Follow the money.</strong> Every path where value enters or leaves.</li><li><strong>Question every assumption.</strong> What if this token charges fees? What if the oracle is stale? What if this is called twice, in the same block, with zero, with the maximum?</li><li><strong>Write a proof-of-concept test</strong> for every suspected bug. If you cannot exploit it in a test, keep digging or move on.</li></ol>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Your security learning path</p><p>Ethernaut, then Damn Vulnerable DeFi, then read real audit reports, then join a competitive audit as a learner. Cyfrin Updraft's free security course covers this whole path.</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"What is the advantage of bound() over vm.assume() in fuzz tests?","options":["bound maps inputs into range instead of discarding them, so fewer runs are wasted","bound makes tests deterministic","assume does not work with uint256"],"answer":0,"explain":"Too many assume rejections can make a fuzz run fail to find enough valid inputs."},{"q":"Which is a good invariant for a simple ETH vault?","options":["Every deposit is exactly 1 ETH","The contract's ETH balance equals total deposits minus total withdrawals","Users can only withdraw once"],"answer":1,"explain":"Invariants are truths that must hold after any sequence of actions."},{"q":"What does a fork test let you do?","options":["Deploy to mainnet for free","Run tests against a local copy of real chain state, including real deployed protocols","Split a contract into two"],"answer":1,"explain":"Forks let you integrate with real tokens and pools safely."},{"q":"Slither reports a possible reentrancy. What is the professional response?","options":["Ignore it: static tools have false positives","Investigate; fix it or document exactly why it is safe, ideally with a test","Delete the function"],"answer":1,"explain":"Every finding gets an explicit decision."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Write a fuzz test","file":"LadderToken.t.sol","lang":"solidity","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write <code>testFuzz_Transfer(uint256 amount)</code>: bound <code>amount</code> between 0 and the owner's balance, prank as <code>owner</code>, transfer <code>amount</code> to <code>alice</code>, and assert alice's balance equals <code>amount</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"function testFuzz_Transfer(uint256 amount) public {\n    amount = bound(amount, 0, token.balanceOf(owner));\n    vm.prank(owner);\n    token.transfer(alice, amount);\n    assertEq(token.balanceOf(alice), amount);\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"function\\s+testFuzz_Transfer\\s*\\(\\s*uint256\\s+amount\\s*\\)\\s*public\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>function testFuzz_Transfer(uint256 amount) public {</code> (the parameter makes it a fuzz test)."},{"re":"amount\\s*=\\s*bound\\(\\s*amount\\s*,\\s*0\\s*,\\s*token\\.balanceOf\\(\\s*owner\\s*\\)\\s*\\)\\s*;","text":"Line 2: <code>amount = bound(amount, 0, token.balanceOf(owner));</code>"},{"re":"vm\\.prank\\(\\s*owner\\s*\\)\\s*;","text":"Line 3: <code>vm.prank(owner);</code>"},{"re":"token\\.transfer\\(\\s*alice\\s*,\\s*amount\\s*\\)\\s*;","text":"Line 4: <code>token.transfer(alice, amount);</code>"},{"re":"assertEq\\(\\s*token\\.balanceOf\\(\\s*alice\\s*\\)\\s*,\\s*amount\\s*\\)\\s*;","text":"Line 5: <code>assertEq(token.balanceOf(alice), amount);</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"Run the safety checks","file":"Terminal","lang":"bash","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Three commands: measure test coverage, run the tests with a gas report, and run Slither on the current folder.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"forge coverage\nforge test --gas-report\nslither .","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*forge\\s+coverage\\s*$","text":"Line 1: <code>forge coverage</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*forge\\s+test\\s+--gas-report\\s*$","text":"Line 2: <code>forge test --gas-report</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*slither\\s+\\.\\s*$","text":"Line 3: <code>slither .</code> (the dot means this folder)."}],"explain":""}],"text":"The testing ladder for contracts Level Question it answers Foundry feature Unit Does this function do what I intended for these inputs? test_... functions Fuzz Does it hold for thousands of random inputs? Test functions with parameters Invariant Do core truths hold after any sequence of calls? invariant_... functions Fork Does it work against real deployed protocols? --fork-url Static analysis Are there known bug patterns? Slither, Aderyn Formal verification Can we prove it for all inputs? Halmos, Certora, Kontrol Fuzz testing Any test function with parameters is fuzzed: Foundry calls it with many random values, and shrinks any failure to a minimal example. function testFuzz_WithdrawNeverExceedsDeposit(uint96 deposit, uint96 withdrawAmount) public { vm.assume(deposit 0); // skip inputs you do not care about withdrawAmount = uint96(bound(withdrawAmount, 0, deposit)); // keep values in a valid range vm.deal(alice, deposit); vm.startPrank(alice); vault.deposit{value: deposit}(); vault.withdraw(withdrawAmount); vm.stopPrank(); assertEq(vault.balanceOf(alice), deposit - withdrawAmount); assertEq(address(vault).balance, deposit - withdrawAmount); } Prefer bound over vm.assume when possible: assume throws inputs away, bound maps them into range. Invariant testing An invariant is something that must always be true, whatever anyone does. For example: The sum of all user balances equals the contract's ETH balance. Total token supply never exceeds the cap. A vault's share price never decreases (absent losses). Foundry calls random sequences of functions on a handler contract, then checks your invariants after each call. // test/invariant/VaultInvariants.t.sol contract Handler is Test { Vault vault; address[] public actors; uint256 public ghostDeposited; // ghost variables track what the totals should be uint256 public ghostWithdrawn; constructor(Vault _vault) { vault = _vault; actors.push(makeAddr( a )); actors.push(makeAddr( b )); } function deposit(uint256 actorSeed, uint96 amount) external { address actor = actors[actorSeed % actors.length]; amount = uint96(bound(amount, 1, 100 ether)); vm.deal(actor, amount); vm.prank(actor); vault.deposit{value: amount}(); ghostDeposited += amount; } function withdraw(uint256 actorSeed, uint96 amount) external { address actor = actors[actorSeed % actors.length]; amount = uint96(bound(amount, 0, vault.balanceOf(actor))); vm.prank(actor); vault.withdraw(amount); ghostWithdrawn += amount; } } contract VaultInvariants is Test { Vault vault; Handler handler; function setUp() public { vault = new Vault(); handler = new Handler(vault); targetContract(address(handler)); // only call the handler's functions } function invariant_SolventAtAllTimes() public view { assertEq(address(vault).balance, handler.ghostDeposited() - handler.ghostWithdrawn()); } } Invariant tests find the bugs nobody thought to write a unit test for: strange orderings, repeated calls, rounding drift. Fork testing Test against real mainnet state (real USDC, real Uniswap pools) on a local copy: forge test --fork-url $MAINNET_RPC_URL --match-contract ForkTest -vvv Coverage and gas forge coverage # which lines and branches your tests hit forge test --gas-report # gas per function forge snapshot # save gas usage; compare after optimising Static analysis uv tool install slither-analyzer slither . Slither flags reentrancy, unprotected functions, shadowing, dangerous calls and dozens more patterns in seconds. Treat each finding as a question: fix it, or write down why it is safe. Aderyn is another fast analyser worth running. How auditors think Understand intent first. Read the docs and specs. What must never happen? List the actors and their powers. Users, admins, keepers, oracles, other contracts. Follow the money. Every path where value enters or leaves. Question every assumption. What if this token charges fees? What if the oracle is stale? What if this is called twice, in the same block, with zero, with the maximum? Write a proof-of-concept ","track":"crypto"},{"id":"crypto-advanced-eng","title":"Upgradeability, gas optimisation and Layer 2s","level":"advanced","minutes":35,"summary":"When and how to use proxies safely, where gas really goes, and how rollups and account abstraction change deployment.","html":"<h2 id=\"upgradeability-a-trade-off-not-a-default\">Upgradeability: a trade-off, not a default</h2>\n<p>Deployed bytecode cannot change. <strong>Proxy patterns</strong> work around that: users call a proxy contract that holds the storage and forwards every call (with <code>delegatecall</code>) to a separate <strong>implementation</strong> contract. To upgrade, the admin points the proxy at a new implementation.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Upgrade logic lives in</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Transparent proxy</strong></td><td>The proxy (with a separate admin)</td><td>Simple mental model, slightly more gas</td></tr><tr><td><strong>UUPS</strong></td><td>The implementation</td><td>Cheaper; you must keep the upgrade function in every version</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Beacon</strong></td><td>A shared beacon contract</td><td>Upgrade many proxies at once</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Rules that prevent disasters:</p>\n<ul><li>Use OpenZeppelin's upgradeable contracts and their <strong>Foundry Upgrades</strong> plugin, which checks storage layout between versions.</li><li><strong>Never reorder, remove or change the type of existing storage variables.</strong> Only append new ones. Otherwise the new code reads old data in the wrong slots.</li><li>Constructors do not run for proxies. Use an <code>initializer</code> function, protect it, and call <code>_disableInitializers()</code> in the implementation's constructor.</li><li>Put upgrade rights behind a multisig and a timelock.</li></ul>\n<p>The honest trade-off: an upgradeable contract is only as trustworthy as whoever can upgrade it. Many protocols launch upgradeable, then renounce or restrict upgrades once mature. Immutability is a feature users value.</p>\n<h2 id=\"where-gas-actually-goes\">Where gas actually goes</h2>\n<p>Storage is by far the most expensive thing a contract does. Writing a new non-zero storage slot costs about 20,000 gas; reading a slot for the first time in a transaction costs about 2,100; memory and arithmetic cost a few gas each.</p>\n<p>Optimisations that matter, in order:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Touch storage less.</strong> Read a storage variable into a local variable once, use it, write it back once.</li><li><strong>Pack variables.</strong> Several small types declared next to each other share one 32-byte slot.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"solidity\"><pre><code class=\"language-solidity\">// 3 slots\nuint256 amount;\nuint64 start;\nuint256 total;\n\n// 2 slots: start and duration are packed together\nuint256 amount;\nuint64 start;\nuint64 duration;</code></pre></div>\n<ol><li><strong>Use <code>constant</code> and <code>immutable</code></strong> for values that never change; they live in the bytecode, not storage.</li><li><strong>Use <code>calldata</code></strong> for read-only array and string parameters.</li><li><strong>Custom errors</strong> instead of revert strings.</li><li><strong>Emit events</strong> for data only off-chain apps need, instead of storing it.</li></ol>\n<p>Then measure with <code>forge test --gas-report</code> and <code>forge snapshot</code>. Never sacrifice readability or safety for tiny savings; a gas optimisation that introduces a bug is the most expensive kind.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"layer-2-rollups\">Layer 2 rollups</h2>\n<p>Rollups run transactions off Ethereum mainnet, then post the data (and proofs) back to it, inheriting much of its security at a fraction of the cost.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>How correctness is ensured</th><th>Withdrawal to L1</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Optimistic</strong></td><td>Assumed valid; anyone can submit a fraud proof during a challenge window</td><td>About 7 days via the native bridge</td><td>Arbitrum, Optimism, Base</td></tr><tr><td><strong>ZK (validity)</strong></td><td>A cryptographic proof is verified on L1 for every batch</td><td>Faster, once the proof is verified</td><td>zkSync, Starknet, Scroll, Linea</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>For developers: most EVM rollups run the same Solidity, the same Foundry and the same viem, with a different chain ID and RPC URL. Watch for differences in <code>block.number</code> and timestamps, precompiles, and gas pricing (L2 fees include the cost of posting data to L1). Bridges between chains are a major source of hacks; use canonical bridges and well-audited messaging.</p>\n<h2 id=\"account-abstraction\">Account abstraction</h2>\n<p>Traditional accounts (EOAs) are a single private key: lose it and everything is lost; every action needs ETH for gas.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>ERC-4337</strong> smart accounts are contracts: they can have multiple signers, spending limits, social recovery, session keys, batched actions, and <strong>paymasters</strong> that sponsor gas (users can pay in USDC or not at all).</li><li><strong>EIP-7702</strong> (live since Ethereum's 2025 Pectra upgrade) lets an existing EOA delegate to smart-account code, bringing batching and sponsorship to normal wallets.</li></ul>\n<p>For dApp builders this means far better onboarding: users sign in with a passkey and never touch a seed phrase or buy ETH for gas first.</p>","quiz":[{"q":"Version 2 of an upgradeable contract inserts a new variable above existing ones. What goes wrong?","options":["Nothing, the compiler fixes it","Storage slots shift, so the new code reads existing data from the wrong places","Gas costs double"],"answer":1,"explain":"Only append new storage variables. Use tooling that validates layouts."},{"q":"Which change usually saves the most gas?","options":["Reducing storage reads and writes","Shortening variable names","Removing comments"],"answer":0,"explain":"Storage operations cost thousands of gas; names and comments cost nothing at runtime."},{"q":"Why do withdrawals from optimistic rollups to Ethereum take about a week via the native bridge?","options":["Validators are slow","There is a challenge window for fraud proofs before the withdrawal is final","Ethereum limits withdrawals"],"answer":1,"explain":"Optimistic rollups assume validity unless challenged within the window."},{"q":"What can an ERC-4337 paymaster do?","options":["Mine blocks","Sponsor users' gas fees, or let them pay in another token","Recover lost seed phrases"],"answer":1,"explain":"Paymasters enable gasless and token-paid transactions for smart accounts."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Values that cost no storage","file":"Token.sol","lang":"solidity","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Two declarations that live in bytecode, not storage: a <code>public constant</code> <code>uint256</code> called <code>MAX_SUPPLY</code> equal to <code>1_000_000_000 ether</code>, and a <code>public immutable</code> <code>address</code> called <code>owner</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"uint256 public constant MAX_SUPPLY = 1_000_000_000 ether;\naddress public immutable owner;","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"uint256\\s+public\\s+constant\\s+MAX_SUPPLY\\s*=\\s*1_000_000_000\\s+ether\\s*;","text":"Line 1: <code>uint256 public constant MAX_SUPPLY = 1_000_000_000 ether;</code>"},{"re":"address\\s+public\\s+immutable\\s+owner\\s*;","text":"Line 2: <code>address public immutable owner;</code> (set once, in the constructor)."}],"explain":""},{"title":"Pack a struct into one slot","file":"Vesting.sol","lang":"solidity","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write a struct <code>Grant</code> with three fields that pack into a single 32-byte storage slot: <code>uint128 amount</code>, <code>uint64 start</code>, <code>uint64 duration</code> (128 + 64 + 64 = 256 bits).</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"struct Grant {\n    uint128 amount;\n    uint64 start;\n    uint64 duration;\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*struct\\s+Grant\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>struct Grant {</code>"},{"re":"uint128\\s+amount\\s*;","text":"<code>uint128 amount;</code>"},{"re":"uint64\\s+start\\s*;","text":"<code>uint64 start;</code>"},{"re":"uint64\\s+duration\\s*;","text":"<code>uint64 duration;</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Three <code>uint256</code> fields would use three slots: about 60,000 gas to write fresh. Packed, it is one slot. <code>uint128</code> still holds amounts up to about 3.4 x 10^38, plenty for 18-decimal tokens.</p>"}],"text":"Upgradeability: a trade-off, not a default Deployed bytecode cannot change. Proxy patterns work around that: users call a proxy contract that holds the storage and forwards every call (with delegatecall ) to a separate implementation contract. To upgrade, the admin points the proxy at a new implementation. Pattern Upgrade logic lives in Notes Transparent proxy The proxy (with a separate admin) Simple mental model, slightly more gas UUPS The implementation Cheaper; you must keep the upgrade function in every version Beacon A shared beacon contract Upgrade many proxies at once Rules that prevent disasters: Use OpenZeppelin's upgradeable contracts and their Foundry Upgrades plugin, which checks storage layout between versions. Never reorder, remove or change the type of existing storage variables. Only append new ones. Otherwise the new code reads old data in the wrong slots. Constructors do not run for proxies. Use an initializer function, protect it, and call _disableInitializers() in the implementation's constructor. Put upgrade rights behind a multisig and a timelock. The honest trade-off: an upgradeable contract is only as trustworthy as whoever can upgrade it. Many protocols launch upgradeable, then renounce or restrict upgrades once mature. Immutability is a feature users value. Where gas actually goes Storage is by far the most expensive thing a contract does. Writing a new non-zero storage slot costs about 20,000 gas; reading a slot for the first time in a transaction costs about 2,100; memory and arithmetic cost a few gas each. Optimisations that matter, in order: Touch storage less. Read a storage variable into a local variable once, use it, write it back once. Pack variables. Several small types declared next to each other share one 32-byte slot. // 3 slots uint256 amount; uint64 start; uint256 total; // 2 slots: start and duration are packed together uint256 amount; uint64 start; uint64 duration; Use constant and immutable for values that never change; they live in the bytecode, not storage. Use calldata for read-only array and string parameters. Custom errors instead of revert strings. Emit events for data only off-chain apps need, instead of storing it. Then measure with forge test --gas-report and forge snapshot . Never sacrifice readability or safety for tiny savings; a gas optimisation that introduces a bug is the most expensive kind. Layer 2 rollups Rollups run transactions off Ethereum mainnet, then post the data (and proofs) back to it, inheriting much of its security at a fraction of the cost. Type How correctness is ensured Withdrawal to L1 Examples Optimistic Assumed valid; anyone can submit a fraud proof during a challenge window About 7 days via the native bridge Arbitrum, Optimism, Base ZK (validity) A cryptographic proof is verified on L1 for every batch Faster, once the proof is verified zkSync, Starknet, Scroll, Linea For developers: most EVM rollups run the same Solidity, the same Foundry and the same viem, with a different chain ID and RPC URL. Watch for differences in block.number and timestamps, precompiles, and gas pricing (L2 fees include the cost of posting data to L1). Bridges between chains are a major source of hacks; use canonical bridges and well-audited messaging. Account abstraction Traditional accounts (EOAs) are a single private key: lose it and everything is lost; every action needs ETH for gas. ERC-4337 smart accounts are contracts: they can have multiple signers, spending limits, social recovery, session keys, batched actions, and paymasters that sponsor gas (users can pay in USDC or not at all). EIP-7702 (live since Ethereum's 2025 Pectra upgrade) lets an existing EOA delegate to smart-account code, bringing batching and sponsorship to normal wallets. For dApp builders this means far better onboarding: users sign in with a passkey and never touch a seed phrase or buy ETH for gas first.","track":"crypto"},{"id":"crypto-advanced-crypto","title":"Advanced cryptography: curves, Merkle proofs and zero knowledge","level":"advanced","minutes":40,"summary":"How elliptic curves and ECDSA work, build and verify Merkle proofs, and an honest introduction to zero-knowledge proofs.","html":"<h2 id=\"elliptic-curves-in-one-page\">Elliptic curves in one page</h2>\n<p>An elliptic curve is a set of points satisfying an equation like <code>y² = x³ + 7</code> (Bitcoin and Ethereum's <strong>secp256k1</strong>), computed over a huge prime field so all numbers wrap around.</p>\n<p>You can &quot;add&quot; two points on the curve to get a third. Adding a starting point <strong>G</strong> to itself <code>k</code> times is called <strong>scalar multiplication</strong>: <code>P = k × G</code>.</p>\n<ul><li>Computing <code>P</code> from <code>k</code> is fast (using doubling, about 256 steps).</li><li>Recovering <code>k</code> from <code>P</code> and <code>G</code> is the <strong>elliptic curve discrete logarithm problem</strong>: no known method does it in any practical time for well-chosen curves.</li></ul>\n<p>That one-way function is the whole trick: your <strong>private key</strong> is <code>k</code>, your <strong>public key</strong> is <code>P = k × G</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"ecdsa-and-why-nonces-matter\">ECDSA and why nonces matter</h2>\n<p>To sign a message hash <code>z</code>, the signer picks a secret one-time number <code>k</code> (the <strong>nonce</strong>), computes a point from it, and produces a signature <code>(r, s)</code> from <code>z</code>, <code>k</code> and the private key. Anyone can verify <code>(r, s)</code> against the public key.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Never reuse an ECDSA nonce</p><p>If the same <code>k</code> is used for two different messages, simple algebra reveals the private key. This famously broke the PlayStation 3's code-signing key in 2010, and weak random number generators have emptied Bitcoin wallets. Modern libraries derive <code>k</code> deterministically (RFC 6979). Another reason never to implement signatures yourself.</p></aside>\n<p><strong>Ed25519</strong> (an Edwards curve signature scheme) is designed to avoid these pitfalls and is the default for SSH keys, Solana and many modern systems.</p>\n<h2 id=\"merkle-trees-and-proofs\">Merkle trees and proofs</h2>\n<p>A Merkle tree hashes items in pairs, then hashes those hashes in pairs, until one <strong>root</strong> remains.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">                 root = H(H12 + H34)\n               /                     \\\n        H12 = H(H1+H2)          H34 = H(H3+H4)\n         /        \\               /        \\\n     H1=H(A)   H2=H(B)       H3=H(C)    H4=H(D)</code></pre></div>\n<p>To prove <code>C</code> is in the set, you only need <code>H4</code> and <code>H12</code>: hash <code>C</code>, combine with <code>H4</code>, combine with <code>H12</code>, and compare with the root. For a million items, a proof is only about 20 hashes.</p>\n<p>This is how airdrops work cheaply: store only the 32-byte root on-chain; each user submits their own proof.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-try=\"1\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">// A tiny Merkle tree with SHA-256 and sorted pairs (the convention OpenZeppelin uses, with keccak256)\nconst enc = new TextEncoder();\nconst toHex = (buf) =&gt; [...new Uint8Array(buf)].map((b) =&gt; b.toString(16).padStart(2, '0')).join('');\nconst hash = async (s) =&gt; toHex(await crypto.subtle.digest('SHA-256', enc.encode(s)));\nconst pair = (a, b) =&gt; hash(a &lt; b ? a + b : b + a);\n\nconst items = ['alice:100', 'bob:250', 'carol:75', 'dave:300'];\nconst leaves = await Promise.all(items.map(hash));\n\n// build the tree level by level\nconst levels = [leaves];\nwhile (levels.at(-1).length &gt; 1) {\n  const prev = levels.at(-1), next = [];\n  for (let i = 0; i &lt; prev.length; i += 2) next.push(await pair(prev[i], prev[i + 1] ?? prev[i]));\n  levels.push(next);\n}\nconst root = levels.at(-1)[0];\nconsole.log('root', root);\n\n// proof for carol: the sibling at each level\nfunction proofFor(index) {\n  const proof = [];\n  for (const level of levels.slice(0, -1)) {\n    const sibling = index % 2 ? level[index - 1] : level[index + 1] ?? level[index];\n    proof.push(sibling);\n    index = Math.floor(index / 2);\n  }\n  return proof;\n}\nconst proof = proofFor(2);\n\nasync function verify(item, proof, root) {\n  let h = await hash(item);\n  for (const sib of proof) h = await pair(h, sib);\n  return h === root;\n}\nconsole.log('carol:75 included?', await verify('carol:75', proof, root));   // true\nconsole.log('carol:999 included?', await verify('carol:999', proof, root)); // false</code></pre></div>\n<p>In Solidity, OpenZeppelin's <code>MerkleProof.verify(proof, root, leaf)</code> does the checking. Off-chain, <code>@openzeppelin/merkle-tree</code> builds compatible trees. Hash leaves twice (or with a distinct prefix) to prevent a known second-preimage trick where an inner node is passed off as a leaf.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"zero-knowledge-proofs\">Zero-knowledge proofs</h2>\n<p>A <strong>zero-knowledge proof</strong> convinces a verifier that a statement is true without revealing why:</p>\n<ul><li>&quot;I know a password that hashes to this value&quot; without revealing the password.</li><li>&quot;I am over 18&quot; without revealing a birthdate.</li><li>&quot;These 10,000 transactions were all executed correctly&quot; with one short proof (this is what ZK rollups do).</li></ul>\n<p>In practice you write the statement as a <strong>circuit</strong> (a program with arithmetic constraints), and a proving system produces a proof that verifies quickly.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Family</th><th>Proof size</th><th>Trusted setup?</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>SNARKs</strong> (Groth16, PLONK)</td><td>Tiny, cheap to verify on-chain</td><td>Often (or universal)</td><td>Most common on Ethereum</td></tr><tr><td><strong>STARKs</strong></td><td>Larger</td><td>No</td><td>Transparent, based on hashes</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Tools to explore later: <strong>Circom</strong> with snarkjs, <strong>Noir</strong> (a Rust-like ZK language), and zkVMs like RISC Zero and SP1 that prove ordinary Rust programs. Start with Noir's tutorials once you are solid in Solidity.</p>\n<h2 id=\"looking-ahead-post-quantum-cryptography\">Looking ahead: post-quantum cryptography</h2>\n<p>Large quantum computers would break elliptic-curve and RSA cryptography (not hash functions or AES-256 to the same degree). In 2024, NIST published the first post-quantum standards, including ML-KEM for key exchange and ML-DSA for signatures, and browsers and messaging apps have begun adopting hybrid schemes. Blockchains are researching migration paths. You do not need to act now, but you should know why &quot;harvest now, decrypt later&quot; matters for long-lived secrets.</p>","quiz":[{"q":"What makes scalar multiplication on an elliptic curve useful for keys?","options":["k x G is easy to compute, but recovering k from the result is infeasible","It is reversible with the right password","It produces 12-word phrases"],"answer":0,"explain":"That one-way function turns a private key into a public key safely."},{"q":"An ECDSA signer reuses the same nonce for two different messages. What happens?","options":["Both signatures are invalid","Anyone can compute the private key from the two signatures","Nothing, nonces are optional"],"answer":1,"explain":"Nonce reuse is catastrophic for ECDSA."},{"q":"A Merkle tree has about one million leaves. Roughly how many hashes are in an inclusion proof?","options":["1,000,000","1,000","About 20"],"answer":2,"explain":"Proof size grows with log2 of the number of leaves."},{"q":"What do ZK rollups use zero-knowledge proofs for?","options":["Hiding all transactions forever","Proving to Ethereum that a large batch of transactions was executed correctly","Generating seed phrases"],"answer":1,"explain":"One small validity proof replaces re-executing every transaction on L1."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Verify a Merkle proof on-chain","file":"Airdrop.sol","lang":"solidity","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Two lines inside a <code>claim(uint256 amount, bytes32[] calldata proof)</code> function:</p>\n<ol><li>Build the leaf the way OpenZeppelin's Merkle tree library does, hashing twice: <code>bytes32 leaf = keccak256(bytes.concat(keccak256(abi.encode(msg.sender, amount))));</code></li><li>Require that <code>MerkleProof.verify(proof, root, leaf)</code> is true, with the message <code>&quot;Not eligible&quot;</code>.</li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"bytes32 leaf = keccak256(bytes.concat(keccak256(abi.encode(msg.sender, amount))));\nrequire(MerkleProof.verify(proof, root, leaf), \"Not eligible\");","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"bytes32\\s+leaf\\s*=\\s*keccak256\\(\\s*bytes\\.concat\\(","text":"Line 1 starts <code>bytes32 leaf = keccak256(bytes.concat(</code>"},{"re":"keccak256\\(\\s*abi\\.encode\\(\\s*msg\\.sender\\s*,\\s*amount\\s*\\)\\s*\\)\\s*\\)\\s*\\)\\s*;","text":"then the inner hash <code>keccak256(abi.encode(msg.sender, amount))</code> and three closing brackets plus a semicolon."},{"re":"require\\(\\s*MerkleProof\\.verify\\(\\s*proof\\s*,\\s*root\\s*,\\s*leaf\\s*\\)\\s*,\\s*[\"']Not eligible[\"']\\s*\\)\\s*;","text":"Line 2: <code>require(MerkleProof.verify(proof, root, leaf), &quot;Not eligible&quot;);</code>"}],"explain":""}],"text":"Elliptic curves in one page An elliptic curve is a set of points satisfying an equation like y² = x³ + 7 (Bitcoin and Ethereum's secp256k1 ), computed over a huge prime field so all numbers wrap around. You can add two points on the curve to get a third. Adding a starting point G to itself k times is called scalar multiplication : P = k × G . Computing P from k is fast (using doubling, about 256 steps). Recovering k from P and G is the elliptic curve discrete logarithm problem : no known method does it in any practical time for well-chosen curves. That one-way function is the whole trick: your private key is k , your public key is P = k × G . ECDSA and why nonces matter To sign a message hash z , the signer picks a secret one-time number k (the nonce ), computes a point from it, and produces a signature (r, s) from z , k and the private key. Anyone can verify (r, s) against the public key. Never reuse an ECDSA nonce If the same k is used for two different messages, simple algebra reveals the private key. This famously broke the PlayStation 3's code-signing key in 2010, and weak random number generators have emptied Bitcoin wallets. Modern libraries derive k deterministically (RFC 6979). Another reason never to implement signatures yourself. Ed25519 (an Edwards curve signature scheme) is designed to avoid these pitfalls and is the default for SSH keys, Solana and many modern systems. Merkle trees and proofs A Merkle tree hashes items in pairs, then hashes those hashes in pairs, until one root remains. root = H(H12 + H34) / \\ H12 = H(H1+H2) H34 = H(H3+H4) / \\ / \\ H1=H(A) H2=H(B) H3=H(C) H4=H(D) To prove C is in the set, you only need H4 and H12 : hash C , combine with H4 , combine with H12 , and compare with the root. For a million items, a proof is only about 20 hashes. This is how airdrops work cheaply: store only the 32-byte root on-chain; each user submits their own proof. // A tiny Merkle tree with SHA-256 and sorted pairs (the convention OpenZeppelin uses, with keccak256) const enc = new TextEncoder(); const toHex = (buf) = [...new Uint8Array(buf)].map((b) = b.toString(16).padStart(2, '0')).join(''); const hash = async (s) = toHex(await crypto.subtle.digest('SHA-256', enc.encode(s))); const pair = (a, b) = hash(a b ? a + b : b + a); const items = ['alice:100', 'bob:250', 'carol:75', 'dave:300']; const leaves = await Promise.all(items.map(hash)); // build the tree level by level const levels = [leaves]; while (levels.at(-1).length 1) { const prev = levels.at(-1), next = []; for (let i = 0; i prev.length; i += 2) next.push(await pair(prev[i], prev[i + 1] ?? prev[i])); levels.push(next); } const root = levels.at(-1)[0]; console.log('root', root); // proof for carol: the sibling at each level function proofFor(index) { const proof = []; for (const level of levels.slice(0, -1)) { const sibling = index % 2 ? level[index - 1] : level[index + 1] ?? level[index]; proof.push(sibling); index = Math.floor(index / 2); } return proof; } const proof = proofFor(2); async function verify(item, proof, root) { let h = await hash(item); for (const sib of proof) h = await pair(h, sib); return h === root; } console.log('carol:75 included?', await verify('carol:75', proof, root)); // true console.log('carol:999 included?', await verify('carol:999', proof, root)); // false In Solidity, OpenZeppelin's MerkleProof.verify(proof, root, leaf) does the checking. Off-chain, @openzeppelin/merkle-tree builds compatible trees. Hash leaves twice (or with a distinct prefix) to prevent a known second-preimage trick where an inner node is passed off as a leaf. Zero-knowledge proofs A zero-knowledge proof convinces a verifier that a statement is true without revealing why: I know a password that hashes to this value without revealing the password. I am over 18 without revealing a birthdate. These 10,000 transactions were all executed correctly with one short proof (this is what ZK rollups do). In practice you write the statement as a circuit (a program with ari","track":"crypto"},{"id":"sec-mindset","title":"The security mindset","level":"beginner","minutes":25,"summary":"What security protects, who attacks and why, how to think about threats, and the US laws (the CFAA and friends) that keep your learning legal.","html":"<h2 id=\"why-a-whole-track-on-security\">Why a whole track on security</h2>\n<p>A website with a bug is embarrassing. A blockchain with a bug can lose millions of dollars in a single transaction, publicly, with no bank to call and no undo button. Blockchain projects are some of the most attacked software on earth because the money is right there in the code.</p>\n<p>So security is not a separate job you hand to someone at the end. It is a way of thinking that every developer needs from day one. This track takes you from that mindset to the level where you could help secure a real protocol launch.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-cia-triad\">The CIA triad</h2>\n<p>Almost every security goal fits one of three words:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Goal</th><th>Means</th><th>Broken when</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Confidentiality</strong></td><td>Only the right people can read it</td><td>Customer emails leak; a private key is exposed</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Integrity</strong></td><td>Nobody can change it without permission</td><td>An attacker edits an order total; a contract's owner is swapped</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Availability</strong></td><td>It works when people need it</td><td>A DDoS takes the shop offline; a contract is permanently frozen</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>When you ask &quot;is this secure?&quot;, ask instead: <strong>secure against what, for which of these three?</strong></p>\n<h2 id=\"who-attacks-and-why\">Who attacks, and why</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Attacker</th><th>Motivation</th><th>Typical methods</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Opportunistic criminals</td><td>Quick money</td><td>Phishing, stolen passwords, automated scanners</td></tr><tr><td>Organised groups and state-backed teams</td><td>Large-scale theft (crypto is a major target)</td><td>Social engineering of developers, malware, supply-chain attacks</td></tr><tr><td>Insiders</td><td>Money, revenge, mistakes</td><td>Abusing access they already have</td></tr><tr><td>Researchers and white hats</td><td>Bounties, reputation</td><td>The same techniques, but they report what they find</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The uncomfortable truth: the most damaging crypto thefts have often come from <strong>people being tricked</strong> (signing a malicious transaction, running a fake &quot;coding test&quot; from a fake recruiter, leaking a key), not from clever maths.</p>\n<h2 id=\"thinking-like-an-attacker-threat-modelling\">Thinking like an attacker: threat modelling</h2>\n<p>A <strong>threat model</strong> is a structured answer to four questions:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>What are we building?</strong> Draw it: users, servers, contracts, wallets, admins.</li><li><strong>What can go wrong?</strong> For every arrow and box, imagine abuse.</li><li><strong>What are we going to do about it?</strong> Prevent, detect, or accept each risk.</li><li><strong>Did we do a good job?</strong> Test it, then revisit after every big change.</li></ol>\n<p>The <strong>attack surface</strong> is everything an attacker can touch: every form, API endpoint, contract function, dependency, admin key and human with access. Smaller attack surface, fewer ways in.</p>\n<h2 id=\"principles-you-will-use-forever\">Principles you will use forever</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Least privilege</strong>: give every person, key and service the minimum access it needs, and nothing more.</li><li><strong>Deny by default</strong>: allow specific things; block everything else. An allow-list beats a block-list.</li><li><strong>Defence in depth</strong>: assume any single protection will fail, and layer them (validation, then authorisation, then monitoring, then a pause switch).</li><li><strong>Fail safely</strong>: when something goes wrong, the system should lock up, not open up.</li><li><strong>Don't trust, verify</strong>: input from users, other contracts, oracles and even your own front end must be checked.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"stay-legal-the-rules-of-hacking-us-law\">Stay legal: the rules of hacking (US law)</h2>\n<p>This course follows <strong>United States law</strong>, because most blockchain companies, exchanges and launchpads operate there or serve US users. It is general awareness, not legal advice.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Law</th><th>What it covers</th><th>What it means for you</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Computer Fraud and Abuse Act (CFAA)</strong>, 18 U.S.C. § 1030</td><td>Accessing a computer &quot;without authorization&quot; or &quot;exceeding authorized access&quot;, and causing damage</td><td>Logging in, scanning for holes or pulling data from a system you have no permission to test can be a federal crime, even &quot;just to look&quot;, and even if it was easy. Victims can also sue under the CFAA.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Wiretap Act</strong> (part of the ECPA), 18 U.S.C. § 2511</td><td>Intercepting other people's communications</td><td>Sniffing someone else's network traffic (on café Wi-Fi, say) is illegal. Capture only your own traffic or a lab network.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>State computer crime laws</strong></td><td>Every state has its own (for example California Penal Code § 502)</td><td>They can apply even where federal prosecutors would not.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>DMCA § 1201</strong></td><td>Breaking technical protections on software and devices</td><td>The Copyright Office grants an exemption for good-faith security research on devices and software you lawfully own, within limits.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Two developments help researchers, but neither is a free pass:</p>\n<ul><li>In <strong>Van Buren v. United States (2021)</strong> the Supreme Court narrowed &quot;exceeds authorized access&quot; to entering parts of a system that are off-limits to you, rather than misusing data you were allowed to see.</li><li>In <strong>2022 the Department of Justice</strong> said it will not charge <strong>good-faith security research</strong> under the CFAA. That is a charging policy, not a law: states and companies can still act, and &quot;I was only testing&quot; has to be true and provable.</li></ul>\n<p>If you live outside the US, your own country's law applies to you as well (in the UK, for example, the Computer Misuse Act 1990).</p>\n<p>The rule is simple: <strong>only test systems you own, or have written permission to test.</strong> That still leaves plenty to practise on:</p>\n<ul><li>Your own machines and apps.</li><li>Deliberately vulnerable practice apps (OWASP Juice Shop, DVWA).</li><li>Legal training platforms and CTFs (TryHackMe, Hack The Box, PortSwigger Web Security Academy, picoCTF, Ethernaut).</li><li>Bug bounty programmes, which give written permission (and usually a legal &quot;safe harbor&quot;) within a published scope.</li><li>The <a href=\"#hackathons\">Ladder CTF</a> on this site.</li></ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Never &quot;test&quot; a live protocol with real funds</p><p>Exploiting a bug in a live smart contract, even to &quot;prove a point&quot; or &quot;rescue&quot; funds, can be theft in law. US prosecutors have brought wire fraud, commodities fraud and market manipulation charges against people who drained protocols and argued that &quot;the code allowed it&quot;. If you find a bug in something live, stop, write it up privately, and report it through the project's bug bounty or security contact.</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"A hacker changes the delivery address on someone else's order. Which part of the CIA triad is broken?","options":["Confidentiality","Integrity","Availability"],"answer":1,"explain":"Data was changed without permission: that is integrity."},{"q":"What does least privilege mean?","options":["Give everyone admin so work is faster","Give each person, key or service only the access it needs","Remove all passwords"],"answer":1,"explain":"Limiting access limits the damage when any one account or key is compromised."},{"q":"You find a website with an obvious security hole. What is the legal and ethical move?","options":["Test it a bit more to be sure, then tell them","Post about it publicly so they fix it fast","Do not probe further; report it privately through their security contact or bug bounty"],"answer":2,"explain":"Testing without permission can be a federal crime under the Computer Fraud and Abuse Act (and break state law too), however good your intentions."},{"q":"Why do attackers so often target people rather than code?","options":["Tricking a person into signing, clicking or running something is often easier than breaking well-tested code","People are always more secure than code","Code cannot be attacked"],"answer":0,"explain":"Social engineering is behind many of the largest crypto thefts."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Deny by default","file":"permissions.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write a function that only allows refunds for an allow-list of roles. Everyone else is denied by default. Four lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>function canRefund(user) {</code></li><li><code>return user.role === 'admin' || user.role === 'support';</code></li><li><code>}</code></li><li><code>console.log(canRefund({ role: 'customer' }));</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"function canRefund(user) {\n  return user.role === 'admin' || user.role === 'support';\n}\nconsole.log(canRefund({ role: 'customer' }));","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"function\\s+canRefund\\s*\\(\\s*user\\s*\\)\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>function canRefund(user) {</code>"},{"re":"user\\.role\\s*===\\s*['\"]admin['\"]\\s*\\|\\|\\s*user\\.role\\s*===\\s*['\"]support['\"]","text":"Line 2 returns true only for the two allowed roles: <code>user.role === 'admin' || user.role === 'support'</code>"},{"re":"canRefund\\(\\s*\\{\\s*role\\s*:\\s*['\"]customer['\"]\\s*\\}\\s*\\)","text":"Last line tests a customer: <code>console.log(canRefund({ role: 'customer' }));</code>"}],"explain":"<p>It prints <code>false</code>. Any role you forgot to think about (a new &quot;intern&quot; role, a typo like &quot;Admin&quot;) is denied automatically. The opposite design, &quot;allow unless the role is banned&quot;, fails open the moment someone invents a new role.</p>"}],"text":"Why a whole track on security A website with a bug is embarrassing. A blockchain with a bug can lose millions of dollars in a single transaction, publicly, with no bank to call and no undo button. Blockchain projects are some of the most attacked software on earth because the money is right there in the code. So security is not a separate job you hand to someone at the end. It is a way of thinking that every developer needs from day one. This track takes you from that mindset to the level where you could help secure a real protocol launch. The CIA triad Almost every security goal fits one of three words: Goal Means Broken when Confidentiality Only the right people can read it Customer emails leak; a private key is exposed Integrity Nobody can change it without permission An attacker edits an order total; a contract's owner is swapped Availability It works when people need it A DDoS takes the shop offline; a contract is permanently frozen When you ask is this secure? , ask instead: secure against what, for which of these three? Who attacks, and why Attacker Motivation Typical methods Opportunistic criminals Quick money Phishing, stolen passwords, automated scanners Organised groups and state-backed teams Large-scale theft (crypto is a major target) Social engineering of developers, malware, supply-chain attacks Insiders Money, revenge, mistakes Abusing access they already have Researchers and white hats Bounties, reputation The same techniques, but they report what they find The uncomfortable truth: the most damaging crypto thefts have often come from people being tricked (signing a malicious transaction, running a fake coding test from a fake recruiter, leaking a key), not from clever maths. Thinking like an attacker: threat modelling A threat model is a structured answer to four questions: What are we building? Draw it: users, servers, contracts, wallets, admins. What can go wrong? For every arrow and box, imagine abuse. What are we going to do about it? Prevent, detect, or accept each risk. Did we do a good job? Test it, then revisit after every big change. The attack surface is everything an attacker can touch: every form, API endpoint, contract function, dependency, admin key and human with access. Smaller attack surface, fewer ways in. Principles you will use forever Least privilege : give every person, key and service the minimum access it needs, and nothing more. Deny by default : allow specific things; block everything else. An allow-list beats a block-list. Defence in depth : assume any single protection will fail, and layer them (validation, then authorisation, then monitoring, then a pause switch). Fail safely : when something goes wrong, the system should lock up, not open up. Don't trust, verify : input from users, other contracts, oracles and even your own front end must be checked. Stay legal: the rules of hacking (US law) This course follows United States law , because most blockchain companies, exchanges and launchpads operate there or serve US users. It is general awareness, not legal advice. Law What it covers What it means for you Computer Fraud and Abuse Act (CFAA) , 18 U.S.C. § 1030 Accessing a computer without authorization or exceeding authorized access , and causing damage Logging in, scanning for holes or pulling data from a system you have no permission to test can be a federal crime, even just to look , and even if it was easy. Victims can also sue under the CFAA. Wiretap Act (part of the ECPA), 18 U.S.C. § 2511 Intercepting other people's communications Sniffing someone else's network traffic (on café Wi-Fi, say) is illegal. Capture only your own traffic or a lab network. State computer crime laws Every state has its own (for example California Penal Code § 502) They can apply even where federal prosecutors would not. DMCA § 1201 Breaking technical protections on software and devices The Copyright Office grants an exemption for good-faith security research on devices and software you lawfully own, ","track":"security"},{"id":"sec-accounts","title":"Passwords, MFA and phishing","level":"beginner","minutes":30,"summary":"Protect the accounts and wallets everything else depends on. Password strength in numbers, which MFA actually works, and how to spot a phish.","html":"<h2 id=\"your-accounts-are-the-keys-to-everything\">Your accounts are the keys to everything</h2>\n<p>Your GitHub account can push code to your projects. Your email can reset every other password. Your wallet signs transactions. Attackers know this, so before you secure any app, secure yourself.</p>\n<h2 id=\"password-strength-is-maths\">Password strength is maths</h2>\n<p>A password's strength is measured in <strong>bits of entropy</strong>: how many guesses an attacker needs, as a power of two.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">entropy (bits) = length x log2(size of character set)</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Password style</th><th>Character set</th><th>Example length</th><th>Entropy</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Lower-case word</td><td>26</td><td>8</td><td>about 38 bits (instant to crack)</td></tr><tr><td>Random, all keyboard symbols</td><td>94</td><td>16</td><td>about 105 bits (safe)</td></tr><tr><td>Diceware passphrase</td><td>7,776 words</td><td>6 words</td><td>about 78 bits (strong and memorable)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Humans are bad at randomness, so the real answer is: <strong>use a password manager</strong> (Bitwarden, 1Password, or your browser's built-in one) to generate a unique random password for every site. Then a leak at one site cannot unlock the others (that attack is called <strong>credential stuffing</strong>).</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"multi-factor-authentication-mfa\">Multi-factor authentication (MFA)</h2>\n<p>A second factor means a stolen password alone is not enough. Not all factors are equal:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Strength</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>SMS codes</td><td>Weak</td><td>SIM-swap attacks move your number to an attacker's phone</td></tr><tr><td>Authenticator app codes (TOTP)</td><td>Good</td><td>But a fake login page can still ask you for the code in real time</td></tr><tr><td>Push approvals</td><td>Good, with care</td><td>&quot;MFA fatigue&quot; attacks spam you until you tap Approve</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Passkeys / hardware keys (FIDO2)</strong></td><td>Best</td><td>Cryptographically bound to the real website, so phishing sites cannot use them</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Turn on the strongest option for email, GitHub, cloud providers, domain registrar and exchanges. For crypto: protect your exchange account with a hardware key, and use a hardware wallet for real funds.</p>\n<h2 id=\"phishing-the-attack-that-works\">Phishing: the attack that works</h2>\n<p>Phishing tricks you into handing over credentials, approving something, or running something. Red flags:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Urgency or fear</strong>: &quot;Your wallet will be frozen in 24 hours.&quot;</li><li><strong>A lookalike address</strong>: <code>rnetamask.io</code>, <code>uniswap-app.co</code>, <code>github.com.login-verify.dev</code>.</li><li><strong>An unexpected attachment, link or download</strong>, especially &quot;a quick coding test&quot; from a recruiter you have never met.</li><li><strong>A request for a seed phrase or private key.</strong> Always a scam. Always.</li></ul>\n<p>Crypto-specific scams to know:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Drainer sites</strong>: a fake airdrop or mint page asks you to sign an approval or permit that lets them take your tokens.</li><li><strong>Address poisoning</strong>: scammers send you tiny transactions from an address that looks like one you use (same first and last characters), hoping you copy it from your history next time.</li><li><strong>Fake support</strong>: in Discord or Telegram, &quot;support&quot; DMs you first. Real support never does.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"checking-a-link-the-right-way\">Checking a link the right way</h2>\n<p>Attackers hide the real domain inside a longer one. The part that matters is the end of the hostname.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-pro\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Your personal security baseline</p><p>Password manager with unique passwords everywhere. Passkeys or hardware keys on email, GitHub and exchanges. A separate browser profile for crypto. A hardware wallet for anything you cannot afford to lose. Never run code or open files from strangers on the machine that holds your keys.</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"Why is SMS the weakest form of MFA?","options":["Texts are too slow","SIM-swap attacks can move your phone number to an attacker","SMS codes are too short"],"answer":1,"explain":"Attackers persuade or bribe mobile carriers to transfer your number."},{"q":"Which MFA type resists phishing sites by design?","options":["Authenticator app codes","Email codes","Passkeys and hardware security keys (FIDO2)"],"answer":2,"explain":"They cryptographically check the real website's domain, so a fake site gets nothing useful."},{"q":"You receive 0 USDC from an address starting 0x71C7 and ending 976F, just like a friend's. What is likely happening?","options":["An airdrop","Address poisoning: they hope you will copy their lookalike address from your history","A refund"],"answer":1,"explain":"Always copy addresses from a trusted source, and check the whole address."},{"q":"A recruiter sends a &quot;take-home test&quot; repo and asks you to run npm install and start it. What is the safe approach?","options":["Treat it as untrusted code: read it first and run it only in an isolated machine or container with no wallets or keys","Run it on your main laptop; recruiters are trustworthy","Run it but close your wallet extension first"],"answer":0,"explain":"Fake job tests delivering malware are a known technique used against crypto developers."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Measure a password's entropy","file":"entropy.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Calculate the entropy of a random 16-character password using all 94 printable keyboard characters. Four lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const length = 16;</code></li><li><code>const charset = 94;</code></li><li><code>const bits = length * Math.log2(charset);</code></li><li><code>console.log(Math.round(bits));</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const length = 16;\nconst charset = 94;\nconst bits = length * Math.log2(charset);\nconsole.log(Math.round(bits));","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+length\\s*=\\s*16","text":"Line 1: <code>const length = 16;</code>"},{"re":"const\\s+charset\\s*=\\s*94","text":"Line 2: <code>const charset = 94;</code>"},{"re":"const\\s+bits\\s*=\\s*length\\s*\\*\\s*Math\\.log2\\(\\s*charset\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3: <code>const bits = length * Math.log2(charset);</code> (log2 with a lower-case L)."},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*Math\\.round\\(\\s*bits\\s*\\)\\s*\\)","text":"Line 4: <code>console.log(Math.round(bits));</code>"}],"explain":"<p>About <strong>105 bits</strong>. Each extra bit doubles the attacker's work, so 105 bits is far beyond any brute-force attack. Try <code>length = 8</code> and <code>charset = 26</code>: about 38 bits, which a modern GPU rig cracks quickly.</p>"},{"title":"Spot the lookalike domain","file":"domain.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Show why <code>includes</code> is a dangerous check and <code>endsWith</code> is the safe one. Three lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const url = new URL('https://wallet.example.com.evil.io/login');</code></li><li><code>console.log(url.hostname.includes('example.com'));</code></li><li><code>console.log(url.hostname.endsWith('.example.com'));</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const url = new URL('https://wallet.example.com.evil.io/login');\nconsole.log(url.hostname.includes('example.com'));\nconsole.log(url.hostname.endsWith('.example.com'));","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+url\\s*=\\s*new\\s+URL\\(\\s*['\"]https://wallet\\.example\\.com\\.evil\\.io/login['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"Line 1: <code>const url = new URL('https://wallet.example.com.evil.io/login');</code>"},{"re":"url\\.hostname\\.includes\\(\\s*['\"]example\\.com['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>console.log(url.hostname.includes('example.com'));</code>"},{"re":"url\\.hostname\\.endsWith\\(\\s*['\"]\\.example\\.com['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3 checks the ending, with the leading dot: <code>url.hostname.endsWith('.example.com')</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Output: <code>true</code> then <code>false</code>. The site is really <code>evil.io</code>; <code>example.com</code> is just a decoration in front of it. Any code (or human) that checks &quot;does it contain the brand name?&quot; gets fooled. Check the end of the hostname, including the dot, or compare against an exact allow-list.</p>"}],"text":"Your accounts are the keys to everything Your GitHub account can push code to your projects. Your email can reset every other password. Your wallet signs transactions. Attackers know this, so before you secure any app, secure yourself. Password strength is maths A password's strength is measured in bits of entropy : how many guesses an attacker needs, as a power of two. entropy (bits) = length x log2(size of character set) Password style Character set Example length Entropy Lower-case word 26 8 about 38 bits (instant to crack) Random, all keyboard symbols 94 16 about 105 bits (safe) Diceware passphrase 7,776 words 6 words about 78 bits (strong and memorable) Humans are bad at randomness, so the real answer is: use a password manager (Bitwarden, 1Password, or your browser's built-in one) to generate a unique random password for every site. Then a leak at one site cannot unlock the others (that attack is called credential stuffing ). Multi-factor authentication (MFA) A second factor means a stolen password alone is not enough. Not all factors are equal: Factor Strength Why SMS codes Weak SIM-swap attacks move your number to an attacker's phone Authenticator app codes (TOTP) Good But a fake login page can still ask you for the code in real time Push approvals Good, with care MFA fatigue attacks spam you until you tap Approve Passkeys / hardware keys (FIDO2) Best Cryptographically bound to the real website, so phishing sites cannot use them Turn on the strongest option for email, GitHub, cloud providers, domain registrar and exchanges. For crypto: protect your exchange account with a hardware key, and use a hardware wallet for real funds. Phishing: the attack that works Phishing tricks you into handing over credentials, approving something, or running something. Red flags: Urgency or fear : Your wallet will be frozen in 24 hours. A lookalike address : rnetamask.io , uniswap-app.co , github.com.login-verify.dev . An unexpected attachment, link or download , especially a quick coding test from a recruiter you have never met. A request for a seed phrase or private key. Always a scam. Always. Crypto-specific scams to know: Drainer sites : a fake airdrop or mint page asks you to sign an approval or permit that lets them take your tokens. Address poisoning : scammers send you tiny transactions from an address that looks like one you use (same first and last characters), hoping you copy it from your history next time. Fake support : in Discord or Telegram, support DMs you first. Real support never does. Checking a link the right way Attackers hide the real domain inside a longer one. The part that matters is the end of the hostname. Your personal security baseline Password manager with unique passwords everywhere. Passkeys or hardware keys on email, GitHub and exchanges. A separate browser profile for crypto. A hardware wallet for anything you cannot afford to lose. Never run code or open files from strangers on the machine that holds your keys.","track":"security"},{"id":"sec-networks","title":"How networks are attacked and defended","level":"beginner","minutes":30,"summary":"IP addresses, ports, protocols and TLS from an attacker's point of view, and why DNS hijacking has emptied DeFi users' wallets.","html":"<h2 id=\"the-map-attackers-start-with\">The map attackers start with</h2>\n<p>Every attack over the internet starts with <strong>reconnaissance</strong>: what machines exist, what doors are open, and what is running behind each door.</p>\n<ul><li>An <strong>IP address</strong> identifies a machine (<code>93.184.215.14</code>).</li><li>A <strong>port</strong> is a numbered door on that machine (0 to 65535). Each service listens on one.</li><li>A <strong>protocol</strong> is the language spoken through the door.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Port</th><th>Service</th><th>Security note</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>22</td><td>SSH (remote login)</td><td>Use keys, not passwords; never expose to everyone if you can avoid it</td></tr><tr><td>53</td><td>DNS</td><td>Turns names into addresses; a favourite target for hijacking</td></tr><tr><td>80</td><td>HTTP</td><td>Unencrypted: anyone on the path can read and modify it</td></tr><tr><td>443</td><td>HTTPS</td><td>Encrypted with TLS</td></tr><tr><td>5432</td><td>PostgreSQL</td><td>Should almost never be reachable from the internet</td></tr><tr><td>8545</td><td>Ethereum JSON-RPC</td><td>An exposed node with unlocked accounts has been drained many times</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Defenders</strong> close every port that is not needed (a <strong>firewall</strong>), keep the rest patched, and log who knocks.</p>\n<h2 id=\"tcp-udp-and-tls-in-one-minute\">TCP, UDP and TLS in one minute</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>TCP</strong> is a reliable, ordered conversation (web, SSH, databases).</li><li><strong>UDP</strong> is fire-and-forget packets (DNS lookups, video calls, games).</li><li><strong>TLS</strong> wraps a TCP connection in encryption and proves the server's identity with a <strong>certificate</strong> signed by a trusted authority. The padlock in your browser means TLS succeeded for that exact domain.</li></ul>\n<p>TLS protects data <strong>in transit</strong>. It does not make a site trustworthy: phishing sites have valid certificates too.</p>\n<h2 id=\"man-in-the-middle-attacks\">Man-in-the-middle attacks</h2>\n<p>If an attacker sits between you and the server (a malicious Wi-Fi hotspot, a compromised router), they can read and change anything <strong>not</strong> protected by TLS. Defences: HTTPS everywhere with HSTS, never ignoring certificate warnings, and a trusted VPN on networks you do not control.</p>\n<h2 id=\"dns-the-weak-link-in-front-of-every-dapp\">DNS: the weak link in front of every dApp</h2>\n<p>Your smart contracts can be perfect and your users can still lose everything if the <strong>website</strong> in front of them is replaced. Attackers have repeatedly hijacked DeFi projects' domains, for example through the domain registrar or DNS provider (Curve Finance's front end was hijacked this way in 2022), and served a copy of the real site that asked users to sign malicious approvals.</p>\n<p>Defences for builders:</p>\n<ul><li>Registrar account with a hardware security key and <strong>registrar lock</strong> turned on.</li><li><strong>DNSSEC</strong> where your provider supports it.</li><li>Monitoring that alerts you if your DNS records or served JavaScript change unexpectedly.</li><li>Content Security Policy and Subresource Integrity for scripts you load.</li><li>Encourage power users to verify contract addresses and to use transaction simulation in their wallet.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"scanning-your-own-machines-only\">Scanning (your own machines only)</h2>\n<p><code>nmap</code> is the classic tool for finding open ports. Run it <strong>only against machines you own or are authorised to test</strong>; scanning other people's systems can break the Computer Fraud and Abuse Act and state computer crime laws, and capturing other people's traffic with Wireshark can break the federal Wiretap Act.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>","quiz":[{"q":"Which port does HTTPS normally use?","options":["22","80","443"],"answer":2,"explain":"80 is plain HTTP; 443 is HTTP over TLS."},{"q":"A site shows a valid padlock. What does that prove?","options":["The site is safe and trustworthy","Your connection to that exact domain is encrypted and the server holds a valid certificate for it","The site has been audited"],"answer":1,"explain":"Phishing sites get certificates too. Always check the domain itself."},{"q":"Why are DNS hijacks so dangerous for dApps?","options":["They break the smart contracts","Users visiting the real domain are served a fake front end that asks them to sign malicious transactions","They slow down the blockchain"],"answer":1,"explain":"The contracts are untouched; the attack happens in front of them."},{"q":"An Ethereum node's RPC port (8545) is open to the internet. Why is that a problem?","options":["Anyone can send it commands, and if accounts on it are unlocked they can be drained","It makes gas more expensive","It is not a problem"],"answer":0,"explain":"Exposed RPC endpoints with unlocked accounts were a classic, repeated source of theft."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Scan yourself and inspect a server","file":"Terminal","lang":"bash","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Two commands: scan your own machine (<code>127.0.0.1</code>) with <code>nmap</code>, using <code>-sV</code> to detect service versions; then fetch just the HTTP response headers of <code>https://example.com</code> with <code>curl -I</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"nmap -sV 127.0.0.1\ncurl -I https://example.com","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*nmap\\s+-sV\\s+127\\.0\\.0\\.1\\s*$","text":"Line 1: <code>nmap -sV 127.0.0.1</code> (127.0.0.1 always means &quot;this machine&quot;)."},{"re":"^\\s*curl\\s+-I\\s+https://example\\.com\\s*$","text":"Line 2: <code>curl -I https://example.com</code> (capital I = headers only)."}],"explain":"<p>The scan lists every port your computer has open and what is listening; you may be surprised. The headers show the server's status code and security headers such as <code>strict-transport-security</code>. Attackers run exactly these commands as their first step, so run them on your own systems first.</p>"}],"text":"The map attackers start with Every attack over the internet starts with reconnaissance : what machines exist, what doors are open, and what is running behind each door. An IP address identifies a machine ( 93.184.215.14 ). A port is a numbered door on that machine (0 to 65535). Each service listens on one. A protocol is the language spoken through the door. Port Service Security note 22 SSH (remote login) Use keys, not passwords; never expose to everyone if you can avoid it 53 DNS Turns names into addresses; a favourite target for hijacking 80 HTTP Unencrypted: anyone on the path can read and modify it 443 HTTPS Encrypted with TLS 5432 PostgreSQL Should almost never be reachable from the internet 8545 Ethereum JSON-RPC An exposed node with unlocked accounts has been drained many times Defenders close every port that is not needed (a firewall ), keep the rest patched, and log who knocks. TCP, UDP and TLS in one minute TCP is a reliable, ordered conversation (web, SSH, databases). UDP is fire-and-forget packets (DNS lookups, video calls, games). TLS wraps a TCP connection in encryption and proves the server's identity with a certificate signed by a trusted authority. The padlock in your browser means TLS succeeded for that exact domain. TLS protects data in transit . It does not make a site trustworthy: phishing sites have valid certificates too. Man-in-the-middle attacks If an attacker sits between you and the server (a malicious Wi-Fi hotspot, a compromised router), they can read and change anything not protected by TLS. Defences: HTTPS everywhere with HSTS, never ignoring certificate warnings, and a trusted VPN on networks you do not control. DNS: the weak link in front of every dApp Your smart contracts can be perfect and your users can still lose everything if the website in front of them is replaced. Attackers have repeatedly hijacked DeFi projects' domains, for example through the domain registrar or DNS provider (Curve Finance's front end was hijacked this way in 2022), and served a copy of the real site that asked users to sign malicious approvals. Defences for builders: Registrar account with a hardware security key and registrar lock turned on. DNSSEC where your provider supports it. Monitoring that alerts you if your DNS records or served JavaScript change unexpectedly. Content Security Policy and Subresource Integrity for scripts you load. Encourage power users to verify contract addresses and to use transaction simulation in their wallet. Scanning (your own machines only) nmap is the classic tool for finding open ports. Run it only against machines you own or are authorised to test ; scanning other people's systems can break the Computer Fraud and Abuse Act and state computer crime laws, and capturing other people's traffic with Wireshark can break the federal Wiretap Act.","track":"security"},{"id":"sec-linux","title":"Linux and the command line for security","level":"beginner","minutes":30,"summary":"Users, permissions, processes, logs and SSH keys. The Linux skills every security engineer, server admin and node operator relies on.","html":"<h2 id=\"why-linux\">Why Linux</h2>\n<p>Most servers, blockchain nodes, cloud machines and security tools run on Linux. Security work means reading logs, checking permissions and locking machines down from a terminal. If you are on Windows, use WSL2 (see <a href=\"#toolkit\">Install everything</a>).</p>\n<h2 id=\"users-and-root\">Users and root</h2>\n<p>Every process runs as a <strong>user</strong>. The <strong>root</strong> user can do anything, which is exactly why you should rarely be it.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">whoami              # which user am I?\nid                  # my user id and groups\nsudo apt update     # run ONE command as root, after typing your password</code></pre></div>\n<p>Least privilege applies here: run services (your web app, your node) as their own unprivileged users, so a hacked service cannot take over the whole machine.</p>\n<h2 id=\"file-permissions\">File permissions</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">-rw-r--r--  1 ada staff  1204 Nov 20 10:12 notes.txt\n |  |  |\n |  |  +-- others: read\n |  +----- group:  read\n +-------- owner:  read and write</code></pre></div>\n<p>Each set is three bits: <strong>r</strong>ead = 4, <strong>w</strong>rite = 2, e<strong>x</strong>ecute = 1. Add them up:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Number</th><th>Letters</th><th>Means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>7</td><td>rwx</td><td>read, write, execute</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>rw-</td><td>read and write</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>r--</td><td>read only</td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>---</td><td>nothing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>So <code>chmod 600 file</code> means owner read and write, nobody else anything. <strong>SSH private keys must be 600</strong>, or SSH refuses to use them. A <code>.env</code> file with secrets should be 600 too.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"processes-and-network-connections\">Processes and network connections</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">ps aux                 # every running process\ntop                    # live view (press q to quit)\nss -tulpn              # which programs are listening on which ports (Linux)\nkill 12345             # stop process 12345</code></pre></div>\n<p>An unexpected process listening on a port is one of the first signs of a compromised machine.</p>\n<h2 id=\"logs-the-security-camera-footage\">Logs: the security camera footage</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">sudo tail -f /var/log/auth.log           # logins and sudo use, live (Debian/Ubuntu)\njournalctl -u ssh --since &quot;1 hour ago&quot;   # one service's recent logs (systemd)\ngrep &quot;Failed password&quot; /var/log/auth.log | wc -l   # count failed SSH logins</code></pre></div>\n<p>Run the last one on any server with SSH open to the internet and you will see thousands of automated password guesses. That is why you use key-only SSH login.</p>\n<h2 id=\"hardening-a-server-in-five-moves\">Hardening a server in five moves</h2>\n<ol><li>Keep it patched: <code>sudo apt update &amp;&amp; sudo apt upgrade</code>.</li><li>SSH with keys only: set <code>PasswordAuthentication no</code> and <code>PermitRootLogin no</code> in <code>/etc/ssh/sshd_config</code>.</li><li>Firewall everything you do not need: <code>sudo ufw default deny incoming</code>, <code>sudo ufw allow 22</code>, <code>sudo ufw enable</code>.</li><li>Run each service as its own unprivileged user.</li><li>Ship logs somewhere the attacker cannot delete them, and alert on anything unusual.</li></ol>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Practise legally</p><p><strong>OverTheWire: Bandit</strong> is a free game that teaches Linux and security by making you SSH into practice servers and find passwords level by level. It is the best next step after this lesson.</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"What does chmod 600 give the owner, group and others?","options":["Owner read and write; group and others nothing","Everyone read and write","Owner execute only"],"answer":0,"explain":"6 = 4 (read) + 2 (write) for the owner; 0 for the others."},{"q":"Why should a web app run as its own unprivileged user instead of root?","options":["It runs faster","If the app is hacked, the attacker only gets that limited user's powers, not the whole machine","Root cannot run web apps"],"answer":1,"explain":"Least privilege contains the damage."},{"q":"Thousands of &quot;Failed password&quot; lines appear in your server's auth log. What is the best response?","options":["Ignore them","Switch SSH to key-only login, disable root login, and consider restricting SSH by IP","Change the password to something longer"],"answer":1,"explain":"Automated guessing never stops; remove passwords from the equation entirely."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Lock down a private key","file":"Terminal","lang":"bash","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Three commands: list the files in <code>~/.ssh</code> in long format (to see permissions), set your private key <code>~/.ssh/id_ed25519</code> to owner read and write only, then print which user you are.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"ls -l ~/.ssh\nchmod 600 ~/.ssh/id_ed25519\nwhoami","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*ls\\s+-l\\s+~/\\.ssh\\s*$","text":"Line 1: <code>ls -l ~/.ssh</code> (long format shows permissions)."},{"re":"^\\s*chmod\\s+600\\s+~/\\.ssh/id_ed25519\\s*$","text":"Line 2: <code>chmod 600 ~/.ssh/id_ed25519</code> (6 = read + write for the owner, 0 for everyone else)."},{"re":"^\\s*whoami\\s*$","text":"Line 3: <code>whoami</code>"}],"explain":"<p>After <code>chmod 600</code>, <code>ls -l</code> shows <code>-rw-------</code> for the key: only you can read it. Wrong permissions on keys are a real and common finding in security reviews.</p>"}],"text":"Why Linux Most servers, blockchain nodes, cloud machines and security tools run on Linux. Security work means reading logs, checking permissions and locking machines down from a terminal. If you are on Windows, use WSL2 (see Install everything ). Users and root Every process runs as a user . The root user can do anything, which is exactly why you should rarely be it. whoami # which user am I? id # my user id and groups sudo apt update # run ONE command as root, after typing your password Least privilege applies here: run services (your web app, your node) as their own unprivileged users, so a hacked service cannot take over the whole machine. File permissions -rw-r--r-- 1 ada staff 1204 Nov 20 10:12 notes.txt | | | | | +-- others: read | +----- group: read +-------- owner: read and write Each set is three bits: r ead = 4, w rite = 2, e x ecute = 1. Add them up: Number Letters Means 7 rwx read, write, execute 6 rw- read and write 4 r-- read only 0 --- nothing So chmod 600 file means owner read and write, nobody else anything. SSH private keys must be 600 , or SSH refuses to use them. A .env file with secrets should be 600 too. Processes and network connections ps aux # every running process top # live view (press q to quit) ss -tulpn # which programs are listening on which ports (Linux) kill 12345 # stop process 12345 An unexpected process listening on a port is one of the first signs of a compromised machine. Logs: the security camera footage sudo tail -f /var/log/auth.log # logins and sudo use, live (Debian/Ubuntu) journalctl -u ssh --since 1 hour ago # one service's recent logs (systemd) grep Failed password /var/log/auth.log | wc -l # count failed SSH logins Run the last one on any server with SSH open to the internet and you will see thousands of automated password guesses. That is why you use key-only SSH login. Hardening a server in five moves Keep it patched: sudo apt update sudo apt upgrade . SSH with keys only: set PasswordAuthentication no and PermitRootLogin no in /etc/ssh/sshd_config . Firewall everything you do not need: sudo ufw default deny incoming , sudo ufw allow 22 , sudo ufw enable . Run each service as its own unprivileged user. Ship logs somewhere the attacker cannot delete them, and alert on anything unusual. Practise legally OverTheWire: Bandit is a free game that teaches Linux and security by making you SSH into practice servers and find passwords level by level. It is the best next step after this lesson.","track":"security"},{"id":"sec-web-attacks","title":"Hacking web apps: the OWASP Top 10 in practice","level":"intermediate","minutes":40,"summary":"Attack and fix injection, XSS, broken access control, path traversal and SSRF, using the same tools professional penetration testers use.","html":"<h2 id=\"from-knowing-to-doing\">From knowing to doing</h2>\n<p>The Web Development track's <a href=\"#lesson-web-security\">security lesson</a> explained the main attacks. This one is hands-on: you will learn how testers actually find them, and practise fixing them in code. Every dApp has a web front end and usually an API, so this is blockchain security too.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-tester-s-toolkit\">The tester's toolkit</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Browser DevTools</strong>: see and replay every request.</li><li><strong>An intercepting proxy</strong> (Burp Suite Community or OWASP ZAP): sits between your browser and the site so you can pause, edit and replay requests. This is the core tool of web testing.</li><li><strong>A safe target</strong>: run <strong>OWASP Juice Shop</strong>, a deliberately vulnerable online shop, on your own machine with Docker:</li></ul>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">docker run --rm -p 3001:3000 bkimminich/juice-shop\n# then open http://localhost:3001 (and find its hidden score board)</code></pre></div>\n<ul><li><strong>PortSwigger Web Security Academy</strong>: free, excellent labs for every vulnerability class.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-big-five-attacker-s-view\">The big five, attacker's view</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Attack</th><th>What the tester tries</th><th>The fix</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Broken access control / IDOR</strong></td><td>Change <code>/api/orders/1001</code> to <code>1002</code>; call admin endpoints as a normal user</td><td>Check ownership and role on the server for every request</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Injection (SQL, command)</strong></td><td>Type <code>' OR '1'='1</code> or <code>; ls</code> into inputs</td><td>Parameterised queries; never build commands from strings</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cross-site scripting (XSS)</strong></td><td>Submit <code>&lt;img src=x onerror=alert(1)&gt;</code> in a name or comment</td><td>Escape output; <code>textContent</code> not <code>innerHTML</code>; Content-Security-Policy</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Path traversal</strong></td><td>Request <code>/files/../../etc/passwd</code></td><td>Resolve the full path and check it stays inside the allowed folder</td></tr><tr><td><strong>SSRF</strong></td><td>Make the server fetch <code>http://169.254.169.254/</code> (cloud metadata) or internal URLs</td><td>Allow-list destinations; block internal IP ranges</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"fix-it-escaping-html\">Fix it: escaping HTML</h2>\n<p>If user input must go into HTML, escape the characters that have special meaning.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"fix-it-path-traversal\">Fix it: path traversal</h2>\n<p>This site's own <code>scripts/serve.mjs</code> has to stop <code>../</code> tricks. The safe pattern: resolve the requested path to an absolute one, then confirm it is still inside the folder you meant to serve.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"how-professionals-report-what-they-find\">How professionals report what they find</h2>\n<p>A finding is only useful if someone can act on it:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Title</strong>: &quot;IDOR in /api/orders lets any user read any order&quot;.</li><li><strong>Severity</strong>: impact times likelihood (critical, high, medium, low).</li><li><strong>Steps to reproduce</strong>: exact requests, so the developer can see it themselves.</li><li><strong>Impact</strong>: what an attacker gains, in business terms.</li><li><strong>Fix</strong>: specific, with code where possible.</li></ol>\n<p>You will practise this format in the <a href=\"#hackathons\">Secure the Shop hackathon</a>.</p>","quiz":[{"q":"What is the core tool of manual web testing?","options":["A port scanner","An intercepting proxy like Burp Suite or OWASP ZAP","A password manager"],"answer":1,"explain":"Proxies let you pause, modify and replay every request between browser and server."},{"q":"Why must &amp; be escaped first in an HTML escaping function?","options":["Otherwise the &amp; in already-created entities like &amp;lt; would be escaped again","Because &amp; is the most dangerous character","Order does not matter"],"answer":0,"explain":"Escaping &amp; last would turn &amp;lt; into &amp;amp;lt;."},{"q":"A server checks <code>if (url.includes('..')) refuse</code>. Why is that weak?","options":["It blocks too much","Attackers can encode the dots (for example %2e%2e) or use other tricks; resolve the real path and check it instead","It is fine"],"answer":1,"explain":"Check the outcome (the resolved path), not the input's spelling."},{"q":"Where should you practise these attacks?","options":["On any website with a login form","On your own apps, deliberately vulnerable apps like Juice Shop, and legal labs like PortSwigger Academy","On a competitor's site, if you report the results"],"answer":1,"explain":"Authorisation is what separates testing from crime."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Write an HTML escaping function","file":"escape.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write <code>escapeHtml(text)</code> that replaces <code>&amp;</code>, <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&gt;</code> and <code>&quot;</code> with their HTML entities, then test it on a classic XSS payload. Replace <code>&amp;</code> first (otherwise you would double-escape the others). Write it exactly in this shape:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">function escapeHtml(text) {\n  return text\n    .replace(/&amp;/g, '&amp;amp;')\n    .replace(/&lt;/g, '&amp;lt;')\n    .replace(/&gt;/g, '&amp;gt;')\n    .replace(/&quot;/g, '&amp;quot;');\n}\nconsole.log(escapeHtml('&lt;img src=x onerror=alert(1)&gt;'));</code></pre></div>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"function escapeHtml(text) {\n  return text\n    .replace(/&/g, '&amp;')\n    .replace(/</g, '&lt;')\n    .replace(/>/g, '&gt;')\n    .replace(/\"/g, '&quot;');\n}\nconsole.log(escapeHtml('<img src=x onerror=alert(1)>'));","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"function\\s+escapeHtml\\s*\\(\\s*text\\s*\\)\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>function escapeHtml(text) {</code>"},{"re":"return\\s+text\\s*$","text":"Line 2 is just <code>return text</code> with the chained calls on the following lines."},{"re":"\\.replace\\(\\s*/&/g\\s*,\\s*['\"]&amp;['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"First replacement: <code>.replace(/&amp;/g, '&amp;amp;')</code>"},{"re":"\\.replace\\(\\s*/</g\\s*,\\s*['\"]&lt;['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"Then <code>.replace(/&lt;/g, '&amp;lt;')</code>"},{"re":"\\.replace\\(\\s*/>/g\\s*,\\s*['\"]&gt;['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"Then <code>.replace(/&gt;/g, '&amp;gt;')</code>"},{"re":"\\.replace\\(\\s*/\"/g\\s*,\\s*['\"]&quot;['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"Then <code>.replace(/&quot;/g, '&amp;quot;');</code> with the semicolon ending the return."}],"explain":"<p>Output: <code>&amp;lt;img src=x onerror=alert(1)&amp;gt;</code>. The browser now displays the text instead of creating an image tag that runs JavaScript. In real projects, prefer your framework's built-in escaping (React does it automatically) or a library like DOMPurify when you must allow some HTML.</p>"},{"title":"Block path traversal in Node","file":"server.js","lang":"js","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write the three-part check used by a safe static file server (<code>path</code>, <code>root</code>, <code>urlPath</code> and <code>res</code> already exist):</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const filePath = path.resolve(root, '.' + urlPath);\nif (!filePath.startsWith(root + path.sep)) {\n  return res.writeHead(403).end('Forbidden');\n}</code></pre></div>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const filePath = path.resolve(root, '.' + urlPath);\nif (!filePath.startsWith(root + path.sep)) {\n  return res.writeHead(403).end('Forbidden');\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+filePath\\s*=\\s*path\\.resolve\\(\\s*root\\s*,\\s*['\"]\\.['\"]\\s*\\+\\s*urlPath\\s*\\)","text":"Line 1: <code>const filePath = path.resolve(root, '.' + urlPath);</code>"},{"re":"if\\s*\\(\\s*!\\s*filePath\\.startsWith\\(\\s*root\\s*\\+\\s*path\\.sep\\s*\\)\\s*\\)\\s*\\{","text":"Line 2: <code>if (!filePath.startsWith(root + path.sep)) {</code> The separator stops <code>/var/www-evil</code> matching <code>/var/www</code>."},{"re":"return\\s+res\\.writeHead\\(\\s*403\\s*\\)\\.end\\(\\s*['\"]Forbidden['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3: <code>return res.writeHead(403).end('Forbidden');</code>"}],"explain":"<p><code>path.resolve</code> collapses every <code>..</code>, so <code>/files/../../etc/passwd</code> becomes <code>/etc/passwd</code>, which does not start with your root folder, so it is refused. Checking the raw string for <code>..</code> instead is a classic mistake: attackers encode it (<code>%2e%2e</code>) to slip past.</p>"}],"text":"From knowing to doing The Web Development track's security lesson explained the main attacks. This one is hands-on: you will learn how testers actually find them, and practise fixing them in code. Every dApp has a web front end and usually an API, so this is blockchain security too. The tester's toolkit Browser DevTools : see and replay every request. An intercepting proxy (Burp Suite Community or OWASP ZAP): sits between your browser and the site so you can pause, edit and replay requests. This is the core tool of web testing. A safe target : run OWASP Juice Shop , a deliberately vulnerable online shop, on your own machine with Docker: docker run --rm -p 3001:3000 bkimminich/juice-shop # then open http://localhost:3001 (and find its hidden score board) PortSwigger Web Security Academy : free, excellent labs for every vulnerability class. The big five, attacker's view Attack What the tester tries The fix Broken access control / IDOR Change /api/orders/1001 to 1002 ; call admin endpoints as a normal user Check ownership and role on the server for every request Injection (SQL, command) Type ' OR '1'='1 or ; ls into inputs Parameterised queries; never build commands from strings Cross-site scripting (XSS) Submit img src=x onerror=alert(1) in a name or comment Escape output; textContent not innerHTML ; Content-Security-Policy Path traversal Request /files/../../etc/passwd Resolve the full path and check it stays inside the allowed folder SSRF Make the server fetch http://169.254.169.254/ (cloud metadata) or internal URLs Allow-list destinations; block internal IP ranges Fix it: escaping HTML If user input must go into HTML, escape the characters that have special meaning. Fix it: path traversal This site's own scripts/serve.mjs has to stop ../ tricks. The safe pattern: resolve the requested path to an absolute one, then confirm it is still inside the folder you meant to serve. How professionals report what they find A finding is only useful if someone can act on it: Title : IDOR in /api/orders lets any user read any order . Severity : impact times likelihood (critical, high, medium, low). Steps to reproduce : exact requests, so the developer can see it themselves. Impact : what an attacker gains, in business terms. Fix : specific, with code where possible. You will practise this format in the Secure the Shop hackathon .","track":"security"},{"id":"sec-crypto-in-practice","title":"Applied cryptography: using crypto correctly","level":"intermediate","minutes":35,"summary":"The mistakes that break real systems (nonce reuse, ECB, fast password hashes, timing leaks, hard-coded keys) and the correct patterns, including HMAC-signed webhooks.","html":"<h2 id=\"good-algorithms-bad-usage\">Good algorithms, bad usage</h2>\n<p>Modern algorithms are rarely broken. Systems are broken because the algorithms are <strong>used wrongly</strong>. Learn these mistakes once and you will spot them in code reviews for the rest of your career.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>Why it breaks things</th><th>Do this instead</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Reusing a nonce/IV with the same key (AES-GCM, ECDSA)</td><td>Leaks plaintext or even the private key</td><td>Fresh random nonce every time; libraries that handle it</td></tr><tr><td>AES in <strong>ECB</strong> mode</td><td>Identical blocks encrypt identically, so patterns show through</td><td>AES-GCM or ChaCha20-Poly1305</td></tr><tr><td>MD5/SHA-1/SHA-256 for passwords</td><td>Far too fast; billions of guesses per second</td><td>Argon2id, scrypt or bcrypt</td></tr><tr><td>Hard-coded keys in source code</td><td>Anyone with the code (or the app bundle) has the key</td><td>Environment secrets, a secrets manager, a KMS or HSM</td></tr><tr><td>Comparing secrets with <code>===</code></td><td>Can leak, through timing, how many characters matched</td><td>Constant-time comparison</td></tr><tr><td>Encrypting without authenticating</td><td>Attackers can tamper with ciphertext undetected</td><td>Authenticated encryption (AEAD), or encrypt-then-MAC</td></tr><tr><td><code>Math.random()</code> for tokens or keys</td><td>Predictable</td><td><code>crypto.getRandomValues</code>, <code>crypto.randomUUID</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"hmac-proving-a-message-came-from-someone-who-knows-the-secret\">HMAC: proving a message came from someone who knows the secret</h2>\n<p>A <strong>MAC</strong> (message authentication code) is like a signature, but with a shared secret instead of a key pair. <strong>HMAC-SHA256</strong> is the standard. Payment providers like Stripe, and many crypto APIs, sign their webhooks this way: they send the message plus an HMAC, and you recompute the HMAC with your shared secret. If it matches, the message is genuine and unchanged.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"password-storage-properly\">Password storage, properly</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">// Node.js: npm install argon2\nimport argon2 from 'argon2';\n\nconst hash = await argon2.hash(password);          // salt is generated and stored inside the hash\nconst valid = await argon2.verify(hash, attempt);  // true or false</code></pre></div>\n<p>A <strong>salt</strong> (random data mixed into each password before hashing) means two users with the same password get different hashes, so attackers cannot use precomputed tables. Argon2id is also deliberately slow and memory-hungry, which cripples GPU cracking rigs.</p>\n<h2 id=\"key-management-is-the-hard-part\">Key management is the hard part</h2>\n<p>Algorithms are free; protecting keys is the real work.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Never in Git.</strong> Use environment variables locally and a secrets manager in production.</li><li><strong>Rotate</strong> keys on a schedule and immediately after any suspected leak.</li><li><strong>Separate keys by purpose</strong>: the key that signs webhooks is not the key that encrypts data.</li><li><strong>Hardware for high-value keys</strong>: HSMs, cloud KMS, hardware wallets, or MPC (multi-party computation, where no single device ever holds the whole key).</li></ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-pro\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Constant-time comparison in Node</p><p>For comparing secrets yourself (API keys, HMACs computed manually), use <code>crypto.timingSafeEqual(a, b)</code> from Node's <code>crypto</code> module, on two buffers of equal length. A normal <code>===</code> returns as soon as one character differs, and that tiny timing difference can be measured.</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"Why is SHA-256 alone wrong for storing passwords?","options":["It is broken","It is designed to be fast, so attackers can try billions of guesses per second","It produces hashes that are too long"],"answer":1,"explain":"Use a slow, salted password hash like Argon2id."},{"q":"What does verifying an HMAC on a webhook prove?","options":["The sender knows the shared secret and the message was not changed","The message is encrypted","The sender is a human"],"answer":0,"explain":"HMAC gives integrity and authenticity, not confidentiality."},{"q":"What is a salt for?","options":["Making passwords shorter","Making identical passwords hash differently, defeating precomputed tables","Encrypting the hash"],"answer":1,"explain":"Each user's random salt is stored with their hash."},{"q":"Why is ECB mode dangerous?","options":["It is slow","Identical plaintext blocks become identical ciphertext blocks, revealing patterns","It needs a nonce"],"answer":1,"explain":"The famous &quot;ECB penguin&quot; image shows the outline surviving encryption."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Sign and verify a webhook with HMAC","file":"hmac.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Using the Web Crypto API, write five lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const enc = new TextEncoder();</code></li><li><code>const key = await crypto.subtle.importKey('raw', enc.encode('webhook-secret'), { name: 'HMAC', hash: 'SHA-256' }, false, ['sign', 'verify']);</code></li><li><code>const sig = await crypto.subtle.sign('HMAC', key, enc.encode('order=42&amp;amount=6800'));</code></li><li><code>const ok = await crypto.subtle.verify('HMAC', key, sig, enc.encode('order=42&amp;amount=6800'));</code></li><li><code>console.log(ok);</code></li></ol>\n<p>Then change the amount in line 4 only, run it again, and watch it fail.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const enc = new TextEncoder();\nconst key = await crypto.subtle.importKey('raw', enc.encode('webhook-secret'), { name: 'HMAC', hash: 'SHA-256' }, false, ['sign', 'verify']);\nconst sig = await crypto.subtle.sign('HMAC', key, enc.encode('order=42&amount=6800'));\nconst ok = await crypto.subtle.verify('HMAC', key, sig, enc.encode('order=42&amount=6800'));\nconsole.log(ok);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+enc\\s*=\\s*new\\s+TextEncoder\\(\\s*\\)","text":"Line 1: <code>const enc = new TextEncoder();</code>"},{"re":"crypto\\.subtle\\.importKey\\(\\s*['\"]raw['\"]\\s*,\\s*enc\\.encode\\(\\s*['\"]webhook-secret['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2 starts <code>const key = await crypto.subtle.importKey('raw', enc.encode('webhook-secret'),</code>"},{"re":"\\{\\s*name\\s*:\\s*['\"]HMAC['\"]\\s*,\\s*hash\\s*:\\s*['\"]SHA-256['\"]\\s*\\}\\s*,\\s*false\\s*,\\s*\\[\\s*['\"]sign['\"]\\s*,\\s*['\"]verify['\"]\\s*\\]","text":"then <code>{ name: 'HMAC', hash: 'SHA-256' }, false, ['sign', 'verify']);</code>"},{"re":"const\\s+sig\\s*=\\s*await\\s+crypto\\.subtle\\.sign\\(\\s*['\"]HMAC['\"]\\s*,\\s*key\\s*,\\s*enc\\.encode\\(\\s*['\"]order=42&amount=6800['\"]\\s*\\)\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3: <code>const sig = await crypto.subtle.sign('HMAC', key, enc.encode('order=42&amp;amount=6800'));</code>"},{"re":"crypto\\.subtle\\.verify\\(\\s*['\"]HMAC['\"]\\s*,\\s*key\\s*,\\s*sig\\s*,","text":"Line 4: <code>const ok = await crypto.subtle.verify('HMAC', key, sig, enc.encode('order=42&amp;amount=6800'));</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*ok\\s*\\)","text":"Line 5: <code>console.log(ok);</code>"}],"explain":"<p>It prints <code>true</code>. Change <code>6800</code> to <code>1</code> in line 4 and it prints <code>false</code>: an attacker who edits the amount cannot produce a valid HMAC without the secret. <code>crypto.subtle.verify</code> also compares in constant time, avoiding the timing-leak mistake from the table.</p>"}],"text":"Good algorithms, bad usage Modern algorithms are rarely broken. Systems are broken because the algorithms are used wrongly . Learn these mistakes once and you will spot them in code reviews for the rest of your career. Mistake Why it breaks things Do this instead Reusing a nonce/IV with the same key (AES-GCM, ECDSA) Leaks plaintext or even the private key Fresh random nonce every time; libraries that handle it AES in ECB mode Identical blocks encrypt identically, so patterns show through AES-GCM or ChaCha20-Poly1305 MD5/SHA-1/SHA-256 for passwords Far too fast; billions of guesses per second Argon2id, scrypt or bcrypt Hard-coded keys in source code Anyone with the code (or the app bundle) has the key Environment secrets, a secrets manager, a KMS or HSM Comparing secrets with === Can leak, through timing, how many characters matched Constant-time comparison Encrypting without authenticating Attackers can tamper with ciphertext undetected Authenticated encryption (AEAD), or encrypt-then-MAC Math.random() for tokens or keys Predictable crypto.getRandomValues , crypto.randomUUID HMAC: proving a message came from someone who knows the secret A MAC (message authentication code) is like a signature, but with a shared secret instead of a key pair. HMAC-SHA256 is the standard. Payment providers like Stripe, and many crypto APIs, sign their webhooks this way: they send the message plus an HMAC, and you recompute the HMAC with your shared secret. If it matches, the message is genuine and unchanged. Password storage, properly // Node.js: npm install argon2 import argon2 from 'argon2'; const hash = await argon2.hash(password); // salt is generated and stored inside the hash const valid = await argon2.verify(hash, attempt); // true or false A salt (random data mixed into each password before hashing) means two users with the same password get different hashes, so attackers cannot use precomputed tables. Argon2id is also deliberately slow and memory-hungry, which cripples GPU cracking rigs. Key management is the hard part Algorithms are free; protecting keys is the real work. Never in Git. Use environment variables locally and a secrets manager in production. Rotate keys on a schedule and immediately after any suspected leak. Separate keys by purpose : the key that signs webhooks is not the key that encrypts data. Hardware for high-value keys : HSMs, cloud KMS, hardware wallets, or MPC (multi-party computation, where no single device ever holds the whole key). Constant-time comparison in Node For comparing secrets yourself (API keys, HMACs computed manually), use crypto.timingSafeEqual(a, b) from Node's crypto module, on two buffers of equal length. A normal === returns as soon as one character differs, and that tiny timing difference can be measured.","track":"security"},{"id":"sec-secure-dev","title":"Secure development and supply-chain security","level":"intermediate","minutes":35,"summary":"Threat-model before you build, scan code and dependencies automatically, keep secrets out of Git, and harden CI against the attacks that have hit crypto projects.","html":"<h2 id=\"security-built-in-not-bolted-on\">Security built in, not bolted on</h2>\n<p>Fixing a design flaw on the whiteboard costs minutes. Fixing it after launch can cost the whole treasury. A <strong>secure development lifecycle</strong> puts a security step into each phase:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Phase</th><th>Security activity</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Design</td><td>Threat model; decide trust boundaries and admin powers</td></tr><tr><td>Build</td><td>Secure defaults, input validation, reviewed libraries</td></tr><tr><td>Review</td><td>Peer code review with a security checklist</td></tr><tr><td>Test</td><td>Unit, fuzz and invariant tests; static analysis (SAST)</td></tr><tr><td>Release</td><td>Dependency and secret scanning in CI; signed releases</td></tr><tr><td>Operate</td><td>Monitoring, alerting, incident response, bug bounty</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"stride-a-checklist-for-what-can-go-wrong\">STRIDE: a checklist for &quot;what can go wrong?&quot;</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Letter</th><th>Threat</th><th>Example</th><th>Typical defence</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>S</strong></td><td>Spoofing</td><td>Faking another user or contract</td><td>Authentication, signatures</td></tr><tr><td><strong>T</strong></td><td>Tampering</td><td>Changing data or a transaction</td><td>Integrity checks, access control</td></tr><tr><td><strong>R</strong></td><td>Repudiation</td><td>&quot;I never did that&quot;</td><td>Logs, events, signatures</td></tr><tr><td><strong>I</strong></td><td>Information disclosure</td><td>Leaked keys, private data</td><td>Encryption, least privilege</td></tr><tr><td><strong>D</strong></td><td>Denial of service</td><td>Unbounded loops, spam</td><td>Limits, rate limiting, pull payments</td></tr><tr><td><strong>E</strong></td><td>Elevation of privilege</td><td>User becomes admin</td><td>Role checks, deny by default</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Walk through every component of your diagram with these six letters. It takes an hour and finds real problems.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-software-supply-chain\">The software supply chain</h2>\n<p>Your app is mostly other people's code: hundreds of npm packages, GitHub Actions, Docker images, compilers. Attackers increasingly go after that chain instead of you:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>event-stream (2018)</strong>: a popular npm package was handed to a new &quot;maintainer&quot;, who added code targeting a Bitcoin wallet app's users.</li><li><strong>Ledger Connect Kit (2023)</strong>: a compromised library used by many dApp front ends briefly served wallet-draining code.</li><li><strong>2025 npm compromises</strong>: attackers phished maintainers of very widely used packages and published versions that tried to swap crypto addresses in web pages.</li></ul>\n<p>Defences:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Commit your lockfile</strong> and install with <code>npm ci</code> in CI, so versions cannot silently change.</li><li><strong>Fewer, well-known dependencies.</strong> Each one is code you are trusting.</li><li><strong>Scan automatically</strong>: <code>npm audit</code>, GitHub Dependabot, and supply-chain tools that flag suspicious new versions.</li><li><strong>Delay upgrades slightly</strong> for critical apps; most malicious versions are caught within days.</li><li><strong>Protect your own publishing</strong>: hardware-key 2FA on npm and GitHub, so you never become the compromised maintainer.</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"secrets-scanning-and-static-analysis\">Secrets scanning and static analysis</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">npm audit --audit-level=high       # known vulnerable dependencies\ngitleaks git -v                    # secrets anywhere in your Git history\nsemgrep scan --config auto         # code patterns that look like vulnerabilities</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"hardening-github-actions\">Hardening GitHub Actions</h2>\n<p>CI has access to your code, your secrets and often your deployments, which makes it a prime target.</p>\n<ul><li>Give the workflow's built-in token <strong>read-only</strong> permissions by default, and grant more only to the job that needs it.</li><li>Pin third-party actions to a full commit SHA rather than a tag that can be moved.</li><li>Never run untrusted pull-request code with access to secrets.</li><li>Keep deployment keys in protected environments that need approval.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>","quiz":[{"q":"Which STRIDE letter covers a normal user gaining admin powers?","options":["S","T","E"],"answer":2,"explain":"Elevation of privilege."},{"q":"Why install dependencies with npm ci in CI?","options":["It installs exactly what the lockfile says, so versions cannot silently change between builds","It is the only command that works in CI","It skips all security checks"],"answer":0,"explain":"Reproducible installs are a supply-chain defence."},{"q":"What is the safest default for a GitHub Actions workflow token?","options":["Write access to everything","Read-only contents, with extra permissions granted only to jobs that need them","No token at all, ever"],"answer":1,"explain":"Least privilege applies to automation too."},{"q":"Several crypto front ends briefly served wallet-draining code without their own code being hacked. How?","options":["Their smart contracts were upgraded","A library they depended on was compromised upstream","Their users had weak passwords"],"answer":1,"explain":"That is a supply-chain attack: compromise one popular package, reach thousands of apps."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Run the three scanners","file":"Terminal","lang":"bash","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Type the three scanning commands from above, in the same order: an npm audit that fails only on high or critical issues, a verbose gitleaks scan of the Git history, and a Semgrep scan with automatic rules.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"npm audit --audit-level=high\ngitleaks git -v\nsemgrep scan --config auto","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*npm\\s+audit\\s+--audit-level=high\\s*$","text":"Line 1: <code>npm audit --audit-level=high</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*gitleaks\\s+git\\s+-v\\s*$","text":"Line 2: <code>gitleaks git -v</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*semgrep\\s+scan\\s+--config\\s+auto\\s*$","text":"Line 3: <code>semgrep scan --config auto</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Put all three in CI so every pull request is checked automatically. Install gitleaks and Semgrep from the Security tools section of <a href=\"#toolkit\">Install everything</a>.</p>"},{"title":"Read-only CI by default","file":".github/workflows/ci.yml","lang":"yaml","run":false,"prompt":"<p>At the top level of a workflow, set the default token permissions so it can only read the repository's contents. Two lines; the second is indented by two spaces.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\"><pre><code class=\"language-yaml\">permissions:\n  contents: read</code></pre></div>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"permissions:\n  contents: read","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^permissions\\s*:\\s*$","text":"Line 1: <code>permissions:</code> at the very start of the line."},{"re":"^ {2}contents\\s*:\\s*read\\s*$","text":"Line 2, indented by exactly two spaces: <code>contents: read</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Now a compromised step cannot push code, create releases or change settings with the workflow's token. In YAML, indentation is structure, which is why this exercise checks your spaces.</p>"}],"text":"Security built in, not bolted on Fixing a design flaw on the whiteboard costs minutes. Fixing it after launch can cost the whole treasury. A secure development lifecycle puts a security step into each phase: Phase Security activity Design Threat model; decide trust boundaries and admin powers Build Secure defaults, input validation, reviewed libraries Review Peer code review with a security checklist Test Unit, fuzz and invariant tests; static analysis (SAST) Release Dependency and secret scanning in CI; signed releases Operate Monitoring, alerting, incident response, bug bounty STRIDE: a checklist for what can go wrong? Letter Threat Example Typical defence S Spoofing Faking another user or contract Authentication, signatures T Tampering Changing data or a transaction Integrity checks, access control R Repudiation I never did that Logs, events, signatures I Information disclosure Leaked keys, private data Encryption, least privilege D Denial of service Unbounded loops, spam Limits, rate limiting, pull payments E Elevation of privilege User becomes admin Role checks, deny by default Walk through every component of your diagram with these six letters. It takes an hour and finds real problems. The software supply chain Your app is mostly other people's code: hundreds of npm packages, GitHub Actions, Docker images, compilers. Attackers increasingly go after that chain instead of you: event-stream (2018) : a popular npm package was handed to a new maintainer , who added code targeting a Bitcoin wallet app's users. Ledger Connect Kit (2023) : a compromised library used by many dApp front ends briefly served wallet-draining code. 2025 npm compromises : attackers phished maintainers of very widely used packages and published versions that tried to swap crypto addresses in web pages. Defences: Commit your lockfile and install with npm ci in CI, so versions cannot silently change. Fewer, well-known dependencies. Each one is code you are trusting. Scan automatically : npm audit , GitHub Dependabot, and supply-chain tools that flag suspicious new versions. Delay upgrades slightly for critical apps; most malicious versions are caught within days. Protect your own publishing : hardware-key 2FA on npm and GitHub, so you never become the compromised maintainer. Secrets scanning and static analysis npm audit --audit-level=high # known vulnerable dependencies gitleaks git -v # secrets anywhere in your Git history semgrep scan --config auto # code patterns that look like vulnerabilities Hardening GitHub Actions CI has access to your code, your secrets and often your deployments, which makes it a prime target. Give the workflow's built-in token read-only permissions by default, and grant more only to the job that needs it. Pin third-party actions to a full commit SHA rather than a tag that can be moved. Never run untrusted pull-request code with access to secrets. Keep deployment keys in protected environments that need approval.","track":"security"},{"id":"sec-wallet-opsec","title":"Wallet, key and team operational security","level":"intermediate","minutes":35,"summary":"How teams protect deployer and admin keys with multisigs and hardware, what blind signing is, and the social-engineering playbook used against crypto builders.","html":"<h2 id=\"the-keys-are-the-castle\">The keys are the castle</h2>\n<p>In a protocol, a handful of keys can often upgrade contracts, move the treasury, mint tokens or pause everything. If one of those keys is stolen, perfect contract code does not matter. <strong>Operational security (opsec)</strong> is how you protect those keys and the people who hold them.</p>\n<h2 id=\"kinds-of-key-in-a-project\">Kinds of key in a project</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>Power</th><th>How to hold it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Deployer</strong></td><td>Deploys contracts; often the initial owner</td><td>Fresh hardware wallet; hand ownership to a multisig straight after deployment</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Admin / owner</strong></td><td>Upgrades, parameters, treasury</td><td>A multisig (for example Safe) with several hardware-wallet signers, behind a timelock</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Guardian / pauser</strong></td><td>Emergency pause only</td><td>Small trusted group, fast to act, cannot move funds</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Operational bots</strong></td><td>Oracles, keepers, rebalancing</td><td>Narrow on-chain permissions; spending limits; alerts</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Developers' personal wallets</strong></td><td>Everyday testing</td><td>Never hold project funds; testnet only on dev machines</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"multisig-done-right\">Multisig done right</h2>\n<p>A <strong>multisig</strong> wallet needs, say, 3 of 5 signers to approve any transaction. One stolen key is not enough.</p>\n<ul><li>Signers use <strong>hardware wallets</strong> and are <strong>different people in different places</strong> (a 3-of-5 where one person holds 3 keys is a 1-of-1).</li><li>Every signer <strong>independently verifies</strong> what they sign: the target contract, function and parameters, on their hardware device's screen if possible, and ideally with an independent simulation tool, not just the same web page.</li><li>Pair the multisig with a <strong>timelock</strong> for upgrades, so the community gets time to react to a malicious change.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"blind-signing-how-the-largest-hack-happened\">Blind signing: how the largest hack happened</h2>\n<p><strong>Blind signing</strong> means approving a transaction you cannot actually read: a hash or raw data instead of human-readable details. In February 2025, attackers stole roughly 1.5 billion dollars from the exchange Bybit. They compromised the infrastructure behind the multisig web interface its signers used, so the signers saw what looked like a routine transfer while their hardware wallets actually signed a transaction that handed control of the wallet to the attackers. Every signer followed the process; the process trusted one web page.</p>\n<p>Lessons:</p>\n<ul><li>Verify transaction details on an <strong>independent</strong> source (a second machine, a separate simulation tool, the hardware wallet's own screen).</li><li>Understand <strong>calldata</strong>: the first 4 bytes of a transaction's data identify the function being called.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>4-byte selector</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>transfer(address,uint256)</code></td><td><code>0xa9059cbb</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>approve(address,uint256)</code></td><td><code>0x095ea7b3</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"the-social-engineering-playbook-against-developers\">The social-engineering playbook against developers</h2>\n<p>State-backed groups have run long campaigns against crypto developers. Patterns to recognise:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Fake job offers</strong> with a &quot;take-home coding test&quot; repo whose install script or dependencies run malware that steals wallet keys and browser sessions.</li><li><strong>Fake investors or partners</strong> who ask you to join a call through a &quot;special&quot; meeting app you must download.</li><li><strong>Compromised contacts</strong>: a real colleague's hacked account sends you a file or link.</li><li><strong>Poisoned packages</strong> with names one letter away from popular ones.</li></ul>\n<p>Defences: run unknown code only in a disposable VM or container; keep keys off development machines entirely; verify unusual requests through a second channel (call the person); hardware wallets for anything that signs.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Separate your worlds</p><p>The machine where you run random npm projects, browser extensions and downloads should never be the machine that holds admin keys or seed phrases. Many teams use a dedicated device, used for nothing but signing.</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"A 3-of-5 multisig has three keys held by the same person on one laptop. What is its real security?","options":["3-of-5","Effectively 1-of-1: compromising that person or laptop is enough","5-of-5"],"answer":1,"explain":"Independence of signers is the whole point."},{"q":"What is blind signing?","options":["Signing with your eyes closed","Approving a transaction whose real details you cannot read or have not independently verified","Signing with a hardware wallet"],"answer":1,"explain":"If you cannot see what you sign, the screen in front of you decides for you."},{"q":"Calldata starts with 0xa9059cbb. Which function is being called?","options":["transfer(address,uint256)","approve(address,uint256)","mint(address)"],"answer":0,"explain":"0xa9059cbb is the selector for ERC-20 transfer; 0x095ea7b3 is approve."},{"q":"A recruiter's coding test asks you to run npm install on their repo. What is the safe move?","options":["Run it on your main laptop","Read it first and run it only in an isolated VM or container with no keys, wallets or saved logins","Refuse all job tests forever"],"answer":1,"explain":"Install scripts run arbitrary code with your user's permissions."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Recognise an approval in raw calldata","file":"selector.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Warn when a transaction's data is an ERC-20 approval. Five lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const APPROVE = '0x095ea7b3';</code></li><li><code>const data = '0x095ea7b3000000000000000000000000';</code></li><li><code>if (data.startsWith(APPROVE)) {</code></li><li><code>console.log('Warning: this transaction grants a token approval');</code></li><li><code>}</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const APPROVE = '0x095ea7b3';\nconst data = '0x095ea7b3000000000000000000000000';\nif (data.startsWith(APPROVE)) {\n  console.log('Warning: this transaction grants a token approval');\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+APPROVE\\s*=\\s*['\"]0x095ea7b3['\"]","text":"Line 1: <code>const APPROVE = '0x095ea7b3';</code> (capitals for the constant's name)."},{"re":"const\\s+data\\s*=\\s*['\"]0x095ea7b3000000000000000000000000['\"]","text":"Line 2: the selector followed by 24 zeros: <code>'0x095ea7b3000000000000000000000000'</code>"},{"re":"if\\s*\\(\\s*data\\.startsWith\\(\\s*APPROVE\\s*\\)\\s*\\)\\s*\\{","text":"Line 3: <code>if (data.startsWith(APPROVE)) {</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*['\"]Warning: this transaction grants a token approval['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"Line 4: the warning text exactly."}],"explain":"<p>Wallet security features and transaction simulators decode calldata in exactly this way. When a &quot;claim your airdrop&quot; page asks you to sign something starting with <code>0x095ea7b3</code>, you are not claiming anything: you are approving someone to spend your tokens.</p>"}],"text":"The keys are the castle In a protocol, a handful of keys can often upgrade contracts, move the treasury, mint tokens or pause everything. If one of those keys is stolen, perfect contract code does not matter. Operational security (opsec) is how you protect those keys and the people who hold them. Kinds of key in a project Key Power How to hold it Deployer Deploys contracts; often the initial owner Fresh hardware wallet; hand ownership to a multisig straight after deployment Admin / owner Upgrades, parameters, treasury A multisig (for example Safe) with several hardware-wallet signers, behind a timelock Guardian / pauser Emergency pause only Small trusted group, fast to act, cannot move funds Operational bots Oracles, keepers, rebalancing Narrow on-chain permissions; spending limits; alerts Developers' personal wallets Everyday testing Never hold project funds; testnet only on dev machines Multisig done right A multisig wallet needs, say, 3 of 5 signers to approve any transaction. One stolen key is not enough. Signers use hardware wallets and are different people in different places (a 3-of-5 where one person holds 3 keys is a 1-of-1). Every signer independently verifies what they sign: the target contract, function and parameters, on their hardware device's screen if possible, and ideally with an independent simulation tool, not just the same web page. Pair the multisig with a timelock for upgrades, so the community gets time to react to a malicious change. Blind signing: how the largest hack happened Blind signing means approving a transaction you cannot actually read: a hash or raw data instead of human-readable details. In February 2025, attackers stole roughly 1.5 billion dollars from the exchange Bybit. They compromised the infrastructure behind the multisig web interface its signers used, so the signers saw what looked like a routine transfer while their hardware wallets actually signed a transaction that handed control of the wallet to the attackers. Every signer followed the process; the process trusted one web page. Lessons: Verify transaction details on an independent source (a second machine, a separate simulation tool, the hardware wallet's own screen). Understand calldata : the first 4 bytes of a transaction's data identify the function being called. Function 4-byte selector transfer(address,uint256) 0xa9059cbb approve(address,uint256) 0x095ea7b3 The social-engineering playbook against developers State-backed groups have run long campaigns against crypto developers. Patterns to recognise: Fake job offers with a take-home coding test repo whose install script or dependencies run malware that steals wallet keys and browser sessions. Fake investors or partners who ask you to join a call through a special meeting app you must download. Compromised contacts : a real colleague's hacked account sends you a file or link. Poisoned packages with names one letter away from popular ones. Defences: run unknown code only in a disposable VM or container; keep keys off development machines entirely; verify unusual requests through a second channel (call the person); hardware wallets for anything that signs. Separate your worlds The machine where you run random npm projects, browser extensions and downloads should never be the machine that holds admin keys or seed phrases. Many teams use a dedicated device, used for nothing but signing.","track":"security"},{"id":"sec-blockchain-threats","title":"Attacking blockchain systems beyond the contract","level":"advanced","minutes":40,"summary":"Bridges, oracles, consensus, MEV, nodes and front ends. The whole system an attacker sees, the incidents that taught each lesson, and the controls that work.","html":"<h2 id=\"the-protocol-is-more-than-its-contracts\">The protocol is more than its contracts</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"#lesson-crypto-security\">smart contract security</a> lesson covered bugs inside contracts. Real protocols are systems: contracts plus oracles, bridges, nodes, keys, front ends, governance and people. Attackers pick the weakest part.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">users -&gt; front end (DNS, CDN, JS libraries) -&gt; wallet -&gt; RPC node -&gt; chain\n                                                                    |-- your contracts\n                                                                    |-- oracles (prices)\n                                                                    |-- bridges (other chains)\n                                         admin keys / multisig / governance --^</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"bridges-the-biggest-honeypots\">Bridges: the biggest honeypots</h2>\n<p>A bridge locks assets on one chain and mints a representation on another. Whoever can convince the bridge that a deposit happened can mint unbacked tokens. Some of the largest losses in crypto history were bridges:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Ronin (2022)</strong>: attackers obtained enough validator keys (5 of 9) to approve fake withdrawals. A key-management failure, not a code bug.</li><li><strong>Wormhole (2022)</strong>: a signature-verification flaw let an attacker mint wrapped ETH without depositing.</li><li><strong>Nomad (2022)</strong>: an initialisation mistake made every message look valid; hundreds of people copied the first attacker's transaction.</li></ul>\n<p>Controls: many independent validators with hardware-protected keys, rate limits on withdrawals, delays on large transfers, and monitoring that can pause the bridge.</p>\n<h2 id=\"oracles\">Oracles</h2>\n<p>Contracts cannot see the outside world, so <strong>oracles</strong> report prices and data. If an attacker can move the price your protocol reads, they can borrow too much or liquidate others unfairly. Defences: decentralised oracle networks, time-weighted prices, sanity bounds, and checking that data is fresh.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"consensus-and-the-chain-itself\">Consensus and the chain itself</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>51% attacks</strong>: on smaller proof-of-work chains, renting enough hash power to rewrite recent blocks has enabled double-spends against exchanges. Defence for integrators: wait for more confirmations on smaller chains.</li><li><strong>Reorgs and finality</strong>: never treat a transaction as final until the chain's finality rules say so.</li><li><strong>L2 sequencers</strong>: most rollups have a single sequencer today; if it goes down, price feeds and liquidations behave strangely. Chainlink publishes sequencer-uptime feeds for this.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"mev-and-the-public-mempool\">MEV and the public mempool</h2>\n<p>Pending transactions are visible. Searchers reorder, insert and copy them to extract value (<strong>MEV</strong>): sandwiching swaps, front-running liquidations, copying profitable transactions. Builder defences: slippage limits and deadlines, commit-reveal designs, batch auctions, and offering private transaction submission to users.</p>\n<h2 id=\"nodes-and-rpc\">Nodes and RPC</h2>\n<p>Your node or RPC provider is part of your trust chain. A malicious or compromised RPC can lie about balances and state to your front end. Use reputable providers, compare results across more than one for critical reads, never expose admin RPC methods publicly, and protect validator keys with remote signers and slashing protection.</p>\n<h2 id=\"governance\">Governance</h2>\n<p>If tokens vote on upgrades, an attacker who can borrow or buy enough votes can pass a malicious proposal (the Beanstalk governance attack in 2022 used a flash loan to do exactly that). Snapshot voting power before proposals, add timelocks, and give a guardian the power to veto obviously malicious actions.</p>","quiz":[{"q":"The Ronin bridge loss happened mainly because:","options":["Of a reentrancy bug","Attackers gained control of enough validator keys to approve fake withdrawals","Of a 51% attack on Ethereum"],"answer":1,"explain":"Key management, not contract code, was the weak point."},{"q":"Why check updatedAt on an oracle price?","options":["Feeds can stop updating, and a stale price can be badly wrong","It saves gas","It prevents reentrancy"],"answer":0,"explain":"Freshness checks stop your protocol acting on outdated data."},{"q":"A user's large swap gets sandwiched. Which setting most directly limits the damage?","options":["A higher gas price","A tight slippage limit (minimum amount out) with a deadline","A longer approval"],"answer":1,"explain":"The swap reverts instead of executing at a manipulated price."},{"q":"Why compare critical reads across more than one RPC provider?","options":["To save money","A single compromised or faulty RPC could return false state to your app","Providers require it"],"answer":1,"explain":"Your RPC is part of what you trust; reduce single points of failure."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Reject stale or broken prices","file":"PriceCheck.sol","lang":"solidity","run":false,"prompt":"<p>After reading a Chainlink-style feed, reject bad data. Three lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>(, int256 price, , uint256 updatedAt, ) = feed.latestRoundData();</code></li><li><code>require(price &gt; 0, &quot;Bad price&quot;);</code></li><li><code>require(block.timestamp - updatedAt &lt; 1 hours, &quot;Stale price&quot;);</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"(, int256 price, , uint256 updatedAt, ) = feed.latestRoundData();\nrequire(price > 0, \"Bad price\");\nrequire(block.timestamp - updatedAt < 1 hours, \"Stale price\");","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*\\(\\s*,\\s*int256\\s+price\\s*,\\s*,\\s*uint256\\s+updatedAt\\s*,\\s*\\)\\s*=\\s*feed\\.latestRoundData\\(\\s*\\)\\s*;","text":"Line 1 destructures five return values, keeping only the 2nd and 4th: <code>(, int256 price, , uint256 updatedAt, ) = feed.latestRoundData();</code>"},{"re":"require\\(\\s*price\\s*>\\s*0\\s*,\\s*[\"']Bad price[\"']\\s*\\)\\s*;","text":"Line 2: <code>require(price &gt; 0, &quot;Bad price&quot;);</code>"},{"re":"require\\(\\s*block\\.timestamp\\s*-\\s*updatedAt\\s*<\\s*1\\s+hours\\s*,\\s*[\"']Stale price[\"']\\s*\\)\\s*;","text":"Line 3: <code>require(block.timestamp - updatedAt &lt; 1 hours, &quot;Stale price&quot;);</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Feeds can stop updating (network trouble, a paused feed, a sequencer outage on an L2). Without these checks, your protocol keeps trusting yesterday's price while the market has moved 30%. Pick the freshness window from the feed's documented update interval.</p>"}],"text":"The protocol is more than its contracts The smart contract security lesson covered bugs inside contracts. Real protocols are systems: contracts plus oracles, bridges, nodes, keys, front ends, governance and people. Attackers pick the weakest part. users - front end (DNS, CDN, JS libraries) - wallet - RPC node - chain |-- your contracts |-- oracles (prices) |-- bridges (other chains) admin keys / multisig / governance --^ Bridges: the biggest honeypots A bridge locks assets on one chain and mints a representation on another. Whoever can convince the bridge that a deposit happened can mint unbacked tokens. Some of the largest losses in crypto history were bridges: Ronin (2022) : attackers obtained enough validator keys (5 of 9) to approve fake withdrawals. A key-management failure, not a code bug. Wormhole (2022) : a signature-verification flaw let an attacker mint wrapped ETH without depositing. Nomad (2022) : an initialisation mistake made every message look valid; hundreds of people copied the first attacker's transaction. Controls: many independent validators with hardware-protected keys, rate limits on withdrawals, delays on large transfers, and monitoring that can pause the bridge. Oracles Contracts cannot see the outside world, so oracles report prices and data. If an attacker can move the price your protocol reads, they can borrow too much or liquidate others unfairly. Defences: decentralised oracle networks, time-weighted prices, sanity bounds, and checking that data is fresh. Consensus and the chain itself 51% attacks : on smaller proof-of-work chains, renting enough hash power to rewrite recent blocks has enabled double-spends against exchanges. Defence for integrators: wait for more confirmations on smaller chains. Reorgs and finality : never treat a transaction as final until the chain's finality rules say so. L2 sequencers : most rollups have a single sequencer today; if it goes down, price feeds and liquidations behave strangely. Chainlink publishes sequencer-uptime feeds for this. MEV and the public mempool Pending transactions are visible. Searchers reorder, insert and copy them to extract value ( MEV ): sandwiching swaps, front-running liquidations, copying profitable transactions. Builder defences: slippage limits and deadlines, commit-reveal designs, batch auctions, and offering private transaction submission to users. Nodes and RPC Your node or RPC provider is part of your trust chain. A malicious or compromised RPC can lie about balances and state to your front end. Use reputable providers, compare results across more than one for critical reads, never expose admin RPC methods publicly, and protect validator keys with remote signers and slashing protection. Governance If tokens vote on upgrades, an attacker who can borrow or buy enough votes can pass a malicious proposal (the Beanstalk governance attack in 2022 used a flash loan to do exactly that). Snapshot voting power before proposals, add timelocks, and give a guardian the power to veto obviously malicious actions.","track":"security"},{"id":"sec-audit-process","title":"Running a professional security audit","level":"advanced","minutes":40,"summary":"Scope, review, prove and report. The end-to-end process auditors follow, how to rate severity, and how to write a finding developers act on.","html":"<h2 id=\"what-an-audit-is-and-is-not\">What an audit is, and is not</h2>\n<p>A security audit is a time-boxed, expert review of a specific version of code against its intended behaviour. It <strong>reduces</strong> risk; it does not prove there are no bugs. Many exploited protocols had been audited. The best teams combine audits with testing, a bug bounty, monitoring and conservative launches.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-audit-process\">The audit process</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Scoping</strong>: exact commit hash, which files, which chains, what is out of scope, how long, and what the protocol is supposed to do.</li><li><strong>Understanding</strong>: read the docs and specs; list the actors and their powers; draw the money flows. Write down the <strong>invariants</strong>: things that must always be true.</li><li><strong>Automated pass</strong>: compile, run the tests, coverage, Slither or Aderyn, and a quick fuzz run. Triage everything they report.</li><li><strong>Manual review</strong>: line by line, with a checklist (access control, external calls, maths, oracles, upgrades, signatures, denial of service, economic attacks).</li><li><strong>Prove it</strong>: write a <strong>proof-of-concept test</strong> for every real finding. If you cannot demonstrate it, rate it lower or keep digging.</li><li><strong>Report</strong>: findings with severity, impact, proof and fix.</li><li><strong>Fix review</strong>: verify every fix, and check the fixes did not introduce new bugs.</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"rating-severity\">Rating severity</h2>\n<p>Severity combines <strong>impact</strong> (how bad if it happens) and <strong>likelihood</strong> (how easy and probable):</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Low likelihood</th><th>Medium likelihood</th><th>High likelihood</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>High impact</strong> (funds lost or stuck)</td><td>Medium</td><td>High</td><td>Critical</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Medium impact</strong> (temporary loss, griefing)</td><td>Low</td><td>Medium</td><td>High</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Low impact</strong> (minor, no fund risk)</td><td>Informational</td><td>Low</td><td>Medium</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Be honest. Inflating severity damages your credibility; downplaying it can cost users their money.</p>\n<h2 id=\"writing-a-finding\">Writing a finding</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">[H-01] Reentrancy in Vault.withdraw allows draining all deposits\n\nSeverity: High (impact: total loss of funds; likelihood: high, any user can call it)\n\nDescription: withdraw() sends ETH before setting the caller's balance to zero.\nA contract whose receive() calls withdraw() again is paid repeatedly.\n\nProof of concept: test/Exploit.t.sol::test_Exploit_Reentrancy drains 10 ETH\nfrom a vault in which the attacker deposited 1 ETH.\n\nRecommendation: set balances[msg.sender] = 0 before the external call\n(checks-effects-interactions) and add OpenZeppelin's ReentrancyGuard.</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"the-proof-of-concept-test\">The proof-of-concept test</h2>\n<p>A finding with a passing exploit test gets fixed. A finding with only a paragraph gets debated.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"where-to-practise-auditing\">Where to practise auditing</h2>\n<ul><li>Read real reports: public audits from established firms, and <strong>Solodit</strong>, a searchable database of thousands of past audit findings.</li><li><strong>Competitive audits</strong> (Code4rena, Sherlock, Cantina, CodeHawks): anyone can audit live codebases for a prize pool. Judges grade your findings, which is the fastest feedback loop available.</li><li>Re-audit old contest codebases and compare your findings with the official report.</li></ul>","quiz":[{"q":"What does an audit guarantee?","options":["That the code has no bugs","Nothing absolute: it is a time-boxed expert review that reduces risk for one specific version","That the protocol is profitable"],"answer":1,"explain":"Audited code is still exploited sometimes. Audits are one layer of defence."},{"q":"A bug lets any user permanently lock all funds, and anyone can trigger it. Severity?","options":["Low","Medium","Critical (high impact, high likelihood)"],"answer":2,"explain":"Loss or freezing of all funds by anyone is as serious as it gets."},{"q":"Why should every finding include a proof-of-concept test?","options":["It proves the issue is real, shows its impact, and becomes a regression test after the fix","Clients require it for payment","It makes the report longer"],"answer":0,"explain":"Demonstrated bugs get fixed; arguments get ignored."},{"q":"What is a fix review?","options":["A second audit of the whole codebase","Checking that each fix resolves its finding without introducing new problems","Reviewing the auditor's invoice"],"answer":1,"explain":"Fixes are new code, and new code can have new bugs."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Write the proof-of-concept test","file":"Exploit.t.sol","lang":"solidity","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write the PoC test for the finding above. Five lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>function test_Exploit_Reentrancy() public {</code></li><li>Deploy the attacker against the vault: <code>Attacker attacker = new Attacker(vault);</code></li><li>Give it 1 ETH: <code>vm.deal(address(attacker), 1 ether);</code></li><li>Run the attack: <code>attacker.attack();</code></li><li>Prove the vault is empty: <code>assertEq(address(vault).balance, 0);</code></li></ol>\n<p>Then close the function with <code>}</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"function test_Exploit_Reentrancy() public {\n    Attacker attacker = new Attacker(vault);\n    vm.deal(address(attacker), 1 ether);\n    attacker.attack();\n    assertEq(address(vault).balance, 0);\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"function\\s+test_Exploit_Reentrancy\\s*\\(\\s*\\)\\s*public\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>function test_Exploit_Reentrancy() public {</code>"},{"re":"Attacker\\s+attacker\\s*=\\s*new\\s+Attacker\\(\\s*vault\\s*\\)\\s*;","text":"Line 2: <code>Attacker attacker = new Attacker(vault);</code>"},{"re":"vm\\.deal\\(\\s*address\\(\\s*attacker\\s*\\)\\s*,\\s*1\\s+ether\\s*\\)\\s*;","text":"Line 3: <code>vm.deal(address(attacker), 1 ether);</code>"},{"re":"attacker\\.attack\\(\\s*\\)\\s*;","text":"Line 4: <code>attacker.attack();</code>"},{"re":"assertEq\\(\\s*address\\(\\s*vault\\s*\\)\\.balance\\s*,\\s*0\\s*\\)\\s*;","text":"Line 5: <code>assertEq(address(vault).balance, 0);</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Run it with <code>forge test --match-test test_Exploit_Reentrancy -vvvv</code> to see the full call trace, including every re-entry. After the fix, the same test should fail, so turn it into a regression test that expects the attack to revert.</p>"}],"text":"What an audit is, and is not A security audit is a time-boxed, expert review of a specific version of code against its intended behaviour. It reduces risk; it does not prove there are no bugs. Many exploited protocols had been audited. The best teams combine audits with testing, a bug bounty, monitoring and conservative launches. The audit process Scoping : exact commit hash, which files, which chains, what is out of scope, how long, and what the protocol is supposed to do. Understanding : read the docs and specs; list the actors and their powers; draw the money flows. Write down the invariants : things that must always be true. Automated pass : compile, run the tests, coverage, Slither or Aderyn, and a quick fuzz run. Triage everything they report. Manual review : line by line, with a checklist (access control, external calls, maths, oracles, upgrades, signatures, denial of service, economic attacks). Prove it : write a proof-of-concept test for every real finding. If you cannot demonstrate it, rate it lower or keep digging. Report : findings with severity, impact, proof and fix. Fix review : verify every fix, and check the fixes did not introduce new bugs. Rating severity Severity combines impact (how bad if it happens) and likelihood (how easy and probable): Low likelihood Medium likelihood High likelihood High impact (funds lost or stuck) Medium High Critical Medium impact (temporary loss, griefing) Low Medium High Low impact (minor, no fund risk) Informational Low Medium Be honest. Inflating severity damages your credibility; downplaying it can cost users their money. Writing a finding [H-01] Reentrancy in Vault.withdraw allows draining all deposits Severity: High (impact: total loss of funds; likelihood: high, any user can call it) Description: withdraw() sends ETH before setting the caller's balance to zero. A contract whose receive() calls withdraw() again is paid repeatedly. Proof of concept: test/Exploit.t.sol::test_Exploit_Reentrancy drains 10 ETH from a vault in which the attacker deposited 1 ETH. Recommendation: set balances[msg.sender] = 0 before the external call (checks-effects-interactions) and add OpenZeppelin's ReentrancyGuard. The proof-of-concept test A finding with a passing exploit test gets fixed. A finding with only a paragraph gets debated. Where to practise auditing Read real reports: public audits from established firms, and Solodit , a searchable database of thousands of past audit findings. Competitive audits (Code4rena, Sherlock, Cantina, CodeHawks): anyone can audit live codebases for a prize pool. Judges grade your findings, which is the fastest feedback loop available. Re-audit old contest codebases and compare your findings with the official report.","track":"security"},{"id":"sec-incident-response","title":"Monitoring and incident response for protocols","level":"advanced","minutes":35,"summary":"Detect attacks in minutes, pause safely, run a war room, communicate honestly, and learn from every incident with a blameless post-mortem.","html":"<h2 id=\"assume-it-will-happen\">Assume it will happen</h2>\n<p>Even well-audited protocols get attacked. The difference between a small incident and a catastrophe is often <strong>how quickly the team detects it and what it can do in the first ten minutes</strong>. That has to be designed and rehearsed before launch.</p>\n<h2 id=\"detect-monitoring-and-alerting\">Detect: monitoring and alerting</h2>\n<p>Watch for the signs of an attack in progress:</p>\n<ul><li>Unusual outflows: TVL dropping sharply, large withdrawals, a new address draining a pool.</li><li>Admin activity: ownership changes, upgrades, role grants, pauses you did not plan.</li><li>Oracle anomalies: prices far from other sources, or stale feeds.</li><li>Mempool signals: a newly deployed contract calling your protocol in strange ways.</li><li>Off-chain: DNS records or served JavaScript changing, unusual logins to the registrar or cloud account.</li></ul>\n<p>Tools such as Forta, Tenderly alerts and Hypernative (and your own scripts reading events) can page the team within seconds. Alerts go to phones, not just email.</p>\n<h2 id=\"contain-the-pause-switch\">Contain: the pause switch</h2>\n<p>A <strong>pause</strong> stops the most dangerous functions while letting users keep their funds safe. OpenZeppelin's <code>Pausable</code> and <code>AccessControl</code> make this straightforward. Give pause power to a small, fast <strong>guardian</strong> group, but never the power to move funds.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"respond-the-first-hour\">Respond: the first hour</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Confirm</strong>: is it real? Check the alert against the chain.</li><li><strong>Contain</strong>: pause, raise the timelock, revoke a compromised role, or move remaining funds to safety through pre-approved procedures.</li><li><strong>War room</strong>: a private channel with a named incident lead, a scribe, technical responders and a communications lead.</li><li><strong>Get help</strong>: the security community runs emergency response for exactly this (for example <strong>SEAL 911</strong>, a volunteer hotline of experienced white hats). Prepare contacts in advance.</li><li><strong>Communicate</strong>: a short, honest public update quickly (&quot;We are investigating an incident affecting X. Deposits are paused. Do not interact with Y.&quot;), then regular updates. Silence breeds panic and scams.</li><li><strong>Trace and recover</strong>: follow the funds, contact exchanges and stablecoin issuers that can freeze assets, and consider a public bounty offer for the return of funds.</li><li><strong>Report it (US)</strong>: file a report with the <strong>FBI's Internet Crime Complaint Center</strong> (ic3.gov) including transaction hashes and addresses, as exchanges and issuers often need a law-enforcement report before they freeze funds. Bring in a lawyer early, because legal duties start running quickly:<ul><li>If personal data was exposed, <strong>state breach-notification laws</strong> (every state has one) set deadlines for telling affected people, and sometimes the state attorney general.</li><li>A <strong>public company</strong> must disclose a material cybersecurity incident on SEC <strong>Form 8-K</strong> within four business days of deciding it is material.</li><li>Regulated firms have their own rules (New York's DFS, for example, requires notice within 72 hours for many incidents). CISA also takes voluntary reports and can help.</li></ul></li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"learn-the-blameless-post-mortem\">Learn: the blameless post-mortem</h2>\n<p>Within days, publish what happened, the timeline, root cause, impact, what worked, what did not, and concrete changes. &quot;Blameless&quot; means focusing on system failures, not scapegoats, so people report problems honestly in future.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-pro\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Rehearse before launch</p><p>Run a tabletop exercise: one person plays the attacker and describes events; the team responds in real time using the actual runbook, keys and tools. You will discover the multisig signer whose hardware wallet is in a drawer abroad, before it matters.</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"Who should hold the power to pause a protocol?","options":["Every token holder","A small, fast guardian group that can pause but cannot move funds","Only the deployer key"],"answer":1,"explain":"Speed matters in an incident, and limited powers limit the damage if a guardian key is lost."},{"q":"What should the first public update during an incident do?","options":["Say nothing until everything is fixed","Briefly and honestly say what is known, what is paused, and what users should avoid","Blame the auditors"],"answer":1,"explain":"Clear early communication protects users and reduces panic and scams."},{"q":"What is a blameless post-mortem?","options":["An honest write-up focused on system failures and fixes rather than punishing individuals","A report with no technical details","A legal document"],"answer":0,"explain":"Blame makes people hide problems; systems thinking prevents repeats."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Add an emergency pause","file":"Vault.sol","lang":"solidity","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write two functions, using OpenZeppelin <code>Pausable</code> and <code>AccessControl</code>:</p>\n<ol><li>A <code>deposit</code> that is <code>external payable whenNotPaused</code> and adds <code>msg.value</code> to <code>balances[msg.sender]</code>.</li><li>A <code>pause</code> that is <code>external onlyRole(GUARDIAN_ROLE)</code> and calls <code>_pause();</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"function deposit() external payable whenNotPaused {\n    balances[msg.sender] += msg.value;\n}\nfunction pause() external onlyRole(GUARDIAN_ROLE) {\n    _pause();\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"function\\s+deposit\\s*\\(\\s*\\)\\s*external\\s+payable\\s+whenNotPaused\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>function deposit() external payable whenNotPaused {</code>"},{"re":"balances\\[\\s*msg\\.sender\\s*\\]\\s*\\+=\\s*msg\\.value\\s*;","text":"Line 2: <code>balances[msg.sender] += msg.value;</code>"},{"re":"function\\s+pause\\s*\\(\\s*\\)\\s*external\\s+onlyRole\\(\\s*GUARDIAN_ROLE\\s*\\)\\s*\\{","text":"The pause function: <code>function pause() external onlyRole(GUARDIAN_ROLE) {</code>"},{"re":"_pause\\(\\s*\\)\\s*;","text":"Inside it: <code>_pause();</code>"}],"explain":"<p>While paused, <code>deposit</code> reverts. Think carefully about <strong>which</strong> functions to pause: pausing withdrawals during an attack may protect funds, but pausing them for too long traps honest users. Many teams let withdrawals continue while pausing deposits, borrowing and anything the attack uses.</p>"}],"text":"Assume it will happen Even well-audited protocols get attacked. The difference between a small incident and a catastrophe is often how quickly the team detects it and what it can do in the first ten minutes . That has to be designed and rehearsed before launch. Detect: monitoring and alerting Watch for the signs of an attack in progress: Unusual outflows: TVL dropping sharply, large withdrawals, a new address draining a pool. Admin activity: ownership changes, upgrades, role grants, pauses you did not plan. Oracle anomalies: prices far from other sources, or stale feeds. Mempool signals: a newly deployed contract calling your protocol in strange ways. Off-chain: DNS records or served JavaScript changing, unusual logins to the registrar or cloud account. Tools such as Forta, Tenderly alerts and Hypernative (and your own scripts reading events) can page the team within seconds. Alerts go to phones, not just email. Contain: the pause switch A pause stops the most dangerous functions while letting users keep their funds safe. OpenZeppelin's Pausable and AccessControl make this straightforward. Give pause power to a small, fast guardian group, but never the power to move funds. Respond: the first hour Confirm : is it real? Check the alert against the chain. Contain : pause, raise the timelock, revoke a compromised role, or move remaining funds to safety through pre-approved procedures. War room : a private channel with a named incident lead, a scribe, technical responders and a communications lead. Get help : the security community runs emergency response for exactly this (for example SEAL 911 , a volunteer hotline of experienced white hats). Prepare contacts in advance. Communicate : a short, honest public update quickly ( We are investigating an incident affecting X. Deposits are paused. Do not interact with Y. ), then regular updates. Silence breeds panic and scams. Trace and recover : follow the funds, contact exchanges and stablecoin issuers that can freeze assets, and consider a public bounty offer for the return of funds. Report it (US) : file a report with the FBI's Internet Crime Complaint Center (ic3.gov) including transaction hashes and addresses, as exchanges and issuers often need a law-enforcement report before they freeze funds. Bring in a lawyer early, because legal duties start running quickly: If personal data was exposed, state breach-notification laws (every state has one) set deadlines for telling affected people, and sometimes the state attorney general. A public company must disclose a material cybersecurity incident on SEC Form 8-K within four business days of deciding it is material. Regulated firms have their own rules (New York's DFS, for example, requires notice within 72 hours for many incidents). CISA also takes voluntary reports and can help. Learn: the blameless post-mortem Within days, publish what happened, the timeline, root cause, impact, what worked, what did not, and concrete changes. Blameless means focusing on system failures, not scapegoats, so people report problems honestly in future. Rehearse before launch Run a tabletop exercise: one person plays the attacker and describes events; the team responds in real time using the actual runbook, keys and tools. You will discover the multisig signer whose hardware wallet is in a drawer abroad, before it matters.","track":"security"},{"id":"sec-formal-methods","title":"Formal verification and advanced fuzzing","level":"expert","minutes":45,"summary":"Move from \"tested\" to \"proven\". Write properties, run symbolic execution with Halmos, and understand where formal methods, Echidna and Medusa fit in a serious security programme.","html":"<h2 id=\"beyond-example-based-testing\">Beyond example-based testing</h2>\n<p>A unit test checks one input. A fuzz test checks thousands of random inputs. <strong>Formal verification</strong> goes further: it checks a property for <strong>every possible input</strong>, by reasoning about the code mathematically. When it says a property holds, it holds, within the model and assumptions you gave it. When it fails, it hands you a concrete counterexample.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Technique</th><th>Explores</th><th>Finds</th><th>Cost</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Unit tests</td><td>Inputs you chose</td><td>Bugs you imagined</td><td>Low</td></tr><tr><td>Fuzzing (Foundry, Echidna, Medusa)</td><td>Many random inputs and call sequences</td><td>Edge cases you did not imagine</td><td>Low to medium</td></tr><tr><td>Symbolic execution (Halmos, hevm, Kontrol)</td><td>All inputs, up to a bound</td><td>Counterexamples, or proof within the bound</td><td>Medium</td></tr><tr><td>Formal verification (Certora Prover and others)</td><td>All inputs and states, via specifications</td><td>Proofs of rules, or violations</td><td>High; needs specialist skill</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"properties-are-everything\">Properties are everything</h2>\n<p>Every one of these tools needs you to state <strong>what must be true</strong>. Good properties come in a few shapes:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Valid states</strong>: <code>totalSupply == sum of all balances</code>; <code>debt &lt;= collateral * maxLTV</code>.</li><li><strong>State transitions</strong>: only the owner can change <code>fee</code>; <code>paused</code> can only become false through <code>unpause</code>.</li><li><strong>Variable relationships</strong>: shares and assets in a vault move in the same direction.</li><li><strong>High-level</strong>: no user can withdraw more than they deposited plus earned interest.</li><li><strong>No unexpected reverts</strong>: a user with enough balance can always withdraw.</li></ul>\n<p>Writing these is the real skill. It forces you to know exactly what the protocol promises, and it is where many bugs are found before any tool even runs.</p>\n<h2 id=\"symbolic-testing-with-halmos\">Symbolic testing with Halmos</h2>\n<p><strong>Halmos</strong> (install with <code>uv tool install halmos</code>) runs your existing Foundry test setup, but treats the inputs of <code>check_</code> functions as <strong>symbolic</strong>: it explores every possible value at once.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"solidity\"><pre><code class=\"language-solidity\">// test/Token.symbolic.t.sol\nfunction check_transferPreservesSupply(address to, uint256 amount) public {\n    uint256 supplyBefore = token.totalSupply();\n    vm.prank(owner);\n    token.transfer(to, amount);           // reverting paths are simply not counted\n    assert(token.totalSupply() == supplyBefore);\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>Run it with:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">halmos</code></pre></div>\n<p>If any <code>to</code> and <code>amount</code> could break the assertion, Halmos prints the exact values. If not, the property holds for every input within its bounds (for example, loop bounds you configure).</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"stateful-fuzzing-echidna-and-medusa\">Stateful fuzzing: Echidna and Medusa</h2>\n<p>Foundry's invariant testing (from the <a href=\"#lesson-crypto-testing\">auditing lesson</a>) has two specialist relatives:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Echidna</strong> (Trail of Bits): mature, property-based fuzzer for Solidity, with powerful corpus and coverage features.</li><li><strong>Medusa</strong>: a newer, parallel fuzzer in the same family, built for speed on multi-core machines.</li></ul>\n<p>Expert teams often run the same properties through several engines, because each explores differently.</p>\n<h2 id=\"know-the-limits\">Know the limits</h2>\n<ul><li>Proofs only cover the properties you wrote. A perfectly proven wrong specification proves nothing useful.</li><li>Tools model the EVM; they may not model other contracts, oracles or economic behaviour.</li><li>Bounds matter: &quot;no counterexample up to 3 loop iterations&quot; is not the same as &quot;never&quot;.</li><li>Formal work complements audits, fuzzing and monitoring; it does not replace them.</li></ul>","quiz":[{"q":"What does a symbolic execution tool like Halmos do with function inputs?","options":["Picks random values","Treats them as symbols representing every possible value, and searches for any that break the property","Uses only zero"],"answer":1,"explain":"That is why it can return a counterexample or show none exists within its bounds."},{"q":"Formal verification proves a property. What can still go wrong?","options":["The property itself may be incomplete or wrong, or the model may miss real-world behaviour","Nothing; proven code is perfectly safe","The proof might change after deployment"],"answer":0,"explain":"A proof is only as good as the specification and assumptions."},{"q":"Which is a valid-state invariant?","options":["Only the owner can call setFee","totalSupply equals the sum of all balances","The contract compiles"],"answer":1,"explain":"Valid-state properties must hold in every reachable state."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Write a symbolic property","file":"Token.symbolic.t.sol","lang":"solidity","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write a Halmos property that no user's balance can ever exceed the total supply. Three lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>function check_balanceNeverExceedsSupply(address user) public view {</code></li><li><code>assert(token.balanceOf(user) &lt;= token.totalSupply());</code></li><li><code>}</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"function check_balanceNeverExceedsSupply(address user) public view {\n    assert(token.balanceOf(user) <= token.totalSupply());\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"function\\s+check_balanceNeverExceedsSupply\\s*\\(\\s*address\\s+user\\s*\\)","text":"Line 1 starts <code>function check_balanceNeverExceedsSupply(address user)</code>. Halmos runs functions whose names start with <code>check_</code>."},{"re":"\\)\\s*public\\s+view\\s*\\{","text":"then <code>public view {</code> (it only reads state)."},{"re":"assert\\(\\s*token\\.balanceOf\\(\\s*user\\s*\\)\\s*<=\\s*token\\.totalSupply\\(\\s*\\)\\s*\\)\\s*;","text":"Line 2: <code>assert(token.balanceOf(user) &lt;= token.totalSupply());</code> (use <code>assert</code>, not <code>assertEq</code>)."}],"explain":"<p>Because <code>user</code> is symbolic, this one function checks every possible address at once. Properties like this are cheap to write and catch whole classes of accounting bugs.</p>"}],"text":"Beyond example-based testing A unit test checks one input. A fuzz test checks thousands of random inputs. Formal verification goes further: it checks a property for every possible input , by reasoning about the code mathematically. When it says a property holds, it holds, within the model and assumptions you gave it. When it fails, it hands you a concrete counterexample. Technique Explores Finds Cost Unit tests Inputs you chose Bugs you imagined Low Fuzzing (Foundry, Echidna, Medusa) Many random inputs and call sequences Edge cases you did not imagine Low to medium Symbolic execution (Halmos, hevm, Kontrol) All inputs, up to a bound Counterexamples, or proof within the bound Medium Formal verification (Certora Prover and others) All inputs and states, via specifications Proofs of rules, or violations High; needs specialist skill Properties are everything Every one of these tools needs you to state what must be true . Good properties come in a few shapes: Valid states : totalSupply == sum of all balances ; debt = collateral * maxLTV . State transitions : only the owner can change fee ; paused can only become false through unpause . Variable relationships : shares and assets in a vault move in the same direction. High-level : no user can withdraw more than they deposited plus earned interest. No unexpected reverts : a user with enough balance can always withdraw. Writing these is the real skill. It forces you to know exactly what the protocol promises, and it is where many bugs are found before any tool even runs. Symbolic testing with Halmos Halmos (install with uv tool install halmos ) runs your existing Foundry test setup, but treats the inputs of check_ functions as symbolic : it explores every possible value at once. // test/Token.symbolic.t.sol function check_transferPreservesSupply(address to, uint256 amount) public { uint256 supplyBefore = token.totalSupply(); vm.prank(owner); token.transfer(to, amount); // reverting paths are simply not counted assert(token.totalSupply() == supplyBefore); } Run it with: halmos If any to and amount could break the assertion, Halmos prints the exact values. If not, the property holds for every input within its bounds (for example, loop bounds you configure). Stateful fuzzing: Echidna and Medusa Foundry's invariant testing (from the auditing lesson ) has two specialist relatives: Echidna (Trail of Bits): mature, property-based fuzzer for Solidity, with powerful corpus and coverage features. Medusa : a newer, parallel fuzzer in the same family, built for speed on multi-core machines. Expert teams often run the same properties through several engines, because each explores differently. Know the limits Proofs only cover the properties you wrote. A perfectly proven wrong specification proves nothing useful. Tools model the EVM; they may not model other contracts, oracles or economic behaviour. Bounds matter: no counterexample up to 3 loop iterations is not the same as never . Formal work complements audits, fuzzing and monitoring; it does not replace them.","track":"security"},{"id":"sec-exploit-dev","title":"Exploit development: thinking like an attacker, ethically","level":"expert","minutes":45,"summary":"How real DeFi exploits are built (reading private state, flash loans, price manipulation, read-only reentrancy) on mainnet forks, and how to disclose responsibly and earn bounties.","html":"<h2 id=\"why-defenders-must-learn-to-attack\">Why defenders must learn to attack</h2>\n<p>You cannot defend against attacks you cannot imagine. Expert auditors and bug hunters think in exploits: given this code and a few million borrowed dollars, how do I make money? Everything in this lesson is practised <strong>only</strong> on local forks, your own contracts, CTFs and bounty programmes that authorise you.</p>\n<h2 id=\"tool-1-everything-on-chain-is-readable\">Tool 1: everything on-chain is readable</h2>\n<p><code>private</code> variables are hidden from other contracts, not from people. Anyone can read any storage slot:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">cast storage 0xVaultAddress 0 --rpc-url $SEPOLIA_RPC_URL</code></pre></div>\n<p>Slot 0 is the first state variable declared. Passwords, &quot;secret&quot; seeds and hidden parameters stored on-chain are public.</p>\n<h2 id=\"tool-2-mainnet-forks\">Tool 2: mainnet forks</h2>\n<p>A fork test runs against a local copy of real chain state: real tokens, real pools, real prices. You can impersonate any address, give yourself any balance, and replay history at a specific block.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">forge test --fork-url $MAINNET_RPC_URL --fork-block-number 21000000 --match-test test_Exploit -vvvv</code></pre></div>\n<p>Pinning <code>--fork-block-number</code> makes the test reproducible: the state it runs on never changes.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"tool-3-flash-loans\">Tool 3: flash loans</h2>\n<p>A <strong>flash loan</strong> lends any amount with no collateral, as long as it is repaid in the same transaction. It turns every attacker into a whale for one block. The classic pattern:</p>\n<ol><li>Borrow a huge amount (flash loan).</li><li>Use it to move something your target trusts: a pool's spot price, a governance vote count, a vault's share price.</li><li>Exploit the target at the manipulated value: borrow too much, mint too many shares, liquidate unfairly.</li><li>Undo the manipulation, repay the loan plus fee, keep the profit.</li></ol>\n<p>Flash loans are not the bug. The bug is a protocol that trusts a value one transaction can move.</p>\n<h2 id=\"advanced-bug-classes-to-study\">Advanced bug classes to study</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>The idea</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Read-only reentrancy</strong></td><td>During a callback, another protocol reads your contract's half-updated state (often a price) and trusts it</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cross-function reentrancy</strong></td><td>Re-entering a <em>different</em> function that shares state with the one mid-update</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Share inflation (ERC-4626)</strong></td><td>Donating assets to an empty vault so the next depositor's shares round down to zero</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Signature issues</strong></td><td>Replay across chains or contracts, malleable signatures, missing deadlines</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Precision and rounding</strong></td><td>Repeating a tiny rounding error thousands of times in one transaction</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Upgrade and initialisation</strong></td><td>Uninitialised implementations, storage collisions after upgrades</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Economic and oracle</strong></td><td>Profitable manipulation without any &quot;bug&quot; in the code at all</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Study real post-mortems for each, rebuild the exploit on a fork, then write the fix and a test proving it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"responsible-disclosure\">Responsible disclosure</h2>\n<p>When you find a real bug in live code:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Stop.</strong> Do not exploit it on mainnet, not even &quot;a little&quot; to prove it.</li><li><strong>Write it up</strong> with a proof of concept on a fork.</li><li><strong>Report privately</strong>: through the project's bug bounty (often on Immunefi) or its published security contact. Never in a public GitHub issue or tweet.</li><li><strong>Give them time to fix</strong> before any public write-up, and coordinate disclosure.</li></ol>\n<p>Bug bounties for critical smart contract bugs can be very large, and a track record of good reports is one of the strongest portfolios in security.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">The &quot;white hat&quot; line is permission</p><p>Taking funds from a live protocol &quot;to keep them safe&quot; without an agreed process can still be treated as theft. Rescues during an active attack are coordinated with the team and experienced responders, not done alone.</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"Why pin --fork-block-number in an exploit test?","options":["It makes the test faster","It fixes the chain state, so the test is reproducible and does not change as the chain moves on","It is required by law"],"answer":1,"explain":"Reproducibility is essential for reports and regression tests."},{"q":"What makes a flash-loan attack possible?","options":["Flash loans themselves are a bug","A protocol trusting a value (price, balance, vote count) that can be moved within one transaction","Low gas prices"],"answer":1,"explain":"Remove the manipulable dependency and the flash loan is harmless."},{"q":"You find a critical bug in a live DeFi protocol. What do you do?","options":["Exploit a small amount to prove it is real","Post it publicly to warn users","Report it privately through the bug bounty or security contact, with a proof of concept on a fork"],"answer":2,"explain":"Private, coordinated disclosure protects users and is how bounties are paid."},{"q":"What is read-only reentrancy?","options":["Another protocol reading your contract's state during a callback, while it is temporarily inconsistent","Re-entering a view function to change state","A reentrancy that only affects reads from storage slot 0"],"answer":0,"explain":"Your own contract may be fine; the protocol trusting your half-updated view is the victim."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Recon and reproduce","file":"Terminal","lang":"bash","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Two commands: read storage slot <code>0</code> of <code>0xVaultAddress</code> using <code>$SEPOLIA_RPC_URL</code>, then run the test <code>test_Exploit</code> on a mainnet fork (<code>$MAINNET_RPC_URL</code>) pinned at block <code>21000000</code>, with maximum verbosity (<code>-vvvv</code>). Flags for the second command in this order: <code>--fork-url</code>, <code>--fork-block-number</code>, <code>--match-test</code>, <code>-vvvv</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"cast storage 0xVaultAddress 0 --rpc-url $SEPOLIA_RPC_URL\nforge test --fork-url $MAINNET_RPC_URL --fork-block-number 21000000 --match-test test_Exploit -vvvv","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*cast\\s+storage\\s+0xVaultAddress\\s+0\\s+--rpc-url\\s+\\$SEPOLIA_RPC_URL\\s*$","text":"Line 1: <code>cast storage 0xVaultAddress 0 --rpc-url $SEPOLIA_RPC_URL</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*forge\\s+test\\s+--fork-url\\s+\\$MAINNET_RPC_URL","text":"Line 2 starts <code>forge test --fork-url $MAINNET_RPC_URL</code>"},{"re":"--fork-block-number\\s+21000000","text":"then <code>--fork-block-number 21000000</code>"},{"re":"--match-test\\s+test_Exploit\\s+-vvvv\\s*$","text":"and ends <code>--match-test test_Exploit -vvvv</code>"}],"explain":""}],"text":"Why defenders must learn to attack You cannot defend against attacks you cannot imagine. Expert auditors and bug hunters think in exploits: given this code and a few million borrowed dollars, how do I make money? Everything in this lesson is practised only on local forks, your own contracts, CTFs and bounty programmes that authorise you. Tool 1: everything on-chain is readable private variables are hidden from other contracts, not from people. Anyone can read any storage slot: cast storage 0xVaultAddress 0 --rpc-url $SEPOLIA_RPC_URL Slot 0 is the first state variable declared. Passwords, secret seeds and hidden parameters stored on-chain are public. Tool 2: mainnet forks A fork test runs against a local copy of real chain state: real tokens, real pools, real prices. You can impersonate any address, give yourself any balance, and replay history at a specific block. forge test --fork-url $MAINNET_RPC_URL --fork-block-number 21000000 --match-test test_Exploit -vvvv Pinning --fork-block-number makes the test reproducible: the state it runs on never changes. Tool 3: flash loans A flash loan lends any amount with no collateral, as long as it is repaid in the same transaction. It turns every attacker into a whale for one block. The classic pattern: Borrow a huge amount (flash loan). Use it to move something your target trusts: a pool's spot price, a governance vote count, a vault's share price. Exploit the target at the manipulated value: borrow too much, mint too many shares, liquidate unfairly. Undo the manipulation, repay the loan plus fee, keep the profit. Flash loans are not the bug. The bug is a protocol that trusts a value one transaction can move. Advanced bug classes to study Class The idea Read-only reentrancy During a callback, another protocol reads your contract's half-updated state (often a price) and trusts it Cross-function reentrancy Re-entering a different function that shares state with the one mid-update Share inflation (ERC-4626) Donating assets to an empty vault so the next depositor's shares round down to zero Signature issues Replay across chains or contracts, malleable signatures, missing deadlines Precision and rounding Repeating a tiny rounding error thousands of times in one transaction Upgrade and initialisation Uninitialised implementations, storage collisions after upgrades Economic and oracle Profitable manipulation without any bug in the code at all Study real post-mortems for each, rebuild the exploit on a fork, then write the fix and a test proving it. Responsible disclosure When you find a real bug in live code: Stop. Do not exploit it on mainnet, not even a little to prove it. Write it up with a proof of concept on a fork. Report privately : through the project's bug bounty (often on Immunefi) or its published security contact. Never in a public GitHub issue or tweet. Give them time to fix before any public write-up, and coordinate disclosure. Bug bounties for critical smart contract bugs can be very large, and a track record of good reports is one of the strongest portfolios in security. The white hat line is permission Taking funds from a live protocol to keep them safe without an agreed process can still be treated as theft. Rescues during an active attack are coordinated with the team and experienced responders, not done alone.","track":"security"},{"id":"sec-launch-security","title":"Capstone: designing security for a blockchain launch","level":"expert","minutes":45,"summary":"Bring every layer together into one security programme for a protocol launch, from threat model to bug bounty, with a readiness checklist you can reuse.","html":"<h2 id=\"security-is-a-programme-not-a-product\">Security is a programme, not a product</h2>\n<p>No single audit, tool or key setup makes a protocol safe. Expert teams run a <strong>security programme</strong>: overlapping layers, each assuming the others might fail. This capstone puts the whole track together as the plan you would present before launching a blockchain project.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-layers\">The layers</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>What &quot;good&quot; looks like</th><th>Lessons</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Design</strong></td><td>Written threat model; minimal admin powers; upgradeability justified or avoided</td><td>Mindset, Secure development</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Code</strong></td><td>Audited libraries; checks-effects-interactions; no manipulable price sources</td><td>Smart contract security</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Testing</strong></td><td>Unit, fuzz and invariant tests; symbolic checks on core properties; fork tests</td><td>Auditing, Formal verification</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Review</strong></td><td>At least one independent audit (two for large TVL) plus a fix review; ideally a competitive audit</td><td>Running an audit</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Keys</strong></td><td>Multisig of independent hardware-wallet signers; timelock on upgrades; separate guardian</td><td>Wallet and key opsec</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Infrastructure</strong></td><td>Hardened registrar and DNS; locked-down CI; dependency and secret scanning</td><td>Networks, Supply chain</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Launch</strong></td><td>Guarded launch: deposit caps, gradual limits, rate limits on withdrawals and bridges</td><td>This lesson</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Operations</strong></td><td>24/7 monitoring with phone alerts; pause runbook; rehearsed incident response</td><td>Incident response</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Community</strong></td><td>Public bug bounty sized to the value at risk; clear security contact; published audits</td><td>Exploit development</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"the-guarded-launch\">The guarded launch</h2>\n<p>Launch small on purpose:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Caps</strong>: limit total deposits (for example, a low TVL ceiling) and raise it in steps as the system proves itself.</li><li><strong>Rate limits</strong>: cap how much can leave per hour or day, so a bug cannot drain everything at once.</li><li><strong>Kill switches with limits</strong>: pausing is fast; unpausing and upgrades go through the multisig and timelock.</li><li><strong>Bug bounty live before launch</strong>, so finders have a legal, paid path from day one.</li><li><strong>Staged rollout across chains</strong>: one chain first; each new chain or bridge is a new attack surface.</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"budgeting\">Budgeting</h2>\n<p>Security spend should scale with value at risk. A useful sanity check: if an attacker could take 10 million dollars, a 30,000 dollar maximum bounty tells them stealing pays far better than reporting. Many serious projects offer a percentage of funds at risk, with a high cap, for critical bugs.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-readiness-review\">The readiness review</h2>\n<p>Before launch day, the team signs off on a checklist. Anything missing either gets done or is explicitly accepted as a risk by named people.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"your-launch-checklist\">Your launch checklist</h2>\n<ul><li>Threat model written, reviewed and kept up to date.</li><li>All admin powers documented publicly, with who holds them.</li><li>Ownership transferred from the deployer to the multisig; deployer key retired.</li><li>Timelock on upgrades and sensitive parameters.</li><li>Unit, fuzz, invariant and symbolic tests passing in CI; coverage reviewed.</li><li>Static analysis triaged; audits complete, published, and fixes reviewed.</li><li>Bug bounty live, sized to the value at risk.</li><li>Monitoring and paging in place for outflows, admin actions, oracles and DNS.</li><li>Incident runbook rehearsed in a tabletop exercise; emergency contacts ready.</li><li>Deposit caps and rate limits configured for the guarded launch.</li><li>Registrar, DNS, CI and npm accounts protected with hardware keys.</li><li>Legal review of the launch and any token (see the <a href=\"#lesson-tok-launch\">launch playbook</a>).</li></ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-pro\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Where to go from here</p><p>Build the <a href=\"#hackathons\">DeFi Security Hackathon</a> project end to end using this checklist, then publish your threat model and report in its README. That single repository demonstrates more senior security skill than a pile of certificates.</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"Why launch with deposit caps?","options":["So an undiscovered bug can only put a limited amount at risk while the system proves itself","To make the token scarce","Because audits require it"],"answer":0,"explain":"Guarded launches limit the blast radius of unknown bugs."},{"q":"An attacker could steal 10 million dollars, but the bug bounty pays at most 30,000. What is the problem?","options":["The bounty is too generous","The incentive to steal is far larger than the incentive to report","Bounties should be secret"],"answer":1,"explain":"Bounties should be sized to the value at risk."},{"q":"Which step should happen immediately after deploying the contracts?","options":["Post the deployer's private key backup to the team chat","Transfer ownership and admin roles from the deployer to the multisig (behind a timelock)","Remove the pause function"],"answer":1,"explain":"The deployer key is a single point of failure; retire it."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Automate the launch readiness check","file":"readiness.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write a tiny readiness check. Three lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const checks = { audited: true, bugBounty: true, multisig: true, monitoring: false };</code></li><li><code>const missing = Object.keys(checks).filter((k) =&gt; !checks[k]);</code></li><li>Print the result with a ternary: <code>console.log(missing.length ? ... : 'Ready to launch');</code> where the <code>...</code> is a template literal (between backticks) reading <code>Not ready: </code> followed by <code>${missing.join(', ')}</code>.</li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const checks = { audited: true, bugBounty: true, multisig: true, monitoring: false };\nconst missing = Object.keys(checks).filter((k) => !checks[k]);\nconsole.log(missing.length ? `Not ready: ${missing.join(', ')}` : 'Ready to launch');","also":["const checks = { audited: true, bugBounty: true, multisig: true, monitoring: false };\nconst missing = Object.keys(checks).filter(k => !checks[k]);\nconsole.log(missing.length ? `Not ready: ${missing.join(', ')}` : 'Ready to launch');"],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+checks\\s*=\\s*\\{\\s*audited\\s*:\\s*true\\s*,\\s*bugBounty\\s*:\\s*true\\s*,\\s*multisig\\s*:\\s*true\\s*,\\s*monitoring\\s*:\\s*false\\s*\\}","text":"Line 1: <code>const checks = { audited: true, bugBounty: true, multisig: true, monitoring: false };</code>"},{"re":"Object\\.keys\\(\\s*checks\\s*\\)\\.filter\\(","text":"Line 2 starts <code>const missing = Object.keys(checks).filter(</code>"},{"re":"\\(?\\s*k\\s*\\)?\\s*=>\\s*!\\s*checks\\[\\s*k\\s*\\]","text":"Keep the keys whose value is false: <code>(k) =&gt; !checks[k]</code>"},{"re":"missing\\.length\\s*\\?\\s*`Not ready: \\$\\{missing\\.join\\(\\s*['\"], ['\"]\\s*\\)\\}`\\s*:\\s*['\"]Ready to launch['\"]","text":"Line 3: a ternary: <code>missing.length ? </code> then a template literal <code>Not ready: ${missing.join(', ')}</code> then <code>: 'Ready to launch'</code>"}],"explain":"<p>It prints <code>Not ready: monitoring</code>. Real teams keep a much longer version of this list in their repository and treat it like a test suite: launch is blocked until every item passes or is formally accepted.</p>"}],"text":"Security is a programme, not a product No single audit, tool or key setup makes a protocol safe. Expert teams run a security programme : overlapping layers, each assuming the others might fail. This capstone puts the whole track together as the plan you would present before launching a blockchain project. The layers Layer What good looks like Lessons Design Written threat model; minimal admin powers; upgradeability justified or avoided Mindset, Secure development Code Audited libraries; checks-effects-interactions; no manipulable price sources Smart contract security Testing Unit, fuzz and invariant tests; symbolic checks on core properties; fork tests Auditing, Formal verification Review At least one independent audit (two for large TVL) plus a fix review; ideally a competitive audit Running an audit Keys Multisig of independent hardware-wallet signers; timelock on upgrades; separate guardian Wallet and key opsec Infrastructure Hardened registrar and DNS; locked-down CI; dependency and secret scanning Networks, Supply chain Launch Guarded launch: deposit caps, gradual limits, rate limits on withdrawals and bridges This lesson Operations 24/7 monitoring with phone alerts; pause runbook; rehearsed incident response Incident response Community Public bug bounty sized to the value at risk; clear security contact; published audits Exploit development The guarded launch Launch small on purpose: Caps : limit total deposits (for example, a low TVL ceiling) and raise it in steps as the system proves itself. Rate limits : cap how much can leave per hour or day, so a bug cannot drain everything at once. Kill switches with limits : pausing is fast; unpausing and upgrades go through the multisig and timelock. Bug bounty live before launch , so finders have a legal, paid path from day one. Staged rollout across chains : one chain first; each new chain or bridge is a new attack surface. Budgeting Security spend should scale with value at risk. A useful sanity check: if an attacker could take 10 million dollars, a 30,000 dollar maximum bounty tells them stealing pays far better than reporting. Many serious projects offer a percentage of funds at risk, with a high cap, for critical bugs. The readiness review Before launch day, the team signs off on a checklist. Anything missing either gets done or is explicitly accepted as a risk by named people. Your launch checklist Threat model written, reviewed and kept up to date. All admin powers documented publicly, with who holds them. Ownership transferred from the deployer to the multisig; deployer key retired. Timelock on upgrades and sensitive parameters. Unit, fuzz, invariant and symbolic tests passing in CI; coverage reviewed. Static analysis triaged; audits complete, published, and fixes reviewed. Bug bounty live, sized to the value at risk. Monitoring and paging in place for outflows, admin actions, oracles and DNS. Incident runbook rehearsed in a tabletop exercise; emergency contacts ready. Deposit caps and rate limits configured for the guarded launch. Registrar, DNS, CI and npm accounts protected with hardware keys. Legal review of the launch and any token (see the launch playbook ). Where to go from here Build the DeFi Security Hackathon project end to end using this checklist, then publish your threat model and report in its README. That single repository demonstrates more senior security skill than a pile of certificates.","track":"security"},{"id":"keys-basics","title":"How keys and accounts get stolen","level":"beginner","minutes":30,"summary":"Passwords, sessions, API keys, OAuth tokens, private keys and seed phrases: what each one unlocks, the eight ways they are stolen, and why launchpads are a favourite target.","html":"<h2 id=\"the-number-one-problem\">The number one problem</h2>\n<p>Most money lost in crypto is not lost to clever maths or exotic smart contract bugs. It is lost because someone got hold of a <strong>key</strong> or took over an <strong>account</strong>: a private key, a seed phrase, an admin login, a session cookie, an API key, a social media account. The same is true for ordinary apps: account takeover is one of the most common and costly attacks on the web.</p>\n<p>This track goes from &quot;what is a key&quot; to running production signing infrastructure and responding to a live compromise. Practise every lesson in the <a href=\"#critical-moment\">Critical Moment</a> simulator.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-you-are-protecting\">What you are protecting</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Secret</th><th>What it unlocks</th><th>Typical lifetime</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Password</strong></td><td>Logging in to an account</td><td>Until changed</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Session cookie / token</strong></td><td>Being logged in <em>right now</em>, without the password or MFA</td><td>Minutes to weeks</td></tr><tr><td><strong>API key</strong></td><td>A service (payments, RPC, AI, email) on your behalf, often with full power</td><td>Until revoked</td></tr><tr><td><strong>OAuth access / refresh token</strong></td><td>A third-party app acting on your account within its scopes</td><td>Hours (access), months (refresh)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Private key</strong></td><td>Signing transactions: total control of a wallet or contract role</td><td>Forever, no reset button</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Seed phrase</strong></td><td>Every private key derived from it</td><td>Forever</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Notice the last two: <strong>there is no &quot;forgot password&quot; for a private key.</strong> Anyone who has it can move the funds, and you cannot stop them.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-eight-ways-secrets-are-stolen\">The eight ways secrets are stolen</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Phishing</strong>: fake login pages, fake wallet pop-ups, fake support staff.</li><li><strong>Infostealer malware</strong>: a fake app, game mod, pirated tool or &quot;coding test&quot; that copies browser cookies, saved passwords, wallet files and <code>.env</code> files.</li><li><strong>Leaks in code</strong>: keys committed to GitHub, pasted in screenshots, logged to error trackers, bundled into front-end JavaScript.</li><li><strong>Password reuse</strong>: one leaked site's password tried everywhere (credential stuffing).</li><li><strong>SIM swaps</strong>: your phone number moved to an attacker, who then receives your SMS codes.</li><li><strong>Over-permissioned third parties</strong>: an OAuth app or API key with far more access than it needs gets compromised.</li><li><strong>Insiders and ex-team members</strong> whose access was never removed.</li><li><strong>Supply chain</strong>: a compromised library or tool that reads secrets from every machine it runs on.</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"why-launchpads-are-prime-targets\">Why launchpads are prime targets</h2>\n<p>A token launchpad (or any launch: presale, airdrop, NFT mint) concentrates every kind of key in one place, under time pressure:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Deployer and owner keys</strong> for the token and sale contracts.</li><li><strong>Presale and treasury wallets</strong> filling with buyers' funds.</li><li><strong>Whitelist or claim signing keys</strong> on a server that approves who can buy or claim.</li><li><strong>Admin panels</strong> for setting prices, dates and allocations.</li><li><strong>Social accounts</strong> (X, Discord, Telegram) that thousands of people trust for the official link.</li></ul>\n<p>Attackers know launches are rushed. A hijacked project X account posting a &quot;mint is live&quot; link to a drainer site, or a deployer key in a leaked <code>.env</code>, has ended many launches in minutes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"recognising-a-secret-in-the-wild\">Recognising a secret in the wild</h2>\n<p>Good engineers can spot secrets at a glance, which is the first step to keeping them out of code. A raw Ethereum private key is <code>0x</code> followed by 64 hex characters.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"the-golden-rules\">The golden rules</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Unique password for every account</strong>, from a password manager.</li><li><strong>Phishing-resistant MFA</strong> (passkeys or hardware keys) on email, GitHub, cloud, registrar, exchanges and social accounts.</li><li><strong>Never type a seed phrase into anything except a hardware wallet or a wallet you are restoring on purpose.</strong></li><li><strong>Least privilege everywhere</strong>: scoped API keys, minimal OAuth scopes, separate wallets for separate jobs.</li><li><strong>Assume any secret that touched an untrusted place is already compromised</strong>, and rotate it.</li></ul>","quiz":[{"q":"Which stolen secret lets an attacker skip both your password and your MFA?","options":["An old password","A valid session cookie or token","Your email address"],"answer":1,"explain":"A session token is proof you already logged in; whoever presents it is treated as you."},{"q":"What happens if your private key leaks?","options":["You reset it with your email","The blockchain freezes the funds","Anyone with it can move the funds, and there is no reset; move what you can and treat the key as burned"],"answer":2,"explain":"Private keys have no recovery process. Prevention is everything."},{"q":"Why are token launches especially risky for key theft?","options":["Many powerful keys and accounts are concentrated in one rushed event that thousands of people are watching","Launchpads use weaker cryptography","Tokens are easy to forge"],"answer":0,"explain":"Deployer keys, presale wallets, signing servers and social accounts all become targets at once."},{"q":"A popular app asks for &quot;full access to your GitHub account&quot; to show you a badge. What principle does that violate?","options":["Defence in depth","Least privilege","Availability"],"answer":1,"explain":"A badge needs read access to public data at most."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Spot a private key","file":"detect.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write a check that flags a string that looks like an Ethereum private key. Three lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const looksLikeKey = (value) =&gt; /^0x[0-9a-fA-F]{64}$/.test(value);</code></li><li><code>console.log(looksLikeKey('0x' + 'ab'.repeat(32)));</code></li><li><code>console.log(looksLikeKey('hello world'));</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const looksLikeKey = (value) => /^0x[0-9a-fA-F]{64}$/.test(value);\nconsole.log(looksLikeKey('0x' + 'ab'.repeat(32)));\nconsole.log(looksLikeKey('hello world'));","also":["const looksLikeKey = value => /^0x[0-9a-fA-F]{64}$/.test(value);\nconsole.log(looksLikeKey('0x' + 'ab'.repeat(32)));\nconsole.log(looksLikeKey('hello world'));"],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+looksLikeKey\\s*=\\s*\\(?\\s*value\\s*\\)?\\s*=>","text":"Line 1 starts <code>const looksLikeKey = (value) =&gt;</code>"},{"re":"/\\^0x\\[0-9a-fA-F\\]\\{64\\}\\$/\\.test\\(\\s*value\\s*\\)","text":"The pattern: <code>/^0x[0-9a-fA-F]{64}$/.test(value)</code>. <code>^</code> and <code>$</code> anchor the start and end; <code>{64}</code> means exactly 64 hex characters."},{"re":"looksLikeKey\\(\\s*['\"]0x['\"]\\s*\\+\\s*['\"]ab['\"]\\.repeat\\(\\s*32\\s*\\)\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2 builds a fake 64-character key: <code>'0x' + 'ab'.repeat(32)</code>"},{"re":"looksLikeKey\\(\\s*['\"]hello world['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3 tests a normal string: <code>looksLikeKey('hello world')</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Output: <code>true</code>, then <code>false</code>. Secret scanners (GitHub secret scanning, gitleaks) use thousands of patterns like this one. You will turn it into a pre-commit hook that blocks commits in the &quot;Secrets out of code&quot; lesson.</p>"}],"text":"The number one problem Most money lost in crypto is not lost to clever maths or exotic smart contract bugs. It is lost because someone got hold of a key or took over an account : a private key, a seed phrase, an admin login, a session cookie, an API key, a social media account. The same is true for ordinary apps: account takeover is one of the most common and costly attacks on the web. This track goes from what is a key to running production signing infrastructure and responding to a live compromise. Practise every lesson in the Critical Moment simulator. What you are protecting Secret What it unlocks Typical lifetime Password Logging in to an account Until changed Session cookie / token Being logged in right now , without the password or MFA Minutes to weeks API key A service (payments, RPC, AI, email) on your behalf, often with full power Until revoked OAuth access / refresh token A third-party app acting on your account within its scopes Hours (access), months (refresh) Private key Signing transactions: total control of a wallet or contract role Forever, no reset button Seed phrase Every private key derived from it Forever Notice the last two: there is no forgot password for a private key. Anyone who has it can move the funds, and you cannot stop them. The eight ways secrets are stolen Phishing : fake login pages, fake wallet pop-ups, fake support staff. Infostealer malware : a fake app, game mod, pirated tool or coding test that copies browser cookies, saved passwords, wallet files and .env files. Leaks in code : keys committed to GitHub, pasted in screenshots, logged to error trackers, bundled into front-end JavaScript. Password reuse : one leaked site's password tried everywhere (credential stuffing). SIM swaps : your phone number moved to an attacker, who then receives your SMS codes. Over-permissioned third parties : an OAuth app or API key with far more access than it needs gets compromised. Insiders and ex-team members whose access was never removed. Supply chain : a compromised library or tool that reads secrets from every machine it runs on. Why launchpads are prime targets A token launchpad (or any launch: presale, airdrop, NFT mint) concentrates every kind of key in one place, under time pressure: Deployer and owner keys for the token and sale contracts. Presale and treasury wallets filling with buyers' funds. Whitelist or claim signing keys on a server that approves who can buy or claim. Admin panels for setting prices, dates and allocations. Social accounts (X, Discord, Telegram) that thousands of people trust for the official link. Attackers know launches are rushed. A hijacked project X account posting a mint is live link to a drainer site, or a deployer key in a leaked .env , has ended many launches in minutes. Recognising a secret in the wild Good engineers can spot secrets at a glance, which is the first step to keeping them out of code. A raw Ethereum private key is 0x followed by 64 hex characters. The golden rules Unique password for every account , from a password manager. Phishing-resistant MFA (passkeys or hardware keys) on email, GitHub, cloud, registrar, exchanges and social accounts. Never type a seed phrase into anything except a hardware wallet or a wallet you are restoring on purpose. Least privilege everywhere : scoped API keys, minimal OAuth scopes, separate wallets for separate jobs. Assume any secret that touched an untrusted place is already compromised , and rotate it.","track":"keys"},{"id":"keys-wallet-setup","title":"Setting up wallets safely","level":"beginner","minutes":30,"summary":"Seed phrases, hardware wallets, passphrases and the four-wallet system that keeps a single mistake from costing you everything.","html":"<h2 id=\"one-wallet-is-a-single-point-of-failure\">One wallet is a single point of failure</h2>\n<p>If the wallet you use to try new dApps is the same wallet that holds your savings, one bad signature can take everything. Professionals separate wallets by job, the same way businesses separate a till from a safe.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-four-wallet-system\">The four-wallet system</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Wallet</th><th>Used for</th><th>Holds</th><th>Kind</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Vault</strong></td><td>Long-term holdings</td><td>Most of your value</td><td>Hardware wallet, rarely connected</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Daily</strong></td><td>Trusted apps you use often</td><td>A spending amount</td><td>Hardware wallet or well-kept browser wallet</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Burner</strong></td><td>New mints, airdrops, unknown sites</td><td>Only what one action needs</td><td>Browser wallet, disposable</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Dev</strong></td><td>Development, testnets, scripts</td><td>Testnet tokens only</td><td>Separate browser profile; never real funds</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Move funds <strong>from</strong> the vault, never connect the vault to random sites, and never let a dev key hold real money.</p>\n<h2 id=\"seed-phrases-the-master-key\">Seed phrases: the master key</h2>\n<p>When you create a wallet you get a 12 or 24-word <strong>seed phrase</strong>. Every account in that wallet is derived from it.</p>\n<ul><li>Write it on paper or stamp it on metal. <strong>Never</strong> a photo, cloud note, email, password manager entry for a vault, or text file.</li><li>Store it somewhere safe from fire and flood, and from other people.</li><li><strong>Test the backup</strong>: on a spare device or the hardware wallet's check feature, confirm the words restore the same first address <em>before</em> you send funds.</li><li>Consider an optional <strong>passphrase</strong> (sometimes called the 25th word): it creates a completely different set of accounts. Someone who finds the 24 words without the passphrase finds only a decoy wallet. Lose the passphrase and you lose the funds, so back it up separately.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"hardware-wallets\">Hardware wallets</h2>\n<p>A hardware wallet keeps the private key on a small device and signs <strong>inside</strong> it. Your computer only ever sees signatures. Even a fully infected laptop cannot extract the key; the worst it can do is trick you into approving a bad transaction, which is why you <strong>read the device's screen</strong> before every approval.</p>\n<p>Buy only from the manufacturer or an authorised reseller, never second-hand, and set it up yourself (a device that arrives with a seed phrase already written on a card is a scam).</p>\n<h2 id=\"development-wallets\">Development wallets</h2>\n<p>For development, generate throwaway keys in code. They never hold real funds.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"signing-safely-every-time\">Signing safely, every time</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Check the site</strong>: typed by you or from a bookmark, never from a DM, ad or reply.</li><li><strong>Read what you sign</strong>: an approval (<code>approve</code>, <code>setApprovalForAll</code>, <code>permit</code>) gives someone power over your tokens. A &quot;free claim&quot; never needs one.</li><li><strong>Simulate</strong>: wallets like Rabby, and transaction simulation features, show what will leave your wallet before you sign.</li><li><strong>Revoke</strong> old approvals regularly with a revoke tool or your wallet's approval manager.</li></ol>","quiz":[{"q":"Which wallet should you use to try a brand-new mint site?","options":["Your vault","Your dev wallet with real funds","A burner wallet holding only what that one action needs"],"answer":2,"explain":"If the site is malicious, the damage is limited to the burner."},{"q":"Your hardware wallet arrived with a card showing a pre-written seed phrase. What should you do?","options":["Use it; the manufacturer generated it for you","Do not use it: it is a scam device, and whoever wrote the phrase can take the funds","Add a passphrase and use it"],"answer":1,"explain":"Legitimate devices always generate a new seed during your own setup."},{"q":"Why can a hardware wallet protect you even on an infected computer?","options":["The private key never leaves the device; it signs internally and shows the real details on its own screen","It installs antivirus software","It encrypts your internet traffic"],"answer":0,"explain":"Malware can still try to trick you, so always read the device screen."},{"q":"What does testing your seed backup mean?","options":["Taking a photo of it","Confirming the written words restore the same first address before you put funds in","Reading it aloud"],"answer":1,"explain":"An untested backup is a guess."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Generate a development wallet","file":"dev-wallet.ts","lang":"ts","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Using viem, generate a throwaway mnemonic and derive its first account. Three lines (the imports are already in place):</p>\n<ol><li><code>const mnemonic = generateMnemonic(english);</code></li><li><code>const account = mnemonicToAccount(mnemonic);</code></li><li><code>console.log('Dev address:', account.address);</code></li></ol>","starter":"import { english, generateMnemonic, mnemonicToAccount } from 'viem/accounts';\n// Testnet only. Never send real funds to an address created in code.","html":"","solution":"import { english, generateMnemonic, mnemonicToAccount } from 'viem/accounts';\nconst mnemonic = generateMnemonic(english);\nconst account = mnemonicToAccount(mnemonic);\nconsole.log('Dev address:', account.address);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+mnemonic\\s*=\\s*generateMnemonic\\(\\s*english\\s*\\)","text":"Line 1: <code>const mnemonic = generateMnemonic(english);</code>"},{"re":"const\\s+account\\s*=\\s*mnemonicToAccount\\(\\s*mnemonic\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>const account = mnemonicToAccount(mnemonic);</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*['\"]Dev address:['\"]\\s*,\\s*account\\.address\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3: <code>console.log('Dev address:', account.address);</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Keep the import line from the starter. Notice what the code does not do: it never prints the mnemonic. Logging secrets, even in development, is how they end up in terminal history, CI logs and screenshots.</p>"}],"text":"One wallet is a single point of failure If the wallet you use to try new dApps is the same wallet that holds your savings, one bad signature can take everything. Professionals separate wallets by job, the same way businesses separate a till from a safe. The four-wallet system Wallet Used for Holds Kind Vault Long-term holdings Most of your value Hardware wallet, rarely connected Daily Trusted apps you use often A spending amount Hardware wallet or well-kept browser wallet Burner New mints, airdrops, unknown sites Only what one action needs Browser wallet, disposable Dev Development, testnets, scripts Testnet tokens only Separate browser profile; never real funds Move funds from the vault, never connect the vault to random sites, and never let a dev key hold real money. Seed phrases: the master key When you create a wallet you get a 12 or 24-word seed phrase . Every account in that wallet is derived from it. Write it on paper or stamp it on metal. Never a photo, cloud note, email, password manager entry for a vault, or text file. Store it somewhere safe from fire and flood, and from other people. Test the backup : on a spare device or the hardware wallet's check feature, confirm the words restore the same first address before you send funds. Consider an optional passphrase (sometimes called the 25th word): it creates a completely different set of accounts. Someone who finds the 24 words without the passphrase finds only a decoy wallet. Lose the passphrase and you lose the funds, so back it up separately. Hardware wallets A hardware wallet keeps the private key on a small device and signs inside it. Your computer only ever sees signatures. Even a fully infected laptop cannot extract the key; the worst it can do is trick you into approving a bad transaction, which is why you read the device's screen before every approval. Buy only from the manufacturer or an authorised reseller, never second-hand, and set it up yourself (a device that arrives with a seed phrase already written on a card is a scam). Development wallets For development, generate throwaway keys in code. They never hold real funds. Signing safely, every time Check the site : typed by you or from a bookmark, never from a DM, ad or reply. Read what you sign : an approval ( approve , setApprovalForAll , permit ) gives someone power over your tokens. A free claim never needs one. Simulate : wallets like Rabby, and transaction simulation features, show what will leave your wallet before you sign. Revoke old approvals regularly with a revoke tool or your wallet's approval manager.","track":"keys"},{"id":"keys-sessions","title":"Sessions, cookies and tokens: what attackers really steal","level":"beginner","minutes":30,"summary":"Why stolen session cookies bypass MFA, how infostealers and adversary-in-the-middle phishing work, and how to set cookies and tokens up safely.","html":"<h2 id=\"logging-in-creates-a-new-secret\">Logging in creates a new secret</h2>\n<p>When you log in, the server checks your password and MFA <strong>once</strong>, then gives your browser a <strong>session</strong>: usually a cookie holding a long random ID, or a token like a JWT. Every later request carries it. To the server, whoever presents that session <strong>is you</strong>.</p>\n<p>That is why attackers increasingly skip passwords entirely and steal sessions:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Infostealer malware</strong> copies cookies straight out of the browser profile.</li><li><strong>Adversary-in-the-middle (AitM) phishing</strong> kits sit between you and the real site: you log in through their proxy, complete your real MFA, and they keep the session cookie the real site issues.</li><li><strong>XSS</strong> on a site can read any cookie that is not <code>HttpOnly</code>.</li><li><strong>Network interception</strong> captures sessions sent over plain HTTP.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"cookie-flags-that-matter\">Cookie flags that matter</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Flag</th><th>Protects against</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>HttpOnly</code></td><td>JavaScript (and therefore XSS) reading the cookie</td></tr><tr><td><code>Secure</code></td><td>Sending the cookie over unencrypted HTTP</td></tr><tr><td><code>SameSite=Lax</code> or <code>Strict</code></td><td>Other sites making your browser send it (CSRF)</td></tr><tr><td>A short <code>Max-Age</code> plus an idle timeout</td><td>Long-lived stolen sessions</td></tr><tr><td>The <code>__Host-</code> name prefix</td><td>Subdomains setting or overriding it</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"server-side-defences\">Server-side defences</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Rotate the session ID</strong> at login and whenever privileges change, so a session planted before login is useless.</li><li><strong>Revoke every session</strong> when the password changes, MFA changes, or the user clicks &quot;sign out everywhere&quot;.</li><li><strong>Re-authenticate</strong> (step-up) before sensitive actions: changing email, adding a withdrawal address, creating API keys, exporting keys.</li><li><strong>Watch for anomalies</strong>: the same session suddenly used from a new country, device or network is a strong signal of theft.</li><li>Keep tokens <strong>out of <code>localStorage</code></strong> for high-value apps: any XSS can read it. An <code>HttpOnly</code> cookie cannot be read by scripts.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"jwts-are-signed-not-secret\">JWTs are signed, not secret</h2>\n<p>A JWT's payload is only Base64URL-encoded: anyone holding the token can read it (try the JWT challenge in the <a href=\"#hackathons\">Ladder CTF</a>). Never put secrets in a JWT, always verify the signature and expiry on the server, and reject <code>alg: none</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>","quiz":[{"q":"Why can a stolen session cookie bypass MFA?","options":["MFA only protects passwords on Tuesdays","MFA is checked at login; the session cookie proves login already happened","Cookies contain the MFA code"],"answer":1,"explain":"Protect sessions as carefully as passwords."},{"q":"Which flag stops JavaScript on the page from reading a cookie?","options":["HttpOnly","Secure","SameSite"],"answer":0,"explain":"HttpOnly blocks document.cookie access, so XSS cannot steal it."},{"q":"A user changes their password after a suspected phish. What must the server also do?","options":["Nothing more","Revoke all existing sessions and tokens, so a stolen session stops working","Email them the new password"],"answer":1,"explain":"A password change alone leaves stolen sessions valid on many systems."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Issue a secure session cookie","file":"session.js","lang":"js","run":false,"prompt":"<p>In an Express app, set the session cookie with every protective flag. Write it in this shape (one option per line):</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">res.cookie('__Host-session', sessionId, {\n  httpOnly: true,\n  secure: true,\n  sameSite: 'lax',\n  path: '/',\n  maxAge: 1000 * 60 * 60 * 8,\n});</code></pre></div>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"res.cookie('__Host-session', sessionId, {\n  httpOnly: true,\n  secure: true,\n  sameSite: 'lax',\n  path: '/',\n  maxAge: 1000 * 60 * 60 * 8,\n});","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"res\\.cookie\\(\\s*['\"]__Host-session['\"]\\s*,\\s*sessionId\\s*,\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>res.cookie('__Host-session', sessionId, {</code> (two underscores before Host)."},{"re":"httpOnly\\s*:\\s*true\\s*,","text":"<code>httpOnly: true,</code> (capital O)."},{"re":"secure\\s*:\\s*true\\s*,","text":"<code>secure: true,</code>"},{"re":"sameSite\\s*:\\s*['\"]lax['\"]\\s*,","text":"<code>sameSite: 'lax',</code> (capital S in the middle)."},{"re":"path\\s*:\\s*['\"]/['\"]\\s*,","text":"<code>path: '/',</code> (the <code>__Host-</code> prefix requires path <code>/</code> and secure)."},{"re":"maxAge\\s*:\\s*1000\\s*\\*\\s*60\\s*\\*\\s*60\\s*\\*\\s*8\\s*,","text":"<code>maxAge: 1000 * 60 * 60 * 8,</code> (8 hours, in milliseconds)."},{"re":"^\\s*\\}\\s*\\)\\s*;?\\s*$","text":"Close the options object and the call: <code>});</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"Read a JWT's payload","file":"jwt.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Decode the middle part of a JWT. Three lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const token = 'eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9.eyJzdWIiOiJhZGEiLCJyb2xlIjoiYWRtaW4ifQ.sig';</code></li><li><code>const payload = JSON.parse(atob(token.split('.')[1]));</code></li><li><code>console.log(payload.role);</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const token = 'eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9.eyJzdWIiOiJhZGEiLCJyb2xlIjoiYWRtaW4ifQ.sig';\nconst payload = JSON.parse(atob(token.split('.')[1]));\nconsole.log(payload.role);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+token\\s*=\\s*['\"]eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9\\.eyJzdWIiOiJhZGEiLCJyb2xlIjoiYWRtaW4ifQ\\.sig['\"]","text":"Line 1: copy the token exactly, three parts separated by dots."},{"re":"token\\.split\\(\\s*['\"]\\.['\"]\\s*\\)\\[\\s*1\\s*\\]","text":"Take the middle part: <code>token.split('.')[1]</code>"},{"re":"JSON\\.parse\\(\\s*atob\\(","text":"Decode it with <code>atob(...)</code> and parse the JSON: <code>JSON.parse(atob(...))</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*payload\\.role\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3: <code>console.log(payload.role);</code>"}],"explain":"<p>It prints <code>admin</code>. Anyone can read a JWT. The security comes from the signature, which the server must check, so an attacker who edits <code>role</code> to <code>admin</code> cannot produce a valid token.</p>"}],"text":"Logging in creates a new secret When you log in, the server checks your password and MFA once , then gives your browser a session : usually a cookie holding a long random ID, or a token like a JWT. Every later request carries it. To the server, whoever presents that session is you . That is why attackers increasingly skip passwords entirely and steal sessions: Infostealer malware copies cookies straight out of the browser profile. Adversary-in-the-middle (AitM) phishing kits sit between you and the real site: you log in through their proxy, complete your real MFA, and they keep the session cookie the real site issues. XSS on a site can read any cookie that is not HttpOnly . Network interception captures sessions sent over plain HTTP. Cookie flags that matter Flag Protects against HttpOnly JavaScript (and therefore XSS) reading the cookie Secure Sending the cookie over unencrypted HTTP SameSite=Lax or Strict Other sites making your browser send it (CSRF) A short Max-Age plus an idle timeout Long-lived stolen sessions The __Host- name prefix Subdomains setting or overriding it Server-side defences Rotate the session ID at login and whenever privileges change, so a session planted before login is useless. Revoke every session when the password changes, MFA changes, or the user clicks sign out everywhere . Re-authenticate (step-up) before sensitive actions: changing email, adding a withdrawal address, creating API keys, exporting keys. Watch for anomalies : the same session suddenly used from a new country, device or network is a strong signal of theft. Keep tokens out of localStorage for high-value apps: any XSS can read it. An HttpOnly cookie cannot be read by scripts. JWTs are signed, not secret A JWT's payload is only Base64URL-encoded: anyone holding the token can read it (try the JWT challenge in the Ladder CTF ). Never put secrets in a JWT, always verify the signature and expiry on the server, and reject alg: none .","track":"keys"},{"id":"keys-secrets-in-code","title":"Keeping secrets out of code","level":"intermediate","minutes":35,"summary":"Why a key pushed to GitHub is stolen in seconds, and the layered scripts that stop it, from .gitignore to pre-commit hooks, push protection and secret managers.","html":"<h2 id=\"seconds-not-days\">Seconds, not days</h2>\n<p>Bots watch every public push to GitHub and npm. A private key or cloud API key that lands in a public repository is usually found and abused within <strong>minutes</strong>, sometimes seconds. Wallets with exposed keys are emptied by automated <strong>sweeper bots</strong> that take any ETH the moment it arrives. Deleting the file in a later commit does not help: the key is in the history, and the bots already have it.</p>\n<p>So the rule is absolute: <strong>a secret that was ever pushed is compromised. Rotate it.</strong></p>\n<h2 id=\"layer-1-never-put-it-in-a-file-git-sees\">Layer 1: never put it in a file Git sees</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\"># .gitignore\n.env\n.env.*\n!.env.example\n*.pem\n*.key\nkeystore/</code></pre></div>\n<p>Commit a <code>.env.example</code> with variable names and fake values; keep the real <code>.env</code> local.</p>\n<h2 id=\"layer-2-a-pre-commit-hook-that-blocks-secrets\">Layer 2: a pre-commit hook that blocks secrets</h2>\n<p>A Git hook is a script Git runs automatically. A <strong>pre-commit</strong> hook runs before every commit and can refuse it. This one looks for anything shaped like a private key in the staged changes.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"layer-3-the-platform\">Layer 3: the platform</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>GitHub push protection</strong> blocks pushes containing known secret formats; <strong>secret scanning</strong> alerts on anything already in history. Turn both on for every repository and organisation.</li><li><strong>CI checks</strong>: run gitleaks on every pull request so nothing slips past a teammate's missing hook.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"layer-4-do-not-keep-secrets-in-files-at-all\">Layer 4: do not keep secrets in files at all</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Instead of a plain <code>.env</code></th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Developer API keys</td><td>A password manager's CLI to inject them at run time (for example <code>op run -- npm start</code> with 1Password)</td></tr><tr><td>Production app secrets</td><td>Your host's secret store or a cloud secrets manager, with access logs</td></tr><tr><td>Deployment private keys</td><td>Foundry's encrypted keystore (<code>cast wallet import</code>) or, better, a hardware wallet</td></tr><tr><td>Production signing keys</td><td>A KMS, HSM or MPC signer (the expert lessons)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"layer-5-limit-the-blast-radius\">Layer 5: limit the blast radius</h2>\n<p>Even well-kept secrets leak eventually, so make each one worth as little as possible:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Scoped keys</strong>: read-only where possible, restricted to specific domains, IPs or contracts.</li><li><strong>Separate keys per environment</strong>: staging keys cannot touch production.</li><li><strong>Spending and rate limits</strong> on paid APIs and hot wallets.</li><li><strong>Short lifetimes and rotation</strong> for everything that allows it.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"when-a-secret-leaks-the-five-minute-checklist\">When a secret leaks: the five-minute checklist</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Revoke or rotate it now</strong>, before investigating. Investigation can wait; attackers do not.</li><li>For a private key with funds: move remaining assets to a fresh, safe wallet (see the recovery lesson for sweeper bots).</li><li>Check logs for use of the leaked secret.</li><li>Remove it from history (<code>git filter-repo</code>) to stop new copies, while still treating it as compromised.</li><li>Add the check that would have caught it.</li></ol>","quiz":[{"q":"A teammate pushed an API key and deleted it in the next commit five minutes later. Is the key safe?","options":["Yes, it is deleted","Only if the repository is private","No: it is in the Git history and may already have been scraped, so revoke it and issue a new one"],"answer":2,"explain":"Treat any pushed secret as compromised."},{"q":"What does a pre-commit hook do when it exits with code 1?","options":["Git cancels the commit","Git commits but warns","Git deletes the file"],"answer":0,"explain":"Non-zero exit means &quot;stop&quot;."},{"q":"Why are local hooks not enough on their own?","options":["They are too slow","They live only on each developer's machine and can be skipped or missing, so CI and push protection add independent layers","Git does not run them"],"answer":1,"explain":"Defence in depth: assume any one layer can fail."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Write a pre-commit hook that blocks private keys","file":".git/hooks/pre-commit","lang":"bash","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write the hook script. Five lines:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">#!/bin/sh\nif git diff --cached | grep -Eq '0x[0-9a-fA-F]{64}'; then\n  echo &quot;Blocked: this commit contains what looks like a private key.&quot;\n  exit 1\nfi</code></pre></div>\n<p>Then make it executable with <code>chmod +x .git/hooks/pre-commit</code>. (Tools like gitleaks and the <code>pre-commit</code> framework do the same job with hundreds of patterns.)</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"#!/bin/sh\nif git diff --cached | grep -Eq '0x[0-9a-fA-F]{64}'; then\n  echo \"Blocked: this commit contains what looks like a private key.\"\n  exit 1\nfi","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^#!/bin/sh\\s*$","text":"Line 1 is the &quot;shebang&quot; that says which shell runs the script: <code>#!/bin/sh</code>"},{"re":"if\\s+git\\s+diff\\s+--cached\\s*\\|","text":"Line 2 starts <code>if git diff --cached |</code> (only what is staged for this commit)."},{"re":"grep\\s+-Eq\\s+['\"]0x\\[0-9a-fA-F\\]\\{64\\}['\"]\\s*;\\s*then","text":"then <code>grep -Eq '0x[0-9a-fA-F]{64}'; then</code> (<code>-E</code> extended regex, <code>-q</code> quiet)."},{"re":"echo\\s+[\"']Blocked: this commit contains what looks like a private key\\.[\"']","text":"Line 3 prints the reason."},{"re":"^\\s*exit\\s+1\\s*$","text":"Line 4: <code>exit 1</code>. Any non-zero exit makes Git cancel the commit."},{"re":"^\\s*fi\\s*$","text":"Line 5 closes the <code>if</code>: <code>fi</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Note: the first line looks like a comment to this checker, so it is not compared, but your real file must start with it. Hooks live only on your machine and are not shared through Git, which is why teams also run the same checks in CI and enable GitHub push protection: defence in depth.</p>"}],"text":"Seconds, not days Bots watch every public push to GitHub and npm. A private key or cloud API key that lands in a public repository is usually found and abused within minutes , sometimes seconds. Wallets with exposed keys are emptied by automated sweeper bots that take any ETH the moment it arrives. Deleting the file in a later commit does not help: the key is in the history, and the bots already have it. So the rule is absolute: a secret that was ever pushed is compromised. Rotate it. Layer 1: never put it in a file Git sees # .gitignore .env .env.* !.env.example *.pem *.key keystore/ Commit a .env.example with variable names and fake values; keep the real .env local. Layer 2: a pre-commit hook that blocks secrets A Git hook is a script Git runs automatically. A pre-commit hook runs before every commit and can refuse it. This one looks for anything shaped like a private key in the staged changes. Layer 3: the platform GitHub push protection blocks pushes containing known secret formats; secret scanning alerts on anything already in history. Turn both on for every repository and organisation. CI checks : run gitleaks on every pull request so nothing slips past a teammate's missing hook. Layer 4: do not keep secrets in files at all Instead of a plain .env Use Developer API keys A password manager's CLI to inject them at run time (for example op run -- npm start with 1Password) Production app secrets Your host's secret store or a cloud secrets manager, with access logs Deployment private keys Foundry's encrypted keystore ( cast wallet import ) or, better, a hardware wallet Production signing keys A KMS, HSM or MPC signer (the expert lessons) Layer 5: limit the blast radius Even well-kept secrets leak eventually, so make each one worth as little as possible: Scoped keys : read-only where possible, restricted to specific domains, IPs or contracts. Separate keys per environment : staging keys cannot touch production. Spending and rate limits on paid APIs and hot wallets. Short lifetimes and rotation for everything that allows it. When a secret leaks: the five-minute checklist Revoke or rotate it now , before investigating. Investigation can wait; attackers do not. For a private key with funds: move remaining assets to a fresh, safe wallet (see the recovery lesson for sweeper bots). Check logs for use of the leaked secret. Remove it from history ( git filter-repo ) to stop new copies, while still treating it as compromised. Add the check that would have caught it.","track":"keys"},{"id":"keys-oauth","title":"Third-party APIs and OAuth done safely","level":"intermediate","minutes":45,"summary":"How OAuth 2.0 and \"Sign in with...\" really work, the attacks against them (consent phishing, stolen tokens, CSRF, open redirects), and the code that defends your app, including PKCE and state.","html":"<h2 id=\"why-oauth-exists\">Why OAuth exists</h2>\n<p>You want an app to post to your X account, read your GitHub repos, or access your Google Drive, <strong>without giving it your password</strong>. OAuth 2.0 lets you grant a limited, revocable permission instead. &quot;Sign in with Google/GitHub/X&quot; is built on the same flow, with OpenID Connect (OIDC) adding an identity token.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-four-roles\">The four roles</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Resource owner</strong></td><td>You</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Client</strong></td><td>The third-party app asking for access</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Authorisation server</strong></td><td>GitHub's or Google's login and consent screens</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Resource server</strong></td><td>The API holding your data</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"the-authorisation-code-flow-with-pkce\">The authorisation code flow with PKCE</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">1. App creates a random code_verifier and its SHA-256 code_challenge, plus a random state.\n2. App sends you to the provider:  /authorize?client_id=..&amp;redirect_uri=..&amp;scope=..&amp;state=..&amp;code_challenge=..\n3. You log in to the PROVIDER (not the app) and approve the listed scopes.\n4. Provider redirects back:        https://app.example/callback?code=..&amp;state=..\n5. App checks state matches, then exchanges code + code_verifier for tokens (server to server).\n6. App calls the API with the access token; uses the refresh token to get new ones later.</code></pre></div>\n<ul><li><strong>state</strong> ties the callback to the login <em>this</em> browser started, blocking CSRF login attacks.</li><li><strong>PKCE</strong> (Proof Key for Code Exchange) means a stolen authorisation <code>code</code> is useless without the <code>code_verifier</code>, which never left the app. It is required for mobile and single-page apps, and recommended for every client.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"checking-the-callback\">Checking the callback</h2>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"attacks-on-oauth-and-the-defences\">Attacks on OAuth, and the defences</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Attack</th><th>How it works</th><th>Defence</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Consent phishing</strong></td><td>A malicious app with a convincing name asks you to grant broad scopes (&quot;CodeReview Pro wants repo, workflow, admin:org&quot;). No password is stolen; you hand over a token.</td><td>Read the scopes; organisations restrict which third-party apps members can authorise; review authorised apps regularly</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Token theft</strong></td><td>Access or refresh tokens leak from logs, <code>localStorage</code>, a breached vendor or a compromised integration</td><td>Store tokens server-side and encrypted; short-lived access tokens; <strong>refresh token rotation</strong>; revoke on suspicion</td></tr><tr><td><strong>CSRF login / code injection</strong></td><td>An attacker makes your browser complete <em>their</em> OAuth flow</td><td><code>state</code> (and PKCE) checked on callback</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Open redirect / redirect URI tricks</strong></td><td>Provider sends the code to an attacker-controlled URL</td><td>Register exact redirect URIs, never wildcards; validate exactly</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Over-scoped tokens</strong></td><td>A harmless feature holds god-mode access</td><td>Request the minimum scopes; incremental consent</td></tr><tr><td><strong>ID token misuse (OIDC)</strong></td><td>Accepting a token meant for another app, expired or unsigned</td><td>Verify signature, <code>iss</code>, <code>aud</code> (your client ID), <code>exp</code> and <code>nonce</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Changing your password does not revoke OAuth apps</p><p>If you granted a malicious app access, changing your password leaves its token working on many platforms. You must <strong>revoke the app's authorisation</strong> (on GitHub: Settings, Applications, Authorised OAuth Apps), then rotate anything it could reach. The <a href=\"#critical-moment\">Critical Moment</a> simulator has a scenario built on exactly this.</p></aside>\n<h2 id=\"using-third-party-apis-from-your-own-code\">Using third-party APIs from your own code</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Keys belong on the server.</strong> If the browser needs data, your server calls the API and returns only what is needed.</li><li><strong>Prefer short-lived, scoped credentials</strong>: GitHub Apps with fine-grained permissions over classic personal access tokens; OIDC federation from CI to cloud instead of long-lived cloud keys.</li><li><strong>Verify webhooks</strong> (HMAC signatures, see the applied cryptography lesson) before trusting them.</li><li><strong>Assume vendors get breached</strong>: one integration's leaked token should not unlock everything.</li></ul>","quiz":[{"q":"What does PKCE protect against?","options":["Weak passwords","An attacker who steals the authorisation code exchanging it for tokens","Phishing emails"],"answer":1,"explain":"Without the code_verifier, the intercepted code is useless."},{"q":"What is the state parameter for?","options":["Tying the callback to the login this browser started, blocking CSRF","Storing the user's country","Encrypting the access token"],"answer":0,"explain":"Generate it randomly, save it in the session, and compare on return."},{"q":"You authorised a fake &quot;CodeReview Pro&quot; GitHub app. What actually stops it?","options":["Changing your GitHub password","Revoking the app's authorisation, then rotating secrets it could have reached","Logging out of GitHub"],"answer":1,"explain":"The app holds its own token; only revocation kills it."},{"q":"Where should your app store users' OAuth refresh tokens?","options":["In localStorage","In a cookie readable by JavaScript","Server-side, encrypted at rest, with rotation and revocation"],"answer":2,"explain":"Refresh tokens are long-lived keys to the user's account on another service."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Generate a PKCE verifier and challenge","file":"pkce.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Create a PKCE pair with the Web Crypto API. Five lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const b64url = (bytes) =&gt; btoa(String.fromCharCode(...bytes)).replace(/\\+/g, '-').replace(/\\//g, '_').replace(/=+$/, '');</code></li><li><code>const verifier = b64url(crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(32)));</code></li><li><code>const digest = await crypto.subtle.digest('SHA-256', new TextEncoder().encode(verifier));</code></li><li><code>const challenge = b64url(new Uint8Array(digest));</code></li><li><code>console.log({ verifier, challenge });</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const b64url = (bytes) => btoa(String.fromCharCode(...bytes)).replace(/\\+/g, '-').replace(/\\//g, '_').replace(/=+$/, '');\nconst verifier = b64url(crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(32)));\nconst digest = await crypto.subtle.digest('SHA-256', new TextEncoder().encode(verifier));\nconst challenge = b64url(new Uint8Array(digest));\nconsole.log({ verifier, challenge });","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+b64url\\s*=\\s*\\(\\s*bytes\\s*\\)\\s*=>\\s*btoa\\(\\s*String\\.fromCharCode\\(\\s*\\.\\.\\.bytes\\s*\\)\\s*\\)","text":"Line 1 starts <code>const b64url = (bytes) =&gt; btoa(String.fromCharCode(...bytes))</code>"},{"re":"\\.replace\\(\\s*/\\\\\\+/g\\s*,\\s*['\"]-['\"]\\s*\\)\\.replace\\(\\s*/\\\\//g\\s*,\\s*['\"]_['\"]\\s*\\)\\.replace\\(\\s*/=\\+\\$/\\s*,\\s*['\"]['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"then turn Base64 into Base64URL: <code>.replace(/\\+/g, '-').replace(/\\//g, '_').replace(/=+$/, '')</code>"},{"re":"const\\s+verifier\\s*=\\s*b64url\\(\\s*crypto\\.getRandomValues\\(\\s*new\\s+Uint8Array\\(\\s*32\\s*\\)\\s*\\)\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: 32 random bytes: <code>b64url(crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(32)))</code>"},{"re":"crypto\\.subtle\\.digest\\(\\s*['\"]SHA-256['\"]\\s*,\\s*new\\s+TextEncoder\\(\\s*\\)\\.encode\\(\\s*verifier\\s*\\)\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3 hashes the verifier: <code>await crypto.subtle.digest('SHA-256', new TextEncoder().encode(verifier))</code>"},{"re":"const\\s+challenge\\s*=\\s*b64url\\(\\s*new\\s+Uint8Array\\(\\s*digest\\s*\\)\\s*\\)","text":"Line 4: <code>const challenge = b64url(new Uint8Array(digest));</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*\\{\\s*verifier\\s*,\\s*challenge\\s*\\}\\s*\\)","text":"Line 5: <code>console.log({ verifier, challenge });</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Send <code>challenge</code> (with <code>code_challenge_method=S256</code>) in step 2; keep <code>verifier</code> private and send it only in the server-side token exchange of step 5. An attacker who intercepts the code cannot reverse the hash to get the verifier.</p>"},{"title":"Reject a forged callback","file":"callback.js","lang":"js","run":false,"prompt":"<p>In your callback handler, refuse any request whose <code>state</code> does not match the one saved in the user's session. Three lines:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">if (url.searchParams.get('state') !== session.oauthState) {\n  throw new Error('OAuth state mismatch: possible CSRF');\n}</code></pre></div>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"if (url.searchParams.get('state') !== session.oauthState) {\n  throw new Error('OAuth state mismatch: possible CSRF');\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"if\\s*\\(\\s*url\\.searchParams\\.get\\(\\s*['\"]state['\"]\\s*\\)\\s*!==\\s*session\\.oauthState\\s*\\)\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>if (url.searchParams.get('state') !== session.oauthState) {</code>"},{"re":"throw\\s+new\\s+Error\\(\\s*['\"]OAuth state mismatch: possible CSRF['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>throw new Error('OAuth state mismatch: possible CSRF');</code>"}],"explain":""}],"text":"Why OAuth exists You want an app to post to your X account, read your GitHub repos, or access your Google Drive, without giving it your password . OAuth 2.0 lets you grant a limited, revocable permission instead. Sign in with Google/GitHub/X is built on the same flow, with OpenID Connect (OIDC) adding an identity token. The four roles Role Example Resource owner You Client The third-party app asking for access Authorisation server GitHub's or Google's login and consent screens Resource server The API holding your data The authorisation code flow with PKCE 1. App creates a random code_verifier and its SHA-256 code_challenge, plus a random state. 2. App sends you to the provider: /authorize?client_id=.. redirect_uri=.. scope=.. state=.. code_challenge=.. 3. You log in to the PROVIDER (not the app) and approve the listed scopes. 4. Provider redirects back: https://app.example/callback?code=.. state=.. 5. App checks state matches, then exchanges code + code_verifier for tokens (server to server). 6. App calls the API with the access token; uses the refresh token to get new ones later. state ties the callback to the login this browser started, blocking CSRF login attacks. PKCE (Proof Key for Code Exchange) means a stolen authorisation code is useless without the code_verifier , which never left the app. It is required for mobile and single-page apps, and recommended for every client. Checking the callback Attacks on OAuth, and the defences Attack How it works Defence Consent phishing A malicious app with a convincing name asks you to grant broad scopes ( CodeReview Pro wants repo, workflow, admin:org ). No password is stolen; you hand over a token. Read the scopes; organisations restrict which third-party apps members can authorise; review authorised apps regularly Token theft Access or refresh tokens leak from logs, localStorage , a breached vendor or a compromised integration Store tokens server-side and encrypted; short-lived access tokens; refresh token rotation ; revoke on suspicion CSRF login / code injection An attacker makes your browser complete their OAuth flow state (and PKCE) checked on callback Open redirect / redirect URI tricks Provider sends the code to an attacker-controlled URL Register exact redirect URIs, never wildcards; validate exactly Over-scoped tokens A harmless feature holds god-mode access Request the minimum scopes; incremental consent ID token misuse (OIDC) Accepting a token meant for another app, expired or unsigned Verify signature, iss , aud (your client ID), exp and nonce Changing your password does not revoke OAuth apps If you granted a malicious app access, changing your password leaves its token working on many platforms. You must revoke the app's authorisation (on GitHub: Settings, Applications, Authorised OAuth Apps), then rotate anything it could reach. The Critical Moment simulator has a scenario built on exactly this. Using third-party APIs from your own code Keys belong on the server. If the browser needs data, your server calls the API and returns only what is needed. Prefer short-lived, scoped credentials : GitHub Apps with fine-grained permissions over classic personal access tokens; OIDC federation from CI to cloud instead of long-lived cloud keys. Verify webhooks (HMAC signatures, see the applied cryptography lesson) before trusting them. Assume vendors get breached : one integration's leaked token should not unlock everything.","track":"keys"},{"id":"keys-ato-defence","title":"Stopping account takeover in your own app","level":"intermediate","minutes":40,"summary":"The defences and scripts that stop credential stuffing, brute force, SIM swaps and session theft in an app or exchange-style platform you build.","html":"<h2 id=\"your-users-will-be-attacked\">Your users will be attacked</h2>\n<p>If your app has logins, it will face <strong>account takeover (ATO)</strong> attempts: bots trying leaked username and password lists, phishing kits copying your login page, SIM swappers and support-desk social engineers. For an app that holds money or wallets (a launchpad, an exchange, a dashboard with withdrawals), ATO is the attack that matters most.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-defence-stack\">The defence stack</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Stops</th><th>How</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Rate limiting</strong></td><td>Brute force and stuffing</td><td>Limit attempts per account, per IP and globally; slow down, then challenge</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Breached-password checks</strong></td><td>Reused leaked passwords</td><td>Reject passwords found in known breaches at sign-up and change</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Phishing-resistant MFA</strong></td><td>Stolen passwords and most phishing</td><td>Offer passkeys (WebAuthn); authenticator apps as a fallback; avoid SMS for high-value accounts</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Session hygiene</strong></td><td>Stolen sessions</td><td>Rotation, short lifetimes, revoke on password or MFA change</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Step-up authentication</strong></td><td>A stolen session doing damage</td><td>Re-check MFA before withdrawals, address changes, API key creation</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Withdrawal protections</strong></td><td>Fast cash-out after takeover</td><td>Address allow-lists, a 24 to 48 hour delay on newly added addresses, email confirmation and alerts</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Anomaly alerts</strong></td><td>Takeovers in progress</td><td>New device, location or IP; impossible travel; many failed logins</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Recovery flow security</strong></td><td>Social engineering the support team</td><td>No password resets over chat; strong identity checks; a cooling-off delay</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"script-1-a-login-rate-limiter\">Script 1: a login rate limiter</h2>\n<p>A minimal in-memory limiter shows the idea. Production systems keep the counters in Redis or a database and expire them over time.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"script-2-checking-breached-passwords-privately\">Script 2: checking breached passwords privately</h2>\n<p>The Have I Been Pwned &quot;Pwned Passwords&quot; service lets you check whether a password appears in known breaches <strong>without sending it</strong>. You send only the first 5 characters of its SHA-1 hash; the service returns every breached hash with that prefix, and you look for the rest locally. This technique is called <strong>k-anonymity</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">async function isBreached(password) {\n  const bytes = await crypto.subtle.digest('SHA-1', new TextEncoder().encode(password));\n  const hash = [...new Uint8Array(bytes)].map((b) =&gt; b.toString(16).padStart(2, '0')).join('').toUpperCase();\n  const res = await fetch(`https://api.pwnedpasswords.com/range/${hash.slice(0, 5)}`);\n  return (await res.text()).includes(hash.slice(5));\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>(SHA-1 is fine here because it is only an index into a public list, not how you store passwords. Store passwords with Argon2id.)</p>\n<h2 id=\"passkeys-the-strongest-login\">Passkeys: the strongest login</h2>\n<p><strong>Passkeys</strong> (WebAuthn / FIDO2) replace passwords with a key pair: the private key stays in the user's device or password manager, and the browser only signs challenges for the <strong>real</strong> domain. A phishing site gets nothing usable. Libraries such as SimpleWebAuthn make adding them to a Node app a day's work, and they are the single biggest ATO reduction you can ship.</p>\n<h2 id=\"withdrawal-protections-for-apps-that-hold-funds\">Withdrawal protections (for apps that hold funds)</h2>\n<p>Takeover is usually followed by a race to cash out. Slow the cash-out down:</p>\n<ul><li>New withdrawal address? Require MFA, email confirmation, and a <strong>24 to 48 hour lock</strong> before it can be used.</li><li>Large or unusual withdrawals trigger manual review.</li><li>Every security change (password, MFA, email, API key, address) sends an alert to the <strong>old</strong> contact details, with a &quot;this wasn't me&quot; link that freezes the account.</li></ul>","quiz":[{"q":"Why limit login attempts per account as well as per IP?","options":["It is faster","Attackers rotate through thousands of IPs, so per-IP limits alone miss slow, distributed attacks on one account","IP addresses are not recorded"],"answer":1,"explain":"Count both, and globally too."},{"q":"How does the Pwned Passwords range API protect the password being checked?","options":["Only the first 5 characters of its hash are sent; matching happens on your side","The password is encrypted in transit","It deletes the password after checking"],"answer":0,"explain":"That is k-anonymity: the service never learns which password you checked."},{"q":"An attacker steals a session and tries to add a new withdrawal address. Which control stops the cash-out?","options":["A longer password policy","Step-up MFA plus a time lock on newly added addresses, with an alert to the real user","Logging the request"],"answer":1,"explain":"Assume sessions can be stolen; protect the dangerous actions themselves."},{"q":"Which MFA option resists phishing sites by design?","options":["SMS codes","Email codes","Passkeys (WebAuthn)"],"answer":2,"explain":"The browser only signs for the genuine domain."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Build a login rate limiter","file":"limiter.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Allow at most 5 login attempts per IP, then block. Write it in this shape:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const attempts = new Map();\nfunction allowLogin(ip) {\n  const count = (attempts.get(ip) || 0) + 1;\n  attempts.set(ip, count);\n  return count &lt;= 5;\n}\nfor (let i = 1; i &lt;= 7; i++) console.log(i, allowLogin('203.0.113.9'));</code></pre></div>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const attempts = new Map();\nfunction allowLogin(ip) {\n  const count = (attempts.get(ip) || 0) + 1;\n  attempts.set(ip, count);\n  return count <= 5;\n}\nfor (let i = 1; i <= 7; i++) console.log(i, allowLogin('203.0.113.9'));","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+attempts\\s*=\\s*new\\s+Map\\(\\s*\\)","text":"Line 1: <code>const attempts = new Map();</code> (a Map stores a count for each IP)."},{"re":"function\\s+allowLogin\\s*\\(\\s*ip\\s*\\)\\s*\\{","text":"Line 2: <code>function allowLogin(ip) {</code>"},{"re":"const\\s+count\\s*=\\s*\\(\\s*attempts\\.get\\(\\s*ip\\s*\\)\\s*\\|\\|\\s*0\\s*\\)\\s*\\+\\s*1","text":"Line 3: <code>const count = (attempts.get(ip) || 0) + 1;</code>"},{"re":"attempts\\.set\\(\\s*ip\\s*,\\s*count\\s*\\)","text":"Line 4: <code>attempts.set(ip, count);</code>"},{"re":"return\\s+count\\s*<=\\s*5","text":"Line 5: <code>return count &lt;= 5;</code>"},{"re":"for\\s*\\(\\s*let\\s+i\\s*=\\s*1\\s*;\\s*i\\s*<=\\s*7\\s*;\\s*i\\+\\+\\s*\\)\\s*console\\.log\\(\\s*i\\s*,\\s*allowLogin\\(\\s*['\"]203\\.0\\.113\\.9['\"]\\s*\\)\\s*\\)","text":"Last line tests 7 attempts: <code>for (let i = 1; i &lt;= 7; i++) console.log(i, allowLogin('203.0.113.9'));</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Attempts 1 to 5 print <code>true</code>, then 6 and 7 print <code>false</code>. Real limiters also count per account (attackers rotate IPs), reset after a time window, and add a CAPTCHA or delay rather than a hard block, so attackers cannot lock real users out on purpose.</p>"}],"text":"Your users will be attacked If your app has logins, it will face account takeover (ATO) attempts: bots trying leaked username and password lists, phishing kits copying your login page, SIM swappers and support-desk social engineers. For an app that holds money or wallets (a launchpad, an exchange, a dashboard with withdrawals), ATO is the attack that matters most. The defence stack Layer Stops How Rate limiting Brute force and stuffing Limit attempts per account, per IP and globally; slow down, then challenge Breached-password checks Reused leaked passwords Reject passwords found in known breaches at sign-up and change Phishing-resistant MFA Stolen passwords and most phishing Offer passkeys (WebAuthn); authenticator apps as a fallback; avoid SMS for high-value accounts Session hygiene Stolen sessions Rotation, short lifetimes, revoke on password or MFA change Step-up authentication A stolen session doing damage Re-check MFA before withdrawals, address changes, API key creation Withdrawal protections Fast cash-out after takeover Address allow-lists, a 24 to 48 hour delay on newly added addresses, email confirmation and alerts Anomaly alerts Takeovers in progress New device, location or IP; impossible travel; many failed logins Recovery flow security Social engineering the support team No password resets over chat; strong identity checks; a cooling-off delay Script 1: a login rate limiter A minimal in-memory limiter shows the idea. Production systems keep the counters in Redis or a database and expire them over time. Script 2: checking breached passwords privately The Have I Been Pwned Pwned Passwords service lets you check whether a password appears in known breaches without sending it . You send only the first 5 characters of its SHA-1 hash; the service returns every breached hash with that prefix, and you look for the rest locally. This technique is called k-anonymity . async function isBreached(password) { const bytes = await crypto.subtle.digest('SHA-1', new TextEncoder().encode(password)); const hash = [...new Uint8Array(bytes)].map((b) = b.toString(16).padStart(2, '0')).join('').toUpperCase(); const res = await fetch(`https://api.pwnedpasswords.com/range/${hash.slice(0, 5)}`); return (await res.text()).includes(hash.slice(5)); } (SHA-1 is fine here because it is only an index into a public list, not how you store passwords. Store passwords with Argon2id.) Passkeys: the strongest login Passkeys (WebAuthn / FIDO2) replace passwords with a key pair: the private key stays in the user's device or password manager, and the browser only signs challenges for the real domain. A phishing site gets nothing usable. Libraries such as SimpleWebAuthn make adding them to a Node app a day's work, and they are the single biggest ATO reduction you can ship. Withdrawal protections (for apps that hold funds) Takeover is usually followed by a race to cash out. Slow the cash-out down: New withdrawal address? Require MFA, email confirmation, and a 24 to 48 hour lock before it can be used. Large or unusual withdrawals trigger manual review. Every security change (password, MFA, email, API key, address) sends an alert to the old contact details, with a this wasn't me link that freezes the account.","track":"keys"},{"id":"keys-launchpad","title":"Launchpad and token-launch key security","level":"advanced","minutes":45,"summary":"Protect a launch end to end, covering deployer and owner keys, presale wallets, whitelist signers, admin panels and the social accounts thousands of buyers trust.","html":"<h2 id=\"a-launch-is-a-key-management-event\">A launch is a key-management event</h2>\n<p>During a launch (a presale, an IDO on a launchpad, an airdrop claim, an NFT mint), more value moves through more keys in less time than at any other point in a project's life. Attackers plan around launch dates. The most common launch disasters are key and account failures:</p>\n<ul><li>A <strong>deployer key</strong> in a leaked <code>.env</code> lets an attacker take ownership and mint.</li><li>A <strong>whitelist signer</strong> key on a web server is stolen and used to sign unlimited allocations.</li><li>The project's <strong>X or Discord</strong> account is hijacked to post a &quot;mint is live&quot; link to a drainer site.</li><li>The <strong>website's domain or front-end library</strong> is compromised to serve malicious transactions.</li><li>A <strong>launchpad admin panel</strong> login is phished, letting the attacker change the sale's receiving address.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"map-every-key-before-launch-day\">Map every key before launch day</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Key / account</th><th>Power</th><th>Control it should have</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Deployer</td><td>Deploys contracts, often becomes owner</td><td>Fresh hardware wallet; transfers ownership to the multisig immediately</td></tr><tr><td>Owner / admin roles</td><td>Mint, pause, upgrade, set prices and recipients</td><td>Multisig (for example 3 of 5) plus a timelock on dangerous changes</td></tr><tr><td>Sale receiving wallet</td><td>Receives raised funds</td><td>Multisig; address published and verified on the explorer</td></tr><tr><td>Whitelist / claim signer</td><td>Authorises who may buy or claim</td><td>Dedicated key in a KMS or HSM, behind a service with rate limits and logs</td></tr><tr><td>Liquidity tokens</td><td>Can pull the trading pool</td><td>Locked or held by the multisig with a public lock</td></tr><tr><td>Launchpad dashboard</td><td>Configures the sale</td><td>SSO plus hardware security keys; minimal admins</td></tr><tr><td>Domain, DNS, hosting</td><td>Controls what buyers see</td><td>Registrar lock, hardware keys, change alerts</td></tr><tr><td>X, Discord, Telegram</td><td>Where buyers get &quot;the link&quot;</td><td>Hardware keys on every admin; few admins; Discord bots with minimal permissions; links pinned in advance</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"signature-based-whitelists-done-safely\">Signature-based whitelists, done safely</h2>\n<p>Many launchpads approve buyers off-chain: a server signs <code>(buyer, maxAmount)</code> and the contract checks the signature. Done carelessly, a single leaked or replayed signature lets attackers buy far more than allowed. A safe claim must bind the signature to <strong>one buyer, one contract, one chain, one use and a deadline</strong>, which EIP-712 structured data and OpenZeppelin's <code>EIP712</code>, <code>ECDSA</code> and <code>Nonces</code> give you.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"the-signer-service\">The signer service</h2>\n<p>The server that signs allowances holds a <strong>hot key</strong>: it must sign automatically, so it cannot be a hardware wallet in someone's drawer. Contain it:</p>\n<ul><li>Store the key in a <strong>KMS or HSM</strong> so the server can request signatures but can never read the key (next lesson).</li><li>Put <strong>policy</strong> in front of it: maximum amount per signature, maximum signatures per minute, only addresses from the allow-list, alerts when limits are hit.</li><li>Make it a <strong>separate service</strong> with its own minimal API, not a function inside the public web server.</li><li>Give the contract a way to <strong>rotate the signer address</strong> (through the multisig) and to pause claims.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"social-accounts-are-part-of-the-attack-surface\">Social accounts are part of the attack surface</h2>\n<p>Buyers trust whatever the project's official accounts post. Before launch:</p>\n<ul><li>Every admin of X, Discord and Telegram uses a <strong>hardware security key</strong>, and the list of admins is as short as possible.</li><li>Discord: no bots with Administrator permission; restrict who can post in announcement channels; disable <code>@everyone</code> pings except for a locked role.</li><li>Publish the official contract address and mint URL <strong>days before</strong> launch, pinned everywhere, and say &quot;we will never post a different link on launch day&quot;.</li><li>Prepare the incident message in advance (&quot;Our X account is compromised; do not click any links&quot;) and decide who posts it, from where.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"launch-day-runbook-excerpt\">Launch-day runbook (excerpt)</h2>\n<ol><li>Deployer deploys, verifies the contracts, and transfers ownership to the multisig. Confirm on the explorer.</li><li>Deployer wallet is emptied and retired.</li><li>Monitoring is live for owner changes, signer changes, large transfers, DNS and front-end changes.</li><li>War room open with the guardian who can pause, a comms lead, and a direct line to the launchpad.</li><li>Any anomaly: pause first, investigate second.</li></ol>","quiz":[{"q":"Why must a whitelist signature include a nonce and a deadline?","options":["To make the signature shorter","So each signature can be used once and only until it expires, stopping replay","Wallets require it"],"answer":1,"explain":"Plus buyer, amount, chain ID and contract address, so it cannot be reused anywhere else either."},{"q":"Where should a launchpad's automatic whitelist signing key live?","options":["In the public web server's .env file","In a KMS or HSM behind a separate signing service with rate limits and policy","In a hardware wallet that someone plugs in for every buyer"],"answer":1,"explain":"It must sign automatically, so protect it with infrastructure and limits."},{"q":"The project's X account posts an unexpected &quot;mint is live&quot; link an hour early. What is the most likely explanation?","options":["An early mint","The account has been hijacked to send buyers to a drainer site","A scheduling mistake"],"answer":1,"explain":"Pre-announced links and a prepared incident message limit the damage."},{"q":"What should happen to the deployer wallet straight after deployment?","options":["Ownership transfers to the multisig, then the deployer wallet is emptied and retired","It stays as the owner for convenience","It is shared with the team for emergencies"],"answer":0,"explain":"The deployer is a single point of failure."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Make claim signatures single-use and expiring","file":"Presale.sol","lang":"solidity","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Inside the presale's <code>claim</code> function, before verifying the signature, add the three checks every signed allowance needs. Three lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>require(block.timestamp &lt;= deadline, &quot;Signature expired&quot;);</code></li><li><code>require(!usedNonces[nonce], &quot;Signature already used&quot;);</code></li><li><code>usedNonces[nonce] = true;</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"require(block.timestamp <= deadline, \"Signature expired\");\nrequire(!usedNonces[nonce], \"Signature already used\");\nusedNonces[nonce] = true;","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"require\\(\\s*block\\.timestamp\\s*<=\\s*deadline\\s*,\\s*[\"']Signature expired[\"']\\s*\\)\\s*;","text":"Line 1: <code>require(block.timestamp &lt;= deadline, &quot;Signature expired&quot;);</code>"},{"re":"require\\(\\s*!\\s*usedNonces\\[\\s*nonce\\s*\\]\\s*,\\s*[\"']Signature already used[\"']\\s*\\)\\s*;","text":"Line 2: <code>require(!usedNonces[nonce], &quot;Signature already used&quot;);</code> (the <code>!</code> means &quot;not&quot;)."},{"re":"usedNonces\\[\\s*nonce\\s*\\]\\s*=\\s*true\\s*;","text":"Line 3 marks it spent: <code>usedNonces[nonce] = true;</code>"}],"explain":"<p>The signed message must also include the buyer's address, the amount, the nonce, the deadline, the chain ID and this contract's address (EIP-712's domain covers the last two). Then a leaked signature is useful only to the buyer it names, once, until the deadline, on this contract.</p>"}],"text":"A launch is a key-management event During a launch (a presale, an IDO on a launchpad, an airdrop claim, an NFT mint), more value moves through more keys in less time than at any other point in a project's life. Attackers plan around launch dates. The most common launch disasters are key and account failures: A deployer key in a leaked .env lets an attacker take ownership and mint. A whitelist signer key on a web server is stolen and used to sign unlimited allocations. The project's X or Discord account is hijacked to post a mint is live link to a drainer site. The website's domain or front-end library is compromised to serve malicious transactions. A launchpad admin panel login is phished, letting the attacker change the sale's receiving address. Map every key before launch day Key / account Power Control it should have Deployer Deploys contracts, often becomes owner Fresh hardware wallet; transfers ownership to the multisig immediately Owner / admin roles Mint, pause, upgrade, set prices and recipients Multisig (for example 3 of 5) plus a timelock on dangerous changes Sale receiving wallet Receives raised funds Multisig; address published and verified on the explorer Whitelist / claim signer Authorises who may buy or claim Dedicated key in a KMS or HSM, behind a service with rate limits and logs Liquidity tokens Can pull the trading pool Locked or held by the multisig with a public lock Launchpad dashboard Configures the sale SSO plus hardware security keys; minimal admins Domain, DNS, hosting Controls what buyers see Registrar lock, hardware keys, change alerts X, Discord, Telegram Where buyers get the link Hardware keys on every admin; few admins; Discord bots with minimal permissions; links pinned in advance Signature-based whitelists, done safely Many launchpads approve buyers off-chain: a server signs (buyer, maxAmount) and the contract checks the signature. Done carelessly, a single leaked or replayed signature lets attackers buy far more than allowed. A safe claim must bind the signature to one buyer, one contract, one chain, one use and a deadline , which EIP-712 structured data and OpenZeppelin's EIP712 , ECDSA and Nonces give you. The signer service The server that signs allowances holds a hot key : it must sign automatically, so it cannot be a hardware wallet in someone's drawer. Contain it: Store the key in a KMS or HSM so the server can request signatures but can never read the key (next lesson). Put policy in front of it: maximum amount per signature, maximum signatures per minute, only addresses from the allow-list, alerts when limits are hit. Make it a separate service with its own minimal API, not a function inside the public web server. Give the contract a way to rotate the signer address (through the multisig) and to pause claims. Social accounts are part of the attack surface Buyers trust whatever the project's official accounts post. Before launch: Every admin of X, Discord and Telegram uses a hardware security key , and the list of admins is as short as possible. Discord: no bots with Administrator permission; restrict who can post in announcement channels; disable @everyone pings except for a locked role. Publish the official contract address and mint URL days before launch, pinned everywhere, and say we will never post a different link on launch day . Prepare the incident message in advance ( Our X account is compromised; do not click any links ) and decide who posts it, from where. Launch-day runbook (excerpt) Deployer deploys, verifies the contracts, and transfers ownership to the multisig. Confirm on the explorer. Deployer wallet is emptied and retired. Monitoring is live for owner changes, signer changes, large transfers, DNS and front-end changes. War room open with the guardian who can pause, a comms lead, and a direct line to the launchpad. Any anomaly: pause first, investigate second.","track":"keys"},{"id":"keys-kms-mpc","title":"Keys in production: KMS, HSM, MPC and remote signers","level":"advanced","minutes":40,"summary":"How serious teams run keys that must sign automatically, using cloud KMS, hardware security modules, MPC wallets, policy engines and remote signers, with no raw private key ever in a server's memory.","html":"<h2 id=\"some-keys-must-be-hot\">Some keys must be hot</h2>\n<p>Relayers that submit transactions, whitelist signers, oracle updaters, market makers, validators and exchange hot wallets all need to sign <strong>automatically</strong>, often thousands of times a day. A hardware wallet in a drawer will not do. The dangerous shortcut is a raw private key in an environment variable on a server: one server compromise, one leaked log, and it is gone.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-options\">The options</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th>How it works</th><th>Strengths</th><th>Watch out for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Cloud KMS</strong> (for example AWS KMS with secp256k1 keys)</td><td>The key is generated and stored inside the provider's hardware; your server asks it to sign</td><td>The key can never be exported; IAM controls who can sign; every signature is logged</td><td>Cloud account security becomes key security; signatures need re-encoding for Ethereum (libraries handle this)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>HSM</strong> (hardware security module)</td><td>Dedicated tamper-resistant hardware you control or rent</td><td>Strongest isolation; regulatory standard</td><td>Cost, operations, availability planning</td></tr><tr><td><strong>MPC wallets</strong></td><td>The key is split into shares held by different parties or machines; they compute a signature together without ever assembling the key</td><td>No single place holds the key; built-in approval policies</td><td>Vendor trust and availability; understand the recovery model</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Smart contract wallets / multisig</strong></td><td>On-chain rules decide what a key can do</td><td>Spending limits and multiple approvers enforced by the chain</td><td>Gas, complexity, contract risk</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Remote signers</strong> (for example Web3Signer for validators)</td><td>Validator clients ask a separate signing service</td><td>Keys off the validator machine; slashing protection database</td><td>Must be highly available and locked down</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"create-a-signing-key-that-can-never-be-exported\">Create a signing key that can never be exported</h2>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"policy-decide-what-a-key-may-sign\">Policy: decide what a key may sign</h2>\n<p>Isolation stops the key being stolen. <strong>Policy</strong> stops it being misused by whoever controls the service:</p>\n<ul><li>Allow-lists of destination addresses and contract functions.</li><li>Amount limits per transaction and per day.</li><li>Rate limits and time windows.</li><li>Multiple approvers above a threshold (built into most MPC platforms and multisigs).</li><li>Automatic alerts, and an automatic pause, when a limit is hit.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"separation-of-duties\">Separation of duties</h2>\n<ul><li>The engineers who deploy the signing service cannot change its policies alone.</li><li>The people who can change policies cannot sign transactions alone.</li><li>Production access requires hardware keys and is logged and reviewed.</li><li>Break-glass access exists, is tested, and sets off alarms when used.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"rotation-as-a-routine\">Rotation as a routine</h2>\n<p>Design every system so a key can be replaced on a normal Tuesday, not only in a crisis: contracts allow the signer address to be rotated through the multisig, services read the active key ID from configuration, and you rehearse the rotation. A key you cannot rotate is a key you will keep using after it leaks.</p>","quiz":[{"q":"What is the main advantage of a cloud KMS key over a private key in an environment variable?","options":["The key cannot be exported; services can only ask it to sign, under IAM control and with every use logged","It is faster","It is free"],"answer":0,"explain":"A server compromise then lets an attacker sign only while they hold access, within policy, and visibly; it cannot walk away with the key."},{"q":"How does an MPC wallet avoid a single point of failure?","options":["It stores the key in several places","The key exists only as shares held by different parties, who compute signatures together without assembling it","It uses a longer key"],"answer":1,"explain":"No single machine or person ever holds the whole key."},{"q":"Why design for routine key rotation?","options":["Auditors like it","So that replacing a leaked or suspect key is fast and practised, not an untested emergency","Keys wear out"],"answer":1,"explain":"If rotation is hard, teams delay it, and leaked keys stay in use."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Create a secp256k1 key in AWS KMS","file":"Terminal","lang":"bash","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Using the AWS CLI, create a KMS key suitable for Ethereum signing. One command (key spec <code>ECC_SECG_P256K1</code>, usage <code>SIGN_VERIFY</code>, description <code>launch-whitelist-signer</code>):</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">aws kms create-key --key-spec ECC_SECG_P256K1 --key-usage SIGN_VERIFY --description &quot;launch-whitelist-signer&quot;</code></pre></div>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"aws kms create-key --key-spec ECC_SECG_P256K1 --key-usage SIGN_VERIFY --description \"launch-whitelist-signer\"","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*aws\\s+kms\\s+create-key","text":"Start with <code>aws kms create-key</code>"},{"re":"--key-spec\\s+ECC_SECG_P256K1","text":"<code>--key-spec ECC_SECG_P256K1</code> is the secp256k1 curve Ethereum uses."},{"re":"--key-usage\\s+SIGN_VERIFY","text":"<code>--key-usage SIGN_VERIFY</code> (this key signs; it does not encrypt)."},{"re":"--description\\s+[\"']launch-whitelist-signer[\"']","text":"<code>--description &quot;launch-whitelist-signer&quot;</code>"}],"explain":"<p>The private key is created inside AWS's hardware and cannot be downloaded by anyone, including you. Your signing service gets an IAM role allowed to call <code>kms:Sign</code> on this one key and nothing else. Every signature is logged in CloudTrail, so you can audit exactly what was signed and when.</p>"}],"text":"Some keys must be hot Relayers that submit transactions, whitelist signers, oracle updaters, market makers, validators and exchange hot wallets all need to sign automatically , often thousands of times a day. A hardware wallet in a drawer will not do. The dangerous shortcut is a raw private key in an environment variable on a server: one server compromise, one leaked log, and it is gone. The options Approach How it works Strengths Watch out for Cloud KMS (for example AWS KMS with secp256k1 keys) The key is generated and stored inside the provider's hardware; your server asks it to sign The key can never be exported; IAM controls who can sign; every signature is logged Cloud account security becomes key security; signatures need re-encoding for Ethereum (libraries handle this) HSM (hardware security module) Dedicated tamper-resistant hardware you control or rent Strongest isolation; regulatory standard Cost, operations, availability planning MPC wallets The key is split into shares held by different parties or machines; they compute a signature together without ever assembling the key No single place holds the key; built-in approval policies Vendor trust and availability; understand the recovery model Smart contract wallets / multisig On-chain rules decide what a key can do Spending limits and multiple approvers enforced by the chain Gas, complexity, contract risk Remote signers (for example Web3Signer for validators) Validator clients ask a separate signing service Keys off the validator machine; slashing protection database Must be highly available and locked down Create a signing key that can never be exported Policy: decide what a key may sign Isolation stops the key being stolen. Policy stops it being misused by whoever controls the service: Allow-lists of destination addresses and contract functions. Amount limits per transaction and per day. Rate limits and time windows. Multiple approvers above a threshold (built into most MPC platforms and multisigs). Automatic alerts, and an automatic pause, when a limit is hit. Separation of duties The engineers who deploy the signing service cannot change its policies alone. The people who can change policies cannot sign transactions alone. Production access requires hardware keys and is logged and reviewed. Break-glass access exists, is tested, and sets off alarms when used. Rotation as a routine Design every system so a key can be replaced on a normal Tuesday, not only in a crisis: contracts allow the signer address to be rotated through the multisig, services read the active key ID from configuration, and you rehearse the rotation. A key you cannot rotate is a key you will keep using after it leaks.","track":"keys"},{"id":"keys-recovery","title":"Compromise recovery: rescuing assets and rotating keys","level":"expert","minutes":45,"summary":"The first hour after a key or account is compromised, including sweeper bots, private transaction bundles, revoking approvals, ownership races and EIP-7702 delegation traps.","html":"<h2 id=\"assume-the-worst-move-fast-in-the-right-order\">Assume the worst, move fast, in the right order</h2>\n<p>When a key or account is compromised, the attacker is usually automated and already acting. Your priorities, in order:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Stop the bleeding</strong>: pause what can be paused, revoke what can be revoked.</li><li><strong>Secure what can still move</strong>: rescue assets and powers that remain.</li><li><strong>Rotate everything the attacker could have touched.</strong></li><li><strong>Investigate and communicate.</strong></li></ol>\n<p>Investigation comes last. Every minute spent reading logs while the key is live is a minute the attacker uses.</p>\n<h2 id=\"account-compromises-web2\">Account compromises (web2)</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sign out all sessions and revoke tokens</td><td>Stolen sessions keep working after a password change</td></tr><tr><td>Change the password, from a clean device</td><td>The infected device may be how they got in</td></tr><tr><td>Remove unknown MFA devices, recovery emails and phone numbers</td><td>Attackers add their own to keep access</td></tr><tr><td>Revoke OAuth apps, API keys and personal access tokens created recently</td><td>Persistence: they may have created long-lived keys</td></tr><tr><td>Check forwarding rules, webhooks, deploy keys and SSH keys</td><td>Quiet ways to keep reading your data</td></tr><tr><td>Rotate every secret the account could see</td><td>Assume it was all copied</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"private-key-compromises-web3\">Private key compromises (web3)</h2>\n<p>A compromised private key cannot be &quot;secured&quot;; the goal is to <strong>get value and powers out</strong> before the attacker does, then never use that key again.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-sweeper-bot-problem\">The sweeper bot problem</h3>\n<p>Attackers attach <strong>sweeper bots</strong> to compromised wallets. The instant ETH arrives (for example, you send gas to rescue tokens), the bot spends it first. Sending gas money to a compromised wallet usually just donates it.</p>\n<h3 id=\"private-bundles\">Private bundles</h3>\n<p>The standard rescue is a <strong>private bundle</strong> (for example via Flashbots or another private builder): one atomic package of transactions, submitted privately and never visible in the public mempool, that:</p>\n<ol><li>Funds the compromised wallet with just enough gas, <strong>and</strong></li><li>Transfers the tokens or NFTs out to a safe wallet, <strong>and</strong></li><li>Uses up the gas, so nothing is left to sweep,</li></ol>\n<p>all in the same block, or not at all. The bot never sees the funding transaction in time. Volunteer white-hat responders (such as SEAL 911) help with exactly this; contact them rather than improvising under stress.</p>\n<h3 id=\"revoke-approvals\">Revoke approvals</h3>\n<p>If the issue is a malicious <strong>approval</strong> rather than a stolen key, revoking it stops further drains. Revoking an ERC-20 approval means setting the allowance to zero.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h3 id=\"ownership-races\">Ownership races</h3>\n<p>If a compromised key is the <strong>owner</strong> of a contract, both you and the attacker can call <code>transferOwnership</code>. Whoever lands first wins. Send the transfer to your multisig via a private bundle immediately, with a high priority fee. Contracts owned by a multisig behind a timelock never put you in this race, which is the real lesson.</p>\n<h3 id=\"eip-7702-delegation-traps\">EIP-7702 delegation traps</h3>\n<p>Since Ethereum's 2025 Pectra upgrade, an ordinary wallet (EOA) can <strong>delegate</strong> to smart-contract code (EIP-7702). Attackers who obtain a key, or trick a user into signing a delegation, can point the wallet at a malicious contract that drains anything sent to it automatically, acting like a permanent on-chain sweeper. After a compromise, check whether the address has a delegation set, and never sign an authorisation you do not fully understand.</p>\n<h2 id=\"after-the-fire\">After the fire</h2>\n<ul><li>Write the timeline while memories are fresh.</li><li>Find the root cause (how did the key leak?) and fix that, not just the symptom.</li><li>Rotate everything in the same trust zone, not only the key you know about.</li><li>Publish an honest post-mortem if users were affected.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"report-the-theft-us\">Report the theft (US)</h2>\n<p>Reporting rarely gets funds back on its own, but it unlocks the people who can freeze them:</p>\n<ul><li>File with the <strong>FBI's Internet Crime Complaint Center</strong> at ic3.gov: what happened, when, the addresses involved and every transaction hash.</li><li>Send that report number to the <strong>exchanges and stablecoin issuers</strong> where the stolen funds land. Many will only freeze assets with a law-enforcement report.</li><li>Keep a timeline and screenshots. You will need them for insurance, for the IRS (theft losses can matter for tax) and for any civil case.</li><li>If you run a platform and users' personal data was exposed too, <strong>state breach-notification laws</strong> apply: talk to a lawyer the same day.</li></ul>","quiz":[{"q":"Why does sending ETH for gas to a compromised wallet usually fail?","options":["A sweeper bot spends any incoming ETH immediately, before your rescue transaction runs","The network blocks compromised wallets","Gas prices double"],"answer":0,"explain":"Use a private bundle that funds and rescues atomically."},{"q":"A contract's owner key leaks. What is the fastest protective move?","options":["Investigate the leak first","Transfer ownership to the multisig through a private bundle with a high priority fee, before the attacker acts","Post on social media"],"answer":1,"explain":"It is a race. Better still: never let a single key own a contract."},{"q":"After an account takeover you change the password. What else is essential?","options":["Nothing","Revoke sessions, remove attacker-added MFA and recovery methods, and revoke new tokens and OAuth apps","Delete the account"],"answer":1,"explain":"Attackers set up persistence; remove all of it."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Revoke a token approval","file":"revoke.ts","lang":"ts","run":false,"prompt":"<p>With wagmi or viem's <code>writeContract</code>, set the allowance for <code>spender</code> back to zero. One call (on one line):</p>\n<p><code>writeContract({ address: TOKEN, abi: erc20Abi, functionName: 'approve', args: [spender, 0n] });</code></p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"writeContract({ address: TOKEN, abi: erc20Abi, functionName: 'approve', args: [spender, 0n] });","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*writeContract\\(\\s*\\{\\s*address\\s*:\\s*TOKEN\\s*,\\s*abi\\s*:\\s*erc20Abi\\s*,","text":"Start <code>writeContract({ address: TOKEN, abi: erc20Abi,</code>"},{"re":"functionName\\s*:\\s*['\"]approve['\"]","text":"Revoking is just another approval: <code>functionName: 'approve'</code>"},{"re":"args\\s*:\\s*\\[\\s*spender\\s*,\\s*0n\\s*\\]","text":"with an amount of zero, as a BigInt: <code>args: [spender, 0n]</code>"},{"re":"\\}\\s*\\)\\s*;?\\s*$","text":"Close with <code>});</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Revoke tools do exactly this for every approval you hold. For NFTs, the equivalent is <code>setApprovalForAll(operator, false)</code>. Revoking from a compromised wallet only helps if the attacker does not also hold the key, so for a stolen key, rescue the assets with a private bundle instead.</p>"}],"text":"Assume the worst, move fast, in the right order When a key or account is compromised, the attacker is usually automated and already acting. Your priorities, in order: Stop the bleeding : pause what can be paused, revoke what can be revoked. Secure what can still move : rescue assets and powers that remain. Rotate everything the attacker could have touched. Investigate and communicate. Investigation comes last. Every minute spent reading logs while the key is live is a minute the attacker uses. Account compromises (web2) Step Why Sign out all sessions and revoke tokens Stolen sessions keep working after a password change Change the password, from a clean device The infected device may be how they got in Remove unknown MFA devices, recovery emails and phone numbers Attackers add their own to keep access Revoke OAuth apps, API keys and personal access tokens created recently Persistence: they may have created long-lived keys Check forwarding rules, webhooks, deploy keys and SSH keys Quiet ways to keep reading your data Rotate every secret the account could see Assume it was all copied Private key compromises (web3) A compromised private key cannot be secured ; the goal is to get value and powers out before the attacker does, then never use that key again. The sweeper bot problem Attackers attach sweeper bots to compromised wallets. The instant ETH arrives (for example, you send gas to rescue tokens), the bot spends it first. Sending gas money to a compromised wallet usually just donates it. Private bundles The standard rescue is a private bundle (for example via Flashbots or another private builder): one atomic package of transactions, submitted privately and never visible in the public mempool, that: Funds the compromised wallet with just enough gas, and Transfers the tokens or NFTs out to a safe wallet, and Uses up the gas, so nothing is left to sweep, all in the same block, or not at all. The bot never sees the funding transaction in time. Volunteer white-hat responders (such as SEAL 911) help with exactly this; contact them rather than improvising under stress. Revoke approvals If the issue is a malicious approval rather than a stolen key, revoking it stops further drains. Revoking an ERC-20 approval means setting the allowance to zero. Ownership races If a compromised key is the owner of a contract, both you and the attacker can call transferOwnership . Whoever lands first wins. Send the transfer to your multisig via a private bundle immediately, with a high priority fee. Contracts owned by a multisig behind a timelock never put you in this race, which is the real lesson. EIP-7702 delegation traps Since Ethereum's 2025 Pectra upgrade, an ordinary wallet (EOA) can delegate to smart-contract code (EIP-7702). Attackers who obtain a key, or trick a user into signing a delegation, can point the wallet at a malicious contract that drains anything sent to it automatically, acting like a permanent on-chain sweeper. After a compromise, check whether the address has a delegation set, and never sign an authorisation you do not fully understand. After the fire Write the timeline while memories are fresh. Find the root cause (how did the key leak?) and fix that, not just the symptom. Rotate everything in the same trust zone, not only the key you know about. Publish an honest post-mortem if users were affected. Report the theft (US) Reporting rarely gets funds back on its own, but it unlocks the people who can freeze them: File with the FBI's Internet Crime Complaint Center at ic3.gov: what happened, when, the addresses involved and every transaction hash. Send that report number to the exchanges and stablecoin issuers where the stolen funds land. Many will only freeze assets with a law-enforcement report. Keep a timeline and screenshots. You will need them for insurance, for the IRS (theft losses can matter for tax) and for any civil case. If you run a platform and users' personal data was exposed too, state breach-notification laws appl","track":"keys"},{"id":"keys-detection","title":"Detecting compromise and responding automatically","level":"expert","minutes":45,"summary":"Build the monitoring and response scripts that catch stolen keys and hijacked accounts in seconds, covering treasury watchers, approval alerts, admin-change alarms and safe auto-pause bots.","html":"<h2 id=\"detection-is-a-race-against-automation\">Detection is a race against automation</h2>\n<p>Attackers use bots that act within seconds of getting a key. If your team learns about a compromise from users on X an hour later, it is already over. Expert teams have <strong>machines watching machines</strong>, with a human paged within a minute.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-to-watch\">What to watch</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Signal</th><th>Where it shows up</th><th>Likely meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Transfer out of the treasury or presale wallet</td><td><code>Transfer</code> events from that address</td><td>Theft, or an unplanned payment</td></tr><tr><td>New <code>Approval</code> from a protocol wallet</td><td><code>Approval</code> events</td><td>Someone preparing a drain</td></tr><tr><td>Owner, admin or signer changed</td><td><code>OwnershipTransferred</code>, <code>RoleGranted</code>, custom events</td><td>Takeover of the contract's controls</td></tr><tr><td>Upgrade or implementation change</td><td><code>Upgraded</code> events on proxies</td><td>Malicious code being swapped in</td></tr><tr><td>Login from a new country or device on an admin account</td><td>Identity provider logs</td><td>Stolen credentials or session</td></tr><tr><td>New OAuth app, API key or deploy key</td><td>Platform audit logs</td><td>Attacker persistence</td></tr><tr><td>DNS records or served JavaScript changed</td><td>DNS and front-end monitors</td><td>Front-end hijack</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"script-watch-the-treasury\">Script: watch the treasury</h2>\n<p>viem can subscribe to contract events and run your code for each new one. Point it at transfers <strong>from</strong> the treasury address, and send an alert to Slack, Discord, Telegram or a paging service.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"automated-response-powerful-so-be-careful\">Automated response: powerful, so be careful</h2>\n<p>Some teams let a <strong>guardian bot</strong> pause the protocol automatically when a rule fires (for example, outflow above 10% of TVL within ten minutes). It can save everything, or cause an outage on a false alarm. Rules for doing it safely:</p>\n<ul><li>The bot holds a key that can <strong>only pause</strong>: never move funds, never unpause, never upgrade.</li><li>Pausing must be <strong>safe to do wrongly</strong>: users' funds stay withdrawable, or the pause is short and reviewed.</li><li>Thresholds are tuned on real history, and every automatic pause pages a human immediately.</li><li>The bot's own key lives in a KMS with tight IAM, and the bot is monitored too.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">// Sketch of a guardian rule (pseudocode-level JavaScript)\nif (outflowLast10Min &gt; 0.1 * totalValueLocked) {\n  await guardian.writeContract({ address: VAULT, abi: vaultAbi, functionName: 'pause' });\n  await sendAlert('Auto-paused: outflow above 10% of TVL in 10 minutes. Investigate now.');\n}</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"detection-for-accounts-and-apis\">Detection for accounts and APIs</h2>\n<ul><li>Turn on and <strong>export audit logs</strong>: GitHub organisation audit log, cloud provider logs, identity provider sign-in logs, launchpad admin logs.</li><li>Alert on: new admin, new OAuth app authorised, new personal access token, MFA removed, login from a new country, repository made public, branch protection disabled.</li><li>Use <strong>canary tokens</strong>: fake credentials planted where a thief would find them (in an old <code>.env</code>, a password manager folder, a cloud bucket). Any use of one is a guaranteed alarm, with no false positives.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"practise-under-pressure\">Practise under pressure</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"#critical-moment\">Critical Moment</a> simulator puts you in these situations against a clock: identify the attack from live evidence, decode what the attacker is doing, and choose the right containment steps in the right order. The <a href=\"#hackathons\">Keys &amp; Accounts hackathon</a> has you build these monitors for real.</p>","quiz":[{"q":"Why should an auto-pause bot's key be able to pause but not unpause?","options":["If the bot or its key is compromised or wrong, the worst it can do is pause; restarting needs human review","Unpausing costs more gas","Contracts cannot be unpaused"],"answer":0,"explain":"Limit automated keys to the safest possible action."},{"q":"What makes a canary token a good alarm?","options":["It encrypts your secrets","It is never used legitimately, so any use means a real intruder found it","It blocks attackers"],"answer":1,"explain":"Zero false positives by design."},{"q":"Which event would reveal an attacker preparing to drain a protocol wallet's tokens?","options":["A Transfer into the wallet","A new Approval from the wallet to an unknown spender","A new block"],"answer":1,"explain":"Approvals usually come before the drain."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Alert on treasury transfers","file":"watch-treasury.ts","lang":"ts","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write the watcher in this shape (the <code>client</code>, <code>TOKEN</code>, <code>TREASURY</code>, <code>erc20Abi</code> and <code>sendAlert</code> already exist):</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\"><pre><code class=\"language-ts\">client.watchContractEvent({\n  address: TOKEN,\n  abi: erc20Abi,\n  eventName: 'Transfer',\n  args: { from: TREASURY },\n  onLogs: (logs) =&gt; sendAlert(`Treasury transfer: ${logs.length} event(s)`),\n});</code></pre></div>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"client.watchContractEvent({\n  address: TOKEN,\n  abi: erc20Abi,\n  eventName: 'Transfer',\n  args: { from: TREASURY },\n  onLogs: (logs) => sendAlert(`Treasury transfer: ${logs.length} event(s)`),\n});","also":["client.watchContractEvent({\n  address: TOKEN,\n  abi: erc20Abi,\n  eventName: 'Transfer',\n  args: { from: TREASURY },\n  onLogs: logs => sendAlert(`Treasury transfer: ${logs.length} event(s)`),\n});"],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*client\\.watchContractEvent\\(\\s*\\{\\s*$","text":"Line 1: <code>client.watchContractEvent({</code>"},{"re":"address\\s*:\\s*TOKEN\\s*,","text":"<code>address: TOKEN,</code>"},{"re":"abi\\s*:\\s*erc20Abi\\s*,","text":"<code>abi: erc20Abi,</code>"},{"re":"eventName\\s*:\\s*['\"]Transfer['\"]\\s*,","text":"<code>eventName: 'Transfer',</code>"},{"re":"args\\s*:\\s*\\{\\s*from\\s*:\\s*TREASURY\\s*\\}\\s*,","text":"Filter on the indexed <code>from</code> argument: <code>args: { from: TREASURY },</code>"},{"re":"onLogs\\s*:\\s*\\(?\\s*logs\\s*\\)?\\s*=>\\s*sendAlert\\(\\s*`Treasury transfer: \\$\\{logs\\.length\\} event\\(s\\)`\\s*\\)","text":"<code>onLogs: (logs) =&gt; sendAlert(...)</code> with a template literal: <code>Treasury transfer: ${logs.length} event(s)</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*\\}\\s*\\)\\s*;?\\s*$","text":"Close with <code>});</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Run it as a small always-on service (with a second, independent RPC provider as backup). Add the same pattern for <code>Approval</code> events from protocol wallets and for ownership and role events on every contract you own.</p>"}],"text":"Detection is a race against automation Attackers use bots that act within seconds of getting a key. If your team learns about a compromise from users on X an hour later, it is already over. Expert teams have machines watching machines , with a human paged within a minute. What to watch Signal Where it shows up Likely meaning Transfer out of the treasury or presale wallet Transfer events from that address Theft, or an unplanned payment New Approval from a protocol wallet Approval events Someone preparing a drain Owner, admin or signer changed OwnershipTransferred , RoleGranted , custom events Takeover of the contract's controls Upgrade or implementation change Upgraded events on proxies Malicious code being swapped in Login from a new country or device on an admin account Identity provider logs Stolen credentials or session New OAuth app, API key or deploy key Platform audit logs Attacker persistence DNS records or served JavaScript changed DNS and front-end monitors Front-end hijack Script: watch the treasury viem can subscribe to contract events and run your code for each new one. Point it at transfers from the treasury address, and send an alert to Slack, Discord, Telegram or a paging service. Automated response: powerful, so be careful Some teams let a guardian bot pause the protocol automatically when a rule fires (for example, outflow above 10% of TVL within ten minutes). It can save everything, or cause an outage on a false alarm. Rules for doing it safely: The bot holds a key that can only pause : never move funds, never unpause, never upgrade. Pausing must be safe to do wrongly : users' funds stay withdrawable, or the pause is short and reviewed. Thresholds are tuned on real history, and every automatic pause pages a human immediately. The bot's own key lives in a KMS with tight IAM, and the bot is monitored too. // Sketch of a guardian rule (pseudocode-level JavaScript) if (outflowLast10Min 0.1 * totalValueLocked) { await guardian.writeContract({ address: VAULT, abi: vaultAbi, functionName: 'pause' }); await sendAlert('Auto-paused: outflow above 10% of TVL in 10 minutes. Investigate now.'); } Detection for accounts and APIs Turn on and export audit logs : GitHub organisation audit log, cloud provider logs, identity provider sign-in logs, launchpad admin logs. Alert on: new admin, new OAuth app authorised, new personal access token, MFA removed, login from a new country, repository made public, branch protection disabled. Use canary tokens : fake credentials planted where a thief would find them (in an old .env , a password manager folder, a cloud bucket). Any use of one is a guaranteed alarm, with no false positives. Practise under pressure The Critical Moment simulator puts you in these situations against a clock: identify the attack from live evidence, decode what the attacker is doing, and choose the right containment steps in the right order. The Keys Accounts hackathon has you build these monitors for real.","track":"keys"},{"id":"enc-why","title":"Encryption, your last line of defence","level":"beginner","minutes":30,"summary":"What encryption protects (and what it cannot), data in transit versus at rest, why the key is everything, and why US breach laws reward you for encrypting.","html":"<h2 id=\"why-encrypt-at-all\">Why encrypt at all?</h2>\n<p>Security is layers. Passwords, MFA and careful code try to stop attackers getting in. <strong>Encryption decides what they get when they do.</strong> A stolen laptop, a leaked database backup or a sniffed Wi-Fi connection is a disaster if the data is readable, and close to a non-event if it is properly encrypted and the key was not taken too.</p>\n<p>Encryption turns readable data (<strong>plaintext</strong>) into scrambled data (<strong>ciphertext</strong>) that only someone holding the right <strong>key</strong> can turn back.</p>\n<h2 id=\"data-has-three-states\">Data has three states</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>State</th><th>Example</th><th>Protected by</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>In transit</strong> (moving across a network)</td><td>Logging in from café Wi-Fi, your app calling an API</td><td>HTTPS/TLS, encrypted DNS, VPNs on untrusted networks</td></tr><tr><td><strong>At rest</strong> (stored)</td><td>A laptop's disk, a database, a backup in the cloud, a USB stick</td><td>Full-disk encryption, encrypted backups, encrypted fields and files</td></tr><tr><td><strong>In use</strong> (being worked on)</td><td>Data in memory while your app or you are using it</td><td>Mostly <em>not</em> encryption: device security, least privilege, isolation</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Real systems need the first two everywhere. The third is the gap attackers aim for.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-encryption-stops-and-what-it-does-not\">What encryption stops, and what it does not</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Threat</th><th>Does encryption help?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Laptop or phone stolen while locked</td><td><strong>Yes</strong>, with full-disk encryption and a strong passcode</td></tr><tr><td>Someone sniffing café Wi-Fi</td><td><strong>Yes</strong>, with HTTPS (and the attacker can still see <em>which</em> sites you visit)</td></tr><tr><td>A database backup leaks from a misconfigured bucket</td><td><strong>Yes</strong>, if the backup was encrypted and the key is stored elsewhere</td></tr><tr><td>Your cloud provider is breached</td><td><strong>Yes</strong>, if you encrypted the data yourself before uploading (client-side)</td></tr><tr><td>Malware running on your unlocked laptop</td><td><strong>No.</strong> Your data is decrypted while you use it, and the malware sees what you see</td></tr><tr><td>You type your password into a phishing site</td><td><strong>No.</strong> The connection to the attacker is encrypted too</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>So encryption is not a replacement for the rest of security: it is what limits the damage when the rest fails.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-key-is-everything\">The key is everything</h2>\n<p>Modern ciphers like <strong>AES-256</strong> and <strong>ChaCha20</strong> are not broken by guessing; there are far too many keys. Encryption fails because of the <strong>key</strong>:</p>\n<ul><li>The key is stored <strong>next to the data</strong> (a key in the same database, in the front-end JavaScript, or in the repo). That is a locked door with the key taped to it.</li><li>The key comes from a <strong>weak password</strong> that can be guessed offline.</li><li>The key <strong>leaks</strong> with everything else because nobody thought about where it lives.</li></ul>\n<p>Good encryption is mostly good key management, which is why later lessons cover password managers, secret stores, KMS and envelope encryption.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"encoding-hashing-and-encryption-are-different-things\">Encoding, hashing and encryption are different things</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Reversible?</th><th>Needs a key?</th><th>Use it for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Encoding</strong> (Base64, hex)</td><td>Yes, by anyone</td><td>No</td><td>Moving binary data around as text. <strong>Not secret at all.</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Hashing</strong> (SHA-256, Argon2)</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>Fingerprints, integrity checks, and (with a slow hash like Argon2) storing passwords</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Encryption</strong> (AES-GCM, ChaCha20-Poly1305)</td><td>Yes, with the key</td><td>Yes</td><td>Keeping data secret while still being able to read it later</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"encryption-and-us-law\">Encryption and US law</h2>\n<p>US law gives you a strong financial reason to encrypt, on top of the security one. This is general awareness, not legal advice:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>State breach-notification laws.</strong> Every US state requires businesses to notify people when their personal information is breached, and most give a <strong>safe harbor for encrypted data</strong>: if the stolen data was encrypted and the key was not taken too, notification is usually not required.</li><li><strong>California (CCPA).</strong> Californians can sue a business, for statutory damages of roughly 100 to 750 dollars per person per incident (adjusted for inflation), when their <strong>nonencrypted and nonredacted</strong> personal information is breached because the business did not keep reasonable security. For a leak of 100,000 users, that number gets very large very fast.</li><li><strong>The FTC Safeguards Rule</strong> requires many non-bank financial companies to encrypt customer information both in transit and at rest.</li></ul>\n<p>Encrypt personal data, and keep the keys somewhere else.</p>","quiz":[{"q":"Your laptop is stolen while it is locked. What protects the files on it?","options":["HTTPS","Full-disk encryption plus a strong login password","Your antivirus"],"answer":1,"explain":"Without full-disk encryption, a thief can pull the drive and read everything on it."},{"q":"Malware is running on your unlocked laptop. Does disk encryption stop it reading your files?","options":["Yes, the files are encrypted","No: the disk is decrypted while you are logged in, so the malware sees what you see","Only if you use AES-256"],"answer":1,"explain":"Encryption protects data at rest and in transit, not data in use on a compromised device."},{"q":"A company encrypts its customer database but stores the key in the same database. What is the problem?","options":["AES is too weak for databases","Anyone who steals the database also gets the key, so the encryption adds almost nothing","Nothing, that is standard practice"],"answer":1,"explain":"Keep keys separate from the data they protect."},{"q":"Why do many US companies encrypt personal data even beyond the security benefit?","options":["Most state breach laws give a safe harbor when breached data was encrypted and the key was not taken","Encryption is illegal to skip in every state","It makes databases faster"],"answer":0,"explain":"Encrypted data that leaks without its key usually does not trigger the costly notification duties."}],"exercises":[{"title":"A key is just random bytes","file":"key.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>An AES-256 key is 32 random bytes (256 bits). Generate one with the browser's secure random generator and print its size in bits:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const key = crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(32));\nconsole.log(key.length * 8, 'bits');</code></pre></div>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const key = crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(32));\nconsole.log(key.length * 8, 'bits');","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+key\\s*=\\s*crypto\\.getRandomValues\\(\\s*new\\s+Uint8Array\\(\\s*32\\s*\\)\\s*\\)","text":"Line 1: <code>const key = crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(32));</code> A <code>Uint8Array(32)</code> is 32 bytes; <code>getRandomValues</code> fills it with cryptographically secure randomness."},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*key\\.length\\s*\\*\\s*8\\s*,\\s*['\"]bits['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>console.log(key.length * 8, 'bits');</code> (8 bits per byte)."}],"explain":"<p>It prints <code>256 bits</code>. Never use <code>Math.random()</code> for keys: it is predictable. Always use <code>crypto.getRandomValues</code> in the browser or <code>crypto.randomBytes</code> in Node.</p>"},{"title":"Base64 is not encryption","file":"encoding.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>A developer &quot;hid&quot; a password by Base64-encoding it. Show that anyone can reverse it in one line:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const hidden = btoa('hunter2');\nconsole.log(hidden);\nconsole.log(atob(hidden));</code></pre></div>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const hidden = btoa('hunter2');\nconsole.log(hidden);\nconsole.log(atob(hidden));","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+hidden\\s*=\\s*btoa\\(\\s*['\"]hunter2['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"Line 1: <code>const hidden = btoa('hunter2');</code> (<code>btoa</code> encodes text as Base64)."},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*hidden\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>console.log(hidden);</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*atob\\(\\s*hidden\\s*\\)\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3: <code>console.log(atob(hidden));</code> (<code>atob</code> decodes it straight back)."}],"explain":"<p>It prints <code>aHVudGVyMg==</code> and then <code>hunter2</code>. Base64, hex and URL-encoding are formats, not protection. If you see &quot;encrypted&quot; data that ends in <code>=</code> or <code>==</code>, try decoding it: the Critical Moment simulator and the CTF are full of exactly this mistake.</p>"}],"text":"Why encrypt at all? Security is layers. Passwords, MFA and careful code try to stop attackers getting in. Encryption decides what they get when they do. A stolen laptop, a leaked database backup or a sniffed Wi-Fi connection is a disaster if the data is readable, and close to a non-event if it is properly encrypted and the key was not taken too. Encryption turns readable data ( plaintext ) into scrambled data ( ciphertext ) that only someone holding the right key can turn back. Data has three states State Example Protected by In transit (moving across a network) Logging in from café Wi-Fi, your app calling an API HTTPS/TLS, encrypted DNS, VPNs on untrusted networks At rest (stored) A laptop's disk, a database, a backup in the cloud, a USB stick Full-disk encryption, encrypted backups, encrypted fields and files In use (being worked on) Data in memory while your app or you are using it Mostly not encryption: device security, least privilege, isolation Real systems need the first two everywhere. The third is the gap attackers aim for. What encryption stops, and what it does not Threat Does encryption help? Laptop or phone stolen while locked Yes , with full-disk encryption and a strong passcode Someone sniffing café Wi-Fi Yes , with HTTPS (and the attacker can still see which sites you visit) A database backup leaks from a misconfigured bucket Yes , if the backup was encrypted and the key is stored elsewhere Your cloud provider is breached Yes , if you encrypted the data yourself before uploading (client-side) Malware running on your unlocked laptop No. Your data is decrypted while you use it, and the malware sees what you see You type your password into a phishing site No. The connection to the attacker is encrypted too So encryption is not a replacement for the rest of security: it is what limits the damage when the rest fails. The key is everything Modern ciphers like AES-256 and ChaCha20 are not broken by guessing; there are far too many keys. Encryption fails because of the key : The key is stored next to the data (a key in the same database, in the front-end JavaScript, or in the repo). That is a locked door with the key taped to it. The key comes from a weak password that can be guessed offline. The key leaks with everything else because nobody thought about where it lives. Good encryption is mostly good key management, which is why later lessons cover password managers, secret stores, KMS and envelope encryption. Encoding, hashing and encryption are different things Reversible? Needs a key? Use it for Encoding (Base64, hex) Yes, by anyone No Moving binary data around as text. Not secret at all. Hashing (SHA-256, Argon2) No No Fingerprints, integrity checks, and (with a slow hash like Argon2) storing passwords Encryption (AES-GCM, ChaCha20-Poly1305) Yes, with the key Yes Keeping data secret while still being able to read it later Encryption and US law US law gives you a strong financial reason to encrypt, on top of the security one. This is general awareness, not legal advice: State breach-notification laws. Every US state requires businesses to notify people when their personal information is breached, and most give a safe harbor for encrypted data : if the stolen data was encrypted and the key was not taken too, notification is usually not required. California (CCPA). Californians can sue a business, for statutory damages of roughly 100 to 750 dollars per person per incident (adjusted for inflation), when their nonencrypted and nonredacted personal information is breached because the business did not keep reasonable security. For a leak of 100,000 users, that number gets very large very fast. The FTC Safeguards Rule requires many non-bank financial companies to encrypt customer information both in transit and at rest. Encrypt personal data, and keep the keys somewhere else.","track":"encrypt"},{"id":"enc-devices","title":"Encrypt your devices, backups and messages","level":"beginner","minutes":30,"summary":"Turn on full-disk encryption on every computer and phone, encrypt your backups, choose messaging apps that are really end-to-end encrypted, and protect your traffic on public Wi-Fi.","html":"<h2 id=\"start-with-your-own-machine\">Start with your own machine</h2>\n<p>Your laptop holds everything an attacker wants: saved browser sessions, password manager caches, SSH keys, <code>.env</code> files, wallet files and seed phrase screenshots you forgot about. If it is stolen and the disk is not encrypted, the thief can remove the drive and read all of it without knowing your password.</p>\n<p><strong>Full-disk encryption</strong> (FDE) fixes that. With it on, the disk is unreadable until you log in.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>System</th><th>Built-in encryption</th><th>Where to turn it on</th><th>Check from the terminal</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>macOS</td><td><strong>FileVault</strong></td><td>System Settings &gt; Privacy &amp; Security &gt; FileVault</td><td><code>fdesetup status</code></td></tr><tr><td>Windows</td><td><strong>BitLocker</strong> (Pro) or <strong>Device encryption</strong> (Home, on supported PCs)</td><td>Settings &gt; Privacy &amp; security &gt; Device encryption</td><td><code>manage-bde -status</code> (in an admin terminal)</td></tr><tr><td>Linux</td><td><strong>LUKS</strong></td><td>Usually chosen when installing (&quot;Encrypt the new installation&quot;)</td><td><code>lsblk -f</code> (look for <code>crypto_LUKS</code>)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>When you turn it on you get a <strong>recovery key</strong>. Store it in your password manager or on paper somewhere safe, never only on the same laptop.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"phones\">Phones</h2>\n<p>Modern iPhones and Android phones encrypt their storage automatically, <strong>but the encryption is only as strong as your passcode</strong>. A 4-digit PIN has 10,000 options.</p>\n<ul><li>Use at least a <strong>6-digit passcode</strong>, or better, an alphanumeric one, plus Face ID or a fingerprint for convenience.</li><li>Turn on <strong>Find My</strong> (iPhone) or <strong>Find My Device</strong> (Android) so you can wipe a lost phone remotely.</li><li>Keep the phone updated: most phone break-in tools rely on old, unpatched bugs.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"backups\">Backups</h2>\n<p>Backups are often the weakest copy of your data: old, forgotten and unencrypted.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Time Machine</strong> (Mac): tick &quot;Encrypt backup&quot; when you choose the disk.</li><li><strong>iPhone backups to a computer</strong>: tick &quot;Encrypt local backup&quot;.</li><li><strong>iCloud</strong>: turn on <strong>Advanced Data Protection</strong> so most of your iCloud data (backups, photos, notes) is end-to-end encrypted, meaning even Apple cannot read it.</li><li><strong>External drives and USB sticks</strong>: format them encrypted (macOS Disk Utility &quot;APFS (Encrypted)&quot;, BitLocker To Go on Windows, or VeraCrypt on any system).</li><li><strong>Never</strong> keep seed phrases, private keys or recovery codes in cloud photos, notes or email, encrypted or not. Those go offline (see the wallet lessons).</li></ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Lose the key, lose the backup</p><p>An encrypted backup you cannot decrypt is worthless. Store the backup password in your password manager, and test a restore once.</p></aside>\n<h2 id=\"messages-who-can-read-them\">Messages: who can read them?</h2>\n<p><strong>End-to-end encryption</strong> (E2EE) means only the people in the conversation can read messages, not the company running the service.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>App</th><th>End-to-end encrypted?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Signal</strong></td><td>Yes, for everything, by default. The best choice for sensitive conversations.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>WhatsApp</strong>, <strong>iMessage</strong></td><td>Yes, for messages (check backups: turn on encrypted backups)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Telegram</strong></td><td><strong>No</strong>, not by default. Normal chats and all groups are readable by Telegram's servers; only one-to-one &quot;secret chats&quot; are E2EE</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Discord</strong>, <strong>Slack</strong>, email</td><td><strong>No</strong>, for messages. The provider (and anyone who breaches it) can read them</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Crypto teams often coordinate on Telegram and Discord. Never share keys, seed phrases, passwords or internal security details there.</p>\n<h2 id=\"your-traffic-on-public-wi-fi\">Your traffic on public Wi-Fi</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>HTTPS</strong> protects the content of each connection. Look for it, and never click through a certificate warning on public Wi-Fi: that warning is exactly what an interception attack looks like.</li><li><strong>Encrypted DNS</strong> (DNS over HTTPS, in your browser's security settings) hides which sites you look up from the local network.</li><li>A <strong>VPN</strong> encrypts everything between you and the VPN provider. It is useful on untrusted Wi-Fi, but it moves your trust to the VPN company rather than removing the need for trust. Use a reputable paid one, or your phone's hotspot instead.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"your-20-minute-encryption-checklist\">Your 20-minute encryption checklist</h2>\n<ol><li>Full-disk encryption on every laptop and desktop, recovery key in your password manager.</li><li>A 6+ digit passcode on your phone, with remote wipe turned on.</li><li>Encrypted backups (and one test restore).</li><li>Signal for sensitive conversations; nothing secret in Telegram, Discord or email.</li><li>Encrypted DNS in your browser, and your phone's hotspot or a VPN on public Wi-Fi.</li></ol>","quiz":[{"q":"Your laptop has FileVault turned off and is stolen. What can the thief read?","options":["Nothing without your password","Almost everything, by removing or booting the drive, including saved sessions and files","Only your photos"],"answer":1,"explain":"Without full-disk encryption, your login password does not protect the data on the disk."},{"q":"Which messaging setup is end-to-end encrypted by default?","options":["Signal","A Telegram group","A Discord server"],"answer":0,"explain":"Telegram groups and Discord messages can be read by the provider's servers."},{"q":"Where should your disk encryption recovery key be stored?","options":["In a text file on the same laptop","In your password manager or written down somewhere safe, away from the laptop","Posted in your team chat so others can help"],"answer":1,"explain":"A recovery key stored on the encrypted device is useless when you need it, and dangerous anywhere public."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Check that FileVault is on","file":"Terminal","lang":"bash","run":false,"prompt":"<p>On a Mac, ask the system whether full-disk encryption is on:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">fdesetup status</code></pre></div>\n<p>You want to see <code>FileVault is On.</code> (On Windows run <code>manage-bde -status</code> as administrator; on Linux run <code>lsblk -f</code>.)</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"fdesetup status","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*fdesetup\\s+status\\s*$","text":"Type <code>fdesetup status</code> (the FileVault setup tool, with the <code>status</code> command)."}],"explain":"<p>If it says <code>FileVault is Off.</code>, turn it on today. Encryption runs in the background and you can keep working. It is the single biggest protection for a lost or stolen laptop.</p>"},{"title":"Check a page is using HTTPS","file":"https-check.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Your web app should refuse to run sensitive features over plain HTTP. Write the check:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">if (location.protocol !== 'https:') {\n  console.log('Not secure: switch to HTTPS before signing in.');\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>(When you press <strong>Run it</strong> here it prints the warning, because the preview is not served over HTTPS.)</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"if (location.protocol !== 'https:') {\n  console.log('Not secure: switch to HTTPS before signing in.');\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*if\\s*\\(\\s*location\\.protocol\\s*!==\\s*['\"]https:['\"]\\s*\\)\\s*\\{\\s*$","text":"Line 1: <code>if (location.protocol !== 'https:') {</code> (note the colon inside <code>'https:'</code>)."},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*['\"]Not secure: switch to HTTPS before signing in\\.['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>console.log('Not secure: switch to HTTPS before signing in.');</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*\\}\\s*$","text":"Line 3: close the block with <code>}</code>."}],"explain":"<p>In real apps you do this on the server instead: redirect every HTTP request to HTTPS and send the HSTS header so browsers never try HTTP again. That is the next level's HTTPS lesson.</p>"}],"text":"Start with your own machine Your laptop holds everything an attacker wants: saved browser sessions, password manager caches, SSH keys, .env files, wallet files and seed phrase screenshots you forgot about. If it is stolen and the disk is not encrypted, the thief can remove the drive and read all of it without knowing your password. Full-disk encryption (FDE) fixes that. With it on, the disk is unreadable until you log in. System Built-in encryption Where to turn it on Check from the terminal macOS FileVault System Settings Privacy Security FileVault fdesetup status Windows BitLocker (Pro) or Device encryption (Home, on supported PCs) Settings Privacy security Device encryption manage-bde -status (in an admin terminal) Linux LUKS Usually chosen when installing ( Encrypt the new installation ) lsblk -f (look for crypto_LUKS ) When you turn it on you get a recovery key . Store it in your password manager or on paper somewhere safe, never only on the same laptop. Phones Modern iPhones and Android phones encrypt their storage automatically, but the encryption is only as strong as your passcode . A 4-digit PIN has 10,000 options. Use at least a 6-digit passcode , or better, an alphanumeric one, plus Face ID or a fingerprint for convenience. Turn on Find My (iPhone) or Find My Device (Android) so you can wipe a lost phone remotely. Keep the phone updated: most phone break-in tools rely on old, unpatched bugs. Backups Backups are often the weakest copy of your data: old, forgotten and unencrypted. Time Machine (Mac): tick Encrypt backup when you choose the disk. iPhone backups to a computer : tick Encrypt local backup . iCloud : turn on Advanced Data Protection so most of your iCloud data (backups, photos, notes) is end-to-end encrypted, meaning even Apple cannot read it. External drives and USB sticks : format them encrypted (macOS Disk Utility APFS (Encrypted) , BitLocker To Go on Windows, or VeraCrypt on any system). Never keep seed phrases, private keys or recovery codes in cloud photos, notes or email, encrypted or not. Those go offline (see the wallet lessons). Lose the key, lose the backup An encrypted backup you cannot decrypt is worthless. Store the backup password in your password manager, and test a restore once. Messages: who can read them? End-to-end encryption (E2EE) means only the people in the conversation can read messages, not the company running the service. App End-to-end encrypted? Signal Yes, for everything, by default. The best choice for sensitive conversations. WhatsApp , iMessage Yes, for messages (check backups: turn on encrypted backups) Telegram No , not by default. Normal chats and all groups are readable by Telegram's servers; only one-to-one secret chats are E2EE Discord , Slack , email No , for messages. The provider (and anyone who breaches it) can read them Crypto teams often coordinate on Telegram and Discord. Never share keys, seed phrases, passwords or internal security details there. Your traffic on public Wi-Fi HTTPS protects the content of each connection. Look for it, and never click through a certificate warning on public Wi-Fi: that warning is exactly what an interception attack looks like. Encrypted DNS (DNS over HTTPS, in your browser's security settings) hides which sites you look up from the local network. A VPN encrypts everything between you and the VPN provider. It is useful on untrusted Wi-Fi, but it moves your trust to the VPN company rather than removing the need for trust. Use a reputable paid one, or your phone's hotspot instead. Your 20-minute encryption checklist Full-disk encryption on every laptop and desktop, recovery key in your password manager. A 6+ digit passcode on your phone, with remote wipe turned on. Encrypted backups (and one test restore). Signal for sensitive conversations; nothing secret in Telegram, Discord or email. Encrypted DNS in your browser, and your phone's hotspot or a VPN on public Wi-Fi.","track":"encrypt"},{"id":"enc-files","title":"Encrypting files and folders with age","level":"beginner","minutes":35,"summary":"Encrypt a file before you send or store it, using age (the modern, simple tool) with public keys or a passphrase, plus where GPG, 7-Zip and encrypted drives fit.","html":"<h2 id=\"when-you-need-file-encryption\">When you need file encryption</h2>\n<p>Full-disk encryption protects files on <em>your</em> machine. The moment a file leaves it (attached to an email, uploaded to Google Drive, copied to a USB stick, sent to a teammate) it is on its own. Encrypt it first when it contains:</p>\n<ul><li>contracts, invoices, ID documents or customer data,</li><li>configuration with real secrets (a <code>.env</code> for a teammate),</li><li>backups of anything sensitive.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"meet-age\">Meet age</h2>\n<p><strong>age</strong> (pronounced like the word, with a hard &quot;g&quot;) is a small, modern encryption tool by Filippo Valsorda, a former Go security lead at Google. It does one job well and is hard to misuse. Install it from the <a href=\"#toolkit\">Install everything</a> page (<code>brew install age</code>, <code>winget install -e --id FiloSottile.age</code> or <code>sudo apt install -y age</code>).</p>\n<p>age uses a <strong>key pair</strong>, like a wallet:</p>\n<ul><li>your <strong>identity</strong> (private key), kept in a file only you have, and</li><li>your <strong>recipient</strong> (public key), starting <code>age1...</code>, which you can share freely. Anyone can encrypt <em>to</em> it; only your identity can decrypt.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">age-keygen -o key.txt\n# Public key: age1ql3z7hjy54pw3hyww5ayyfg7zqgvc7w3j2elw8zmrj2kg5sfn9aqmcac8p</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"passphrase-mode\">Passphrase mode</h2>\n<p>No key pair? <code>age -p</code> asks for a passphrase instead (and offers to generate a strong one):</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">age -p -o contract.pdf.age contract.pdf\nage -d -o contract.pdf contract.pdf.age</code></pre></div>\n<p>The passphrase is turned into a key with <strong>scrypt</strong>, a deliberately slow function, so guessing is expensive. Still, a weak passphrase can be guessed offline forever once the file is out there. Use a long, generated one, and send it through a <strong>different channel</strong> from the file (file by email, passphrase by Signal or your password manager's sharing feature).</p>\n<h2 id=\"encrypt-a-whole-folder-for-backup\">Encrypt a whole folder for backup</h2>\n<p>age encrypts one stream of data, so pack a folder first with <code>tar</code> and pipe it straight into age. The unencrypted archive is never written to disk.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"other-tools-you-will-meet\">Other tools you will meet</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Use it when</th><th>Watch out</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>GPG</strong> (GnuPG)</td><td>Signing Git commits and software releases, or when someone insists on PGP email</td><td>Complicated and easy to misuse; prefer age for plain file encryption</td></tr><tr><td><strong>7-Zip</strong> with AES-256</td><td>Sending an encrypted file to a non-technical person</td><td>Choose AES-256, not the old &quot;ZipCrypto&quot;, which is broken. Tick &quot;Encrypt file names&quot;</td></tr><tr><td><strong>VeraCrypt</strong> or an encrypted APFS/BitLocker drive</td><td>A whole USB stick or external disk</td><td>Unmount it when you are done; it is only protected while locked</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Your password manager</strong></td><td>Sharing a single password, API key or recovery code</td><td>Use its secure share feature instead of chat or email</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"habits-that-matter-more-than-the-tool\">Habits that matter more than the tool</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Never put the key next to the file.</strong> An encrypted zip with the password in the same email is not encrypted.</li><li><strong>Avoid plaintext copies.</strong> Deleted files on SSDs can linger; it is better never to write the plaintext (use pipes, as above) and to rely on full-disk encryption.</li><li><strong>Back up your identity file</strong>, or nothing you encrypted to it can ever be opened again.</li><li><strong>Treat encrypted secrets as having a lifetime</strong>: if a <code>.age</code> file of API keys might have leaked along with its identity, rotate the keys.</li></ul>","quiz":[{"q":"You share an age public key (age1...) with a colleague. What can they do with it?","options":["Encrypt files that only you can decrypt","Decrypt your files","Log in as you"],"answer":0,"explain":"Public keys encrypt; only the matching private identity decrypts."},{"q":"Why pipe tar straight into age instead of creating project.tar.gz first?","options":["It is faster to type","The unencrypted archive is never written to disk, so no plaintext copy is left behind","age cannot read files"],"answer":1,"explain":"Avoiding plaintext copies is easier than trying to securely delete them later."},{"q":"You email an encrypted zip and put the password in the same email. How secure is it?","options":["Very, the zip is AES-256","Barely: anyone who gets the email gets both the file and the password","Secure as long as the subject line is vague"],"answer":1,"explain":"Send the secret through a different channel from the file."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Encrypt and decrypt a file with age","file":"Terminal","lang":"bash","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Three commands. Make a key pair, encrypt <code>notes.txt</code> to a recipient listed in <code>recipients.txt</code> (one public key per line), then decrypt it with your identity:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">age-keygen -o key.txt\nage -R recipients.txt -o notes.txt.age notes.txt\nage -d -i key.txt -o notes.txt notes.txt.age</code></pre></div>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"age-keygen -o key.txt\nage -R recipients.txt -o notes.txt.age notes.txt\nage -d -i key.txt -o notes.txt notes.txt.age","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*age-keygen\\s+-o\\s+key\\.txt\\s*$","text":"Line 1: <code>age-keygen -o key.txt</code> writes a new identity to <code>key.txt</code> and prints its public key."},{"re":"^\\s*age\\s+-R\\s+recipients\\.txt\\s+-o\\s+notes\\.txt\\.age\\s+notes\\.txt\\s*$","text":"Line 2: <code>age -R recipients.txt -o notes.txt.age notes.txt</code> (<code>-R</code> = a file of recipients, <code>-o</code> = the output file, then the input)."},{"re":"^\\s*age\\s+-d\\s+-i\\s+key\\.txt\\s+-o\\s+notes\\.txt\\s+notes\\.txt\\.age\\s*$","text":"Line 3: <code>age -d -i key.txt -o notes.txt notes.txt.age</code> (<code>-d</code> decrypt, <code>-i</code> your identity file)."}],"explain":"<p><code>-r age1...</code> encrypts to a single public key typed on the command line; <code>-R file</code> reads several, so one file can be opened by everyone on your team. age can also encrypt to people's existing SSH public keys (<code>-R ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub</code>), which is handy for developers.</p>"},{"title":"Encrypted folder backup in one line","file":"Terminal","lang":"bash","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Archive the <code>project</code> folder and encrypt it to your recipients in one pipeline:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">tar -czf - project | age -R recipients.txt &gt; project.tar.gz.age</code></pre></div>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"tar -czf - project | age -R recipients.txt > project.tar.gz.age","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*tar\\s+-czf\\s+-\\s+project\\s*\\|","text":"Start with <code>tar -czf - project |</code> (<code>c</code> create, <code>z</code> gzip, <code>f -</code> write to standard output instead of a file)."},{"re":"\\|\\s*age\\s+-R\\s+recipients\\.txt\\s*>\\s*project\\.tar\\.gz\\.age\\s*$","text":"Then <code>| age -R recipients.txt &gt; project.tar.gz.age</code> (the <code>&gt;</code> saves age's output to a file)."}],"explain":"<p>To restore: <code>age -d -i key.txt project.tar.gz.age | tar -xzf -</code>. Upload the <code>.age</code> file anywhere (cloud storage, a second drive): without your identity file it is just random-looking bytes.</p>"}],"text":"When you need file encryption Full-disk encryption protects files on your machine. The moment a file leaves it (attached to an email, uploaded to Google Drive, copied to a USB stick, sent to a teammate) it is on its own. Encrypt it first when it contains: contracts, invoices, ID documents or customer data, configuration with real secrets (a .env for a teammate), backups of anything sensitive. Meet age age (pronounced like the word, with a hard g ) is a small, modern encryption tool by Filippo Valsorda, a former Go security lead at Google. It does one job well and is hard to misuse. Install it from the Install everything page ( brew install age , winget install -e --id FiloSottile.age or sudo apt install -y age ). age uses a key pair , like a wallet: your identity (private key), kept in a file only you have, and your recipient (public key), starting age1... , which you can share freely. Anyone can encrypt to it; only your identity can decrypt. age-keygen -o key.txt # Public key: age1ql3z7hjy54pw3hyww5ayyfg7zqgvc7w3j2elw8zmrj2kg5sfn9aqmcac8p Passphrase mode No key pair? age -p asks for a passphrase instead (and offers to generate a strong one): age -p -o contract.pdf.age contract.pdf age -d -o contract.pdf contract.pdf.age The passphrase is turned into a key with scrypt , a deliberately slow function, so guessing is expensive. Still, a weak passphrase can be guessed offline forever once the file is out there. Use a long, generated one, and send it through a different channel from the file (file by email, passphrase by Signal or your password manager's sharing feature). Encrypt a whole folder for backup age encrypts one stream of data, so pack a folder first with tar and pipe it straight into age. The unencrypted archive is never written to disk. Other tools you will meet Tool Use it when Watch out GPG (GnuPG) Signing Git commits and software releases, or when someone insists on PGP email Complicated and easy to misuse; prefer age for plain file encryption 7-Zip with AES-256 Sending an encrypted file to a non-technical person Choose AES-256, not the old ZipCrypto , which is broken. Tick Encrypt file names VeraCrypt or an encrypted APFS/BitLocker drive A whole USB stick or external disk Unmount it when you are done; it is only protected while locked Your password manager Sharing a single password, API key or recovery code Use its secure share feature instead of chat or email Habits that matter more than the tool Never put the key next to the file. An encrypted zip with the password in the same email is not encrypted. Avoid plaintext copies. Deleted files on SSDs can linger; it is better never to write the plaintext (use pipes, as above) and to rely on full-disk encryption. Back up your identity file , or nothing you encrypted to it can ever be opened again. Treat encrypted secrets as having a lifetime : if a .age file of API keys might have leaked along with its identity, rotate the keys.","track":"encrypt"},{"id":"enc-https","title":"HTTPS and TLS done right","level":"intermediate","minutes":35,"summary":"What TLS actually guarantees, forcing HTTPS with redirects and HSTS, secure cookies, encrypting connections between your own services, and watching certificate logs for attacks on your domain.","html":"<h2 id=\"what-tls-gives-you\">What TLS gives you</h2>\n<p>HTTPS is HTTP inside <strong>TLS</strong> (Transport Layer Security). Every HTTPS connection gets three guarantees:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Confidentiality</strong>: nobody on the network can read the traffic.</li><li><strong>Integrity</strong>: nobody can change it in transit without the connection failing.</li><li><strong>Authentication</strong>: the server proved it owns the domain, using a <strong>certificate</strong> signed by a certificate authority (CA) your browser trusts.</li></ol>\n<p>TLS uses both kinds of encryption: a public-key <strong>handshake</strong> (with forward secrecy, so recording traffic today does not help an attacker who steals the server's key later) agrees a fresh symmetric key, and then fast <strong>AES-GCM</strong> or <strong>ChaCha20-Poly1305</strong> encrypts the actual data. Use <strong>TLS 1.3</strong> where you can, TLS 1.2 at minimum, and nothing older.</p>\n<h2 id=\"getting-certificates\">Getting certificates</h2>\n<ul><li>Hosting platforms (Vercel, Netlify, Cloudflare, GitHub Pages) issue and renew certificates automatically. Just make sure HTTPS is enforced.</li><li>On your own server, <strong>Let's Encrypt</strong> gives free certificates, and tools like <strong>Caddy</strong> (automatic) or <strong>certbot</strong> renew them for you. Certificates are short-lived on purpose, so automate renewal and alert if it fails.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"never-allow-plain-http\">Never allow plain HTTP</h2>\n<p>Serving HTTPS is not enough if the first request goes out over HTTP. On café Wi-Fi an attacker can intercept that first request and keep you on an HTTP copy of the site (an &quot;SSL stripping&quot; attack). Two defences:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Redirect</strong> every <code>http://</code> request to <code>https://</code> (status 301 or 308).</li><li>Send the <strong>HSTS</strong> header (HTTP Strict Transport Security). It tells the browser: for this domain, <em>never</em> use HTTP again, and never let the user click through a certificate warning.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"http\"><pre><code class=\"language-http\">Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains; preload</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>max-age</code> is in seconds (two years here). <code>includeSubDomains</code> covers every subdomain. <code>preload</code> lets you submit the domain to the browsers' built-in list at hstspreload.org, so even the very first visit is protected.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"check-any-site-from-the-terminal\">Check any site from the terminal</h2>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"cookies-must-be-https-only-too\">Cookies must be HTTPS-only too</h2>\n<p>A session cookie sent once over HTTP can be stolen. Mark every session cookie:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"http\"><pre><code class=\"language-http\">Set-Cookie: session=abc123; Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax; Path=/</code></pre></div>\n<ul><li><code>Secure</code>: only ever sent over HTTPS.</li><li><code>HttpOnly</code>: JavaScript cannot read it, so an XSS bug cannot steal it.</li><li><code>SameSite=Lax</code>: not sent on most cross-site requests, which blocks most CSRF.</li></ul>\n<p>Also fix <strong>mixed content</strong>: an HTTPS page that loads a script over HTTP undoes everything, because that script can be swapped on the network.</p>\n<h2 id=\"encrypt-traffic-between-your-own-services\">Encrypt traffic between your own services</h2>\n<p>&quot;It is inside our network&quot; is not a security boundary. Encrypt internal connections too:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Databases</strong>: require TLS and verify the certificate. For Postgres, connection strings take <code>sslmode=verify-full</code>, which checks both encryption and the server's identity.</li><li><strong>RPC and APIs</strong>: use <code>https://</code> and <code>wss://</code> endpoints, never plain HTTP, especially for anything that signs or submits transactions.</li><li><strong>Service to service</strong>: mutual TLS (both sides present certificates) is standard in larger systems and service meshes.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"watch-for-attacks-on-your-domain\">Watch for attacks on your domain</h2>\n<p>Every publicly trusted certificate is recorded in public <strong>Certificate Transparency</strong> logs. Search yours at crt.sh. Two uses:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Detect mis-issuance</strong>: a certificate for your domain you did not request means someone may control your DNS or is attacking a CA.</li><li><strong>Spot phishing</strong>: look for lookalike domains (<code>launchpad-claim.example</code>, <code>1aunchpad.example</code>) getting certificates, a common first step before a phishing campaign.</li></ul>\n<p>You can also publish a <strong>CAA</strong> DNS record that says which CAs may issue certificates for your domain:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">launchpad.example.  CAA  0 issue &quot;letsencrypt.org&quot;</code></pre></div>","quiz":[{"q":"What does the HSTS header tell a browser?","options":["To cache the page for two years","To only ever connect to this domain over HTTPS, and to refuse to let the user bypass certificate errors","To encrypt cookies with AES"],"answer":1,"explain":"HSTS closes the first-request HTTP gap that SSL stripping attacks exploit."},{"q":"Why mark session cookies HttpOnly as well as Secure?","options":["Secure keeps them off plain HTTP; HttpOnly stops JavaScript (and so XSS) from reading them","HttpOnly makes cookies load faster","They mean the same thing"],"answer":0,"explain":"Each flag blocks a different way of stealing the cookie."},{"q":"Your app talks to its database inside a private cloud network. Should that connection use TLS?","options":["No, private networks are safe","Yes: assume the internal network can be breached, and verify the database's certificate","Only for production"],"answer":1,"explain":"Treating the inside of the network as trusted is how one foothold becomes a full breach."},{"q":"What can Certificate Transparency logs warn you about?","options":["Slow page loads","Certificates issued for your domain or lookalike domains, which can signal hijacking or phishing","Expired passwords"],"answer":1,"explain":"Every public certificate is logged, so attackers' certificates are visible too."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Send the HSTS header from Express","file":"server.js","lang":"js","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Add a middleware that sends HSTS on every response. Four lines:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">app.use((req, res, next) =&gt; {\n  res.setHeader('Strict-Transport-Security', 'max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains; preload');\n  next();\n});</code></pre></div>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"app.use((req, res, next) => {\n  res.setHeader('Strict-Transport-Security', 'max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains; preload');\n  next();\n});","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*app\\.use\\(\\s*\\(\\s*req\\s*,\\s*res\\s*,\\s*next\\s*\\)\\s*=>\\s*\\{\\s*$","text":"Line 1: <code>app.use((req, res, next) =&gt; {</code> (a middleware runs for every request)."},{"re":"res\\.setHeader\\(\\s*['\"]Strict-Transport-Security['\"]\\s*,\\s*['\"]max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains; preload['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>res.setHeader('Strict-Transport-Security', 'max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains; preload');</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*next\\(\\s*\\)\\s*;?\\s*$","text":"Line 3: <code>next();</code> passes the request on to your routes."},{"re":"^\\s*\\}\\s*\\)\\s*;?\\s*$","text":"Line 4: close with <code>});</code>"}],"explain":"<p>In practice the <strong>helmet</strong> package sets HSTS and several other security headers in one line (<code>app.use(helmet())</code>). Start with a short <code>max-age</code> (say a day) while testing, because once browsers have seen a long HSTS header, they will refuse HTTP for that long. Only add <code>preload</code> when every subdomain is ready for HTTPS for good.</p>"},{"title":"Look for HSTS on a live site","file":"Terminal","lang":"bash","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Fetch only the response headers (<code>-I</code>), silently (<code>-s</code>), and filter for the HSTS header:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">curl -sI https://launchpad.example | grep -i strict-transport-security</code></pre></div>\n<p>No output means the site does not send HSTS.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"curl -sI https://launchpad.example | grep -i strict-transport-security","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*curl\\s+-sI\\s+https://launchpad\\.example\\s*\\|","text":"Start with <code>curl -sI https://launchpad.example |</code>"},{"re":"\\|\\s*grep\\s+-i\\s+strict-transport-security\\s*$","text":"Then <code>| grep -i strict-transport-security</code> (<code>-i</code> ignores upper and lower case)."}],"explain":"<p>Try it against your own sites and a few big ones. For a full report on certificate, protocol versions and ciphers, run your domain through the free SSL Labs server test (ssllabs.com/ssltest).</p>"}],"text":"What TLS gives you HTTPS is HTTP inside TLS (Transport Layer Security). Every HTTPS connection gets three guarantees: Confidentiality : nobody on the network can read the traffic. Integrity : nobody can change it in transit without the connection failing. Authentication : the server proved it owns the domain, using a certificate signed by a certificate authority (CA) your browser trusts. TLS uses both kinds of encryption: a public-key handshake (with forward secrecy, so recording traffic today does not help an attacker who steals the server's key later) agrees a fresh symmetric key, and then fast AES-GCM or ChaCha20-Poly1305 encrypts the actual data. Use TLS 1.3 where you can, TLS 1.2 at minimum, and nothing older. Getting certificates Hosting platforms (Vercel, Netlify, Cloudflare, GitHub Pages) issue and renew certificates automatically. Just make sure HTTPS is enforced. On your own server, Let's Encrypt gives free certificates, and tools like Caddy (automatic) or certbot renew them for you. Certificates are short-lived on purpose, so automate renewal and alert if it fails. Never allow plain HTTP Serving HTTPS is not enough if the first request goes out over HTTP. On café Wi-Fi an attacker can intercept that first request and keep you on an HTTP copy of the site (an SSL stripping attack). Two defences: Redirect every http:// request to https:// (status 301 or 308). Send the HSTS header (HTTP Strict Transport Security). It tells the browser: for this domain, never use HTTP again, and never let the user click through a certificate warning. Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains; preload max-age is in seconds (two years here). includeSubDomains covers every subdomain. preload lets you submit the domain to the browsers' built-in list at hstspreload.org, so even the very first visit is protected. Check any site from the terminal Cookies must be HTTPS-only too A session cookie sent once over HTTP can be stolen. Mark every session cookie: Set-Cookie: session=abc123; Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax; Path=/ Secure : only ever sent over HTTPS. HttpOnly : JavaScript cannot read it, so an XSS bug cannot steal it. SameSite=Lax : not sent on most cross-site requests, which blocks most CSRF. Also fix mixed content : an HTTPS page that loads a script over HTTP undoes everything, because that script can be swapped on the network. Encrypt traffic between your own services It is inside our network is not a security boundary. Encrypt internal connections too: Databases : require TLS and verify the certificate. For Postgres, connection strings take sslmode=verify-full , which checks both encryption and the server's identity. RPC and APIs : use https:// and wss:// endpoints, never plain HTTP, especially for anything that signs or submits transactions. Service to service : mutual TLS (both sides present certificates) is standard in larger systems and service meshes. Watch for attacks on your domain Every publicly trusted certificate is recorded in public Certificate Transparency logs. Search yours at crt.sh. Two uses: Detect mis-issuance : a certificate for your domain you did not request means someone may control your DNS or is attacking a CA. Spot phishing : look for lookalike domains ( launchpad-claim.example , 1aunchpad.example ) getting certificates, a common first step before a phishing campaign. You can also publish a CAA DNS record that says which CAs may issue certificates for your domain: launchpad.example. CAA 0 issue letsencrypt.org","track":"encrypt"},{"id":"enc-app-data","title":"Encrypting sensitive data in your app (AES-GCM)","level":"intermediate","minutes":45,"summary":"Decide what to hash, what to encrypt and what to leave alone, then encrypt fields like phone numbers and users' API tokens with AES-256-GCM in Node, with unique IVs, authentication tags and keys kept out of the database.","html":"<h2 id=\"hash-encrypt-or-neither\">Hash, encrypt, or neither?</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Data</th><th>Do this</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Passwords</td><td><strong>Hash</strong> with Argon2id (or bcrypt)</td><td>You never need the password back, only to check it</td></tr><tr><td>Data you must read back: phone numbers, addresses, date of birth, KYC documents, users' OAuth refresh tokens and API keys, private notes</td><td><strong>Encrypt</strong> in your application (this lesson)</td><td>A stolen database or backup should be useless without a key held elsewhere</td></tr><tr><td>Public data: product names, prices, public wallet addresses</td><td>Neither</td><td>Encryption costs complexity; spend it where it protects someone</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>&quot;But my database is already encrypted at rest.&quot; Disk-level encryption in your cloud database protects against someone walking off with the physical disk. It does <strong>not</strong> help against SQL injection, a leaked database password, a too-wide backup bucket or a curious insider, because the database decrypts for anyone who can query it. <strong>Application-level</strong> encryption keeps those fields ciphertext everywhere except inside your app's memory.</p>\n<h2 id=\"aes-256-gcm-in-one-picture\">AES-256-GCM in one picture</h2>\n<p><strong>AES-GCM</strong> is an <strong>authenticated</strong> cipher (AEAD): it encrypts <em>and</em> adds a 16-byte <strong>authentication tag</strong>, so any tampering with the ciphertext is detected on decryption. You need:</p>\n<ul><li>a <strong>key</strong>: 32 random bytes for AES-256, stored outside the database,</li><li>a fresh <strong>IV</strong> (also called a nonce) for every encryption: 12 random bytes, never reused with the same key,</li><li>and you get back the <strong>ciphertext</strong> plus the <strong>tag</strong>.</li></ul>\n<p>Store <code>iv + tag + ciphertext</code> together (the IV and tag are not secret). Reusing an IV with the same key is catastrophic: it can reveal plaintexts and let attackers forge messages (the expert lesson shows exactly how).</p>\n<h2 id=\"try-it-in-the-browser-first\">Try it in the browser first</h2>\n<p>The Web Crypto API does AES-GCM natively. Run this in the Playground:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-try=\"1\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const key = await crypto.subtle.generateKey({ name: 'AES-GCM', length: 256 }, true, ['encrypt', 'decrypt']);\nconst iv = crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(12));\nconst data = new TextEncoder().encode('+1 415 555 0142');\n\nconst ciphertext = await crypto.subtle.encrypt({ name: 'AES-GCM', iv }, key, data);\nconsole.log('ciphertext bytes:', new Uint8Array(ciphertext));\n\nconst back = await crypto.subtle.decrypt({ name: 'AES-GCM', iv }, key, ciphertext);\nconsole.log('decrypted:', new TextDecoder().decode(back));</code></pre></div>\n<p>Change one byte of <code>ciphertext</code> before decrypting and you get an error instead of garbage. That is the authentication tag doing its job.</p>\n<h2 id=\"encrypting-a-field-in-node\">Encrypting a field in Node</h2>\n<p>On the server, Node's built-in <code>crypto</code> module does the same. This function returns one Base64 string holding everything needed to decrypt later.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"where-the-key-lives\">Where the key lives</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\"># Generate a key once\nopenssl rand -base64 32</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const key = Buffer.from(process.env.FIELD_KEY, 'base64'); // 32 bytes, from your secret store</code></pre></div>\n<ul><li>Load it from your host's secret store or a secrets manager (next lesson), <strong>never</strong> from code or the database.</li><li>Use different keys for staging and production.</li><li>For larger systems, do not hold the master key in the app at all: use <strong>envelope encryption</strong> with a KMS (the advanced lesson), so every decryption is permission-checked and logged.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"two-upgrades-professionals-add\">Two upgrades professionals add</h2>\n<p><strong>1. Bind the ciphertext to its row.</strong> An attacker who can write to your database could copy Alice's encrypted phone number into Bob's row. Add <strong>associated data</strong> (AAD), such as the user ID, which is authenticated but not encrypted:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">cipher.setAAD(Buffer.from(userId));   // when encrypting\ndecipher.setAAD(Buffer.from(userId)); // when decrypting: a swapped value now fails</code></pre></div>\n<p><strong>2. Search without decrypting.</strong> You cannot <code>WHERE phone = ?</code> on random-looking ciphertext. Store a <strong>blind index</strong> next to it: an HMAC of the normalised value with a <em>separate</em> key, which you can look up exactly without revealing the value.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const phoneIndex = createHmac('sha256', indexKey).update(normalise(phone)).digest('hex');</code></pre></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Use a library in production</p><p>Writing these helpers teaches you how it works. In production, prefer a well-reviewed library that handles formats, key rotation and AAD for you: libsodium (<code>crypto_secretbox</code> or XChaCha20-Poly1305), Google's Tink, or your cloud provider's encryption SDK.</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"Your cloud database has &quot;encryption at rest&quot; turned on. An attacker finds a SQL injection bug. What do they get?","options":["Only ciphertext","Readable data, because the database decrypts for anyone who can query it; only application-level encryption keeps sensitive fields as ciphertext","Nothing, encryption at rest blocks queries"],"answer":1,"explain":"Disk encryption protects disks, not queries."},{"q":"Why must every AES-GCM encryption use a new IV with the same key?","options":["To make the ciphertext shorter","Reusing an IV can expose plaintexts and lets attackers forge valid ciphertexts","Because IVs are secret and get used up"],"answer":1,"explain":"IVs are not secret, but they must be unique per key."},{"q":"Which should you hash rather than encrypt?","options":["User passwords","Users' phone numbers","Users' OAuth refresh tokens"],"answer":0,"explain":"You need phone numbers and tokens back; you never need the password itself back."},{"q":"What does associated data (AAD) prevent?","options":["Brute-force attacks on the key","Someone moving a valid ciphertext from one user's row to another's without detection","IV reuse"],"answer":1,"explain":"AAD binds a ciphertext to its context, so a swapped value fails authentication."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Write encrypt()","file":"field-crypto.js","lang":"js","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write the encryption half. Seven lines:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">import { randomBytes, createCipheriv, createDecipheriv } from 'node:crypto';\n\nexport function encrypt(plaintext, key) {\n  const iv = randomBytes(12);\n  const cipher = createCipheriv('aes-256-gcm', key, iv);\n  const data = Buffer.concat([cipher.update(plaintext, 'utf8'), cipher.final()]);\n  return Buffer.concat([iv, cipher.getAuthTag(), data]).toString('base64');\n}</code></pre></div>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"import { randomBytes, createCipheriv, createDecipheriv } from 'node:crypto';\n\nexport function encrypt(plaintext, key) {\n  const iv = randomBytes(12);\n  const cipher = createCipheriv('aes-256-gcm', key, iv);\n  const data = Buffer.concat([cipher.update(plaintext, 'utf8'), cipher.final()]);\n  return Buffer.concat([iv, cipher.getAuthTag(), data]).toString('base64');\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"import\\s*\\{\\s*randomBytes\\s*,\\s*createCipheriv\\s*,\\s*createDecipheriv\\s*\\}\\s*from\\s*['\"]node:crypto['\"]","text":"Line 1: <code>import { randomBytes, createCipheriv, createDecipheriv } from 'node:crypto';</code>"},{"re":"export\\s+function\\s+encrypt\\(\\s*plaintext\\s*,\\s*key\\s*\\)\\s*\\{","text":"Line 2: <code>export function encrypt(plaintext, key) {</code>"},{"re":"const\\s+iv\\s*=\\s*randomBytes\\(\\s*12\\s*\\)","text":"A fresh random 12-byte IV every single time: <code>const iv = randomBytes(12);</code>"},{"re":"const\\s+cipher\\s*=\\s*createCipheriv\\(\\s*['\"]aes-256-gcm['\"]\\s*,\\s*key\\s*,\\s*iv\\s*\\)","text":"<code>const cipher = createCipheriv('aes-256-gcm', key, iv);</code>"},{"re":"const\\s+data\\s*=\\s*Buffer\\.concat\\(\\s*\\[\\s*cipher\\.update\\(\\s*plaintext\\s*,\\s*['\"]utf8['\"]\\s*\\)\\s*,\\s*cipher\\.final\\(\\s*\\)\\s*\\]\\s*\\)","text":"<code>const data = Buffer.concat([cipher.update(plaintext, 'utf8'), cipher.final()]);</code> (<code>update</code> encrypts, <code>final</code> finishes)."},{"re":"return\\s+Buffer\\.concat\\(\\s*\\[\\s*iv\\s*,\\s*cipher\\.getAuthTag\\(\\s*\\)\\s*,\\s*data\\s*\\]\\s*\\)\\.toString\\(\\s*['\"]base64['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"Pack IV (12 bytes), tag (16 bytes) and ciphertext together: <code>return Buffer.concat([iv, cipher.getAuthTag(), data]).toString('base64');</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*\\}\\s*$","text":"Close the function with <code>}</code>."}],"explain":"<p>The tag is only available after <code>cipher.final()</code>, which is why <code>getAuthTag()</code> comes in the return line. The output looks like <code>k3Jd...==</code> and is safe to store in a normal text column.</p>"},{"title":"Write decrypt()","file":"field-crypto.js","lang":"js","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Now reverse it: split the bytes back into IV (first 12), tag (next 16) and ciphertext (the rest), give the tag to the decipher, and decrypt. If anything was tampered with, <code>final()</code> throws.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">export function decrypt(payload, key) {\n  const buf = Buffer.from(payload, 'base64');\n  const decipher = createDecipheriv('aes-256-gcm', key, buf.subarray(0, 12));\n  decipher.setAuthTag(buf.subarray(12, 28));\n  return Buffer.concat([decipher.update(buf.subarray(28)), decipher.final()]).toString('utf8');\n}</code></pre></div>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"export function decrypt(payload, key) {\n  const buf = Buffer.from(payload, 'base64');\n  const decipher = createDecipheriv('aes-256-gcm', key, buf.subarray(0, 12));\n  decipher.setAuthTag(buf.subarray(12, 28));\n  return Buffer.concat([decipher.update(buf.subarray(28)), decipher.final()]).toString('utf8');\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"export\\s+function\\s+decrypt\\(\\s*payload\\s*,\\s*key\\s*\\)\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>export function decrypt(payload, key) {</code>"},{"re":"const\\s+buf\\s*=\\s*Buffer\\.from\\(\\s*payload\\s*,\\s*['\"]base64['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>const buf = Buffer.from(payload, 'base64');</code>"},{"re":"const\\s+decipher\\s*=\\s*createDecipheriv\\(\\s*['\"]aes-256-gcm['\"]\\s*,\\s*key\\s*,\\s*buf\\.subarray\\(\\s*0\\s*,\\s*12\\s*\\)\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3: the IV is the first 12 bytes: <code>createDecipheriv('aes-256-gcm', key, buf.subarray(0, 12))</code>"},{"re":"decipher\\.setAuthTag\\(\\s*buf\\.subarray\\(\\s*12\\s*,\\s*28\\s*\\)\\s*\\)","text":"Line 4: the tag is bytes 12 to 28: <code>decipher.setAuthTag(buf.subarray(12, 28));</code>"},{"re":"return\\s+Buffer\\.concat\\(\\s*\\[\\s*decipher\\.update\\(\\s*buf\\.subarray\\(\\s*28\\s*\\)\\s*\\)\\s*,\\s*decipher\\.final\\(\\s*\\)\\s*\\]\\s*\\)\\.toString\\(\\s*['\"]utf8['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"Line 5: decrypt everything from byte 28 on: <code>return Buffer.concat([decipher.update(buf.subarray(28)), decipher.final()]).toString('utf8');</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*\\}\\s*$","text":"Close the function with <code>}</code>."}],"explain":"<p>Never catch and ignore the error from <code>final()</code>: it means the data or the tag was changed, or the wrong key was used. Treat it as a security event, not a glitch.</p>"}],"text":"Hash, encrypt, or neither? Data Do this Why Passwords Hash with Argon2id (or bcrypt) You never need the password back, only to check it Data you must read back: phone numbers, addresses, date of birth, KYC documents, users' OAuth refresh tokens and API keys, private notes Encrypt in your application (this lesson) A stolen database or backup should be useless without a key held elsewhere Public data: product names, prices, public wallet addresses Neither Encryption costs complexity; spend it where it protects someone But my database is already encrypted at rest. Disk-level encryption in your cloud database protects against someone walking off with the physical disk. It does not help against SQL injection, a leaked database password, a too-wide backup bucket or a curious insider, because the database decrypts for anyone who can query it. Application-level encryption keeps those fields ciphertext everywhere except inside your app's memory. AES-256-GCM in one picture AES-GCM is an authenticated cipher (AEAD): it encrypts and adds a 16-byte authentication tag , so any tampering with the ciphertext is detected on decryption. You need: a key : 32 random bytes for AES-256, stored outside the database, a fresh IV (also called a nonce) for every encryption: 12 random bytes, never reused with the same key, and you get back the ciphertext plus the tag . Store iv + tag + ciphertext together (the IV and tag are not secret). Reusing an IV with the same key is catastrophic: it can reveal plaintexts and let attackers forge messages (the expert lesson shows exactly how). Try it in the browser first The Web Crypto API does AES-GCM natively. Run this in the Playground: const key = await crypto.subtle.generateKey({ name: 'AES-GCM', length: 256 }, true, ['encrypt', 'decrypt']); const iv = crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(12)); const data = new TextEncoder().encode('+1 415 555 0142'); const ciphertext = await crypto.subtle.encrypt({ name: 'AES-GCM', iv }, key, data); console.log('ciphertext bytes:', new Uint8Array(ciphertext)); const back = await crypto.subtle.decrypt({ name: 'AES-GCM', iv }, key, ciphertext); console.log('decrypted:', new TextDecoder().decode(back)); Change one byte of ciphertext before decrypting and you get an error instead of garbage. That is the authentication tag doing its job. Encrypting a field in Node On the server, Node's built-in crypto module does the same. This function returns one Base64 string holding everything needed to decrypt later. Where the key lives # Generate a key once openssl rand -base64 32 const key = Buffer.from(process.env.FIELD_KEY, 'base64'); // 32 bytes, from your secret store Load it from your host's secret store or a secrets manager (next lesson), never from code or the database. Use different keys for staging and production. For larger systems, do not hold the master key in the app at all: use envelope encryption with a KMS (the advanced lesson), so every decryption is permission-checked and logged. Two upgrades professionals add 1. Bind the ciphertext to its row. An attacker who can write to your database could copy Alice's encrypted phone number into Bob's row. Add associated data (AAD), such as the user ID, which is authenticated but not encrypted: cipher.setAAD(Buffer.from(userId)); // when encrypting decipher.setAAD(Buffer.from(userId)); // when decrypting: a swapped value now fails 2. Search without decrypting. You cannot WHERE phone = ? on random-looking ciphertext. Store a blind index next to it: an HMAC of the normalised value with a separate key, which you can look up exactly without revealing the value. const phoneIndex = createHmac('sha256', indexKey).update(normalise(phone)).digest('hex'); Use a library in production Writing these helpers teaches you how it works. In production, prefer a well-reviewed library that handles formats, key rotation and AAD for you: libsodium ( crypto_secretbox or XChaCha20-Poly1305), Google's Tink, or your cloud provider's encryption SDK.","track":"encrypt"},{"id":"enc-secrets","title":"Encrypted secrets: SOPS, secret managers and CI","level":"intermediate","minutes":35,"summary":"Stop passing .env files around. Keep secrets encrypted in Git with SOPS and age, inject them at run time from a secret manager, and give CI short-lived access instead of long-lived keys.","html":"<h2 id=\"the-env-problem\">The .env problem</h2>\n<p>Every team starts the same way: a <code>.env</code> file with the database URL, API keys and an RPC key, shared in a chat message &quot;just this once&quot;. Copies end up in Slack history, on old laptops and in backups. When someone leaves or a laptop is stolen, nobody knows which keys to rotate.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"#lesson-keys-secrets-in-code\">secrets lesson</a> showed how to keep secrets <strong>out of Git</strong>. This lesson is about where they live instead, and how to keep them <strong>encrypted</strong> until the moment your app needs them.</p>\n<h2 id=\"three-good-options\">Three good options</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>How it works</th><th>Best for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Secret manager</strong> (AWS Secrets Manager, Google Secret Manager, Doppler, Infisical, 1Password)</td><td>Secrets live encrypted in a service; your app or CLI fetches them at run time with its own identity; every read is logged</td><td>Production and teams</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Encrypted files in Git</strong> with <strong>SOPS</strong> + age (or a KMS)</td><td>The config file is committed, but every secret value inside it is encrypted; only people or machines with a key can decrypt</td><td>Small teams, infrastructure repos, GitOps</td></tr><tr><td><strong>CI platform secrets</strong> (GitHub Actions secrets and environments)</td><td>Stored encrypted by the platform and injected into jobs</td><td>Deploy pipelines</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"sops-encrypted-config-you-can-still-review\">SOPS: encrypted config you can still review</h2>\n<p><strong>SOPS</strong> (Secrets OPerationS) encrypts the <em>values</em> in a YAML, JSON or <code>.env</code> file but leaves the <em>keys</em> readable. Diffs stay meaningful (&quot;someone changed <code>RPC_URL</code>&quot;) without revealing anything.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\"><pre><code class=\"language-yaml\"># secrets.enc.yaml (safe to commit)\nRPC_URL: ENC[AES256_GCM,data:7Qmq...,iv:...,tag:...,type:str]\nDATABASE_URL: ENC[AES256_GCM,data:Lw0f...,iv:...,tag:...,type:str]\nsops:\n  age:\n    - recipient: age1ql3z7hjy54pw3hyww5ayyfg7zqgvc7w3j2elw8zmrj2kg5sfn9aqmcac8p</code></pre></div>\n<p>Under the hood it is envelope encryption: a random data key encrypts the values with AES-256-GCM, and that data key is encrypted to each recipient (age keys, PGP keys or cloud KMS keys).</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"secret-managers-nothing-on-disk-at-all\">Secret managers: nothing on disk at all</h2>\n<p>With a secret manager, the app asks for secrets when it starts, using an identity (a cloud role, a service token) rather than a key stored next to it. A neat pattern for local development is a template that contains only <em>references</em>:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\"># .env.tpl (safe to commit: no secrets, only pointers)\nRPC_URL=op://Launchpad/RPC/url\nDATABASE_URL=op://Launchpad/Postgres/connection-string</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">op run --env-file=.env.tpl -- npm start</code></pre></div>\n<p>The 1Password CLI swaps each <code>op://</code> reference for the real value in memory, only for that process. Doppler (<code>doppler run -- npm start</code>) and Infisical work the same way.</p>\n<h2 id=\"ci-secrets-in-github-actions\">CI: secrets in GitHub Actions</h2>\n<p>GitHub encrypts Actions secrets (with libsodium sealed boxes) before they are stored, masks them in logs, and only exposes them to the jobs you choose.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"better-than-any-stored-secret-short-lived-credentials\">Better than any stored secret: short-lived credentials</h2>\n<p>The best long-lived cloud key is no key. With <strong>OIDC</strong>, GitHub Actions proves its identity to AWS, Google Cloud or Azure and receives credentials that expire in minutes, scoped to one repository and branch. Nothing to leak, nothing to rotate. Use it for every deploy pipeline that touches the cloud.</p>\n<h2 id=\"rules-that-keep-secrets-secret\">Rules that keep secrets secret</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>One secret, one place.</strong> If a value exists in three systems, you will forget one when rotating.</li><li><strong>Least privilege.</strong> Each app and pipeline reads only its own secrets.</li><li><strong>Rotation is a feature.</strong> If rotating a key takes a day of work, it will not happen during an incident. Practise it.</li><li><strong>Revoke on departure.</strong> When someone leaves, remove their age key from SOPS recipients and rotate every secret they could decrypt.</li></ul>","quiz":[{"q":"What does SOPS encrypt in a YAML file?","options":["The whole file, including key names","The values, leaving key names readable so diffs and reviews still make sense","Only comments"],"answer":1,"explain":"Readable structure with encrypted values is what makes SOPS files safe to commit."},{"q":"Why is <code>op run --env-file=.env.tpl -- npm start</code> safer than a .env file?","options":["The template holds only references; real values are fetched at run time and never written to disk","It encrypts the npm packages","It deletes the database after use"],"answer":0,"explain":"Nothing secret sits in the project folder to be copied, committed or stolen."},{"q":"What is the advantage of OIDC for CI deployments?","options":["It makes builds faster","The pipeline gets short-lived cloud credentials on demand, so there is no long-lived key to leak or rotate","It removes the need for tests"],"answer":1,"explain":"The safest secret is the one that does not exist."},{"q":"A teammate with access to your SOPS files leaves the company. What should you do?","options":["Nothing, the files are encrypted","Remove their key from the recipients, re-encrypt, and rotate every secret they could read","Delete the Git history"],"answer":1,"explain":"They may still have decrypted copies: rotation is the only real fix."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Encrypt, read and use a secrets file with SOPS","file":"Terminal","lang":"bash","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Three commands. Encrypt <code>secrets.yaml</code> to your team's age public key (kept in an environment variable), print the decrypted values, then run your app with the secrets injected as environment variables, without ever writing a plaintext file:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">sops --encrypt --age &quot;$AGE_PUBLIC_KEY&quot; secrets.yaml &gt; secrets.enc.yaml\nsops --decrypt secrets.enc.yaml\nsops exec-env secrets.enc.yaml 'npm start'</code></pre></div>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"sops --encrypt --age \"$AGE_PUBLIC_KEY\" secrets.yaml > secrets.enc.yaml\nsops --decrypt secrets.enc.yaml\nsops exec-env secrets.enc.yaml 'npm start'","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*sops\\s+--encrypt\\s+--age\\s+[\"']\\$AGE_PUBLIC_KEY[\"']\\s+secrets\\.yaml\\s*>\\s*secrets\\.enc\\.yaml\\s*$","text":"Line 1: <code>sops --encrypt --age &quot;$AGE_PUBLIC_KEY&quot; secrets.yaml &gt; secrets.enc.yaml</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*sops\\s+--decrypt\\s+secrets\\.enc\\.yaml\\s*$","text":"Line 2: <code>sops --decrypt secrets.enc.yaml</code> (it finds your age identity in <code>~/.config/sops/age/keys.txt</code> or <code>SOPS_AGE_KEY_FILE</code>)."},{"re":"^\\s*sops\\s+exec-env\\s+secrets\\.enc\\.yaml\\s+[\"']npm start[\"']\\s*$","text":"Line 3: <code>sops exec-env secrets.enc.yaml 'npm start'</code> runs the command with the secrets as environment variables."}],"explain":"<p>After encrypting, delete the plaintext <code>secrets.yaml</code> and add it to <code>.gitignore</code>. To change a value later, run <code>sops secrets.enc.yaml</code>: it decrypts into your editor and re-encrypts when you save. A <code>.sops.yaml</code> file in the repository can list the recipients so nobody has to remember the flags.</p>"},{"title":"Pass a secret to a deploy step","file":".github/workflows/deploy.yml","lang":"yaml","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Give the deploy step the <code>RPC_URL</code> secret as an environment variable. Four lines, indentation matters in YAML:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\"><pre><code class=\"language-yaml\">steps:\n  - run: npm run deploy\n    env:\n      RPC_URL: ${{ secrets.RPC_URL }}</code></pre></div>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"steps:\n  - run: npm run deploy\n    env:\n      RPC_URL: ${{ secrets.RPC_URL }}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^steps:\\s*$","text":"Line 1: <code>steps:</code> at the left edge."},{"re":"^ {2}-\\s+run:\\s*npm\\s+run\\s+deploy\\s*$","text":"Line 2: two spaces, then <code>- run: npm run deploy</code>"},{"re":"^ {4}env:\\s*$","text":"Line 3: four spaces, then <code>env:</code> (it belongs to the same step as <code>run</code>)."},{"re":"^ {6}RPC_URL:\\s*\\$\\{\\{\\s*secrets\\.RPC_URL\\s*\\}\\}\\s*$","text":"Line 4: six spaces, then <code>RPC_URL: ${{ secrets.RPC_URL }}</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Pass secrets only to the steps that need them, never to the whole job. Put production secrets in a GitHub <strong>environment</strong> with required reviewers, so a deploy needs a human approval. Never <code>echo</code> a secret, and remember that a pull request from a fork does not get your secrets, for good reason.</p>"}],"text":"The .env problem Every team starts the same way: a .env file with the database URL, API keys and an RPC key, shared in a chat message just this once . Copies end up in Slack history, on old laptops and in backups. When someone leaves or a laptop is stolen, nobody knows which keys to rotate. The secrets lesson showed how to keep secrets out of Git . This lesson is about where they live instead, and how to keep them encrypted until the moment your app needs them. Three good options Option How it works Best for Secret manager (AWS Secrets Manager, Google Secret Manager, Doppler, Infisical, 1Password) Secrets live encrypted in a service; your app or CLI fetches them at run time with its own identity; every read is logged Production and teams Encrypted files in Git with SOPS + age (or a KMS) The config file is committed, but every secret value inside it is encrypted; only people or machines with a key can decrypt Small teams, infrastructure repos, GitOps CI platform secrets (GitHub Actions secrets and environments) Stored encrypted by the platform and injected into jobs Deploy pipelines SOPS: encrypted config you can still review SOPS (Secrets OPerationS) encrypts the values in a YAML, JSON or .env file but leaves the keys readable. Diffs stay meaningful ( someone changed RPC_URL ) without revealing anything. # secrets.enc.yaml (safe to commit) RPC_URL: ENC[AES256_GCM,data:7Qmq...,iv:...,tag:...,type:str] DATABASE_URL: ENC[AES256_GCM,data:Lw0f...,iv:...,tag:...,type:str] sops: age: - recipient: age1ql3z7hjy54pw3hyww5ayyfg7zqgvc7w3j2elw8zmrj2kg5sfn9aqmcac8p Under the hood it is envelope encryption: a random data key encrypts the values with AES-256-GCM, and that data key is encrypted to each recipient (age keys, PGP keys or cloud KMS keys). Secret managers: nothing on disk at all With a secret manager, the app asks for secrets when it starts, using an identity (a cloud role, a service token) rather than a key stored next to it. A neat pattern for local development is a template that contains only references : # .env.tpl (safe to commit: no secrets, only pointers) RPC_URL=op://Launchpad/RPC/url DATABASE_URL=op://Launchpad/Postgres/connection-string op run --env-file=.env.tpl -- npm start The 1Password CLI swaps each op:// reference for the real value in memory, only for that process. Doppler ( doppler run -- npm start ) and Infisical work the same way. CI: secrets in GitHub Actions GitHub encrypts Actions secrets (with libsodium sealed boxes) before they are stored, masks them in logs, and only exposes them to the jobs you choose. Better than any stored secret: short-lived credentials The best long-lived cloud key is no key. With OIDC , GitHub Actions proves its identity to AWS, Google Cloud or Azure and receives credentials that expire in minutes, scoped to one repository and branch. Nothing to leak, nothing to rotate. Use it for every deploy pipeline that touches the cloud. Rules that keep secrets secret One secret, one place. If a value exists in three systems, you will forget one when rotating. Least privilege. Each app and pipeline reads only its own secrets. Rotation is a feature. If rotating a key takes a day of work, it will not happen during an incident. Practise it. Revoke on departure. When someone leaves, remove their age key from SOPS recipients and rotate every secret they could decrypt.","track":"encrypt"},{"id":"enc-envelope","title":"Envelope encryption, key rotation and crypto-shredding","level":"advanced","minutes":45,"summary":"How production systems encrypt huge amounts of data without the master key ever leaving a KMS, using data keys, key hierarchies, rotation without re-encrypting everything, encrypted backups that survive a public bucket, and deleting data by deleting keys.","html":"<h2 id=\"the-problem-with-one-big-key\">The problem with one big key</h2>\n<p>In the last lessons your app held the encryption key. That works, but if the app server is compromised, the key goes with it, and every record it ever encrypted is exposed. You also cannot tell who decrypted what.</p>\n<p>A <strong>KMS</strong> (AWS KMS, Google Cloud KMS, Azure Key Vault) keeps master keys inside hardware security modules: the key material never leaves, and every use is permission-checked and logged. But you cannot send gigabytes through it: KMS calls are slow, cost money, and AWS KMS will only encrypt up to 4 KB directly.</p>\n<p><strong>Envelope encryption</strong> gets the best of both.</p>\n<h2 id=\"how-the-envelope-works\">How the envelope works</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Your app asks KMS for a <strong>data key</strong> (<code>GenerateDataKey</code>). KMS returns it twice: in <strong>plaintext</strong>, and <strong>encrypted</strong> under the master key (the &quot;key encryption key&quot;, KEK)</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>The app encrypts the data locally with AES-256-GCM using the plaintext data key (fast, any size)</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>The app stores the <strong>ciphertext</strong> together with the <strong>encrypted data key</strong>, then throws the plaintext data key away</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>To decrypt later, the app sends the encrypted data key to KMS (<code>Decrypt</code>). KMS checks the caller is allowed, logs the request, and returns the plaintext data key</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>The app decrypts the data locally, then forgets the data key again</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The data is sealed in an envelope (the data key), and the envelope is sealed by a key that never leaves the HSM.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"what-the-envelope-buys-you\">What the envelope buys you</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>The master key never leaves the HSM.</strong> An attacker on your server can only decrypt while they have access, and every attempt is logged.</li><li><strong>Access control in one place.</strong> A KMS key policy says which roles may encrypt and which may decrypt. Your backup job can be allowed to encrypt but <em>not</em> decrypt, so a compromised backup server cannot read old backups.</li><li><strong>Rotation without re-encrypting.</strong> Turn on automatic key rotation: KMS creates new key material on a schedule but keeps the old versions to decrypt old envelopes. New data uses the new material; nothing has to be rewritten.</li><li><strong>Emergency re-wrap.</strong> If you suspect a master key is compromised, decrypt and re-encrypt only the small data keys under a new master key, not terabytes of data.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"backups-that-survive-a-public-bucket\">Backups that survive a public bucket</h2>\n<p>Storage services encrypt at rest by default, but that encryption is transparent: anyone the bucket lets in gets plaintext. Two layers fix that:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>SSE-KMS</strong> on the bucket, with a key policy that only your restore role can use. Requests for SSE-KMS objects must be signed by an identity with <code>kms:Decrypt</code>, so even if someone accidentally makes the bucket public, anonymous downloads fail.</li><li><strong>Client-side encryption</strong> before upload (envelope encryption as above, or <code>age</code> from the beginner lesson), so the file is ciphertext no matter where it ends up:</li></ol>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">pg_dump &quot;$DATABASE_URL&quot; | gzip | age -R backup-recipients.txt &gt; db-$(date +%F).sql.gz.age\naws s3 cp db-$(date +%F).sql.gz.age s3://launchpad-backups/ --sse aws:kms --sse-kms-key-id &quot;$BACKUP_KMS_KEY&quot;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Always test restores. An encrypted backup you cannot decrypt is the same as no backup.</p>\n<h2 id=\"crypto-shredding-deleting-data-by-deleting-keys\">Crypto-shredding: deleting data by deleting keys</h2>\n<p>Deleting a user's data everywhere (databases, replicas, logs, years of backups) is close to impossible. Encrypting each user's records with <strong>their own data key</strong> makes it easy: delete the key and every copy becomes permanent noise. This is called <strong>crypto-shredding</strong>, and it is a practical way to honour deletion requests under laws like California's CCPA.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-typical-key-hierarchy\">A typical key hierarchy</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Example</th><th>Lives in</th><th>Rotated</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Root / master key (KEK)</td><td><code>alias/launchpad-data</code></td><td>KMS / HSM, never exported</td><td>Automatically, yearly or faster</td></tr><tr><td>Data keys (DEK)</td><td>One per file, per backup, or per user</td><td>Stored encrypted next to the data</td><td>New key for each new object</td></tr><tr><td>Derived keys</td><td>Blind-index HMAC key, per-tenant keys</td><td>Derived or wrapped from the above</td><td>With their parent</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","quiz":[{"q":"In envelope encryption, what is stored next to the encrypted data?","options":["The master key","The data key, encrypted under the master key","The plaintext data key"],"answer":1,"explain":"The encrypted data key is useless without permission to ask KMS to decrypt it."},{"q":"Why can a backup job be allowed to encrypt but not decrypt?","options":["So a compromised backup server cannot read past backups, while still being able to create new ones","Because KMS does not support decryption","To make backups smaller"],"answer":0,"explain":"Separate permissions limit what any one compromised system can do."},{"q":"A bucket of database backups is accidentally made public. Which setup keeps the data safe?","options":["Default storage encryption at rest","Backups encrypted client-side (or with SSE-KMS and a restrictive key policy) before upload","Long file names that are hard to guess"],"answer":1,"explain":"Default at-rest encryption decrypts transparently for anyone the bucket allows."},{"q":"What is crypto-shredding?","options":["Encrypting data twice","Shredding old hard drives","Deleting the key that encrypts a user's data, making every copy, including backups, unreadable"],"answer":2,"explain":"It turns &quot;find and delete every copy&quot; into &quot;delete one key&quot;."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Get a data key from AWS KMS","file":"backup.js","lang":"js","run":false,"prompt":"<p>With the AWS SDK for JavaScript v3, request a 256-bit data key. <code>kms</code> is a <code>KMSClient</code> and <code>GenerateDataKeyCommand</code> is already imported. Three lines:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const { Plaintext, CiphertextBlob } = await kms.send(\n  new GenerateDataKeyCommand({ KeyId: process.env.KMS_KEY_ID, KeySpec: 'AES_256' })\n);</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>Plaintext</code> is the 32-byte key for AES-GCM; <code>CiphertextBlob</code> is the encrypted copy you store next to your data.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const { Plaintext, CiphertextBlob } = await kms.send(\n  new GenerateDataKeyCommand({ KeyId: process.env.KMS_KEY_ID, KeySpec: 'AES_256' })\n);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s*\\{\\s*Plaintext\\s*,\\s*CiphertextBlob\\s*\\}\\s*=\\s*await\\s+kms\\.send\\(","text":"Line 1: <code>const { Plaintext, CiphertextBlob } = await kms.send(</code>"},{"re":"new\\s+GenerateDataKeyCommand\\(\\s*\\{\\s*KeyId\\s*:\\s*process\\.env\\.KMS_KEY_ID\\s*,\\s*KeySpec\\s*:\\s*['\"]AES_256['\"]\\s*\\}\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>new GenerateDataKeyCommand({ KeyId: process.env.KMS_KEY_ID, KeySpec: 'AES_256' })</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*\\)\\s*;?\\s*$","text":"Line 3: close the call with <code>);</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Then call your <code>encrypt()</code> from the AES-GCM lesson with <code>Plaintext</code> as the key, save <code>{ encryptedKey: CiphertextBlob, data }</code>, and overwrite the plaintext key buffer with <code>Plaintext.fill(0)</code> when you are done.</p>"},{"title":"Unwrap the data key to decrypt","file":"restore.js","lang":"js","run":false,"prompt":"<p>To read a record back, ask KMS to decrypt the stored data key. Naming the key you expect (<code>KeyId</code>) is best practice, so a swapped envelope cannot trick you into using a different key:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const { Plaintext } = await kms.send(\n  new DecryptCommand({ CiphertextBlob: record.encryptedKey, KeyId: process.env.KMS_KEY_ID })\n);</code></pre></div>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const { Plaintext } = await kms.send(\n  new DecryptCommand({ CiphertextBlob: record.encryptedKey, KeyId: process.env.KMS_KEY_ID })\n);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s*\\{\\s*Plaintext\\s*\\}\\s*=\\s*await\\s+kms\\.send\\(","text":"Line 1: <code>const { Plaintext } = await kms.send(</code>"},{"re":"new\\s+DecryptCommand\\(\\s*\\{\\s*CiphertextBlob\\s*:\\s*record\\.encryptedKey\\s*,\\s*KeyId\\s*:\\s*process\\.env\\.KMS_KEY_ID\\s*\\}\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>new DecryptCommand({ CiphertextBlob: record.encryptedKey, KeyId: process.env.KMS_KEY_ID })</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*\\)\\s*;?\\s*$","text":"Line 3: <code>);</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Every one of these calls appears in CloudTrail with the caller's identity and time. If an attacker steals your database, they get ciphertext and encrypted data keys: useless without permission to call <code>Decrypt</code> on that KMS key. Watching for unusual <code>Decrypt</code> volume is one of the best breach alarms you can have.</p>"}],"text":"The problem with one big key In the last lessons your app held the encryption key. That works, but if the app server is compromised, the key goes with it, and every record it ever encrypted is exposed. You also cannot tell who decrypted what. A KMS (AWS KMS, Google Cloud KMS, Azure Key Vault) keeps master keys inside hardware security modules: the key material never leaves, and every use is permission-checked and logged. But you cannot send gigabytes through it: KMS calls are slow, cost money, and AWS KMS will only encrypt up to 4 KB directly. Envelope encryption gets the best of both. How the envelope works Step What happens 1 Your app asks KMS for a data key ( GenerateDataKey ). KMS returns it twice: in plaintext , and encrypted under the master key (the key encryption key , KEK) 2 The app encrypts the data locally with AES-256-GCM using the plaintext data key (fast, any size) 3 The app stores the ciphertext together with the encrypted data key , then throws the plaintext data key away 4 To decrypt later, the app sends the encrypted data key to KMS ( Decrypt ). KMS checks the caller is allowed, logs the request, and returns the plaintext data key 5 The app decrypts the data locally, then forgets the data key again The data is sealed in an envelope (the data key), and the envelope is sealed by a key that never leaves the HSM. What the envelope buys you The master key never leaves the HSM. An attacker on your server can only decrypt while they have access, and every attempt is logged. Access control in one place. A KMS key policy says which roles may encrypt and which may decrypt. Your backup job can be allowed to encrypt but not decrypt, so a compromised backup server cannot read old backups. Rotation without re-encrypting. Turn on automatic key rotation: KMS creates new key material on a schedule but keeps the old versions to decrypt old envelopes. New data uses the new material; nothing has to be rewritten. Emergency re-wrap. If you suspect a master key is compromised, decrypt and re-encrypt only the small data keys under a new master key, not terabytes of data. Backups that survive a public bucket Storage services encrypt at rest by default, but that encryption is transparent: anyone the bucket lets in gets plaintext. Two layers fix that: SSE-KMS on the bucket, with a key policy that only your restore role can use. Requests for SSE-KMS objects must be signed by an identity with kms:Decrypt , so even if someone accidentally makes the bucket public, anonymous downloads fail. Client-side encryption before upload (envelope encryption as above, or age from the beginner lesson), so the file is ciphertext no matter where it ends up: pg_dump $DATABASE_URL | gzip | age -R backup-recipients.txt db-$(date +%F).sql.gz.age aws s3 cp db-$(date +%F).sql.gz.age s3://launchpad-backups/ --sse aws:kms --sse-kms-key-id $BACKUP_KMS_KEY Always test restores. An encrypted backup you cannot decrypt is the same as no backup. Crypto-shredding: deleting data by deleting keys Deleting a user's data everywhere (databases, replicas, logs, years of backups) is close to impossible. Encrypting each user's records with their own data key makes it easy: delete the key and every copy becomes permanent noise. This is called crypto-shredding , and it is a practical way to honour deletion requests under laws like California's CCPA. A typical key hierarchy Level Example Lives in Rotated Root / master key (KEK) alias/launchpad-data KMS / HSM, never exported Automatically, yearly or faster Data keys (DEK) One per file, per backup, or per user Stored encrypted next to the data New key for each new object Derived keys Blind-index HMAC key, per-tenant keys Derived or wrapped from the above With their parent","track":"encrypt"},{"id":"enc-e2e","title":"End-to-end and client-side encryption","level":"advanced","minutes":45,"summary":"Build features where even your own servers cannot read users' data. Derive keys from passphrases safely, encrypt to someone's public key with sealed boxes, understand forward secrecy, and see how wallet keystores protect private keys.","html":"<h2 id=\"why-encrypt-before-the-server-sees-it\">Why encrypt before the server sees it?</h2>\n<p>Everything so far trusted your servers: data was encrypted, but your app held or could fetch the keys. <strong>Client-side</strong> (or <strong>end-to-end</strong>) encryption removes that trust. Data is encrypted on the user's device, and the server stores only ciphertext it cannot read.</p>\n<p>That is how password managers, Signal and encrypted-notes apps work. A full breach of the company's servers leaks nothing readable.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>You gain</th><th>You give up</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A server breach exposes only ciphertext</td><td>Server-side search, sorting and analytics on that data</td></tr><tr><td>Insiders and subpoenas cannot reveal content</td><td>If the user loses their key or passphrase, the data is gone, unless you design recovery</td></tr><tr><td>A strong trust story for users</td><td>More complex code, and the client code itself must be trustworthy</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"turning-a-passphrase-into-a-key\">Turning a passphrase into a key</h2>\n<p>Users remember passphrases, not 32 random bytes. A <strong>key derivation function</strong> (KDF) turns one into the other, deliberately slowly, with a random <strong>salt</strong> so identical passphrases give different keys:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Argon2id</strong>: the best choice (memory-hard, so GPU cracking rigs struggle). Available in libsodium and most server languages.</li><li><strong>scrypt</strong>: also memory-hard. Used by age's passphrase mode and Ethereum keystores.</li><li><strong>PBKDF2</strong>: the only one built into the browser's Web Crypto API. Make it slow: OWASP recommends at least <strong>600,000</strong> iterations of PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"encrypting-to-someone-else-sealed-boxes\">Encrypting to someone else: sealed boxes</h2>\n<p>Sometimes the sender should be able to encrypt but not decrypt, like a user uploading ID documents that only your compliance team may read. Public-key encryption does this. With <strong>libsodium</strong>, a <strong>sealed box</strong> encrypts a message to a public key; only the holder of the matching private key can open it, and not even the sender can read it back.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">import sodium from 'libsodium-wrappers';\nawait sodium.ready;\n\n// Compliance team, once: keep privateKey in their KMS or an offline machine\nconst { publicKey, privateKey } = sodium.crypto_box_keypair();\n\n// In the user's browser: encrypt the document to the team's public key\nconst sealed = sodium.crypto_box_seal(documentBytes, publicKey);\n\n// Only the compliance team can open it\nconst opened = sodium.crypto_box_seal_open(sealed, publicKey, privateKey);</code></pre></div>\n<p>GitHub uses exactly this to encrypt Actions secrets in your browser before they reach its servers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"forward-secrecy-protecting-yesterday-s-messages\">Forward secrecy: protecting yesterday's messages</h2>\n<p>If a long-term private key is stolen, can the attacker decrypt everything it ever protected? With simple public-key encryption, yes. <strong>Forward secrecy</strong> prevents that by using short-lived (ephemeral) keys for each session or message and deleting them afterwards:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>TLS 1.3</strong> always uses ephemeral key exchange, so a stolen server key does not decrypt recorded traffic.</li><li><strong>Signal's protocol</strong> goes further with a &quot;double ratchet&quot; that changes keys with every message, so compromising a phone today does not reveal last month's chats.</li></ul>\n<p>Never design your own messaging protocol. Use Signal's libraries or MLS (Messaging Layer Security, a 2023 IETF standard for group messaging).</p>\n<h2 id=\"how-wallets-encrypt-private-keys\">How wallets encrypt private keys</h2>\n<p>An Ethereum <strong>keystore file</strong> (what <code>cast wallet import</code> and many wallets create) is client-side encryption in action:</p>\n<ol><li>Your password goes through <strong>scrypt</strong> with a random salt to make a key.</li><li>That key encrypts the private key with <strong>AES-128-CTR</strong>.</li><li>A <strong>MAC</strong> (a Keccak hash of part of the derived key plus the ciphertext) detects a wrong password before anything is decrypted.</li></ol>\n<p>The file is safe to back up, <em>but only as strong as the password</em>. A stolen keystore with a short password can be cracked offline at leisure. Browser extension wallets also keep a password-encrypted vault file, which is why infostealer malware copies those vault files and cracks weak passwords later.</p>\n<h2 id=\"designing-recovery-honestly\">Designing recovery honestly</h2>\n<p>If only the user holds the key, a forgotten passphrase means lost data. Real products offer recovery without breaking the model:</p>\n<ul><li>a <strong>recovery key</strong> shown once at sign-up for the user to store offline,</li><li>the key encrypted to <strong>several of the user's devices</strong>,</li><li><strong>social or threshold recovery</strong>: the key split into shares held by trusted contacts (Shamir's secret sharing), any few of which can restore it.</li></ul>\n<p>What you must not do is quietly keep a copy on the server &quot;just in case&quot;. Then it is not end-to-end encryption any more.</p>","quiz":[{"q":"What does a salt add to passphrase-based key derivation?","options":["It makes the key secret","It makes identical passphrases produce different keys, which defeats precomputed cracking tables","It replaces the need for many iterations"],"answer":1,"explain":"Salts are not secret; they make each derivation unique."},{"q":"Why use 600,000 PBKDF2 iterations instead of 1,000?","options":["Each passphrase guess costs an attacker 600 times more work","It makes the ciphertext stronger against quantum computers","Browsers require it"],"answer":0,"explain":"Slowness is the defence against offline guessing."},{"q":"A user encrypts a document with crypto_box_seal to your team's public key. Who can decrypt it?","options":["The user and your team","Only whoever holds the team's private key","Anyone with the public key"],"answer":1,"explain":"Sealed boxes are anonymous and one-way: even the sender cannot open them again."},{"q":"An attacker steals a TLS server's private key and has recordings of last year's traffic. With TLS 1.3, can they decrypt it?","options":["Yes, all of it","No: TLS 1.3 uses ephemeral key exchange, so the server key does not reveal past session keys","Only the cookies"],"answer":1,"explain":"That is forward secrecy."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Derive an AES key from a passphrase in the browser","file":"vault.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Derive a non-extractable AES-256-GCM key from a passphrase with PBKDF2. The passphrase and a random 16-byte salt are already there. Add the two <code>await</code> steps and log the result:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const passphrase = 'correct horse battery staple';\nconst salt = crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(16));\n\nconst material = await crypto.subtle.importKey('raw', new TextEncoder().encode(passphrase), 'PBKDF2', false, ['deriveKey']);\nconst key = await crypto.subtle.deriveKey(\n  { name: 'PBKDF2', salt, iterations: 600000, hash: 'SHA-256' },\n  material,\n  { name: 'AES-GCM', length: 256 },\n  false,\n  ['encrypt', 'decrypt']\n);\nconsole.log(key.algorithm);</code></pre></div>","starter":"const passphrase = 'correct horse battery staple';\nconst salt = crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(16));","html":"","solution":"const passphrase = 'correct horse battery staple';\nconst salt = crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(16));\n\nconst material = await crypto.subtle.importKey('raw', new TextEncoder().encode(passphrase), 'PBKDF2', false, ['deriveKey']);\nconst key = await crypto.subtle.deriveKey(\n  { name: 'PBKDF2', salt, iterations: 600000, hash: 'SHA-256' },\n  material,\n  { name: 'AES-GCM', length: 256 },\n  false,\n  ['encrypt', 'decrypt']\n);\nconsole.log(key.algorithm);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+material\\s*=\\s*await\\s+crypto\\.subtle\\.importKey\\(\\s*['\"]raw['\"]\\s*,\\s*new\\s+TextEncoder\\(\\s*\\)\\.encode\\(\\s*passphrase\\s*\\)\\s*,\\s*['\"]PBKDF2['\"]\\s*,\\s*false\\s*,\\s*\\[\\s*['\"]deriveKey['\"]\\s*\\]\\s*\\)","text":"Step 1 imports the passphrase as PBKDF2 key material: <code>const material = await crypto.subtle.importKey('raw', new TextEncoder().encode(passphrase), 'PBKDF2', false, ['deriveKey']);</code>"},{"re":"const\\s+key\\s*=\\s*await\\s+crypto\\.subtle\\.deriveKey\\(","text":"Step 2 starts <code>const key = await crypto.subtle.deriveKey(</code>"},{"re":"\\{\\s*name\\s*:\\s*['\"]PBKDF2['\"]\\s*,\\s*salt\\s*,\\s*iterations\\s*:\\s*600000\\s*,\\s*hash\\s*:\\s*['\"]SHA-256['\"]\\s*\\}","text":"The first argument sets the slowness: <code>{ name: 'PBKDF2', salt, iterations: 600000, hash: 'SHA-256' },</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*material\\s*,\\s*$","text":"Then the key material: <code>material,</code>"},{"re":"\\{\\s*name\\s*:\\s*['\"]AES-GCM['\"]\\s*,\\s*length\\s*:\\s*256\\s*\\}","text":"Then what kind of key you want: <code>{ name: 'AES-GCM', length: 256 },</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*false\\s*,\\s*$","text":"<code>false,</code> makes the key non-extractable: JavaScript can use it but never read its bytes."},{"re":"\\[\\s*['\"]encrypt['\"]\\s*,\\s*['\"]decrypt['\"]\\s*\\]","text":"Then what it may be used for: <code>['encrypt', 'decrypt']</code> and close with <code>);</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*key\\.algorithm\\s*\\)","text":"Finally <code>console.log(key.algorithm);</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Press <strong>Run it</strong>: there is a noticeable pause while 600,000 iterations run. That pause is the point: an attacker guessing passphrases pays it for every single guess. Store the salt (not secret) alongside the ciphertext so you can derive the same key next time.</p>"}],"text":"Why encrypt before the server sees it? Everything so far trusted your servers: data was encrypted, but your app held or could fetch the keys. Client-side (or end-to-end ) encryption removes that trust. Data is encrypted on the user's device, and the server stores only ciphertext it cannot read. That is how password managers, Signal and encrypted-notes apps work. A full breach of the company's servers leaks nothing readable. You gain You give up A server breach exposes only ciphertext Server-side search, sorting and analytics on that data Insiders and subpoenas cannot reveal content If the user loses their key or passphrase, the data is gone, unless you design recovery A strong trust story for users More complex code, and the client code itself must be trustworthy Turning a passphrase into a key Users remember passphrases, not 32 random bytes. A key derivation function (KDF) turns one into the other, deliberately slowly, with a random salt so identical passphrases give different keys: Argon2id : the best choice (memory-hard, so GPU cracking rigs struggle). Available in libsodium and most server languages. scrypt : also memory-hard. Used by age's passphrase mode and Ethereum keystores. PBKDF2 : the only one built into the browser's Web Crypto API. Make it slow: OWASP recommends at least 600,000 iterations of PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256. Encrypting to someone else: sealed boxes Sometimes the sender should be able to encrypt but not decrypt, like a user uploading ID documents that only your compliance team may read. Public-key encryption does this. With libsodium , a sealed box encrypts a message to a public key; only the holder of the matching private key can open it, and not even the sender can read it back. import sodium from 'libsodium-wrappers'; await sodium.ready; // Compliance team, once: keep privateKey in their KMS or an offline machine const { publicKey, privateKey } = sodium.crypto_box_keypair(); // In the user's browser: encrypt the document to the team's public key const sealed = sodium.crypto_box_seal(documentBytes, publicKey); // Only the compliance team can open it const opened = sodium.crypto_box_seal_open(sealed, publicKey, privateKey); GitHub uses exactly this to encrypt Actions secrets in your browser before they reach its servers. Forward secrecy: protecting yesterday's messages If a long-term private key is stolen, can the attacker decrypt everything it ever protected? With simple public-key encryption, yes. Forward secrecy prevents that by using short-lived (ephemeral) keys for each session or message and deleting them afterwards: TLS 1.3 always uses ephemeral key exchange, so a stolen server key does not decrypt recorded traffic. Signal's protocol goes further with a double ratchet that changes keys with every message, so compromising a phone today does not reveal last month's chats. Never design your own messaging protocol. Use Signal's libraries or MLS (Messaging Layer Security, a 2023 IETF standard for group messaging). How wallets encrypt private keys An Ethereum keystore file (what cast wallet import and many wallets create) is client-side encryption in action: Your password goes through scrypt with a random salt to make a key. That key encrypts the private key with AES-128-CTR . A MAC (a Keccak hash of part of the derived key plus the ciphertext) detects a wrong password before anything is decrypted. The file is safe to back up, but only as strong as the password . A stolen keystore with a short password can be cracked offline at leisure. Browser extension wallets also keep a password-encrypted vault file, which is why infostealer malware copies those vault files and cracks weak passwords later. Designing recovery honestly If only the user holds the key, a forgotten passphrase means lost data. Real products offer recovery without breaking the model: a recovery key shown once at sign-up for the user to store offline, the key encrypted to several of the user's devices , social or threshold recovery : the key spl","track":"encrypt"},{"id":"enc-attacks","title":"How encryption gets broken (and how not to be next)","level":"expert","minutes":50,"summary":"Real attacks on real encryption, done hands-on: nonce reuse, unauthenticated ciphertext, padding oracles, keys shipped to the client, weak password-derived keys and side channels, plus how professionals test crypto code.","html":"<h2 id=\"aes-is-fine-the-way-it-is-used-is-not\">AES is fine. The way it is used is not.</h2>\n<p>Nobody breaks AES-256 by brute force. Real-world encryption fails through <strong>misuse</strong>: a reused nonce, a missing integrity check, a key in the wrong place, a password too weak for the KDF protecting it. This lesson goes deeper than the <a href=\"#lesson-sec-crypto-in-practice\">applied cryptography</a> lesson: you will carry out the most common attack yourself.</p>\n<h2 id=\"attack-1-nonce-reuse-the-two-time-pad\">Attack 1: nonce reuse (the two-time pad)</h2>\n<p>AES-CTR, AES-GCM and ChaCha20 are <strong>stream ciphers</strong> under the hood: they generate a <strong>keystream</strong> from the key and nonce, and XOR it with your data.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">ciphertext = plaintext XOR keystream(key, nonce)</code></pre></div>\n<p>Reuse the same key and nonce for two messages and the keystream cancels out:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">c1 XOR c2 = (p1 XOR ks) XOR (p2 XOR ks) = p1 XOR p2</code></pre></div>\n<p>If the attacker knows or guesses <em>either</em> message (a greeting, a JSON field name, a file header), they recover the other one completely. With AES-GCM it is even worse: a repeated nonce also leaks the internal authentication key, letting the attacker <strong>forge</strong> messages that pass verification.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"attack-2-ciphertext-without-integrity\">Attack 2: ciphertext without integrity</h2>\n<p>Encryption alone hides data; it does not stop someone <strong>changing</strong> it. With CTR mode, flipping a bit in the ciphertext flips the same bit in the plaintext, so <code>&quot;amount&quot;:100</code> can become <code>&quot;amount&quot;:900</code> without knowing the key. With CBC mode, flipping bits in one block makes controlled changes in the next.</p>\n<p><strong>Padding oracle attacks</strong> are the famous version: if a server using CBC answers &quot;bad padding&quot; differently from &quot;bad data&quot;, an attacker can decrypt any ciphertext a byte at a time just by sending modified versions and watching the replies. This broke real systems for over a decade (the 2002 Vaudenay attack, POODLE against SSL 3.0, Lucky 13 against TLS).</p>\n<p><strong>The fix is non-negotiable: use authenticated encryption</strong> (AES-GCM, ChaCha20-Poly1305, libsodium's secretbox) so any modification fails to decrypt. If you are stuck with CBC in a legacy system, encrypt-then-MAC and compare MACs in constant time before decrypting anything.</p>\n<h2 id=\"attack-3-the-key-went-to-the-client\">Attack 3: the key went to the client</h2>\n<p>If your front-end JavaScript, mobile app or browser extension contains the key, so does every attacker's copy of it. Bundles are readable (view source, DevTools, or <code>apktool</code> for Android apps), and a &quot;hidden&quot; key is found in minutes. Common versions:</p>\n<ul><li>an API &quot;encryption key&quot; in a React bundle,</li><li>a hard-coded AES key in a mobile app that &quot;encrypts&quot; local data,</li><li>a launchpad front end that signs claim messages with a private key it ships to the browser.</li></ul>\n<p>Anything the client can decrypt, the user (and malware on their machine) can decrypt. Keep keys server-side or in a KMS, or use public-key designs where the client only needs a public key.</p>\n<h2 id=\"attack-4-weak-keys-from-weak-passwords\">Attack 4: weak keys from weak passwords</h2>\n<p>When a key comes from a password, the attacker does not attack AES; they guess passwords offline, as fast as their GPUs allow. Password managers are the classic example: after a 2022 breach at a major password manager, attackers stole encrypted vaults. Vaults protected by strong master passwords and high KDF settings held; some older accounts still used far lower PBKDF2 iteration counts than current guidance, and investigators have since linked large crypto thefts to vaults that were cracked.</p>\n<p>Defences: Argon2id or scrypt with real memory cost, or PBKDF2 with 600,000+ iterations; a minimum passphrase strength; and <strong>re-encrypting old data when you raise the cost</strong>, not only new data.</p>\n<h2 id=\"attack-5-side-channels\">Attack 5: side channels</h2>\n<p>The maths can be perfect while the implementation leaks:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Timing</strong>: comparing a MAC or token with <code>===</code> stops at the first wrong byte, so response times reveal how many bytes were right. Use a constant-time comparison (<code>crypto.timingSafeEqual</code> in Node).</li><li><strong>Error messages</strong>: &quot;invalid padding&quot; versus &quot;invalid MAC&quot; versus &quot;user not found&quot; are all oracles.</li><li><strong>Compression before encryption</strong>: if attacker-controlled text is compressed together with a secret, the ciphertext length reveals the secret (the CRIME and BREACH attacks on HTTPS).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"attack-6-downgrade-and-confusion\">Attack 6: downgrade and confusion</h2>\n<p>Attackers love making you use the weaker option:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Protocol downgrade</strong>: forcing old TLS versions or ciphers. Disable everything below TLS 1.2.</li><li><strong>Algorithm confusion</strong>: a JWT library that trusts the token's own <code>alg</code> header can be tricked into accepting <code>alg: none</code>, or into verifying an RS256 token with HMAC using the public key as the secret. Always pin the algorithm you expect.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"how-professionals-test-crypto-code\">How professionals test crypto code</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Use high-level, misuse-resistant libraries</strong> (libsodium, Tink, age) instead of raw primitives.</li><li><strong>Run known-answer and attack test vectors</strong>: Project Wycheproof publishes thousands of test cases for edge cases and known attacks against common algorithms.</li><li><strong>Grep for red flags</strong>: <code>Math.random</code>, <code>createCipher(</code> (the old, deprecated Node API), <code>'aes-256-cbc'</code> without a MAC, <code>'ecb'</code>, constant IVs, keys in source, <code>===</code> on MACs.</li><li><strong>Get crypto-heavy code reviewed by someone who does this for a living.</strong> Audits of wallets and bridges routinely find exactly these bugs.</li></ul>","quiz":[{"q":"Two messages were encrypted with AES-CTR under the same key and nonce. What does XOR-ing the two ciphertexts give an attacker?","options":["The key","The XOR of the two plaintexts, so knowing one message reveals the other","Nothing useful"],"answer":1,"explain":"The keystream cancels out. That is why nonces must never repeat."},{"q":"Why is AES-CBC without a MAC dangerous?","options":["CBC is too slow","Ciphertext can be modified undetectably, and padding errors can become an oracle that decrypts data","CBC cannot encrypt text"],"answer":1,"explain":"Integrity is not optional. Use authenticated encryption."},{"q":"A mobile app encrypts local data with an AES key hard-coded in the app. Who can decrypt it?","options":["Only the company","Anyone who downloads the app and extracts the key","Nobody, AES-256 is unbreakable"],"answer":1,"explain":"A key you ship to users is a key you have published."},{"q":"Why compare MACs with crypto.timingSafeEqual instead of ===?","options":["=== can stop at the first different byte, and the timing difference lets attackers guess the MAC byte by byte","=== does not work on strings","timingSafeEqual is faster"],"answer":0,"explain":"Constant-time comparison removes the timing side channel."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Recover a message from a reused nonce","file":"two-time-pad.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>A team chat encrypted two messages with AES-CTR using the same key and the same nonce. You do not know the key. You do know the first message, because it was the daily standup reminder. Add three lines to recover the second message:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const xor = (a, b) =&gt; a.map((byte, i) =&gt; byte ^ b[i]);\nconst recovered = xor(xor(c1, c2), p1);\nconsole.log(new TextDecoder().decode(recovered));</code></pre></div>","starter":"const hex = (h) => Uint8Array.from(h.match(/../g), (x) => parseInt(x, 16));\nconst c1 = hex('4be3e688dd732b01479edbe3528e2b0a376d4782a76564b9b032a80e5c166dd826c814ba6d');\nconst c2 = hex('48cbb690d76b235f4781d6ff529f3e08327c42d2ee7b2ba0bb76fc12561631c42fc8349449');\nconst p1 = new TextEncoder().encode('GM team, the standup moved to 3pm now');","html":"","solution":"const hex = (h) => Uint8Array.from(h.match(/../g), (x) => parseInt(x, 16));\nconst c1 = hex('4be3e688dd732b01479edbe3528e2b0a376d4782a76564b9b032a80e5c166dd826c814ba6d');\nconst c2 = hex('48cbb690d76b235f4781d6ff529f3e08327c42d2ee7b2ba0bb76fc12561631c42fc8349449');\nconst p1 = new TextEncoder().encode('GM team, the standup moved to 3pm now');\n\nconst xor = (a, b) => a.map((byte, i) => byte ^ b[i]);\nconst recovered = xor(xor(c1, c2), p1);\nconsole.log(new TextDecoder().decode(recovered));","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+xor\\s*=\\s*\\(\\s*a\\s*,\\s*b\\s*\\)\\s*=>\\s*a\\.map\\(\\s*\\(\\s*byte\\s*,\\s*i\\s*\\)\\s*=>\\s*byte\\s*\\^\\s*b\\[\\s*i\\s*\\]\\s*\\)","text":"Line 1, a byte-by-byte XOR helper: <code>const xor = (a, b) =&gt; a.map((byte, i) =&gt; byte ^ b[i]);</code> (<code>^</code> is XOR in JavaScript)."},{"re":"const\\s+recovered\\s*=\\s*xor\\(\\s*xor\\(\\s*c1\\s*,\\s*c2\\s*\\)\\s*,\\s*p1\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>c1 XOR c2</code> gives <code>p1 XOR p2</code>; XOR that with the known <code>p1</code> to get <code>p2</code>: <code>const recovered = xor(xor(c1, c2), p1);</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*new\\s+TextDecoder\\(\\s*\\)\\.decode\\(\\s*recovered\\s*\\)\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3: turn the bytes back into text: <code>console.log(new TextDecoder().decode(recovered));</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Press <strong>Run it</strong> and read the secret message. You never touched the key. The fix: a fresh random 96-bit nonce for every AES-GCM message (as in the intermediate lesson), a counter you can prove never repeats, or a cipher with a 192-bit nonce such as <strong>XChaCha20-Poly1305</strong>, where random nonces are safe even for huge numbers of messages. <strong>AES-GCM-SIV</strong> goes further and stays safe even if a nonce does repeat (it only reveals that two messages were identical).</p>"}],"text":"AES is fine. The way it is used is not. Nobody breaks AES-256 by brute force. Real-world encryption fails through misuse : a reused nonce, a missing integrity check, a key in the wrong place, a password too weak for the KDF protecting it. This lesson goes deeper than the applied cryptography lesson: you will carry out the most common attack yourself. Attack 1: nonce reuse (the two-time pad) AES-CTR, AES-GCM and ChaCha20 are stream ciphers under the hood: they generate a keystream from the key and nonce, and XOR it with your data. ciphertext = plaintext XOR keystream(key, nonce) Reuse the same key and nonce for two messages and the keystream cancels out: c1 XOR c2 = (p1 XOR ks) XOR (p2 XOR ks) = p1 XOR p2 If the attacker knows or guesses either message (a greeting, a JSON field name, a file header), they recover the other one completely. With AES-GCM it is even worse: a repeated nonce also leaks the internal authentication key, letting the attacker forge messages that pass verification. Attack 2: ciphertext without integrity Encryption alone hides data; it does not stop someone changing it. With CTR mode, flipping a bit in the ciphertext flips the same bit in the plaintext, so amount :100 can become amount :900 without knowing the key. With CBC mode, flipping bits in one block makes controlled changes in the next. Padding oracle attacks are the famous version: if a server using CBC answers bad padding differently from bad data , an attacker can decrypt any ciphertext a byte at a time just by sending modified versions and watching the replies. This broke real systems for over a decade (the 2002 Vaudenay attack, POODLE against SSL 3.0, Lucky 13 against TLS). The fix is non-negotiable: use authenticated encryption (AES-GCM, ChaCha20-Poly1305, libsodium's secretbox) so any modification fails to decrypt. If you are stuck with CBC in a legacy system, encrypt-then-MAC and compare MACs in constant time before decrypting anything. Attack 3: the key went to the client If your front-end JavaScript, mobile app or browser extension contains the key, so does every attacker's copy of it. Bundles are readable (view source, DevTools, or apktool for Android apps), and a hidden key is found in minutes. Common versions: an API encryption key in a React bundle, a hard-coded AES key in a mobile app that encrypts local data, a launchpad front end that signs claim messages with a private key it ships to the browser. Anything the client can decrypt, the user (and malware on their machine) can decrypt. Keep keys server-side or in a KMS, or use public-key designs where the client only needs a public key. Attack 4: weak keys from weak passwords When a key comes from a password, the attacker does not attack AES; they guess passwords offline, as fast as their GPUs allow. Password managers are the classic example: after a 2022 breach at a major password manager, attackers stole encrypted vaults. Vaults protected by strong master passwords and high KDF settings held; some older accounts still used far lower PBKDF2 iteration counts than current guidance, and investigators have since linked large crypto thefts to vaults that were cracked. Defences: Argon2id or scrypt with real memory cost, or PBKDF2 with 600,000+ iterations; a minimum passphrase strength; and re-encrypting old data when you raise the cost , not only new data. Attack 5: side channels The maths can be perfect while the implementation leaks: Timing : comparing a MAC or token with === stops at the first wrong byte, so response times reveal how many bytes were right. Use a constant-time comparison ( crypto.timingSafeEqual in Node). Error messages : invalid padding versus invalid MAC versus user not found are all oracles. Compression before encryption : if attacker-controlled text is compressed together with a secret, the ciphertext length reveals the secret (the CRIME and BREACH attacks on HTTPS). Attack 6: downgrade and confusion Attackers love making you use the weaker option: Protocol downgrade :","track":"encrypt"},{"id":"enc-post-quantum","title":"Post-quantum encryption and crypto agility","level":"expert","minutes":40,"summary":"What quantum computers would break and what they would not, \"harvest now, decrypt later\", the new NIST standards and the US migration timeline, what it means for blockchains, and how to build systems that can swap algorithms without a rewrite.","html":"<h2 id=\"what-a-quantum-computer-would-break\">What a quantum computer would break</h2>\n<p>A large, error-corrected quantum computer does not exist yet, and nobody knows exactly when one will. If one is built, two algorithms matter:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Algorithm</th><th>Affects</th><th>Impact</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Shor's algorithm</strong></td><td>RSA, Diffie-Hellman, elliptic curves (ECDH, ECDSA, Ed25519, and the <strong>secp256k1</strong> curve behind Bitcoin and Ethereum signatures)</td><td><strong>Broken.</strong> Private keys can be computed from public keys</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Grover's algorithm</strong></td><td>Symmetric ciphers and hashes (AES, ChaCha20, SHA-256, Keccak)</td><td>Weakened, roughly halving the security level. <strong>AES-256 and SHA-256 remain fine</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>So symmetric encryption, the subject of most of this track, survives. The public-key parts (key exchange and signatures) need replacing.</p>\n<h2 id=\"harvest-now-decrypt-later\">Harvest now, decrypt later</h2>\n<p>Why worry before the machine exists? Because encrypted traffic can be <strong>recorded today and decrypted later</strong>. Anything that must stay secret for ten or more years (medical records, state secrets, long-lived business data, private keys sent over a network) is already at risk if its key exchange is classical.</p>\n<p>Signatures are less urgent in that respect: a forged signature only matters once a quantum computer can produce it. The exception is anything long-lived that is anchored to a public key, such as root certificates, firmware signing keys, and blockchain accounts.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-new-standards\">The new standards</h2>\n<p>In August 2024 NIST published the first post-quantum standards:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Standard</th><th>Formerly</th><th>Job</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>FIPS 203: ML-KEM</strong></td><td>Kyber</td><td>Key exchange (replaces ECDH)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>FIPS 204: ML-DSA</strong></td><td>Dilithium</td><td>Digital signatures (replaces ECDSA and RSA signatures)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>FIPS 205: SLH-DSA</strong></td><td>SPHINCS+</td><td>Hash-based signatures, slower and larger but based on very conservative assumptions</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>NIST later picked <strong>HQC</strong> as a backup key-exchange algorithm built on different maths, and a further signature standard (FN-DSA, from Falcon) is on the way.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-us-timeline\">The US timeline</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>2022</strong>: National Security Memorandum 10 set the goal of moving US federal systems to quantum-resistant cryptography by <strong>2035</strong>, and the Quantum Computing Cybersecurity Preparedness Act required agencies to inventory their quantum-vulnerable systems.</li><li><strong>NSA's CNSA 2.0</strong> sets deadlines for national security systems to prefer and then require post-quantum algorithms, finishing in the early to mid 2030s.</li><li><strong>NIST's draft transition plan (IR 8547)</strong> proposes deprecating today's quantum-vulnerable public-key algorithms around <strong>2030</strong> and disallowing them by <strong>2035</strong>.</li></ul>\n<p>Government suppliers and regulated industries will follow these dates, and everyone else's vendors will follow them.</p>\n<h2 id=\"it-is-already-happening\">It is already happening</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Hybrid key exchange</strong> (classical X25519 plus ML-KEM, so you stay safe even if one of them is broken) is on by default in current Chrome, Edge and Firefox, and on Cloudflare and other big providers. A large share of web traffic already uses it.</li><li><strong>OpenSSH 10</strong> made a hybrid ML-KEM key exchange its default.</li><li><strong>Signal</strong> (PQXDH) and <strong>Apple iMessage</strong> (PQ3) added post-quantum protection to their messaging protocols.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"what-it-means-for-blockchains\">What it means for blockchains</h2>\n<p>Bitcoin and Ethereum accounts are secured by ECDSA on secp256k1, which Shor's algorithm would break. The details matter:</p>\n<ul><li>An address is a <strong>hash</strong> of a public key. While the public key has never been revealed, a quantum attacker has nothing to work on.</li><li>The public key is revealed the first time an account <strong>sends</strong> a transaction (and was revealed immediately in early Bitcoin &quot;pay to public key&quot; outputs). Those accounts would be exposed.</li><li>Migrating means new signature schemes, new address types and a way to move funds safely before attackers can. Proposals exist in both ecosystems, and smart-contract wallets (account abstraction) make it easier on Ethereum to change how an account verifies signatures.</li></ul>\n<p>The practical takeaways today: avoid reusing addresses where you can, keep an eye on your chain's migration plans, and design new protocols so the signature scheme can change.</p>\n<h2 id=\"crypto-agility-build-for-change\">Crypto agility: build for change</h2>\n<p>The real lesson of post-quantum is that <strong>algorithms have a shelf life</strong>. Systems that hard-code one cipher everywhere need a rewrite to change it. Agile systems can rotate algorithms like they rotate keys:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Version every ciphertext and signature</strong> with the algorithm and key that produced it.</li><li><strong>Keep crypto behind one module</strong> in your codebase, so a change happens in one place.</li><li><strong>Keep an inventory</strong>: where do you use RSA, ECDH, ECDSA, and which data must stay secret for how long?</li><li><strong>Test rotation</strong>: decrypt old versions, write new ones, and migrate in the background.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>","quiz":[{"q":"Which survives a large quantum computer with only a modest loss of security?","options":["ECDSA on secp256k1","RSA-2048","AES-256 and SHA-256"],"answer":2,"explain":"Grover's algorithm roughly halves symmetric security; 256-bit keys leave plenty."},{"q":"What is &quot;harvest now, decrypt later&quot;?","options":["Recording encrypted traffic today to decrypt once a quantum computer can break the key exchange","Mining tokens now to sell later","Backing up keys before they expire"],"answer":0,"explain":"It is why post-quantum key exchange is being deployed years before such a computer exists."},{"q":"Why is hybrid key exchange (X25519 plus ML-KEM) used rather than ML-KEM alone?","options":["ML-KEM does not work on phones","The connection stays secure as long as either algorithm holds, which protects against a flaw in the newer one","It is required for HTTP/1.1"],"answer":1,"explain":"Defence in depth applies to algorithms too."},{"q":"Why put a version and key ID on every ciphertext?","options":["To make it longer","So you can rotate keys and change algorithms later while still reading old data","Because AES requires it"],"answer":1,"explain":"That is crypto agility: change is a deployment, not a rewrite."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Check your SSH supports post-quantum key exchange","file":"Terminal","lang":"bash","run":false,"prompt":"<p>List the key-exchange algorithms your SSH client supports and filter for the post-quantum ones:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">ssh -Q kex | grep -i -e mlkem -e sntrup</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>mlkem768x25519-sha256</code> and <code>sntrup761x25519-sha512</code> are hybrid post-quantum key exchanges. No output means your OpenSSH is old: update it.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"ssh -Q kex | grep -i -e mlkem -e sntrup","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*ssh\\s+-Q\\s+kex\\s*\\|","text":"Start with <code>ssh -Q kex |</code> (<code>-Q kex</code> queries the supported key-exchange algorithms)."},{"re":"\\|\\s*grep\\s+-i\\s+-e\\s+mlkem\\s+-e\\s+sntrup\\s*$","text":"Then <code>| grep -i -e mlkem -e sntrup</code> (each <code>-e</code> adds a pattern)."}],"explain":"<p>Your servers decide too: check <code>sshd -T | grep kexalgorithms</code> on a server you run. For TLS, a recent OpenSSL (3.5 or later) supports ML-KEM hybrids, and most CDNs enable them for you.</p>"},{"title":"Version your ciphertexts","file":"envelope-format.js","lang":"js","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write a tiny format that records the version and key ID with every ciphertext, and refuses versions it does not know:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const VERSION = 'v1';\n\nexport const pack = (keyId, iv, data) =&gt; [VERSION, keyId, iv.toString('base64'), data.toString('base64')].join(':');\n\nexport function unpack(text) {\n  const [version, keyId, iv, data] = text.split(':');\n  if (version !== VERSION) throw new Error(`Unsupported ciphertext version: ${version}`);\n  return { keyId, iv: Buffer.from(iv, 'base64'), data: Buffer.from(data, 'base64') };\n}</code></pre></div>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const VERSION = 'v1';\n\nexport const pack = (keyId, iv, data) => [VERSION, keyId, iv.toString('base64'), data.toString('base64')].join(':');\n\nexport function unpack(text) {\n  const [version, keyId, iv, data] = text.split(':');\n  if (version !== VERSION) throw new Error(`Unsupported ciphertext version: ${version}`);\n  return { keyId, iv: Buffer.from(iv, 'base64'), data: Buffer.from(data, 'base64') };\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+VERSION\\s*=\\s*['\"]v1['\"]","text":"Line 1: <code>const VERSION = 'v1';</code>"},{"re":"export\\s+const\\s+pack\\s*=\\s*\\(\\s*keyId\\s*,\\s*iv\\s*,\\s*data\\s*\\)\\s*=>\\s*\\[\\s*VERSION\\s*,\\s*keyId\\s*,\\s*iv\\.toString\\(\\s*['\"]base64['\"]\\s*\\)\\s*,\\s*data\\.toString\\(\\s*['\"]base64['\"]\\s*\\)\\s*\\]\\.join\\(\\s*['\"]:['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"<code>pack</code> joins the four parts with colons (Base64 never contains a colon): <code>export const pack = (keyId, iv, data) =&gt; [VERSION, keyId, iv.toString('base64'), data.toString('base64')].join(':');</code>"},{"re":"export\\s+function\\s+unpack\\(\\s*text\\s*\\)\\s*\\{","text":"<code>export function unpack(text) {</code>"},{"re":"const\\s*\\[\\s*version\\s*,\\s*keyId\\s*,\\s*iv\\s*,\\s*data\\s*\\]\\s*=\\s*text\\.split\\(\\s*['\"]:['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"Split it back with array destructuring: <code>const [version, keyId, iv, data] = text.split(':');</code>"},{"re":"if\\s*\\(\\s*version\\s*!==\\s*VERSION\\s*\\)\\s*throw\\s+new\\s+Error\\(\\s*`Unsupported ciphertext version: \\$\\{version\\}`\\s*\\)","text":"Refuse unknown versions loudly: <code>if (version !== VERSION)</code> then <code>throw new Error(...)</code> with a template literal (backticks) containing Unsupported ciphertext version: ${version}"},{"re":"return\\s*\\{\\s*keyId\\s*,\\s*iv\\s*:\\s*Buffer\\.from\\(\\s*iv\\s*,\\s*['\"]base64['\"]\\s*\\)\\s*,\\s*data\\s*:\\s*Buffer\\.from\\(\\s*data\\s*,\\s*['\"]base64['\"]\\s*\\)\\s*\\}","text":"Return the parts as bytes: <code>return { keyId, iv: Buffer.from(iv, 'base64'), data: Buffer.from(data, 'base64') };</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*\\}\\s*$","text":"Close the function with <code>}</code>."}],"explain":"<p>A stored value now looks like <code>v1:kms-2026-11:Hq3...:9fK...</code>. When you move to a new algorithm (or a hybrid post-quantum scheme for anything public-key), add <code>v2</code>, teach <code>unpack</code> to read both, write only <code>v2</code>, and re-encrypt <code>v1</code> records in the background. The key ID tells you which key to fetch, so key rotation works the same way.</p>"}],"text":"What a quantum computer would break A large, error-corrected quantum computer does not exist yet, and nobody knows exactly when one will. If one is built, two algorithms matter: Algorithm Affects Impact Shor's algorithm RSA, Diffie-Hellman, elliptic curves (ECDH, ECDSA, Ed25519, and the secp256k1 curve behind Bitcoin and Ethereum signatures) Broken. Private keys can be computed from public keys Grover's algorithm Symmetric ciphers and hashes (AES, ChaCha20, SHA-256, Keccak) Weakened, roughly halving the security level. AES-256 and SHA-256 remain fine So symmetric encryption, the subject of most of this track, survives. The public-key parts (key exchange and signatures) need replacing. Harvest now, decrypt later Why worry before the machine exists? Because encrypted traffic can be recorded today and decrypted later . Anything that must stay secret for ten or more years (medical records, state secrets, long-lived business data, private keys sent over a network) is already at risk if its key exchange is classical. Signatures are less urgent in that respect: a forged signature only matters once a quantum computer can produce it. The exception is anything long-lived that is anchored to a public key, such as root certificates, firmware signing keys, and blockchain accounts. The new standards In August 2024 NIST published the first post-quantum standards: Standard Formerly Job FIPS 203: ML-KEM Kyber Key exchange (replaces ECDH) FIPS 204: ML-DSA Dilithium Digital signatures (replaces ECDSA and RSA signatures) FIPS 205: SLH-DSA SPHINCS+ Hash-based signatures, slower and larger but based on very conservative assumptions NIST later picked HQC as a backup key-exchange algorithm built on different maths, and a further signature standard (FN-DSA, from Falcon) is on the way. The US timeline 2022 : National Security Memorandum 10 set the goal of moving US federal systems to quantum-resistant cryptography by 2035 , and the Quantum Computing Cybersecurity Preparedness Act required agencies to inventory their quantum-vulnerable systems. NSA's CNSA 2.0 sets deadlines for national security systems to prefer and then require post-quantum algorithms, finishing in the early to mid 2030s. NIST's draft transition plan (IR 8547) proposes deprecating today's quantum-vulnerable public-key algorithms around 2030 and disallowing them by 2035 . Government suppliers and regulated industries will follow these dates, and everyone else's vendors will follow them. It is already happening Hybrid key exchange (classical X25519 plus ML-KEM, so you stay safe even if one of them is broken) is on by default in current Chrome, Edge and Firefox, and on Cloudflare and other big providers. A large share of web traffic already uses it. OpenSSH 10 made a hybrid ML-KEM key exchange its default. Signal (PQXDH) and Apple iMessage (PQ3) added post-quantum protection to their messaging protocols. What it means for blockchains Bitcoin and Ethereum accounts are secured by ECDSA on secp256k1, which Shor's algorithm would break. The details matter: An address is a hash of a public key. While the public key has never been revealed, a quantum attacker has nothing to work on. The public key is revealed the first time an account sends a transaction (and was revealed immediately in early Bitcoin pay to public key outputs). Those accounts would be exposed. Migrating means new signature schemes, new address types and a way to move funds safely before attackers can. Proposals exist in both ecosystems, and smart-contract wallets (account abstraction) make it easier on Ethereum to change how an account verifies signatures. The practical takeaways today: avoid reusing addresses where you can, keep an eye on your chain's migration plans, and design new protocols so the signature scheme can change. Crypto agility: build for change The real lesson of post-quantum is that algorithms have a shelf life . Systems that hard-code one cipher everywhere need a rewrite to change it. Agile systems can rotat","track":"encrypt"},{"id":"tok-basics","title":"Tokenomics 101: supply, market cap and FDV","level":"beginner","minutes":20,"summary":"The numbers every token has, how to calculate them, and why a \"cheap\" token often is not.","html":"<h2 id=\"what-tokenomics-is\">What tokenomics is</h2>\n<p><strong>Tokenomics</strong> (token economics) is the design of a token's supply, distribution and incentives: how many exist, who gets them and when, what they are used for, and what makes people want to hold or use them. Good tokenomics cannot save a useless product, but bad tokenomics can sink a good one.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-three-supply-numbers\">The three supply numbers</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Max supply</strong></td><td>The most that can ever exist. Bitcoin: 21 million. Some tokens have no cap.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total supply</strong></td><td>Tokens created so far (minus any burned), including locked ones.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Circulating supply</strong></td><td>Tokens actually unlocked and able to trade today.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"market-cap-and-fdv\">Market cap and FDV</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">Market cap = price x circulating supply\nFDV (fully diluted valuation) = price x max supply</code></pre></div>\n<p><strong>Example.</strong> A token trades at $0.50. Max supply is 1,000,000,000. Only 100,000,000 (10%) circulate.</p>\n<ul><li>Market cap = $0.50 x 100M = <strong>$50 million</strong></li><li>FDV = $0.50 x 1B = <strong>$500 million</strong></li><li>FDV is <strong>10x</strong> the market cap.</li></ul>\n<p>That gap is the supply still to come. If the other 900 million tokens unlock over four years, demand must grow roughly tenfold just to keep the price at $0.50. This <strong>low float, high FDV</strong> pattern has burned many buyers.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"price-is-not-value\">Price is not value</h2>\n<p>A token at $0.0001 is not &quot;cheap&quot; and a token at $3,000 is not &quot;expensive&quot;. Price on its own means nothing without supply.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Token</th><th>Price</th><th>Circulating supply</th><th>Market cap</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A</td><td>$0.0001</td><td>900 trillion</td><td>$90 billion</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>$40.00</td><td>2 million</td><td>$80 million</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Token A &quot;only&quot; needs to reach $1 to make you rich? That would be a market cap of $900 trillion, many times the value of every company on Earth. This trap is called <strong>unit bias</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"supply-over-time\">Supply over time</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Fixed supply</strong> (Bitcoin): new coins are issued on a schedule that halves roughly every four years, approaching 21 million.</li><li><strong>Inflationary</strong>: new tokens keep being created (often as staking rewards). Holders who do not earn the new tokens are diluted.</li><li><strong>Deflationary pressure</strong>: tokens are <strong>burned</strong> (destroyed). Ethereum burns the base fee of every transaction, so its supply can shrink when the network is busy.</li><li><strong>Elastic / stable</strong>: supply expands and contracts to hold a target price (stablecoins). Fiat-backed stablecoins like USDC instead mint and redeem against real reserves.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"questions-to-ask-about-any-token\">Questions to ask about any token</h2>\n<ol><li>What is the max supply, and how much circulates today?</li><li>What is the FDV to market cap ratio?</li><li>When do the locked tokens unlock, and who gets them?</li><li>Is new supply being created? By whom, and why?</li><li>What is the token <strong>used for</strong>, and would anyone want it if the price stopped going up?</li></ol>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-try\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Try it</p><p>Open the <a href=\"#token-sim\">Tokenomics Simulator</a> and load the <strong>Low float, high FDV</strong> preset. Look at the FDV to market cap ratio, then the circulating supply curve. That gap is what this lesson is about.</p></aside>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>","quiz":[{"q":"Price $2, circulating supply 50 million, max supply 500 million. What is the market cap?","options":["$100 million","$1 billion","$250 million"],"answer":0,"explain":"Market cap uses circulating supply: 2 x 50M = $100M. FDV would be $1B."},{"q":"In the same example, what does the 10x gap between FDV and market cap suggest?","options":["The token is undervalued by 10x","90% of the supply is still to arrive, which will dilute holders unless demand grows a lot","The token has no max supply"],"answer":1,"explain":"The gap is future supply. It has to be absorbed by future demand."},{"q":"Token X costs $0.00001. Why is it not necessarily cheap?","options":["Low-priced tokens are always scams","Value depends on price times supply; with a huge supply, the market cap may already be enormous","Prices below one cent are not allowed on exchanges"],"answer":1,"explain":"Unit bias: always compare market caps, never prices."},{"q":"What does burning tokens do?","options":["Permanently removes them from supply","Locks them for a year","Sends them to the team"],"answer":0,"explain":"Burned tokens are destroyed. Whether that supports price depends on how burning compares with new issuance and demand."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Market cap and FDV","file":"valuation.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Five lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const price = 0.5;</code></li><li><code>const circulating = 100_000_000;</code></li><li><code>const maxSupply = 1_000_000_000;</code></li><li>Print the market cap: <code>price * circulating</code></li><li>Print the FDV: <code>price * maxSupply</code></li></ol>\n<p>(Underscores in numbers are allowed and just make them readable.)</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const price = 0.5;\nconst circulating = 100_000_000;\nconst maxSupply = 1_000_000_000;\nconsole.log(price * circulating);\nconsole.log(price * maxSupply);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+price\\s*=\\s*0\\.5","text":"Line 1: <code>const price = 0.5;</code>"},{"re":"const\\s+circulating\\s*=\\s*100_000_000","text":"Line 2: <code>const circulating = 100_000_000;</code>"},{"re":"const\\s+maxSupply\\s*=\\s*1_000_000_000","text":"Line 3: <code>const maxSupply = 1_000_000_000;</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*price\\s*\\*\\s*circulating\\s*\\)","text":"Line 4: <code>console.log(price * circulating);</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*price\\s*\\*\\s*maxSupply\\s*\\)","text":"Line 5: <code>console.log(price * maxSupply);</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Output: <code>50000000</code> (a $50M market cap) and <code>500000000</code> (a $500M FDV). Nine-tenths of the valuation is supply that does not exist on the market yet.</p>"},{"title":"The FDV gap","file":"ratio.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Three lines: <code>const marketCap = 50_000_000;</code>, <code>const fdv = 500_000_000;</code>, then print <code>fdv / marketCap</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const marketCap = 50_000_000;\nconst fdv = 500_000_000;\nconsole.log(fdv / marketCap);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+marketCap\\s*=\\s*50_000_000","text":"Line 1: <code>const marketCap = 50_000_000;</code> (capital C)."},{"re":"const\\s+fdv\\s*=\\s*500_000_000","text":"Line 2: <code>const fdv = 500_000_000;</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*fdv\\s*\\/\\s*marketCap\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3: <code>console.log(fdv / marketCap);</code>"}],"explain":""}],"text":"What tokenomics is Tokenomics (token economics) is the design of a token's supply, distribution and incentives: how many exist, who gets them and when, what they are used for, and what makes people want to hold or use them. Good tokenomics cannot save a useless product, but bad tokenomics can sink a good one. The three supply numbers Metric Meaning Max supply The most that can ever exist. Bitcoin: 21 million. Some tokens have no cap. Total supply Tokens created so far (minus any burned), including locked ones. Circulating supply Tokens actually unlocked and able to trade today. Market cap and FDV Market cap = price x circulating supply FDV (fully diluted valuation) = price x max supply Example. A token trades at $0.50. Max supply is 1,000,000,000. Only 100,000,000 (10%) circulate. Market cap = $0.50 x 100M = $50 million FDV = $0.50 x 1B = $500 million FDV is 10x the market cap. That gap is the supply still to come. If the other 900 million tokens unlock over four years, demand must grow roughly tenfold just to keep the price at $0.50. This low float, high FDV pattern has burned many buyers. Price is not value A token at $0.0001 is not cheap and a token at $3,000 is not expensive . Price on its own means nothing without supply. Token Price Circulating supply Market cap A $0.0001 900 trillion $90 billion B $40.00 2 million $80 million Token A only needs to reach $1 to make you rich? That would be a market cap of $900 trillion, many times the value of every company on Earth. This trap is called unit bias . Supply over time Fixed supply (Bitcoin): new coins are issued on a schedule that halves roughly every four years, approaching 21 million. Inflationary : new tokens keep being created (often as staking rewards). Holders who do not earn the new tokens are diluted. Deflationary pressure : tokens are burned (destroyed). Ethereum burns the base fee of every transaction, so its supply can shrink when the network is busy. Elastic / stable : supply expands and contracts to hold a target price (stablecoins). Fiat-backed stablecoins like USDC instead mint and redeem against real reserves. Questions to ask about any token What is the max supply, and how much circulates today? What is the FDV to market cap ratio? When do the locked tokens unlock, and who gets them? Is new supply being created? By whom, and why? What is the token used for , and would anyone want it if the price stopped going up? Try it Open the Tokenomics Simulator and load the Low float, high FDV preset. Look at the FDV to market cap ratio, then the circulating supply curve. That gap is what this lesson is about.","track":"tokenomics"},{"id":"tok-allocation","title":"Allocation and utility","level":"beginner","minutes":25,"summary":"Who receives tokens and why, what a token is actually for, and how value flows (or does not) to holders.","html":"<h2 id=\"who-gets-the-tokens\">Who gets the tokens</h2>\n<p>Every token's supply is split between groups. A typical launch includes some of these:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Purpose</th><th>What to look for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Team</strong></td><td>Pay and motivate builders</td><td>Long vesting (3 to 4 years) with a 12-month cliff</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Investors</strong></td><td>Funding before launch</td><td>Vesting; their entry price versus yours</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Advisors</strong></td><td>Expertise and network</td><td>Small share, vested</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Community / ecosystem</strong></td><td>Grants, rewards, incentives, partnerships</td><td>Who controls it, and how it is released</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Treasury</strong></td><td>Long-term operations, run by the project or a DAO</td><td>Transparency, multisig or governance control</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Liquidity</strong></td><td>Seeding trading pools at launch</td><td>Locked or protocol-owned</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Airdrop</strong></td><td>Reward early users, decentralise ownership</td><td>Sybil resistance (one person, many wallets)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>There is no perfect split. Combined team, investor and advisor (&quot;insider&quot;) shares are frequently somewhere in the 30 to 50% range at launch; much higher than that tells you who the project is really built for. Context matters: what matters most is that the split is justified, disclosed and vested.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-is-the-token-for\">What is the token for?</h2>\n<p>A token needs a job. Common <strong>utilities</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Utility</th><th>Example</th><th>Creates demand because</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Gas / fees</strong></td><td>ETH pays for every Ethereum transaction</td><td>Using the network requires it</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Staking / security</strong></td><td>Validators stake ETH to secure the chain</td><td>Locked tokens earn rewards and secure the system</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Governance</strong></td><td>Holders vote on protocol upgrades and treasury spend</td><td>Control over something valuable</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Collateral</strong></td><td>Deposited to borrow against</td><td>Unlocks liquidity for the holder</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Access</strong></td><td>Hold or spend to use features, content or communities</td><td>The product requires it</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Rewards / points</strong></td><td>Earned for activity</td><td>Drives usage (but is also new supply to sell)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Fee share / buybacks</strong></td><td>Protocol revenue flows to stakers or buys tokens</td><td>Links token value to real business performance</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"the-two-questions-that-matter-most\">The two questions that matter most</h2>\n<p><strong>1. Would the product work just as well without the token?</strong> If yes, the token is probably a fundraising tool, and its price depends on speculation alone.</p>\n<p><strong>2. How does value flow to holders?</strong> A protocol can make lots of money while its token captures none of it. Look for a clear, sustainable link: fees used to buy back and burn, real yield to stakers, or demand created by using the product.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sources-and-sinks\">Sources and sinks</h2>\n<p>Think of a token economy as a bathtub:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Sources (taps)</strong>: unlocks, emissions, rewards, airdrops. Each adds supply that someone might sell.</li><li><strong>Sinks (drains)</strong>: fees burned, tokens locked for staking or governance, tokens spent on in-app items, buybacks.</li></ul>\n<p>If the taps run faster than the drains and demand is not growing, the price falls. Most failed tokens simply had more taps than drains.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Red flags in an allocation</p><p>Insiders above about 40%. Short or no vesting for team and investors. A large &quot;marketing&quot; or &quot;ecosystem&quot; bucket controlled by one wallet with no schedule. Undisclosed allocations. A token whose only use is &quot;staking to earn more of the same token&quot;.</p></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-pro\"><p class=\"callout-title\">For marketers and founders</p><p>If you help brands launch tokens or token-gated communities, lead with utility and fairness, not price. Projects that publish clear allocations, on-chain vesting contracts and a real reason to hold the token build trust that hype cannot.</p></aside>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>","quiz":[{"q":"What is the main risk if team and investors hold 60% of supply with short vesting?","options":["Insiders can sell large amounts early and control governance votes","The token becomes deflationary","Gas fees rise"],"answer":0,"explain":"Concentrated, quickly-unlocking insider supply is one of the clearest red flags."},{"q":"A protocol earns millions in fees, but none of it reaches token holders and the token has no use in the product. What does that mean?","options":["The token price must rise with revenue","The token captures little or none of the protocol's value, so its price relies on speculation","The protocol is illegal"],"answer":1,"explain":"Revenue and token value are separate unless there is a mechanism linking them."},{"q":"Which of these is a token sink?","options":["A monthly team unlock","Staking rewards paid in new tokens","A fee that is burned every time the product is used"],"answer":2,"explain":"Sinks remove or lock supply. Unlocks and emissions are sources."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Check an allocation adds up","file":"allocation.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Three lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const allocation = { team: 18, investors: 17, community: 45, treasury: 20 };</code></li><li><code>const total = Object.values(allocation).reduce((sum, pct) =&gt; sum + pct, 0);</code></li><li><code>console.log(total === 100 ? 'Adds up' : 'Check the numbers');</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const allocation = { team: 18, investors: 17, community: 45, treasury: 20 };\nconst total = Object.values(allocation).reduce((sum, pct) => sum + pct, 0);\nconsole.log(total === 100 ? 'Adds up' : 'Check the numbers');","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+allocation\\s*=\\s*\\{\\s*team\\s*:\\s*18\\s*,\\s*investors\\s*:\\s*17\\s*,\\s*community\\s*:\\s*45\\s*,\\s*treasury\\s*:\\s*20\\s*\\}","text":"Line 1 is an object: <code>{ team: 18, investors: 17, community: 45, treasury: 20 }</code>"},{"re":"Object\\.values\\(\\s*allocation\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2 starts from <code>Object.values(allocation)</code>, which gives <code>[18, 17, 45, 20]</code>."},{"re":"\\.reduce\\(\\s*\\(\\s*sum\\s*,\\s*pct\\s*\\)\\s*=>\\s*sum\\s*\\+\\s*pct\\s*,\\s*0\\s*\\)","text":"then <code>.reduce((sum, pct) =&gt; sum + pct, 0)</code>"},{"re":"total\\s*===\\s*100\\s*\\?\\s*['\"]Adds up['\"]\\s*:\\s*['\"]Check the numbers['\"]","text":"Line 3: <code>console.log(total === 100 ? 'Adds up' : 'Check the numbers');</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"Flag insider-heavy allocations","file":"insiders.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Four lines: <code>const team = 18;</code>, <code>const investors = 17;</code>, <code>const insiders = team + investors;</code>, then print <code>insiders &gt; 40 ? 'Red flag' : 'OK'</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const team = 18;\nconst investors = 17;\nconst insiders = team + investors;\nconsole.log(insiders > 40 ? 'Red flag' : 'OK');","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+team\\s*=\\s*18","text":"Line 1: <code>const team = 18;</code>"},{"re":"const\\s+investors\\s*=\\s*17","text":"Line 2: <code>const investors = 17;</code>"},{"re":"const\\s+insiders\\s*=\\s*team\\s*\\+\\s*investors","text":"Line 3: <code>const insiders = team + investors;</code>"},{"re":"insiders\\s*>\\s*40\\s*\\?\\s*['\"]Red flag['\"]\\s*:\\s*['\"]OK['\"]","text":"Line 4: <code>console.log(insiders &gt; 40 ? 'Red flag' : 'OK');</code>"}],"explain":""}],"text":"Who gets the tokens Every token's supply is split between groups. A typical launch includes some of these: Group Purpose What to look for Team Pay and motivate builders Long vesting (3 to 4 years) with a 12-month cliff Investors Funding before launch Vesting; their entry price versus yours Advisors Expertise and network Small share, vested Community / ecosystem Grants, rewards, incentives, partnerships Who controls it, and how it is released Treasury Long-term operations, run by the project or a DAO Transparency, multisig or governance control Liquidity Seeding trading pools at launch Locked or protocol-owned Airdrop Reward early users, decentralise ownership Sybil resistance (one person, many wallets) There is no perfect split. Combined team, investor and advisor ( insider ) shares are frequently somewhere in the 30 to 50% range at launch; much higher than that tells you who the project is really built for. Context matters: what matters most is that the split is justified, disclosed and vested. What is the token for? A token needs a job. Common utilities : Utility Example Creates demand because Gas / fees ETH pays for every Ethereum transaction Using the network requires it Staking / security Validators stake ETH to secure the chain Locked tokens earn rewards and secure the system Governance Holders vote on protocol upgrades and treasury spend Control over something valuable Collateral Deposited to borrow against Unlocks liquidity for the holder Access Hold or spend to use features, content or communities The product requires it Rewards / points Earned for activity Drives usage (but is also new supply to sell) Fee share / buybacks Protocol revenue flows to stakers or buys tokens Links token value to real business performance The two questions that matter most 1. Would the product work just as well without the token? If yes, the token is probably a fundraising tool, and its price depends on speculation alone. 2. How does value flow to holders? A protocol can make lots of money while its token captures none of it. Look for a clear, sustainable link: fees used to buy back and burn, real yield to stakers, or demand created by using the product. Sources and sinks Think of a token economy as a bathtub: Sources (taps) : unlocks, emissions, rewards, airdrops. Each adds supply that someone might sell. Sinks (drains) : fees burned, tokens locked for staking or governance, tokens spent on in-app items, buybacks. If the taps run faster than the drains and demand is not growing, the price falls. Most failed tokens simply had more taps than drains. Red flags in an allocation Insiders above about 40%. Short or no vesting for team and investors. A large marketing or ecosystem bucket controlled by one wallet with no schedule. Undisclosed allocations. A token whose only use is staking to earn more of the same token . For marketers and founders If you help brands launch tokens or token-gated communities, lead with utility and fairness, not price. Projects that publish clear allocations, on-chain vesting contracts and a real reason to hold the token build trust that hype cannot.","track":"tokenomics"},{"id":"tok-vesting","title":"Vesting, cliffs and unlock schedules","level":"intermediate","minutes":30,"summary":"Calculate exactly how many tokens are unlocked on any date, read unlock charts, and spot dangerous cliffs.","html":"<h2 id=\"the-vocabulary\">The vocabulary</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>TGE (token generation event)</strong>: launch day, when trading starts.</li><li><strong>TGE unlock</strong>: the percentage of an allocation released immediately.</li><li><strong>Cliff</strong>: a waiting period with no further unlocks.</li><li><strong>Vesting period</strong>: the time over which the rest unlocks, usually monthly or continuously (every block).</li><li><strong>Linear vesting</strong>: equal amounts per period.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"two-common-cliff-styles\">Two common cliff styles</h2>\n<p><strong>Style A: linear from the cliff.</strong> &quot;12-month cliff, then 24 months linear.&quot; Nothing until month 12, then an equal slice every month until month 36.</p>\n<p><strong>Style B: lump at the cliff.</strong> &quot;36-month vesting with a 12-month cliff.&quot; Tokens accrue from day one, but none can be claimed until month 12, when everything accrued so far (12/36 = 33%) unlocks at once. Then the rest vests monthly. This is common in employment-style deals and creates a big one-day <strong>unlock wall</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"#token-sim\">Tokenomics Simulator</a> has a switch for each style. Try both with the same numbers and watch the bars.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-maths\">The maths</h2>\n<p>For one allocation, the fraction unlocked at month <code>m</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-try=\"1\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">function unlockedFraction(m, { tge = 0, cliff = 0, vest = 0, lumpAtCliff = false }) {\n  if (m &lt; cliff) return tge;                          // only the TGE unlock so far\n  if (vest === 0) return 1;                           // everything at the cliff\n  const vested = lumpAtCliff\n    ? Math.min(1, m / (cliff + vest))                 // accrued since launch\n    : Math.min(1, (m - cliff) / vest);                // linear starting at the cliff\n  return tge + (1 - tge) * vested;\n}\n\nconst team = { tge: 0, cliff: 12, vest: 24 };\nfor (const m of [0, 6, 11, 12, 13, 24, 36, 48]) {\n  console.log(`month ${m}: ${(unlockedFraction(m, team) * 100).toFixed(1)}% of team tokens unlocked`);\n}\n\nconst teamLump = { ...team, lumpAtCliff: true };\nconsole.log('lump style at month 12:', (unlockedFraction(12, teamLump) * 100).toFixed(1) + '%');</code></pre></div>\n<p>Add every allocation together, multiply by max supply, and you have the circulating supply curve.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"why-vesting-matters-to-price\">Why vesting matters to price</h2>\n<p>Every unlock gives someone tokens they may sell. Investors who bought at $0.02 are very happy to sell at $0.30. What matters is not only how much unlocks, but:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Relative size</strong>: a 2% unlock when 10% circulates grows supply by 20% in a day.</li><li><strong>Holder cost basis</strong>: early investors are deep in profit; airdrop recipients paid nothing.</li><li><strong>Holder intent</strong>: a treasury unlock that goes to a grants programme is different from a seed investor unlock.</li></ul>\n<p>Markets tend to anticipate large unlocks, so price pressure often appears in the weeks before, not just on the day.</p>\n<h2 id=\"reading-an-unlock-chart\">Reading an unlock chart</h2>\n<p>Sites like Tokenomist (formerly Token Unlocks) show schedules for live tokens. Look for:</p>\n<ol><li>The <strong>circulating supply today</strong> versus max supply.</li><li><strong>Steps</strong> in the curve: cliff dates for insiders.</li><li>The <strong>slope</strong> after the cliffs: steady monthly pressure.</li><li><strong>When the curve flattens</strong>: after that, new supply comes only from emissions.</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"vesting-on-chain\">Vesting on-chain</h2>\n<p>Promises in a PDF are not vesting. Trustworthy projects lock allocations in <strong>vesting contracts</strong> anyone can inspect. OpenZeppelin's <code>VestingWallet</code> releases tokens linearly between a start time and an end time to a beneficiary, and its cliff variant adds a cliff. Projects often use audited vesting platforms too. Either way, verify on a block explorer that the tokens are really locked.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Design guidelines that build trust</p><p>Team and investors: a 12-month cliff and 2 to 4 years of vesting. Avoid one-day walls by vesting monthly (or per block) after the cliff. Publish the schedule and contract addresses. Avoid tiny launch floats that let insiders print a huge paper valuation.</p></aside>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>","quiz":[{"q":"Team allocation: 0% at TGE, 12-month cliff, then 24 months linear (style A). What fraction is unlocked at month 24?","options":["100%","50%","33%"],"answer":1,"explain":"(24 - 12) / 24 = 0.5. Fully unlocked at month 36."},{"q":"Same numbers, but style B (lump at cliff, 36 months total). What unlocks at month 12?","options":["Nothing","About 33% in one go","50%"],"answer":1,"explain":"12 of 36 months have accrued, all released together when the cliff ends."},{"q":"10% of supply circulates. A 3% unlock happens next week. By how much does circulating supply grow?","options":["3%","30%","13%"],"answer":1,"explain":"3 / 10 = 30% more circulating tokens. Relative size matters more than the headline number."},{"q":"What makes a vesting schedule trustworthy?","options":["It is described in the whitepaper","The team promises on social media","The tokens are locked in a public vesting contract you can verify on-chain"],"answer":2,"explain":"Code anyone can read beats promises anyone can break."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Write a vesting function","file":"vesting.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write <code>unlocked(month, cliff, vest)</code>: return <code>0</code> before the cliff, otherwise <code>Math.min(1, (month - cliff) / vest)</code>. Then print <code>unlocked(24, 12, 24)</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"function unlocked(month, cliff, vest) {\n  if (month < cliff) return 0;\n  return Math.min(1, (month - cliff) / vest);\n}\nconsole.log(unlocked(24, 12, 24));","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"function\\s+unlocked\\s*\\(\\s*month\\s*,\\s*cliff\\s*,\\s*vest\\s*\\)\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>function unlocked(month, cliff, vest) {</code>"},{"re":"if\\s*\\(\\s*month\\s*<\\s*cliff\\s*\\)\\s*return\\s+0","text":"Line 2: <code>if (month &lt; cliff) return 0;</code>"},{"re":"return\\s+Math\\.min\\(\\s*1\\s*,\\s*\\(\\s*month\\s*-\\s*cliff\\s*\\)\\s*\\/\\s*vest\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3: <code>return Math.min(1, (month - cliff) / vest);</code>"},{"re":"unlocked\\(\\s*24\\s*,\\s*12\\s*,\\s*24\\s*\\)","text":"After the function: <code>console.log(unlocked(24, 12, 24));</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Output: <code>0.5</code>. Twelve months after a 12-month cliff, half of a 24-month vest has unlocked. <code>Math.min(1, ...)</code> caps it at 100% after the vest ends.</p>"},{"title":"How big is that unlock, really?","file":"unlock.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Circulating supply is 10% and 3% unlocks next week. Three lines: <code>const circulating = 10;</code>, <code>const unlock = 3;</code>, then print <code>(unlock / circulating) * 100</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const circulating = 10;\nconst unlock = 3;\nconsole.log((unlock / circulating) * 100);","also":["const circulating = 10;\nconst unlock = 3;\nconsole.log(unlock / circulating * 100);"],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+circulating\\s*=\\s*10","text":"Line 1: <code>const circulating = 10;</code>"},{"re":"const\\s+unlock\\s*=\\s*3","text":"Line 2: <code>const unlock = 3;</code>"},{"re":"unlock\\s*\\/\\s*circulating\\s*\\)?\\s*\\*\\s*100","text":"Line 3: <code>console.log((unlock / circulating) * 100);</code>"}],"explain":""}],"text":"The vocabulary TGE (token generation event) : launch day, when trading starts. TGE unlock : the percentage of an allocation released immediately. Cliff : a waiting period with no further unlocks. Vesting period : the time over which the rest unlocks, usually monthly or continuously (every block). Linear vesting : equal amounts per period. Two common cliff styles Style A: linear from the cliff. 12-month cliff, then 24 months linear. Nothing until month 12, then an equal slice every month until month 36. Style B: lump at the cliff. 36-month vesting with a 12-month cliff. Tokens accrue from day one, but none can be claimed until month 12, when everything accrued so far (12/36 = 33%) unlocks at once. Then the rest vests monthly. This is common in employment-style deals and creates a big one-day unlock wall . The Tokenomics Simulator has a switch for each style. Try both with the same numbers and watch the bars. The maths For one allocation, the fraction unlocked at month m : function unlockedFraction(m, { tge = 0, cliff = 0, vest = 0, lumpAtCliff = false }) { if (m cliff) return tge; // only the TGE unlock so far if (vest === 0) return 1; // everything at the cliff const vested = lumpAtCliff ? Math.min(1, m / (cliff + vest)) // accrued since launch : Math.min(1, (m - cliff) / vest); // linear starting at the cliff return tge + (1 - tge) * vested; } const team = { tge: 0, cliff: 12, vest: 24 }; for (const m of [0, 6, 11, 12, 13, 24, 36, 48]) { console.log(`month ${m}: ${(unlockedFraction(m, team) * 100).toFixed(1)}% of team tokens unlocked`); } const teamLump = { ...team, lumpAtCliff: true }; console.log('lump style at month 12:', (unlockedFraction(12, teamLump) * 100).toFixed(1) + '%'); Add every allocation together, multiply by max supply, and you have the circulating supply curve. Why vesting matters to price Every unlock gives someone tokens they may sell. Investors who bought at $0.02 are very happy to sell at $0.30. What matters is not only how much unlocks, but: Relative size : a 2% unlock when 10% circulates grows supply by 20% in a day. Holder cost basis : early investors are deep in profit; airdrop recipients paid nothing. Holder intent : a treasury unlock that goes to a grants programme is different from a seed investor unlock. Markets tend to anticipate large unlocks, so price pressure often appears in the weeks before, not just on the day. Reading an unlock chart Sites like Tokenomist (formerly Token Unlocks) show schedules for live tokens. Look for: The circulating supply today versus max supply. Steps in the curve: cliff dates for insiders. The slope after the cliffs: steady monthly pressure. When the curve flattens : after that, new supply comes only from emissions. Vesting on-chain Promises in a PDF are not vesting. Trustworthy projects lock allocations in vesting contracts anyone can inspect. OpenZeppelin's VestingWallet releases tokens linearly between a start time and an end time to a beneficiary, and its cliff variant adds a cliff. Projects often use audited vesting platforms too. Either way, verify on a block explorer that the tokens are really locked. Design guidelines that build trust Team and investors: a 12-month cliff and 2 to 4 years of vesting. Avoid one-day walls by vesting monthly (or per block) after the cliff. Publish the schedule and contract addresses. Avoid tiny launch floats that let insiders print a huge paper valuation.","track":"tokenomics"},{"id":"tok-amm","title":"AMMs, liquidity and price impact","level":"intermediate","minutes":35,"summary":"How x*y=k sets prices, why big trades move them, what liquidity providers earn and risk, and how launches use pools.","html":"<h2 id=\"from-order-books-to-formulas\">From order books to formulas</h2>\n<p>Traditional exchanges match buyers and sellers in an <strong>order book</strong>. An <strong>Automated Market Maker (AMM)</strong> replaces that with a pool of two tokens and a formula. Anyone can trade against the pool at any time, and anyone can add liquidity to it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-constant-product-formula\">The constant product formula</h2>\n<p>Uniswap v2 popularised the simplest design. A pool holds <code>x</code> of TOKEN and <code>y</code> of USDC, and trades must keep:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">x * y = k   (constant)\nprice of TOKEN in USDC = y / x</code></pre></div>\n<p>When you buy TOKEN, you add USDC (y goes up) and remove TOKEN (x goes down). To keep <code>k</code> constant, each extra token costs more than the last. The pool can never run out: the price just rises towards infinity.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-worked-trade\">A worked trade</h2>\n<p>Pool: 1,000,000 TOKEN and 250,000 USDC, so the price is <strong>$0.25</strong>. Fee: 0.3%. You buy with 10,000 USDC.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">USDC in after fee = 10,000 x 0.997                = 9,970\nTOKEN out         = x * in / (y + in)\n                  = 1,000,000 x 9,970 / 259,970   = 38,350.6 TOKEN\nYour average price = 10,000 / 38,350.6            = $0.2608\nPool price after   = 260,000 / 961,649.4           = $0.2704</code></pre></div>\n<p>You paid an average of 4.3% more than the starting price: about 4% from your own trade moving the price along the curve (<strong>price impact</strong>), plus the 0.3% fee. Bigger trades and smaller pools mean more impact. Run the numbers yourself in the <a href=\"#amm-lab\">AMM Lab</a>.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-try=\"1\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">function quote({ x, y, fee = 0.003 }, usdcIn) {\n  const inAfterFee = usdcIn * (1 - fee);\n  const out = (x * inAfterFee) / (y + inAfterFee);\n  const avg = usdcIn / out;\n  return { out, avg, impact: avg / (y / x) - 1, newPrice: (y + usdcIn) / (x - out) };\n}\n\nconst pool = { x: 1_000_000, y: 250_000 };\nfor (const size of [100, 1_000, 10_000, 50_000]) {\n  const q = quote(pool, size);\n  console.log(`Spend ${size} USDC: get ${q.out.toFixed(0)} TOKEN, impact ${(q.impact * 100).toFixed(2)}%`);\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"slippage-protection\">Slippage protection</h2>\n<p>Between signing your swap and it landing in a block, other trades can move the price. Your <strong>slippage tolerance</strong> sets the worst price you accept (as a minimum amount out). Too tight and the transaction fails; too loose and bots can sandwich you. A deadline stops a stale transaction executing hours later.</p>\n<h2 id=\"liquidity-providers\">Liquidity providers</h2>\n<p>Anyone can deposit both tokens (at the current ratio) and receive <strong>LP tokens</strong> representing their share of the pool. Every trade's fee stays in the pool, so LP shares grow in value with trading volume.</p>\n<p>The catch is <strong>impermanent loss</strong>: when the price moves, the pool automatically sells the rising token and buys the falling one. Compared with simply holding both, an LP ends up with less.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">IL = 2 * sqrt(r) / (1 + r) - 1      where r = new price / old price\n\nprice x2   -&gt; about -5.7%\nprice x4   -&gt; -20%\nprice x0.5 -&gt; about -5.7%</code></pre></div>\n<p>LPs profit only when fees earned exceed impermanent loss. Pools of two assets that move together (like two stablecoins) have little IL.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"concentrated-liquidity\">Concentrated liquidity</h2>\n<p>Uniswap v3 and later designs let LPs provide liquidity only within a price range. Capital is far more efficient (deeper liquidity near the current price), but positions need active management: if the price leaves your range, you earn no fees and hold only one token.</p>\n<h2 id=\"liquidity-at-launch\">Liquidity at launch</h2>\n<p>New tokens usually launch by seeding a pool. The ratio chosen sets the launch price, and the size sets how much price impact traders suffer.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Thin liquidity</strong> means wild price swings; a few buyers can pump it and a few sellers can crash it.</li><li>If the team holds the LP tokens, they can withdraw all the liquidity at any time. That is the classic <strong>rug pull</strong>. Locked or burned LP tokens, or protocol-owned liquidity, are signs of good faith.</li></ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Check before you trade a new token</p><p>Is the LP locked, and until when? Can the owner mint, pause or blacklist? Are buy and sell taxes adjustable? Who holds the top wallets? Block explorers' holder tabs and token scanners help, but reading the verified contract is best.</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"A pool holds 500,000 TOKEN and 100,000 USDC. What is TOKEN's price?","options":["$5.00","$0.20","$0.50"],"answer":1,"explain":"Price = y / x = 100,000 / 500,000."},{"q":"Why does a large buy get a worse average price than a small one in the same pool?","options":["Each unit bought moves the price along the x*y=k curve, so later units cost more","Large traders pay a higher fee percentage","Pools refuse large orders"],"answer":0,"explain":"That is price impact. Deeper pools reduce it."},{"q":"TOKEN's price doubles after you provide liquidity. Ignoring fees, compared with holding, you have:","options":["About 5.7% more","About 5.7% less","Exactly the same"],"answer":1,"explain":"Impermanent loss at 2x is about -5.7%. Fees have to make up the difference."},{"q":"What makes a &quot;rug pull&quot; possible after a launch?","options":["Locked LP tokens","The team holding unlocked LP tokens and withdrawing all the liquidity","Using a 0.3% fee"],"answer":1,"explain":"Whoever holds the LP tokens controls the liquidity."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Write the swap formula","file":"amm.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write <code>getAmountOut(amountIn, reserveIn, reserveOut)</code> with a 0.3% fee: <code>inWithFee</code> is <code>amountIn * 0.997</code>, and the function returns <code>(reserveOut * inWithFee) / (reserveIn + inWithFee)</code>. Then print <code>getAmountOut(10000, 250000, 1000000)</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"function getAmountOut(amountIn, reserveIn, reserveOut) {\n  const inWithFee = amountIn * 0.997;\n  return (reserveOut * inWithFee) / (reserveIn + inWithFee);\n}\nconsole.log(getAmountOut(10000, 250000, 1000000));","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"function\\s+getAmountOut\\s*\\(\\s*amountIn\\s*,\\s*reserveIn\\s*,\\s*reserveOut\\s*\\)\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>function getAmountOut(amountIn, reserveIn, reserveOut) {</code>"},{"re":"const\\s+inWithFee\\s*=\\s*amountIn\\s*\\*\\s*0\\.997","text":"Line 2: <code>const inWithFee = amountIn * 0.997;</code>"},{"re":"return\\s*\\(\\s*reserveOut\\s*\\*\\s*inWithFee\\s*\\)\\s*\\/\\s*\\(\\s*reserveIn\\s*\\+\\s*inWithFee\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3: <code>return (reserveOut * inWithFee) / (reserveIn + inWithFee);</code>"},{"re":"getAmountOut\\(\\s*10000\\s*,\\s*250000\\s*,\\s*1000000\\s*\\)","text":"Last line: <code>console.log(getAmountOut(10000, 250000, 1000000));</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Output: about <code>38350.58</code> tokens, matching the worked example. This is the same maths Uniswap v2's contracts run, written with integers on-chain.</p>"},{"title":"Impermanent loss in one line","file":"il.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write an arrow function <code>il</code> that takes <code>r</code> and returns <code>(2 * Math.sqrt(r)) / (1 + r) - 1</code>, then print <code>il(2)</code> and <code>il(4)</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const il = (r) => (2 * Math.sqrt(r)) / (1 + r) - 1;\nconsole.log(il(2));\nconsole.log(il(4));","also":["const il = r => (2 * Math.sqrt(r)) / (1 + r) - 1;\nconsole.log(il(2));\nconsole.log(il(4));"],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+il\\s*=\\s*\\(?\\s*r\\s*\\)?\\s*=>","text":"Line 1 starts <code>const il = (r) =&gt;</code>"},{"re":"\\(\\s*2\\s*\\*\\s*Math\\.sqrt\\(\\s*r\\s*\\)\\s*\\)\\s*\\/\\s*\\(\\s*1\\s*\\+\\s*r\\s*\\)\\s*-\\s*1","text":"The formula: <code>(2 * Math.sqrt(r)) / (1 + r) - 1</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*il\\(\\s*2\\s*\\)\\s*\\)","text":"<code>console.log(il(2));</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*il\\(\\s*4\\s*\\)\\s*\\)","text":"<code>console.log(il(4));</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Output: about <code>-0.057</code> (a 5.7% loss when the price doubles) and <code>-0.2</code> (20% when it quadruples), compared with just holding.</p>"}],"text":"From order books to formulas Traditional exchanges match buyers and sellers in an order book . An Automated Market Maker (AMM) replaces that with a pool of two tokens and a formula. Anyone can trade against the pool at any time, and anyone can add liquidity to it. The constant product formula Uniswap v2 popularised the simplest design. A pool holds x of TOKEN and y of USDC, and trades must keep: x * y = k (constant) price of TOKEN in USDC = y / x When you buy TOKEN, you add USDC (y goes up) and remove TOKEN (x goes down). To keep k constant, each extra token costs more than the last. The pool can never run out: the price just rises towards infinity. A worked trade Pool: 1,000,000 TOKEN and 250,000 USDC, so the price is $0.25 . Fee: 0.3%. You buy with 10,000 USDC. USDC in after fee = 10,000 x 0.997 = 9,970 TOKEN out = x * in / (y + in) = 1,000,000 x 9,970 / 259,970 = 38,350.6 TOKEN Your average price = 10,000 / 38,350.6 = $0.2608 Pool price after = 260,000 / 961,649.4 = $0.2704 You paid an average of 4.3% more than the starting price: about 4% from your own trade moving the price along the curve ( price impact ), plus the 0.3% fee. Bigger trades and smaller pools mean more impact. Run the numbers yourself in the AMM Lab . function quote({ x, y, fee = 0.003 }, usdcIn) { const inAfterFee = usdcIn * (1 - fee); const out = (x * inAfterFee) / (y + inAfterFee); const avg = usdcIn / out; return { out, avg, impact: avg / (y / x) - 1, newPrice: (y + usdcIn) / (x - out) }; } const pool = { x: 1_000_000, y: 250_000 }; for (const size of [100, 1_000, 10_000, 50_000]) { const q = quote(pool, size); console.log(`Spend ${size} USDC: get ${q.out.toFixed(0)} TOKEN, impact ${(q.impact * 100).toFixed(2)}%`); } Slippage protection Between signing your swap and it landing in a block, other trades can move the price. Your slippage tolerance sets the worst price you accept (as a minimum amount out). Too tight and the transaction fails; too loose and bots can sandwich you. A deadline stops a stale transaction executing hours later. Liquidity providers Anyone can deposit both tokens (at the current ratio) and receive LP tokens representing their share of the pool. Every trade's fee stays in the pool, so LP shares grow in value with trading volume. The catch is impermanent loss : when the price moves, the pool automatically sells the rising token and buys the falling one. Compared with simply holding both, an LP ends up with less. IL = 2 * sqrt(r) / (1 + r) - 1 where r = new price / old price price x2 - about -5.7% price x4 - -20% price x0.5 - about -5.7% LPs profit only when fees earned exceed impermanent loss. Pools of two assets that move together (like two stablecoins) have little IL. Concentrated liquidity Uniswap v3 and later designs let LPs provide liquidity only within a price range. Capital is far more efficient (deeper liquidity near the current price), but positions need active management: if the price leaves your range, you earn no fees and hold only one token. Liquidity at launch New tokens usually launch by seeding a pool. The ratio chosen sets the launch price, and the size sets how much price impact traders suffer. Thin liquidity means wild price swings; a few buyers can pump it and a few sellers can crash it. If the team holds the LP tokens, they can withdraw all the liquidity at any time. That is the classic rug pull . Locked or burned LP tokens, or protocol-owned liquidity, are signs of good faith. Check before you trade a new token Is the LP locked, and until when? Can the owner mint, pause or blacklist? Are buy and sell taxes adjustable? Who holds the top wallets? Block explorers' holder tabs and token scanners help, but reading the verified contract is best.","track":"tokenomics"},{"id":"tok-incentives","title":"Staking, emissions, burns and real yield","level":"intermediate","minutes":30,"summary":"Separate real returns from printed ones, design emission schedules, and understand when burns actually matter.","html":"<h2 id=\"emissions-the-printing-press\">Emissions: the printing press</h2>\n<p><strong>Emissions</strong> are new tokens created over time, usually to reward stakers, validators or liquidity providers. They are a cost paid by every holder through dilution. Done well, they buy something valuable: network security, liquidity, early users. Done badly, they pay people to farm and dump.</p>\n<p>Common schedules:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Schedule</th><th>Shape</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fixed per block</td><td>Same amount forever, so inflation % slowly falls</td><td>Many early chains</td></tr><tr><td>Halving</td><td>Cut in half on a schedule</td><td>Bitcoin, about every 4 years</td></tr><tr><td>Exponential decay</td><td>A % less each month</td><td>Many DeFi liquidity programmes</td></tr><tr><td>Dynamic</td><td>Depends on how much is staked or on usage</td><td>Ethereum's issuance under proof of stake</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"nominal-versus-real-yield\">Nominal versus real yield</h2>\n<p>&quot;Stake and earn 40% APY!&quot; Paid in what?</p>\n<p>If you earn 40% more tokens per year, and total supply also grows 40% per year from those same emissions, your <strong>share of the network is unchanged</strong>. You only avoided being diluted; non-stakers paid for it. And if the price falls because farmers sell their rewards, you can lose money while your token balance grows.</p>\n<p><strong>Real yield</strong> comes from actual revenue: trading fees, borrowing interest, protocol income, paid in ETH or stablecoins or used to buy tokens from the market. Ask every time: <em>where does this yield come from?</em> If the answer is &quot;new tokens&quot;, it is dilution with extra steps. If nobody can answer, be very careful.</p>\n<h2 id=\"burns\">Burns</h2>\n<p>Burning removes tokens permanently. It is often marketed as automatically good. It only helps when:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">net supply change = new issuance - tokens burned</code></pre></div>\n<p>is low or negative <strong>and</strong> demand holds up. Ethereum's EIP-1559 burns the base fee of every transaction, so when network usage is high, more ETH can be burned than issued. A &quot;burn&quot; of tokens the team never intended to sell, or a tiny burn alongside big emissions, changes little.</p>\n<p><strong>Buybacks</strong> use protocol revenue to buy tokens on the market (then burn them or send them to stakers or the treasury). They link token demand to business performance, which is why many mature protocols favour them.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"mercenary-capital\">Mercenary capital</h2>\n<p>In 2020's &quot;DeFi summer&quot;, protocols handed out huge token emissions to attract liquidity. Capital flooded in, farmed the rewards, sold them, and left the moment rewards dropped. Lessons the industry learned:</p>\n<ul><li>Reward <strong>behaviour you want to last</strong> (long-term locking, real usage) rather than just parked capital.</li><li>Taper emissions and announce schedules clearly.</li><li>Measure <strong>retention after rewards end</strong>, not total value locked during them.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Points programmes</strong> (track activity now, convert to tokens later) are the modern version. Same economics, delayed.</p>\n<h2 id=\"model-it\">Model it</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-try=\"1\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">// 5 years of supply: 100M initial, 20M emitted in year 1 decaying 30% a year, plus fee burns\nlet supply = 100_000_000;\nlet emission = 20_000_000;\nconst yearlyBurn = 4_000_000; // assume steady usage burns 4M tokens a year\n\nfor (let year = 1; year &lt;= 5; year++) {\n  const before = supply;\n  supply = supply + emission - yearlyBurn;\n  const inflation = (supply / before - 1) * 100;\n  console.log(`Year ${year}: emitted ${(emission / 1e6).toFixed(1)}M, supply ${(supply / 1e6).toFixed(1)}M, net inflation ${inflation.toFixed(1)}%`);\n  emission *= 0.7;\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>Try changing the burn to 10M and the decay to 0.5. When does net inflation turn negative?</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-pro\"><p class=\"callout-title\">The sustainability test</p><p>Imagine the token price drops 80% and stays there for a year. Do validators or stakers still have a reason to stay? Does the treasury still cover two years of costs? Do users still use the product? Designs that pass this test survive bear markets.</p></aside>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>","quiz":[{"q":"Staking pays 25% APY in new tokens, and supply inflates 25% a year from those rewards. What happens to a staker's share of total supply?","options":["It grows 25% a year","It stays about the same; non-stakers are diluted instead","It shrinks"],"answer":1,"explain":"Nominal yield from emissions mostly redistributes value from non-stakers to stakers."},{"q":"Which is real yield?","options":["Rewards paid in newly minted governance tokens","Stakers receiving a share of trading fees paid in ETH or stablecoins","A bonus for referring friends, paid in new tokens"],"answer":1,"explain":"Real yield comes from revenue that users actually paid."},{"q":"A protocol emits 10M tokens a year and burns 1M a year. What is net issuance?","options":["9M tokens a year of new supply","Deflationary, because it burns","11M tokens a year"],"answer":0,"explain":"Net change is issuance minus burn. A small burn does not offset large emissions."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Inflationary or deflationary?","file":"net.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Four lines: <code>const emitted = 10_000_000;</code>, <code>const burned = 1_000_000;</code>, <code>const net = emitted - burned;</code>, then print <code>net &gt; 0 ? 'Inflationary' : 'Deflationary'</code> and <code>net</code> in the same <code>console.log</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const emitted = 10_000_000;\nconst burned = 1_000_000;\nconst net = emitted - burned;\nconsole.log(net > 0 ? 'Inflationary' : 'Deflationary', net);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+emitted\\s*=\\s*10_000_000","text":"Line 1: <code>const emitted = 10_000_000;</code>"},{"re":"const\\s+burned\\s*=\\s*1_000_000","text":"Line 2: <code>const burned = 1_000_000;</code>"},{"re":"const\\s+net\\s*=\\s*emitted\\s*-\\s*burned","text":"Line 3: <code>const net = emitted - burned;</code>"},{"re":"net\\s*>\\s*0\\s*\\?\\s*['\"]Inflationary['\"]\\s*:\\s*['\"]Deflationary['\"]\\s*,\\s*net","text":"Line 4: <code>console.log(net &gt; 0 ? 'Inflationary' : 'Deflationary', net);</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"Real return after dilution","file":"real.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Three lines: <code>const apy = 0.25;</code>, <code>const inflation = 0.25;</code>, then print <code>((1 + apy) / (1 + inflation) - 1) * 100</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const apy = 0.25;\nconst inflation = 0.25;\nconsole.log(((1 + apy) / (1 + inflation) - 1) * 100);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+apy\\s*=\\s*0\\.25","text":"Line 1: <code>const apy = 0.25;</code>"},{"re":"const\\s+inflation\\s*=\\s*0\\.25","text":"Line 2: <code>const inflation = 0.25;</code>"},{"re":"\\(\\s*\\(\\s*1\\s*\\+\\s*apy\\s*\\)\\s*\\/\\s*\\(\\s*1\\s*\\+\\s*inflation\\s*\\)\\s*-\\s*1\\s*\\)\\s*\\*\\s*100","text":"Line 3: <code>console.log(((1 + apy) / (1 + inflation) - 1) * 100);</code> Count the brackets."}],"explain":"<p>Output: <code>0</code>. A 25% yield paid in new tokens, while supply grows 25%, leaves your real share exactly where it was.</p>"}],"text":"Emissions: the printing press Emissions are new tokens created over time, usually to reward stakers, validators or liquidity providers. They are a cost paid by every holder through dilution. Done well, they buy something valuable: network security, liquidity, early users. Done badly, they pay people to farm and dump. Common schedules: Schedule Shape Example Fixed per block Same amount forever, so inflation % slowly falls Many early chains Halving Cut in half on a schedule Bitcoin, about every 4 years Exponential decay A % less each month Many DeFi liquidity programmes Dynamic Depends on how much is staked or on usage Ethereum's issuance under proof of stake Nominal versus real yield Stake and earn 40% APY! Paid in what? If you earn 40% more tokens per year, and total supply also grows 40% per year from those same emissions, your share of the network is unchanged . You only avoided being diluted; non-stakers paid for it. And if the price falls because farmers sell their rewards, you can lose money while your token balance grows. Real yield comes from actual revenue: trading fees, borrowing interest, protocol income, paid in ETH or stablecoins or used to buy tokens from the market. Ask every time: where does this yield come from? If the answer is new tokens , it is dilution with extra steps. If nobody can answer, be very careful. Burns Burning removes tokens permanently. It is often marketed as automatically good. It only helps when: net supply change = new issuance - tokens burned is low or negative and demand holds up. Ethereum's EIP-1559 burns the base fee of every transaction, so when network usage is high, more ETH can be burned than issued. A burn of tokens the team never intended to sell, or a tiny burn alongside big emissions, changes little. Buybacks use protocol revenue to buy tokens on the market (then burn them or send them to stakers or the treasury). They link token demand to business performance, which is why many mature protocols favour them. Mercenary capital In 2020's DeFi summer , protocols handed out huge token emissions to attract liquidity. Capital flooded in, farmed the rewards, sold them, and left the moment rewards dropped. Lessons the industry learned: Reward behaviour you want to last (long-term locking, real usage) rather than just parked capital. Taper emissions and announce schedules clearly. Measure retention after rewards end , not total value locked during them. Points programmes (track activity now, convert to tokens later) are the modern version. Same economics, delayed. Model it // 5 years of supply: 100M initial, 20M emitted in year 1 decaying 30% a year, plus fee burns let supply = 100_000_000; let emission = 20_000_000; const yearlyBurn = 4_000_000; // assume steady usage burns 4M tokens a year for (let year = 1; year = 5; year++) { const before = supply; supply = supply + emission - yearlyBurn; const inflation = (supply / before - 1) * 100; console.log(`Year ${year}: emitted ${(emission / 1e6).toFixed(1)}M, supply ${(supply / 1e6).toFixed(1)}M, net inflation ${inflation.toFixed(1)}%`); emission *= 0.7; } Try changing the burn to 10M and the decay to 0.5. When does net inflation turn negative? The sustainability test Imagine the token price drops 80% and stays there for a year. Do validators or stakers still have a reason to stay? Does the treasury still cover two years of costs? Do users still use the product? Designs that pass this test survive bear markets.","track":"tokenomics"},{"id":"tok-mechanisms","title":"Mechanism design: ve-tokens, bonding curves and game theory","level":"advanced","minutes":40,"summary":"The designs advanced protocols use to align incentives, the maths behind them, and how they fail.","html":"<h2 id=\"thinking-in-incentives\">Thinking in incentives</h2>\n<p><strong>Mechanism design</strong> is designing the rules of a game so that people acting in their own interest produce the outcome you want. Every token system is a game: holders, stakers, traders, LPs, insiders, attackers. For each design decision, ask: <em>what is the most profitable thing a selfish, well-funded, clever person could do here?</em> Then make sure that thing is good for the system, or impossible.</p>\n<h2 id=\"game-theory-you-will-use\">Game theory you will use</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Nash equilibrium</strong>: a situation where nobody gains by changing strategy alone. Your design's equilibrium is what will actually happen, whatever the whitepaper hopes.</li><li><strong>Prisoner's dilemma</strong>: everyone would be better off if all held, but each individual is better off selling first. Unlock events often play out this way.</li><li><strong>Coordination and Schelling points</strong>: people converge on obvious focal points (round-number prices, unlock dates, snapshot rumours).</li><li><strong>Reflexivity</strong>: price affects fundamentals, which affect price. Rising price raises collateral values and staking APY in dollar terms, attracting more buyers, until it reverses and the loop runs backwards.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"vote-escrow-ve-tokenomics\">Vote-escrow (ve) tokenomics</h2>\n<p>Popularised by Curve: you lock tokens for a chosen time (up to 4 years) and receive non-transferable <strong>veTokens</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">voting power = tokens locked x (time remaining / maximum lock time)</code></pre></div>\n<p>Lock 1,000 tokens for 4 years: 1,000 veTokens. For 1 year: 250. Power decays linearly as the unlock date approaches, unless you extend the lock.</p>\n<p>veToken holders typically get:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Governance votes</strong>, including <strong>gauge votes</strong> that direct where new emissions go.</li><li><strong>Boosted rewards</strong> on their own liquidity.</li><li>A <strong>share of protocol fees</strong>.</li></ul>\n<p>Why it works: it rewards long-term commitment and removes supply from the market. Side effects: protocols compete to direct emissions, creating <strong>bribe markets</strong> where projects pay veToken holders to vote for their pools, and meta-protocols that pool locks. Liquidity of locked positions becomes a problem of its own.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"bonding-curves\">Bonding curves</h2>\n<p>A <strong>bonding curve</strong> is a contract that mints tokens when you buy and burns them when you sell, at a price set by a formula of the current supply:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">linear curve:  price(s) = m * s + b\ncost to buy from supply s1 to s2 = area under the curve\n                                 = m/2 * (s2^2 - s1^2) + b * (s2 - s1)</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-try=\"1\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">// Linear bonding curve: price rises 0.000001 USDC per token minted, starting at 0.01\nconst m = 0.000001, b = 0.01;\nconst price = (s) =&gt; m * s + b;\nconst cost = (s1, s2) =&gt; (m / 2) * (s2 ** 2 - s1 ** 2) + b * (s2 - s1);\n\nlet supply = 0;\nfor (const buy of [10_000, 10_000, 10_000]) {\n  const paid = cost(supply, supply + buy);\n  supply += buy;\n  console.log(`bought ${buy}, paid ${paid.toFixed(2)} USDC, price now ${price(supply).toFixed(4)}`);\n}\nconsole.log('reserve held by the curve:', cost(0, supply).toFixed(2), 'USDC');</code></pre></div>\n<p>Properties: always liquid (the contract is the counterparty), price discovery without an order book, and the reserve always covers selling everyone back down the curve. Risks: early buyers profit directly from later buyers, which drives speculation; front-running on each purchase; the shape of the curve hard-codes who wins. Many token launchpads use curves to bootstrap a token before moving it to an AMM.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"governance-attacks\">Governance attacks</h2>\n<p>If voting power is just token balance at the time of voting, an attacker can <strong>borrow</strong> tokens with a flash loan, vote, and repay in one transaction. In 2022 the Beanstalk protocol lost around $180 million this way: the attacker borrowed enough to pass a proposal sending the treasury to themselves.</p>\n<p>Defences:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Snapshot</strong> voting power at a past block, before the proposal.</li><li><strong>Timelocks</strong> between a vote passing and execution, so users can react.</li><li><strong>Quorums</strong>, vote delays, and guardians who can veto clearly malicious proposals.</li><li>Locked voting (ve-tokens) where power cannot be borrowed.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"when-designs-fail-stablecoin-death-spirals\">When designs fail: stablecoin death spirals</h2>\n<p>Algorithmic stablecoins that rely on a sister token to absorb volatility are the textbook reflexivity failure. In May 2022, TerraUSD lost its peg; redemptions minted ever more LUNA, LUNA's price collapsed, which made the peg less credible, which caused more redemptions. Tens of billions of dollars of value disappeared within days. The lesson: a mechanism that only works while confidence holds will eventually be tested by a loss of confidence.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-pro\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Stress-test every mechanism</p><p>For each design, write down: the intended equilibrium; what a whale with $50 million and a flash loan does; what happens if the price falls 90% in a week; what happens if the team disappears. If any answer is &quot;it collapses&quot;, redesign before launch.</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"You lock 2,000 tokens for 2 years in a ve system with a 4-year maximum. What is your initial voting power?","options":["2,000","1,000","4,000"],"answer":1,"explain":"2,000 x (2 / 4) = 1,000, decaying to zero as the unlock approaches."},{"q":"How did the 2022 Beanstalk governance attack work?","options":["The attacker flash-borrowed voting power, passed a malicious proposal and repaid within one transaction","The attacker guessed the admin's private key","A bug in an AMM pool"],"answer":0,"explain":"Voting power based on current balance can be borrowed. Snapshots and timelocks prevent it."},{"q":"On a linear bonding curve, who profits most if lots of people buy later?","options":["Early buyers, because later buys raise the price they can sell at","Late buyers","Nobody, prices are fixed"],"answer":0,"explain":"The curve guarantees rising prices with supply, transferring value from later to earlier buyers."},{"q":"What is reflexivity in token economies?","options":["Tokens that reflect light","A feedback loop where price changes alter fundamentals, which push price further the same way","Mirroring a token on another chain"],"answer":1,"explain":"Reflexive loops amplify booms and busts. Algorithmic stablecoins are the classic example."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Vote-escrow voting power","file":"ve.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write an arrow function <code>votingPower</code> that takes <code>tokens</code> and <code>yearsLocked</code> and returns <code>tokens * (yearsLocked / 4)</code>. Print <code>votingPower(2000, 2)</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const votingPower = (tokens, yearsLocked) => tokens * (yearsLocked / 4);\nconsole.log(votingPower(2000, 2));","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+votingPower\\s*=\\s*\\(\\s*tokens\\s*,\\s*yearsLocked\\s*\\)\\s*=>","text":"Line 1 starts <code>const votingPower = (tokens, yearsLocked) =&gt;</code>"},{"re":"tokens\\s*\\*\\s*\\(\\s*yearsLocked\\s*\\/\\s*4\\s*\\)","text":"then <code>tokens * (yearsLocked / 4);</code>"},{"re":"votingPower\\(\\s*2000\\s*,\\s*2\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>console.log(votingPower(2000, 2));</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"Cost to buy on a bonding curve","file":"curve.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write <code>cost</code> as an arrow function of <code>s1</code> and <code>s2</code>: <code>(0.000001 / 2) * (s2 ** 2 - s1 ** 2) + 0.01 * (s2 - s1)</code>. Print <code>cost(0, 10000)</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const cost = (s1, s2) => (0.000001 / 2) * (s2 ** 2 - s1 ** 2) + 0.01 * (s2 - s1);\nconsole.log(cost(0, 10000));","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+cost\\s*=\\s*\\(\\s*s1\\s*,\\s*s2\\s*\\)\\s*=>","text":"Line 1 starts <code>const cost = (s1, s2) =&gt;</code>"},{"re":"\\(\\s*0\\.000001\\s*\\/\\s*2\\s*\\)\\s*\\*\\s*\\(\\s*s2\\s*\\*\\*\\s*2\\s*-\\s*s1\\s*\\*\\*\\s*2\\s*\\)","text":"first term: <code>(0.000001 / 2) * (s2 ** 2 - s1 ** 2)</code>"},{"re":"\\+\\s*0\\.01\\s*\\*\\s*\\(\\s*s2\\s*-\\s*s1\\s*\\)","text":"second term: <code>+ 0.01 * (s2 - s1);</code>"},{"re":"cost\\(\\s*0\\s*,\\s*10000\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>console.log(cost(0, 10000));</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Output: about <code>150</code>: the first 10,000 tokens cost 150 USDC in total, matching the lesson's example (floating-point maths may show a tiny rounding tail).</p>"}],"text":"Thinking in incentives Mechanism design is designing the rules of a game so that people acting in their own interest produce the outcome you want. Every token system is a game: holders, stakers, traders, LPs, insiders, attackers. For each design decision, ask: what is the most profitable thing a selfish, well-funded, clever person could do here? Then make sure that thing is good for the system, or impossible. Game theory you will use Nash equilibrium : a situation where nobody gains by changing strategy alone. Your design's equilibrium is what will actually happen, whatever the whitepaper hopes. Prisoner's dilemma : everyone would be better off if all held, but each individual is better off selling first. Unlock events often play out this way. Coordination and Schelling points : people converge on obvious focal points (round-number prices, unlock dates, snapshot rumours). Reflexivity : price affects fundamentals, which affect price. Rising price raises collateral values and staking APY in dollar terms, attracting more buyers, until it reverses and the loop runs backwards. Vote-escrow (ve) tokenomics Popularised by Curve: you lock tokens for a chosen time (up to 4 years) and receive non-transferable veTokens . voting power = tokens locked x (time remaining / maximum lock time) Lock 1,000 tokens for 4 years: 1,000 veTokens. For 1 year: 250. Power decays linearly as the unlock date approaches, unless you extend the lock. veToken holders typically get: Governance votes , including gauge votes that direct where new emissions go. Boosted rewards on their own liquidity. A share of protocol fees . Why it works: it rewards long-term commitment and removes supply from the market. Side effects: protocols compete to direct emissions, creating bribe markets where projects pay veToken holders to vote for their pools, and meta-protocols that pool locks. Liquidity of locked positions becomes a problem of its own. Bonding curves A bonding curve is a contract that mints tokens when you buy and burns them when you sell, at a price set by a formula of the current supply: linear curve: price(s) = m * s + b cost to buy from supply s1 to s2 = area under the curve = m/2 * (s2^2 - s1^2) + b * (s2 - s1) // Linear bonding curve: price rises 0.000001 USDC per token minted, starting at 0.01 const m = 0.000001, b = 0.01; const price = (s) = m * s + b; const cost = (s1, s2) = (m / 2) * (s2 ** 2 - s1 ** 2) + b * (s2 - s1); let supply = 0; for (const buy of [10_000, 10_000, 10_000]) { const paid = cost(supply, supply + buy); supply += buy; console.log(`bought ${buy}, paid ${paid.toFixed(2)} USDC, price now ${price(supply).toFixed(4)}`); } console.log('reserve held by the curve:', cost(0, supply).toFixed(2), 'USDC'); Properties: always liquid (the contract is the counterparty), price discovery without an order book, and the reserve always covers selling everyone back down the curve. Risks: early buyers profit directly from later buyers, which drives speculation; front-running on each purchase; the shape of the curve hard-codes who wins. Many token launchpads use curves to bootstrap a token before moving it to an AMM. Governance attacks If voting power is just token balance at the time of voting, an attacker can borrow tokens with a flash loan, vote, and repay in one transaction. In 2022 the Beanstalk protocol lost around $180 million this way: the attacker borrowed enough to pass a proposal sending the treasury to themselves. Defences: Snapshot voting power at a past block, before the proposal. Timelocks between a vote passing and execution, so users can react. Quorums , vote delays, and guardians who can veto clearly malicious proposals. Locked voting (ve-tokens) where power cannot be borrowed. When designs fail: stablecoin death spirals Algorithmic stablecoins that rely on a sister token to absorb volatility are the textbook reflexivity failure. In May 2022, TerraUSD lost its peg; redemptions minted ever more LUNA, LUNA's price collapsed, which made the peg ","track":"tokenomics"},{"id":"tok-launch","title":"Launch playbook, red flags and the rules","level":"advanced","minutes":35,"summary":"How serious teams launch tokens, a checklist for judging any token, and the US rules (SEC, CFTC, FinCEN, OFAC, FTC, IRS and the states) you must know before selling or marketing one.","html":"<h2 id=\"a-responsible-launch-step-by-step\">A responsible launch, step by step</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Product first.</strong> A live product with real users, and a clear answer to &quot;why does this need a token?&quot;</li><li><strong>Design and model</strong> supply, allocation, vesting and incentives. Stress-test them (see the modelling lesson).</li><li><strong>Legal review</strong> in every country where you will market or sell. Do this before you announce anything.</li><li><strong>Build and audit</strong> the token, vesting, staking and any distribution contracts. Publish the audit.</li><li><strong>Distribution plan</strong>: who gets tokens and how. Airdrops need sybil filtering (one person, one allocation).</li><li><strong>Liquidity plan</strong>: how the first pools are seeded and whether LP tokens are locked or protocol-owned. If market makers are involved, publish the terms.</li><li><strong>Transparency</strong>: publish the full allocation, the vesting schedule and every contract address before launch.</li><li><strong>Launch day</strong>: monitor contracts, pools and social channels; have an incident plan.</li><li><strong>After launch</strong>: report regularly on treasury, unlocks and emissions. Trust is earned over years.</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"distribution-methods\">Distribution methods</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>How it works</th><th>Watch out for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Airdrop</strong></td><td>Free tokens to past users or a community</td><td>Farmers with thousands of wallets; instant sell pressure</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Points then airdrop</strong></td><td>Track activity, convert to tokens later</td><td>Users feel misled if conversion disappoints</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Fair launch</strong></td><td>No pre-sale; everyone buys at the same terms</td><td>Bots and snipers at launch</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Liquidity bootstrapping pool (LBP)</strong></td><td>Price starts high and falls over days so buyers can find a fair price</td><td>Complex for newcomers</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Public sale (ICO / IDO)</strong></td><td>Sell tokens for funds</td><td>Heavy securities-law implications</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Bonding curve launchpad</strong></td><td>Price rises with each mint, then migrates to an AMM</td><td>Highly speculative; early-buyer advantage</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"red-flag-checklist-for-any-token\">Red-flag checklist for any token</h2>\n<p>Use this before buying, promoting or advising on a token:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Contract</strong>: unverified source code; owner can mint without limit, pause trading, blacklist wallets, or change fees and taxes; a proxy that the team can upgrade instantly.</li><li><strong>Liquidity</strong>: LP tokens unlocked or held by one wallet; tiny liquidity relative to market cap.</li><li><strong>Holders</strong>: a handful of wallets hold most of the supply (check the explorer's holders tab).</li><li><strong>Tokenomics</strong>: low float with a huge FDV; insiders above about 40%; short or no vesting; undisclosed allocations.</li><li><strong>Yield</strong>: high APY with no revenue source.</li><li><strong>Team and marketing</strong>: anonymous team plus pressure to buy now; paid promotions without disclosure; guaranteed returns.</li><li><strong>Honeypot behaviour</strong>: you can buy but cannot sell (test with a tiny amount, or use a scanner, before anything else).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"the-rules-us-regulation-you-must-know\">The rules: US regulation you must know</h2>\n<p>This course follows <strong>United States law</strong>: most token projects, exchanges and launchpads either operate in the US or serve US users, and US agencies have been the most active enforcers. This is general awareness, <strong>not legal advice</strong>. US crypto rules have been changing quickly since 2025, through new laws, court cases and agency guidance, so speak to a qualified US securities lawyer before launching or promoting a token.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Who</th><th>What they police</th><th>Why it matters for a token</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>SEC</strong> (Securities and Exchange Commission)</td><td>Securities: offering, selling and promoting investments</td><td>If your token is an investment contract, selling it needs registration or an exemption, and promoters must disclose that they are paid</td></tr><tr><td><strong>CFTC</strong> (Commodity Futures Trading Commission)</td><td>Commodities and their derivatives (bitcoin and ether have been treated as commodities)</td><td>Fraud and manipulation in commodity markets, and anything leveraged or derivative</td></tr><tr><td><strong>FinCEN</strong> (Treasury)</td><td>The Bank Secrecy Act: money transmission and anti-money-laundering</td><td>Businesses that transmit value for others may need to register as a money services business and run KYC and AML programmes</td></tr><tr><td><strong>OFAC</strong> (Treasury)</td><td>Sanctions</td><td>Dealing with sanctioned people, countries or listed crypto addresses is illegal for US persons</td></tr><tr><td><strong>FTC</strong> (Federal Trade Commission)</td><td>Deceptive advertising and paid endorsements</td><td>Influencers and agencies must clearly disclose paid promotions; misleading claims are illegal</td></tr><tr><td><strong>IRS</strong></td><td>Tax</td><td>Digital assets are treated as property; trades, airdrops and staking rewards usually create tax events, and brokers now report crypto sales on Form 1099-DA</td></tr><tr><td><strong>States</strong></td><td>Money transmitter licences, plus regimes such as New York's BitLicense and California's Digital Financial Assets Law</td><td>Serving residents of some states needs a licence</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"is-your-token-a-security-the-howey-test\">Is your token a security? The Howey test</h3>\n<p>The SEC and the courts apply the <strong>Howey test</strong> (from a 1946 Supreme Court case about orange groves): it is an <strong>investment contract</strong>, and so a security, if people <strong>invest money</strong> in a <strong>common enterprise</strong> expecting <strong>profits from the efforts of others</strong>. The label does not matter; the economic reality does. Selling tokens to fund development while promising the team will make them valuable is the classic way to fail the test.</p>\n<p>Since 2025 the SEC has narrowed its approach to crypto (for example, staff statements that typical meme coins are not securities), and Congress has passed stablecoin legislation (the <strong>GENIUS Act</strong>, 2025) and worked on wider market-structure rules. Treat any summary, including this one, as a starting point for a lawyer, not an answer.</p>\n<h3 id=\"marketing-a-token-in-the-us\">Marketing a token in the US</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Disclose payment, every time.</strong> The FTC's Endorsement Guides require a clear disclosure of any paid relationship (&quot;#ad&quot;, &quot;Paid partnership&quot;), and if the token is a security, Section 17(b) of the Securities Act requires promoters to disclose what they were paid. In 2022 a celebrity agreed to pay the SEC 1.26 million dollars over an Instagram post promoting a crypto token without disclosing that she had been paid for it.</li><li><strong>No promises of returns.</strong> Saying a token will rise in value is exactly what makes it look like an investment contract, and can be fraud if untrue.</li><li><strong>Know who you are selling to.</strong> Many launches block US users or restrict sales to accredited investors under exemptions such as Regulation D, precisely because of these rules.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"if-you-also-sell-outside-the-us\">If you also sell outside the US</h3>\n<p>Other regions have their own regimes, for example the EU's <strong>MiCA</strong> regulation (white papers and licensed service providers) and the UK's FCA financial promotion rules for crypto marketing. A US-compliant launch is not automatically compliant elsewhere.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Never promise price or returns</p><p>&quot;This will 10x&quot;, &quot;guaranteed APY&quot;, &quot;get in before the pump&quot;: statements like these can turn marketing into an unlawful financial promotion or misrepresentation, and they destroy trust when they fail. Talk about the product, the utility and the risks.</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"A token's contract owner can change the sell tax to 99% at any time. What is this?","options":["A normal feature","A major red flag: the owner could stop holders selling profitably (a honeypot)","A way to reduce gas"],"answer":1,"explain":"Adjustable taxes, blacklists and unlimited minting give the owner power over your money."},{"q":"A brand pays you to post about its new token to your US followers. What do US rules require at a minimum?","options":["Nothing, social posts are free speech","Only that you own some of the token yourself","A clear disclosure that you are paid (FTC), plus disclosure of your compensation under securities law if the token is a security, and no promises of returns"],"answer":2,"explain":"Undisclosed paid promotion has cost promoters dearly: one celebrity paid the SEC 1.26 million dollars over a single Instagram post."},{"q":"Which US agency maintains the sanctions list that includes specific crypto addresses?","options":["The SEC","OFAC, part of the Treasury","The FTC"],"answer":1,"explain":"US persons must not deal with sanctioned people or addresses; screen deposits and claims against the list."},{"q":"Under the Howey test, what makes a token sale look like a securities offering?","options":["People invest money expecting profits from the efforts of the team","The token uses the ERC-20 standard","The token has a fixed supply"],"answer":0,"explain":"The economic reality matters, not the label."},{"q":"What is a sensible first step before launching a token?","options":["Announce the launch date to build hype","Make sure there is a real product and a clear reason for the token, then get legal advice","List it on as many exchanges as possible"],"answer":1,"explain":"A token amplifies a product; it cannot replace one. Legal review comes before any announcement."}],"exercises":[{"title":"An automated red-flag checker","file":"redflags.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Six lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const token = { insiderShare: 0.55, lpLocked: false, ownerCanMint: true };</code></li><li><code>const flags = [];</code></li><li><code>if (token.insiderShare &gt; 0.4) flags.push('Insiders above 40%');</code></li><li><code>if (!token.lpLocked) flags.push('Liquidity not locked');</code></li><li><code>if (token.ownerCanMint) flags.push('Owner can mint');</code></li><li><code>console.log(flags);</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const token = { insiderShare: 0.55, lpLocked: false, ownerCanMint: true };\nconst flags = [];\nif (token.insiderShare > 0.4) flags.push('Insiders above 40%');\nif (!token.lpLocked) flags.push('Liquidity not locked');\nif (token.ownerCanMint) flags.push('Owner can mint');\nconsole.log(flags);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+token\\s*=\\s*\\{\\s*insiderShare\\s*:\\s*0\\.55\\s*,\\s*lpLocked\\s*:\\s*false\\s*,\\s*ownerCanMint\\s*:\\s*true\\s*\\}","text":"Line 1: <code>const token = { insiderShare: 0.55, lpLocked: false, ownerCanMint: true };</code>"},{"re":"const\\s+flags\\s*=\\s*\\[\\s*\\]","text":"Line 2: an empty array: <code>const flags = [];</code>"},{"re":"if\\s*\\(\\s*token\\.insiderShare\\s*>\\s*0\\.4\\s*\\)\\s*flags\\.push\\(\\s*['\"]Insiders above 40%['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3: <code>if (token.insiderShare &gt; 0.4) flags.push('Insiders above 40%');</code>"},{"re":"if\\s*\\(\\s*!\\s*token\\.lpLocked\\s*\\)\\s*flags\\.push\\(\\s*['\"]Liquidity not locked['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"Line 4 uses <code>!</code> for &quot;not&quot;: <code>if (!token.lpLocked) flags.push('Liquidity not locked');</code>"},{"re":"if\\s*\\(\\s*token\\.ownerCanMint\\s*\\)\\s*flags\\.push\\(\\s*['\"]Owner can mint['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"Line 5: <code>if (token.ownerCanMint) flags.push('Owner can mint');</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*flags\\s*\\)","text":"Line 6: <code>console.log(flags);</code>"}],"explain":""}],"text":"A responsible launch, step by step Product first. A live product with real users, and a clear answer to why does this need a token? Design and model supply, allocation, vesting and incentives. Stress-test them (see the modelling lesson). Legal review in every country where you will market or sell. Do this before you announce anything. Build and audit the token, vesting, staking and any distribution contracts. Publish the audit. Distribution plan : who gets tokens and how. Airdrops need sybil filtering (one person, one allocation). Liquidity plan : how the first pools are seeded and whether LP tokens are locked or protocol-owned. If market makers are involved, publish the terms. Transparency : publish the full allocation, the vesting schedule and every contract address before launch. Launch day : monitor contracts, pools and social channels; have an incident plan. After launch : report regularly on treasury, unlocks and emissions. Trust is earned over years. Distribution methods Method How it works Watch out for Airdrop Free tokens to past users or a community Farmers with thousands of wallets; instant sell pressure Points then airdrop Track activity, convert to tokens later Users feel misled if conversion disappoints Fair launch No pre-sale; everyone buys at the same terms Bots and snipers at launch Liquidity bootstrapping pool (LBP) Price starts high and falls over days so buyers can find a fair price Complex for newcomers Public sale (ICO / IDO) Sell tokens for funds Heavy securities-law implications Bonding curve launchpad Price rises with each mint, then migrates to an AMM Highly speculative; early-buyer advantage Red-flag checklist for any token Use this before buying, promoting or advising on a token: Contract : unverified source code; owner can mint without limit, pause trading, blacklist wallets, or change fees and taxes; a proxy that the team can upgrade instantly. Liquidity : LP tokens unlocked or held by one wallet; tiny liquidity relative to market cap. Holders : a handful of wallets hold most of the supply (check the explorer's holders tab). Tokenomics : low float with a huge FDV; insiders above about 40%; short or no vesting; undisclosed allocations. Yield : high APY with no revenue source. Team and marketing : anonymous team plus pressure to buy now; paid promotions without disclosure; guaranteed returns. Honeypot behaviour : you can buy but cannot sell (test with a tiny amount, or use a scanner, before anything else). The rules: US regulation you must know This course follows United States law : most token projects, exchanges and launchpads either operate in the US or serve US users, and US agencies have been the most active enforcers. This is general awareness, not legal advice . US crypto rules have been changing quickly since 2025, through new laws, court cases and agency guidance, so speak to a qualified US securities lawyer before launching or promoting a token. Who What they police Why it matters for a token SEC (Securities and Exchange Commission) Securities: offering, selling and promoting investments If your token is an investment contract, selling it needs registration or an exemption, and promoters must disclose that they are paid CFTC (Commodity Futures Trading Commission) Commodities and their derivatives (bitcoin and ether have been treated as commodities) Fraud and manipulation in commodity markets, and anything leveraged or derivative FinCEN (Treasury) The Bank Secrecy Act: money transmission and anti-money-laundering Businesses that transmit value for others may need to register as a money services business and run KYC and AML programmes OFAC (Treasury) Sanctions Dealing with sanctioned people, countries or listed crypto addresses is illegal for US persons FTC (Federal Trade Commission) Deceptive advertising and paid endorsements Influencers and agencies must clearly disclose paid promotions; misleading claims are illegal IRS Tax Digital assets are treated as property; trades, airdrops and stak","track":"tokenomics"},{"id":"tok-modelling","title":"Modelling a token economy in code","level":"advanced","minutes":40,"summary":"Turn a design into a model, run scenarios and stress tests in Python, and present results honestly.","html":"<h2 id=\"why-model\">Why model</h2>\n<p>A model forces vague ideas (&quot;emissions will reward the community&quot;) into numbers you can question (&quot;emissions add 18% supply in year one while projected fee burn removes 2%&quot;). Its job is to <strong>compare designs and find failure modes</strong>, not to predict a price. Anyone who claims their model predicts token prices is selling something.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-modelling-process\">The modelling process</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Goals</strong>: what must the design achieve? (Secure the network, reward long-term users, fund development for five years.)</li><li><strong>Actors</strong>: who participates, and what do they want? (Team, investors, stakers, LPs, users, speculators.)</li><li><strong>Flows</strong>: every source of new supply and every sink, as a function of time and behaviour.</li><li><strong>Assumptions</strong>: user growth, fees per user, what share of unlocked tokens get sold. Write them down explicitly.</li><li><strong>Scenarios</strong>: bear, base and bull versions of the assumptions.</li><li><strong>Stress tests</strong>: extreme but plausible shocks.</li><li><strong>Decide</strong>: change the design until it survives the bad scenarios.</li></ol>\n<p>Start in a spreadsheet; move to code when you need many scenarios, randomness or agent behaviour. Use the <a href=\"#token-sim\">Tokenomics Simulator</a> for the supply side first.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-supply-and-sell-pressure-model-in-python\">A supply and sell-pressure model in Python</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">uv init token-model &amp;&amp; cd token-model\nuv add pandas matplotlib</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\"><pre><code class=\"language-python\"># model.py\nimport pandas as pd\n\nMAX_SUPPLY = 1_000_000_000\nALLOCATIONS = {\n    # name: (share, tge_unlock, cliff_months, vest_months, sell_ratio_of_unlocks)\n    &quot;team&quot;:       (0.18, 0.00, 12, 36, 0.20),\n    &quot;investors&quot;:  (0.17, 0.00, 12, 24, 0.50),\n    &quot;ecosystem&quot;:  (0.25, 0.05,  0, 48, 0.30),\n    &quot;community&quot;:  (0.15, 0.00,  0, 48, 0.40),\n    &quot;treasury&quot;:   (0.12, 0.10,  6, 36, 0.10),\n    &quot;liquidity&quot;:  (0.07, 1.00,  0,  0, 0.00),\n    &quot;airdrop&quot;:    (0.06, 0.50,  0,  6, 0.60),\n}\n\ndef unlocked(month, tge, cliff, vest):\n    if month &lt; cliff:\n        return tge\n    if vest == 0:\n        return 1.0\n    return tge + (1 - tge) * min(1.0, (month - cliff) / vest)\n\ndef run(months=48, monthly_buy_usd=1_500_000, price=0.10):\n    rows, prev = [], {name: 0.0 for name in ALLOCATIONS}\n    for m in range(months + 1):\n        row = {&quot;month&quot;: m, &quot;circulating&quot;: 0.0, &quot;sell_tokens&quot;: 0.0}\n        for name, (share, tge, cliff, vest, sell_ratio) in ALLOCATIONS.items():\n            amount = share * MAX_SUPPLY * unlocked(m, tge, cliff, vest)\n            new = amount - prev[name]\n            prev[name] = amount\n            row[&quot;circulating&quot;] += amount\n            row[&quot;sell_tokens&quot;] += new * sell_ratio\n        row[&quot;sell_usd&quot;] = row[&quot;sell_tokens&quot;] * price\n        row[&quot;buy_usd&quot;] = monthly_buy_usd\n        row[&quot;pressure_ratio&quot;] = row[&quot;sell_usd&quot;] / row[&quot;buy_usd&quot;]   # above 1 means more sellers than buyers at this price\n        rows.append(row)\n    return pd.DataFrame(rows)\n\nif __name__ == &quot;__main__&quot;:\n    for name, buy in {&quot;bear&quot;: 500_000, &quot;base&quot;: 1_500_000, &quot;bull&quot;: 4_000_000}.items():\n        df = run(monthly_buy_usd=buy)\n        worst = df.loc[df[&quot;pressure_ratio&quot;].idxmax()]\n        months_over = int((df[&quot;pressure_ratio&quot;] &gt; 1).sum())\n        print(f&quot;{name:&gt;4}: worst month {int(worst.month)} (ratio {worst.pressure_ratio:.1f}), &quot;\n              f&quot;{months_over} months with more selling than buying&quot;)</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">uv run model.py</code></pre></div>\n<p>This is deliberately simple. It already answers useful questions: which months need the most demand, how sensitive the design is to investor selling, and whether moving the investor cliff from 12 to 18 months helps.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"going-further\">Going further</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Monte Carlo</strong>: draw assumptions (growth, sell ratios) from ranges instead of single values, run thousands of times, and report the spread of outcomes, not one line.</li><li><strong>Agent-based models</strong>: simulate individual holders with simple rules (&quot;stake if APY &gt; 8%, sell 30% of unlocks if price fell this month&quot;) and watch emergent behaviour. Frameworks: <strong>radCAD</strong> or <strong>cadCAD</strong> in Python.</li><li><strong>Real data</strong>: calibrate assumptions against similar live tokens using Dune queries (actual staking rates, holder behaviour around unlocks, fee revenue).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"stress-tests-to-always-run\">Stress tests to always run</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Shock</th><th>Question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Price falls 80% for 12 months</td><td>Is the treasury still funded? Do stakers stay?</td></tr><tr><td>Demand stays flat for two years</td><td>How bad is unlock pressure?</td></tr><tr><td>Largest holder sells everything</td><td>What happens to pool prices and governance?</td></tr><tr><td>Fee revenue falls 90%</td><td>Does real yield vanish and stakers leave?</td></tr><tr><td>Emissions doubled by a governance vote</td><td>Can the design survive its own governance?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"presenting-results-honestly\">Presenting results honestly</h2>\n<ul><li>Lead with the assumptions and the scenario ranges.</li><li>Show charts of supply, unlocks and pressure, not price predictions.</li><li>State what the model does not capture (sentiment, market-wide crashes, regulation).</li><li>Keep the code and data public so others can check it. That transparency is itself a trust signal.</li></ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-pro\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Portfolio project</p><p>Pick a real token with a published schedule. Rebuild its unlock model, compare it with on-chain data, run the three scenarios, and write a two-page memo with charts and recommendations. That one piece of work demonstrates more tokenomics skill than any certificate.</p></aside>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>","quiz":[{"q":"What is a tokenomics model best used for?","options":["Predicting the exact future token price","Comparing designs and finding failure modes under different assumptions","Replacing a legal review"],"answer":1,"explain":"Models test designs. They cannot predict markets."},{"q":"Why run bear, base and bull scenarios instead of one forecast?","options":["Outcomes depend heavily on uncertain assumptions; a design should survive the bad cases, not just the expected one","Investors require exactly three scenarios","It makes the charts look better"],"answer":0,"explain":"Robust designs are judged by their worst plausible outcomes."},{"q":"In the Python model, what does a pressure_ratio above 1 mean?","options":["The token is undervalued","In that month, the dollar value of expected selling from unlocks exceeds assumed buying demand at that price","Unlocks have finished"],"answer":1,"explain":"It flags months where the design needs more demand than assumed to hold the price."}],"exercises":[{"title":"The unlock function in Python","file":"model.py","lang":"python","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write the <code>unlocked</code> function from the model above in Python. Indent with 4 spaces (Tab does it for you):</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\"><pre><code class=\"language-python\">def unlocked(month, tge, cliff, vest):\n    if month &lt; cliff:\n        return tge\n    if vest == 0:\n        return 1.0\n    return tge + (1 - tge) * min(1.0, (month - cliff) / vest)</code></pre></div>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"def unlocked(month, tge, cliff, vest):\n    if month < cliff:\n        return tge\n    if vest == 0:\n        return 1.0\n    return tge + (1 - tge) * min(1.0, (month - cliff) / vest)","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^def\\s+unlocked\\s*\\(\\s*month\\s*,\\s*tge\\s*,\\s*cliff\\s*,\\s*vest\\s*\\)\\s*:","text":"Line 1: <code>def unlocked(month, tge, cliff, vest):</code> ending with a colon."},{"re":"^ {4}if\\s+month\\s*<\\s*cliff\\s*:","text":"Line 2, indented 4 spaces: <code>if month &lt; cliff:</code>"},{"re":"^ {8}return\\s+tge\\s*$","text":"Line 3, indented 8 spaces: <code>return tge</code>"},{"re":"^ {4}if\\s+vest\\s*==\\s*0\\s*:","text":"Line 4, back to 4 spaces: <code>if vest == 0:</code>"},{"re":"^ {8}return\\s+1\\.0\\s*$","text":"Line 5, 8 spaces: <code>return 1.0</code>"},{"re":"^ {4}return\\s+tge\\s*\\+\\s*\\(\\s*1\\s*-\\s*tge\\s*\\)\\s*\\*\\s*min\\(\\s*1\\.0\\s*,\\s*\\(\\s*month\\s*-\\s*cliff\\s*\\)\\s*\\/\\s*vest\\s*\\)","text":"Line 6, 4 spaces: <code>return tge + (1 - tge) * min(1.0, (month - cliff) / vest)</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"Set up and run the model","file":"Terminal","lang":"bash","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Four commands: create a uv project called <code>token-model</code>, move into it, add <code>pandas</code> and <code>matplotlib</code>, then run <code>model.py</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"uv init token-model\ncd token-model\nuv add pandas matplotlib\nuv run model.py","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*uv\\s+init\\s+token-model\\s*$","text":"Line 1: <code>uv init token-model</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*cd\\s+token-model\\s*$","text":"Line 2: <code>cd token-model</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*uv\\s+add\\s+pandas\\s+matplotlib\\s*$","text":"Line 3: <code>uv add pandas matplotlib</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*uv\\s+run\\s+model\\.py\\s*$","text":"Line 4: <code>uv run model.py</code>"}],"explain":""}],"text":"Why model A model forces vague ideas ( emissions will reward the community ) into numbers you can question ( emissions add 18% supply in year one while projected fee burn removes 2% ). Its job is to compare designs and find failure modes , not to predict a price. Anyone who claims their model predicts token prices is selling something. The modelling process Goals : what must the design achieve? (Secure the network, reward long-term users, fund development for five years.) Actors : who participates, and what do they want? (Team, investors, stakers, LPs, users, speculators.) Flows : every source of new supply and every sink, as a function of time and behaviour. Assumptions : user growth, fees per user, what share of unlocked tokens get sold. Write them down explicitly. Scenarios : bear, base and bull versions of the assumptions. Stress tests : extreme but plausible shocks. Decide : change the design until it survives the bad scenarios. Start in a spreadsheet; move to code when you need many scenarios, randomness or agent behaviour. Use the Tokenomics Simulator for the supply side first. A supply and sell-pressure model in Python uv init token-model cd token-model uv add pandas matplotlib # model.py import pandas as pd MAX_SUPPLY = 1_000_000_000 ALLOCATIONS = { # name: (share, tge_unlock, cliff_months, vest_months, sell_ratio_of_unlocks) team : (0.18, 0.00, 12, 36, 0.20), investors : (0.17, 0.00, 12, 24, 0.50), ecosystem : (0.25, 0.05, 0, 48, 0.30), community : (0.15, 0.00, 0, 48, 0.40), treasury : (0.12, 0.10, 6, 36, 0.10), liquidity : (0.07, 1.00, 0, 0, 0.00), airdrop : (0.06, 0.50, 0, 6, 0.60), } def unlocked(month, tge, cliff, vest): if month cliff: return tge if vest == 0: return 1.0 return tge + (1 - tge) * min(1.0, (month - cliff) / vest) def run(months=48, monthly_buy_usd=1_500_000, price=0.10): rows, prev = [], {name: 0.0 for name in ALLOCATIONS} for m in range(months + 1): row = { month : m, circulating : 0.0, sell_tokens : 0.0} for name, (share, tge, cliff, vest, sell_ratio) in ALLOCATIONS.items(): amount = share * MAX_SUPPLY * unlocked(m, tge, cliff, vest) new = amount - prev[name] prev[name] = amount row[ circulating ] += amount row[ sell_tokens ] += new * sell_ratio row[ sell_usd ] = row[ sell_tokens ] * price row[ buy_usd ] = monthly_buy_usd row[ pressure_ratio ] = row[ sell_usd ] / row[ buy_usd ] # above 1 means more sellers than buyers at this price rows.append(row) return pd.DataFrame(rows) if __name__ == __main__ : for name, buy in { bear : 500_000, base : 1_500_000, bull : 4_000_000}.items(): df = run(monthly_buy_usd=buy) worst = df.loc[df[ pressure_ratio ].idxmax()] months_over = int((df[ pressure_ratio ] 1).sum()) print(f {name: 4}: worst month {int(worst.month)} (ratio {worst.pressure_ratio:.1f}), f {months_over} months with more selling than buying ) uv run model.py This is deliberately simple. It already answers useful questions: which months need the most demand, how sensitive the design is to investor selling, and whether moving the investor cliff from 12 to 18 months helps. Going further Monte Carlo : draw assumptions (growth, sell ratios) from ranges instead of single values, run thousands of times, and report the spread of outcomes, not one line. Agent-based models : simulate individual holders with simple rules ( stake if APY 8%, sell 30% of unlocks if price fell this month ) and watch emergent behaviour. Frameworks: radCAD or cadCAD in Python. Real data : calibrate assumptions against similar live tokens using Dune queries (actual staking rates, holder behaviour around unlocks, fee revenue). Stress tests to always run Shock Question Price falls 80% for 12 months Is the treasury still funded? Do stakers stay? Demand stays flat for two years How bad is unlock pressure? Largest holder sells everything What happens to pool prices and governance? Fee revenue falls 90% Does real yield vanish and stakers leave? Emissions doubled by a governance vote Can the design survive its own governance? Presenting re","track":"tokenomics"}];
